   "name": "Russia-Ukraine War",
   "status": "untested",
   "status_label": "AUGUST 28: THE AGENDA OF THE UNANNOUNCED MOSCOW VISIT WAS DISCLOSED AND IT LINKED THE TWO WARS. CIA DIRECTOR JOHN RATCLIFFE URGED RUSSIAN OFFICIALS NOT TO TAKE ACTIONS THAT COULD ESCALATE TENSIONS WITH ESTONIA, LATVIA AND LITHUANIA, AND USED THE SAME AUGUST 25 VISIT TO PRESS MOSCOW TO REDUCE ITS MILITARY AND ECONOMIC SUPPORT FOR IRAN. THE UNDERLYING US ASSESSMENT IS THAT RUSSIA MAY TEST NATO'S READINESS TO RESPOND TO LIMITED AGGRESSION IN THE COMING YEARS VIA A LIMITED MILITARY OPERATION OR A HYBRID ATTACK, WITH THE BALTIC STATES AMONG THE MOST LIKELY TARGETS. PESKOV CONFIRMED THE MEETING WAS WITH RUSSIAN INTELLIGENCE REPRESENTATIVES AND NOT WITH PUTIN, WHO WAS BRIEFED AFTERWARDS; TRUMP CALLED THE TRIP 'SEMI-ROUTINE' AND POSSIBLY LINKED TO ENDING THE UKRAINE WAR; RATCLIFFE BRIEFED TRUMP AT THE WHITE HOUSE ON RETURN. THE FIGHTING DID NOT PAUSE FOR IT. OVERNIGHT INTO AUGUST 27 RUSSIA STRUCK KYIV AND OTHER CITIES WITH BALLISTIC MISSILES AND DRONES — SIX EXPLOSIONS IN THE CAPITAL INSIDE A MINUTE AROUND 3 A.M., AT LEAST SEVEN INJURED, TARGETS INCLUDING WAREHOUSES, PORTS AND ENERGY AND INDUSTRIAL FACILITIES PER FOREIGN MINISTER SYBIHA — AND UKRAINE DOWNED SEVEN BALLISTIC MISSILES, ONE ANTI-SHIP CRUISE MISSILE AND ABOUT 90% OF THE DRONES. UKRAINE'S DEEP-STRIKE CAMPAIGN REACHED THE REFINING BASE: THE NORSI PLANT AT KSTOVO, RUSSIA'S FOURTH-LARGEST REFINERY AND SECOND-LARGEST GASOLINE PRODUCER, SUSPENDED CRUDE PROCESSING AFTER AN OVERNIGHT DRONE STRIKE — ITS FIFTH DRONE-RELATED SHUTDOWN IN UNDER FIVE MONTHS — AND UKRAINIAN DRONES ARE REPORTED TO HAVE SHUT ALL MAJOR LUKOIL REFINERIES. A UKRAINIAN STRIKE HIT A BUS IN RUSSIAN-CONTROLLED LUHANSK, KILLING NINE. EU STATES ARE PLANNING A RENEWED EFFORT TO LEVERAGE FROZEN RUSSIAN SOVEREIGN ASSETS FOR UKRAINE. ZELENSKY AWARDED ELON MUSK THE ORDER OF FREEDOM FOR STARLINK. NET RUSSIAN GAINS FOR 2026 REMAIN 81.1 SQUARE KILOMETRES AND THE LINE IS STATIC. NO NATO TROOP COMMITMENT. || AUGUST 27: THE HIGHEST-LEVEL AMERICAN VISIT TO MOSCOW SINCE JANUARY HAPPENED WITHOUT ANNOUNCEMENT. CIA DIRECTOR JOHN RATCLIFFE FLEW TO MOSCOW ON AUGUST 25 ABOARD A US GOVERNMENT TRANSPORT FROM JOINT BASE ANDREWS, DETECTED THROUGH PUBLIC FLIGHT-TRACKING DATA — THE FIRST KNOWN VISIT BY A US SPY CHIEF SINCE WILLIAM BURNS MET PUTIN IN NOVEMBER 2021. THE KREMLIN SAID HE MET RUSSIAN INTELLIGENCE COUNTERPARTS; PESKOV SAID PUTIN DID NOT PLAN TO MEET HIM AND NOTHING OF THE AGENDA HAS BEEN DISCLOSED. TRUMP ADMINISTRATION OFFICIALS HAD ASKED KYIV SEVERAL DAYS EARLIER TO PAUSE STRIKES ON MOSCOW AND OTHER CITIES FROM MONDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY. ON THE SAME DAY PESKOV SAID THERE WERE NO PLANS FOR TALKS WITH WITKOFF OR KUSHNER THIS WEEK — 'NO, THERE ARE NO SUCH PLANS.' THE WAR DID NOT PAUSE: RUSSIAN ATTACKS KILLED FOUR AND INJURED 95 ACROSS UKRAINE OVER AUGUST 25-26, WITH 163 DRONES LAUNCHED OVERNIGHT INTO AUGUST 26, 132 DOWNED AND STRIKES AT 18 LOCATIONS. A RUSSIAN FPV DRONE CARRIED OUT A DOUBLE-TAP ON RESCUE WORKERS IN KRAMATORSK, HITTING AN ARMOURED STATE EMERGENCY SERVICE AMBULANCE — THE SECOND ATTACK ON RESCUERS THAT WEEK AFTER KRYVYI RIH ON AUGUST 21. KRAMATORSK CARRIED 26 INJURED AND 37 DAMAGED HIGH-RISES; IN CHERNIHIV OBLAST A DRONE KILLED A 39-YEAR-OLD WOMAN AND HER FOUR-YEAR-OLD DAUGHTER. UKRAINE STRUCK A WILDBERRIES LOGISTICS HUB IN TAMBOV OBLAST, WITH FIRE ACROSS MORE THAN 100,000 SQUARE METRES. ISW RECORDS RUSSIA ESCALATING STRIKES ON UKRAINIAN-MOLDOVAN BORDER INFRASTRUCTURE AND UKRAINIAN ADVANCES IN THE SLOVYANSK AND HULYAIPOLE DIRECTIONS AND WESTERN ZAPORIZHIA OBLAST. NO NATO TROOP COMMITMENT WAS ANNOUNCED. || AUGUST 25: UKRAINE'S 35TH INDEPENDENCE DAY WAS MARKED UNDER A 143-DRONE, EIGHT-MISSILE OVERNIGHT BARRAGE. Russia launched TWO ONIKS ANTI-SHIP MISSILES, SIX KH-31P AND KH-59 GUIDED AIR-LAUNCHED MISSILES AND 143 DRONES (roughly a third jet-powered) into Ukraine overnight into AUGUST 24; air defences downed 109 drones, one Oniks, two Kh-31P/Kh-59 and two Banderol munitions, with strikes recorded at 22 LOCATIONS and debris at five. RUSSIAN ATTACKS KILLED EIGHT AND INJURED 43 over the day: KHARKIV OBLAST 5 killed / 23 injured including two children, with two women killed and six injured in the LOZOVA community including an eleven-year-old girl with burns; DONETSK 2 killed / 7 injured, both deaths in KRAMATORSK; SUMY 1 killed / 12 injured; ZAPORIZHZHIA and DNIPROPETROVSK 3 injured each; CHERNIHIV 1 injured. THE CEREMONY'S MOST SIGNIFICANT FACT WAS AN ABSENCE: European leaders travelled to Kyiv and NO UNITED STATES REPRESENTATIVE ATTENDED. British Prime Minister ANDY BURNHAM, on his first foreign trip since taking office, pledged that MBDA WOULD RELEASE CLASSIFIED DATA ON UK COMPONENTS FOR THE SCALP MISSILE to enable Ukrainian local assembly lines — production capability rather than stock, which is what sponsors transfer when inventories are constrained. Norwegian Prime Minister JONAS GAHR STORE committed $9 BILLION for 2027, matching this year's record total. Zelensky pressed the roughly 34-nation COALITION OF THE WILLING for expanded air defence and the EU for accelerated delivery of its promised €90 BILLION LOAN against a $27 BILLION military budget deficit. ISW assessed that Russian forces recently advanced EAST OF SLOVYANSK, shaping for frontal assaults toward the city and the rest of the FORTRESS BELT, and that Putin continues to set unrealistic deadlines for seizing all of Donetsk Oblast; on AUGUST 23 Zelensky reportedly presented a joint UKRAINE-US-EUROPE proposal offering significant Ukrainian concessions, unreciprocated by the Kremlin. NO NATO COMMITMENT AND NO FOREIGN TROOP DEPLOYMENT WAS ANNOUNCED. || AUGUST 24: THE LINE HOLDS AND THE NEGOTIATING LANGUAGE MOVES A NOTCH WITH A PRECONDITION BUILT IN. On AUGUST 23 Russian guided bombs struck IZYUM, wounding two and damaging at least twenty buildings, vehicles and power lines; overnight Ukrainian forces struck A RADAR STATION AND A STORAGE AND LAUNCH SITE FOR UNMANNED SURFACE VESSELS in occupied CRIMEA. On AUGUST 21 DEPUTY FOREIGN MINISTER SERGEY RYABKOV said Moscow is ready to listen to 'NEW IDEAS' for ending the war provided they align with PUTIN'S POSITION and with 'REALITIES ON THE GROUND' — a formula that converts occupied territory into a starting assumption. PRESIDENT ZELENSKY said Ukraine has developed a plan with its US AND EUROPEAN PARTNERS envisaging A CEASEFIRE, A FREE ECONOMIC ZONE AND A WITHDRAWAL OF TROOPS. Ukraine's General Staff put cumulative Russian personnel losses at ABOUT 1,476,580 as of August 23 (Ukrainian claim). NET RUSSIAN TERRITORIAL GAINS FOR 2026 REMAIN 81.1 SQUARE KILOMETRES. The Iran war continues to reach this theatre through the interceptor market: Ukrainian forces are developing domestic solutions for targeting Russian ballistic launchers and jet-powered drones specifically to circumvent dependence on foreign materiel, and NORTH KOREAN DRONE OPERATORS remain reported in KURSK OBLAST. || AUGUST 23: THE INTERCEPTOR GAP HAS STOPPED BEING A SUPPLY PROBLEM FOR KYIV AND BECOME A DESIGN BRIEF. ISW'S AUGUST 21 ASSESSMENT records UKRAINIAN FORCES ADAPTING TACTICS AND DOMESTICALLY DEVELOPING TECHNOLOGICAL SOLUTIONS TO TARGET RUSSIAN BALLISTIC MISSILE LAUNCHERS AND JET-POWERED DRONES SPECIFICALLY IN ORDER TO CIRCUMVENT THE NEED FOR FOREIGN MATERIEL AMID GLOBAL AIR-DEFENCE INTERCEPTOR SHORTAGES — a recipient state's judgement about the reliability of the American arsenal, expressed in procurement rather than in rhetoric, and the clearest second-order consequence yet of the Iran war's munitions drain. The same assessment records RUSSIA LAUNCHING 135 LONG-RANGE STRIKE DRONES and UKRAINIAN STRIKES ON RUSSIAN MILITARY AIRFIELDS AND OIL INFRASTRUCTURE. NORTH KOREA HAS REPORTEDLY DEPLOYED DRONE OPERATORS TO KURSK OBLAST — a shift from manpower to capability transfer. A SENIOR RUSSIAN OFFICIAL REITERATED THE KREMLIN'S UNWILLINGNESS TO NEGOTIATE MEANINGFULLY, and Peskov confirmed on July 22 that PUTIN'S JUNE 2024 MAXIMALIST TERMS REMAIN THE BASIS OF RUSSIAN CONDITIONS. NET RUSSIAN GAINS FOR 2026 STAND AT 81.1 SQUARE KILOMETRES. || AUGUST 22: MOSCOW IS NOW BUILDING ITS CAMPAIGN AROUND THE INTERCEPTOR GAP THE IRAN WAR CREATED. UKRAINE'S GUR DEPUTY HEAD MAJ. GEN. VADYM SKIBITSKYI SAID ON AUGUST 10 that Russia has been REPRIORITISING BALLISTIC AND CRUISE MISSILE PRODUCTION SINCE APRIL 2026 SPECIFICALLY TO EXPLOIT UKRAINIAN PATRIOT SHORTAGES, and has MORE THAN TRIPLED MONTHLY BALLISTIC LAUNCHES BETWEEN APRIL AND JULY. The shortage is downstream of the Iran war — CSIS puts remaining US Patriot stocks under 827 from ~2,200 pre-war, 60%+ OF PATRIOT AND THAAD EXPENDED, THREE-PLUS YEARS TO REBUILD — after ZELENSKY SAID ON AUGUST 1 THAT UKRAINE HAS RUN OUT OF INTERCEPTORS, one of twenty-seven ballistic missiles downed. UKRAINE'S STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS CENTRE REPORTED ON AUGUST 8, CITING US INTELLIGENCE, THAT RUSSIA IS PREPARING A MAJOR STRIKE CAMPAIGN AGAINST KYIV'S ENERGY INFRASTRUCTURE AHEAD OF INDEPENDENCE DAY ON AUGUST 24; ZELENSKY SAID THE SAME DAY UKRAINE HAS EFFECTIVELY NO UNDAMAGED POWER PLANTS LEFT. ON AUGUST 20 RUSSIAN DRONES PENETRATED MOLDOVAN AND ROMANIAN AIRSPACE AND ROMANIA SCRAMBLED TWO F-16s TO DESTROY AN EXPLOSIVE NAVAL DRONE NEAR A BLACK SEA GAS PLATFORM; ISW ASSESSED THAT THE KREMLIN HAS ACCEPTED THE RISK THAT ITS WAR WILL DAMAGE OTHER STATES INCLUDING NATO MEMBERS — and no Article 4 consultation followed. ISW ALSO ASSESSED THAT WAR VETERANS WILL LIKELY BE SEATED IN THE STATE DUMA AT THE SEPTEMBER 2026 ELECTIONS as part of militarising Russian society, and that MOSCOW'S RESPONSE TO DOMESTIC GASOLINE SHORTAGES REMAINS INADEQUATE. UKRAINIAN FORCES ADVANCED NORTHEAST OF KHARKIV CITY; ISW'S STANDING ASSESSMENT THAT RUSSIA'S SPRING-SUMMER 2026 OFFENSIVE FAILED TO ACHIEVE OPERATIONALLY SIGNIFICANT GAINS IS UNREVISED. NO TALKS CONVENED. (ISW August 8, 10 and 20, 2026 via Kyiv Post and Critical Threats; RFE/RL; CNBC; WSJ and Reuters via Just Security, August 21, 2026; CSIS.) || AUGUST 2: THE INTERCEPTOR GAP BECOMES THE WAR'S CENTRAL FACT — AND IT LINKS TO IRAN. Russia's overnight barrage into AUGUST 1 killed AT LEAST NINE and wounded THIRTY across KYIV and surrounding regions, FOUR CHILDREN among the casualties — and UKRAINE SHOT DOWN ONLY ONE OF TWENTY-SEVEN BALLISTIC MISSILES. ZELENSKY SAID UKRAINE HAS RUN OUT OF INTERCEPTORS and warned its air defences are being OVERWHELMED; it was the SECOND MAJOR AERIAL ASSAULT IN TWO DAYS and the second ballistic attack on Kyiv in a week, read in Kyiv as a deliberate Russian effort to EXPOSE THE SHORTAGE rather than take ground. THE MISSING WEAPON IS PATRIOT — and CSIS puts remaining US stocks at FEWER THAN 827 INTERCEPTORS from ~2,200 PRE-WAR, MORE THAN 60% OF PATRIOT AND THAAD EXPENDED IN THE IRAN WAR, THREE-PLUS YEARS TO REBUILD, with three sources telling CNN the figures track internal government numbers. Zelensky had pressed Trump on JULY 28 for LICENCES TO PRODUCE PATRIOT INTERCEPTORS IN UKRAINE; that request now sits against a supplier whose own inventory is the constraint. ACROSS TWO BARRAGES THE BALLISTIC INTERCEPT RATE IS TWO OF THIRTY-SIX. UKRAINE ANSWERED ASYMMETRICALLY, SINKING A RUSSIAN CONTAINER SHIP IN THE BLACK SEA. ISW ASSESSED ON JULY 31 that Russia's NORTHERN GROUPING OF FORCES AND HIGH COMMAND ARE LIKELY LYING TO THE KREMLIN about gains in northern Ukraine, that battlefield evidence DOES NOT CORROBORATE claimed advances in NORTHERN SUMY AND KHARKIV, and that Ukrainian forces are COUNTERATTACKING — within its standing judgement that the spring-summer 2026 offensive has failed to achieve operationally significant gains. POLAND FORMALLY PROTESTED TO THE RUSSIAN AMBASSADOR over the JULY 30 KH-101 detonation near Tarnawa-Kolonia and asserts the missile was RECENTLY MANUFACTURED; ARTICLE 4 WAS NOT INVOKED. (CNN; The Washington Post; UPI; ISW via Kyiv Post; Al Jazeera; CSIS; Military Times, July 28 – August 1, 2026.) || AUGUST 1: THE WAR PUTS ORDNANCE ON NATO SOIL. A projectile assessed by experts as a RUSSIAN KH-101 CRUISE MISSILE crossed into POLISH AIRSPACE during Russia's overnight barrage and DETONATED IN A FIELD NEAR TARNAWA-KOLONIA, outside LUBLIN, leaving a TEN-METRE CRATER. NATO's military headquarters said the alliance ACTIVATED A WIDE RANGE OF AIR DEFENCES and SCRAMBLED FIGHTERS, and NATO said it would \"DEFEND TERRITORY\"; WARSAW BLAMED MOSCOW; no Polish casualties. The same barrage — 70+ MISSILES AND ~280 DRONES against KYIV and LVIV — killed AT LEAST EIGHT, INCLUDING THREE CHILDREN, with later counts of nine and ten; ZELENSKY renewed his air-defence plea and ordered asymmetric measures. UKRAINE ANSWERED DEEP: on JULY 31 long-range strikes hit OIL REFINERIES AND WILDBERRIES LOGISTICS FACILITIES in VOLGOGRAD, TATARSTAN and KRASNODAR, after Zelensky's July 30 video address promising to INTENSIFY STRIKES ON RUSSIAN TERRITORY. THE LINE BARELY MOVED: 226 COMBAT CLASHES by 22:00 July 30, heaviest at KOSTIANTYNIVKA and POKROVSK; THE ECONOMIST puts Russia's 30-day gain at ZERO SQUARE MILES and DEEPSTATE the four-week net at TEN (against 31 in the prior period). Negotiator SERGIY KYSLYTSYA argues Kyiv now has leverage because the deep-strike campaign is causing Russian fuel shortages; 66% OF UKRAINIANS want a negotiated end as soon as possible. (The Washington Post; Al Jazeera; Foreign Policy; The Irish Times; Stars and Stripes; CNN; NPR; TIME; Mezha/Bukvy; Charter'97; ISW; Russia Matters, July 28-31, 2026.) || JULY 31: RUSSIA FIRES 70+ MISSILES AND ~280 DRONES OVERNIGHT INTO JULY 30, KILLING AT LEAST 8 (later reports 9 and 10), WITH KYIV AND LVIV THE MAIN TARGETS — AND A RUSSIAN CRUISE MISSILE CROSSES INTO POLAND. NATO confirmed the incursion and SCRAMBLED JETS; the missile landed in a rural, uninhabited area. TUSK at an emergency meeting: \"all the indications are that it was a Russian Kh-101 ballistic missile, but we want to be 100% CERTAIN about the type of missile and who launched it\" — no direct threat, investigation ordered. (The Kh-101 is in fact an air-launched CRUISE missile.) THE DECISIVE NUMBER IS THE INTERCEPT RATIO: Ukraine downed 55 of 70+ missiles and 265 of ~280 drones BUT ONLY ONE OF NINE BALLISTIC MISSILES — precisely the gap Patriot fills, two days after Zelensky pressed Trump and US defence firms for faster and larger Patriot deliveries, and while CENTCOM runs waves twice the size of their predecessors out of the same magazine. Context: the Senate advanced the Lindsey O. Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act of 2026 by 86-12 on July 28. (Just Security; NPR/AP; The Philadelphia Inquirer/AP; Daily Herald/AP, July 30, 2026.) || JULY 30: THE SENATE ADVANCES THE GRAHAM RUSSIA-AND-IRAN SANCTIONS BILL 86-12 WITH ZELENSKY WATCHING FROM THE GALLERY, hours after Graham's funeral and following his closed-door appeal to senators. The LINDSEY O. GRAHAM SANCTIONING RUSSIA AND IRAN ACT OF 2026 authorises TARIFFS OF UP TO 200% on the largest purchasers of Russian oil and gas — including CHINA and INDIA — and expands sanctions on Russian banks, political elites, oligarchs and the SHADOW FLEET. AT THE WHITE HOUSE Zelensky offered UKRAINIAN DRONE-COUNTERMEASURE EXPERTISE in exchange for PATRIOT MISSILES — putting two US-supported wars in direct competition for a magazine CENTCOM has drawn down by 11,000+ munitions in 16 days. PESKOV'S DENIAL that any aerial-ceasefire talks exist stands unretracted. ON THE GROUND (July 28): 226 CLASHES, POKROVSK hottest with 29 ASSAULTS and 32 Russians killed there; 59 airstrikes with 190 guided bombs, 6,125 KAMIKAZE DRONES, 2,047 attacks on positions; 54 attacks in Northern Slobozhanshchyna/Kursk. ZELENSKY (July 27): Russia is preparing VORONEZH OBLAST bases for ~30,000 MORE NORTH KOREAN TROOPS atop ~15,000 already deployed. (Kyiv Post; RFE/RL; Axios; ABC News; Ukrinform; Just Security; AEI, July 27-29, 2026.) || JULY 29 (Day 153): ZELENSKY GETS THE OVAL OFFICE, THE SENATE MOVES THE GRAHAM BILL, AND MOSCOW DENIES THE AIR CEASEFIRE EXISTS. Zelensky met Trump behind closed doors on the morning of July 28 and wrote afterwards, 'Good meeting with President Trump in the Oval Office. Thank you for all that we are doing together to protect the lives of Ukrainians and achieve peace'; a White House official said 'now is the time to end the war.' The Ukrainian ask was an AIR-ONLY CEASEFIRE, narrower than the stalled comprehensive settlement — but KREMLIN SPOKESMAN DMITRY PESKOV DENIED any aerial-ceasefire talks are under way, and Moscow warned intensified Ukrainian strikes inside Russia would prolong the war. THE SENATE took a procedural vote Tuesday night on the 'LINDSEY O. GRAHAM SANCTIONING RUSSIA AND IRAN ACT OF 2026' — renamed for its late author on the day of his funeral and expanded at Trump's request to add Iran — with 60+ cosponsors, setting up final passage later in the week; it targets Russian officials, oligarchs and family members, foreign persons, Russian banks and the SHADOW FLEET, plus stiff tariffs on CHINA, INDIA and others buying Russian energy. ON THE GROUND: 226 combat engagements July 28, POKROVSK hottest with 29 Russian assaults, 27 repelled in the KOSTIANTYNIVKA sector near Kostiantynivka, Illinivka and toward Stepanivka, after the Khortytsia group's July 27 confirmation of Russian advances on the wedge between the two towns. ISW: tactical gains possible at Kostiantynivka this summer, operational victory over the 'belt of fortresses' unlikely. NETANYAHU AND ZELENSKY spoke in person at Graham's funeral — their first face-to-face since September 2023, serious but cordial; a formal meeting was explored and declined. Ukraine's FM told Iran's to 'end any support for Russia's war'; Iran's FM demanded 'restitution for losses' over the July 25 Caspian strike. (Al Jazeera; Channels Television; Kyiv Post; Reuters via WSAU; CNBC; ABC News; Ukrinform; ISW; The Times of Israel, July 28, 2026.) || JULY 28 (Day 152): ZELENSKY TO THE WHITE HOUSE TUESDAY WITH AN AIR-CEASEFIRE PROPOSAL — the same day as Netanyahu, alongside Sen. Lindsey Graham's funeral. Ukrainian and US officials have been working on a proposal for a ceasefire in the AIR war to put to Russian officials, a narrower ask than the comprehensive settlement that has repeatedly stalled over Russian demands that Ukraine withdraw from territory it still holds. ON THE GROUND: Ukraine's KHORTYTSIA group confirmed July 27 that Russian troops ADVANCED between POKROVSK and KOSTIANTYNIVKA in Donetsk Oblast; the Pokrovsk axis remains Russia's main effort with a push toward Dnipropetrovsk Oblast, and DeepState reports Russian forces inside Pokrovsk with a 'search and destruction' operation under way. ISW's standing assessment: Russian forces lack the density for simultaneous offensives against both towns, and Kostiantynivka gains remain small-group infiltration. ZELENSKY WARNS OF 30,000 MORE NORTH KOREAN TROOPS with facilities readied in VORONEZH since June, atop ~11,000 DPRK personnel in Kursk and an estimated 25-40% DPRK share of Russian artillery ammunition. Kyiv requests the European 'FREYJA' anti-ballistic prototype by mid-2027. IRAN'S parliamentary security chief EBRAHIM AZIZI threatens retaliation for Ukraine's July 25 Caspian strike on an Iranian vessel. (Kyiv Post; HNGN; Bloomberg/Liga; Ukrinform/Yahoo; Critical Threats/ISW; Just Security; CBS News, July 27, 2026.) || JULY 27: KYIV OPENS A CASPIAN FRONT AGAINST IRANIAN MILITARY CARGO. Zelensky confirmed long-range strikes on a Russian warship and 'vessels used in military cargo shipments involving Iran,' plus Kirov (~1,200 km), Tyumen, Yekaterinburg and Rostov-on-Don; Iran said one sailor was killed, summoned Ukraine's chargé d'affaires, and Araghchi called it a 'blatant UN Charter violation' done 'at Israel's behest' that 'cannot go unanswered.' Zelensky alleged Russia feeds Iran satellite imagery of Gulf states and US facilities with 'a clear correlation' to Iranian strikes. Overnight July 26: Ukrainian strikes on a Chornomornaftogaz facility and drone-relay equipment in occupied Crimea and a bridge in occupied Donetsk; Russian attacks killed at least 7 civilians and injured 58 in a day, with ballistic missiles on Kyiv. Zelensky warned Putin is 'preparing the conditions for expanding mobilization' and seeking 30,000 more North Korean troops. Tokayev called for freezing the war and reviving a revised Istanbul framework. No US-brokered round in session; no NATO troop commitment. || JULY 26: HIGH-TEMPO ATTRITION, TALKS FROZEN. July 25 strikes killed 15 (12 in partly occupied Zaporizhzhia from Ukrainian attacks; 3 in Sumy from a Russian drone strike). The July 24 24-hour picture: 192 combat engagements (24 in the Pokrovsk direction), 5 missiles, 186 guided bombs across 56 airstrikes, 5,746 one-way attack drones, 2,297 shellings; 3 Iskander-M/S-400 ballistic missiles at Kyiv Oblast's Bucha district (1 intercepted, 2 struck). ISW: confirmed Russian advances near Kryva Luka, Hlushkivka and Novodanilovka against decelerating overall momentum; Peskov reaffirms Putin's June 2024 maximalist terms as the precondition for talks. ~20% occupied, no confirmed net change; no US ground involvement. (AP/Washington Times; ISW; GlobalSecurity, July 22-25, 2026.) || JULY 25: Kostiantynivka STILL CONTESTED — Russia again claims to hold the town, Ukraine denies; Moscow proposed a six-hour local ceasefire for a body handover and Peskov mocked Zelensky's offer to meet Putin in the city. No confirmed net change; ISW-adjacent analysis holds the Russian spring-summer 2026 offensive failed to achieve operationally significant advances (~20% held, no net change). (Al Jazeera; Critical Threats/ISW; Russia Matters, July 2026.) || JULY 23: Kostiantynivka STILL CONTESTED — Russia again claims to hold the town, Ukraine denies; no confirmed net change and no US ground surge. Zelensky announced military-leadership changes to 'force Russia to peace,' reiterating the aim of 'victory over the enemy'; Russia still holds ~20% of Ukraine. (Forbes/Katya Soldak; Al Jazeera, July 23, 2026.) || JULY 19-20: Kostiantynivka STILL CONTESTED and peace talks STILL STALLED — no confirmed Russian territorial gains despite Moscow's July 3 'seizure' claim; Ukraine's 19th Army Corps (Madyar) reports pushing infiltrators back out at ~53:3 losses; Russia holds ~20% with no confirmed net change. Zelensky reoffered to meet Putin in the town if truly captured; polls hold ~60% of Russians / ~66% of Ukrainians favoring negotiations. No US ground surge. (CNN; GlobalSecurity; Russia Matters, July 2026.) || JULY 18: Kostiantynivka stays CONTESTED — CNN (July 16) reports Putin's capture claim is false and 'exposes the slow pace of Russia's bloody advance,' with Russian and Ukrainian positions inside the town interspersed; Zelensky urged Putin to meet him there if it is truly captured. The Economist put Russia's gain at ~31 sq km over 30 days. No US ground surge. (CNN; Russia Matters; Economist, July 2026.)  || JULY 15-16 — KOSTIANTYNIVKA STAYS CONTESTED; PUTIN REJECTS TALKS AND SIGNALS ESCALATION: Russian and Ukrainian positions remain interspersed inside the 'fortress belt' city (frontline hard to fix); Putin rejected calls to negotiate and was reported likely to escalate, emboldened by Ukraine's drone strikes on Russian refineries/ports (Syzran July 12, Omsk July 6). Zelensky again sought face-to-face talks but rejects Moscow as a venue; polls show ~60% of Russians and ~66% of Ukrainians favor negotiations (open-question: kostiantynivka-control-contested-july-2026). No US ground surge; the US withdrew another Brigade Combat Team from Europe. || JULY 14-15 — KOSTIANTYNIVKA STILL CONTESTED; PEACE TALKS STAY STALLED: Russia's 'liberation' claim remained disputed — Ukraine's General Staff (Maj. Andriy Kovalev) said some Russian infantry had entered the city but it 'remained under the control of the Ukrainian Armed Forces,' Zelensky again called Putin's claim a lie, and the Kremlin (Peskov) insisted the city was 'already part of Russia' while renewing its Moscow-meeting invitation; Russia's MoD proposed a six-hour local ceasefire for a body handover, which Ukraine rejected (open-question: kostiantynivka-control-contested-july-2026). Ukraine's deep-strike/energy-attrition campaign continued; no US ground surge (the US withdrew another Brigade Combat Team from Europe). || JULY 12-13 — UKRAINE'S DEEP-STRIKE CAMPAIGN CONTINUES; PEACE TALKS STAY STALLED: overnight July 12 Ukraine's HUR/Unmanned Systems Forces struck the Syzran oil refinery in Samara Oblast (~800 km; ~9M t/yr; supplies the Russian Air Force) and reportedly hit 10 tankers and 4 ferries in the Sea of Azov, following the July 6 Omsk strike. Kostiantynivka stays CONTESTED (Russia's July 3 'liberation' claim denied; ISW: infiltration, not control). Trilateral talks unlikely to resume before end of summer (FT: possibly not before 2027). No US ground surge; the US withdrew another Brigade Combat Team from Europe and France/UK remain the only would-be post-ceasefire ground contributors. || JULY 11 — TALKS STAY STALLED; US NATO ENVOY SAYS KYIV 'SEIZED THE INITIATIVE,' TIME FOR MOSCOW TO NEGOTIATE: Putin's maximalist aims are unchanged (recognition of occupied land, no NATO, force limits, sanctions relief) and Russia 'touts war with NATO' amid battlefield losses; front-line summaries note Kherson-direction airstrikes/artillery July 11, and ~66% of Ukrainians favor a negotiated end. No US ground-deployment surge. || JULY 8-9 — NATO ANKARA SUMMIT CONCLUDES; UKRAINE'S DEEP-STRIKE CAMPAIGN CONTINUES: The Ankara summit (July 7-8) closed with a push for a 5% defense-spending goal and continued Ukraine support; Zelensky met Trump bilaterally (pressing for Patriots and interceptors) and Germany is financing two Patriot systems. Notably, no new US ground-deployment surge was announced — and the Pentagon's European force-posture review may DRAW DOWN US forces in Eastern Europe (no rotational brigade retained in Romania). On July 8 Ukrainian drones struck Russia's Saratov oil refinery and a Tatarstan petrochemical plant, hit 9 tankers in the Sea of Azov, and struck a Voronezh air base (Su-34/Su-35); Russia cut Kerch Bridge rail service (11 of 18 routes cancelled from July 8). The Kremlin continues to reject direct negotiations. Kostiantynivka stays contested; Russia holds ~20% with no confirmed net change. || JULY 7-8 — NATO ANKARA SUMMIT: allies confirmed €70B ($80B) in 2026 Ukraine military aid (largely repackaged existing NATO/EU funding) and a goal of at least an equivalent 2027 level; Trump and Zelensky met bilaterally, with Zelensky pressing for Patriot systems and interceptor missiles after the July 6 Russian barrage on the Kyiv region killed ~22 (15 in Kyiv city) and injured ~90. Germany is financing two Patriot systems. Kostiantynivka stayed contested — Russia's July 3 'liberation' claim disputed by Kyiv; ISW assesses small-group infiltration, not consolidated control. Russia holds ~20% with no confirmed net change. No US ground-deployment surge. || JULY 6-7 — UKRAINE'S DEEPEST STRIKE OF THE WAR (OMSK); RUSSIA'S MASS BARRAGE ON KYIV; TRUMP-PUTIN CALL AHEAD OF ANKARA NATO SUMMIT: On July 6 Ukrainian drones struck the Omsk refinery — Russia's largest, ~2,500-3,000 km away — the deepest strike of the full-scale war (also Yaroslavl/Slavneft-Yanos and Ust-Luga). Russia answered with a mass overnight barrage on Kyiv (~351 drones + 68 missiles) that killed ~22 and injured ~90 nationwide (15 killed in Kyiv city). Trump held a ~90-min call with Putin ('businesslike and very constructive') and spoke with Zelensky ahead of the NATO summit in Ankara (July 7-8), where he was set to meet Zelensky and Germany is financing two Patriot systems. Kostiantynivka stays contested — Russia proposed a local ceasefire (Ukraine rejected it) and ISW puts Russian presence at ~37% of the city (~4.3% of Lyman), calling Putin's 96% claim 'greatly exaggerated.' Russia holds ~20% with no confirmed net change. No US ground-deployment surge. || JULY 5-6 — KOSTIANTYNIVKA CONTROL STILL CONTESTED; DEEP-STRIKE WAR CONTINUES: Russia's July 3 claim (Gerasimov to Putin) that it had 'liberated' Kostiantynivka stayed disputed — Ukraine's 19th Army Corps commander Madyar reported pushing Russian infiltrators back out at a ~3:53 loss ratio, and ISW/DeepState still assess small-group infiltration, not consolidated control (DeepState: ~12 sq mi net Russian gain over the four weeks to June 23). Ukraine kept up its refinery campaign (Lukoil-Nizhegorodorgsintez, Russia's 4th-largest, halted after a July 2 strike; Ufa hit twice in a week; St. Petersburg oil terminal / Kronstadt July 4; 8 of Russia's top 10 refineries hit in 2026). Russia pressed mass strikes on Kyiv (the July 1-2 barrage killed at least 17-31). After a ~40-min July 4 Zelensky-Trump call, Zelensky pressed allies to accelerate Patriot deliveries and warned of another large-scale Russian attack; talks stay stalled (Putin insists on the 2022 Istanbul Protocols); Russia holds ~20% with no confirmed net change. No US ground-deployment surge. || JULY 4-5 — KOSTIANTYNIVKA CONTROL CONTESTED: Russia's Gerasimov told Putin (July 3) that Russian forces had 'liberated' Kostiantynivka and were closing on Lyman, and Putin hailed it; on July 4 Zelensky and Ukraine's General Staff flatly denied it ('another Russian lie'), with Zelensky challenging Putin to meet him there, and ISW assessed NO confirmed advances July 3 (gains still small-group infiltration, not consolidated control). Pokrovsk stayed heaviest (22 assaults repelled; 268 combat engagements July 3). Ukraine struck the St. Petersburg Oil Terminal and Kronstadt (July 4, ~1,100 km — among the deepest strikes on Putin's home city; 8 of Russia's top 10 refineries hit in 2026). Russia kept up mass strikes (overnight attacks injured ~23 in Kyiv; a July 4 drone hit a Poltava gas facility; after the July 1-2 Kyiv barrage of 74 missiles + 496 drones killed 20-31). A ~40-min Zelensky-Trump call (July 4, 'the best conversation in all this time') agreed to strengthen air defenses and expand joint drone/weapons production, days after a US DoD pause on Patriot and precision-artillery shipments; Zelensky named Patriots his top priority with Merz. Talks stay stalled (Putin still insists on the 2022 Istanbul Protocols); Russia holds ~20% with no confirmed net change; a think-tank estimate put total war casualties above 2 million (up to 600,000 deaths). No US ground-deployment surge. || JULY 1 — WAR REPORT CARD: peace talks remain ON HOLD but the US, Ukraine and Europe 'appear ready to restart negotiations,' while Putin reiterates Russia's original aims — negotiating only on the basis of the 2022 Istanbul Protocols (terms amounting to Ukrainian capitulation). Polling shifts toward a deal: ~60% of Russians support peace negotiations and ~66% of Ukrainians want the government to seek an end to the war as soon as possible. On the ground, KOSTIANTYNIVKA stays Russia's main Donbas effort but gains are limited to small-group infiltration with NO consolidated control — Ukraine's tactical situation is deteriorating and Ukrainian forces are counterattacking; the Russian MoD and Putin are 'falsely framing' the city's seizure as imminent. (Russia Matters/ISW, July 1, 2026.) || JUNE 21 — UKRAINE EXTENDS ITS RUSSIAN-ENERGY STRIKE CAMPAIGN: after the June 18 strike on Moscow's Kapotnya (Moscow Oil) refinery — the largest attack on the capital since 2022 — Ukraine struck a CRIMEAN OIL TERMINAL OVERNIGHT JUNE 21. On the ground, KOSTIANTYNIVKA is now Russia's main objective (Russian forces entered its NE part, tactical breakthrough in the west). Formal peace talks remain FROZEN. || JUNE 5-6: Lavrov reaffirmed the April 2022 Istanbul draft as Moscow's basis for any settlement (Russia as 'guarantor state,' veto over responses to future aggression, strict Ukrainian neutrality/force limits); Russia still claims full Luhansk (Kyiv denies). Over the past year Russia netted +1,427 sq mi (~0.6% of Ukraine) but recorded a net territorial LOSS in April 2026; next Istanbul round proposed June 20-30. — PRIOR: JUNE 4-5: Russia maintains its claim of full control over Luhansk oblast (Kyiv's Joint Forces denies it, citing no changes there) ahead of US-envoy talks; next Istanbul round proposed June 20-30 after the brief June 2 round (1,000-1,200 POW swap + 6,000-body exchange, no ceasefire). — PRIOR: grinding war — JUNE 2: ISTANBUL SECOND DIRECT ROUND ENDS QUICKLY — NEW 1,000-1,200-FOR-1,000-1,200 POW SWAP + 6,000-BODY EXCHANGE + WOUNDED-EXCHANGE COMMISSION AGREED, BUT NO CEASEFIRE; RUSSIA OFFERED ONLY A 2-3 DAY 'COLLECT-THE-DEAD' PAUSE (ZELENSKY CRITICIZED); UKRAINE PROPOSED NEXT ROUND JUNE 20-30; EU DRAFT: READY TO 'STEP UP' BUT 'CEASEFIRE MUST COME FIRST'; POKROVSK SECTOR HEAVIEST — PRIOR grinding war — MAY 28: 189 FRONTLINE COMBAT CLASHES IN 24H (MOST ACTIVE RUSSIAN ASSAULTS ON POKROVSK + HULIAIPOLE SECTORS); ZELENSKY-ANNOUNCED LONG-RANGE OPS CONTINUE; SOUFAN CENTER (MAY 28) FLAGS POSSIBLE TURNING POINT (SLOWING RUSSIAN ADVANCES + UKRAINE DRONE EDGE) BUT WARNS UKRAINE COULD STRUGGLE WITHOUT SUSTAINED US/EUROPEAN SUPPORT; 1,000-FOR-1,000 POW EXCHANGE CONTINUES; PUTIN STILL REFUSES TO MEET ZELENSKY OUTSIDE MOSCOW; SECOND DIRECT RUSSIA-UKRAINE ROUND STILL EXPECTED ~JUNE 2 — PRIOR grinding war — MAY 27: 296 FRONTLINE COMBAT CLASHES IN 24H (POKROVSK SECTOR HEAVIEST — 53 RUSSIAN ASSAULTS REPELLED NEAR NYKANORIVKA/KOTLYNE/RODYNSKE/POKROVSK/HRYSHYNE/UDACHNE); RUSSIAN MLRS SHELLING ON KHERSON KORABELNYI DISTRICT INCLUDING CHILDREN'S PLAYGROUND; DRONE STRIKE ON ODESA SHOPPING MALL INJURES 8; ZELENSKY ANNOUNCES NEW LONG-RANGE OPERATIONS AFTER MEETING SYRSKYI + HNATOV; UNMANNED SYSTEMS FORCES STRIKE TARGETS IN OCCUPIED LUHANSK/DONETSK/ZAPORIZHZHIA; SECOND DIRECT RUSSIA-UKRAINE ROUND STILL EXPECTED ~JUNE 2; 1,000-FOR-1,000 POW EXCHANGE CONTINUES; PUTIN STILL REFUSES TO MEET ZELENSKY OUTSIDE MOSCOW — PRIOR grinding war — MAY 22: 253 FRONTLINE COMBAT CLASHES IN 24H (POKROVSK SECTOR HEAVIEST — 52 RUSSIAN ASSAULTS REPELLED; HIGH ACTIVITY ALSO ON HULIAIPOLE + KOSTIANTYNIVKA DIRECTIONS); RUSSIAN TOTAL COMBAT LOSSES SINCE FEB 24 2022 REACH ~1,353,860; 1,000-FOR-1,000 POW EXCHANGE CONTINUES (US GUARANTOR); PUTIN STILL REFUSES TO MEET ZELENSKY OUTSIDE MOSCOW; SECOND DIRECT ROUND EXPECTED ~JUNE 2 — PRIOR MAY 20-21: 177 FRONTLINE COMBAT CLASHES (POKROVSK AXIS HEAVIEST, RUSSIA -40+ PERSONNEL); RUSSIAN DRONES STRUCK SUMY (RESIDENTIAL BUILDING DESTROYED), CHUHUIV/KHARKIV, AND KRYVYI RIH (INDUSTRIAL FACILITY) MAY 20; 1,000-FOR-1,000 POW EXCHANGE PROCEEDS; PUTIN STILL REFUSES TO MEET ZELENSKY OUTSIDE MOSCOW; SECOND DIRECT ROUND EXPECTED ~JUNE 2 — PRIOR grinding war — MAY 18-19: BLACK SEA GRAIN INITIATIVE OFFICIALLY ENDS MAY 18 (RUSSIA REFUSING NEW UKRAINIAN SHIPMENTS ABSENT REMOVAL OF RESTRICTIONS ON ITS OWN AGRICULTURAL/FERTILIZER EXPORTS) — RUSSIAN SHAHED-TYPE DRONE STRUCK A CHINESE COMMERCIAL VESSEL IN UKRAINE'S TERRITORIAL WATERS NIGHT OF MAY 18 (UKR NAVY SPOX PLETENCHUK) — FIRST STAGE OF 1,000-FOR-1,000 POW EXCHANGE PROCEEDED; PUTIN STILL REFUSES TO MEET ZELENSKY OUTSIDE MOSCOW; SECOND ROUND EXPECTED ~JUNE 2 — PRIOR grinding war — MAY 15: ISTANBUL DIRECT TALKS HELD — FIRST UMEROV-MEDINSKY ROUND PRODUCED 1,000-FOR-1,000 POW EXCHANGE AGREEMENT BUT NO CEASEFIRE; TALKS LASTED LESS THAN 2 HOURS; MOSCOW DEMANDED KYIV WITHDRAW FROM DONETSK + LUHANSK + ZAPORIZHZHIA + KHERSON OBLASTS — UKRAINE INSISTED ON 'FULL AND UNCONDITIONAL CEASEFIRE' 30 DAYS ON LAND/AIR/SEA — MEDINSKY 'SATISFIED WITH OUTCOME'; AGREED EACH SIDE WOULD PRESENT VISION OF FUTURE CEASEFIRE IN DETAIL — TRUMP CALLED FOR PUTIN MEETING AFTER NO BREAKTHROUGH — UKRAINE HANDED OFFICIAL LIST OF FORCIBLY DEPORTED CHILDREN — PRIOR MAY 13-14: PUTIN IN EFFECT REJECTS CEASEFIRE OFFER; APPOINTS AIDE VLADIMIR MEDINSKY TO LEAD LOWER-LEVEL ISTANBUL DELEGATION MAY 15 — ZELENSKY EN ROUTE TO ANKARA TO MEET ERDOGAN MAY 13; SAID HE WILL SEND DEFENSE MINISTER UMEROV-LED DELEGATION FROM ANKARA TO ISTANBUL DESPITE 'NOBODY WHO ACTUALLY MAKES DECISIONS' ON MOSCOW SIDE — POKROVSK SECTOR NEAR 50 RUSSIAN ATTACKS MAY 12-13; CLASHES NEAR RODYNSKE, NOVOOLEKSANDRIVKA, HRYSHYNE, BILYTSKE, POKROVSK, KOTLYNE, UDACHNE, MURAVKA, NOVOPIDHORODNIE, MOLODETSKE — EPICENTER OF FIGHTING SHIFTS TOWARD RODYNSKE + KRASNYI LIMAN (NORTH POKROVSK) ALONG LAST CORRIDOR FOR UKRAINIAN FORCES DEFENDING MYRNOHRAD — DONETSK TERRITORIAL DEMAND UNRESOLVED (MOSCOW WANTS KYIV TO WITHDRAW FROM AREAS RUSSIA FAILED TO CAPTURE; UKRAINE REFUSED) — PRIOR MAY 12-13: PUTIN PROPOSED ISTANBUL TALKS MAY 15; ZELENSKY MAY 13 ANNOUNCED HE'LL FLY TO ANKARA TO MEET ERDOGAN, READY TO FLY TO ISTANBUL IF PUTIN ATTENDS —PRIOR MAY 9-10: TRUMP 3-DAY CEASEFIRE COLLAPSING IN PRACTICE — ZELENSKY: RUSSIA CARRIED OUT 140+ ATTACKS ON FRONTLINE POSITIONS BY EARLY MORNING MAY 10, ALONGSIDE 10 ASSAULTS + 850+ DRONE ATTACKS — KREMLIN: 264 UKRAINIAN DRONES SHOT DOWN OVERNIGHT, INCLUDING TARGETING MOSCOW + PERM REGION (URALS, 1,600+ KM FROM FRONT) — POKROVSK MAY 9-10: 30+ RUSSIAN ASSAULTS REPELLED; 200+ COMBAT CLASSES IN 24H; UKRAINE AIR ASSAULT FORCES IDENTIFY RUSSIA'S 90TH TANK DIVISION AT POKROVSK — MAY 9 VICTORY DAY: MOSCOW PARADE SCALED DOWN — INFANTRY ONLY, NO TANKS/MISSILES/ARMORED VEHICLES FOR FIRST TIME IN ~2 DECADES — MOBILE INTERNET CUT FOR MILLIONS DURING PARADE — FOREIGN MEDIA ACCREDITATIONS REVOKED — ONLY CONFIRMED FOREIGN ATTENDEES: BELARUS LUKASHENKO, LAOS PRES SISOULITH, MALAYSIA SULTAN IBRAHIM (SHORTEST LIST IN MODERN HISTORY) — TRUMP ANNOUNCED 3-DAY RUSSIA-UKRAINE CEASEFIRE MAY 9-11 WITH 1,000-FOR-1,000 PRISONER EXCHANGE; KREMLIN + ZELENSKY CONFIRMED; CEASEFIRE COLLAPSED 'ALMOST IMMEDIATELY' PER DEFENSE NEWS — POKROVSK MAY 8 PRIOR: 33 RUSSIAN ASSAULTS REPELLED; ~106,000 RUSSIAN PERSONNEL CONCENTRATED ON POKROVSK AXIS",
   "summary": "AUGUST 28: The agenda of Ratcliffe's unannounced August 25 Moscow visit was disclosed on August 27 — a warning against actions escalating tensions with Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania, and a request that Russia reduce military and economic support for Iran, linking the two wars in a single channel. Underlying US assessments hold that Russia may test NATO's readiness with a limited operation or hybrid attack in coming years, with the Baltics most likely. Peskov: the meeting was with Russian intelligence representatives, not Putin, who was briefed afterwards; Trump called it 'semi-routine.' Overnight into August 27 Russia struck Kyiv and other cities with ballistic missiles and drones — six explosions in the capital inside a minute, at least seven injured, targets including warehouses, ports and energy and industrial facilities per FM Sybiha — with Ukraine downing seven ballistic missiles, one anti-ship cruise missile and ~90% of drones. Ukrainian drones forced the NORSI refinery at Kstovo, Russia's fourth-largest and second-largest gasoline producer, to suspend crude processing, its fifth drone-related shutdown in under five months, with all major Lukoil refineries reported shut. A Ukrainian strike hit a bus in Russian-controlled Luhansk, killing nine. Net Russian gains for 2026 hold at 81.1 km². No NATO troop commitment. || AUGUST 27: CIA Director John Ratcliffe made an unannounced visit to Moscow on August 25 — the first by a US spy chief since Burns in November 2021 — meeting Russian intelligence counterparts, with the agenda undisclosed and Putin declining to meet him; Washington had asked Kyiv to pause strikes on Moscow Monday-Wednesday. Peskov said the same day there were no plans for talks with Witkoff or Kushner. Russian attacks killed four and injured 95 across Ukraine over August 25-26 (163 drones, 132 downed, 18 locations), including an FPV double-tap on rescue workers in Kramatorsk. Ukraine struck a Wildberries hub in Tambov Oblast. ISW records Russian escalation against Ukrainian-Moldovan border infrastructure and Ukrainian advances near Slovyansk and Hulyaipole. No NATO troop commitment. || AUGUST 25: Ukraine marked 35 years of independence under a 143-drone, eight-missile overnight barrage that killed eight and injured 43 across six oblasts, worst in Kharkiv. European leaders came to Kyiv and no US representative attended; Britain's Andy Burnham pledged MBDA release of classified UK component data for SCALP local assembly and Norway's Jonas Gahr Store committed $9bn for 2027. Zelensky pressed the Coalition of the Willing for air defence and the EU for its €90bn loan against a $27bn military deficit. ISW records Russian advances east of Slovyansk and an August 23 Ukraine-US-Europe proposal with significant Ukrainian concessions that the Kremlin has not reciprocated. No NATO troop commitment. || AUGUST 23: Ukraine is now engineering around American resupply rather than waiting for it. ISW's August 21 assessment records Ukrainian forces developing domestic technological solutions to target Russian ballistic launchers and jet-powered drones expressly to avoid dependence on foreign materiel amid global interceptor shortages — the Iran war's munitions drain translated into Ukrainian doctrine. Russia launched 135 long-range strike drones in the assessed period; Ukraine struck Russian military airfields and oil infrastructure. North Korea has deployed drone operators to Kursk Oblast, upgrading its contribution from manpower to transferable capability. The diplomatic track is closed: a senior Russian official restated unwillingness to negotiate meaningfully, and the Kremlin's conditions remain Putin's June 2024 maximalist terms. Net Russian territorial gains for 2026 stand at 81.1 square kilometres — a rate that cannot deliver the Kremlin's stated objective on any timeline it has named. || AUGUST 22: THE CROSS-THEATRE LINK IS NO LONGER INFERENTIAL — IT IS RUSSIAN DOCTRINE. Ukraine's military intelligence states that Moscow has been reprioritising ballistic and cruise missile production since April in order to exploit Ukrainian Patriot shortages, and has more than tripled monthly ballistic launches between April and July. Those shortages trace to American expenditure in the Iran war. The next blow is telegraphed: US intelligence relayed on August 8 that Russia is preparing a major campaign against Kyiv's energy infrastructure ahead of Independence Day on August 24, and Zelensky says Ukraine effectively has no undamaged power plants left — a force striking the civilian grid because it cannot move the line. NATO spillover crossed from accident to accepted risk: Russian drones entered Moldovan and Romanian airspace on August 20 and Romania scrambled two F-16s to destroy an explosive naval drone near a Black Sea gas platform, with ISW assessing that the Kremlin has accepted the risk of damaging NATO states. No Article 4 consultation followed. On the ground Ukrainian forces advanced northeast of Kharkiv and ISW's judgement of a failed Russian spring-summer offensive stands. No negotiating round convened in this cycle. || AUGUST 1 (Day 156): A Russian cruise missile detonated on NATO territory in eastern Poland during the July 29-30 barrage, drawing scrambled fighters, activated air defences and a NATO statement that it would defend territory — the boundary tested, not crossed. The barrage itself, 70+ missiles and ~280 drones against Kyiv and Lviv, killed at least eight and by later counts ten, including three children. Ukraine struck refineries and logistics hubs in Volgograd, Tatarstan and Krasnodar on July 31 and has made that campaign the basis of its negotiating argument. The front is nearly static: 226 clashes on July 30, heaviest at Kostiantynivka and Pokrovsk, with Russia's monthly territorial gain estimated at between zero and ten square miles. || JULY 27 (Day 152): Zelensky takes an air-ceasefire proposal to the White House on July 28, the same day as Netanyahu. Russia advanced between Pokrovsk and Kostiantynivka per Ukraine's Khortytsia group, with the Pokrovsk axis still the main effort and reports of Russian forces inside the town; ISW judges Russian density insufficient for simultaneous offensives on both. Zelensky warns Russia is preparing to receive 30,000 more North Korean troops via Voronezh and asks Europe for the Freyja anti-ballistic prototype by mid-2027. Iran threatened retaliation for Ukraine's Caspian strike, hardening the new direct Ukraine-Iran vector. || JULY 26: The Ukraine and Iran theatres connected directly. Ukraine struck a Russian warship and Iran-linked military cargo vessels in the Caspian Sea alongside deep strikes on Kirov, Tyumen, Yekaterinburg and Rostov-on-Don; Iran reported a sailor killed and summoned Ukraine's chargé d'affaires. Zelensky alleged Russian satellite imagery of Gulf and US sites is reaching Iranian targeting. On the front, Ukraine hit a Chornomornaftogaz facility and Russian drone-relay equipment in occupied Crimea and a bridge in occupied Donetsk; Russian attacks killed at least seven civilians and injured 58, with ballistic missiles striking a Kyiv residential building. Kostiantynivka remains contested with no confirmed net change. Zelensky warned of an expanding Russian mobilization and a request for 30,000 more North Korean troops; Tokayev proposed freezing the war. || JULY 25: The front stayed static within noise while the diplomatic track stayed frozen. Ukrainian attacks in partly occupied Zaporizhzhia killed 12 and a Russian drone strike in Sumy killed 3. The preceding 24 hours logged 192 combat engagements — 24 in the Pokrovsk direction — with 5,746 one-way attack drones, 186 guided bombs and 2,297 shellings, plus three Iskander-M/S-400 ballistic missiles fired at Kyiv Oblast's Bucha district. ISW recorded confirmed Russian advances near Kryva Luka and Hlushkivka (Lyman/Kupiansk) and Novodanilovka (Orikhiv) while assessing overall momentum as decelerating. Peskov reaffirmed Putin's June 2024 maximalist terms — full Ukrainian withdrawal from held areas of four oblasts and abandonment of NATO accession — as the precondition for negotiations, and Moscow reiterated refusal to talk directly to Kyiv. Russia holds ~20% with no confirmed net change; Kostiantynivka stays contested; no US or NATO ground troops are committed (open-question: kostiantynivka-control-contested-july-2026). || JULY 22-23: Peace talks stay stalled and control of Kostiantynivka stays contested. Ukrainian forces reported repelling ~25 Russian assaults on the Kostiantynivka direction and ~33 on the Pokrovsk direction; Russia claims incremental gains between the two towns while holding ~20% of the front with no confirmed net change. UN monitors said Ukrainian civilian casualties in June hit their highest monthly level since April 2022 (126 killed / 907 wounded), with long-range strikes and drones responsible for ~45%. Ukraine's deep-strike campaign on Russian oil refineries continued. No US ground involvement (open-question: kostiantynivka-control-contested-july-2026). || JULY 14-15 (Roundup): Peace talks stay stalled and control of Kostiantynivka stays contested. Russia's early-July 'liberation' claim remained disputed: Ukraine's General Staff spokesman Maj. Andriy Kovalev acknowledged some Russian infantry had entered the embattled Donetsk-oblast city but said it 'remained under the control of the Ukrainian Armed Forces,' with troops holding positions on its outskirts; Zelensky again dismissed Putin's claim as a lie for media attention; the Kremlin's Dmitry Peskov mocked Zelensky's proposed meeting there, saying the city was 'already part of Russia' and renewing Putin's invitation to Moscow. Russia's Defence Ministry proposed a six-hour local ceasefire to hand over Ukrainian bodies, which Kyiv rejected (open-question: kostiantynivka-control-contested-july-2026). Ukraine kept up its deep-strike/energy campaign; no US ground-deployment surge (the US withdrew another Brigade Combat Team from Europe). (Al Jazeera; Kyiv Independent; Russia Matters, July 5-14, 2026.) || JULY 11 (Roundup): Peace talks remain frozen. The US Permanent Representative to NATO said 'Kyiv has seized the initiative, it's time for Moscow to negotiate,' while Putin's demands stayed maximalist and Russia touted a 'war with NATO' amid losses; Kherson-direction strikes continued July 11 and ~66% of Ukrainians want a negotiated end. No US ground-deployment surge. (Pravda NATO; Russia Matters; Al Jazeera, July 1-11, 2026.) || [JUNE 15-16 UPDATE] Mid-June: the war continued with no breakthrough. Ukrainian drone strikes hit St. Petersburg on June 12 (smoke over the city) as Putin spoke at Russia's flagship economic forum; on June 15 Putin reiterated that any negotiations must happen on Moscow's terms, with his war aims unchanged. Heavy fighting continued on the Pokrovsk and Huliaipole sectors; Ukraine still holds ~80% of its territory (~20% occupied). The theatre keeps stressing US bandwidth but has triggered no US ground-deployment surge. (Kyiv Independent; Critical Threats / ISW, June 12-15, 2026.) [JUNE 5-6] FM Sergei Lavrov reaffirmed Moscow's insistence on the April 2022 Istanbul draft as the basis for a settlement — Russia as a 'guarantor state' with veto power over responses to future aggression and strict neutrality/force caps on Ukraine. Russia maintained its claim of full control over Luhansk oblast (Kyiv denied any change). Over June 2025–June 2026 Russia made a net gain of 1,427 sq mi (~0.6% of Ukraine) but recorded a net LOSS in April 2026; Ukrainian drones killed/incapacitated 35,200+ Russian troops in April, the fifth straight month Moscow's losses outran mobilization. The next direct round is proposed for June 20-30. (Russia Matters; Kyiv Independent, June 2026). [JUNE 3-4] Russia claimed full control of Luhansk oblast (Kyiv denied) ahead of US-envoy talks; fighting continued in the Pokrovsk sector. Following the brief June 2 Istanbul round (a 1,000-1,200 POW swap, a 6,000-body exchange and a wounded-exchange commission, but no ceasefire), Ukraine proposed the next round for June 20-30. (PBS NewsHour; Kyiv Independent; Wikipedia, June 2-4, 2026.) || [JUNE 2] The second direct Russia-Ukraine round in Istanbul ended in under an hour: the sides agreed a new prisoner exchange of at least 1,000 (possibly 1,200) each, a 6,000-body swap, and a commission for seriously wounded troops, but no ceasefire — Russia offered only a 2-3 day pause to retrieve the dead (Zelensky criticized) and Ukraine proposed the next round for June 20-30. An EU summit draft said the bloc was ready to 'step up' but a 'ceasefire must come first.' (Kyiv Independent; PBS NewsHour; Euronews, June 2, 2026). [JUNE 2] A second direct round was held in Istanbul: both sides exchanged memoranda and agreed a new prisoner swap but reached no ceasefire and stayed far apart — Russia seeking recognition of annexed regions and Ukrainian withdrawal within 30 days, Ukraine seeking a full ceasefire, an all-for-all POW exchange, the return of abducted children, and a NATO/EU path; the Pokrovsk sector remained heaviest. (Kyiv Independent; RFE/RL, June 2, 2026). [MAY 31-JUNE 1] A second direct round of talks remained expected around June 2; the Pokrovsk sector stayed the heaviest along the front, with Russia still unable to advance significantly west of Pokrovsk/Myrnohrad and both sides claiming frontline progress. [MAY 30-31] A second direct round of talks remains expected ~June 2; the Pokrovsk sector stays the heaviest along the front, with Russia unable to advance significantly west of Pokrovsk/Myrnohrad since December 2025. POW/humanitarian exchanges continue. MAY 17: Istanbul aftermath — both sides remain far apart on the key conditions for stopping the war; senior officials' comments indicate no convergence. The agreed 1,000-for-1,000 POW exchange proceeds; a second round of direct talks is expected ~June 2 with each side to table a memorandum of its peace terms; 