OLD — SIX NATIONWIDE COLLAPSES IN 2026, BLACKOUTS ABOVE TWENTY HOURS A DAY, UP TO 105 CONSECUTIVE DAYS WITHOUT STEADY POWER — AND HAVANA MUNICIPALITIES SAW NEIGHBOURHOOD PROTESTS AFTER OUTAGES EXCEEDING 48 HOURS. COLOMBIA'S PRESIDENT ABELARDO DE LA ESPRIELLA, IN OFFICE SINCE AUGUST 7, CONTINUES THE HARDLINE SECURITY PROGRAMME AND THE SEVERANCE OF TIES WITH CUBA AND NICARAGUA. || AUGUST 25: NO MATERIAL CHANGE, AND THE CUBAN FIGURES ARE THE ONLY ONES THAT MOVED. Cuba's national electricity system has now collapsed SIX TIMES IN 2026 — twice in March, three times within ten days in July and once on AUGUST 2-3 — with blackouts EXCEEDING TWENTY HOURS A DAY and some communities recorded at UP TO 105 CONSECUTIVE DAYS without steady electricity. The island lost its principal fuel supplier after the JANUARY 3 seizure of Maduro and the subsequent US oil blockade, with Mexican imports also halted under Washington's pressure; sanctions run under EXECUTIVE ORDER 14404, Visa and Mastercard processing has been lost, and the United Nations describes a humanitarian emergency endangering lives. Foreign Minister BRUNO RODRÍGUEZ said there has been 'NO PROGRESS' toward ending the impasse. In VENEZUELA the Caracas government-opposition talks opened on AUGUST 6 under JORGE RODRÍGUEZ and DINORAH FIGUERA continue with no published electoral timetable; DELCY RODRÍGUEZ remains acting president with her 2018 US sanctions lifted in April; MADURO and CILIA FLORES remain in US custody with trial set for JUNE 1, 2027 before Judge Alvin Hellerstein in the Southern District of New York on four counts. COLOMBIA unchanged. || AUGUST 24: NO MATERIAL CHANGE. The Caracas government-opposition talks opened AUGUST 6 under JORGE RODRÍGUEZ and DINORAH FIGUERA continue with NO PUBLISHED ELECTORAL TIMETABLE; DELCY RODRÍGUEZ remains acting president, having made significant changes to the armed forces and met many of Washington's demands, with US sanctions on her lifted in APRIL. MADURO AND CILIA FLORES remain 