 AND CILIA FLORES still face trial before JUDGE ALVIN HELLERSTEIN on JUNE 1, 2027. IN COLOMBIA A TRUCK BOMB DETONATED BESIDE A POLICE STATION IN CÚCUTA on the Venezuelan border on AUGUST 1, INJURING ELEVEN — EIGHT POLICE OFFICERS AND THREE CIVILIANS — SIX DAYS BEFORE ABELARDO DE LA ESPRIELLA'S AUGUST 7 INAUGURATION IN CALI. DEFENCE MINISTER PEDRO SÁNCHEZ blamed ELN 'NARCOTERRORISTS' and offered a reward above $64,000. PRESIDENT PETRO WAS IN HAVANA, having travelled JULY 31 to discuss with DÍAZ-CANEL the US 'POLICY OF STRANGULATION' and the Caribbean vessel strikes, and DID NOT COMMENT. CUBA'S ENERGY AND MINES MINISTRY SAYS ALL FUEL RESERVES ARE EXHAUSTED, with blackouts of 20-22 HOURS A DAY in some areas — the THIRD NATIONWIDE OUTAGE OF 2026 and EIGHTH SINCE LATE 2024. De la Espriella takes office AUGUST 7 in Cali, will SEVER RELATIONS WITH CUBA AND NICARAGUA ('In my administration, there will be no ties with tyrannies') and CLOSE 14 EMBASSIES AND 15 CONSULATES. (Venezuelanalysis; TimesLIVE; Business Standard; LatinAmerican Post; WLRN; France 24; CBS News; Malay Mail; Cubaheadlines; MercoPress; Euronews; Caribbean National Weekly, July 27 – August 1, 2026.) || AUGUST 1: VENEZUELA'S US-BACKED TRANSITION TALKS OPEN TODAY — WITHOUT MARÍA CORINA MACHADO. National Assembly chief JORGE RODRÍGUEZ, BROTHER OF INTERIM PRESIDENT DELCY RODRÍGUEZ, heads the government delegation; opposition figure DINORAH FIGUERA, who leads the bloc that won the 2015 parliamentary majority, heads the other. The agenda is a ROADMAP FOR POLITICAL TRANSITION covering INSTITUTIONAL REFORMS, ELECTORAL CONDITIONS and GUARANTEES FOR POLITICAL PARTICIPATION. MACHADO HAS RULED OUT PARTICIPATING while stating she DOES NOT OPPOSE the talks proceeding — an absence that has left her base sceptical before the process begins. MADURO AND CILIA FLORES remain set for trial before JUDGE ALVIN HELLERSTEIN in Manhattan on JUNE 1, 2027. CUBA's fuel crisis and COLOMBIA's standoff with Washington are unchanged this cycle. (France 24; The Manila Times; Latin America Reports; Ground News; Cubaheadlines, July 31 - August 1, 2026.) || JULY 31: VENEZUELA'S FORMAL TRANSITION TALKS SET FOR AUGUST 1, with acting President DELCY RODRÍGUEZ leading one delegation and MARÍA CORINA MACHADO OUTSIDE THE ROOM; Maduro and Cilia Flores are set to stand trial in New York on JUNE 1, 2027. CUBA ANSWERS ITS FOURTH ISLAND-WIDE GRID COLLAPSE OF 2026 (July 27, all ten million without power) WITH THE LARGEST PRIVATE-SECTOR LIBERALISATION OF THE CRISIS — easing restrictions on private business and approving private imports of goods and medicines — against a fuel shortage critical since January driven by the US interdiction of Venezuelan oil begun in February 2026. COLOMBIA: president-elect ABELARDO DE LA ESPRIELLA takes office AUGUST 7 and has said he will sever relations with Havana and close 14 embassies and 15 consulates. (Just Security, 'Early Edition: July 30, 2026'; ABC News; NPR; AP; CBS News, 2026.) || JULY 30: VENEZUELA'S TRANSITION TALKS OPEN AUGUST 1 WITH MACHADO OUTSIDE THE ROOM. Rival marches crossed CARACAS on July 28 ahead of talks between a delegation led by acting president DELCY RODRÍGUEZ and a MACHADO-LESS opposition group, following the agreement between the 2015 National Assembly's representatives and the interim government. Leftist demonstrators said Rodríguez negotiates 'WITH A GUN TO HER HEAD'; MACHADO declared the transition already under way and demanded elections. The US legal track is unchanged: DOJ seeks HEAD-OF-STATE IMMUNITY for Rodríguez against the $314M July 14 judgment, and MADURO'S TRIAL stays set for JUNE 1, 2027. CUBA SUFFERED ITS FOURTH ISLAND-WIDE GRID COLLAPSE OF 2026 — all 10 MILLION without power at midday Monday, on infrastructure decaying for decades against a fuel shortage critical since January. HAVANA called COLOMBIA'S severance an 'ACT OF SUBORDINATION' to Washington: DE LA ESPRIELLA will close 14 EMBASSIES and 15 CONSULATES and cut ties with CUBA and NICARAGUA on taking office AUGUST 7. (Jamaica Observer; Caracas Chronicles; MercoPress; Press TV; Xinhua; The City Paper Bogotá; ColombiaOne; Latin America Daily Briefing, July 26-29, 2026.) || JULY 29 (Day 153): THE TRANSITION MOVES FROM PROMISE TO CALENDAR — RUBIO says formal talks on Venezuela's democratic transition BEGIN IN EARLY AUGUST, with the agenda opening AUGUST 1, after an agreement between representatives of the 2015 NATIONAL ASSEMBLY and the interim government building on June meetings; the two sides agreed to establish a formal forum for reconciliation talks and to begin a transition process aimed at political reconciliation, democratic reforms and national recovery. SIMULTANEOUSLY WASHINGTON MOVED TO SHIELD THE COUNTERPARTY IT DEALS WITH: a July 20 Justice Department filing asked a Florida federal court to recognise HEAD-OF-STATE IMMUNITY for acting president DELCY RODRÍGUEZ in the 2025 civil suit brought by three Americans who say they were imprisoned and tortured under Maduro, arguing the executive branch alone determines immunity and noting the State Department recognised her as head of state in March; on July 14 the judge awarded the plaintiffs $314 MILLION, with Rodríguez not included in that ruling. MADURO and CILIA FLORES remain held in Brooklyn with trial set for JUNE 1, 2027 (SDNY); both plead not guilty. CUBA has now suffered its FOURTH ISLAND-WIDE BLACKOUT of 2026 (~10 million without power) under the halted Venezuelan crude supply and Executive Order 14380, which authorises tariffs on any country supplying Cuba with fuel — the blocked shipments covered ~60% of the ~100,000 bpd the grid needs — and Havana's foreign ministry REJECTED Colombian president-elect ABELARDO DE LA ESPRIELLA's announced closure of Colombia's Havana embassy and severing of ties; he also plans to close the Managua embassy and restore relations with Washington and Israel. Handover in Bogotá is AUGUST 7. (MercoPress; Curacao Chronicle; PolitiFact; Latin Times; Al Jazeera; CNN; Newsweek; Últimas Noticias, July 2026.) || JULY 28 (Day 152): MADURO'S TRIAL DOCKETED FOR JUNE 1, 2027 — a US judge set the date on July 22 for Nicolás Maduro and Cilia Flores, who pleaded not guilty to drug-trafficking and firearms charges after being seized by US troops in the January raid. Acting president DELCY RODRÍGUEZ continues normalising with Washington — she has restructured the armed forces, met many US demands, had her own sanctions lifted in April, and is pressing for the rest to go. CUBA'S GRID KEEPS COLLAPSING under the combination of the halted Venezuelan crude supply and Executive Order 14380 of January 29, which authorises tariffs on any country supplying Cuba with fuel; the blocked shipments covered about 60% of the ~100,000 bpd the power system needs, and the island suffered national outages twice within five days in early July and again mid-month. COLOMBIA'S president-elect takes office AUGUST 7 following the June 21 runoff; Trump's endorsement of Abelardo de la Espriella — who has vowed to join the Americas Counter Cartel initiative — was rejected by President Petro as election interference, and Caracas is watching the handover closely. (CNN; ABC News; Democracy Now!; OilPrice; CRS; Americas Quarterly, July 2026.) || JULY 27: NO MATERIAL CHANGE. Maduro and Cilia Flores are set for trial in the Southern District of New York on June 1, 2027 (Judge Alvin K. Hellerstein, July 22); both pleaded not guilty. Delcy Rodríguez remains acting president amid reported Chavista disillusionment over crisis response. Cuba's grid has collapsed repeatedly through July — the July 10 failure was the fourth of 2026 and ninth since late 2024, after a record 2,341 MW deficit on July 8 affecting 73% of the population — under the US oil blockade in force since January. Colombia's incoming de la Espriella administration will close its embassies in Cuba and Nicaragua, reversing Petro's regional alignment. || JULY 26: TRIAL CLOCK SET, GRID STILL FAILING. The Maduro/Cilia Flores trial stands set for June 1, 2027 (SDNY), with sovereign-immunity challenges to be filed from early September and oral argument November 17; both remain held in Brooklyn and deny the charges. Delcy Rodríguez continues as acting president in Caracas with the succession dispute unresolved. Cuba suffered another total grid collapse — ~10 million without power, public transport largely halted, tens of thousands of surgeries cancelled, scattered pot-banging protests in Havana ('turn on the lights') — with the island producing only ~40% of the oil it consumes after the US blockade and the Venezuelan-crude cutoff. Outlets differ on the running count of 2026 total outages. (ABC News; US News; Fox News; CBS News; Al Jazeera, July 2026.) || JULY 25: US District Judge Alvin K. Hellerstein (SDNY) set a June 1, 2027 trial for ousted Venezuelan president Nicolas Maduro and wife Cilia Flores, who pleaded not guilty to drug-trafficking and firearms charges after their January seizure by US troops; acting president Delcy Rodriguez continues to meet many US demands. Cuba's US-blockade-driven energy crisis persists with repeated island-wide blackouts (~10M without power). (CNN; ABC News; GlobalSecurity, July 22-23, 2026.) || JULY 23: Maduro and Cilia Flores appeared July 22 before US District Judge Alvin K. Hellerstein (SDNY, Manhattan) for a pretrial hearing; the judge SET a June 1, 2027 trial date at both sides' request. Defense counsel Barry Pollack will file sovereign-immunity challenges to the indictment beginning in early September, with oral arguments November 17; both remain held in Brooklyn and deny the charges. Venezuela stays under acting President Delcy Rodriguez, the succession dispute unresolved. Cuba's grid crisis persists under the US oil blockade. || JULY 18: Venezuela's constitutional crisis stays unresolved under acting President Delcy Rodriguez; the Maduro/Flores SDNY hearing stays July 22. Cuba's grid crisis persists under the US oil blockade and the Venezuelan-crude cutoff. (CBS News; Wikipedia '2026 US intervention in Venezuela,' July 2026.)  || JULY 15-16 — CUBA'S GRID CRISIS PERSISTS under the US oil blockade and the Venezuelan-crude cutoff after the mid-July island-wide collapses (third in under 10 days, July 14); FM Bruno Rodríguez told the UN the embargo is 'collective punishment.' VENEZUELA's constitutional crisis stays unresolved under interim president Delcy Rodríguez; Maduro/Flores SDNY hearing stays JULY 22; Colombia's de la Espriella inaugurated AUG 7. No US ground move; the ~15,000-troop Caribbean posture is unchanged — the pressure on Cuba is economic (oil blockade), not manpower. || JULY 14-15 — CUBA'S THIRD ISLAND-WIDE BLACKOUT IN UNDER 10 DAYS (July 14, ~11am local; fifth total outage of 2026; ~10M without power) deepens the grid crisis under the US oil blockade and the Venezuelan-crude cutoff; FM Bruno Rodríguez told the UN the embargo is a 'systematic violation of human rights'/'collective punishment.' VENEZUELA's constitutional crisis stays unresolved (Rodríguez interim mandate lapsed, no 'absolute absence' declared); Maduro/Flores SDNY hearing stays JULY 22; Colombia's de la Espriella inaugurated AUG 7. No US ground move; the ~15,000-troop Caribbean posture is unchanged — the pressure on Cuba is economic (oil blockade), not manpower. || JULY 10-13 — CUBA'S SECOND ISLAND-WIDE BLACKOUT IN UNDER A WEEK (July 10, ~935 MW vs ~3,100 MW demand; fourth total outage of 2026) deepens the grid crisis under the US oil blockade and the Venezuelan-crude cutoff; VENEZUELA'S CONSTITUTIONAL CRISIS stays unresolved — the July 3 expiry of the 180-day 'absolute absence' limit passed with no National Assembly declaration and a Supreme Court 'forced absence' concept the opposition calls unconstitutional (Rodriguez ~63% disapproval). Maduro/Flores SDNY hearing stays JULY 22; Colombia's de la Espriella inaugurated AUG 7. No US ground move; the ~15,000-troop Caribbean posture is unchanged. || JULY 11-12 — VENEZUELA CONSTITUTIONAL CRISIS DEEPENS: acting President Delcy Rodríguez's 180-day interim mandate has lapsed without the National Assembly declaring Maduro's 'absolute absence,' and the NGO Acceso a la Justicia warns of 'blatant and unprecedented unconstitutionality' (Rodríguez ~63% disapproval). Maduro/Flores's SDNY hearing stays JULY 22; Cuba's grid/fuel crisis persists under the US oil blockade; Colombia's de la Espriella takes office AUG 7. No US ground-force moves. || JULY 8-9 — CUBA GRID RESTORING; VENEZUELA/COLOMBIA STEADY: Cuba continued restarting its national grid after the July 6 island-wide collapse (third nationwide blackout in six months), with the US oil blockade and the Venezuelan-crude cutoff keeping the energy crisis acute. VENEZUELA: acting President Delcy Rodríguez faces the dispute that her 180-day interim mandate has lapsed; Maduro/Flores's next SDNY hearing stays July 22. COLOMBIA: de la Espriella handover Aug 7. No US ground-force moves. || JULY 6-8 — CUBA TOTAL BLACKOUT: Cuba's national grid collapsed at midday July 6 (third nationwide blackout in six months / fourth total outage of 2026), cutting power to ~10 million; UNE served ~1% of Havana's demand, restoration underway July 7-8 — driven by the de facto US oil blockade and the cutoff of Venezuelan crude (Díaz-Canel: no fuel imports since January bar one Russian cargo). VENEZUELA: acting President Delcy Rodríguez faces a dispute that her 180-day interim mandate has lapsed; Maduro/Flores's next SDNY hearing stays July 22. COLOMBIA: de la Espriella handover Aug 7. No US ground-force moves. || JULY 5-7 — CUBA GRID COLLAPSE; VENEZUELA MANDATE DISPUTE: An island-wide blackout took Cuba's entire national grid offline July 6 as the US oil blockade and the cutoff of Venezuelan crude deepen the energy crisis (20-22 hr daily cuts). Acting President Delcy Rodríguez alleged destabilization attempts during the post-earthquake emergency (twin June quakes killed 2,295+) and faces a dispute that her 180-day interim mandate has lapsed; Maduro and Flores's next SDNY hearing stays set for July 22; Colombia's transition to Trump-endorsed de la Espriella (handover Aug 7) continued. No US ground-force moves. || JULY 1-4 — Little net change: Maduro and Cilia Flores's next Manhattan federal hearing remains set for JULY 22 (postponed from June 30); Colombia's far-right, Trump-endorsed president-elect Abelardo de la Espriella still takes office AUGUST 7; the Rodríguez government continues its US-cooperation pattern (reports of further detained-American releases); Cuba's fuel/energy/humanitarian emergency keeps deepening under the US oil blockade. || JUNE 21-22 — COLOMBIA SHIFTS FAR-RIGHT: the June 21 presidential runoff was won narrowly by far-right, Trump-endorsed newcomer ABELARDO DE LA ESPRIELLA (~49.65%) OVER LEFTIST IVÁN CEPEDA (~48.70%) — a ~0.95-POINT MARGIN ON RECORD TURNOUT; HE TAKES OFFICE AUGUST 7. VENEZUELA: the Rodríguez government continues granting US-brokered oil concessions (PDVSA-Shell); MADURO'S NEXT MANHATTAN FEDERAL HEARING REMAINS SET FOR JULY 22 (POSTPONED FROM JUNE 30). CUBA: the fuel/energy/humanitarian emergency keeps deepening under the US oil blockade. || in flux — JUNE 19-20: VENEZUELA — MADURO + FLORES NEXT NYC COURT DATE POSTPONED FROM JUNE 30 TO JULY 22, 2026 (PROSECUTOR JAY CLAYTON CITED SECURE-TRANSPORT/SECURITY LOGISTICS TIED TO THE 2026 FIFA WORLD CUP — METLIFE STADIUM HOSTING MATCHES INCL. THE JULY 19 FINAL); DELCY RODRIGUEZ REMAINS ACTING PRESIDENT; CUBA FUEL/GRID CRISIS PERSISTS UNDER THE US BLOCKADE || in flux — JUNE 5: CIA DIR. RATCLIFFE VISITED HAVANA, BRINGING A PARAMILITARY LEADER FROM THE MADURO-CAPTURE OP (CBS); MADURO + FLORES NEXT NYC COURT DATE JUNE 30; RODRIGUEZ STILL ACTING PRESIDENT; CUBA GRID/FUEL CRISIS PERSISTS — in flux — MAY 28-30: VENEZUELA — MADURO + FLORES NEXT NYC COURT DATE JUNE 30, 2026; RODRIGUEZ GOVT CONTINUES CARACAS-COOPERATION PATTERN (OIL CUTOFF TO CUBA, PDVSA OPENING, PRISONER RELEASES) — CUBA: GRID CRISIS PERSISTS AFTER MAY 14 EASTERN-PROVINCES COLLAPSE + MAY 18 5TH NATIONWIDE COLLAPSE; ENERGY MIN: STOCKS 'VIRTUALLY RUN OUT'; US EMBASSY CALLS GRID 'INCREASINGLY UNSTABLE'; CHINA-ASSISTED SOLAR BUILDOUT CONTINUES; US-CUBA TALKS TRACK OPEN — TRUMP ADMIN INDICTED RAÚL CASTRO (MAY 21) OVER 1996 'BROTHERS TO THE RESCUE' SHOOTDOWN — PRIOR in flux — MAY 17-19: VENEZUELA — RODRIGUEZ GOVT DEPORTED ALEX SAAB (MADURO'S FORMER 'MONEY MAN', DEMOTED/FIRED BY RODRIGUEZ) TO THE US MAY 17; SAAB CHARGED MAY 18 IN MIAMI FEDERAL COURT WITH MONEY LAUNDERING (DECADE-OLD FOOD-IMPORT CONTRACT SCHEME), MAY TESTIFY VS MADURO; CONTINUES CARACAS-COOPERATION PATTERN 'GENERATING ENORMOUS GOODWILL IN WASHINGTON' — CUBA: STATE-TV 'CON FILO' HOST MICHEL TORRES CORONA DECRIES SAAB EXTRADITION ('EITHER MADURO IS CORRUPT OR THE RODRÍGUEZ SIBLINGS ARE TRAITORS') — PRIOR in flux — MAY 14-15: CUBA GRID COLLAPSE — ANTONIO GUITERAS LARGEST POWER PLANT SHUT DOWN MAY 14 AT 4:58AM (BOILER LEAK); NATIONAL ELECTRIC SYSTEM SEN AVAILABILITY DROPPED TO 636 MW VS 2,420 MW DEMAND (74% UNMET) — POWER OUTAGES STRETCHED 24 CONSECUTIVE HOURS IN HAVANA; ALL EASTERN PROVINCES GUANTÁNAMO TO CIEGO DE ÁVILA STRIPPED OF POWER — ENERGY MIN VICENTE DE LA O: 'WE HAVE ABSOLUTELY NO FUEL (OIL) AND ABSOLUTELY NO DIESEL' — RUSSIAN UNIVERSAL TANKER (270K BBL DIESEL) STILL ADRIFT ~1,600KM FROM CUBA; ETA PUSHED TO MAY 15 — VENEZUELA RODRIGUEZ AT THE HAGUE MAY 11-12 REJECTS TRUMP '51ST STATE' MUSINGS — PRIOR MAY 12-13: TRUMP TRUTH SOCIAL POST TUESDAY MAY 12 — 'CUBA IS ASKING FOR HELP, AND WE ARE GOING TO TALK!!' — Trump labels Cuba 'A FAILED COUNTRY'; SURPRISE PIVOT FROM 'TAKEOVER' RHETORIC TO TALKS-ANNOUNCEMENT, NO AGENDA DISCLOSED — TRUMP ANNOUNCEMENT MADE AS HE DEPARTED FOR BEIJING — VENEZUELA ACTING PRES DELCY RODRIGUEZ AT THE HAGUE MAY 11 (FIRST OUT-OF-VENEZUELA TRIP SINCE MADURO ABDUCTION JAN 3) REJECTS TRUMP 'SERIOUS CONSIDERATION' OF VENEZUELA AS 51ST STATE: 'IS NOT FORESEEN AND WOULD NEVER BE FORESEEN' — RODRIGUEZ ALSO REPORTEDLY COMPLIANT WITH US DEMANDS INCLUDING STOPPING OIL TO CUBA + OPENING PDVSA TO FOREIGN FIRMS + RELEASING POLITICAL PRISONERS — Cuba power deficit ~1,500+ MW; Universal tanker still adrift Atlantic; Kolodkin reserves depleted — Petro-Rodriguez Apr 24 cooperative pledge under strain",
   "summary": "AUGUST 28: No new grid figure or rationing measure was published in Havana or Caracas, so the record is carried forward rather than inflated. Cuba: six nationwide collapses in 2026, blackouts above twenty hours a day, up to 105 consecutive days without steady power in some communities, neighbourhood protests in Havana after outages beyond 48 hours. Venezuela: pot-banging protests continue over blackouts and voltage damage, with road blockades and burning tyres in Turmero, Aragua; Delcy Rodríguez's electricity and water savings plan, blamed on 'Super Niño' and an earthquake, is openly rejected; Caracas talks under Jorge Rodríguez and Dinorah Figuera continue with no electoral timetable. Maduro and Cilia Flores remain in US custody with trial set for June 1, 2027 on four counts including narco-terrorism conspiracy. Colombia's de la Espriella government continues its hardline programme and the severance of ties with Cuba and Nicaragua. || AUGUST 27: Pot-banging protests multiplied across Venezuela over blackouts, with road blockades and burning tyres in Turmero, Aragua, against four-to-five-hour daily cuts; Delcy Rodríguez activated a national electricity and water savings plan blaming 'Super Niño' and an earthquake, and the measures were openly rejected. Caracas talks continue without an electoral timetable; Maduro and Cilia Flores face trial June 1, 2027. Cuba's figures hold at six nationwide collapses in 2026 and up to 105 consecutive days without steady power in some communities, with neighbourhood protests in Havana after outages beyond 48 hours. Colombia's de la Espriella government continues its hardline programme and the severance of ties with Cuba and Nicaragua. || AUGUST 25: No material change. Cuba has suffered six nationwide grid collapses in 2026 with blackouts above twenty hours a day and some communities up to 105 consecutive days without steady power; FM Bruno Rodríguez reports 'no progress' on ending the fuel impasse, which dates from the January 3 seizure of Maduro and runs under Executive Order 14404. Caracas talks continue without an electoral timetable under Delcy Rodríguez as acting president; Maduro and Cilia Flores face trial on June 1, 2027. || AUGUST 23: The theatre is static. Venezuela's US-backed dialogue has been running since August 6 without producing the electoral timetable that is its stated purpose; Delcy Rodríguez remains acting president; Maduro and Cilia Flores await a June 1, 2027 trial in Manhattan and Maduro has endorsed the talks from jail. Cuba is still recovering from its sixth nationwide blackout of the year on August 2-3, the run of collapses dating from the January severing of the island's main oil supply. Colombia's transition completed on August 7 with de la Espriella's inauguration in Cali, and Petro's fraud allegations continue without institutional effect. || AUGUST 22: THREE TRACKS, ALL MOVING. Venezuela's ninth government-opposition dialogue in twelve years opened in Caracas on August 6 and survived its first weeks, with a published agenda covering democratic institutions, the electoral system and political participation — but its opposition counterparty is contested, with Machado uninvited and she and Edmundo González stating that their camp had no involvement. Colombia changed direction on August 7 when Abelardo de la Espriella was inaugurated in Cali, the first Colombian inauguration outside Bogotá, committed to reversing Petro's 'total peace' approach, to 'Plan Patriota 2.0' with Washington, and to severing ties with Venezuela, Nicaragua and Cuba; two alleged ELN members were killed in an August 10 raid near the Venezuelan border. Cuba's grid collapsed for the sixth time this year on August 2 with storms disrupting restoration, the proximate cause still fuel — Venezuelan supply withdrawn under US pressure, Mexican imports ceased, thermoelectric plants failing for want of parts — and on August 20 Washington sanctioned nine Cuban state-owned companies and the Cuban Institute's leadership. Maduro and Cilia Flores remain in US custody awaiting a June 1, 2027 trial. || AUGUST 1 (Day 156): The formal US-backed transition negotiation opens in Venezuela with Jorge Rodríguez — the interim president's brother — leading the government side and Dinorah Figuera the opposition side, and with María Corina Machado outside the room by her own choice. The structure is consistent both with a genuine transition and with managed continuity; nothing in the opening resolves which. Maduro's trial remains set for June 1, 2027. No material change in Cuba or Colombia this cycle. || JULY 27 (Day 152): Maduro's trial is set for June 1, 2027. Delcy Rodríguez's government continues normalising with Washington after April's sanctions relief and is pressing for the remainder to be lifted. Cuba's grid has failed repeatedly through July under the fuel blockade and the Venezuelan cutoff — the starkest confirmed item in this theatre. Colombia's president-elect is sworn in August 7, with Trump's endorsement of de la Espriella still contested by Petro as interference. || JULY 23: Maduro and Cilia Flores appeared July 22 before US District Judge Alvin K. Hellerstein (SDNY, Manhattan) for a pretrial hearing; the judge SET a June 1, 2027 trial date at both sides' request. Defense counsel Barry Pollack will file sovereign-immunity challenges to the indictment beginning in early September, with oral arguments November 17; both remain held in Brooklyn and deny the charges. Venezuela stays under acting President Delcy Rodriguez, the succession dispute unresolved. Cuba's grid crisis persists under the US oil blockade. || JULY 22: Maduro/Flores trial hearing scheduled to resume today at the SDNY in Manhattan (postponed from June 30 partly over World Cup transport logistics); both held in Brooklyn, denying charges; solidarity protest planned. Acting President Delcy Rodriguez's government holds in Caracas; Cuba's grid stays fragile under the US oil blockade. || JULY 14-15 (Roundup): Cuba's national grid collapsed again on July 14 (~11am local) — the third nationwide blackout in under 10 days and the fifth total outage of 2026 — cutting power to ~10 million as the US oil blockade and the Venezuelan-crude cutoff (plus aging generation) starve the island of fuel; FM Bruno Rodríguez told the UN the US embargo amounts to a 'systematic violation of the human rights of an entire people' and 'collective punishment.' Venezuela's constitutional crisis stayed unresolved under acting President Delcy Rodríguez; Maduro and Flores's next SDNY hearing stays July 22; Colombia's Trump-endorsed de la Espriella takes office Aug 7. No US ground-force move — the coercion is economic, applied without US manpower. (Al Jazeera; WHBL; Cubaheadlines, July 14, 2026.) || JULY 11-12 (Roundup): Venezuela's constitutional crisis deepened as Rodríguez's 180-day interim mandate lapsed without the National Assembly declaring Maduro's 'absolute absence'; experts and the NGO Acceso a la Justicia question her government's legitimacy ('a realm of blatant and unprecedented unconstitutionality'). Maduro and Flores's next SDNY hearing stays July 22; Cuba's energy crisis persists under the US oil blockade and the Venezuelan-crude cutoff; Colombia's Trump-endorsed de la Espriella takes office Aug 7. No US ground-force moves. (Cubaheadlines; PBS NewsHour; Atlantic Council, July 2026.) || JUNE 19-20: Venezuela's removed president Nicolás Maduro and his wife Cilia Flores had their next New York court date postponed from June 30 to July 22, 2026; federal prosecutor Jay Clayton cited the need to avoid 'scheduling and logistical issues related to providing secure transportation and security measures' tied to the 2026 FIFA World Cup (the New York/New Jersey region hosting matches at MetLife Stadium, including the July 19 final). Delcy Rodríguez remains acting president of Venezuela; Cuba's fuel and grid crisis persists under the US blockade. (Cubaheadlines; PBS News; Wikipedia '2026 United States intervention in Venezuela,' June 2026.)  || [JUNE 15-16 UPDATE] Mid-June: situation broadly unchanged. Delcy Rodriguez remains acting president of Venezuela following the January US intervention and Maduro's capture; Maduro's US trial in New York continues. Cuba's oil/economic crisis persists under the American fuel blockade after the cutoff of Venezuelan oil. No major new June 15-16 developments. (CNN; PBS NewsHour; Wikipedia '2026 Cuban crisis,' June 2026.) [JUNE 5] CBS News reported CIA Director John Ratcliffe visited Havana for talks with senior Cuban officials, bringing a paramilitary leader involved in the Maduro-capture operation and introducing him as the operator who 'killed their people' in Venezuela. Maduro and Cilia Flores remain set to return to a Manhattan federal court June 30; Delcy Rodriguez continues as acting president in Caracas amid Cuba's persistent grid/fuel crisis. (CBS News; NBC News; ABC News, June 2026). [JUNE 1-2] Maduro and Cilia Flores remained set to return to a Manhattan federal court on June 30; Cuba's grid crisis persisted amid the loss of Venezuelan oil. (PBS; Fox Business, 2026). [MAY 31-JUNE 1] Maduro and Cilia Flores were set to return to a Manhattan federal court on June 30; Cuba's grid crisis persisted amid the loss of Venezuelan oil. MAY 15-16: CUBA grid collapse continues — Antonio Guiteras thermoelectric plant shut, SEN availability ~636 MW vs ~2,420 MW demand (74% unmet); Energy Minister: 'absolutely no fuel.' VENEZUELA: Maduro trial proceeds in NYC (judge weighing key issues, no ruling yet); Delcy Rodriguez acting president (US lifted her sanctions Apr 10); María Corina Machado weighing return; US-managed transition framing continues. || PRIOR: MAY 14 CUBA GRID COLLAPSE: Cuba experiencing one of its worst energy crises in decades following partial breakdown of National Electric System (SEN) on May 14. Failure began at 6:09 a.m. May 14, shortly after the country's largest power plant, ANTONIO GUITERAS thermoelectric plant, shut down at 4:58 a.m. due to a boiler leak. By 6:30 a.m. SEN's available capacity dwindled to mere 636 MW while demand reached 2,420 MW — leaving 1,790 MW of demand unmet (74% of total demand) — per Cuba Headlines + Al Jazeera + NPR + FMT + CiberCuba. Power outages in Havana stretched to 24 consecutive hours Thursday May 14; collapse stripped power from all eastern provinces from Guantánamo to Ciego de Ávila. Energy Min VICENTE DE LA O confirmed: 'We have absolutely no fuel (oil) and absolutely no diesel' (Jamaica Observer + FMT + CiberCuba). RUSSIAN UNIVERSAL TANKER (270K bbl diesel; left Vysotsk in January) still adrift in North Atlantic ~1,600km from Cuba; ETA pushed back to May 15 per Russian news + CubaHeadlines. CNN MAY 13: As US starves Cuba of oil, island pulling off one of fastest solar revolutions on the planet with China's help. UN declared humanitarian emergency April: 96K+ surgeries postponed, 1M+ relying on tanker trucks for water, 500K children facing reduced school hours. MAY 14 SOLAR DRIVE: Chinese-assisted solar buildout continuing under US blockade pressure. VENEZUELA: Rodriguez at The Hague (first out-of-Venezuela since Maduro abduction Jan 3) responded to Trump's May 11-12 'seriously considering' Venezuela 51st state Truth Social posts — said country 'would never surrender its independence': 'if there is one thing Venezuelan men and women have, it is that we love our independence process' (CNN, WSLS, RIO Times, Washington Post). Rodriguez was at ICJ on Guyana Essequibo dispute. PRIOR MAY 12-13 — TRUMP CUBA TALKS ANNOUNCEMENT: Trump on Truth Social Tuesday May 12 'Cuba is asking for help, and we are going to talk!!' — described communist-ruled island as 'a failed country.' Announcement made as Trump departed for China; no concrete details or negotiation agenda released; neither White House nor State Dept has explained how talks will unfold or US demands; no immediate Cuban government response. Marks surprise pivot from 'takeover' rhetoric (May 1 'almost immediately' Forum Club at Palm Beach) to talks-announcement track. Context: 240+ new sanctions since Jan 2026; at least 7 oil tankers intercepted; Cuba energy imports reduced 80-90%; blackouts affecting 55%+ of territory up to 25 hours daily; projected GDP contraction -7.2% for 2026. VENEZUELA ACTING PRES DELCY RODRIGUEZ at The Hague Monday May 11 (first time she's left Venezuela since Maduro abduction Jan 3) rejects Trump's 'serious consideration' of annexing Venezuela as 51st US state: 'is not foreseen and would never be foreseen.' Rodriguez reportedly compliant with US demands including stopping oil deliveries to Cuba, opening PDVSA to foreign firms, releasing political prisoners (Al Jazeera). Rodriguez heads to Hague for Guyana ICJ land dispute case. PRIOR MAY 3 — TRUMP MAY 1 EXECUTIVE ORDER EXPANDED CUBA SANCTIONS + 'TAKING OVER' RHETORIC: Trump signed executive order May 1 broadening US sanctions on Cuban government — targets persons, entities and affiliates supporting security apparatus; includes corruption + human rights violators; can apply to 'any foreign person' operating in 'energy, defense and related materiel, metals and mining, financial services, or security sector of the Cuban economy.' Speaking at Forum Club of the Palm Beaches in West Palm Beach Friday May 1, Trump said US would 'take over' Cuba 'almost immediately'; described scenario in which USS Abraham Lincoln (CVN-72) would be positioned ~100 yards off Cuban coast as it transits back from CENTCOM ('We'll bring it in, have it stop roughly 100 yards off the shore, and they'll say, thank you very much, we surrender'). Comments tied to ongoing US-Israeli war against Iran. President Diaz-Canel labeled Trump admin 'fascist'; 'no aggressor, no matter how powerful, will find surrender in Cuba'; asserted 'every Cuban is armed' against 'imminent US military threat.' CUBA MAY 2-3: power deficit ~1,500+ MW; Russian Universal tanker (251K bbl diesel; original ETA Apr 29) still moving erratically across North Atlantic, delayed at least until end of May per CiberCuba/CubaHeadlines; OFAC General License 134A speculated as cause of course deviation. Russian Anatoli Kolodkin tanker reserves (delivered Mar 30) rapidly depleting. Hermanos Diaz refinery (Santiago) still processing domestic heavy crude (forced substitution); Cuba's own oil production: ~40K bpd vs domestic demand 90-110K bpd. Health system backlog 96K+ pending surgeries (incl 11K children); ~1M people dependent on water trucking; worst since 1990s 'Special Period.' May Day 'La Patria Se Defiende' rallies May 1: 500K+ marchers per gov claim; Raul Castro + Diaz-Canel + Bruno Rodriguez at José Martí Anti-Imperialist Tribune opposite US Embassy. PETRO-RODRIGUEZ Apr 24 Caracas visit (first head-of-state since Maduro abduction Jan 3) holds; military cooperation pledge on 2,200km border. RODRIGUEZ exceeded constitutional 90-day acting-presidency cap (Apr 21). MADURO PLEADED NOT GUILTY (Apr 13); next hearing Mar 17, 2027. US Embassy resuming operations. 100+ demonstrations in March (highest since ACLED 2018). GDP -7.2% forecast. Colombia: FARC dissident clashes intensified; EMC attacks drove 35% increase in violence in Cauca; Colombia internal security raised at UNSC Apr 21.",
