   "status_label": "AUGUST 28: THE CONFRONTATION MOVED FROM OIL TO BANKS, NETWORKS AND THE INDO-PACIFIC IN A SINGLE CYCLE. REPORTING ON AUGUST 27 INDICATED THE ADMINISTRATION IS CONSIDERING SANCTIONS AGAINST CHINESE BANKS CONDUCTING BUSINESS WITH IRAN — THE MEASURE OPERATION ECONOMIC OUTCAST DEFERRED BEHIND A 'CURE PERIOD,' AND THE ONE THAT WOULD ACTUALLY TEST LIN JIAN'S PLEDGE TO 'TAKE ALL NECESSARY MEASURES TO FIRMLY SAFEGUARD ITS OWN RIGHTS AND INTERESTS.' ON THE SAME DAY THE JUSTICE DEPARTMENT ANNOUNCED THAT CHINESE HACKERS CARRIED OUT A STRING OF INTRUSIONS AGAINST FEDERAL AGENCIES AND HOSPITALS OVER THE PAST YEAR, MOSTLY LINKED TO THE STATE-SPONSORED GROUP QTFY, OPENING A CYBER FRONT ALONGSIDE THE TARIFF AND OIL TRACKS. AND PENTAGON OFFICIALS WERE REPORTED CONCERNED THAT A PROLONGED IRAN CONFLICT COULD EMBOLDEN CHINA IN THE INDO-PACIFIC — THE OVERSTRETCH THESIS, ATTRIBUTED FOR THE FIRST TIME TO SERVING OFFICIALS IN THE DEPARTMENT RUNNING THE WAR RATHER THAN TO OUTSIDE CRITICS. THE VOLUMES KEEP FALLING WITHOUT A DESIGNATION HAVING BITTEN: KPLER PUTS CHINESE IMPORTS OF IRANIAN CRUDE VIA HORMUZ AT ~530,000 BPD, 48% BELOW PRE-WAR AND 72% BELOW THE OCTOBER 2024 PEAK, WITH THE BLOCKADE, FREIGHT AND ROUTE LENGTH DOING THE WORK. THE TARIFF TRACK IS UNCHANGED AND STILL POINTED AT THE SEPTEMBER 24 XI-TRUMP SUMMIT, WITH THE TRUCE EXPIRING NOVEMBER 10 AND THE 12.5% FORCED-LABOUR TARIFF IN FORCE SINCE JULY 24. TAIWAN: NO ESCALATION. || AUGUST 27: BEIJING MOVED FROM OBJECTION TO A THREAT OF COUNTERMEASURES AND TEHRAN THANKED IT BY NAME. LIN JIAN SAID ON AUGUST 26 THAT 'CHINA WILL TAKE ALL NECESSARY MEASURES TO FIRMLY SAFEGUARD ITS OWN RIGHTS,' RESTATING THAT TRADE WITH IRAN 'SHOULD NOT BE DISRUPTED' AND 'COMPLIES WITH INTERNATIONAL LAW,' AND CONDEMNING THE US MEASURES AS ECONOMIC WARFARE; PARLIAMENT SPEAKER GHALIBAF THANKED CHINA FOR DECLINING TO COMPLY. THE VOLUME KEEPS FALLING WITHOUT ANY DESIGNATION HAVING BITTEN: KPLER DATA PUBLISHED AUGUST 26 PUTS CHINESE IMPORTS OF IRANIAN CRUDE THROUGH HORMUZ AT ABOUT 530,000 BARRELS A DAY, 48% BELOW PRE-WAR AND 72% BELOW THE OCTOBER 2024 PEAK OF 1.9 MILLION, WITH THE RED SEA ROUTE AT ~1.02 MILLION IN AUGUST AGAINST AN APRIL PEAK OF 2.62 MILLION. SHANDONG TEAPOTS ARE SUBSTITUTING BRAZILIAN LAPA AND IRAQI BASRAH; SUEZ FREIGHT RUNS $10-11 A BARREL AGAINST ~$5 VIA YANBU PRE-HOUTHI AND THE CAPE ROUTE TAKES ~50 DAYS AGAINST 25-27. THE 7.5% SECTION 301 OVERCAPACITY TARIFF REMAINS PREPARED AHEAD OF THE SEPTEMBER 24 XI-TRUMP SUMMIT, WHICH WOULD TAKE SECOND-TERM DUTIES TO ~20% AGAINST A CURRENT 12.5% REPLACEMENT RATE, WITH THE TRUCE EXPIRING NOVEMBER 10. PLA SORTIE RATES AROUND TAIWAN HAVE REVERTED TO THE PRE-LAI BASELINE SINCE THE START OF 2026. || AUGUST 25: TWO AMERICAN INSTRUMENTS WERE POINTED AT BEIJING ON THE SAME DAY AND THEY PULL IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS. OPERATION ECONOMIC OUTCAST'S OPENING DESIGNATIONS INCLUDE PERSONS, ENTITIES AND VESSELS IN CHINA AND HONG KONG among roughly 60 across five jurisdictions, and the administration signalled that CHINA IS NOT EXEMPT from the secondary-sanctions architecture; Bessent framed the choice as commercial ties with Tehran or access to the American financial system, with 'no one above the reach of US sanctions.' BEIJING'S POSITION IS UNCHANGED AND ITS COUNTERMEASURES PRE-DATE THE ANNOUNCEMENT: the Foreign Ministry restated that sanctions do not 'fit the interest of any party' and that 'sanctions and pressure tactics are not the solution'; China has ordered its companies to disregard US restrictions on independent refiners importing Iranian crude and has invoked its 2021 BLOCKING STATUTE prohibiting compliance with foreign sanctions it deems illegitimate. SEPARATELY AND ON THE SAME DAY, the United States was reported to be preparing a 7.5% SECTION 301 OVERCAPACITY TARIFF on Chinese goods ahead of a XI-TRUMP SUMMIT IN WASHINGTON ON SEPTEMBER 24 — a rate that would take second-term duties to roughly 20%, precisely the ceiling Washington committed to on JULY 27, with the current replacement rate at 12.5% and the bilateral trade truce expiring NOVEMBER 10. AND THE VOLUME THAT MATTERS WAS ACHIEVED WITHOUT EITHER INSTRUMENT: Chinese imports of Iranian crude remain around 534,000 BARRELS A DAY in August against about 1.4 MILLION a day in 2025, a fall of roughly 60% year-on-year produced by naval interdiction rather than by a designation list. || AUGUST 24: THE ENFORCEMENT COLLISION ARRIVES AND THE VOLUMES SHOW THE BLOCKADE HAS ALREADY DONE WHAT THE SANCTIONS ARE MEANT TO DO. CHINA'S IRANIAN CRUDE IMPORTS HAVE FALLEN TO ABOUT 534,000 BARRELS A DAY SO FAR IN AUGUST against an average of 1.4 MILLION A DAY LAST YEAR — a roughly 60% year-on-year collapse achieved by NAVAL INTERDICTION BEFORE A SINGLE NEW DESIGNATION HAS BEEN ISSUED. Beijing's position is unchanged: foreign ministry spokesman LIN JIAN says 'SANCTIONS AND PRESSURE TACTICS ARE NOT THE SOLUTION' and that such measures do not 'FIT THE INTEREST OF ANY PARTY'; the MINISTRY OF COMMERCE has invoked the 2021 SANCTIONS-BLOCKING MECHANISM directing Chinese firms not to comply with US designations of refineries importing Iranian crude, the first explicit order of its kind, and Chinese embassy spokesman LIU PENGYU says normal trade 'SHOULD NOT BE SUBJECT TO INTERFERENCE OR DISRUPTION.' BESSENT has told third countries they are 'EITHER WITH US OR AGAINST US.' The collision is scheduled against a calendar: XI JINPING IS DUE IN WASHINGTON AROUND SEPTEMBER 24, arriving September 23 and departing September 25 for a single day of White House meetings, and is reported to be SKIPPING THE UN GENERAL ASSEMBLY to limit the trip to the Trump meeting. Taiwan was quiet: the HAN KUANG exercises ran to AUGUST 14 emphasising resilience and dispersal, with no new PLA activity reported. || AUGUST 23: BEIJING REJECTED THE SANCTIONS ARCHITECTURE BY NAME BEFORE IT WAS PUBLISHED. On AUGUST 21-22 the foreign ministry restated that China OPPOSES 'ILLICIT UNILATERAL SANCTIONS THAT LACK BASIS IN INTERNATIONAL LAW AND UN SECURITY COUNCIL MANDATE' and does not recognise the measures against Iran and its trading partners; spokesman LIN JIAN said 'SANCTIONS AND PRESSURE WILL NOT HELP RESOLVE THE ISSUE.' BESSENT HAS TOLD THIRD COUNTRIES THEY ARE 'EITHER WITH US OR AGAINST US' and that 'ANY REMAINING TIE TO TEHRAN WILL HASTEN A NATION'S ECONOMIC OBLIVION, WHETHER THAT TIE BE PURPOSEFULLY CONSTRUCTED OR WILLFULLY IGNORED,' promising the Treasury will 'PUT ITS FULL MIGHT AND FORCE TOWARD ENFORCING AGAINST YOU.' CHINA BOUGHT MORE THAN 80% OF IRAN'S SHIPPED OIL IN 2025 AND HAS ALREADY INVOKED ITS BLOCKING RULE directing Chinese companies not to comply, building on a May 2026 blocking order. THE COMMERCIAL FACTS HAVE MOVED WITHOUT BEIJING'S CONSENT ANYWAY: Chinese imports of Iranian crude fell to 534,000 BPD IN AUGUST FROM 823,000 IN JULY with ~40 MILLION BARRELS STRANDED IN MALAYSIAN WATERS. THE TARIFF TRACK RUNS SEPARATELY: Section 301 forced-labour tariffs of 10-12.5% on 60 countries took effect JULY 24, with CHINA AT 12.5% (+2.5 POINTS OVERALL) and TAIWAN NOT SUBJECT TO CHINA SECTION 301. THE ENFORCEMENT COLLISION IS SCHEDULED FOR MONDAY AUGUST 24. || AUGUST 22: THE SANCTIONS PHASE COLLIDES WITH BEIJING, AND THE COMMERCIAL FACTS MOVE WITHOUT BEIJING'S CONSENT. CHINA REJECTED US PRESSURE ON AUGUST 20-21, restating that SANCTIONS WILL NOT RESOLVE DISPUTES, as Trump threatened penalties on any country extending Tehran 'ANY TYPE OF LIFELINE' and Bessent promised 'THE GREATEST COORDINATED ECONOMIC ISOLATION IN THE HISTORY OF THE WORLD.' YET CHINESE IMPORTS OF IRANIAN CRUDE FELL TO 534,000 BPD IN AUGUST FROM 823,000 IN JULY, with FEWER SEPTEMBER AND OCTOBER CARGO OFFERS, prices up, CHINESE INDEPENDENT 'TEAPOT' REFINERS ON ALERT FOR BUYER-SPECIFIC DESIGNATIONS, and ROUGHLY 40 MILLION BARRELS OF IRANIAN CRUDE STRANDED IN MALAYSIAN WATERS. Beijing's substantive position is unchanged — settle through negotiation, carry out the MOU, avoid resorting to force, SUPPORT FOR IRAN DIPLOMATIC ONLY; the REUTERS MANPADS REPORT REMAINS DENIED BY BOTH BEIJING AND ISLAMABAD AND UNVERIFIED. ON TAIWAN, TAIPEI RAN HAN KUANG FROM AUGUST 5-14 — its largest annual drills, with air-raid exercises and a deliberate temporary MOBILE-INTERNET SLOWDOWN — and on AUGUST 21 TAIWAN'S COAST GUARD WARNED OFF A CHINESE RESEARCH SHIP in western waters, the THIRD APPEARANCE SINCE MAY; no new PLA blockade-style exercise was announced. HONG KONG CONVICTED LEE CHEUK-YAN AND CHOW HANG-TUNG UNDER THE NATIONAL SECURITY LAW ON AUGUST 21 over the Tiananmen vigils. THE CALENDAR IS UNMOVED: XI'S WHITE HOUSE VISIT EXPECTED AROUND SEPTEMBER 24, tariff exclusions on 178 Chinese products to NOVEMBER 10, 2026, the USTR 12.5% SECTION 301 FORCED-LABOUR TARIFF (effective JULY 24) the operative new measure, against the May Beijing summit's unresolved Taiwan warning. THE BALANCE FACT REMAINS AMERICAN: 60%+ OF PATRIOT AND THAAD EXPENDED, ~1/3 OF THE TOMAHAWK INVENTORY DEPLETED AFTER 1,000+ FIRED, ANSWERED BY A $22.9BN SEVEN-YEAR RAYTHEON CONTRACT. Roughly 40% of Chinese oil imports transit HORMUZ, where traffic runs at 1-13 vessels a day against 73. (Reuters and AP via Just Security, August 21, 2026; CNBC; Asharq Al-Awsat; CSIS.) || AUGUST 2: NO NEW DEVELOPMENT — AND THE CYCLE'S RELEVANT FACT IS AMERICAN, NOT CHINESE. Beijing's position is unchanged: settle disputes through negotiations, carry out the MOU, avoid resorting to force; support for Iran remains DIPLOMATIC ONLY; the REUTERS MANPADS REPORT (up to 400 QW-12/FN-16 launchers, $60-70m, via a Hong Kong intermediary) REMAINS DENIED BY BOTH BEIJING AND ISLAMABAD AND UNVERIFIED. The trade calendar is unmoved: the USTR's 12.5% SECTION 301 FORCED-LABOUR TARIFF (effective JULY 24) is the operative new measure, tariff exclusions on 178 Chinese products run to NOVEMBER 10, 2026, and XI'S WHITE HOUSE VISIT is still expected around SEPTEMBER 24, against the May Beijing summit's unresolved Taiwan warning. THE MATERIAL NEW FACT BEARS ON THE BALANCE RATHER THAN THE RELATIONSHIP: CSIS ASSESSES THAT MORE THAN 60% OF US PATRIOT AND THAAD INTERCEPTOR STOCKS HAVE BEEN EXPENDED IN THE IRAN WAR, WITH THREE-PLUS YEARS TO REBUILD, AND ITS STATED STRATEGIC CONCERN IS THE ABILITY TO RESPOND TO ANOTHER HIGH-INTENSITY CONTINGENCY BEFORE INVENTORIES RECOVER — a constraint that applies to any Taiwan scenario and was produced entirely by a different theatre. Roughly 40% of Chinese oil imports transit HORMUZ, where transits fell 77% in a single day this cycle. (CSIS; CNN; Military Times; Chinese Foreign Ministry; USTR; Reuters, July 24 – August 1, 2026.) || AUGUST 1: BEIJING HOLDS THE DIPLOMATIC LINE WHILE ITS ENERGY EXPOSURE EASES. China's Foreign Ministry position is unchanged — urging both sides to SETTLE DISPUTES THROUGH NEGOTIATIONS, CARRY OUT THE MOU and AVOID RESORTING TO FORCE — and Beijing's support for Iran remains DIPLOMATIC ONLY; the REUTERS MANPADS REPORT (up to 400 QW-12/FN-16 launchers, $60-70m, via a Hong Kong intermediary) REMAINS DENIED BY BOTH BEIJING AND ISLAMABAD AND UNVERIFIED. CHINA'S JULY CRUDE IMPORTS ARE POISED TO REBOUND FROM A DECADE LOW. Roughly 40% of Chinese oil imports transit HORMUZ; IRANIAN CRUDE was 11.6% of China's seaborne imports in 2026 to date, recent flows ~1.252 MB/D out of KHARG, with 200M+ BARRELS reported stored on tankers near China. On trade, the USTR's 12.5% SECTION 301 FORCED-LABOUR TARIFF (effective JULY 24) remains the operative new measure, raising the overall burden on Chinese goods by about 2.5 POINTS. An IRANIAN STRIKE KILLED A WORKER AT A CHINESE COMPANY'S BUILDING IN KUWAIT on July 30 — Beijing has issued no retaliatory posture. (Newsweek; Chinese Foreign Ministry; Bloomberg via Yahoo Finance; Kpler; USTR; China Briefing; Reuters, July 2026.) || JULY 31: THE RELATIONSHIP IS PULLED BOTH WAYS IN A SINGLE CYCLE. The Reuters report that Iran will receive within weeks the first of UP TO 400 CHINESE QW-12/FN-16 MANPADS worth $60-70m — routed from Urumqi through Pakistan via the Hong Kong intermediary Zhongqing Baoshang International Investment — remains UNVERIFIED and categorically denied (Beijing: \"completely groundless\"; Pakistan's military: \"absolutely concocted and false\"). IN THE SAME CYCLE AN IRANIAN MISSILE DESTROYED A CHINESE COMPANY'S BUILDING IN NORTHERN KUWAIT AND KILLED A WORKER THERE — a direct Chinese grievance against Tehran. Neither event produced a stated change in Chinese policy. THE DIPLOMATIC CALENDAR IS UNCHANGED: Xi's White House visit is still expected around SEPTEMBER 24 and USTR tariff exclusions on 178 Chinese products run to NOVEMBER 10, against the May 14-15 Beijing summit's unresolved Taiwan warning — Xi telling Trump that mishandling Taiwan would put the relationship in \"GREAT JEOPARDY.\" (Reuters via U.S. News & World Report, July 28, 2026; Al Arabiya English, July 30, 2026; CNBC; CSIS; Al Jazeera, May 2026.) || JULY 30: THE MOST CONSEQUENTIAL US-CHINA DEVELOPMENT OF THE CYCLE IS AN ARMS TRANSFER, NOT A TARIFF. REUTERS reported, on three sources, that IRAN WILL RECEIVE WITHIN WEEKS the first shipment of up to 400 CHINESE-MADE SHOULDER-FIRED AIR-DEFENCE LAUNCHERS — 300-400 QW-12 and FN-16 MANPADS worth $60-70M, contracted through the Hong Kong intermediary ZHONGQING BAOSHANG INTERNATIONAL INVESTMENT and routed from URUMQI through PAKISTAN — Tehran's largest known effort to rebuild short-range air defence since the war exposed those gaps. BEIJING DENIED IT: 'The relevant reports are completely groundless. China has consistently played a role in promoting peace and ending the conflict.' PAKISTAN'S MILITARY called the claims 'absolutely concocted and false.' THE DIPLOMATIC AND TARIFF CALENDAR IS NONETHELESS INTACT: XI'S WHITE HOUSE VISIT holds for ~SEPTEMBER 24 alongside UNGA; USTR has extended tariff exclusions on 178 CHINESE PRODUCTS to NOVEMBER 10, 2026; the July 24 SECTION 301 forced-labour duties are fully operative with the in-transit window closed July 28. The Senate's Graham bill would authorise up to 200% tariffs on Chinese purchases of Russian energy, coupling this theatre to Ukraine. PLA JOINT COMBAT READINESS PATROLS around TAIWAN continue. (Reuters via U.S. News; The Times of Israel; Iran International; CSIS; USTR; China Briefing, July 28-29, 2026.) || JULY 29 (Day 153): THE JULY 24 REGIME COMPLETES ITS TRANSITION — the in-transit exemption expired ON THE CYCLE DATE. Goods shipped before July 24 were exempt only if they ARRIVED BEFORE JULY 28, 2026, so the Section 301 forced-labour package is now applying without carve-out. The USTR action imposed a 12.5% Section 301 duty on Chinese goods for failure to prohibit imports produced with forced labour — raising the overall tariff on Chinese goods by about 2.5 points — inside a wider package of 10-12.5% duties covering roughly 60 economies and more than 80 countries INCLUDING TAIWAN, which replaced the Section 122 10% global tariff when it hit its 150-day statutory limit. China remains most heavily taxed at about 35% above MFN on most goods and well over 100% on certain strategic products. RUNNING ALONGSIDE: MOFCOM confirms the two sides have 'AGREED IN PRINCIPLE' to include AGRICULTURAL products in a reciprocal tariff-reduction framework with indicative two-way targets. DIPLOMACY: RUBIO and WANG YI met for 90 MINUTES on July 22 on the sidelines of the ASEAN foreign ministers' meeting in MANILA, discussing XI JINPING'S PLANNED US VISIT and a possible SEPTEMBER leaders' summit; Wang called it 'pragmatic, positive and constructive' and urged 'strategic stability' and 'peaceful coexistence,' Rubio said differences remain but the job is to manage them and called the Beijing-Manila dispute over Second Thomas Shoal 'very negative and benefiting no one.' Russia, Japan, South Korea, India, Australia, Canada, Britain and the EU also attended. A NEW VECTOR OPENED FROM THE UKRAINE FILE: the Senate's Graham bill would impose stiff SECONDARY TARIFFS on China and India for buying Russian energy. ENERGY: China moves ~5.4 mb/d through Hormuz and takes an estimated 80-90% of Iranian exports; it ranks fourth among economies most exposed to the closure. (USTR; Trade Compliance Resource Hub; China Briefing; TaiwanPlus; South China Morning Post; Business Recorder; CNBC; Zero Carbon Analytics, July 2026.) || JULY 28 (Day 152): THE JULY 24 SECTION 301 REGIME IS OPERATIVE — the 10% Section 122 global tariff, ruled unlawful by the Court of International Trade in May 2026 but kept in force by a Federal Circuit stay, hit its 150-day statutory limit on July 24 and was replaced the same day by SECTION 301 FORCED-LABOUR DUTIES of 10-12.5% covering roughly 60 economies INCLUDING TAIWAN. China remains most heavily taxed at about 35% above MFN on most goods and well over 100% on certain strategic products. RUNNING ALONGSIDE: China's Ministry of Commerce confirmed the two sides have 'AGREED IN PRINCIPLE' to include AGRICULTURAL products in a framework for reciprocal tariff reductions and to set indicative targets for expanding two-way agricultural trade. ENERGY: Chinese seaborne crude imports are expected to average ~7.8 mb/d in July, rebounding from a more-than-decade low as Gulf flows partially resumed after the June interim agreement — before the July escalation snarled Hormuz again; China moves ~5.4 mb/d through the Strait and takes an estimated 80-90% of Iranian exports, and has been in talks with Tehran on safe passage. MILITARY: Rubio has voiced concern over China's increasing regional military activity while Hegseth describes relations as 'better than they've been in many years,' making no mention of Taiwan. (Trade Compliance Resource Hub; TaiwanPlus; China Briefing; PIIE; Bloomberg/Yahoo Finance; Bruegel, July 2026.) || JULY 27: THE TARIFF LEVEL SURVIVES THE INSTRUMENT. The Section 122 10% tariff hit its 150-day statutory limit on July 24 and was replaced the same day by Section 301 forced-labour duties of 10-12.5% on ~60 economies including Taiwan; China's combined duties stand ~35% above MFN and well over 100% on some strategic goods. Taiwan and the EU were the only partners given a 10% rate not stacked on existing MFN duties. The Taipei Times argued July 27 that the wave signals a durable shift in the trade order. Waltz said Trump warned Putin and Xi that 'bad things will happen' if they keep assisting Iran; Beijing separately confirmed support for Pakistani mediation between Washington and Tehran. || JULY 26: TARIFFS LIVE AND ASYMMETRIC. With the Section 301 forced-labour duties of 10-12.5% on ~60 economies in force since July 24, Taiwan and the EU emerged as the only two partners given a 10% rate NOT stacked on existing MFN duties — the package's most favourable treatment, which Taipei assesses could advantage Taiwanese traditional manufacturers over Japanese and Chinese rivals. China at 12.5% on top of existing Section 301/232 duties (~35% above MFN on most goods, >100% on some strategic products) signalled it would look past the levies ahead of Xi's planned US trip, with reciprocal-cut talks worth ~$30B a side continuing. Managed friction, not rupture; no new military clash reported. (Taipei Times, July 25; Bloomberg; SCMP, July 24, 2026.) || JULY 25: New US tariffs of 10-12.5% on ~60 economies took effect July 24 (China at 12.5%; Taiwan, EU, Japan, South Korea, India covered) as the temporary Section 122 duties expired — a Section 301 forced-labor action covering >99% of US trade (fuel, food, USMCA-covered goods exempt). Bloomberg: the China rate 'might irk Beijing without provoking retaliation'; China signals it will look past the levies ahead of Xi's planned US trip, with both sides accelerating talks on reciprocal tariff cuts each valued at ~$30B. (NBC News; Bloomberg; SCMP, July 24, 2026.) || JULY 24: The US imposed a fresh tariff wave on ~60 countries (incl. China, Japan, South Korea, India, EU) hours before the temporary 10% Section 122 blanket levy expired — 10-12.5% under a Section 301 forced-labour finding; China to a 12.5% added rate (~35%+ effective). The broader Trump-Xi truce otherwise holds toward ~November 2026; Taiwan unchanged at ~10% + Section 232 metals; no military escalation. A separate Section 301 excess-capacity probe (16 economies) stays open. (SCMP; USTR; tariff trackers, July 24, 2026.) || JULY 18: The tariff truce holds. USTR's Section 301 completion deadline is ~July 20 (proposed 12.5% duties on 46 countries incl. China); the Section 122 10% tariff is set to expire ~July 24, leaving China at a ~35%+ effective rate; the broader US-China truce runs to ~November 2026. Taiwan unchanged (10% + 25% semiconductor Section 232). (USTR; tariff trackers, July 2026.)  || fragile truce — JULY 15-16: no new US-China escalation. The Trump-Xi tariff truce held; the base Section 122 10% tariff is set to sunset ~July 24 (ruled unlawful by the CIT May 7, stayed by the Federal Circuit June 11, so CBP keeps collecting pending appeal), with Section 301 measures — leaving China at an effective ~35% — the intended replacement. Taiwan sits at a 15% deal rate. No US-China military escalation reported. || fragile truce — JULY 14-15: no new US-China escalation. The Trump-Xi tariff truce held (base Section 122 10% tariff still set to expire ~July 24, Section 301 measures intended to replace it for China); Trump was reported weighing a Taiwan arms package after the summit, and Taiwan's new Littoral Combat Command (commissioned July 1) integrates US-produced missile systems and an unmanned-surface-vessel unit. The China-Russia 'Joint Sea-2026' drills (July 6-13) had concluded. No US-China military escalation. || fragile truce — JULY 11-13: no new US-China escalation. The base Section 122 10% tariff expires ~July 24 (Section 301 measures intended to replace it for China; a USTR proposal floats 12.5% on ~46 countries, July 20 deadline); China resumed US soybean buying (COFCO booked ~6 cargoes). China-Russia 'Joint Sea-2026' drills near Qingdao completed their practical phase July 11 (exercise runs July 6-13), with a joint Pacific patrol to follow; Taiwan tracked a record 110+ Chinese naval/coast-guard vessels along the First Island Chain and reported permanent China Coast Guard patrols east of Taiwan. No US-China military escalation. || fragile truce — JULY 11: the sides 'agreed in principle' to add agricultural products to a reciprocal tariff-reduction framework, even as Taiwan-adjacent military signaling continued (China Coast Guard patrols east of Taiwan July 4; China-Russia 'Joint Sea-2026' drills July 6-13; a July 6 PLA SLBM test in the South Pacific). The base Section 122 10% tariff expires ~July 24, with Section 301 measures intended to replace it for China; the ~30%+ effective China rate holds. No US-China military escalation. || fragile truce — JULY 8-9: No new escalation. The base Section 122 10% tariff is set to expire ~July 24, with the administration intending to replace it for China (and Thailand, Vietnam, India) via Section 301 measures in late July (effective China rate holds ~30%+). The July 7 USTR Section 301 forced-labor hearing stands; no new Taiwan military event. President Lee Jae-myung of South Korea attended the NATO Ankara summit as a partner. || fragile truce — JULY 7-8: USTR held its July 7 hearing on the proposed Section 301 forced-labor tariffs (10%/12.5% duties across ~60 economies incl. China; July 6 comment deadline). The base Section 122 10% tariff expires ~July 24, with the administration intending to replace it for China (and Thailand, Vietnam, India) via Section 301 measures. The ~30% effective China tariff holds; no new Taiwan military event. || fragile truce — JULY 6-7: USTR held July 7 hearings on proposed Section 301 forced-labor tariffs (60-country probe; July 6 comment deadline); the base Section 122 10% tariff expires ~July 24, with the administration intending to replace it for China (and Thailand, Vietnam, India) with Section 301 measures. The ~30% effective China tariff holds; no new Taiwan military event. || fragile truce — JULY 2026: the ~30% effective China tariff holds, but the Section 122 10% global tariff is set to expire ~July 24 and the administration intends to replace it for China (and Thailand, Vietnam, India) with Section 301 tariffs in late July; USTR's proposed 12.5% Section 301 forced-labor tariff on China is advancing. Taiwan sits at a 15% headline rate (~12.3% effective) after the Feb 2026 deal. || fragile truce",
   "summary": "AUGUST 28: Reporting indicates the administration is considering sanctions against Chinese banks doing business with Iran — the escalation Operation Economic Outcast deferred behind a cure period and the one measure that would test Lin Jian's pledge to take 'all necessary measures.' The Justice Department announced that Chinese hackers, mostly linked to the state-sponsored group QTFY, carried out a string of intrusions against federal agencies and hospitals over the past year. Pentagon officials were reported concerned that a prolonged Iran conflict could embolden China in the Indo-Pacific — the overstretch thesis attributed to serving officials rather than outside commentators. Kpler puts Chinese imports of Iranian crude via Hormuz at ~530,000 bpd, 48% below pre-war and 72% below the October 2024 peak, driven by the blockade and freight costs rather than by any enforced designation. The 7.5% Section 301 overcapacity tariff remains prepared ahead of the September 24 Xi-Trump summit; the truce expires November 10; the 12.5% forced-labour tariff has been in force since July 24. Taiwan: the 15% tariff rate stands and PLA sortie rates remain at the pre-Lai baseline. || AUGUST 27: Lin Jian said China 'will take all necessary measures to firmly safeguard its own rights,' restating that trade with Iran should not be disrupted and complies with international law; Ghalibaf thanked Beijing for declining to comply. Kpler data published August 26 puts Chinese imports of Iranian crude via Hormuz at ~530,000 bpd, 48% below pre-war and 72% below the October 2024 peak, with the Red Sea route at ~1.02m bpd against an April peak of 2.62m; Shandong teapots are substituting Brazilian and Iraqi barrels. The 7.5% Section 301 overcapacity tariff remains prepared ahead of the September 24 Xi-Trump summit, with the truce expiring November 10. PLA sortie rates around Taiwan have reverted to the pre-Lai baseline. || AUGUST 25: Operation Economic Outcast's first designations name entities and vessels in China and Hong Kong and the administration says China is not exempt from secondary sanctions; Beijing restates that 'sanctions and pressure tactics are not the solution,' has told its firms to ignore US restrictions and has invoked its 2021 blocking statute. The same day, the US was reported preparing a 7.5% Section 301 overcapacity tariff ahead of a September 24 Xi-Trump summit in Washington — exactly the headroom to the 20% cap Washington committed to on July 27, with the trade truce expiring November 10. Chinese imports of Iranian crude remain ~534,000 bpd against ~1.4m bpd in 2025, a collapse produced by the blockade before any designation issued. || AUGUST 23: The economic phase of the Iran war has one load-bearing assumption — third-country compliance — and the largest third country refused in public before the package was published. Beijing restated on August 21-22 that it opposes unilateral sanctions lacking a UN mandate and does not recognise the US measures; Lin Jian said sanctions and pressure will not resolve the issue. Washington has named China as the target of the third-party mechanism, with Bessent telling countries they are either with the US or against it and warning of 'economic oblivion.' China bought more than 80% of Iran's shipped oil in 2025 and has a standing blocking rule instructing its firms not to comply. Chinese purchases nonetheless fell from 823,000 to 534,000 barrels a day between July and August — produced by blockade and insurance, not by designation, which is a different causal claim from the one Washington is about to test. Section 301 forced-labour tariffs took effect July 24 with China at 12.5%; Taiwan is not subject to them, and no new Taiwan military incident was reported. || AUGUST 22: BEIJING REFUSES THE ARCHITECTURE WHILE ITS PURCHASES FALL BY A THIRD. The economic phase the administration announced on August 20-21 depends on secondary designations against third countries, and China rejected the pressure outright. The commercial facts moved anyway: Chinese imports of Iranian crude fell from 823,000 barrels a day in July to 534,000 in August, offers for September and October cargoes declined, independent refiners moved to reduce exposure, and roughly 40 million barrels sit stranded in Malaysian waters — the blockade achieving through interdiction and counterparty risk what sanctions alone had not. On Taiwan the cycle's activity was Taiwanese and defensive: the Han Kuang exercise on August 5-14, and a coast-guard warning to a Chinese research vessel on August 21. Xi's White House visit is still expected around September 24. The capability fact that bears on any Pacific contingency remains American — more than 60% of Patriot and THAAD stocks expended, about a third of the Tomahawk inventory gone, and a $22.9 billion seven-year contract required to rebuild it. || JULY 27 (Day 152): The Section 301 forced-labour tariffs that replaced the expiring Section 122 global tariff on July 24 are now the operative regime — 10-12.5% across ~60 economies including Taiwan, with China still most heavily taxed. Negotiation continues in parallel: agriculture is 'agreed in principle' for inclusion in a reciprocal reduction framework. Chinese July crude imports rebound toward ~7.8 mb/d from a decade low. Taiwan's exposure this cycle is tariff policy, not force. || JULY 26-27: Section 122 lapsed on its 150-day clock July 24 and Section 301 forced-labour duties of 10-12.5% took its place the same day across ~60 economies including Taiwan — the level held, the legal authority changed. China's combined duties remain ~35% above MFN, well over 100% on certain strategic goods. Taiwan and the EU alone received a 10% rate not added to existing MFN duties. The Iran war crossed into the file: Waltz said Trump warned Xi and Putin against continued assistance to Tehran, while China backs Pakistani mediation. || JULY 25: The July 24 Section 301 tariff wave (10-12.5% on ~60 economies, >99% of US trade; fuel, food and USMCA-covered goods exempt) settled into an asymmetric pattern. Taiwan and the EU alone received 10% not added to existing MFN duties — the most favourable treatment in the package — potentially improving Taiwanese traditional manufacturers' position against Japanese and Chinese rivals. China's 12.5% stacks on existing Section 301/232 duties for a combined burden around 35% above MFN and well over 100% on some strategic products, yet Beijing signalled it would look past the levies before Xi's planned US visit while teams accelerate a reciprocal-cut framework valued near $30B a side. No new military clash in the Taiwan Strait. || JULY 23: The Section 122 10% flat tariff (which replaced the struck-down IEEPA tariffs on Feb 24, 2026) reaches its hard 150-day statutory sunset on July 24, 2026; a proposed Section 301 replacement (10-12.5% across the forced-labor-investigation countries) is under discussion, and the broader US-China tariff truce runs to November 2026. Taiwan (down to 10% from 32% under Section 122) stays flagged by Beijing as the most sensitive issue. No new military clash reported. || JULY 11 (Roundup): US-China held a fragile trade truce — Beijing's Commerce Ministry said the sides 'agreed in principle' to include agricultural products in a reciprocal tariff-reduction framework — while Taiwan-adjacent signaling continued: China Coast Guard patrols east of Taiwan (July 4), the China-Russia 'Joint Sea-2026' exercise off Qingdao (July 6-13), and a July 6 PLA SLBM test in the South Pacific. The base Section 122 10% tariff expires ~July 24. No US-China military escalation. (AEI China-Taiwan Updates, July 2-10; China Briefing, 2026.) || [JUNE 15-16 UPDATE] Mid-June: relations in a 'stabilization' phase after the Trump-Xi trade truce, but Taiwan friction persists — in early June Taiwan's coast guard expelled four Chinese government ships that entered restricted waters off the island's southern tip, with both sides trading warnings. Xi has signaled willingness to make 2026 a 'landmark year' for US-China relations while warning Taiwan is the 'most important' issue. No military escalation in the Iran-war window. (CNBC; SCMP; ChinaPower/CSIS, May-June 2026.) [JUNE 1-2] The one-year trade truce reached at the May 14-15 Beijing Trump-Xi summit (which paused China's extreme rare-earth export-licensing regime) continued to hold, expiring around November 2026 with no clear signs yet of an extension; Taiwan remained the central flashpoint after Xi's warning that mishandling it could put 'the entire relationship in great jeopardy.' (CFR; CNBC, May 2026). [MAY 30-31] At the Shangri-La Dialogue in Singapore, Secretary of War Hegseth said the US seeks a 'stable equilibrium' with China in the Indo-Pacific, praised Philippines/Australia/Indonesia/Malaysia/Singapore for 'burden-sharing,' urged Vietnam and India to improve readiness, and warned Beijing not to disrupt the status quo; Chinese DM Dong Jun skipped the forum a second year. It was the first US Indo-Pacific policy signal since the May 14 Trump-Xi Beijing summit, where the two sides agreed to reset ties and manage disputes. MAY 15-16: Trump-Xi Beijing summit CONCLUDED with divergent readouts. Trump claimed China would buy '200 Boeing jets' + 'fantastic trade deals'; Beijing's official statement contained ZERO mention of specific commercial agreements (China never confirmed Boeing), only vague references to opening doors to US business. Xi emphasised Taiwan as the 'most important issue in China-US relations,' warning of 'clashes and even conflicts' if mishandled; White House readouts OMITTED Taiwan; Trump declined Taiwan questions. Both claimed progress 'stabilising ties' but 'differences remain.' || PRIOR: TAIWAN HAN KUANG 42 EXERCISES BEGIN APR 11: 14-day unscripted tabletop war games incorporating lessons from Iran + Venezuela wars. Tests PLA port/airport seizure scenarios + decentralized command. Live exercises in August. Record $11.1B Taiwan arms deal — shadow over Beijing visit. Trump-Xi summit confirmed May 14-15 in Beijing. US-China goods trade deficit declined 30% in 2025. China + Russia VETOED UN Security Council Hormuz resolution (Apr 7). US bipartisan senators visited Taiwan urging $40B supplementary military budget. KMT Chairman Eric Chu visited China Apr 7 for 'peace mission.' China's 2026 defense budget up 7% to ~$278B.",
