   "name": "Brent Crude ($/bbl)",
   "value": "AUGUST 27 — THE PRINTS DIVERGED WITHIN A SINGLE SESSION AND THE DIVERGENCE IS LOGGED RATHER THAN RESOLVED. TradingEconomics carried BRENT AT $89.48, UP $1.86 OR 2.12% on the day, which would break the three-session slide; the same service's own market commentary described Brent 'SLIPPING TOWARD $87 ON THURSDAY, EXTENDING ITS DECLINE TO A FOURTH STRAIGHT SESSION' on signs of diplomatic progress, and carried Brent just below $89 on August 26 after two consecutive declines. Fox News Digital carried crude 'NEAR $82 THURSDAY, MAINTAINING LOSSES FOR THE THIRD CONSECUTIVE DAY,' and a separate TradingEconomics WTI blurb had crude falling toward $81 on a fourth straight session. THE RANGE OF RECORD FOR AUGUST 27 IS THEREFORE BRENT $87-$89.48 AND WTI $81-$82. WHAT EVERY PRINT AGREES ON IS THE ATTRIBUTED CAUSE, AND IT IS DE-ESCALATORY IN EVERY CASE: the Iran-Oman revenue-sharing report, Trump's claim that ten million barrels moved through the Strait on Tuesday, and a sanctions package that spared Iran's trading partners and was judged less aggressive than markets had feared. THE ATTACK DID NOT PRICE. A tanker was struck by an unknown projectile in the Strait of Hormuz on the morning of August 27 under UKMTO Warning 121-26 — the second vessel hit this week — and no print attributes any part of the day's move to it. There has been no strike inside Iran since August 1, so the price continues to track sanctions and diplomacy rather than supply events. Levels remain roughly 20% above the pre-crisis baseline near $72, far below the ~$126-130 April wartime peak, and far below what a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (TradingEconomics Brent crude and WTI pages and news blurbs, August 26-27, 2026; Fox News Digital live updates, August 27, 2026.) || AUGUST 25-26 — BRENT FELL FOR A THIRD CONSECUTIVE SESSION, THROUGH THE SANCTIONS PACKAGE, THE DEMINING ANNOUNCEMENT AND THE OMAN CORRIDOR. On AUGUST 25 Brent declined more than 3% to about $89 a barrel, its lowest since AUGUST 19, with WTI near $82, the softest since AUGUST 17; one print carried Brent at $89.44, -2.67% over 24 hours. On AUGUST 26 Brent fell about 3% again to roughly $86 — Investing.com carried $85.52 against a previous close of $85.75 on a $85.16-$86.10 range — with WTI off about 2.8% to about $80 (one print -$1.37/-1.7% to $80.99). THE ATTRIBUTED CAUSES ARE ALL DE-ESCALATORY: traders judged the August 24 sanctions less disruptive than a war because Treasury gave partners time to comply without naming target countries or a timeline; the State Department was reported redeploying diplomats to the Middle East, read as evidence against a full-scale resumption; and the Iran-Oman framework for a temporary shipping corridor plus joint mine-clearing was priced as supply relief. OLE HANSEN of Saxo Bank said the move from military confrontation to economic sanctions 'had helped calm nerves across energy markets'; BBH strategists called the package 'MORE OF A WARNING SHOT THAN A DECISIVE BLOW'; TIM WATERER of KCM cautioned that 'prices had not fully deflated' because Iran retains the ability to disrupt shipping. THE STRAITS.LIVE BOARD DIVERGES FROM THE FUTURES PRINTS AND THE DIVERGENCE IS LOGGED RATHER THAN RESOLVED: it carried $87.39 at its 26 AUGUST 2300Z timestamp, +0.38% over 24 hours and about 21.4% above the ~$72 pre-crisis baseline. There has been no strike inside Iran since August 1, so the price continues to track sanctions and diplomacy rather than supply events. Still far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130-plus a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (Reuters via Yahoo Finance, 'Oil prices fall to one-week low as Iran sanctions disappoint,' August 25, 2026; CNBC, 'Oil hits one-week low as investors shrug off latest U.S. sanctions on Iran,' August 25, 2026; CNBC, 'U.S. crude hovers around $80 as oil prices sink on Hormuz deal hopes,' August 26, 2026; RTÉ, 'Oil falls again as investors shrug off sanctions on Iran,' August 25, 2026; Investing.com Brent futures, August 26, 2026; straits.live, 26 August 2026 2300Z.) || AUGUST 24 — BRENT FELL ON THE DAY THE 'SINGLE GREATEST FINANCIAL OFFENSIVE EVER MARSHALLED' WAS ANNOUNCED, AND THE PUBLISHED FIGURES DIVERGE. Reuters carried Brent off 1.4% to about $93 a barrel and WTI off 1.6% to about $85.70; CNBC carried Brent off 2.5% to $92.06 and WTI off 2.5% to $84.89; the straits.live board carried $92.10 at its 24 August timestamp, -1.67% over 24 hours and about 27.9% above the ~$72 pre-crisis baseline. The move followed a weekly gain of more than 5% and is attributed across outlets to PROFIT-TAKING after a two-week rally rather than to a judgement on the sanctions themselves. The audit records the direction plainly: Operation Economic Outcast was unveiled with five sectoral determinations and nearly 60 designations, and the price of the commodity it targets went DOWN on the day. There has been no strike inside Iran since August 1, so the price continues to track sanctions architecture rather than supply events. Still far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130-plus a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (CNBC, 'Oil price today: WTI, Brent, U.S. sanctions, Iran,' August 24, 2026; Reuters via HDFC Sky, 'Oil Prices Dip on Profit Booking as US Sanctions Loom,' August 24, 2026; EnergyNow, 'Oil Prices Pull Back as Markets Await New U.S. Sanctions on Iran,' August 2026; straits.live status board, 24 August 2026.) || AUGUST 23 (WEEKEND — FUTURES MARKETS CLOSED) — BRENT HELD AT $94.39 WITH NO 24-HOUR MOVEMENT. The straits.live board carried $94.39 at its 23 August 2026 2300Z timestamp, +0.00% ($0.00) over 24 hours against a pre-crisis baseline of about $72, a premium of roughly 31.1%. That is the Friday August 21 settle carried unchanged across a closed weekend, after a second consecutive weekly gain of roughly 6% and the highest level since late July. THE PRICE IS NOW PARKED ON A SCHEDULED EVENT RATHER THAN A SUPPLY EVENT: there has been no strike inside Iran since August 1, and the market is waiting on Bessent's 2 p.m. ET Monday news conference and the secondary-sanctions architecture it is expected to describe. Still far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130-plus a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (straits.live, 23 August 2026 2300Z; CNBC, 'Oil prices are little changed after Iran's president indicates Tehran wants war to end soon,' August 21, 2026; TradingEconomics Brent crude series.) || AUGUST 22 — BRENT FUTURES CLOSED THE WEEK AT $93.93 AGAINST A PREVIOUS CLOSE OF $93.78, ON A DAY RANGE OF $92.76-$94.82, COMPLETING A SECOND CONSECUTIVE WEEKLY GAIN OF ROUGHLY 6% AND HOLDING THE HIGHEST LEVEL SINCE LATE JULY. The straits.live board carried $94.39 at its 22 August 2256Z timestamp with markets closed, +0.71% over 24 hours at the prior close; TradingEconomics recorded $93.87 on August 21, +0.10% on the day. THE MOVE REMAINS SANCTIONS-DRIVEN RATHER THAN SUPPLY-EVENT-DRIVEN — there has been no strike inside Iran since August 1, and the market is pricing the removal of Iranian barrels through blockade and the designations due on Monday August 24 rather than through destruction. Still far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130-plus a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (Eastern Herald, 'Oil Prices Today, August 22, 2026: Brent Near $94 and Frozen Until Monday'; TradingEconomics Brent crude series; straits.live, 22 August 2026 2256Z.) || AUGUST 21 — BRENT TRADED BETWEEN ROUGHLY $93.40 AND $95.29 AND POSTED A SECOND CONSECUTIVE WEEKLY GAIN OF MORE THAN 5%, ITS HIGHEST LEVEL SINCE LATE JULY. Prices rose more than 2% after Trump threatened 'economic warfare' against Iran and Treasury Secretary Bessent promised 'the toughest sanctions in history,' with global crude touching nearly $94 a barrel. Fortune recorded $95.29 at 8am ET on August 21 (−$0.11, −0.11% on the day, +6.92% on the month against $89.12, +40.54% year-on-year against $67.80); other prints showed Brent dipping 0.3% to $93.40 intraday and settling near $94.39; the straits.live board carried $94.03 at its August 21 timestamp, +0.64% over 24 hours. On August 18 straits.live had carried $91.38 (+0.29%). THE MOVE IS BEING DRIVEN BY THE SANCTIONS ANNOUNCEMENT AND THE COLLAPSE OF THE 60-DAY NEGOTIATING WINDOW, NOT BY A NEW SUPPLY EVENT — there has been no strike on Iranian territory since August 1, and the market is pricing the removal of Iranian barrels through blockade and secondary designations rather than through destruction. Still far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130-plus a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (TradingEconomics; Fortune, 'Current price of oil as of August 21, 2026'; CNBC, 'Oil prices head for second weekly rise as U.S. vows to turn up economic pressure on Iran,' August 21, 2026; HDFC Sky; straits.live, August 18 and 21, 2026.) || AUGUST 1 (WEEKEND — MARKETS CLOSED; JULY 31 SETTLE RESTATED) — BRENT SETTLED $87.93, UP $1.05 (+1.21%), CLOSING JULY UP 22.86% AND 26.21% YEAR-ON-YEAR. TradingEconomics carried $87.93 as the July 31 settle and the straits.live board carried the same $87.93 at 22:59 UTC on August 1, +2.38% over 24 hours against a roughly $72 pre-crisis baseline (+22.1%). THIS REVISES DOWN THE $90.24 INTRADAY PRINT LOGGED IN THE PREVIOUS CYCLE by about $2.30; the direction and the monthly magnitude are unchanged. Futures markets were closed for the weekend, so no August 1 or August 2 settle exists. The level held even as the reported multi-day campaign against Iranian power plants and refineries failed to begin and as Hormuz transits fell 77% in a day — the market is pricing bounded coercion, not a supply rupture, and remains far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (TradingEconomics; straits.live, July 31 – August 1, 2026.) || JULY 31 — BRENT ROSE 3.87% TO $90.24 AND CLOSED JULY UP ROUGHLY 24%, ITS STRONGEST MONTH SINCE MARCH. TradingEconomics recorded $90.24/bbl, up $3.36 (+3.87%) on the day, with analysts projecting $101.25 by quarter-end; the straits.live board showed $90.24 with a 24-hour change of +2.36% at 20:52 UTC against a pre-crisis baseline of about $72 (+25.3%). The move prices in the reported multi-day campaign against Iranian POWER PLANTS AND REFINERIES — that is, the announced destruction of Iranian refining capacity is being taken as a bounded, priced event rather than a supply rupture. Still far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (TradingEconomics; straits.live, July 31, 2026.) || JULY 30 — BRENT EASED BACK BELOW $90 WHILE HOLDING MOST OF THE PRIOR SESSION'S SURGE. The straits.live tracker showed $89.45, down 1.11% (−$1.00) over 24 hours at 22:59Z; TradingEconomics recorded Brent falling below $91 and then below $90 intraday after surging 7.9% on July 29. The two-day pattern prices the resumption of US strikes on Iranian territory as a bounded event rather than a supply rupture: the market took the \"heavy wave\" and the IRGC's same-day retaliation vow without returning to the $100.69 July 23 settle, and remains far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (straits.live; TradingEconomics; Bloomberg, July 30, 2026.) || JULY 29 — BRENT REVERSED AND CROSSED BACK ABOVE $90. The straits.live tracker showed $90.40, up 3.43% (+$3.00) over 24 hours at 23:03Z, with a 7-day range of $101.47 high to $82.51 low; CNBC headlined 'Brent oil jumps back above $90 after Trump threatens to hit Iran hard,' having reported a July 28 close of $84.09, down 4.8%. The move ends the three-session slide that had taken roughly $14 off the July 23 settle of $100.69, and it is priced off three distinct escalations in one cycle: Iran's claimed ballistic strike on Muwaffaq Salti Air Base, the IRGC's strikes on three tankers in the Strait, and the joint US-Saudi air strikes in Iraq. Still far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (straits.live; CNBC, July 29, 2026.) || JULY 28 — Brent fell a THIRD consecutive session but the slide flattened sharply. TradingEconomics recorded $86.58, down 1.54% on the day; the straits.live tracker showed $87.66, −0.11% over 24 hours, at 22:59Z. That is a move of roughly $1-2 against the $9-13 falls of the two prior sessions, and a cumulative decline of about $14 from the July 23 settle of $100.69. The IRGC's intercepted ballistic-missile launch at US forces came at 5:45 p.m. ET, after the day's pricing, so the day's quotes do not reflect it. Still far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (TradingEconomics; straits.live; Fortune, July 28, 2026.) || JULY 27 — Brent extended the pause-driven collapse for a SECOND session. TradingEconomics recorded $90.28, down 8.23% on the day, with WTI at $82.62, down 8.68%. UPI/Yahoo Finance reported Brent closing down 8.7% at $88.36 and US crude off 7.5% at $82.61; CBS cited Brent -6.8% at $85.49 intraday; CNBC headlined 'Brent crude below $90'; the straits.live tracker showed $87.76, -4.87%, at 23:03 UTC. Sources differ on the settle but agree on direction and magnitude: a two-session fall of roughly $10-15 from the July 23 settle of $100.69, reversing a week in which prices had gained more than 9% and Brent touched $102. Still far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (TradingEconomics; UPI/Yahoo Finance; CBS News; CNBC; straits.live, July 27, 2026.) || JULY 26 — Brent PLUNGED on the second day of the strike pause, falling more than 5% to about $91.20/bbl (Fox News Digital); TradingEconomics showed $91.45, down 7.05% on the day, still up about 23.7% on the month and 31.9% year-on-year. That is a fall of roughly $9 from the July 23 settle of $100.69 and the sharpest single-day decline of the resumed phase. Still far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (Fox News Digital; TradingEconomics, July 26, 2026.) || JULY 25 — Brent ended the week in the $96-98 range; reports differ on the precise Friday July 24 settle (~$97 per CNBC/Yahoo Finance; $98.38, -2.29%, per TradingEconomics) after the July 23 spike that closed at $100.69 (+7%). Brent was up more than 12% on the week and roughly 30% on the month. Weekend quotes are indicative only (futures markets closed): one outlet reported an early-Saturday move back above $100 on the Houthi strikes at Jizan and Yanbu, another a retreat to ~$96 on reports Pakistan is exploring renewed US-Iran talks with Chinese encouragement. Still below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (CNBC; TradingEconomics; TechTimes; Fox News, July 24-25, 2026.) || JULY 25 — Brent RETREATED ~4% on Friday July 24 to settle near $97/bbl — its biggest one-day drop since late June — after briefly topping $100 and closing at $100.69 (+7%) on July 23, as traders judged crude was still traversing Middle East routes despite the Houthi Red Sea attack on two Saudi tankers, with Kazakhstan output cuts adding supply. Still below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (Yahoo Finance; CNBC, July 24, 2026.) || JULY 24 — Brent TOPPED $100/bbl for the first time since May before settling; Thursday July 23 futures gained ~4.6% to $98.44 and US WTI rose ~3.8% to $90.14, putting Brent on track for a ~35.3% monthly gain (a top-three monthly jump in a decade) as the US ran a 12th straight night, Trump refused a ceasefire and weighed a 'massive attack,' and the Houthis ATTACKED Saudi vessels in the Red Sea (a live second chokepoint atop the shut Hormuz). Still below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (CNBC; CNN Business, July 23, 2026.) || JULY 23 — Brent SURGED again: up almost 5% to more than $95/bbl on July 22 — its highest in nearly six weeks — as the US downplayed diplomacy (Rubio: Tehran 'not serious'), ran an 11th straight night of strikes, and the Houthi Bab el-Mandeb blockade threatened Saudi Arabia's Red Sea export route, leaving oil facing 'no way out' with both Hormuz and the Red Sea compromised. Still below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (NBC News; CNN Business; TradingEconomics, July 22, 2026.) || JULY 22 — Brent held near a one-month high, settling ~$91/bbl on July 21 (TradingEconomics cited $91.10, +2.11% on the day; another print ~$89.93), extending gains as the US ran a 10th straight night, Trump threatened Iran's Pickaxe Mountain nuclear site, and the Houthis declared a Bab el-Mandeb blockade that turned back Saudi tankers — a second chokepoint threat atop the functionally shut Hormuz. Still below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply; a live 10-day ceasefire proposal capped the upside. (TradingEconomics; Fortune; CNBC, July 21, 2026.) || JULY 21 — Oil pushed HIGHER: Brent crossed ~$90/bbl on Monday July 20 (its highest in over a month, ~+30% from early-July lows), extending gains as the US ran a ninth-to-tenth straight night of strikes (including Bushehr) and Iran immobilized a second tanker at Hormuz. Still below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply; analysts flag a $100+ retest if intensity persists. (TradingEconomics; CNBC; Al Jazeera, July 20, 2026.) || JULY 20 — Oil held ELEVATED at multi-week highs as the war crossed the threshold of the first US combat deaths of the resumed phase and the US ran an eighth 'punishment' night that struck the Darkhovin nuclear plant and widened into Khuzestan. The last reliable mainstream print remained Brent ~$88/bbl (July 17 close, +~14% on the week); no fresh corroborated settle materially above that was available for July 18-20 (markets were into the weekend). Prices are up more than 14% on the week on the Hormuz risk premium but still far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply; analysts continued to flag a $100+ retest if the intensity persists. (CNBC; Al Jazeera; TradingEconomics, July 14-17, 2026.) || JULY 19 — Oil stayed ELEVATED at multi-week highs as the air war deepened into an eighth night and widened into central Iran, with Hormuz functionally shut. The last reliable mainstream print was Brent ~$88/bbl on the July 17 close (+~14% on the week); no fresh, corroborated settle materially above that was available for July 18-19. A far higher ~$113/bbl figure circulated via a single unverified aggregator (houseofsaud.com) and could NOT be corroborated by mainstream market reporting (CNBC/Bloomberg/Al Jazeera) — NOT adopted here. Still far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (CNBC; Al Jazeera; TradingEconomics, July 15-18, 2026.) || JULY 18 — Oil SURGED again: Brent advanced ~4.6% to close near $88.10/bbl and WTI ~4.5% to ~$82.49 on July 17, with Brent up more than 10% over roughly a week to its highest in over a month, after the US Treasury's 60-day Iranian-oil sanctions waiver expired at 12:01am EDT July 17 and the US ran a seventh night of strikes. Still elevated but far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (Al Jazeera; NBC News; CNBC, July 17, 2026.)  || JULY 17 — Oil HELD its elevated one-month-high range without a fresh spike as the market judged the sixth day of strikes to be staying on the air/naval rung. Brent settled ~$84.95/bbl (+22c) July 15 and closed below $85; WTI traded above $80 after adding more than 11% over three prior sessions. Analysts said Hormuz traffic was 'grinding to a halt,' back to or below the pre-MOU pace, keeping prices elevated. Still >15% above the late-February pre-war level, but far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (Bloomberg, 'Latest Oil Market News for July 16'; CNBC; Al Jazeera, July 15-16, 2026.) || JULY 16 — Brent HELD its one-month high but did NOT spike further as the market judged the escalation to be staying on the air/naval rung. Brent's September contract settled ~$84.95/bbl (+22 cents, ~+0.3%) and WTI's August contract ~$79.60 (+26 cents) on July 15 — 'little changed'/'steadying' after the prior day's ~10% weekly surge and the intraday move above $86. Still >15% above the late-February pre-war level; one analyst said the escalation 'shows how expectations of a rapid opening of the Strait were premature' and warned of a retest of $100 if the intensity persists for weeks — but far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (CNBC, 'Oil little changed after U.S. reimposes naval blockade on Iran, launches more airstrikes,' July 15, 2026; Bloomberg; Fortune, July 15, 2026.) || JULY 15 — Brent SURGED to a one-month high as the US re-imposed its Hormuz blockade and ran a fourth night of strikes. Brent's September contract climbed above $85/bbl — reported at ~$85.92 as of 08:00 GMT July 14, its highest since June 15 — after rising ~3.8% on July 14 and ~9.6% the prior session, and traded above $86 intraday; the week's gain topped 10%; up ~19% from the late-February pre-conflict level. The move followed Trump's July 14 announcement that the US would reimpose a naval blockade of Iranian ports and seek a 20% 'toll'/reimbursement on Hormuz cargo (a fee he reversed the same day), plus a fourth straight night of US strikes and three more Iranian tanker attacks. Still war-risk repricing far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US ground commitment would imply; analysts continued to warn of $100+ if the closure persists. (Al Jazeera, 'Oil hits 1-month high as US-Iran fighting clouds Strait of Hormuz outlook,' July 14, 2026; TradingEconomics; CNBC, July 13-14, 2026.) || JULY 14 — Brent's September contract jumped more than 4% to about $78.82/bbl as of 08:00 GMT on July 13 — its highest since June 22 — as the US and Iran kept trading strikes over control of the Strait into a third day of Iran's closure; WTI rose in step. A firmer, higher print than Day 137's ~$76 last-confirmed level, but still war-risk repricing far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US ground commitment would imply; analysts warned of $100+ if the closure persists. (Al Jazeera; Bloomberg; American Bazaar, July 12-13, 2026.) || JULY 13 — Brent held near $75.5-76/bbl at the last confirmed print (July 10 close; ~4.7-5% weekly gain), keeping a Hormuz risk premium as Iran's closure ran into a second day; no clean July 12-13 settle was available with the closure in flux, and analysts warned of $100+ if it persists. Still far below the ~$126 April peak and the $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. || JULY 11-12 — Brent held above $76/bbl and was on track for a weekly gain of nearly 5%, keeping a Hormuz risk premium after Iran's July 12 IRGC re-closure while continued US-Iran technical talks capped the upside; still far below the ~$126 April peak and the $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. (TradingEconomics/CNBC; Al Jazeera, July 10-11, 2026.) || JULY 10-11 — Brent held near $77/bbl (eased from the July 8 settle of $78.02, +5.2%), as the July 7-9 breach-retaliation spike gave way to a US 'strike-then-pause for diplomacy' posture, Iran's reported search for a deal, and Pakistan/Qatar mediation; Trump's 'total control of their Oil and Gas Markets' seizure threat did not move prices to a war-restart level. War-risk repricing, still far below the ~$126 April peak and the $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. (TradingEconomics; CNBC, July 9-10, 2026.) || JULY 9 — Brent EASED back toward $77/bbl (from the July 8 settle of $78.02, +5.2%; WTI $73.52), snapping a two-day rally even as the US ran a second-day ~90-target strike wave and Iran retaliated against four Gulf/Levant states. War-risk repricing, still far below the early-2026 wartime peaks above $110 and the $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. (Al Jazeera, 'Oil surges as US strikes Iran'; TradingEconomics, Brent crude, July 9, 2026.) || JULY 8 — Brent JUMPED 5.2% to settle at $78.02/bbl (WTI up in step) after US CENTCOM launched 'additional strikes' on Iran and Trump declared the ceasefire 'over' and threatened to bomb/blockade Iran again; prices eased off session highs after Trump's same-day walk-back ('I don't think it's going to start again'). War-risk repricing, still far below the early-2026 wartime peaks above $110 and the $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. (CNBC, 'Oil jumps as U.S. strikes on Iran,' July 8, 2026.) || JULY 7 — Brent SPIKED off its four-month low after Iran attacked three tankers near Hormuz and the US launched retaliatory strikes plus revoked Iran's oil-sales waiver: Brent rose ~5.5% to about $76/bbl (September) and settled ~$74.16 (+3%); WTI advanced ~5% to about $72 (August). War-risk repricing, still far below the early-2026 wartime peaks above $110 and the $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. (CNBC; CBS News, July 7, 2026.) || JULY 6-7 — Brent held near its pre-war ~$72/bbl after OPEC+ agreed July 5 to raise August output by 188,000 bpd; Saudi crude exports ran ~90% of the pre-war baseline, UAE exports topped 3.9M bpd, and total Hormuz flows stayed above 10M bpd. De-escalation pricing, far below the early-2026 wartime peaks above $110 and the $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. (TradingEconomics/CNBC; CBS News, July 3-6, 2026.) || JULY 5-6 — OPEC+ agreed July 5 to raise August output by 188,000 bpd; US oil markets reopened July 6 after the Independence Day weekend with Brent near its pre-war ~$72/bbl (last print Fri July 3 ~$71.30-72.49), as Saudi crude exports held ~90% of the pre-war baseline, UAE exports topped 3.9M bpd, and total Hormuz flows ran above 10M bpd. De-escalation pricing, far below the early-2026 wartime peaks above $110 and the $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. (CBS News, OPEC+ update; TradingEconomics/CNBC, July 3-5, 2026.) || JULY 4-5 — Oil markets CLOSED for the US Independence Day weekend; no fresh settlement printed. The last trade (Fri July 3) held Brent near $72/bbl (intraday ~$71.30-72.49), close to late-February pre-war levels, as Saudi crude exports rebounded to ~90% of the pre-war baseline alongside a UAE recovery and the gradual Hormuz reopening. De-escalation pricing, far below the early-2026 wartime peaks above $110 and the $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. (HDFCSky, 'Oil Price Today, July 3, 2026'; TradingEconomics, Brent crude, July 3, 2026.) || JULY 2-3 — Brent slid to a FOUR-MONTH LOW as Middle East tensions eased and Hormuz shipping gradually reopened. Brent fell to about $71.54/bbl on July 3 (-0.36%), extending a decline that ran through July 2; Trump's remark that Iran had 'come a long way' after the Doha round contributed to the drop, and he said prices were 'charging down.' Far below the early-2026 wartime peaks above $110 and the $130+ a US ground commitment would imply — de-escalation pricing. (TradingEconomics/CNBC; Gulf News, July 2-3, 2026.) || JUNE 29-30 — Brent eased back toward a four-month low as the breach-retaliation cycle broke. Brent rose only slightly to about $69.70/bbl on June 29 after the US and Iran agreed to halt mutual attacks ahead of the June 30 Doha talks, rebounding marginally off the ~$72/bbl four-month low set June 26 (lowest since Feb 27); the impact of the weekend clashes stayed 'relatively contained' (analyst Ipek Ozkardeskaya) given strategic-reserve releases and resumed tanker movements. Trump said oil prices were 'charging down' after the Hormuz reopening. Far below the early-2026 wartime peaks above $110 and the $130+ a US ground commitment would imply — de-escalation pricing. (TradingEconomics/CNBC, Brent crude, June 29, 2026.) || JUNE 28-29 — The broadened breach-retaliation cycle reintroduced risk premium but did not undo the de-escalation repricing. Brent had closed the prior week near a four-month low (~$72/bbl on June 26, lowest since Feb 27, after an over-10% weekly drop), driven by supply-side de-escalation (Gulf exports ~75% of pre-war, Saudi loading resumed at Ras Tanura, Hormuz reopening). The June 28 escalation — a 10-target US strike near Hormuz and Iran's missile/drone attack on US bases in Kuwait and Bahrain — added fresh geopolitical risk, but no confirmed June 28-29 settle was available in reporting as the weekend exchange unfolded; prices remained far below the early-2026 wartime peaks above $110 and the $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. (CNBC; TradingEconomics, Brent crude, June 26-28, 2026; Al Jazeera Economy, June 28, 2026.) || JUNE 27-28 — Brent stayed in DE-ESCALATION pricing through a second straight day of the breach-retaliation cycle. Brent had fallen below $73/bbl on June 26 — its lowest since February 27 — and posted an over-10% weekly drop, the largest in a month; the June 27 Iranian drone strike on the M/T Kiku tanker and the second, larger US airstrike produced only a brief, partial rebound. Structural drivers were supply-side de-escalation: Gulf exports recovered to ~75% of pre-war levels (~4.8M bpd through Hormuz, highest since Feb 28), Saudi Arabia resumed loading at its Ras Tanura terminal, and the UAE, Kuwait and Qatar boosted output. Trump confirmed the Strait remains open. Far below the early-2026 wartime peaks above $110 and the sustained $130+ a US ground commitment would imply — the inverse of ground-commitment pricing. (CNBC, 'U.S. military attacks Iranian targets after commercial tanker hit in the Strait of Hormuz,' June 27, 2026; TradingEconomics, Brent crude, June 26-27, 2026; Al Jazeera Economy, June 27, 2026.) || JUNE 26-27 — Brent EASED despite the first US strikes on Iran since the MOU, falling more than 2% on June 26 (after spiking as much as 4%) to around $74/bbl (TradingEconomics: $74.43, -1.11%; Al Jazeera cited August Brent ~$73.85), as rising Hormuz shipping activity outweighed the US-Iran kinetic exchange (Iran's June 25 drone attack on the M/V Ever Lovely + the June 26 US retaliatory airstrikes). Brent is down ~19% over the month and only ~2% above pre-conflict levels; Treasury Secretary Bessent said crude had fallen below pre-conflict levels and gasoline would ease. Far below the early-2026 wartime peaks above $110 and the sustained $130+ a US ground commitment would imply — an air-register flare-up, not war-scare repricing. (Al Jazeera Economy, 'Oil prices ease after spiking over halt to Strait of Hormuz evacuation plan,' June 26, 2026; TradingEconomics, Brent crude, June 26, 2026.) || JUNE 25-26 — Brent REVERSED higher, climbing 2.1% to $75.26/bbl on June 25 after Iran fired on a cargo vessel near Oman, partially retracing the prior day's war-period low (WTI had briefly dipped below $70 on June 24). The strike refueled Middle-East risk fears. Still far below the early-2026 wartime peaks above $110 and well under the sustained $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. (CNBC, 'Oil reverses losses, rises after an Iran attack on cargo vessel,' June 25, 2026.) || JUNE 24-25 — Brent fell below ~$74/bbl on June 24, its lowest since late February, as rising Hormuz tanker traffic and progress in the US-Iran talks eased supply fears. Far below the early-2026 wartime peaks above $110; de-escalation/deal pricing with a thin residual risk premium, NOT the sustained $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. (TradingEconomics; CNBC, June 24, 2026.) || JUNE 23-24 — Brent fell to ~$75/bbl on June 23 (down ~0.3% on the day and ~4.5% over five days), a war-period low, after dropping nearly 4% to $77.39 on June 22 when VP Vance reported progress in the Iran talks and confirmed the Strait of Hormuz remained open. Far below the early-2026 wartime peaks above $110; de-escalation/deal pricing with a thin residual risk premium, NOT the sustained $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. (Reuters via CNBC; Al Jazeera Economy, June 22-23, 2026.) || JUNE 22-23 — Brent (August) slipped ~0.38% to ~$80.26/bbl while US WTI (July) traded ~1% higher at ~$77.52 after Qatar and Pakistan announced a 'roadmap toward a final deal within 60 days' at the Bürgenstock talks, which outweighed the lingering June 20 Hormuz-closure noise (a communications channel to keep the strait open was agreed). Far below the early-2026 wartime peaks above $110; de-escalation/deal pricing with a residual risk premium, NOT the sustained $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. (CNBC, 'Brent crude slips as Qatar, Pakistan announce 60-day roadmap for U.S.-Iran deal,' June 22, 2026.) || JUNE 21-22 — Brent traded around $80/bbl (last firm close $80.57 on June 19) and WTI around $79 (up ~3% on June 21) as markets weighed Iran's disputed June 20 Hormuz closure against the reconvening Swiss talks and the still-lifted US blockade. Far below the early-2026 wartime peaks above $110; de-escalation/deal pricing with a residual risk premium, NOT the sustained $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. (CBS News live blog; CNBC; Al Jazeera, June 19-21, 2026.) || JUNE 20-21 — Brent traded around $80/bbl and WTI around $77 after RISING on June 19, as Lebanon fighting erupted and Hormuz traffic stayed slow; Iran's June 20 re-closure of the Strait of Hormuz added fresh upward pressure to the war-risk premium even as the underlying trajectory since the June 17 signing remained one of de-escalation pricing. Still far below the early-2026 wartime peaks above $110; deal/de-escalation pricing with a renewed risk premium, NOT the sustained $130+ that ground-commitment pricing would require. (Al Jazeera Economy, 'Oil prices rise as Lebanon fighting erupts and Hormuz traffic still slow'; oilprice.com, June 19-20, 2026.) || JUNE 19-20 — Brent STEADIED near $80/bbl on Friday, edging up from ~$77 on the renewed uncertainty after the in-person US-Iran follow-on talks in Switzerland were postponed (Vance trip canceled), but stayed on track for a SHARP WEEKLY DECLINE as improving Hormuz shipping conditions kept unwinding the war-risk premium. Far below the early-2026 wartime peaks above $110; de-escalation / deal pricing, NOT ground-commitment pricing. (TradingEconomics, Brent crude; CNBC, June 19, 2026.) || JUNE 18 — Oil held a THREE-MONTH-PLUS LOW. Brent traded around $77/bbl on June 18, below $80, after the US and Iran signed the ceasefire MOU (electronically, June 17) and CENTCOM lifted the naval blockade — continuing to unwind the war-risk premium as Hormuz began reopening. De-escalation / deal pricing, NOT ground-commitment pricing. (TradingEconomics; market reporting, June 18, 2026.) || JUNE 17 — Oil fell a FOURTH straight session. Brent traded around $79/bbl on Wednesday — a three-month low — pressured by expectations of increased supply once the interim deal takes effect Friday: immediate resumption of Iranian exports, resumption of third-country Hormuz transits, higher OPEC+ export quotas, and increased UAE output (UAE exited the cartel during the conflict). De-escalation / deal pricing, NOT ground-commitment pricing. (IG International; TradingEconomics, June 16-17, 2026.) || JUNE 16-17 — Oil extended its slide a FOURTH straight session on deal optimism. Brent fell more than 3% to near $80/bbl on Tuesday June 16 (the lowest since early March), after Monday's 4.7% drop to ~$83.17; WTI fell in tandem in the longest losing streak of the year. Trump at the G7: 'the oil is plummeting down and the stock market is shooting up like a rocket today.' De-escalation / deal pricing, not ground-commitment pricing. (CNBC; PBS NewsHour; TradingEconomics; OilPrice.com, June 16, 2026.) || JUNE 15-16 — Oil EXTENDED its plunge to a THREE-MONTH LOW on the deal. At Monday June 15's close US WTI fell 4.8% to about $80.75/bbl and Brent fell 4.7% to about $83.17/bbl — both their lowest since the first week of March (just after the Feb 28 war start). Global equities soared (Nikkei +5.5%, Kospi +5.7%, Taiex +2.7%). NBC: whether prices fall much further 'is highly questionable'; analysts cautioned Hormuz won't normalize quickly (mine-clearing 'months' to 'over a year'). DE-ESCALATION / deal pricing, NOT ground-commitment pricing. (NBC News; Al Jazeera; OilPrice.com; TradingEconomics, June 15, 2026.) || JUNE 14-15 — Oil PLUNGED on the deal. As Sunday-evening trading opened shortly after the June 14 'deal reached' announcement, US WTI fell more than 4.5% to about $80/bbl (lowest since the first week of March) and Brent tumbled roughly 4% to about $83 (also its lowest since early March), later trading under ~$87. Trump authorized the toll-free reopening of Hormuz and the immediate removal of the naval blockade ('Let the oil flow!'); the move unwound the war-risk premium against the EIA June outlook (which had assumed a still-closed strait keeping Brent ~$105). DE-ESCALATION / deal pricing, NOT ground-commitment pricing. (AOL/Yahoo Finance; CBS News; Bloomberg; EIA June STEO, June 14-15, 2026.) || JUNE 13-14 — Brent HELD its ~2-month low in the HIGH-$80s (US WTI ~$88) on signing-day optimism, after Trump posted that the deal was 'scheduled to get signed tomorrow' with Hormuz to open 'OPEN TO ALL' immediately after; the US energy secretary said Strait of Hormuz ship traffic was increasing. Structural floor (Hormuz still formally closed + dual blockade) intact pending an actual signing/reopening; DE-ESCALATION pricing, NOT ground-commitment pricing. (CNBC, 'Oil prices fall after U.S. Energy secretary says Hormuz ship traffic is increasing,' June 9-13, 2026; Bloomberg, June 13, 2026.) || JUNE 12-13 — Brent EXTENDED its slide toward ~$88.40-$88.65/bbl in early trading — its LOWEST LEVEL IN NEARLY TWO MONTHS — after settling near $90.38 (down ~2.9%) on June 11, as Pakistan's report of a 'final, agreed upon text' and a weekend-signing-in-Europe timeline outweighed an overnight Iranian drone attack on Indian Hormuz shipping. The US national pump average eased to ~$4.11/gal. Structural floor (Hormuz still closed + dual blockade) intact; DE-ESCALATION pricing, NOT ground-commitment pricing. (TradingEconomics, Brent crude, June 12, 2026; CNBC, June 11-12, 2026.) || JUNE 11-12 — Brent SANK ~4.2% to ~$89.15/bbl — its first move below $90 this cycle — after earlier settling near $90.38 (down ~3%), as Trump's cancellation of further strikes and announcement of a negotiated 'settlement' outweighed the day's kinetic exchange and Iran's Hormuz 'closed to all ships' declaration. Rystad Energy: the market is better positioned to absorb disruption than in past crises (record US crude exports, softer Chinese demand, alternative routes). Structural floor (Hormuz closure + dual blockade) intact; DE-ESCALATION pricing, NOT ground-commitment pricing. (CNBC, 'Trump calls off Iran strikes,' June 11, 2026.) || JUNE 10-11 — Brent held the low-$90s: it edged up about 13 cents (~0.14%) to ~$94.38/bbl as the Iran-Israel halt held into a third day and investors 'awaited clarity,' after climbing as much as 5% the prior session on renewed Israeli strikes before paring gains once Iran ended operations; analysts noted markets are 'not convinced the truce will hold.' OPEC+ kept its July quota increase of 188,000 bpd. Still above the pre-war $60-70 band but far below the sustained $130+ that ground-commitment pricing would require; structural floor (Hormuz closure + US blockade) intact; RISK/ESCALATION pricing, NOT ground-commitment pricing. (CNBC, 'Oil rises slightly as investors await clarity after Iran-Israel halt attacks,' June 9, 2026; TradingEconomics, Brent crude, June 9-10, 2026.) || JUNE 9-10 — Brent FELL BELOW ~$93/bbl on Tuesday June 9, surrendering most of the prior session's gains, as Iran and Israel held their mutual halt and US-Iran deal optimism grew (Trump: agreement in its 'final throes,' possibly 2-3 days, Hormuz to open 'on signing'); a continued round trip from the ~$98 intraday spike on June 8. OPEC+ kept its July quota increase of 188,000 bpd. Still above the pre-war $60-70 band but far below the sustained $130+ that ground-commitment pricing would require; structural floor (Hormuz closure + US blockade) intact; RISK/ESCALATION pricing now FADING toward de-escalation, NOT ground-commitment pricing. (TradingEconomics, Brent crude, June 9, 2026; CNBC, June 9, 2026.) || JUNE 8-9 — Brent crossed ~$98/bbl intraday Monday June 8 on the direct Israel-Iran exchange, then EASED back to ~$94 after Iran announced it had ended operations against Israel; WTI jumped more than 4% above $94 on June 8. Prices faded on ceasefire optimism and US-Tehran progress signals; OPEC+ approved a July quota increase of 188,000 bpd. Still well above the pre-war $60-70 band but far below the sustained $130+ that ground-commitment pricing would require; structural floor (Hormuz closure + US blockade) intact; RISK/ESCALATION pricing, NOT ground-commitment pricing — the brief spike round-tripped within a day as the exchange halted. (TradingEconomics, Brent crude, June 8-9, 2026; CNBC, June 8, 2026.) || JUNE 7-8 — Brent held near ~$94-97/bbl: futures fell below $94 on Friday June 5 (extending a ~2.8% drop) and Trump cited ~$97 on June 7; the weekend Israel-Iran exchange added only a modest premium, offset by demand worries after Chinese crude imports fell to a ~10-year low. Still well above the pre-war $60-70 band but far below the sustained $130+ that ground-commitment pricing would require; structural floor (Hormuz closure + US blockade) intact; RISK/ESCALATION pricing, NOT ground-commitment pricing — notably, oil did NOT spike even as Iran fired on Israel for the first time since April. (TradingEconomics, Brent crude, June 5-8, 2026; CBS News live updates, June 7-8, 2026.) || JUNE 6 — Brent EASED to ~$94.81/bbl (down from ~$95.25 on June 5) even as the day's second kinetic flare-up widened the war to Bahrain; reduced Chinese crude imports (softer demand) weighed on prices. Remains above the pre-war $60-70 band and far below the sustained $130+ that ground-commitment pricing would require; structural floor (Hormuz closure + US blockade) intact; RISK/ESCALATION pricing, NOT ground-commitment pricing — the price EASED despite the Kuwait/Bahrain strikes. (Fortune, 'Current price of oil as of June 6, 2026'; TradingEconomics, June 6, 2026.) || JUNE 5 — Brent held the mid-$90s: ~$95.25/bbl on June 5 (+0.23% on the day), with an intraday print near $97.44 at the NY open; well above the pre-war $60-70 and far below the sustained $130+ that ground-commitment pricing would require; structural floor (Hormuz closure + US blockade) intact; RISK/ESCALATION pricing, NOT ground-commitment pricing, with Rezaei's 'deadlock'/'wider war' rhetoric a modest premium. (Fortune, 'Current price of oil as of June 5, 2026.') || JUNE 4 — Brent EASED to ~$97.95/bbl by mid-morning (down ~$3.41 from the prior morning) as the June 3 Gulf flare-up cooled, then ticked back above $97 (third straight session of gains) after Trump said Iran had agreed not to pursue a nuclear weapon and floated a Khamenei meeting; still ~$32.50 above year-earlier levels and well below the sustained $130+ that ground-commitment pricing would require; structural floor (Hormuz closure + US blockade) intact; RISK/ESCALATION pricing, NOT ground-commitment pricing. (Fortune, 'Current price of oil as of June 4, 2026.') || JUNE 3-4 — Brent ROSE on the Gulf flare-up: prices climbed more than 3% as the US and Iran traded strikes and Iran's June 3 missile/drone barrage damaged Kuwait's airport; Brent traded in the high-$90s, with Fortune citing an intraday print near $101/bbl on June 3 (and TradingEconomics near $96.9, +0.93%, on June 3). Remains above the pre-war $60-70 and well below the sustained $130+ that ground-commitment pricing would require; structural floor (Hormuz closure + US blockade) intact; ESCALATION-RISK pricing, NOT ground-commitment pricing. (Fortune; TradingEconomics; The National, June 3, 2026.) || JUNE 2 (TUE) — Brent traded near $95/bbl after jumping about 5% on June 1 (intraday spike near 7%) on Iran's June 1 suspension of US talks, then easing as Trump signaled the deal was 'back on track' and reachable 'over the next week.' Remains above the pre-war $60-70 and well below the sustained $130+ that ground-commitment pricing would require; structural floor (Hormuz closure + US blockade) intact; suspension-risk pricing, NOT ground-commitment pricing. (CNBC; Reuters/TradingEconomics, June 1-2, 2026). || JUNE 2 (TUE) — Brent held a $90-100 band after capping its worst month since 2020 (ended May ~$92.56/bbl, -17-19% on the month); analysts expect the band to persist 'for at least the next couple of months' pending clarity on a lasting deal. Trump's request for MOU edits (rather than a signature) and Iran's $12B frozen-assets precondition keep a residual risk premium in the price. Remains above the pre-war $60-70 and well below the sustained $130+ that ground-commitment pricing would require; structural floor (Hormuz closure + US blockade) intact; NOT pricing ground commitment. (CNBC; Reuters/TradingEconomics, May 29-June 1, 2026). || JUNE 1 (MON) — Oil markets reopen after the weekend with Brent having ended May near $92.56/bbl, capping a ~17-19% monthly decline — its WORST MONTH SINCE 2020. Analysts expect prices to hold a $90-100 band 'for at least the next couple of months' pending clarity on a lasting deal; Trump's request for MOU edits (rather than a signature) keeps a residual risk premium in the price. Prices remain above the pre-war $60-70 and well below the sustained $130+ that ground-commitment pricing would require; structural floor (Hormuz closure + US blockade) intact; NOT pricing ground commitment. (Reuters/TradingEconomics; CNBC, May 29-June 1, 2026). || MAY 30-31 (WEEKEND) — Oil markets CLOSED for the weekend; no new settlement. Brent ended the trading week near $92/bbl (Fri May 29 close ~$92.05, down ~1.2-1.8%), capping a ~19% decline over May — its WORST MONTH SINCE MARCH 2020 (COVID demand collapse) — and is off ~20% from the 2026 peak on US-Iran deal optimism. The heads of four major global economic organizations warned oil reserves are being depleted 'at a record pace' amid the near-total Hormuz closure. Prices remain above the pre-war $60-70 (a residual risk premium) and well below the sustained $130+ that ground-commitment pricing would require; structural floor (Hormuz closure + US blockade) intact; NOT pricing ground commitment. (CNBC; RFE/RL; Fox News, May 29-30, 2026). || MAY 29 (FRI) CLOSE — Oil FELL AGAIN on deal optimism + month-end positioning: Brent settled ~$92.05/bbl (-1.77%) and WTI ~$87.36/bbl (-1.73%). Brent fell about 19% over May — its WORST MONTH SINCE MARCH 2020 (COVID demand collapse) — and is down roughly 20% from the 2026 peak (CNBC: 'Oil drops 20% from 2026 peak on optimism over U.S.-Iran ceasefire talks'; 'Brent oil price posts biggest monthly loss in six years'). The slide tracked Trump's promised 'final determination' Situation Room meeting (which ended without a decision) and the still-unsigned 60-day MOU's negotiated Hormuz reopening. Prices remain above the pre-war $60-70 (a residual risk premium) and well below the sustained $130+ that ground-commitment pricing would require; structural floor (Hormuz closure + US blockade) intact; NOT pricing ground commitment. || PRIOR MAY 28 (THU) — Oil PARTLY REBOUNDED on the tentative-deal news after the prior day's >5% crash: Brent crude was at ~$96.29/bbl (+2.1%) and WTI futures returned above $90/bbl (+~2.4%). CNBC ('Oil markets are betting on a swift end to the Iran war. Investors may regret it') noted low-to-mid-$90s prices still carry a 'clear risk premium' that is unlikely to unwind to the pre-war $60-70 level. The move tracks the double-pending 60-day MOU (Axios: negotiators agree, Trump 'a couple of days,' Iran unconfirmed). Two-state deal-close framing intact ($90s-low $100s; ceasefire-collapse $120+); structural floor intact (Hormuz closure + US blockade); NOT pricing ground commitment (would require sustained $130+). || PRIOR MAY 27 (WED) CLOSE — Brent crude lost more than 5% to settle at ~$94.29/bbl, the biggest single-day drop since the early-deal optimism windows of April. WTI fell more than 5% to close at ~$88.68/bbl. The drop was triggered by Secretary of State Rubio's statement that the U.S. would give the Iran talks 'every chance to succeed' and President Trump's Cabinet-meeting framing of Iran as 'negotiating on fumes' (with the explicit 'I don't care about the midterms' disclaiming of any election off-ramp). The move is squarely two-state deal-close pricing ($90s-low $100s; ceasefire-collapse $120+); both prints remain well below the sustained $130+ that ground-commitment pricing would require. Structural floor intact (Hormuz closure + US blockade); NOT pricing ground commitment. PRIOR MAY 26-27 — Brent crude futures gained more than 3% to close ~$99.58/bbl Tuesday May 26 on Iran's vow to retaliate for US 'self-defense' strikes (2 IRGC mine-laying boats + Bandar Abbas SAM site); WTI fell ~3% to settle ~$93.89/bbl (mixed: Hormuz risk premium up, demand softness + deal-close framing down). Both prints sit well below the sustained $130+ that ground-commitment pricing would require. Two-state asset framing intact (deal-close $90s-low $100s; ceasefire-collapse $120+); structural floor intact (Hormuz closure + US blockade); NOT pricing ground commitment. PRIOR MAY 25-26 — Oil markets CLOSED Monday May 25 for the US Memorial Day + UK spring bank holiday; no fresh confirmed print is yet available in reporting as markets reopen Tue May 26. Last close Brent ~$105/bbl Fri May 22-23 (-~6% on the week, ~50% above pre-war Feb 28 levels; WTI high-$90s); Wikipedia's 2026 fuel-crisis tracker cites North Sea Dated around $110/bbl after swings from a $144 high to below $100. Trump's May 25 'time is on our side' / 'not to rush' posture + the emerging 60-day MOU (negotiated Hormuz reopening +