   "name": "Hormuz Tanker Traffic",
   "value": "AUGUST 27 — THE SIX-MONTH FIGURE WAS PUBLISHED AND IT IS A COLLAPSE OF NEARLY 95%. Al Jazeera's August 27 analysis puts transits at ABOUT FIVE VESSELS A DAY BETWEEN JULY 15 AND AUGUST 23 against a PRE-WAR BASELINE OF ABOUT 100 A DAY. GULF CRUDE EXPORTS ARE DOWN 47%, from roughly 17 million barrels a day to about 9 million; direct crude through the strait averages 2.2 million barrels a day; analysts estimate 5-7 MILLION BARRELS A DAY of supply disrupted; and combined Gulf shipments fell from about 400 million barrels in February to about 200 million in July. The strait carries 33% of global seaborne crude and 29% of LPG flows. PORT CALLS BY COUNTRY SHOW THE DISTRIBUTION: KUWAIT -86%; UAE -69%, from 78 daily calls to 24; QATAR, IRAQ AND BAHRAIN -66 TO -68%; SAUDI ARABIA ONLY -15%, because it has pipeline capacity and a Red Sea coast. RICHARD MATTHEWS of Gibson Shipbrokers stated the structural finding without hedge: 'THERE IS NO ALTERNATIVE MARITIME ROUTE. THERE ARE SOME PIPELINES, BUT THERE'S NO ALTERNATIVE.' THIS SITS AGAINST THE ADMINISTRATION'S OWN FIGURES AND THE GAP IS NOW A STANDING FEATURE OF THE RECORD: Trump said on the Glenn Beck programme on August 26 that TEN MILLION BARRELS passed through the Strait on Tuesday and that the waterway is 'functioning'; Energy Secretary Chris Wright said on August 11 that flows out of the Gulf had reached about 15 million barrels a day with a seven-day Hormuz average near 9 million, against an IEA 2025 baseline of 19.87 million and JMIC counts of 42 US-facilitated transits over August 9-10 versus roughly 138 a day historically. The audit does not adjudicate the two series and carries the dispute in the open-questions register. A tanker was hit by an unknown projectile in the Strait on August 27 under UKMTO Warning 121-26, the second vessel struck this week. (Al Jazeera, August 27, 2026; gCaptain, August 11, 2026; Reason, August 12, 2026; UKMTO Warning 121-26.) || AUGUST 25-27 — THE STRAIT WAS DECLARED DEMINED AND THE TRAFFIC DID NOT MOVE. IMF PortWatch via straits.live recorded THREE TRANSITS FOR AUGUST 23 against an approximately 85-a-day baseline — 4% of normal — and Reuters reported that ONLY TWO VESSELS TRANSITED ON MONDAY AUGUST 24, THE FEWEST SINCE EARLY MAY. Those are the readings that bracket the AUGUST 25 announcement that all mines had been cleared from the international waters; no recovery in transits attributable to the clearance has yet been recorded. THE ROUTE DISPUTE SHARPENED RATHER THAN RESOLVED. The previous cycle logged CNN Business/KPLER data putting MORE THAN 80% OF LIQUIDS TRANSITS over two weeks on the OMANI ROUTE. This cycle Iran's own shipping monitoring agency, via MEHR, said the Indian tanker HAANA planned to transit the SOUTHERN ROUTE — the Oman Corridor — 'but received a warning that it had to stop,' and turned back; the same agency said 'NO VESSEL TRAFFIC HAS BEEN OBSERVED ON THE SOUTHERN ROUTE OF THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ OVER THE PAST 24 HOURS.' Fars reported the IRGC continuing to warn ships by radio to use the Iranian-designated corridor instead. THE TWO DATASETS ARE MEASURING DIFFERENT THINGS — completed transits against permitted transits — AND THE AUDIT RECORDS BOTH WITHOUT ADJUDICATING. THE CORRIDOR THAT MIGHT CHANGE THE NUMBER EXISTS ONLY ON PAPER: a joint Iranian-Omani temporary navigational corridor, seven miles wide, entry beginning in Iranian territorial waters, Iranian permission and surveillance required, military vessels barred, with a permanent arrangement to be negotiated in 30-60 days — and no coordinates, start date, flag eligibility or revenue mechanism published. THE BOARD'S OTHER READINGS AT 26 AUGUST 2300Z: Crisis Pressure 91 (Extreme); Escalation Forecast 52 (Elevated, cooling); war-risk insurance 40.0x pre-crisis on the board and 7.5-10% of hull value in Al Jazeera's market reporting against 1-3% pre-war; 20,170 vessels flagged high-risk in the 24-hour window; market odds of a reopening by August 31 below 1% and by December 31 at 35%. Pre-war baseline was roughly 130 ships a day carrying more than a fifth of world oil and LNG. (straits.live and IMF PortWatch, 23-26 August 2026; Reuters via Yahoo Finance, August 25, 2026; TASS/Mehr, 'Iran detains Indian tanker on southern route in Strait of Hormuz,' August 26, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Why Hormuz remains high risk for ships despite US claims of mine-clearing,' August 26, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Iran, Oman agree on temporary Hormuz route: What we know,' August 26, 2026.) || AUGUST 24-25 — THE FIRST DATASET OF THE WAR THAT POINTS ONE WAY, SET AGAINST BOARDS THAT STILL POINT THE OTHER. CNN Business, working from KPLER ship-tracking data, reported that MORE THAN 80% OF LIQUIDS TRANSITS OVER THE PRECEDING TWO WEEKS TOOK THE OMANI ROUTE — the UN-authorised channel Iran opposes — or were DARK TRANSITS THAT LIKELY TOOK IT, concluding that Iran has LOST SIGNIFICANT CONTROL of the waterway and that the US Navy is gaining ground. Gulf News separately reported SOUTHERN-CORRIDOR TRAFFIC UP ROUGHLY 400% OVER TWO WEEKS to NEARLY 200 SHIPS WEEKLY. Against that, the straits.live board still carries ONE TRANSIT on its latest daily datapoint against a 73-A-DAY pre-crisis baseline (1% of typical volume), 370 HULLS HOLDING POSITION AWAY FROM BERTH, EIGHT OF NINE MAJOR CONTAINER CARRIERS routing via the CAPE OF GOOD HOPE, and market odds of a DECEMBER 31 REOPENING AT 34% with AUGUST 31 UNDER 1%. CENTCOM has now redirected 71 commercial vessels, disabled three and boarded two, and disclosed for the first time that MORE THAN 40 HUMANITARIAN AID VESSELS have been permitted passage. THE ESTIMATES REMAIN IRRECONCILABLE — a route-share statistic and an absolute-volume statistic are not in conflict as arithmetic but are in direct conflict as claims about control — AND THE AUDIT LOGS THE SPREAD RATHER THAN PICKING A NUMBER, while recording that the newest and best-sourced dataset has changed direction. WAR RISK unchanged at ~$10.0M PER VLCC TRANSIT, 40x PRE-CRISIS, with SIX P&I CLUBS WITHDRAWN. (CNN Business, 'Iran has lost significant control of the Strait of Hormuz,' August 18-19, 2026; Gulf News, August 24, 2026; straits.live status board, 24 August 2026; U.S. Central Command via Iran International, August 24, 2026.) || AUGUST 23-24 — THE TRAFFIC ESTIMATES ARE NOW OPENLY IRRECONCILABLE AND THE AUDIT LOGS THE SPREAD RATHER THAN PICKING A NUMBER. CNN reported on August 23 that the number of ships using the Strait stands at 20% OF THE PRE-WAR AVERAGE. The straits.live board carried ONE TRANSIT on its most recent daily datapoint against a 73-A-DAY PRE-CRISIS BASELINE — 1% of typical volume — and describes the waterway as closed to commercial shipping on war-risk insurance grounds. An AMERICAN OFFICIAL quoted in reporting on the 'economic D-Day' announcement said US forces are FACILITATING 15-20 TRANSITS A NIGHT carrying ROUGHLY TEN MILLION BARRELS OF OIL A DAY, about half the pre-war volume, and said: 'The Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps can be a nuisance, but they don't control the strait. We do.' Other reporting puts oil flow at 8-9 MILLION BARRELS A DAY moving largely by DARK TRANSIT, with the US Navy chartering tankers running with transponders off. These figures cannot all be true. Al Jazeera's route-share analysis of AUGUST 1-19 remains the only published attribution dataset: 236 hulls, 83 openly on Iran's route, three openly on the US-backed Omani route, two on the pre-war central route and 148 dark. WAR RISK: ~$10.0M PER VLCC PASSAGE, 40× PRE-CRISIS, WITH SIX P&I CLUBS HAVING WITHDRAWN COVERAGE. (CNN live updates, August 23, 2026; straits.live, 23 August 2026 2300Z; The Times of Israel, August 20-21, 2026; Al Jazeera, August 20, 2026.) || AUGUST 20-22 — THE ROUTE-SHARE DATA IS NOW THE STORY AND IT CUTS AGAINST THE AMERICAN CLAIM OF CONTROL. Al Jazeera's August 20 analysis of AUGUST 1-19 counted 236 VESSELS OF ALL CARGO TYPES crossing the Strait: 83 OPENLY USING IRAN'S PRESCRIBED NORTHERN ROUTE, THREE OPENLY USING THE US-BACKED OMANI ROUTE, TWO USING THE PRE-WAR CENTRAL ROUTE AND 148 (62%) GOING DARK OR UNCLASSIFIED. Of 112 OIL AND GAS VESSELS: 21 IRANIAN ROUTE, TWO OMANI ROUTE, 89 DARK (more than 80%). Al Jazeera's conclusion: 'THE FEAR OF OFFENDING TEHRAN APPEARS TO OUTWEIGH TRUMP'S THREATS,' and Iran 'STILL RETAINS SIGNIFICANT CAPACITY' to influence traffic. Iran International reported on August 22 that NEARLY 200 SHIPS TRANSITED LAST WEEK, UP FROM ABOUT 150 the week before, with over 80% using Omani routes or going dark. UKMTO data cited by CBS put the WEEK ENDING AUGUST 7 AT 21 INBOUND AND 18 OUTBOUND — ROUGHLY 4% OF PRE-CONFLICT LEVELS — and still assesses the risk to mariners as SEVERE. PortWatch's most recent published day, AUGUST 16, LOGGED ONE TRANSIT against a 73-PER-DAY PRE-CRISIS BASELINE. straits.live at 2256Z on August 22 carried DAY 175, 331 HULLS HOLDING POSITION AWAY FROM BERTH on a seven-day range of 201-530, WAR-RISK COVER AT 40× PRE-CRISIS (~$10.0 MILLION TO INSURE ONE VLCC), and MARKET-IMPLIED ODDS OF A REOPENING BY SEPTEMBER 1 BELOW 1%. AND IRAN DEMONSTRATED ALLOCATION RATHER THAN CLOSURE: on AUGUST 22 IRNA said Tehran had GRANTED A NUMBER OF IRAQI OIL TANKERS PERMISSION TO TRANSIT after repeated Baghdad requests — the first named state-level exemption of the war. (Al Jazeera, 'Are ships passing Hormuz more willing to defy Iran or US? What data shows,' August 20, 2026; Iran International live blog, August 22, 2026; CBS News live updates, August 21-22, 2026; straits.live, 22 August 2026 2256Z; Reuters/IRNA via Investing.com, August 22, 2026.) || AUGUST 9-21 — TRAFFIC REMAINS NEAR ZERO AND THE STRAIT IS NOW CONTESTED BY ANNOUNCEMENT RATHER THAN BY BATTLE. PortWatch logged A SINGLE TRANSIT ON AUGUST 9 against a pre-crisis baseline of 73 A DAY — roughly 1.4% of normal. Traffic ran at about 4% of pre-war levels in the week to August 15; FIVE VESSELS CROSSED ON SATURDAY AUGUST 15 AND NONE ON SUNDAY AUGUST 16, against thirty-one the previous weekend; weekly crossings fell from 19 on AUGUST 11 to 3 on AUGUST 16. THIRTEEN VESSELS TRANSITED ON THURSDAY AUGUST 16 (four inbound, nine outbound), the best day of the period, most of them product tankers. straits.live counted 314 HULLS HOLDING POSITION AWAY FROM BERTH ON AUGUST 18 AND 386 ON AUGUST 21, with 542 Hormuz-area port arrivals in the 24 hours to August 21 and the Hormuz Index composite in its EXTREME band. WAR-RISK COVER FOR A TRANSIT IS RUNNING NEAR 10% OF HULL VALUE, with insurers trimming quotas and some withdrawing entirely. Estimation itself is degrading: more vessels transit with trackers off and ship-to-ship transfers disguise volumes. ON AUGUST 6-7 IRAN ANNOUNCED AN OMAN-BROKERED TRANSIT ARRANGEMENT that BARS AMERICAN AND ISRAELI VESSELS OUTRIGHT, FINES VIOLATORS UP TO 20% OF CARGO VALUE, denies passage to countries and individuals deemed to have damaged Iran until compensation is paid, and assigns Iran navigation guidance, traffic monitoring, maritime security and environmental protection, with Iranian officials overseeing inbound and Omani officials outbound traffic; WASHINGTON REJECTED THE PLAN AND ANY 'IMPEDIMENTS.' Iran's parliament speaker stated the strait 'WILL NOT REOPEN UNTIL US SANCTIONS ARE LIFTED AND FROZEN ASSETS RELEASED.' TRUMP SAID ON AUGUST 17 'WE HAVE TOTAL CONTROL OVER THE STRAIT,' called the blockade a 'WALL OF STEEL' and said he liked 'THE IDEA OF DECLARING IT A TERRITORY.' Both claims are consistent with the same traffic data — see open question `hormuz-control-attribution-august-2026`. (straits.live Strait of Hormuz Status briefs, August 13, 16, 18 and 21, 2026; PortWatch; Lloyd's List Intelligence Strait of Hormuz Brief, August 5, 2026; Business Insurance, 'Hormuz war risk rates hold at 10%'; CNN, August 13 and 15, 2026; NPR, August 6-7, 2026; Bloomberg, August 6, 2026; CBS News, August 17-18, 2026.) || AUGUST 1 — TRANSITS COLLAPSED 77% IN A SINGLE DAY, FROM 22 VESSELS TO FIVE. Ship-tracking data reported by Gulf News showed traffic through the strait dropping 77% on Thursday, from 22 vessels the previous day to five. The straits.live board at 22:59 UTC on August 1 held its established framing — latest published commercial transit volume of 10 vessels against an 88/day pre-crisis baseline, or 11% of normal — with 371 vessels holding position away from berth, 36 tankers running AIS-dark over 24 hours, war-risk insurance at 8.0 times pre-crisis rates (approximately $2.5 million per VLCC), eight of the nine largest container lines routing via the Cape of Good Hope, and market-implied odds of the strait reopening by August 15 at 2%. Two tanker incidents were logged by UKMTO in Omani waters on August 1. The collapse occurred with no US strike wave and no new Iranian declaration — the threat alone was sufficient. (Gulf News; straits.live; UKMTO via SCMP and Middle East Eye, August 1, 2026.) || JULY 31 — TRANSITS HIT A TWO-WEEK HIGH AND THE PRIOR CYCLE'S FLAGGED DISCONTINUITY RESOLVED. S&P Global recorded 28 VESSEL TRANSITS ON JULY 29, the highest since July 14, driven by RESUMED LNG TANKER TRAFFIC after a two-week gap plus several dark transits; Iran-linked vessels fell to 60% of movements from a 68% average in the second half of July. Bab el-Mandeb crossings rose to 39, and VLCCs increasingly gathered at Egypt's SIDI KERIR terminal, complicating voyage logistics. The straits.live board REVERTED TO ITS PRIOR FRAMING — latest recorded daily volume of 10 transits (July 23) against a typical pre-crisis 88/day, or 11% OF NORMAL THROUGHPUT — which supports the previous cycle's assessment that the July 30 readings reflected a zone-definition change rather than a real surge. Iran's Fars agency said the IRGC STOPPED TWO TANKERS and FOUR OTHERS ALTERED COURSE. War-risk insurance for supertankers stands at 8.0x peacetime; prediction markets priced a 1% probability of normalised traffic by August 1. (S&P Global via webindia123; straits.live 31 JUL 2026 2259Z; TradingEconomics/Fars, July 31, 2026.) || JULY 30 — THE TRACKER'S READINGS BREAK COMPARABILITY WITH THE PRIOR CYCLE AND ARE FLAGGED. straits.live at 22:59Z reported 35 tankers transiting, 1,357 vessels anchored or stopped in the zone (5-hour range 1,255-1,390) and 9 tankers running AIS-dark over 24 hours. The prior cycle's readings were 10-13 transiting, 339 stranded and 68 dark. All three metrics moved by factors of roughly 3-8x in directions that do not reconcile as a single physical event — transits up sharply, strandings up fourfold, dark tankers down sevenfold — and the tracker published no methodology note. This project records the figures as printed and treats the discontinuity as most likely a change in the counted zone rather than an overnight transformation of the Strait. THE INDEPENDENT BASELINE IS UNCHANGED: IMF PortWatch's most recent count remains 10 commercial transits on July 23 against an 88-per-day pre-crisis baseline — about 11% of normal throughput. War-risk insurance held at 8.0× pre-crisis (~$2.5m for a VLCC); the tracker's Crisis Pressure index read 92 (extreme) and its Escalation Forecast composite 54 (elevated). The IRGC asserted it retains \"full control\" of the Strait, and its July 29 declaration that third-party mine-clearing vessels are \"legitimate targets\" stands. Convoys continue to move only under naval escort. (straits.live, July 30, 2026 22:59Z; IMF PortWatch; CNBC; The Hill, July 30, 2026.) || JULY 29 — A SECOND DAY OF EASING FROM A STILL-CLOSED BASELINE, AGAINST FRESH STRIKES ON TRANSITING SHIPS. The straits.live tracker at 23:03Z recorded 339 vessels anchored or stopped in the Hormuz zone, down from 405, with a 7-day range of 297-540, and 68 tankers running dark over 24 hours, down from 77. IMF PortWatch's most recent count remains 10 commercial transits on July 23 against an 88-per-day pre-crisis baseline — about 11% of normal throughput — and the tracker's closure day count stood at 151. Cutting against the easing: the IRGC said it struck and stopped THREE oil tankers in the Strait, claiming they 'continued to move along an unsafe and illegal route, ignoring our warnings' — the first Iranian strikes on Hormuz shipping claimed since the pause — and Iran declared that any third-party mine-clearing vessel brought near the Strait 'will be considered legitimate targets.' Convoys continue to move only under naval escort. (straits.live; IMF PortWatch; Tasnim News Agency; Democracy Now, July 29, 2026.) || JULY 28 — THE FIRST MEASURABLE EASING OF THE RESUMED PHASE, from a still-closed baseline. The straits.live tracker at 22:59Z recorded 405 vessels stranded (down from 426), 77 tankers running dark (down from 97), 13 tankers transiting and 107 transiting vessels overall. IMF PortWatch's most recent count remains 10 transits on July 23 against an 88-per-day pre-crisis baseline — about 11% of normal throughput. The tracker's Crisis Pressure Index stood at 90 (Extreme), its escalation forecast at 55 (Elevated), VLCC war-risk insurance at 8.0× pre-crisis levels, and the market-implied probability of a normal reopening by August 31 at 11%. Four days of strike pause produced a partial unwinding of the backlog but no reopening; the US naval blockade of Iranian ports continued unpaused throughout. (straits.live, July 28, 2026 22:59Z; IMF PortWatch; NBC News data graphics.) || JULY 27 — The Strait remains effectively closed and the pause has not yet produced a measurable reopening. The straits.live tracker recorded 426 vessels stranded across the zone, 97 tankers running dark with no AIS broadcast, and 14 tankers in transit as of 23:03 UTC, against the 15 AIS-visible transits IMF PortWatch logged on July 19 and an 88-per-day pre-crisis baseline. Trackers caution that with roughly 90-100 tankers dark, true flow may exceed AIS-derived counts. The tanker waiting area off Kharg Island was reported at 24 vessels on July 26, up from 21 the previous day. (straits.live, July 27, 2026; IMF PortWatch; Lloyd's List Intelligence.) || JULY 26 — Traffic effectively STOPPED. Windward recorded ONE vessel — a Comoros-flagged general cargo ship, outbound via the northern corridor — transiting the Strait of Hormuz in the 24 hours to July 25; ZERO vessels transited inbound, ZERO tankers crossed in either direction, and a 12-hour stretch that morning logged no crossings at all. Against the 88-per-day pre-crisis baseline that is under 2%, below the 15 transits IMF PortWatch recorded on July 19. Roughly 85 tankers continue to run dark, and the IMO still assesses the waterway as too dangerous for commercial transit. Iran's foreign ministry said the Strait's status 'remains unchanged' while the Oman talks proceed. (Windward Daily Intelligence; IMF PortWatch; Fortune/AP, July 25-26, 2026.) || JULY 25 — Hormuz remains functionally shut and the picture is now partly unobservable: IMF PortWatch recorded 15 transits on July 19 against an 88-per-day pre-crisis baseline, with roughly 85 tankers running dark (no AIS broadcast) and therefore untracked, and the IMO continuing to assess the waterway as too dangerous for commercial vessels. Late-June/early-July averages had run ~25 ships/day against ~100 a year earlier. An Omani delegation was in Tehran on July 24-25 discussing 'appropriate mechanisms for managing ship traffic in the Strait' — a co-administration formula the US and Gulf states reject. (IMF PortWatch; Statista; Windward; Middle East Eye, July 19-25, 2026.) || JULY 24 — Hormuz remains functionally shut (well under 5% of pre-war flows) and the disruption widened: the Houthis moved from their declared Bab el-Mandeb blockade to an ACTUAL attack on Saudi crude vessels in the Red Sea, threatening the alternate export route and leaving oil, per CNN, with 'no way out.' (CNN; NPR, July 23, 2026.) || JULY 23 — Hormuz stays functionally shut: shipping-analytics firm Kpler said only 3 commodity vessels crossed the Strait on Tuesday, down from 4 on Monday (well below 5% of pre-war flows). Disruption spread to the Red Sea, where two tankers carrying Saudi crude to Asia turned back near Bab el-Mandeb after the Houthi blockade threat. (Middle East Eye/Kpler; CNN Business, July 22, 2026.) || JULY 22 — Hormuz stays functionally shut (well below 5% of pre-war flows) after ~8 transits Thursday, the lowest in three weeks; Iran struck more shipping (two Greek-owned tankers; a tanker abandoned off Oman's Limah coast). A proposed 10-day ceasefire 'middle corridor' between Omani and Iranian waters is the mediators' path to reopening it. || JULY 21 — Only ~8 ships transited the Strait of Hormuz on Thursday, the LOWEST in three weeks, as US-Iran violence ramped up again; Iran said it immobilized a second oil tanker and intercepted four vessels transiting the Strait over the weekend. Traffic remains far below the ~100+/week pre-war norm — functionally shut (<5% of pre-war flows). (CBS News; NPR, July 20, 2026.) || JULY 19 — Hormuz stayed functionally BLOCKED under the re-imposed US blockade and Iranian attacks; transits remained at a small fraction of the pre-war baseline (reported around a handful of vessels per day vs a ~88-125/day pre-crisis norm), leaving the chokepoint effectively shut as the eighth night of strikes widened into central Iran. (Al Jazeera; CNBC, July 17-18, 2026.) || JULY 18 — 'WORST CASE SCENARIO': maritime-risk analysts said Hormuz transits fell below 5% of the pre-war baseline (~2% throughput), leaving the chokepoint functionally blocked under the re-imposed US blockade and Iranian attacks on shipping. (CNBC, 'Oil tankers face worst case scenario in Hormuz,' July 17, 2026.)  || JULY 17 — Traffic stayed sharply depressed and, per analysts, was 'grinding to a halt,' back to or below the pre-MOU pace under the re-imposed blockade: maritime tracking (Kpler) showed only ~21 ships transiting the Strait of Hormuz on July 14 (Tuesday), primarily crude, LPG, methanol and iron-ore carriers. The US widened strikes into northern Iran and, under the blockade, disabled an unladen tanker headed for Kharg Island — maritime interdiction, not a port seizure. (Kpler via CBS News/NPR; Bloomberg, July 15-16, 2026.) || JULY 16 — Traffic stayed sharply depressed under the re-imposed blockade: maritime tracking showed only ~21 ships transiting the Strait of Hormuz on July 15. Within ~17 hours of restarting the blockade the US said it had 'redirected' two commercial vessels attempting to breach it and struck a tanker trying to skirt it — maritime interdiction, not a seizure. (CNN, 'US military says it has redirected 2 commercial vessels since restarting blockade,' July 15, 2026; NPR, July 15, 2026.) || JULY 15 — Crossings dropped by MORE THAN HALF from the prior week as the US re-imposed its naval blockade of Iranian ports (July 14) and ran a fourth night of strikes: only ~57 transits were recorded Friday-Sunday (vs a pre-war ~130 vessels/day). The US DoE said 8.5M barrels passed through the Strait the previous day 'with military assistance,' as Washington cast itself as the Strait's 'guardian.' Iran's July 12 IRGC 'closed until further notice' declaration remained in force and Iran hit three more tankers July 14 (Stolt Magnesium; UAE-owned Mombasa B, Al Bahyah). The chokepoint is now contested by a re-imposed US blockade plus air strikes on one side and Iranian closure/tanker attacks on the other — a maritime/air contest, not a US ground seizure. (Al Jazeera; CBS News; CNBC; NPR, July 13-14, 2026.) || JULY 14 — Third day of Iran's IRGC 'closed until further notice' declaration; vessel-tracking held transit near ~a third of the pre-war baseline amid the continuing open-vs-closed dispute (US CENTCOM: 'open... traffic is flowing'; Iran PGSA: passage 'is currently not possible'). Oman's two-corridor draft stayed under discussion, Tehran's sign-off pending. (Al Jazeera; CBS News; CNN, July 12-13, 2026.) || JULY 13 — Second day of Iran's IRGC 'closed until further notice' declaration; vessel-tracking put transit at only ~33-35% of the pre-war baseline amid a public open-vs-closed dispute (US CENTCOM: 'open... traffic is flowing'; Iran PGSA: passage 'is currently not possible'). Oman's two-corridor proposal remained under discussion. || JULY 12 — Iran's IRGC Navy declared the Strait 'closed until further notice' and fired warning shots at a vessel on an 'unapproved route,' deepening the near-standstill; Oman's proposed two-corridor scheme (Omani-waters Southern Corridor free; Iranian-waters Northern Corridor requiring prior Iranian approval) is not yet finalized. Traffic stays far below the pre-war 125-140 sailings/day. (Times of Israel; Al Jazeera, July 11-12, 2026.) || JULY 9-10 — Traffic near a STANDSTILL: only ~2 tankers transited in the early hours of July 9 after averaging ~40 sailings/day over the prior two weeks (vs a pre-war 125-140 daily average); owners increasingly disabled AIS transponders and marine-war underwriters reviewed cover. Rystad's Jorge Leon: tanker traffic 'has essentially stopped, which tells you more about risk perception right now than any statement from Washington or Tehran.' The Strait stayed physically open (blockade lifted, Day 23). (Al-Monitor, 'Oil tanker traffic through Hormuz at near standstill,' July 9, 2026.) || JULY 9-10 — Traffic stayed cautious but the Strait remained physically open (blockade lifted, Day 22) as the US-Iran exchange widened to a two-sided regional one; Iran warned via PressTV it would close Hormuz and respond with overwhelming force to fresh attacks, but no closure or reimposed US blockade was executed. (Al Jazeera; NBC News, July 9, 2026.) || JULY 8 — Traffic stayed cautious as US 'additional strikes' hit 60+ IRGC small boats used to harass shipping and Trump threatened to blockade Iran again; the Strait stayed physically open (blockade lifted, Day 21) but shipowners re-priced war risk after the July 7 tanker attacks. No US reimposition of the blockade was ordered. (Al Jazeera; CNBC, July 8, 2026.) || JULY 7 — Traffic disrupted anew as Iran attacked three tankers off Oman's coast; at least eight Japan-linked vessels (including five VLCCs) exited Hormuz via a route near Iran, and shipowners turned cautious again. Overall flows had been running above 10M bpd on an uneven recovery; the attacks reintroduced diversion and slowdown risk on the southern/Omani route. Still well below the pre-war ~130 vessels/day. (CNBC; Al Jazeera, July 7, 2026.) || JULY 6-7 — Traffic held an uneven recovery with total Hormuz flows above 10M bpd; ~40 vessels/day returning as conditions stabilize (northern/Iranian and southern/Omani routes open; central channel still clearing mines). Still below the pre-war ~130/day. (CNBC; Travel And Tour World, July 2026.) || JULY 5-6 — Total Strait of Hormuz crude/condensate flows recovered above 10M bpd (Saudi exports ~90% of pre-war, UAE >3.9M bpd), though tanker traffic stayed below the pre-crisis ~84/day baseline and many vessels ran with AIS off. Reopening is proceeding by negotiation; Iran reaffirmed mid-August transit 'service fees' (US rejects) and warned against 'extra-regional' naval movements, while the UK/France backed an Oman-led free-navigation effort. (CNBC/EIA; Wikipedia/PortWatch, July 1-5, 2026.) || JULY 4-5 — Recovery stayed gradual and uneven through the funeral-week pause; no new incidents. PortWatch/monitoring showed 43 transits July 1 (24 inbound, 19 outbound) against a pre-crisis baseline of ~84/day, with many vessels running with AIS off (so real counts likely higher). Iran's deputy FM issued a 'serious warning' to France and the UK over their naval movements in the Strait and reiterated 'service fees' from mid-August, while the UK and France pledged to work with Oman on 'safe transit for ships of all nations.' Still far below pre-war norms; reopening by negotiation, not a US seizure. (Wikipedia '2026 Strait of Hormuz crisis'/PortWatch; CBS News, July 1-4, 2026.) || JULY 2-3 — Traffic kept recovering as the Doha round and the ceasefire held: 258 vessels transited the Strait in the prior week, up from 138 the week before, though still far below the pre-war ~130/day; major container lines resumed Gulf services. Iran's joint military command warned all tankers to use its Tehran-designated routes or face a 'forceful response,' and state media reported a foreign container ship ran aground outside the designated corridor. (CBS News; Gulf News; The Bridge Chronicle, July 2-3, 2026.) || JUNE 29-30 — Traffic stayed thinned by the weekend clashes but recovery resumed. After the June 27-28 strikes, 29 commodity vessels crossed Hormuz on Saturday and 12 on Sunday, down from ~70 the prior Wednesday; following the June 29 US-Iran stand-down major container lines resumed Gulf services and the first commercial flight from Tehran landed in Dubai, with Persian Gulf exports near ~75% of pre-war (~4.8M bpd, still below the ~15M bpd pre-war). Hundreds of vessels remain stranded inside the Gulf and shipowners stay cautious pending demining. (CBS News; Euronews; The National; TradingEconomics, June 29, 2026.) || JUNE 28-29 — Hormuz stayed open through the broadened exchange. Oil flows remained near ~4.8 million bpd since the US-Iran deal (the highest since the war began Feb 28, still below the ~15M bpd pre-war), with Gulf exports near ~75% of pre-war and the US-Navy JMIC's widened transit route near Oman in effect. Iran reasserted that the Strait is under its 'total oversight and management' (Araghchi) but did not re-close it; the US kept it open. (Al Jazeera; RFE/RL, June 28, 2026.) || JUNE 27-28 — Hormuz kept reopening even through the two-day kinetic exchange. Oil flows rose to ~4.8 million bpd since the US-Iran deal — the highest since the war began on Feb 28, though still well below the ~15 million bpd of pre-war transits — with Gulf exports back to roughly 75% of pre-war levels. The US-Navy Joint Maritime Information Center (JMIC) announced a WIDENED route through the Strait near Oman on June 27, allowing increased naval traffic in both directions; Trump confirmed the Strait remains open and traffic continues. (CNBC; 2026 Strait of Hormuz crisis timeline; RFE/RL, June 24-27, 2026.) || JUNE 26-27 — Traffic held a war-period high even through the kinetic exchange: 70 vessels transited the Strait on June 25 (the most in a single day since March 1) versus ~35 the prior day, via the open northern (Iranian) and southern (Omani) routes; the UN IMO's shepherding/evacuation program stayed paused after the June 25 Ever Lovely drone strike, and on June 26 the IRGC turned back three foreign tankers that crossed 'without coordinating with Iranian authorities.' The US naval blockade remains lifted; the central channel stays closed pending ~80 mines. Still under a quarter of the pre-war ~120-130/day. (Al Jazeera Economy; Fox News, June 26, 2026.) || JUNE 25-26 — Traffic continued but the UN International Maritime Organization PAUSED its program to shepherd/evacuate ships through the Strait after Iran struck a cargo vessel near Oman on June 25; the IRGC Navy warned safe passage would be permitted only via Tehran-designated routes. Recovery had reached ~30 crossings/day (>20 tankers / ~35M barrels since the reopening) — still under a quarter of the pre-war ~120-130/day; the central channel stays closed pending ~80 mines. (NBC News; CBC News, June 25, 2026.) || JUNE 24-25 — Traffic held its war-period high: 109 vessels transited Saturday-Monday and ~25 ships crossed the prior Thursday (13 west-to-east, 12 east-to-west), still a fraction of the pre-war ~120-130/day. Open via the northern (Iranian) and southern (Omani) routes; the central channel stays closed pending ~80 mines. Tehran allowing toll-free transit for the 60-day window; Iran's Persian Gulf Strait Authority said planned fees are suspended during that window. (Fox Business; CNBC; Al Jazeera, June 22-24, 2026.) || JUNE 23-24 — Traffic rose to its highest since the conflict began at end-February: 109 vessels transited the Strait of Hormuz from Saturday through Monday, still below the pre-war ~120-130/day, with a backlog of hundreds awaiting passage and the central channel still being de-mined. Iran asserted that transits now require its authorization and signaled possible transit charges via a compulsory-insurance framework, even as the US naval blockade remained lifted. (AOL/Fox Business; CNBC; Times of Israel, June 22-23, 2026.) || JUNE 22-23 — The June 20 closure dispute was diplomatically defused: at the Bürgenstock talks (June 22) Iran and the US agreed to set up communication lines to keep the Strait of Hormuz open, meant to ensure it stays 'fully open.' Traffic remained well below the pre-war ~120-130 transits/day and reopening is still gradual (central channel mined; ~500+ vessels were waiting to exit the Gulf), but the status moved from a hard Iranian closure declaration toward a managed, communications-channel reopening — a negotiated mechanism, not a US ground seizure. (CBS News; CNBC, June 22, 2026.) || JUNE 20-22 — Traffic remained well below the pre-war ~120-130 transits/day. The US (CENTCOM) reported 55 merchant ships carrying >17 million barrels transited the strait on June 20 despite Iran's closure declaration ('traffic continues to flow'), with an estimated 500+ vessels still waiting to exit the Gulf; the closure-vs-flow status is disputed between Iran and the US. (CNN live; CBS News live blog, June 20-21, 2026.) || JUNE 20-21 — IRAN RE-CLOSED THE STRAIT FROM ITS OWN SIDE. After four days of a tentative reopening, Iran's IRGC declared the Strait of Hormuz closed again on June 20 in protest at Israel's continued Lebanon strikes, which Tehran casts as a US failure to enforce the MOU; Iran warned of 'reciprocal action.' The US naval blockade itself remains LIFTED (CENTCOM enforcement 'ceased') — the new closure is an Iranian coercive measure, not a return of US interdiction. Traffic, already a trickle (Lloyd's List had ~550 ships still to exit the Gulf; central route closed pending ~80 mines), is again disrupted; ~80M barrels of crude were reported lined up to exit. Talks to resolve it were due to reconvene Sunday June 21 (Vance to Bürgenstock). Negotiated/coerced reopening contest, not a ground seizure. (Al Jazeera, 'Iran closes Strait of Hormuz as Pakistan says new talks with US due Sunday'; oilprice.com, June 20, 2026.) || JUNE 19-20 — REOPENING CONTINUES, STILL A TRICKLE. CENTCOM said 'American forces are not impeding the transit of vessels to or from Iranian ports' and that 'all U.S. military blockade enforcement efforts have ceased'; sanctioned Iranian tankers entered the strait and Grimaldi Group, Cosco and Knutsen vessels were among those transiting (at least 10 Thursday morning per CBS). But Lloyd's List estimated ~550 merchant ships still need to exit the Gulf, the central channel stays closed pending clearance of ~80 mines, and full normalization is 'weeks' away. Negotiated reopening, not a ground seizure. (CBS News; CNBC; Lloyd's List, June 18-19, 2026.) || JUNE 18 — REOPENING BEGAN. With the MOU signed (June 17) and the US blockade lifted, commercial traffic returned to the Strait of Hormuz for the first time in ~110 days: Windward counted ~18 vessels transiting between 6 p.m. June 17 and 2 p.m. UTC June 18 (a French-flagged LNG tanker, two Hong Kong-flagged tankers, an Italian-flagged vehicle carrier, a Japanese-controlled oil tanker, Saudi-flagged tankers); CBS reported at least 10 transits Thursday morning. Ships are using the northern (Iranian) and southern (Omani) routes while the central channel stays closed pending clearance of ~80 mines; analyst Michelle Wiese Bockmann called it 'a sign of confidence' that will 'start as a trickle,' and CNBC said full normalization will take weeks. VP Vance said '12.5 million barrels of oil passed through... last night' (a war-era high) but warned 'no tolls or no deal,' against Iran's signal it will 'charge fees for services' jointly with Oman. Still far below the ~135 ships/day pre-war norm; negotiated reopening, not a ground seizure. (Windward via CBS/CNBC; Lloyd's List; Al Jazeera, June 17-18, 2026.) || JUNE 17 — Strait still CLOSED in practice despite the ceasefire. ADNOC CEO Sultan Al Jaber said Hormuz is not open because Iran is restricting and conditioning traffic, with ~230 loaded oil tankers waiting inside the Gulf; third-country transit is expected to resume only once the deal takes effect (Friday June 19 signing). Traffic remains far below the ~120-140 ships/day pre-war baseline. (Wikipedia '2026 Strait of Hormuz crisis'; industry reporting, June 2026.) || JUNE 16-17 — Hormuz remains effectively closed; current traffic is ~12-15 ships/day against a pre-war average of 120-140. Reopening begins only after Friday's signing, starting with mine removal by an international naval mission (US, UK, France, Germany) using drone boats and minesweepers; insurers/shippers estimate 40-50 days before transiting in volume, against the MOU's 'within 30 days' removal target. Negotiated-reopening pathway, not a ground seizure. (Jerusalem Post; Globe and Mail; Marine Insight; Claims Journal, June 16, 2026.) || JUNE 15-16 — Hormuz remains effectively closed to commercial traffic pending Friday's signing; under the finalized MOU it reopens for mine removal after the deal is signed, with Iran clearing its own mines. Analysts cautioned normalization will be gradual — war-risk insurer Svein Ringbakken said restoration to normal 'would take months' even at full capacity, and shipping executive S.V. Anchan predicted 'over a year.' Trump had authorized the toll-free reopening; the US energy secretary said Hormuz ship traffic was increasing. (Al Jazeera; NBC News, June 15, 2026.) || JUNE 14-15 — Trump authorized the toll-free reopening of the Strait of Hormuz and the immediate removal of the US naval blockade as part of the June 14 deal; Defense Secretary Hegseth said the reopening would begin 'immediately' but contingent on Iranian performance, targeting pre-war volumes within ~30 days, with Iran responsible for clearing its own mines (potentially into mid-2027). Actual transits remained minimal as of this update and some tankers reportedly paused amid Israel's June 14 Lebanon strikes; the reopening is authorized but not yet realized pending the Friday signing. (PBS NewsHour; CBS News; Axios, June 14, 2026.) || JUNE 13-14 — The Strait of Hormuz remains effectively CLOSED pending a signing, but the trajectory points to reopening: Trump said the deal would be signed Sunday June 14 and that 'immediately after it is signed, the Hormuz Strait is OPEN TO ALL,' and the US energy secretary said Strait ship traffic was increasing (some flow moving with transponders off / Navy coordination). The reported 14-point MOU reopens Hormuz without tolls within 30 days and lifts the US blockade — a negotiated reopening, not a ground seizure — but the document is unsigned and Iran has 'not yet reached a final decision.' Traffic still near-zero (~36/week vs ~130/day pre-war) as of this update. (Bloomberg; CNBC, June 9-13, 2026.) || JUNE 12-13 — The Strait of Hormuz remains effectively CLOSED (IRGC 'closed to all ships,' June 10), with traffic near-zero (~36/week vs ~130/day pre-war) and the conflict still kinetic at sea: overnight June 11-12 Iranian drones fired on Indian commercial shipping leaving the strait and US forces downed two Iranian drones (Trump: 'totally unacceptable'). The reported 14-point MOU would reopen Hormuz without tolls within 30 days and lift the US blockade — but it is unsigned and Iran conditions final talks on a prior $12B frozen-assets release and an end to the blockade; the UK is reported set to host a ~35-country meeting to coordinate reopening. Negotiated-reopening pathway, not a ground seizure. (CNBC; NBC News; House of Commons Library CBP-10636, June 12, 2026.) || JUNE 11-12 — The IRGC declared the Strait of Hormuz 'CLOSED UNTIL FURTHER NOTICE' to ALL ships on June 10, warning all traffic would be targeted — a hardening of the chokepoint beyond the prior dual blockade; two oil tankers reportedly hit. Trump's June 11 settlement announcement would reopen Hormuz (no tolls, Iran clears mines, US lifts blockade) ON SIGNING, but nothing is signed and Iran has not confirmed. Traffic stays near-zero (~36/week vs ~130/day pre-war). || JUNE 10-11 — Strait of Hormuz remains effectively closed under the dual US-Iran blockade; traffic near-zero (~36 transits/week, ~9/day during the war vs ~100-130/day pre-war). With the 60-day MOU still unsigned, Trump reaffirmed the Strait would open 'immediately upon signing,' keeping the negotiated-reopening pathway in place rather than a ground seizure; analysts still estimate 4-6 months to normalize. (House of Commons Library CBP-10636; Al Jazeera, June 9-10, 2026.) || JUNE 9-10 — Strait of Hormuz remains effectively closed under the dual US-Iran blockade; traffic near-zero (~36 transits/week, ~9/day during the war vs ~100-130/day pre-war). Trump said the Strait would open 'immediately upon signing' a US-Iran deal he described as days away, reaffirming a negotiated reopening rather than a ground seizure; analysts still estimate 4-6 months to normalize. (House of Commons Library CBP-10636; Al Jazeera, June 9, 2026.) || JUNE 7-8 — Traffic stayed paralysed at near-zero (~36 transits in the prior seven days) vs ~100-130 cargo vessels/day pre-war; analysts still estimate 4-6 months to normalize once a deal lands. The June 7 Israel-Iran escalation did not change the maritime picture. (TradingEconomics; CBS News, June 7-8, 2026.) || JUNE 6 — Traffic stayed near-zero (~36 transits in the prior seven days vs ~100-130/day pre-war); the June 6 Iranian drone launches toward the Strait (4 downed by CENTCOM) reinforced the closure; analysts still estimate 4-6 months to normalize once a deal lands. (CENTCOM; Fox News, June 6, 2026.) || JUNE 5 — Traffic stayed paralysed: only ~7 vessels transited the Strait of Hormuz on Friday with 4 more departures over the weekend (~36 in the seven days to Friday), versus ~100-130 cargo vessels per day before the war; analysts estimate 4-6 months to normalize once a deal lands. Blockade in force; Strait still effectively closed. (Gulf News; World Oil; NBC News, June 2026.) || JUNE 4 — Traffic remains collapsed: only ~36 ships transited the Strait of Hormuz in the seven days to Friday, versus an average of more than 130 ships per day before the war began (NPR, June 1, 2026). Blockade Day 54; the waterway has been largely closed since Feb 28, 2026. || JUNE 4 — Traffic remains near-zero under the maritime blockade; the MOU is still unsigned and talks suspended since June 1; the IRGC's Hormuz-shipping threat stands (Iran 'preparing to fully close' the Strait) and Iran now threatens to activate a SECOND chokepoint at the Bab al-Mandeb (Quds Force chief Qa'ani, June 2). US-Iran strikes overnight June 2-3 and Iran's June 3 Gulf barrage further suppress any transit. (GlobalSecurity.org; India TV News, June 2-3, 2026.) || JUNE 3 — Traffic remains near-zero under the maritime blockade; the MOU is unsigned and talks were suspended by Iran on June 1; the IRGC's Hormuz-shipping threat stands and Iranian state media says Tehran is preparing to fully close the Strait; Lloyd's List assesses the waterway closed with no Western-allied transits since May 4; Asia LNG spot prices remain up >140%; Japan/South Korea drawing on their joint stockpile; Pakistan/Bangladesh most exposed. (GlobalSecurity.org; Lloyd's List, June 2, 2026). || JUNE 2 — Traffic remains near-zero under the maritime blockade with the MOU still unsigned; Asia LNG spot prices up >140%; Japan and South Korea releasing oil from their joint stockpile; Pakistan/Bangladesh most exposed (Qatar+UAE supply ~99% of Pakistan's LNG). (Zero Carbon Analytics; Gulf International Forum, 2026). || JUNE 1 — Traffic remains near-zero under the maritime blockade; Asia LNG spot prices up >140%; Japan and South Korea releasing oil from their joint stockpile; Pakistan/Bangladesh most exposed (Qatar+UAE supply ~99% of Pakistan's LNG). (Zero Carbon Analytics; Gulf International Forum, 2026). || MAY 30-31 — Near-zero; the Strait of Hormuz remains effectively CLOSED and the US naval blockade of Iranian ports remains in force (Hegseth, Shangri-La: blockade 'very much still in place'). CENTCOM tally rose to ~115-116 commercial vessels redirected + 5 disabled (the fifth, M/V Lian Star, after 20+ warnings) — up from 100+ redirected / 4 disabled (May 23). The 60-day MOU's negotiated reopening (no tolls + Iranian mine-clearing + proportional blockade lift) remains unsigned. Project Freedom remains PAUSED. || MAY 29-30 — Near-zero; the Strait of Hormuz remains effectively CLOSED and the US naval blockade remains in force. No fresh CENTCOM count released (last milestone: 100+ commercial vessels redirected + 4 disabled, May 23). Project Freedom remains PAUSED. The 60-day MOU would reopen the Strait to 'unrestricted' shipping (no tolls) with Iran clearing all mines within 30 days and a PROPORTIONAL lifting of the US blockade as commercial traffic resumes — but Trump's May 29 Situation Room meeting ended without signing, the deal is double-pending (Trump undecided; Iran/Mojtaba Khamenei unconfirmed), and operational conditions are unchanged. Low-grade kinetic skirmishing continued in/near the Strait (US drone shoot-downs + a Bandar Abbas ground-control-station strike; disputed Iran aircraft-downing claim near Bushehr) without reopening traffic. || PRIOR MAY 28-29 — Near-zero; the Strait of Hormuz remains effectively CLOSED and the US naval blockade remains in force. No fresh CENTCOM count released (last milestone: 100+ commercial vessels redirected + 4 disabled, May 23). Project Freedom remains PAUSED. The tentative 60-day MOU (Axios, May 28) would reopen the Strait to 'unrestricted' shipping (no tolls, no harassment) with Iran clearing all mines within 30 days — but the deal is double-pending (Trump 'a couple of days'; Iran unconfirmed) and operational conditions are unchanged. || PRIOR MAY 27-28 — Near-zero; the Strait of Hormuz remains effectively CLOSED and the US naval blockade remains in force. No fresh CENTCOM count released; last milestone stands at 100+ commercial vessels redirected (+4 disabled, 26 humanitarian-aid vessels passed) since Apr 13. Project Freedom remains PAUSED. The emerging 60-day MOU would reopen Hormuz without tolls + Iran clears its deployed mines once signed, but that is not yet in effect; Iran continues to insist the strait will 'remain under Iranian management' until the blockade is lifted. The Trump Cabinet meeting + Rubio 'every chance' framing did NOT change the operational status of the strait. PRIOR MAY 26-27 — Near-zero; the Strait of Hormuz remains effectively CLOSED and the US naval blockade remains in force. The first kinetic US-Iran exchange of the Project Freedom pause (CENTCOM 'self-defense' strikes May 26 on 2 IRGC mine-laying boats in the Strait + Bandar Abbas SAM site) did NOT change the operational closure or trigger reopening; CENTCOM 'continues to defend its forces while using restraint during the ongoing ceasefire.' Iran-deployed mines remain in the strait pending the emerging 60-day MOU's mine-clearing clause. Iranian delegation in Doha (Araghchi + Qalibaf + Hemmati) discussed the disposition of the strait but no operational change to vessel transits occurred. PRIOR MAY 25-26 — Near-zero; the Strait of Hormuz remains closed and the US naval blockade is held 'in full force and effect until an agreement is reached, certified, and signed' (Trump, May 24-25). The emerging 60-day MOU would reopen Hormuz without tolls with Iran clearing its deployed mines once signed, but that is not yet in effect; Iran insists the strait will 'remain under Iranian management.' Major Asian importers exposed: Japan (~1.6M bpd via the strait; 95% of crude from the Middle East), South Korea (imports down ~1 mb/d; energy ministry says no supply problem for over a year), and India (largest combined oil+LNG exposure). PRIOR MAY 24-25 — Near-zero; the Strait of Hormuz remains closed and the US naval blockade is held 'in full force and effect until an agreement is reached, certified, and signed' (Trump, May 24). The emerging 60-day MOU would reopen Hormuz without tolls with Iran clearing its deployed mines once signed, but that is not yet in effect; IRGC-linked outlets insist the strait will 'remain under Iranian supervision.' PRIOR MAY 23-24 — Strait remains effectively CLOSED to commercial traffic on the May 23-24 decision weekend; the converging 60-day ceasefire-extension framework proposes a GRADUAL Hormuz reopening WITHOUT tolls, but Iran Fars insists the strait will 'remain under Iranian management' and no reopening has been implemented. CENTCOM has now redirected MORE THAN 100 commercial vessels since Apr 13 (+4 disabled, 26 humanitarian-aid vessels passed). The IRGC-coordinated PGSA toll/permit regime remains the disputed alternative to the framework's freedom-of-navigation clause. PRIOR MAY 22-23 — Strait remains effectively CLOSED to commercial traffic; CENTCOM running tally still 85 vessels redirected + 4 disabled (May 18 figure; no fresh count released May 22-23). The IRGC-coordinated 'Persian Gulf Strait Authority' (PGSA) toll/permit regime remains operational; Rubio (May 21) called any Iranian tolling system 'completely illegal' and said it would make a deal 'unfeasible.' The leaked Al-Arabiya interim-deal draft (May 22, unconfirmed) would 'guarantee freedom of navigation in the Arabian Gulf and the Strait of Hormuz' — the negotiated alternative to the toll regime — but no operational change to vessel transits occurred May 22-23. More than 1,500 vessels / ~22,500 mariners remain trapped. Commercial transit Day 39 / PROJECT FREEDOM PAUSED. PRIOR MAY 20-21 — Strait remains effectively CLOSED to commercial traffic. CENTCOM running tally still 85 vessels redirected + 4 disabled (May 18 figure; no fresh count released). The IRGC-coordinated 'Persian Gulf Strait Authority' (PGSA) toll/permit regime still operational; no recorded surge in transits since May 4. Putin-Xi joint statement (May 20 Beijing) urged a 'comprehensive ceasefire' and warned 'resuming hostilities is even more inadvisable' — Beijing-Moscow positioning structurally favors reopening but with no operational change to vessel transits May 20-21. Saudi FM Faisal bin Farhan publicly called for Hormuz freedom of navigation to be restored 'to its state prior to February 28th 2026.' Commercial transit Day 38 / PROJECT FREEDOM PAUSED. PRIOR MAY 19-20 — Strait remains effectively CLOSED to commercial traffic; the IRGC-coordinated 'Persian Gulf Strait Authority' (PGSA) toll/permit regime remains operational; no significant change in Hormuz transit since May 4 (no recorded transits) through May 20. Cumulatively ~1-2 vessels/day vs ~97-140/day pre-war baseline. The fresh 'two or three days' deadline + 'another big hit' threat keeps any partial reopening contingent on deal closure, not in motion. Commercial transit Day 37 / PROJECT FREEDOM PAUSED — MAY 18-19: Strait of Hormuz remains effectively CLOSED. CENTCOM running tally: 85 commercial vessels redirected + 4 disabled since the Apr 13 blockade (up from 81 May 17). Source divergence noted: one tracker cited ~55 vessel crossings for the week May 11-17 (recovering from a wartime-low ~19 the prior week but well below peacetime norms), while CENTCOM/Lloyd's-List-style daily figures still show ~1-2 vessels/day (~2% of the pre-war ~97/day baseline) — the gap reflects differing counting methodologies (all transits vs. commercial laden tankers); effective closure persists either way. 'Dual blockade' (US Navy blockading Iran; Iran's IRGC-run Persian Gulf Strait Authority toll/transit regime) continues; China-Iran cargo RAIL corridor still partially bypassing the maritime blockade. Commercial transit Day 36 / PROJECT FREEDOM PAUSED — MAY 17-18: Strait of Hormuz remains effectively CLOSED — ~1.6 total vessels/day (~2% of the pre-war ~97/day baseline); ~0.6 tankers/day (~1% of pre-war ~55/day). 'Dual blockade' persists (US Navy blockading Iran; Iran blockading the Gulf via the IRGC-run Persian Gulf Strait Authority toll/transit regime). IRGC says it prevented vessel passage in the preceding 24 hours (Zolfaghari, May 17). CENTCOM running tally: 81 commercial vessels diverted + 4 disabled since blockade Apr 13 (up from ~75 May 15); USS Abraham Lincoln conducting Arabian Sea flight operations. China-Iran cargo RAIL corridor (~1 every 3-4 days) continues partially bypassing the maritime blockade. PRIOR — Commercial transit Day 35 / PROJECT FREEDOM PAUSED — MAY 16-17: Iran's 'Persian Gulf Strait Authority' (PGSA) now operational — a formal single-window IRGC-coordinated toll/transit regime (own official email; vessels must submit ownership, insurance, crew and cargo details before paying toll). Strait remains effectively CLOSED with no recorded transits since May 4 (per Maritime Executive, The Hill, NPR, Lloyd's List, The Week). Five named states (China, Russia, India, Iraq, Pakistan) nominally permitted; Araghchi says 'non-hostile vessels' can transit 'in co-ordination with Iranian authorities.' CENTCOM ~75 commercial vessels redirected since blockade Apr 13; IMO ~20,000 seafarers stranded on ~2,000 vessels. China-Iran cargo RAIL corridor expanded (~1/wk → 1 every 3-4 days) bypassing the maritime blockade. PRIOR MAY 15-16: CENTCOM ~75 commercial vessels redirected since blockade Apr 13; IMO ~20,000 seafarers stranded on ~2,000 vessels. Effective closure continues. China-Iran cargo RAIL corridor expanded (~1/week → 1 every 3-4 days) bypassing the maritime blockade. Araghchi: Hormuz 'open for commercial vessels as far as we are concerned' (disputed by blockade reality). Commercial transit Day 33 / PROJECT FREEDOM PAUSED — MAY 14: HONDURAS-FLAGGED HUI CHUAN ('FLOATING ARMORY') SEIZED BY IRAN 38nm NE OF FUJAIRAH; being towed toward Iranian territorial waters per UKMTO + BBC + Lloyd's List + Fox News + Ynet + CBS + JPost + UPI + Al Arabiya — vessel had been anchored ~1 month, operators (Vanguard) said it stored rifles/ammunition for maritime-security teams protecting commercial shipping from piracy. MAY 13: INDIAN-FLAGGED MSV HAJI ALI livestock vessel (~4,000 sheep/goats) sank off Oman near Limah at ~03:30 May 13 after explosion + fire; all 14 Indian crew rescued by Oman Coast Guard; cause unconfirmed but suspected drone/missile strike per maritime sources; India MEA condemns 'attack' as 'unacceptable.' CENTCOM MAY 14: 67 commercial vessels REDIRECTED + 4 DISABLED + 15 humanitarian-aid vessels allowed to pass since blockade Apr 13. Hapag-Lloyd CEO Rolf Habben Jansen: $50-60M/week extra container-shipping costs from Hormuz blockage. PRIOR MAY 13: CHINESE SUPERTANKER YUAN HUA HU (Very Large Crude Carrier, 2 million bbl Iraqi crude) sailed through Strait of Hormuz May 13 past Iran's LARAK ISLAND into Gulf of Oman — third Chinese state-owned VLCC transit since war began; vessel had been stranded in Gulf for 2+ months; passed through Iranian-controlled Larak corridor WITHOUT PAYING TOLLS per China COSCO Shipping official to WSJ: Tehran 'gesture' timed to Trump-Xi summit. Tanker now testing US blockade enforcement zone where Gulf of Oman meets Arabian Sea — 24-hour test underway. Iran-China oil leverage gesture from Tehran ahead of Trump-Xi May 14-15 summit. KHARG ISLAND ~20 SQ MI (52 SQ KM) SUSPECTED OIL SLICK on Copernicus Sentinel imagery May 6-8 persists — potentially largest slick of war; Iran denies, blames European tanker waste; ~80,000 bbls estimated. CENTCOM running tally 61 vessels redirected since Apr 13. IMO: ~20,000 seafarers stranded on ~2,000 vessels in Strait. April Hormuz traffic only 191 vessels (vs typical ~3,000/month) = ~5% of pre-war norm. Aramco CEO: market won't normalize until 2027 if Hormuz disruption past mid-June; 100M-bbl/week loss. SOUTH KOREA government concluded May 11-12: HMM Namu hit by TWO 'unidentified aerial objects' striking stern ~1 minute apart May 4 (7m × 5m hole in hull) — 'EXTERNAL STRIKE' confirmed; likely Iranian UAVs per Bloomberg/Ynet; Iran 'firmly and categorically' denies. BLOCKADE REMAINS in full force. Project Freedom paused since May 5-6. Seizure scoreboard 4-2 US-Iran (US holds 4: Touska/Tifani/Majestic X/LPG Sevan; Iran holds 2: MSC Francesca/Epaminondas; Iran also seized Ocean Koi May 8-10 making scoreboard arguably 4-3). DoD: 31 tankers / 53M bbl Iranian oil stuck.",
