   "value": "AUGUST 27-28 — PENTAGON TOTAL HOLDS AT 18 KILLED: 14 in Operation Epic Fury since February 28 and four in overseas operations since July 7. No new US military death was reported in this cycle, across a twenty-seventh consecutive night with no confirmed US or Israeli strike inside Iranian territory. The Intercept's standing dispute is unchanged: it records at least one officially recognised death omitted from the Defense Casualty Analysis System, and reported on August 4 that the Pentagon has adopted a counting method a US government official described as 'designed to obscure the count.' (The Intercept, August 25 and August 4, 2026; Defense Department figures.) || AUGUST 25-27 — PENTAGON TOTAL HOLDS AT 18 KILLED: 14 in Operation Epic Fury since February 28 and four in overseas operations since July 7. No new US military death was reported in this double cycle, across a twenty-fifth and twenty-sixth consecutive night with no confirmed US or Israeli strike inside Iranian territory. The Intercept's AUGUST 25 accounting again records at least one death omitted from the Defense Casualty Analysis System despite official recognition — Major Sorffly Davius of the New York Army National Guard — and the audit carries the omission alongside the official figure without resolving it. (The Intercept, 'Sixty More U.S. Soldiers Reported Injured in Iran War,' August 25, 2026; Stars and Stripes, August 21, 2026.) || AUGUST 24 — PENTAGON TOTAL HOLDS AT 18 KILLED: 14 in Operation Epic Fury since February 28 and four in overseas operations since July 7. No new US military death was reported in this cycle, a twenty-fourth consecutive night without a confirmed US or Israeli strike inside Iranian territory since the August 1 cancellation of the energy campaign. Four of the dead have been named in open sourcing: 1st Lt. Tyler James Feehan, Pvt. Isabella Gonzales, Sgt. Angel S. Rampersad and Sgt. Michael Emmanuel Swinton. Independent compilations put the verified KIA range at 18-20. || AUGUST 23 — PENTAGON TOTAL HOLDS AT 18 KILLED: 14 in Operation Epic Fury since February 28 and four in overseas operations since July 7. No new US military death was reported in this cycle, a twenty-third consecutive night without a confirmed strike inside Iranian territory. (Defense Casualty Analysis System via The Washington Post and Stars and Stripes, August 21-23, 2026.) || AUGUST 22 — PENTAGON TOTAL HOLDS AT 18 KILLED: 14 in Operation Epic Fury since February 28 and four in overseas operations since July 7. No new US military death was reported in this cycle, a twenty-second consecutive night without a confirmed US or Israeli strike inside Iran. (The Washington Post, 'More than 750 U.S. service members wounded in Iran war,' August 21, 2026; Defense Casualty Analysis System reporting via Stars and Stripes and The Mirror US, August 21-22, 2026.) || AUGUST 21 — THE PENTAGON'S PUBLISHED FIGURE HOLDS AT 18 KILLED, split between OPERATION EPIC FURY (14 KILLED SINCE FEBRUARY 28) and OVERSEAS OPERATIONS SINCE JULY 7 (4 KILLED). No new US combat death was reported in this cycle, consistent with the absence of US strikes inside Iran since August 1. The four most recently identified are 1ST LT. TYLER JAMES FEEHAN, 25; PVT. ISABELLA GONZALES, 19; and SGT. ANGEL RAMPERSAD, 28 — Army air defenders killed on JULY 17 at MUWAFFAQ SALTI AIR BASE, JORDAN — and ARMY SGT. MICHAEL EMMANUEL SWINTON, 30, killed on JULY 18 at IRBIL AIR BASE, IRAQ during the controlled detonation of unexploded ordnance from a downed Iranian drone. Independent aggregators continue to publish figures ranging from 15 to 20 on different accounting bases; the divergence is recorded, not reconciled. (Stars and Stripes, 'Pentagon adds 60 wounded to Iran war casualty count,' August 21, 2026; The Washington Post, 'More than 750 U.S. service members wounded in Iran war,' August 21, 2026; Wikipedia, 'List of U.S. service members killed during the 2026 Iran war'; iranwarlive.com; militaryspend.org.) || AUGUST 1 — NO NEW US COMBAT DEATHS REPORTED; the toll holds at 18 killed in action on this project's running tally. Divergence flagged: the Pentagon's published figure after the July reclassification into 'Operation Epic Fury' and a new 'Overseas Operations' category is also 18 killed, while Wikipedia's infobox carries 19 military dead plus one contractor, and militaryspend.org's tracker (last updated July 30) gives a range of 18-20. The differences are accounting-basis artefacts and are recorded rather than reconciled. (Stars and Stripes; CNN Politics; militaryspend.org, July 26-30, 2026.) || JULY 31 — THE OFFICIAL TOLL HOLDS AT 18 KILLED IN ACTION. ABC News's running tally recorded 18 killed in action, unchanged across the cycle, against 685 wounded. The figure corresponds to the 14 acknowledged combat deaths plus the 4 still carried in the unreconciled 'Overseas Operations' category. Iran's claim to have killed US personnel at Al Azraq remains neither confirmed nor denied by CENTCOM. (ABC News live updates, July 31, 2026.) || JULY 30 — NO NEW US COMBAT DEATHS REPORTED. CENTCOM said the July 28 Iranian ballistic attack was fully intercepted with no casualties, and reported no US losses in the July 29-30 strike wave. The IRGC claimed it killed US personnel at Al Azraq Air Base in Jordan; CENTCOM denied that any US aircraft were destroyed or damaged but did NOT address the personnel claim, and The Washington Times reported requesting clarification without receiving it. The official toll holds at 14 killed, with 4 further deaths in the unreconciled 'Overseas Operations' category — three soldiers killed by Iranian missiles and drones at a base in Jordan on July 17 and one killed in Iraq on July 18 during a controlled detonation of an Iranian drone. The reclassification remains unexplained. (CENTCOM; The Washington Times; CNN Politics, July 26 and 30, 2026.) || JULY 28 — NO NEW US COMBAT DEATHS. The IRGC's ballistic-missile launch at US forces at 5:45 p.m. ET was fully intercepted and no casualties were reported. The official Defense Casualty Analysis System Iran-war death toll remains at 14, with four July deaths still held in the separate 'Overseas Operations' category created July 26 and dated from July 7; the Pentagon has neither restored the figures nor published the accounting rule behind the change. The Intercept reported the same day that recent Iranian strikes killed four US service members across multiple bases — including three air-defence personnel at Jordan's Muwaffaq Salti Air Base on July 17-18 — and asserted that the Pentagon drastically understates casualty figures. CONTESTED-UNRESOLVED. (CENTCOM via CBS News; The Intercept; Washington Post, July 26-28, 2026.) || JULY 27 — No new US combat deaths reported on the third pause day. The official Defense Casualty Analysis System Iran-war death toll remains at 14 following the July 26 reclassification that moved four July deaths into a new 'Overseas Operations' category (4 killed, 207 wounded, dated from July 7); the Pentagon has not restored the figures or published the accounting rule behind the change. (Washington Post; CNBC; AP, July 26-27, 2026.) || JULY 26 — THE OFFICIAL TOLL WAS CUT. The Pentagon's Defense Casualty Analysis System reduced the Iran-war death count from 18 to 14 by moving four service members killed in July into a newly created 'Overseas Operations' category dated from July 7: the three killed in the July 17 Iranian missile-and-drone strike on a Jordan base and the one killed July 18 in a drone detonation in Iraq, among them Sgt. Angel S. Rampersad of Ozone Park, New York. Seven of the 14 remaining are classified 'hostile.' Pentagon press secretary Joel Valdez called the changes 'anomalies'; spokesman Sean Parnell called it a 'temporary data disruption.' No new US combat deaths were reported on the strike-pause day. (The Washington Post; CNBC; The Philadelphia Inquirer/AP; KSAT, July 26, 2026.) || JULY 25 — Cumulative US service members killed since the war began Feb 28, 2026 reported at 18 (Fortune's running toll alongside >3,400 Iranian, 24 Israeli civilian, 38 Israeli military and >4,000 Lebanese deaths). No new US combat death was reported on July 25, the first night without US strikes after 13 consecutive nights. Outlets continue to differ on the running count (high-teens). (Fortune, July 25, 2026.) || JULY 24 (Day 148) — CENTCOM reported another US service member killed — in northern Iraq during the controlled detonation of a downed Iranian drone — and said it had located unidentified remains in Jordan tied to an earlier attack that left two dead and one MIA. Cumulative US KIA since the war began Feb 28 continues to be reported around the high-teens/~18-19 (outlets differ on the running count). (CNN; CENTCOM via CNN, July 23, 2026.) || JULY 23 (Day 147) — The Pentagon identified a 4th US service member killed in recent days; Middle East Eye reported the death of a third/fourth soldier raised the cumulative US toll since the war began on February 28 to 18 (outlets continue to differ on the running count; the prior standing briefing figure was ~17). These remain AIR/MISSILE-strike losses on bases, not ground-combat deaths. (CBS News; Middle East Eye, July 22, 2026.) || JULY 21 (Day 145) — Cumulative US KIA in the theatre reported at ~17 after the July 17 Jordan deaths and a July 19 Iraq death; The Intercept named Pvt. Isabella Gonzales (19) and Lt. Tyler James Feehan (25) among the dead and said a third (previously missing) soldier's remains had been identified. Reporting remains inconsistent across outlets. (CBS News; The Intercept; Casualties of the 2026 Iran war, July 20, 2026.) || JULY 20 (Day 144) — US KIA RISE FOR THE FIRST TIME IN THE RESUMED (POST-MOU) PHASE: an Iranian ballistic-missile/drone strike on a US base in Jordan (July 17) killed 2 US service members (1 missing, 4 wounded/medevac'd, later discharged) — described by CENTCOM/Fox as 'the first confirmed U.S. combat fatalities since the current conflict resumed' — and CENTCOM reported a further US service member killed in Iraq (July 19). Al Jazeera cited a cumulative ~16 US service members killed since late February; reporting is inconsistent (some outlets say 1 vs 2 for the Jordan strike; one is still listed missing), so the exact running tally is uncertain, but the count clearly rose from the prior standing 15 (13 combat). These deaths are what the eighth-night 'punishment' strike wave and the intensified air campaign responded to — and they crossed the reported US-casualty tripwire without triggering a ground landing. (Fox News; Al Jazeera; ABC News; The National, July 18-19, 2026.) || JUNE 14-15 — No change to US KIA (15 total / 13 combat) as the deal track dominated; no new kinetic US-Iran exchange reported. || JUNE 13-14 — No change to US KIA (15 total / 13 combat) on the scheduled signing day; no new kinetic US-Iran exchange reported as the deal track dominated. || JUNE 12-13 — No change to US KIA (15 total / 13 combat). The overnight June 11-12 Iranian drone attack on Indian shipping in the Strait was repelled with no US casualties (two Iranian drones downed). || JUNE 11-12 — No change to US KIA (15 total / 13 combat). A US Apache helicopter was downed in the Strait of Hormuz (~June 9) but both pilots were rescued uninjured; the renewed June 9-10 US-Iran exchange produced no confirmed new US KIA. || JUNE 4 — No confirmed change to US KIA from the June 2-3 exchange: the IRGC claimed it struck a US airbase ('all predetermined targets destroyed'), but no new US KIA have been confirmed in open reporting. Count holds at 15 KIA (13 combat). || 15 confirmed KIA",
