   "name": "US Military Wounded",
   "value": "AUGUST 27-28 — THE PUBLISHED TOTAL HOLDS AT 776, WITH 758 WOUNDED, AND NO NEW ADDITION WAS REPORTED THIS CYCLE. The Intercept's August 25 accounting of Defense Department figures remains the figure of record: 776 TROOPS WOUNDED OR KILLED — 18 KILLED AND 758 WOUNDED — after 60 wounded were added on August 21, moving the count from 697 to 757. Other outlets carried 774 and 775 in the same window and the divergence is logged rather than resolved. More than 90% of the newest additions are Army; the service split of record remains 580+ Army, 86 Navy, 64 Air Force, 19 Marines. Roughly 345 casualties have occurred since July 7, when the second ceasefire collapsed — approximately 44% of the entire war's total in under two months. The undercount dispute is unchanged. (The Intercept, August 25, 2026; Stars and Stripes, August 21, 2026; Washington Post, August 21, 2026.) || AUGUST 25 — THE PUBLISHED TOTAL MOVES TO 776, WITH 758 WOUNDED, AND THE UNDERCOUNT DISPUTE IS RESTATED IN DETAIL. The Intercept's AUGUST 25 accounting of Defense Department figures puts the toll at 776 TROOPS WOUNDED OR KILLED — 18 KILLED AND 758 WOUNDED — after 60 wounded were added on AUGUST 21, when the published wounded figure rose from 697 to 757. Other outlets carried 774 and 775 in the same window; the audit records 776/758 as the most recent accounting and notes the spread. MORE THAN 90% OF THE NEWEST ADDITIONS ARE ARMY SOLDIERS, consistent with the service split entered in the previous cycle — more than 580 Army, 86 Navy, 64 Air Force and 19 MARINES, the Marines at roughly 2.5% across 182 days. About 345 of the casualties have occurred since JULY 7. THE DOCUMENTED OMISSIONS: more than 200 sailors treated for smoke inhalation and lacerations after the USS GERALD R. FORD fire of MARCH 12 are excluded from the counts; at least one officially recognised death is omitted; and on APRIL 21 the wounded-in-action figure DECLINED BY 15 without explanation. A government official characterised the approach as an 'ACCOUNTING TRICK' — 'They wanted the heat on them to slow down after the really devastating attacks' — having previously called it a 'casualty cover-up.' Independent tallies counting casualties DCAS omits put the true wounded total above 900. (The Intercept, August 25, 2026; Stars and Stripes, 'Pentagon adds 60 wounded to Iran war casualty count,' August 21, 2026; The Washington Post, 'More than 750 U.S. service members wounded in Iran war,' August 21, 2026; Newsmax, August 21, 2026.) || AUGUST 24 — THE PUBLISHED WOUNDED FIGURE HOLDS AT 757, FOR A TOTAL OF 775 CASUALTIES, AND THE SERVICE BREAKDOWN IS ENTERED FOR THE FIRST TIME. Of the 757 wounded since the war began in February, MORE THAN 580 ARE ARMY SOLDIERS, 86 ARE SAILORS, 64 ARE AIRMEN AND 19 ARE MARINES. That composition is a substantive datapoint for this audit rather than a detail: after 180 days, the service that would carry an opposed amphibious lodgement has absorbed roughly 2.5% of the wounded, which is the casualty signature of base defence and blockade enforcement rather than of preparation for a landing. No addition has been recorded since the single-day jump of 60 on August 21 that took the wounded count from 697. The Defense Casualty Analysis System's methodology remains disputed: independent tallies counting casualties DCAS omits put the true wounded total ABOVE 900, and DoD has still given no public account of when or where the injuries occurred. (The Washington Post, August 21, 2026; Stars and Stripes, August 21, 2026; The Intercept, 'The Pentagon Found a New Way to Undercount U.S. Casualties in Iran,' August 4, 2026.) || AUGUST 23 — THE PUBLISHED WOUNDED FIGURE HOLDS AT 757, FOR A TOTAL OF 775 CASUALTIES. No addition was recorded after the single-day jump of 60 on August 21 that took the wounded count from 697. The split remains 14 killed and 418 wounded in Operation Epic Fury since February 28 and four killed and 339 wounded in overseas operations since July 7. The Department has still given no account of when or where the August 21 batch of injuries occurred, and the lag between an injury entering the military medical system and appearing in the casualty database remains the stated explanation. (Defense Casualty Analysis System via The Washington Post, 'More than 750 U.S. service members wounded in Iran war,' August 21, 2026; Stars and Stripes, 'Pentagon adds 60 wounded to Iran war casualty count,' August 21, 2026.) || AUGUST 21-22 — THE PUBLISHED WOUNDED FIGURE IS 756-757 AND OUTLETS DIVERGE BY ONE ON THE SAME DAY'S DATA. The Pentagon's count rose in a single day from 697 to 756-757, an addition of 59-60, taking total US casualties to 774-775; The Washington Post reported 'more than 750 wounded,' [your]NEWS logged 774 total on a +59 jump and Stars and Stripes logged 775 on a +60 jump. The Department gave no account of when or where the additional injuries occurred and did not respond to a request for more information. The split is 14 killed / 418 wounded in Operation Epic Fury since February 28 and four killed / 339 wounded in overseas operations since July 7. The Intercept reported on August 4 that the Pentagon had found 'a new way to undercount U.S. casualties in Iran.' (The Washington Post, August 21, 2026; Stars and Stripes, 'Pentagon adds 60 wounded to Iran war casualty count,' August 21, 2026; [your]NEWS, 'Pentagon Raises Iran War Casualty Total to 774 as Wounded Count Jumps by 59,' August 21, 2026.) || AUGUST 21 — THE PENTAGON ADDED 60 WOUNDED TO ITS ROLLS IN A SINGLE DAY, TAKING THE PUBLISHED WOUNDED TOTAL FROM 697 TO 757 AND TOTAL US CASUALTIES TO 775. The split is OPERATION EPIC FURY (418 WOUNDED SINCE FEBRUARY 28) and OVERSEAS OPERATIONS SINCE JULY 7 (339 WOUNDED). The Department did not provide specifics on when or where the injuries occurred and did not respond to a request for more information. THE ACCOUNTING ITSELF REMAINS CONTESTED: The Intercept reported on AUGUST 4 that 'The Pentagon Found a New Way to Undercount U.S. Casualties in Iran,' and CNN reported on JULY 26 that the count had passed 600 as the Pentagon 'quietly changed accounting.' A large unexplained batch update is equally consistent with reporting lag and with systematic undercounting; the divergence is recorded, not reconciled — see open question `pentagon-casualty-reclassification-july-2026`. (Stars and Stripes, August 21, 2026; The Washington Post, August 21, 2026; [your]NEWS, August 21, 2026; The Intercept, August 4, 2026; CNN Politics, July 26, 2026.) || AUGUST 1 — NO NEW WOUNDED FIGURE PUBLISHED; this project's running tally holds at 685 wounded, unchanged from July 31. A LEDGER DIVERGENCE IS FLAGGED THIS CYCLE: the Pentagon's published total, combining Operation Epic Fury with the new 'Overseas Operations' category tracking casualties from July 7 onward, is 624 wounded — 61 below the running tally carried here — and Wikipedia's infobox and militaryspend.org's tracker both also carry 624. The gap is an accounting-basis difference arising from the July reclassification, in which the Pentagon split the war into two periods for the first time and revised totals substantially. It is recorded here rather than silently reconciled. (Stars and Stripes, 'US troop casualties in Iran war surpass 600 as fighting pauses,' July 27, 2026; CNN Politics, 'Iran war casualty count eclipses 600 as Pentagon quietly changes accounting,' July 26, 2026; Task & Purpose; militaryspend.org, July 30, 2026.) || JULY 31 — WOUNDED ROSE 32 TO 685, THE LARGEST SINGLE-CYCLE INCREASE SINCE THE PENTAGON'S JULY RECLASSIFICATION. ABC News's tally recorded 685 total wounded on July 31, up from 653 reported at 9:06 PM GMT on July 30, which had itself been 11 higher than the Tuesday figure. The rise spans the period of Iran's ballistic attack on Jordan, the July 29-30 US 'heavy wave' and Iran's drone retaliation against Gulf air bases; the Pentagon has not attributed the increase to specific incidents, and the earlier removal of four deaths and 207 wounded from the official tally remains unexplained. (ABC News live updates, July 30-31, 2026.) || JULY 30 — TOTAL WOUNDED HOLDS AT 642 ACROSS BOTH CATEGORIES with no new figure published for the July 29-30 cycle. The Pentagon's stated rationale for the new 'Overseas Operations' category — that \"Operation Epic Fury has concluded\" — still comes without any date for the operation's formal end, and the reclassification of 4 deaths and 207 wounded out of the official Iran-war totals has not been reversed. Of the 207 wounded since July 7, 182 are soldiers, 21 Navy and 4 airmen. Officials continue to attribute the data changes to \"anomalies\" and \"temporary data disruptions.\" (Associated Press via KSAT; CNN Politics; Task & Purpose, July 26-30, 2026.) || JULY 29 — THE PENTAGON PUT A COMBINED NUMBER ON THE RECORD AND NAMED THE RATIONALE, BUT NOT THE DATE. A Pentagon official said that 'because Operation Epic Fury has concluded, the military has decided to classify casualties tied to the latest strikes in the new category,' and would not offer a date for the operation's formal end. The Defense Casualty Analysis System's TOTAL WOUNDED across the Operation Epic Fury and 'Overseas Operations' categories now stands at 642. The four reclassified deaths are the three soldiers killed when Iranian ballistic missiles and drones struck a base in Jordan on July 17 and one killed in Iraq on July 18 during a controlled detonation of an Iranian drone. Marco Rubio's May characterisation — 'we achieved the objectives of that operation' — is the closest thing to a public end-point for Epic Fury. Pentagon spokespeople continue to say the data changes are not an effort to conceal the war's human cost but the result of 'anomalies' and 'temporary data disruptions.' No new US wounded were reported this cycle: the Iranian missiles were intercepted by Jordanian air defences and the joint US-Saudi strikes in Iraq produced no reported US casualties. (Associated Press via KSAT, U.S. News & World Report and The Washington Times, July 28, 2026.) || JULY 28 — No new US wounded reported; the Iranian missile launch was fully intercepted. Official Iran-war wounded remains at 420 after the July 26 reclassification moved 207 wounded into the new 'Overseas Operations' category, against a pre-reclassification figure of 482; officials continue to say the true total exceeds 500, and more than 400 service members have been airlifted for treatment since February 28. The Intercept reported July 28 that at least nine US outposts have been struck since July 9, several sustaining 'significant' damage, and that the Pentagon drastically understates casualty figures. CONTESTED-UNRESOLVED. (CBS News; Washington Post; The Intercept, July 26-28, 2026.) || JULY 27 — Official Iran-war wounded remains at 420 after the July 26 reclassification moved 207 wounded into the new 'Overseas Operations' category. US officials continue to say the true figure exceeds 500, and CBS reported more than 400 service members airlifted for treatment since Operation Epic Fury began on February 28, most for non-combat causes. No restoration or explanatory guidance published. (CBS News; Washington Post, July 26-27, 2026.) || JULY 26 — The official Iran-war wounded count fell from 482 to 420, with 207 wounded moved into the new 'Overseas Operations' category alongside four deaths, dated from July 7. US officials told reporters the true wounded figure exceeds 500. CBS reported more than 400 US service members have been airlifted out for treatment since Operation Epic Fury began in February, most for non-combat causes. The Pentagon had said on July 23-24 that nearly 100 service members were injured (mostly minor) since July 7; the reclassified figure of 207 is materially higher. This is the second unexplained downward revision of the war's casualty accounting after the May 11-12 episode in which the WIA tally rose to 428 then dropped to 413. (The Washington Post; CNBC; AP; CBS News, July 26, 2026.) || JULY 24 (Day 148) — The Pentagon said nearly 100 US service members have been injured (mostly minor) since July 7, when the resumed strike-and-retaliation phase intensified. (CNN; CENTCOM via CNN, July 23, 2026.) || JULY 21 (Day 145) — The Intercept quoted a US official saying Iranian attacks injured 'FAR MORE' troops than the Pentagon admits — 'dozens' treated after recent strikes, with cumulative US casualties (killed AND wounded) 'at least 446,' a ~15% rise since the April 8 ceasefire; 4-10 injured at Muwaffaq Salti Air Base, Jordan. This is a contested single-official aggregate, not a reconciled DoD roster. (The Intercept, July 20, 2026.) || 413 (Pentagon May 12 figure) — Tally had risen to 428 May 11 (Mon); declined to 413 May 12 (Tue) — Pentagon scrubbed 15 wounded-in-action troops from count without public comment per The Intercept ('definition of a cover-up')",
   "change": "AUGUST 27-28 — Holds at 776: 18 killed and 758 wounded. No new addition reported this cycle. ~345 casualties since July 7, about 44% of the war's total in under two months. Service split of record: 580+ Army, 86 Navy, 64 Air Force, 19 Marines. || AUGUST 25 — Total moves to 776: 18 killed and 758 wounded, after 60 wounded were added on August 21 (697 to 757). Other outlets carried 774/775 in the same window. >90% of the newest additions are Army; the service split of record remains 580+ Army, 86 Navy, 64 Air Force, 19 Marines. ~345 casualties since July 7. Undercount dispute restated: 200+ USS Gerald R. Ford fire casualties excluded, one recognised death omitted, April 21 WIA count fell 15 unexplained; independent tallies above 900. || AUGUST 24 — Holds at 757 wounded and 775 total. NEW: service split of the wounded — 580+ Army, 86 Navy, 64 Air Force, 19 Marines. Independent tallies above 900; DoD still gives no account of when or where.",
