   "name": "Israel Casualties (from Iran)",
   "value": "AUGUST 27 — No new Israeli casualties from Iranian fire were reported. The cumulative figure carried in open sourcing remains 71 killed since February 28 — 40 military and 31 civilian — with at least 7,693 injured. The active front was Lebanon rather than Iran: on August 27 Hezbollah launched TWO EXPLOSIVE DRONES at Israeli soldiers operating inside the security zone in south-eastern Lebanon, and the IDF responded with a wave of strikes on Hezbollah targets in southern Lebanon. No Israeli casualties from the drone attack were reported. (JNS, August 27, 2026; Haaretz, August 27, 2026.) || AUGUST 24 — No new Israeli casualties from Iranian fire were reported this cycle. The cumulative figure carried in open sourcing is 71 killed since February 28 — 40 military and 31 civilian — with at least 7,693 injured. Israeli operations continued in southern Lebanon, where the strikes of August 15-16 remain the most recent published toll, and in Gaza. || AUGUST 23 — No new Israeli casualties from Iranian fire were reported this cycle. The cumulative figure carried in open sourcing is 71 killed since February 28 — 40 military and 31 civilian — with at least 7,693 injured in ballistic missile attacks. Israeli kinetic activity in the period ran outward rather than inward: a drone strike in the south-western Syrian village of Beit Jin caused injuries, and operations continued in Gaza's Khan Yunis and in southern Lebanon. (Wikipedia, '2026 Iran war,' retrieved August 24, 2026; The Jerusalem Post live updates, August 23, 2026; The Washington Post/AP, August 22-23, 2026.) || AUGUST 22 — No new Israeli casualties from Iranian fire reported this cycle. The most recent logged event remains the three IDF soldiers seriously wounded by a Hezbollah drone action near the Ali Taher ridge in southern Lebanon on August 15. || AUGUST 15 — AT LEAST THREE IDF SOLDIERS WERE SERIOUSLY WOUNDED by a Hezbollah drone operation near the ALI TAHER RIDGE in southern Lebanon, prompting the Israeli strikes on Ansar and Nabatieh that killed at least eleven. No Iranian missile or drone strike on Israeli territory was reported in this cycle. Cross-source cumulative counts put the Israeli death toll across the war at approximately 57; this project's prior running tally of 24 Israeli civilians and 38 Israeli troops killed since February 28, 2026 is carried forward, with the divergence in accounting bases recorded and not reconciled. (The Times of Israel, August 2026; Al Jazeera, August 16, 2026; militaryspend.org; iranwarlive.com.) || JULY 25 — Running cumulative Israeli toll reported at 24 Israeli civilians and 38 Israeli troops killed since the war began Feb 28, 2026. Israel continued intermittent strikes in southern Lebanon despite the ceasefire framework, including blasts across the south on July 24 and a strike on an ambulance in Nabatieh al-Fawqa that injured two medics. (Fortune; Al Jazeera, July 24-25, 2026.) || JUNE 7 — Iran fired its first direct missile barrage at Israel since the April 8 ceasefire. Iron Dome/Arrow intercepted missiles but the IDF said 'the defense is not hermetic'; the IDF's initial statement reported no immediate casualties, while subsequent reporting (NPR) described an Iranian cluster-warhead missile striking a building in Ramat Gan and killing two residents in their 70s, with debris damaging the Old City of Jerusalem (near Al-Aqsa/Temple Mount, the Church of the Holy Sepulchre, and the Jewish Quarter). Casualty figures diverge across early reporting. (NPR; PBS NewsHour; Axios, June 7, 2026.) || 26+ civilians killed / 7,183+ wounded",
