  {
   "name": "Kuwait Casualties (June 3 airport strike)",
   "value": "JULY 31 — IRANIAN DRONES STRUCK US FACILITIES AT TWO KUWAITI AIR BASES WITH NO CASUALTIES REPORTED. Iran's army said it targeted aircraft shelters, satellite communication systems and equipment storage facilities used by the US military at AHMAD AL-JABER AIR BASE, and the IRGC claimed attacks on US assets at ALI AL-SALEM AIR BASE. Neither Kuwaiti nor US authorities detailed damage, and no casualties were reported — in contrast to the July 30 strike that killed one worker at a Chinese company's building in northern Kuwait. Kuwait has consistently declined to publicise the cost of the retaliation it absorbs. (CNBC; Al Jazeera; IranWire, July 31, 2026.) || JULY 30 — A NEW FATAL IRANIAN STRIKE IN NORTHERN KUWAIT. Kuwait's Ministry of Defence said an Iranian strike hit a building belonging to a Chinese company in the country's north, killing ONE WORKER and causing extensive material damage; spokesman Saud Al-Atwan described it as \"Iranian aggression\" and the armed forces said they would take \"all necessary measures to protect the nation's sovereignty and safeguard its security and stability.\" The worker's nationality was not stated in the initial official account. This adds to Kuwait's running toll from the June 3 airport strike and the July utilities attacks. (Al Arabiya English; Business Recorder; The Washington Post; Shafaq News; WION, July 30, 2026.) || JULY 21 (Day 145) — Cumulative Kuwait toll from the July Iranian strikes on its power-and-water infrastructure reported at 4 SOLDIERS and 7 CIVILIANS KILLED and 82 SOLDIERS and 105 CIVILIANS WOUNDED; a Kuwaiti power-and-water plant burned for a third consecutive day. Kuwait hosts a large US military presence. (ABC News; NPR; Casualties of the 2026 Iran war, July 20, 2026.) || JULY 18 — Iran struck a Kuwaiti water-desalination plant and a power plant; Kuwait's military said several members of its ground forces were wounded and military and civilian facilities were damaged in a ballistic-missile-and-drone attack. (CNBC; France 24; Al Jazeera, July 17, 2026.)  || JULY 16 — In Iran's July 15 multi-state salvo (dozens of missiles and drones at US-hosting Gulf Arab states), missile-alert warnings sounded in Kuwait and Bahrain and Jordan shot down three incoming Iranian missiles; Iran said it targeted US sites in Jordan, Kuwait and Bahrain. No confirmed new Kuwaiti fatalities reported from the July 15 salvo. (AP; Al Jazeera, July 15, 2026.) || JULY 9 — Iran's IRGC struck a Patriot interceptor site in Kuwait with ballistic/cruise missiles and drones in retaliation for US strikes; Kuwait's Defense Ministry reported at least one person injured (stable) and intercepts of 3 ballistic missiles, 1 cruise missile and 10 drones, with damage at several sites during interception. (NPR; The National, July 9, 2026.) ||  || JUNE 28-29 — Iran's IRGC fired ballistic missiles and drones at the US Ali Al Salem airbase in Kuwait (and the US Fifth Fleet HQ in Bahrain), the first Iranian strike on US Gulf bases since June 10-11; a US official said all were intercepted or failed to reach their targets with no US injuries. Kuwait condemned 'repeated heinous Iranian aggressions.' No new confirmed Kuwaiti civilian casualties reported from this barrage. (Al Jazeera; RFE/RL; CBS News, June 28, 2026.) || JUNE 10-11 — Iran barraged US forces at Kuwait's Ali Al Salem and Ahmad Al-Jaber airbases (part of a wider strike also hitting Bahrain and Jordan) in retaliation for the renewed US strikes; Kuwait closed its airspace at 4:50 a.m. June 11 as a precaution. No new confirmed Kuwaiti civilian casualties reported from this barrage. || JUNE 6 — No new Kuwait casualties from the June 6 barrage: of seven Iranian ballistic missiles fired at Kuwait and Bahrain, six were intercepted and one failed to reach its target; Kuwait separately expelled two Iranian embassy staff over the June 3 attack. Prior June 3 toll holds: 1 killed (Indian national) + 63 wounded at Kuwait International Airport. (CENTCOM; Fox News; Gulf News, June 6, 2026.) || 1 killed / 63 wounded — JUNE 3: Iran fired 13 ballistic missiles and 17 drones at Gulf targets at dawn; Kuwait intercepted them, but falling debris struck Kuwait International Airport, killing 1 and wounding 63 and badly damaging the terminal (roof destroyed, fire) one day after it reopened. Kuwait Airways suspended all flights; Emirates, IndiGo and other carriers diverted or cancelled. (NBC News; CBS News; Travel And Tour World, June 3, 2026.)",
