  {
   "name": "Ships Trapped in Gulf",
   "value": "AUGUST 27-28 — NO NEW HULL COUNT WAS PUBLISHED AND THE FIGURE OF RECORD REMAINS 370 HULLS HOLDING POSITION AWAY FROM BERTH as of AUGUST 24, up from 331 on August 22 and 314 on August 18, against a seven-day range wide enough that the level should be read as volatile rather than monotonically rising. The structural picture behind the count was quantified this cycle in port-call terms: KUWAIT -86%, UAE -69% (78 daily calls to 24), QATAR, IRAQ AND BAHRAIN -66 TO -68%, and SAUDI ARABIA -15%. The workarounds continue — ADNOC running a shuttle service with transponders off, Saudi Arabia running ship-to-ship transfers under government-backed insurance, and TankerTrackers counting more than fifteen ship-to-ship operations moving about 25 million barrels in the Gulf of Oman from 'every country in the region, minus Iran.' War-risk insurance remains at 7.5-10% of hull value against 1-3% before the war, and a tanker was struck by an unknown projectile in the Strait on AUGUST 27 under UKMTO Warning 121-26, the second this week. || AUGUST 26 — THE BOARD PUBLISHED NO NEW HULL COUNT THIS CYCLE AND THE FIGURE OF RECORD REMAINS 370 HULLS HOLDING POSITION AWAY FROM BERTH as of AUGUST 24, up from 331 on August 22 and 314 on August 18, against a seven-day range wide enough that the level should be read as volatile rather than monotonic. What the board did carry at its 26 AUGUST 2300Z timestamp was 20,170 VESSELS FLAGGED HIGH-RISK in the 24-hour window, Crisis Pressure at 91 (Extreme) and an Escalation Forecast of 52 (Elevated, cooling). The commercial adaptation continues around the backlog: TankerTrackers counted more than fifteen SHIP-TO-SHIP TRANSFER OPERATIONS moving about 25 MILLION BARRELS in the Gulf of Oman, from 'every country in the region, minus Iran,' and ADNOC is reported offering a shuttle service with transponders switched off. (straits.live, 26 August 2026 2300Z; Fox News Digital live updates, August 25, 2026; Al Jazeera, August 26, 2026.) || AUGUST 24 — 370 HULLS HOLDING POSITION AWAY FROM BERTH on the straits.live board, up from 331 on August 22 and 314 on August 18, against a seven-day range wide enough that the level should be read as volatile rather than trending. Eight of nine major container carriers are routing via the CAPE OF GOOD HOPE. War-risk cover is unchanged at about $10.0M PER VLCC TRANSIT, forty times pre-crisis, with SIX P&I CLUBS having withdrawn coverage and the waterway described as closed to commercial shipping on insurance grounds. Market odds of a reopening by AUGUST 31 stand UNDER 1% and by DECEMBER 31 at 34%. || AUGUST 22 — 331 HULLS HOLDING POSITION AWAY FROM BERTH at the straits.live 2256Z timestamp, down from 386 on August 21 and 314 on August 18, on a seven-day range of 201-530 — a range wide enough that the level should be read as volatile rather than trending. Roughly 390 ships were being described as holding inside the Gulf earlier in the week. War-risk cover stands at 40× pre-crisis levels, about $10.0 million to insure a single VLCC transit, sea mines remain uncleared, convoys are moving under naval escort, and four of the world's largest container carriers say they have stopped using the Strait. (straits.live Strait of Hormuz status board, Day 175, 22 August 2026 2256Z.) || AUGUST 18-21 — THE HOLDING FLEET GREW BY ROUGHLY A QUARTER IN THREE DAYS. straits.live counted 314 HULLS HOLDING POSITION AWAY FROM BERTH at 23:53 UTC on AUGUST 18 (against a recent range of 299-305) and 386 ON AUGUST 21, with a separate situation-report line citing 423 VESSELS STRANDED AWAY FROM BERTH. Hormuz-area port arrivals ran at 478 in the 24 hours to August 18 and 542 in the 24 hours to August 21. The Hormuz Index state composite and Crisis Pressure composite both sat in the EXTREME band, with the Escalation Forecast composite ELEVATED and easing. Reported incidents in the period include one cargo ship declared a CONSTRUCTIVE TOTAL LOSS after being struck by projectiles off Yemen and TWO BULK CARRIERS STRUCK BY PROJECTILES IN THE STRAIT, with two crew deaths recorded. (straits.live Strait of Hormuz Status briefs, August 18 and August 21, 2026.) || AUGUST 1 — 371 VESSELS HOLDING POSITION AWAY FROM BERTH and 36 tankers running AIS-dark over 24 hours, per the straits.live board at 22:59 UTC. NOTE ON SERIES COMPARABILITY: this is the board's 'stranded / holding position' count, which is a different and narrower measure than the 1,364-stranded figure logged on July 31 from the same tracker's broader series; the two are not directly comparable and the discontinuity is flagged rather than presented as a fall. Eight of the nine largest container lines continue to route via the Cape of Good Hope. (straits.live, August 1, 2026.) || JULY 31 — 1,364 STRANDED AND 85 DARK TANKERS ON A BOARD THAT REVERTED TO ITS PRIOR FRAMING. The straits.live tracker showed 1,364 vessels stranded or anchored in the zone at 22:59Z, within a 29-hour range of 1,105 to 1,397 and up 109 on 29 hours earlier, alongside 85 AIS-dark tankers in the last 24 hours against a 7-day baseline of 42.4 — a 100% excess. With the transit series back on the 10-per-day/88-baseline framing, the stranded figure is again read as the accumulated backlog of the closure rather than a measurement artefact. (straits.live, 31 JUL 2026 2259Z.) || JULY 30 — THE STRANDED COUNT AS PRINTED IS 1,357, AND IT IS NOT COMPARABLE TO THE PRIOR CYCLE'S 339. straits.live at 22:59Z reported 1,357 vessels anchored or stopped in the Hormuz zone with a 5-hour range of 1,255-1,390, against 339 the day before, while simultaneously reporting transits UP (35 from 10-13) and dark tankers DOWN (9 from 68). Those three moves do not reconcile as a single physical event and no methodology note was published; the figure is recorded as printed and flagged as a probable change in the counted zone. No independent source corroborated a fourfold overnight increase in strandings. (straits.live, July 30, 2026 22:59Z.) || JULY 29 — THE BACKLOG EASED A SECOND CONSECUTIVE DAY. The straits.live tracker recorded 339 vessels stranded (anchored or stopped) across the Hormuz zone at 23:03Z, down from 405, against a 7-day range of 297-540, with 68 tankers running dark over 24 hours, down from 77. No updated count was published for the tanker waiting area off Kharg Island, last reported at 24 vessels on July 26. The easing runs against the cycle's kinetic direction: the IRGC struck and stopped three transiting tankers and Iran declared third-party mine-clearing vessels legitimate targets, while more than 20 US warships continue to enforce the blockade of Iranian ports. UN News continues to report stranded seafarers trapped aboard as Hormuz shipping stalls. (straits.live; Tasnim News Agency; Democracy Now; UN News, July 29, 2026.) || JULY 28 — The backlog eased for the first time in the resumed phase. The straits.live tracker recorded 405 vessels stranded (anchored or stopped) across the Hormuz zone at 22:59Z, down from 426, with 77 tankers running dark, down from 97. Thirteen tankers were transiting. No updated count was published for the tanker waiting area off Kharg Island, last reported at 24 vessels on July 26. Four days of strike pause produced a partial unwinding, not a reopening; the US naval blockade of Iranian ports remained in full effect throughout. (straits.live, July 28, 2026; Lloyd's List Intelligence.) || JULY 27 — The straits.live tracker recorded 426 vessels stranded (anchored or stopped) across the Hormuz zone, with 97 tankers running dark. The tanker waiting area off Kharg Island reached 24 vessels on July 26, up from 21 the previous day. Three days of strike pause have not yet cleared the backlog. (straits.live, July 27, 2026; Lloyd's List Intelligence.) || JULY 16 — Only ~21 ships transited Hormuz on July 15 as the re-imposed US blockade and Iran's attacks kept most vessels cautious/stranded; the running tally of the tanker-war reached ~56 confirmed shipping incidents and 17 seafarer fatalities. (2026 Strait of Hormuz crisis tracking; Fortune, July 15, 2026.) || JULY 13 — Hundreds of vessels remained cautious/stranded as the Strait stayed effectively closed for a second day; major carriers (Maersk, MSC, CMA CGM, Hapag-Lloyd) had suspended Hormuz transits and the Joint Maritime Information Center kept its threat level at 'severe.' || JUNE 29-30 — Hundreds of vessels remain stranded inside the Persian Gulf and shipowners stay cautious as demining is unresolved (Iran insists on demining alone; an Iran-Oman Strait-management track opened June 29). Major container lines nonetheless resumed Gulf services after the US-Iran stand-down. (CBS News; Euronews, June 29, 2026.) || JUNE 17 — ADNOC CEO Sultan Al Jaber said ~230 loaded oil tankers are waiting inside the Gulf with the strait still effectively closed; transit expected to resume once the deal takes effect after the Friday June 19 signing. (Wikipedia '2026 Strait of Hormuz crisis,' June 2026.) || ~600+ (including 325 tankers); ~41 LOADED OIL TANKERS WITH 69M BBL IRANIAN OIL VALUED ~$6B+ STRANDED PER BLOCKADE — ~8 VLCCs ANCHORED OFF KHARG; 7 DARK-AIS TANKERS NEAR CHABAHAR — US HOLDS 4 (TOUSKA, TIFANI, MAJESTIC X, LPG SEVAN); IRAN HOLDS 2 (MSC FRANCESCA, EPAMINONDAS) — SCOREBOARD 4-2 US-IRAN — CENTCOM/CNN MAY 2 AGGREGATION: 48 VESSELS TURNED BACK SINCE APR 13 (UP FROM 42 APR 30) — IRAN PARLIAMENT ADVANCING 12-ARTICLE HORMUZ LAW (ISRAELI BARRED + US/ALLIED SHIPS NEED WAR-REPARATIONS PERMIT + 20% CARGO SEIZURE) — COOPER VISITS USS TRIPOLI IN ARABIAN SEA SATURDAY — USS GERALD R. FORD CONTINUES DEPARTING MIDDLE EAST AFTER 309-DAY RECORD DEPLOYMENT",
   "change": "AUGUST 27-28 — No new hull count; figure of record holds at 370 away from berth as of August 24. Port calls quantified: Kuwait -86%, UAE -69%, Qatar/Iraq/Bahrain -66-68%, Saudi -15%. War-risk insurance 7.5-10% of hull value vs 1-3% pre-war. Second tanker of the week hit by a projectile (UKMTO 121-26). || AUGUST 26 — No new hull count published; the figure of record remains 370 holding away from berth as of August 24. Board at 26 Aug 2300Z: 20,170 vessels flagged high-risk, Crisis Pressure 91, Escalation Forecast 52 (cooling). TankerTrackers: 15+ ship-to-ship operations moving ~25m barrels in the Gulf of Oman, 'every country in the region, minus Iran.' || AUGUST 24 — 370 hulls holding position away from berth (331 on August 22; 314 on August 18). 8 of 9 major container carriers on the Cape route. War risk ~$10.0m per VLCC, 40x pre-crisis, six P&I clubs withdrawn. Reopening odds: <1% by August 31, 34% by December 31.",
