  {
   "name": "Blockade Boardings / Seizures",
   "value": "AUGUST 27-28 — CENTCOM PUBLISHED NO NEW TALLY AND THE FIGURE OF RECORD REMAINS 71 COMMERCIAL VESSELS REDIRECTED, 3 DISABLED AND 2 BOARDED as of AUGUST 24 since the JULY 14 resumption, up from 70 on August 23, 68 on August 21, 67 on August 20, 64 on August 17, 44 on August 3 and 35 on July 27, plus more than 40 humanitarian vessels permitted through. THE MATERIAL DEVELOPMENT IS LEGAL RATHER THAN NUMERICAL AND IT IS THE MOST STRUCTURALLY SIGNIFICANT BLOCKADE ITEM SINCE THE JULY RESUMPTION: the JUSTICE DEPARTMENT is preparing to activate MARITIME PRIZE COURTS — dormant since the Second World War and of negligible use since 1898 — to give federal prosecutors a faster route to condemn oil and other cargo taken from enemy or neutral vessels as UNITED STATES PROPERTY, to be sold with proceeds transferred to the Treasury. US Attorney AARON REITZ, whose Houston office is partnering with department headquarters, confirmed DOJ is 'NOW REVIVING' an 'ANCIENT BODY OF MARITIME LAW.' The stated purposes are to strengthen the blockade and to offset the cost of the conflict; the reported trigger is that the civil-forfeiture route used against a seized Venezuelan supertanker has bogged down under interventions by the shipping company and by families of Iranian terrorism victims. Plans are described as unfinalised. The White House restated the instrument on AUGUST 27: 'the naval blockade continues to be in effect as well, which has proven hugely successful.' (Bloomberg, August 26, 2026; Fox Business and Newsmax, August 26-27, 2026; ABC News live updates, August 27, 2026.) || AUGUST 25-27 — CENTCOM PUBLISHED NO NEW TALLY IN THIS CYCLE AND THE FIGURE OF RECORD REMAINS 71 COMMERCIAL VESSELS REDIRECTED, 3 DISABLED AND 2 BOARDED as of AUGUST 24 since the JULY 14 resumption, up from 70 on August 23, 68 on August 21, 67 on August 20, 64 on August 17, 44 on August 3 and 35 on August 2 — alongside the first-time disclosure that MORE THAN 40 HUMANITARIAN AID VESSELS have been permitted through. More than 20 warships support the blockade. THE BLOCKADE IS NOW THE STATED CENTRE OF AMERICAN STRATEGY RATHER THAN ITS ADJUNCT: US officials told Axios on AUGUST 25 that Washington will focus on sanctions, the naval blockade and 'moving ever-increasing amounts of oil out of the Strait of Hormuz' in place of new strikes. Iran's Executive Vice-President Ghaempanah stated the effect from the other side: 'WITH THE US NAVAL BLOCKADE, WE CANNOT IMPORT GASOLINE.' (US Central Command releases through August 24, 2026; Axios, August 25, 2026; Iran International live blog, August 26, 2026.) || AUGUST 24 — CENTCOM TALLY REACHED 71 COMMERCIAL VESSELS REDIRECTED, 3 DISABLED AND 2 BOARDED, up from 70 as of August 23, 68 as of August 21, 67 as of August 20, 64 on August 17, 44 on August 3 and 35 on August 2, dated from the JULY 14 resumption of the blockade, with more than 20 warships supporting. AND A FIGURE APPEARS IN THIS RECORD FOR THE FIRST TIME: CENTCOM says MORE THAN 40 HUMANITARIAN AID VESSELS HAVE BEEN PERMITTED PASSAGE. A blockade operating a declared humanitarian exception is a different instrument from one that does not, and the disclosure is logged. One interdiction in a day remains far below the early-August pace, consistent with traffic that has largely stopped presenting itself rather than with relaxed enforcement. (U.S. Central Command via Iran International live blog, August 24, 2026, 20:44 GMT+1.) || AUGUST 23 — CENTCOM TALLY REACHED 70 COMMERCIAL VESSELS REDIRECTED, 3 DISABLED AND 2 BOARDED, up from 68 as of August 21, 67 as of August 20, 64 on August 17, 44 on August 3 and 35 on August 2. CENTCOM dates the count from the JULY 14 RESUMPTION of the naval blockade and says US forces 'continue to strictly enforce the U.S. blockade against Iran,' with more than 20 warships supporting. The pace of interdiction — two hulls in two days — remains far below the early-August rate, which is consistent with traffic that has largely stopped presenting itself rather than with relaxed enforcement. (TASS, '70 vessels redirected by US military since resumption of blockade of Iran — CENTCOM,' August 23, 2026; U.S. Central Command via X, August 23, 2026.) || AUGUST 21-22 — CENTCOM TALLY REACHED 68 COMMERCIAL VESSELS REDIRECTED, 3 DISABLED AND 2 BOARDED as of August 21, up from 67 as of August 20, 64 on August 17, 44 on August 3 and 35 on August 2. CENTCOM published imagery of an MH-60R Sea Hawk landing aboard USS ROSS (DDG-71), described as ONE OF MORE THAN 20 WARSHIPS SUPPORTING THE BLOCKADE, and said US forces 'continue to strictly enforce the U.S. blockade against Iran.' THE PACE HAS SLOWED SHARPLY: one redirection in the day to August 21 against 23 in the fortnight to August 17, consistent with traffic that has largely stopped presenting itself rather than with relaxed enforcement. (U.S. Central Command via X, August 21, 2026; ANI, August 22, 2026; Middle East Eye, August 22, 2026.) || AUGUST 2-20 — THE BLOCKADE TALLY NEARLY DOUBLED IN THREE WEEKS AND THE ENFORCEMENT TURNED KINETIC. CENTCOM's running count moved from 35 COMMERCIAL VESSELS REDIRECTED, 2 DISABLED AND 2 BOARDED ON AUGUST 2, to 44 REDIRECTED ON AUGUST 3, to 64 REDIRECTED BY AUGUST 17, to 67 REDIRECTED, 3 DISABLED AND 2 BOARDED AS OF AUGUST 20. THE DISABLING EVENT OF THE CYCLE: on AUGUST 11 a US Navy helicopter fired TWO HELLFIRE MISSILES INTO THE ENGINE ROOM OF THE PANAMA-FLAGGED CONTAINER SHIP VELA NOVA in the GULF OF OMAN, disabling its steering gear, after the crew ignored repeated warnings while attempting to reach an Iranian port in violation of the blockade reimposed on JULY 13 following the collapse of the June 17 MOU. Cumulative figures across the war's two blockade periods are reported as three ships seized and 85 vessels intercepted. SEPARATELY, PIRACY HAS FILLED THE ENFORCEMENT VACUUM: on AUGUST 20 Somali pirates hijacked the ERITREAN-FLAGGED TANKER SIBU 1 — sanctioned by the US Treasury in December as part of Iran's shadow fleet — 136 nautical miles east of AL MUKALLA, YEMEN, the second hijacking in four days, with Danish Shipping reporting 13 PIRACY INCIDENTS OFF SOMALIA IN THE FIRST SEVEN MONTHS OF 2026 against five in all of 2025 — the highest in a decade. (U.S. Central Command via X, August 3 and August 20, 2026; OANN; RSBN; Al Jazeera, 'US military fires on cargo vessel it said sought to break Iran blockade,' August 11, 2026; NewsNation; gCaptain; CNBC, August 20, 2026.) || AUGUST 1 — CENTCOM'S RUNNING TALLY OF COMMERCIAL VESSELS REDIRECTED SINCE THE BLOCKADE RESTART ROSE TO 24, from 17 reported on July 26, alongside two vessels disabled and two boarded. Iran's side of the double blockade continues: the IRGC Navy has stopped vessels attempting to transit outside the designated route with warning shots, and Tehran maintains that transit is 'not possible' because of the 'aggressive actions' of the US military. (CBS News live updates, August 1, 2026; US Central Command via CBS and Al Jazeera, July 26 – August 1, 2026.) || JULY 31 — THE CENTCOM TALLY HELD WHILE IRAN'S COUNTER-INTERDICTION CONTINUED. The US figure remains 18 vessels stopped, 2 disabled and 2 boarded with 20+ warships committed, with no new stop announced this cycle. On the Iranian side, Fars reported that the Revolutionary Guards STOPPED TWO TANKERS from transiting the waterway while FOUR OTHERS ALTERED COURSE. Iran's declaration that mine-clearing vessels and equipment brought near the Strait 'will be considered legitimate targets' stands unretracted. (CENTCOM; Fars via TradingEconomics, July 31, 2026.) || JULY 29 — THE BLOCKADE TALLY HELD WHILE IRAN'S COUNTER-INTERDICTION RESUMED. CENTCOM's figures are unchanged from July 28: more than 20 US Navy warships enforcing the blockade, 18 commercial vessels stopped from entering or exiting Iranian ports, 2 vessels disabled and 2 boarded. Against that, the IRGC said it had struck and stopped THREE oil tankers in the Strait, claiming they 'continued to move along an unsafe and illegal route, ignoring our warnings' — its first claimed strikes on Hormuz shipping since the July 24-26 halt — and Deputy Foreign Minister Kazem Gharibabadi declared that mine-clearing vessels or equipment brought near the Strait 'will be considered legitimate targets,' that demining is Iran's own responsibility, and that US warships are not permitted near the Strait. The strikes formally meet the trigger for Trump's July 22 doctrine of destroying one Iranian bridge or power plant per ship attacked, which had not been invoked as of the cycle close. (CENTCOM via CBS News; Tasnim News Agency; Democracy Now; Al Jazeera, July 29, 2026.) || JULY 28 — THE TALLY ROSE AND TRUMP CLAIMED THE STRAIT. CENTCOM's figures as of July 28: more than 20 US Navy warships enforcing the blockade, 18 COMMERCIAL VESSELS STOPPED from entering or exiting Iranian ports (up from 12 redirected on July 27), 2 vessels disabled and 2 boarded. Trump: 'We control the Strait. Our navy is incredible. Not one ship has gotten to Iran during any blockade.' The blockade ran unpaused through all four days of the strike halt, and Iran's Khatam al-Anbiya Central Headquarters continues to characterise it as 'an expansion of the war in the region.' The IRGC has separately turned back six vessels with warning shots, and one tanker was reported to have struck a mine. (CENTCOM via Fox News Digital and CBS News live blogs, July 28, 2026; WANA, July 27, 2026.) || JULY 27 — The blockade continued in full effect through the third day of the strike pause, and drew Iran's sharpest institutional response yet: the Khatam al-Anbiya Central Headquarters warned the US naval blockade 'will be regarded as an expansion of the war in the region,' citing three days of US threats against Iranian vessels, commercial ships and oil tankers in Iran's coastal and territorial waters. CENTCOM's running tally since re-imposition on July 14 stands at 12 vessels redirected, 2 disabled and 2 boarded; the IRGC has separately turned back six vessels with warning shots, and one tanker was reported to have struck a mine. (WANA; CENTCOM via JNS; CBS News, July 26-27, 2026.) || JULY 26 — The blockade remained 'in full effect' through the strike pause. The running US Navy tally reached 12 commercial vessels REDIRECTED, 2 DISABLED and 2 BOARDED since the blockade was re-imposed on July 14. Iran's Revolutionary Guard has separately stopped six vessels with warning shots, and one oil tanker was reported to have struck a mine. The most recent US actions were the disabling of the Mozambique-flagged M/T Lavine in the Gulf of Oman on July 24 after at least four blockade-run attempts, and the verification boarding of the Comoros-flagged M/T Charminar in the Arabian Sea, which was cleared onward. Interdiction and visit-board-search — not a port seizure or landing. (Al Jazeera; Fortune/AP; CBS News; CENTCOM, July 24-26, 2026.) || JULY 25 — US blockade enforcement continued through the strike pause. CENTCOM disabled the M/T Lavine in the Gulf of Oman after the crew ignored repeated warnings and attempted to breach the blockade at least four times, firing into the ship's engine room; US forces separately completed a verification boarding of the Comoros-flagged M/T Charminar in the Arabian Sea, which was cleared to continue its voyage. Since the blockade was re-imposed on July 14 the running tally is 12 commercial vessels redirected and 2 disabled. Interdiction and visit-board-search-and-seizure — not a port seizure or landing. Trump said damage to ships 'will be paid for by Iranian money that the United States has in its possession and controls.' (AP via Türkiye Today; NewsNation; Fox News; CENTCOM, July 25, 2026.) || JULY 18 — US NAVAL BLOCKADE ACTIVE. A US fighter jet disabled the Curacao-flagged tanker M/T Belma (Hellfire into its smokestack) as it steamed toward Kharg Island trying to run the blockade, and US Marines BOARDED the commercial vessel M/T Wen Yao to enforce it — interdiction and visit-board-search-and-seizure, not a port seizure or landing. (The Hill; NPR; CBS News, July 16-17, 2026.)  || JULY 17 — US NAVAL BLOCKADE ACTIVE. The US said it DISABLED an unladen (empty) oil tanker that was HEADED FOR KHARG ISLAND and trying to run/avoid the blockade — the interdiction campaign's closest approach to the island, but a maritime action, not a port seizure or landing on Kharg. This adds to the two commercial vessels 'redirected' and the tanker struck within ~17 hours of the July 14 restart. Still interdictions/redirections at sea, not an occupation. (NPR; CNN; CBS News, July 15-16, 2026.) || JULY 16 — US NAVAL BLOCKADE ACTIVE. Within ~17 hours of restarting the blockade (July 14, 4pm ET) the US said it had 'redirected' two commercial vessels attempting to breach it, and CENTCOM reported striking a tanker trying to skirt it. These are interdictions/redirections at sea, not port seizures or an occupation. In a US combat first, three 'Corsair' one-way attack sea drones struck the Bandar Abbas naval base. (CNN; NPR; CBS News; Task & Purpose, July 12-15, 2026.) || JULY 15 — US NAVAL BLOCKADE RE-IMPOSED. On July 14 (4pm ET) the US restarted its blockade of Iranian ports — ending the ~27-day 'lifted' period that had run since the June 17 MOU — with CENTCOM citing 'more than 20 US Navy warships and hundreds of military aircraft operating' in the region. Trump paired it with a 20% Hormuz 'toll'/'guardian of the Strait' framing that he reversed the same day. This is a return of the US MARITIME-interdiction instrument (blockade + boarding capability), the sharpest US coercion of the chokepoint since the June lift — but it is a naval/air instrument, NOT a ground seizure or Kharg landing. No specific new boarding/seizure tally was published as the blockade restarted. (CBS News; NPR; CNBC; Al Jazeera, July 13-14, 2026.) || JULY 13 — US naval blockade STILL LIFTED (Day 25); no US boardings or seizures. The coercive closure in force at Hormuz is IRAN's unilateral IRGC declaration (now in its second day), not a US reimposition; the US response stayed diplomatic/air. (Al Jazeera; CNBC, July 12-13, 2026.) || JULY 12 — US naval blockade STILL LIFTED (Day 24); no US boardings or seizures. The coercive closure now in force at Hormuz is IRAN's unilateral IRGC declaration, not a US reimposition; the US response stayed diplomatic/air. (RFE/RL; CNN, July 11-12, 2026.) || JULY 10-11 — US naval blockade STILL LIFTED (Day 23); no US boardings or seizures. Despite Trump's 'total control of their Oil and Gas Markets' seizure threat, no reimposition was ordered; the US posture shifted to 'strike-then-pause for diplomacy.' (CNN; NPR, July 9-10, 2026.) || JULY 9-10 — US naval blockade STILL LIFTED (Day 22); no US boardings or seizures. Despite Trump declaring the ceasefire 'over,' threatening to blockade Iran again, and hitting Kharg from the air, no reimposition was ordered; the US response to Iran's tanker attacks and cross-Gulf retaliation stayed an air/stand-off strike package. (NBC News; Al Jazeera, July 9-10, 2026.) ||  || JULY 8-9 — US naval blockade STILL LIFTED (Day 21); no US boardings or seizures. Even after Trump declared the ceasefire 'over' and threatened to blockade Iran again, no reimposition was ordered; the US response to Iran's tanker attacks stayed a two-wave air-strike package plus the July 7 oil-waiver revocation. (NBC News; PBS NewsHour, July 8, 2026.) || JULY 7-8 — US naval blockade STILL LIFTED (Day 20); no US boardings or seizures. Despite the July 7 escalation, the US response to Iran's tanker attacks was an AIR-strike package and an oil-waiver revocation, not a reinstated interdiction/boarding operation. (CBS News; NBC News, July 7, 2026.) || JULY 1-4 — US naval blockade STILL LIFTED (Day 16); no US boardings or seizures. The period's US activity was diplomatic — the July 1-2 Doha round and then a one-week pause of talks for Khamenei's funeral — not an interdiction operation. The April 13 interdiction campaign stays dismantled, the inverse of the predicted seizure. (CNN; Al Jazeera; Fox News Digital, July 1-3, 2026.) || JUNE 28-29 — US naval blockade STILL LIFTED (Day 11); no US boardings or seizures. The day's escalation was an air/missile exchange — a 10-target US strike near Hormuz and Iran's missile/drone retaliation on US bases in Kuwait and Bahrain — not a return to blockade enforcement. (Al Jazeera; ABC News, June 28, 2026.) || JUNE 25-26 — US naval blockade STILL LIFTED (Day 8); no US boardings or seizures. The day's maritime incident was the reverse direction — IRAN fired on a cargo vessel near Oman (June 25) and the IRGC Navy threatened to take 'action' against ships not using its designated routes, prompting the UN IMO to pause its Hormuz shepherding program. (NBC News; The Times of Israel, June 25, 2026.) || JUNE 18 — BLOCKADE LIFTED. US Central Command announced the naval blockade of Iranian ports has been lifted 'in accordance with the president's direction,' though US military vessels remain on station to monitor compliance — physically ending the April 13 maritime-interdiction campaign rather than escalating it. No new boardings/seizures; the codified direction is now the dismantling of the maritime instrument, the inverse of reinforcement, with Hormuz reopening by negotiated mine-clearing. (CBS News live updates, June 18, 2026.) || JUNE 15-16 — The US naval blockade has NOT yet been physically lifted: Trump authorized its removal and Iran said it ends 'immediately and completely,' but the finalized MOU sets a complete lifting 'within 30 days.' No new boardings/seizures reported on June 15-16; the codified direction is drawdown of the maritime instrument, not reinforcement. (Al Jazeera; NBC News, June 15, 2026.) || JUNE 14-15 — Trump authorized the IMMEDIATE REMOVAL of the US naval blockade as part of the June 14 deal; Iran's deputy FM said the blockade would end Sunday night. CENTCOM's cumulative tally before the lift stood at ~141 commercial vessels redirected + ~9 disabled. The blockade's lifting (pending the Friday signing) ends the maritime interdiction campaign rather than escalating it. (CBS News; PBS NewsHour; Axios, June 14, 2026.) || JUNE 4 — CENTCOM's published tally stands at 118 commercial vessels redirected + ~5-6 disabled: on June 2 a US Hellfire from a fighter jet DISABLED an oil tanker heading for Kharg Island after it 'ignored repeated warnings,' the cycle's notable new enforcement action; CENTCOM has cited more than 8,000 Iranian military targets and 130 vessels struck across the campaign. No consolidated new June 4 figure published. (CNBC/Wikipedia, '2026 Strait of Hormuz campaign,' June 2-3, 2026.) || JUNE 3 — CENTCOM tally holds at 118 commercial vessels redirected + 5 disabled (120+ total affected since the blockade began); no new figure published this cycle; US Navy maintains operational readiness despite the unsigned MOU and Iran's June 1 suspension of talks. (GlobalSecurity.org, June 2, 2026). || JUNE 2 — CENTCOM tally holds at 118 commercial vessels redirected + 5 disabled (120+ total affected since the blockade began); US Navy maintains operational readiness despite the ceasefire and the unsigned MOU. (Fox News live blog; CENTCOM, May 31-June 1, 2026). || JUNE 1 — CENTCOM tally rises to 118 commercial vessels redirected + 5 disabled (120+ total affected since the blockade began); US Navy maintains operational readiness despite the ceasefire. (Fox News live blog, May 31, 2026). || MAY 30-31 — CENTCOM blockade tally rose to ~115-116 commercial vessels redirected + 5 DISABLED, the fifth being the M/V Lian Star (disabled after 'more than 20 warnings') — up from 100+ redirected / 4 disabled (May 23). Seizure scoreboard otherwise unchanged at 4-2 US-Iran (US holds Touska/Tifani/Majestic X/LPG Sevan; Iran holds MSC Francesca/Epaminondas). Blockade 'very much still in place' (Hegseth, Shangri-La, May 30). (Fox News live, May 30, 2026). || MAY 26-27 — No new boardings/seizures reported despite the first kinetic US-Iran exchange of the Project Freedom pause; seizure scoreboard unchanged at 4-2 US-Iran (US holds Touska/Tifani/Majestic X/LPG Sevan; Iran holds MSC Francesca/Epaminondas). The May 26 CENTCOM 'self-defense strikes' targeted 2 IRGC mine-laying boats (destroyed in the Strait of Hormuz) + a SAM site near Bandar Abbas — kinetic destruction, NOT VBSS boarding/seizure. CENTCOM's last milestone (May 23): MORE THAN 100 commercial vessels redirected since the Apr 13 blockade, with 4 vessels disabled and 26 humanitarian-aid vessels permitted to pass; more than 15,000 US troops enforcing. Blockade remains in force. PRIOR MAY 25-26 — No new boardings/seizures reported; seizure scoreboard unchanged at 4-2 US-Iran (US holds Touska/Tifani/Majestic X/LPG Sevan; Iran holds MSC Francesca/Epaminondas). CENTCOM's last milestone (May 23): MORE THAN 100 commercial vessels redirected since the Apr 13 blockade, with 4 vessels disabled and 26 humanitarian-aid vessels permitted to pass; more than 15,000 US troops enforcing. Blockade held 'in full force and effect until an agreement is reached, certified, and signed' (Trump, May 24-25). PRIOR MAY 23-25 — CENTCOM milestone: MORE THAN 100 commercial vessels redirected since the Apr 13 blockade began (up from 85), with 4 vessels disabled and 26 humanitarian-aid vessels permitted to pass; more than 15,000 US troops enforcing. Blockade held 'in full force and effect until an agreement is reached, certified, and signed' (Trump, May 24). PRIOR MAY 23-24 — CENTCOM MILESTONE (May 23): US forces have now redirected MORE THAN 100 commercial vessels since the Apr 13 blockade (up from 85), with 4 disabled and 26 humanitarian-aid vessels permitted to pass; more than 15,000 US troops are enforcing the blockade. No new ship boardings/seizures reported May 22-24 (seizure scoreboard unchanged at 4-2 US-Iran: US holds Touska/Tifani/Majestic X/LPG Sevan; Iran holds MSC Francesca/Epaminondas). PRIOR MAY 20-21 — CENTCOM running tally remains 85 commercial vessels redirected + 4 disabled since the Apr 13 blockade (May 18 figure; no fresh count published May 19-21). Zero new boardings/seizures reported May 19-21. PRIOR MAY 19-20 — CENTCOM running tally remains 85 commercial vessels redirected + 4 disabled since the Apr 13 blockade (May 18 figure; no fresh count published May 19-20). Zero new boardings/seizures reported May 19-20. 6 total — US HOLDS 4 (Touska Apr 19 Gulf of Oman, Tifani Apr 21 Bay of Bengal, MAJESTIC X Apr 23 INDIAN OCEAN, LPG SEVAN APR 25 CENTRAL ARABIAN SEA — first gas-carrier target); IRAN HOLDS 2 (MSC Francesca + Epaminondas, Strait of Hormuz, Apr 22) — SCOREBOARD NOW 4-2 US-IRAN — NO NEW SEIZURES APR 26-29; CENTCOM APR 29: 29 VESSELS TURNED BACK (REVISED LOWER FROM 39 APR 28); TRUMP REJECTS HORMUZ-FOR-BLOCKADE OFFER VIA AXIOS APR 29 ('STUFFED PIG'); BLOCKADE STAYS UNTIL NUCLEAR DEAL",
   "change": "AUGUST 27-28 — No new CENTCOM tally; holds at 71 redirected, 3 disabled, 2 boarded as of August 24, plus 40+ humanitarian vessels permitted. NEW AND STRUCTURAL: DOJ reviving Civil War-era maritime prize courts to condemn seized Iranian tankers and cargo as US property for sale with proceeds to the Treasury — to strengthen the blockade and offset the war's cost. US Attorney Aaron Reitz confirms DOJ is 'now reviving' an 'ancient body of maritime law.' White House: the blockade 'has proven hugely successful.' || AUGUST 25-27 — No new CENTCOM tally; figure of record holds at 71 redirected, 3 disabled, 2 boarded as of August 24, plus 40+ humanitarian vessels permitted through. Axios: US officials name sanctions, the blockade and moving oil out of the Strait as the strategy in place of new strikes. Iran's Executive VP: 'with the US naval blockade, we cannot import gasoline.' || AUGUST 24 — 71 vessels redirected, 3 disabled, 2 boarded since the July 14 resumption (70 on August 23; 68 on August 21). NEW: more than 40 humanitarian aid vessels permitted passage. More than 20 warships enforcing.",
