   "name": "Qatar Gas Production",
   "value": "AUGUST 28 — Unchanged on the production side: the IMF April vintage projects Qatar at -14.7% for 2026, with roughly 17% of LNG export capacity lost and about $20bn of annual revenue foregone since the March Ras Laffan strike and the onset of Hormuz disruption. Al Jazeera's August 27 port-call dataset puts Qatar among the hardest-hit at -66 to -68% in daily calls, alongside Iraq and Bahrain and behind only Kuwait at -86% and the UAE at -69%. THE DOHA MOVEMENT THIS CYCLE WAS DIPLOMATIC AND IT WAS DELIVERED: Prime Minister and Foreign Minister SHEIKH MOHAMMED BIN ABDULRAHMAN BIN JASSIM AL THANI spent AUGUST 27 in Tehran, meeting Araghchi, Pezeshkian, Ghalibaf and security chief Mohsen Rezai on the temporary corridor, the joint mine-clearing proposal and the conditions for renewed US-Iran dialogue, saying 'dialogue and diplomatic solutions remain the optimal path for addressing disputes.' Rezai's answer was that Washington 'must first take practical steps to fulfil Iran's conditions.' Qatar brokered the June arrangement that briefly halted hostilities. || AUGUST 27 — Unchanged on the production side: the IMF April vintage projects Qatar at -14.7% for 2026, with roughly 17% of LNG export capacity lost and about $20bn of annual revenue foregone since the March Ras Laffan strike and the onset of Hormuz disruption. THE DOHA MOVEMENT THIS CYCLE IS DIPLOMATIC: Prime Minister and Foreign Minister SHEIKH MOHAMMED BIN ABDULRAHMAN BIN JASSIM AL THANI arrives in Tehran on THURSDAY AUGUST 27 for de-escalation talks with senior Iranian officials, focused on FREEDOM OF NAVIGATION IN THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ, with Doha's stated objective the restoration of navigation to the STATUS QUO THAT EXISTED BEFORE FEBRUARY 28 — a broader objective than the temporary Iranian-Omani corridor announced two days earlier, and the two tracks have not been reconciled. Downstream, LNG carrier rates remain around $300,000 a day, a 650% increase. (International Monetary Fund World Economic Outlook, April 2026 vintage; Qatar Tribune, August 26, 2026; Middle East Monitor, August 26, 2026; Zero Carbon Analytics and Gulf International Forum shipping-rate compilations.) || AUGUST 24 — Unchanged: the IMF April vintage projects Qatar at -14.7% for 2026, with roughly 17% of LNG export capacity lost and about $20bn of annual revenue foregone since the March Ras Laffan strike and the onset of Hormuz transit difficulty. South Korea and Japan continue to face critical LNG deficits from Qatari and Emirati supply; South Korea is the only affected state responding with concrete renewable-expansion plans. || AUGUST 22 — QATAR IS TIGHTENING PUBLIC SPENDING AGAINST A PROJECTED 14.7% CONTRACTION IN 2026 (IMF, April vintage). The March Ras Laffan strike and Hormuz transit difficulty have removed an estimated 17% OF LNG EXPORT CAPACITY, implying annual revenue losses of about $20 BILLION. Doha recorded a FIRST-QUARTER BUDGET DEFICIT OF QR10.3 BILLION ($2.83 BILLION) — MORE THAN TWENTY TIMES the year-earlier shortfall — on GOVERNMENT REVENUES DOWN 23.5% YEAR ON YEAR. Unshipped gas is being flared rather than exported. (GCC Business News, 'Qatar's LNG crisis threatens to deepen fiscal pressure'; Stimson Center, 'Iran Conflict Hits Foundations of Gulf Economies'; IMF Regional Economic Outlook, April 2026.) || AUGUST 2026 — QATAR IS NOW FORECAST TO CONTRACT OUTRIGHT IN 2026, alongside Kuwait the deepest-hit of the Gulf economies. Iran's MARCH 2026 STRIKE ON THE RAS LAFFAN LNG COMPLEX eliminated AN ESTIMATED 17% OF QATAR'S LNG EXPORT CAPACITY, and QatarEnergy's force majeure declaration at Ras Laffan — the world's largest LNG liquefaction facility — stands. Roughly 20% of global LNG supply transits the Strait of Hormuz. Downstream, Qatar and the UAE account for 99% of Pakistan's LNG imports, 72% of Bangladesh's and 53% of India's; South Korea takes 14% of its LNG from the two and Japan 6%. (AGBI, 'Kuwait and Qatar likely to face deepest economic hit from war,' August 2026; The Conversation; Stimson Center; Wood Mackenzie; Zero Carbon Analytics.) || AUGUST 1 — QATAR CANNOT MEET ITS LONG-TERM ASIAN DELIVERY SCHEDULES. The world's leading LNG exporter is reported unable to fulfil long-term delivery contracts to Asia on time, producing a ripple of energy deficits from Seoul to Singapore. South Korea and Japan face critical deficits in LNG supply from Qatar and the UAE; Qatar and the UAE together account for 99% of Pakistan's LNG imports, and Pakistan and Bangladesh are assessed as especially vulnerable because of limited storage and procurement flexibility. Vulnerability scoring puts Japan highest at 6.4, South Korea at 5.3 and India at 4.9. (Gulf International Forum; Zero Carbon Analytics; ORF Middle East; S&P Global, 2026.) || 0 (halted); Ras Laffan damaged",
   "change": "AUGUST 28 — Production unchanged: IMF projects Qatar at -14.7% for 2026, ~17% of LNG export capacity lost, ~$20bn of annual revenue foregone. Port calls -66 to -68%. Qatar's PM delivered his Tehran visit on August 27, meeting Araghchi, Pezeshkian, Ghalibaf and Rezai; Rezai restated that the US must first take practical steps. || AUGUST 27 — Production unchanged: IMF projects Qatar at -14.7% for 2026, ~17% of LNG export capacity lost, ~$20bn of annual revenue foregone. Doha's move is diplomatic: the Qatari PM arrives in Tehran today for de-escalation talks on freedom of navigation, seeking a return to the pre-February 28 status quo — broader than the Iranian-Omani corridor. LNG carrier rates ~$300,000/day (+650%). || AUGUST 24 — Unchanged: IMF projects Qatar at -14.7% for 2026; ~17% of LNG export capacity lost and ~$20bn of annual revenue foregone. South Korea and Japan still facing critical LNG deficits.",
