   "name": "Ukraine Territory Occupied",
   "value": "AUGUST 27 — NET RUSSIAN GAINS FOR 2026 REMAIN 81.1 SQUARE KILOMETRES AND THE LINE IS EFFECTIVELY STATIC, WHILE THE STRIKE WAR INTENSIFIED AND THE INTELLIGENCE CHANNEL CARRIED A NATO WARNING. Overnight into AUGUST 27 Russia struck Kyiv and other Ukrainian cities with ballistic missiles and drones; several consecutive explosions were heard in Kyiv around 3 a.m., at least six inside about a minute, injuring AT LEAST SEVEN PEOPLE, with Foreign Minister ANDRII SYBIHA reporting targets including warehouses, ports, and energy and industrial facilities. Ukrainian air defences downed SEVEN BALLISTIC MISSILES, ONE ANTI-SHIP CRUISE MISSILE and NEARLY 90% OF THE DRONES. Ukraine's deep-strike campaign reached the Russian refining base: the NORSI plant at KSTOVO in Nizhny Novgorod Oblast — Russia's FOURTH-LARGEST REFINERY and SECOND-LARGEST GASOLINE PRODUCER — SUSPENDED CRUDE PROCESSING after an overnight drone strike, its FIFTH DRONE-RELATED SHUTDOWN IN UNDER FIVE MONTHS, and Ukrainian drones are reported to have shut ALL MAJOR LUKOIL REFINERIES. A UKRAINIAN STRIKE HIT A BUS IN RUSSIAN-CONTROLLED LUHANSK, KILLING NINE. The diplomatic channel remains shut on Ukraine itself while CIA Director JOHN RATCLIFFE's August 25 Moscow visit was reported on August 27 to have carried a warning against escalating tensions with Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania and a request that Russia reduce support for Iran; Peskov confirmed the meeting was with Russian intelligence representatives and not with Putin. EU states are planning a renewed effort to leverage frozen Russian sovereign assets for Ukraine funding. NO NATO MEMBER HAS COMMITTED GROUND TROOPS. || AUGUST 25-26 — NET RUSSIAN GAINS FOR 2026 REMAIN 81.1 SQUARE KILOMETRES AND THE LINE IS EFFECTIVELY STATIC, WHILE THE STRIKE WAR CONTINUED AND THE DIPLOMATIC CHANNEL INVERTED. Overnight into AUGUST 26 Russia launched 163 DRONES, of which Ukrainian air defences downed 132, with strikes recorded at 18 LOCATIONS and debris at three; an earlier partial morning count carried 150 drones plus one ISKANDER-M and one KH-31P, with 124 downed by 9 a.m. Russian attacks over AUGUST 25-26 KILLED FOUR AND INJURED 95: DONETSK 1 killed / 8 injured with KRAMATORSK carrying 26 injured and 37 high-rises damaged; CHERNIHIV 2 killed — a 39-year-old woman and her four-year-old daughter — and 14 injured across 28 attacks; KHERSON 1 killed / 12 injured; KHARKIV 23 injured including two children; ZAPORIZHZHIA 9 injured across 935 attacks on 47 settlements; DNIPROPETROVSK 6 injured. A Russian FPV drone conducted a DOUBLE-TAP on rescue workers in Kramatorsk, striking an armoured State Emergency Service ambulance — the second attack on rescuers that week after the Kryvyi Rih shopping-centre strike of August 21. ISW's AUGUST 25 assessment records Russia ESCALATING STRIKES ON UKRAINIAN-MOLDOVAN BORDER INFRASTRUCTURE alongside its campaign against port and maritime infrastructure, the Kremlin managing concerns over Putin's decree setting conditions for the nationalisation of critical infrastructure, and a further military satellite launched on August 24 possibly tied to the RASSVET Starlink analogue; the AUGUST 24 assessment records a Ukrainian SEAD/DEAD campaign against Russian air defences in border regions and Ukrainian advances in the SLOVYANSK and HULYAIPOLE directions and WESTERN ZAPORIZHIA OBLAST. Ukraine struck a WILDBERRIES logistics hub in TAMBOV OBLAST, with fire across more than 100,000 square metres. Cumulative Russian personnel losses per the Ukrainian General Staff stand at approximately 1,480,750, including about 1,450 in 24 hours. (The Kyiv Independent, August 26, 2026; ISW via Kyiv Post, August 24-25, 2026; Ukrainian General Staff, August 26, 2026.) || AUGUST 24 — NET RUSSIAN GAINS FOR 2026 REMAIN 81.1 SQUARE KILOMETRES AND THE LINE IS EFFECTIVELY STATIC, while the strike war escalated for the anniversary. Overnight into AUGUST 24 Russia launched TWO ONIKS anti-ship missiles, SIX KH-31P AND KH-59 guided air-launched missiles and 143 DRONES, roughly a third of them jet-powered; Ukrainian air defences downed 109 drones, one Oniks, two Kh-31P/Kh-59 and two Banderol munitions, with strikes recorded at 22 locations and debris at five. Eight people were killed and 43 injured across six oblasts: Kharkiv 5 killed and 23 injured including two children, Donetsk 2 killed and 7 injured, Sumy 1 killed and 12 injured, Zaporizhzhia and Dnipropetrovsk 3 injured each, Chernihiv 1 injured. ISW assessed Russian advances EAST OF SLOVYANSK, shaping for frontal assaults toward the city and the Fortress Belt, and recorded an AUGUST 23 Ukraine-US-Europe proposal offering significant Ukrainian concessions that the Kremlin has not reciprocated. || AUGUST 23 — NET RUSSIAN GAINS FOR 2026 REMAIN 81.1 SQUARE KILOMETRES AND THE LINE IS EFFECTIVELY STATIC. The August 23 kinetic record is two-sided and routine by this war's standards: Russian guided bombs struck IZYUM, wounding two people and damaging at least twenty buildings, vehicles and power lines, while Ukrainian forces struck A RADAR STATION AND A SITE USED TO STORE AND LAUNCH UNMANNED SURFACE VESSELS in temporarily occupied CRIMEA overnight. Ukraine's General Staff put cumulative Russian personnel losses since February 2022 at ABOUT 1,476,580 as of August 23; the figure is a Ukrainian claim and is recorded as such. On the diplomatic track, RYABKOV said on AUGUST 21 that Moscow will listen to 'new ideas' that align with Putin's position and with 'realities on the ground,' while ZELENSKY said Ukraine has developed a plan with US and European partners envisaging a ceasefire, a free economic zone and a withdrawal of troops. NORTH KOREAN DRONE OPERATORS remain reported in KURSK OBLAST. (ISW, August 21-23, 2026; Ukrinform, August 23, 2026; Al Jazeera, August 21, 2026.) || AUGUST 22 — NET RUSSIAN GAINS FOR 2026 STAND AT 81.1 SQUARE KILOMETRES, a figure depressed in part by the Ukrainian spring-summer counteroffensive. The line is effectively static. ISW's August 21 assessment records Russia launching 135 long-range strike drones, Ukrainian strikes on Russian military airfields and oil infrastructure, North Korean drone operators deployed to Kursk Oblast, and a senior Russian official reiterating unwillingness to negotiate meaningfully. (Institute for the Study of War, August 21, 2026, via Kyiv Post; Critical Threats, August 2, 2026.) || AUGUST 2026 — THE LINE STILL DOES NOT MOVE, AND RUSSIA HAS SHIFTED ITS EFFORT TO THE GRID. Ukrainian forces recently advanced northeast of KHARKIV CITY. ISW's standing assessment that Russia's SPRING-SUMMER 2026 OFFENSIVE HAS FAILED TO ACHIEVE OPERATIONALLY SIGNIFICANT GAINS has not been revised, and its JULY 31 finding that Russia's NORTHERN GROUPING OF FORCES AND HIGH COMMAND ARE LIKELY MISREPORTING GAINS TO THE KREMLIN — with battlefield evidence failing to corroborate claims of advances in northern SUMY and KHARKIV oblasts — stands. The substitute for territorial progress is the civilian energy system: Ukraine's Strategic Communications Centre reported on AUGUST 8, citing US intelligence, that Russia is preparing A MAJOR STRIKE CAMPAIGN AGAINST KYIV'S ENERGY INFRASTRUCTURE AHEAD OF INDEPENDENCE DAY ON AUGUST 24, and ZELENSKY SAID THE SAME DAY THAT UKRAINE HAS EFFECTIVELY NO UNDAMAGED POWER PLANTS LEFT. (ISW, 'Russian Offensive Campaign Assessment,' August 8, 10 and 20, 2026, via Kyiv Post and Critical Threats.) || JULY 31 — ~20% OCCUPIED WITH THE SLOWEST MONTHLY CHANGE OF THE CURRENT PHASE, AND THE WAR'S ORDNANCE ON NATO SOIL. The Economist estimated Russia gained ZERO SQUARE MILES over the thirty days to July 28; DeepState OSINT data put the net gain at TEN SQUARE MILES over the four weeks from June 30 to July 28, against thirty-one in the preceding four-week period. Ukraine's General Staff recorded 226 COMBAT CLASHES by 22:00 on July 30, heaviest on the KOSTIANTYNIVKA and POKROVSK axes. Separately, a projectile assessed as a RUSSIAN KH-101 crossed into POLISH airspace and detonated near TARNAWA-KOLONIA outside Lublin, leaving a ten-metre crater; NATO scrambled fighters, activated air defences and said it would 'defend territory.' (Russia Matters, July 29, 2026; ISW/Critical Threats, July 30, 2026; The Washington Post; Al Jazeera; Foreign Policy, July 30, 2026.) || JULY 28 — ~20% occupied with no confirmed net change. Ukraine's General Staff recorded 226 combat engagements on July 28; the POKROVSK sector remained the hottest with 29 Russian assaults, and Ukrainian forces repelled 27 assaults in the KOSTIANTYNIVKA sector near Kostiantynivka, Illinivka and toward Stepanivka. This follows the Khortytsia group's July 27 confirmation, via Suspilne, of Russian advances between Pokrovsk and Kostiantynivka. ISW assesses Russian forces may achieve tactical gains at Kostiantynivka this summer but are unlikely to achieve operational victories against the Donetsk 'belt of fortresses,' and lack the density for simultaneous offensives against both towns. Diplomatically, Zelensky met Trump in the Oval Office on July 28 carrying an AIR-ONLY ceasefire proposal; Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov denied any aerial-ceasefire talks are under way. The Senate advanced the 'Lindsey O. Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act of 2026' on a procedural vote the same day. (Ukrinform; ISW/Critical Threats; Al Jazeera; Reuters; CNBC, July 28, 2026.) || JULY 27 — ~20% occupied with no confirmed net change. Ukraine's Khortytsia group confirmed Russian advances in the area between Pokrovsk and Kostiantynivka in Donetsk Oblast as the summer offensive continues; the Pokrovsk axis remains Russia's main effort, with DeepState and front-line accounts reporting Russian forces inside Pokrovsk and a 'search and destruction' operation under way. ISW's standing assessment is that Russian forces lack the density to sustain simultaneous offensives against both towns and that Kostiantynivka gains remain small-group infiltration. Zelensky meets Trump at the White House July 28 with an air-ceasefire proposal, and warns of 30,000 more North Korean troops staging via Voronezh. (Ukrinform/Yahoo; Critical Threats/ISW; Kyiv Post; Just Security, July 27, 2026.) || JULY 26 — Russia holds ~20% with no confirmed net change; Kostiantynivka remains CONTESTED. Overnight into July 26 Ukraine struck a Chornomornaftogaz facility and Russian strike-drone relay equipment in occupied Crimea and a bridge in occupied Donetsk Oblast; Russian attacks across Ukraine killed at least 7 civilians and injured 58 over the preceding day, with ballistic missiles striking a Kyiv residential building. Ukraine's General Staff put cumulative Russian losses at 1,438,990 since Feb 24, 2022. Zelensky warned Putin is 'preparing the conditions for expanding mobilization' and is seeking 30,000 more North Korean troops. (Kyiv Independent; UPI, July 25-26, 2026.) || JULY 13 — Russia holds ~20% with no confirmed net change; Kostiantynivka stays CONTESTED (Russia's July 3 'liberation' claim denied by Kyiv; ISW: infiltration, not control). Overnight July 12 Ukraine struck the Syzran refinery (Samara, ~800 km) and reportedly hit 10 tankers and 4 ferries in the Sea of Azov. || JULY 8 — Russia holds ~20% with no confirmed net change; Kostiantynivka stays contested. Ukrainian drones struck Russia's Saratov oil refinery and a Tatarstan petrochemical plant, hit 9 tankers in the Sea of Azov, and struck a Voronezh air base (Su-34/Su-35); Russia cut Kerch Bridge rail service (11 of 18 routes cancelled from July 8). At the NATO Ankara summit no US ground-deployment surge was announced; the Pentagon force-posture review may DRAW DOWN US forces in Eastern Europe. (Russia Matters, 'War Report Card, July 8, 2026'; NATO Ankara summit.) || JULY 6-7 — Russia holds ~20% with no confirmed net change; Kostiantynivka contested (ISW: ~37% Russian presence, ~4.3% of Lyman; Putin's 96% claim 'greatly exaggerated'). Ukraine struck Omsk (deepest of the war); Russia hit Kyiv (~22 killed). (ISW; NPR, July 6, 2026.) || MAY 30-31 — ~20% occupied (broadly unchanged). The Pokrovsk sector remains the heaviest along the front; Russia has been unable to make significant advances west of Pokrovsk/Myrnohrad since December 2025. A second direct Russia-Ukraine round remains expected ~June 2; POW/humanitarian-track exchanges continue. (Wikipedia 'Pokrovsk offensive'; Russia Matters; Ukrinform, May 2026). || MAY 29 — ~20% occupied (broadly unchanged). Ukraine's General Staff reported 189 combat engagements May 28, with the most active Russian assaults on the Pokrovsk + Huliaipole sectors; Zelensky-announced long-range operations continue; the Soufan Center (May 28) flagged a possible turning point (slowing Russian advances + Ukraine's drone edge) while warning Ukraine could struggle without sustained US/European support; second direct round of talks still expected ~June 2. || PRIOR MAY 28 — ~20% occupied (broadly unchanged). Ukraine's General Staff reported 256 combat engagements May 27 with nearly 50 in the Pokrovsk sector; Zelensky announced new long-range operations after meeting Syrskyi + Hnatov. The Soufan Center (May 28) assessed Russia's slowing advances + Ukraine's reestablished drone advantage may mark a turning point, but Ukraine could struggle without sustained US-European support. Russia still demands all of Donbas + NATO denial; Ukraine rules out ceding territory; second direct round of talks expected ~June 2. || PRIOR ~20% — MAY 7: Russia shot down 347 UKRAINIAN DRONES OVERNIGHT — Ukraine's 2nd-largest aerial attack since invasion; drones destroyed over 20 Russian regions including Moscow. Pokrovsk-direction Russian advance attempts: Nikanorivka, Rodinske, Novooleksandrivka, Shevchenko, Pokrovsk, Grishine, Sergiyivka, Molodetske; Russian forces 5,649 kamikaze-drone uses + 1,862 settlement/position shellings. Russia unilateral MAY 8-9 Victory Day ceasefire begins May 8 with 'massive strike on Kyiv' threat backstop. Moscow May 9 parade WITHOUT TANKS/MISSILES first time in ~2 decades (Ukrainian drone fears); mobile internet + texting to be shut down in Moscow May 9. PRIOR MAY 6: Russia +1,050 personnel to ~1,337,170 total (Ukrainian General Staff); Russia broke Ukraine's unilateral May 6 ceasefire within minutes (108 drones + 3 missiles + 2 BMs + 1 Kh-31; 26 civilians killed + 118+ injured); Pokrovsk 34 attacks May 5 (155 combat clashes total).",
   "change": "AUGUST 27 — Net Russian gains hold at 81.1 km² for 2026; line static. Kyiv struck overnight by ballistic missiles and drones, at least seven injured; Ukraine downed 7 ballistic missiles, 1 anti-ship cruise missile and ~90% of drones. NORSI refinery (4th-largest, 2nd-largest gasoline producer) suspended crude processing after a drone strike, its 5th shutdown in under five months; all major Lukoil refineries reported shut. Ukrainian strike on a bus in Russian-controlled Luhansk killed nine. Ratcliffe's Moscow agenda disclosed: Baltic warning plus an Iran decoupling request. || AUGUST 25-26 — Net Russian gains hold at 81.1 km² for 2026; line static. 163 drones overnight into August 26, 132 downed, 18 locations struck. Russian attacks killed 4 and injured 95 across six oblasts; FPV double-tap on rescuers in Kramatorsk. Ukraine hit a Wildberries hub in Tambov Oblast. ISW: Russian escalation on the Ukrainian-Moldovan border; Ukrainian advances near Slovyansk and Hulyaipole. Cumulative Russian personnel losses ~1,480,750. || AUGUST 24 — Net Russian gains hold at 81.1 km² for 2026; line static. Independence Day barrage: 2 Oniks, 6 Kh-31P/Kh-59, 143 drones; 109 drones and 5 missiles downed; 8 killed, 43 injured across six oblasts. ISW records advances east of Slovyansk and an August 23 Ukrainian concession proposal unreciprocated.",
