  {
   "name": "Cuba Fuel Imports",
   "value": "AUGUST 27-28 — NO NEW GRID COLLAPSE WAS REPORTED AND NO NEW FIGURE WAS PUBLISHED. The record therefore stands: Cuba's national electricity system has collapsed SIX TIMES IN 2026 — twice in March, three times within ten days in July and once on AUGUST 2-3, when thunderstorms disrupted the restoration attempt and produced two nationwide outages within hours — with blackouts EXCEEDING TWENTY HOURS A DAY and some communities at up to 105 CONSECUTIVE DAYS without steady power. Neighbourhood protests have been recorded in Havana municipalities after outages beyond forty-eight hours. The grid remains fragile after repeated collapses, worsened by fuel shortages tied to US sanctions and to the collapse of Venezuelan supply. THE SUPPLIER IS NOW IN THE SAME CONDITION: Venezuela is running daily cuts of four to five hours with government-imposed rationing, pot-banging protests, and road blockades and burning tyres recorded in Turmero, Aragua. || AUGUST 25-27 — NO NEW GRID COLLAPSE WAS REPORTED AND THE PUBLIC-ORDER DIMENSION IS ENTERED. Cuba's national electricity system has collapsed SIX TIMES IN 2026 — twice in March, three times within ten days in July and once on AUGUST 2-3 — with blackouts EXCEEDING TWENTY HOURS A DAY and some communities recorded at UP TO 105 CONSECUTIVE DAYS without steady electricity. NEWLY DOCUMENTED: in several municipalities, particularly in HAVANA, residents staged NEIGHBOURHOOD PROTESTS after blackouts lasting MORE THAN 48 HOURS, banging pots and pans and burning rubbish piled on street corners. The island lost its principal fuel supplier after the JANUARY 3 seizure of Maduro and the subsequent US oil blockade, with Mexican imports also halted under Washington's pressure; sanctions run under EXECUTIVE ORDER 14404, Visa and Mastercard processing has been lost, and the United Nations describes a humanitarian emergency. Foreign Minister BRUNO RODRÍGUEZ says there has been 'NO PROGRESS.' THE SAME CRISIS HAS NOW CROSSED TO THE SUPPLIER: pot-banging protests have multiplied across VENEZUELA, with road blockades and burning tyres in TURMERO, ARAGUA against four-to-five-hour daily cuts and government rationing. (The Washington Times, August 7, 2026; Wikipedia compilation '2024-2026 Cuba blackouts'; Venezuelanalysis and Cuba Headlines, August 2026.) || AUGUST 24 — NO NEW GRID EVENT REPORTED, AND THE CUMULATIVE FIGURES ARE CONSOLIDATED. Cuba's national electricity system has collapsed SIX TIMES IN 2026 — twice in March, three times within ten days in July and once on AUGUST 2-3. Reporting describes blackouts EXCEEDING TWENTY HOURS A DAY, with some communities recorded at UP TO 105 CONSECUTIVE DAYS without steady electricity. The island lost its principal fuel supplier after the JANUARY 3 seizure of Maduro and the subsequent US oil blockade, with Mexican imports also halted under Washington's pressure; sanctions run under EXECUTIVE ORDER 14404, Visa and Mastercard processing has been lost, and the United Nations describes a humanitarian emergency endangering lives. Foreign Minister BRUNO RODRÍGUEZ said there has been 'NO PROGRESS' toward ending the impasse. Industrial output and transport remain crippled. || AUGUST 22 — NO NEW GRID EVENT REPORTED THIS CYCLE. The island is still recovering from the SIXTH NATIONWIDE BLACKOUT OF 2026 on AUGUST 2-3, when the national electricity system collapsed late on Sunday and thunderstorms disrupted the restoration effort the following day, taking generating units offline again. The run of collapses dates from the Trump administration's effective severing of Cuba from its main oil source in January; roughly ten million people were affected, and Havana residents have been sleeping on the Malecón to escape the heat during outages. (CNN, August 3, 2026; Al Jazeera, August 3, 2026; PBS NewsHour, August 2026.) || AUGUST 2-3 — CUBA'S NATIONAL GRID COLLAPSED LATE ON AUGUST 2, THE ISLAND'S SIXTH NATIONWIDE BLACKOUT OF 2026, plunging about ten million people into darkness; thunderstorms then disrupted the restoration effort on AUGUST 3. The grid had already failed THREE TIMES IN NINE DAYS IN JULY. The proximate cause remains fuel: Venezuela, historically Cuba's principal supplier of crude and petroleum products, has cut back under US pressure and Mexican imports have ceased, while ageing thermoelectric plants break down repeatedly for want of parts. The Energy and Mines Ministry's earlier statement that all fuel reserves are exhausted stands, with blackouts of 20-22 hours a day in some areas. ON AUGUST 20 the US Treasury and State Department imposed FRESH SANCTIONS ON NINE CUBAN STATE-OWNED COMPANIES AND THE LEADERSHIP OF THE CUBAN INSTITUTE, targeting the mining and construction sectors. (CNN, 'Storms disrupt Cuba's power restoration,' August 3, 2026; Al Jazeera, August 3, 2026; NBC News; AP News via Just Security Early Edition, August 21, 2026.) || AUGUST 1 — ALL FUEL RESERVES REPORTED EXHAUSTED. Cuba's energy and mines ministry states that fuel reserves are now exhausted, with some areas experiencing electricity blackouts of 20 to 22 hours a day. The island has suffered its third nationwide outage of 2026 and its eighth since late 2024, with transport, hospitals, water supply, industry and public services disrupted and shortages of food, drinking water and medicine deepening. The causal chain is unchanged: the US de facto oil blockade and the interdiction of Venezuelan oil begun in February 2026, compounded by reported reductions in Venezuelan and Mexican supply. Colombia's incoming president severs relations with Havana from August 7. (Caribbean National Weekly; Al Jazeera; PBS NewsHour; Class24, 2026.) || JULY 28 — No relief. Cuba has now suffered its FOURTH island-wide blackout of 2026, leaving about 10 million people without power, under the combination of the halted Venezuelan crude supply after Maduro's January capture and Executive Order 14380 of January 29, which authorises tariffs on any country directly or indirectly supplying Cuba with fuel; the blocked shipments had covered about 60% of the roughly 100,000 barrels a day the island's power system requires. Havana's foreign ministry separately rejected Colombian president-elect Abelardo de la Espriella's announced closure of Colombia's Havana embassy and severing of bilateral ties ahead of the August 7 handover. (Newsweek; Al Jazeera; Últimas Noticias; TechTimes, July 2026.) || JULY 27 — No relief. Cuba's national grid has collapsed repeatedly through July — twice within five days in early July and again mid-month — under the combination of the halted Venezuelan crude supply after Maduro's January capture and Executive Order 14380 of January 29, which authorises tariffs on any country directly or indirectly supplying Cuba with fuel. The blocked shipments had covered about 60% of the roughly 100,000 barrels a day the island's power system requires. (Democracy Now!; TechTimes; OilPrice; Bloomberg, July 2026.) || JULY 15 — Cuba's national grid COLLAPSED AGAIN on July 14 (~11am local) — the THIRD nationwide blackout in under 10 days and the fifth total outage of 2026 — leaving ~10 million without power. FM Bruno Rodríguez told the UN the US fuel embargo and sanctions amounted to a 'systematic violation of the human rights of an entire people' / 'collective punishment.' The blackout is driven by severe fuel shortages from the loss of Venezuelan oil after Maduro's January removal, the US oil blockade, and aging generation infrastructure — a second-order effect of US economic pressure applied WITHOUT US ground manpower. (Al Jazeera; WHBL; Cubaheadlines; YourNews, July 14, 2026.) || JULY 13 — Cuba suffered a SECOND island-wide blackout in under a week (began ~4:30pm local July 10; fourth total outage of 2026), with only ~935 MW of capacity against ~3,100 MW demand; public transport largely halted and tens of thousands of surgeries were canceled. The US oil blockade and the Venezuelan-crude cutoff remain the drivers. No US ground-force move. || JULY 8-9 — Cuba continued restarting its national grid after the July 6 island-wide collapse (the third nationwide blackout in six months); the underlying crisis — the US oil blockade and the cutoff of Venezuelan crude — is unresolved. No US ground-force move. (NBC/CBS Cuba coverage, July 6-8, 2026.) || JULY 6-8 — Cuba's national grid TOTALLY COLLAPSED at midday July 6 (third nationwide blackout in six months / fourth total outage of 2026), cutting power to ~10 million; UNE served ~1% of Havana's demand by late afternoon, with restoration underway July 7-8. Driven by the de facto US oil blockade and the cutoff of Venezuelan crude (Díaz-Canel: no fuel imports since January except one Russian cargo). (Washington Post; Bloomberg; Fox News, July 6, 2026.) || JULY 6-7 — An island-wide blackout took Cuba's entire national grid offline July 6; some areas endure 20-22 hr daily power cuts as the US oil blockade and the cutoff of Venezuelan crude deepen the energy crisis. (Bloomberg; Washington Post, July 6, 2026.) || MAY 20-21 — Cuba's grid still in severe crisis after eastern-provinces collapse May 14 and a 5th nationwide grid collapse reported May 18 (NaturalNews citing Cuban energy authorities); diesel and fuel-oil stocks 'virtually run out' per Energy Minister De La O'levy (May 14). US Embassy in Havana May 19-20 reissued a security alert calling the national electric grid 'increasingly unstable.' Russian Universal tanker (270K bbl diesel) still adrift in North Atlantic; no confirmed arrival May 20-21. PRIOR MAY 19-20 — Cuban Energy and Mines Minister Vincent De La O'levy (May 14) warned diesel and fuel-oil stocks have 'virtually run out'; the government acknowledges 'almost nothing' is left; eastern provinces hit by a major grid collapse May 14 plunging Holguin/Granma/Santiago into hours of blackout (NPR). Power-cuts of 20-22 hours/day reported in some areas. US fuel-tanker blockade since February 2026 the proximate driver; ongoing humanitarian impact per UN OHCHR (food supply, water systems, hospitals threatened). -100% — MAY 14 ENERGY MIN VICENTE DE LA O: 'WE HAVE ABSOLUTELY NO FUEL (OIL) AND ABSOLUTELY NO DIESEL' — ANTONIO GUITERAS LARGEST POWER PLANT SHUT DOWN MAY 14 AT 4:58AM (BOILER LEAK); SEN AVAILABLE 636 MW VS DEMAND 2,420 MW (74% UNMET, 1,790 MW DEFICIT) — POWER OUTAGES 24 CONSECUTIVE HOURS IN HAVANA; ALL EASTERN PROVINCES GUANTÁNAMO TO CIEGO DE ÁVILA STRIPPED OF POWER — RUSSIAN UNIVERSAL TANKER (270K BBL DIESEL) ETA PUSHED TO MAY 15; STILL ADRIFT ~1,600KM FROM CUBA — TRUMP MAY 12 'CUBA IS ASKING FOR HELP, AND WE ARE GOING TO TALK!!' TRUTH SOCIAL POST — CNN MAY 13: CUBA PULLING OFF ONE OF FASTEST SOLAR REVOLUTIONS ON PLANET WITH CHINA'S HELP — Cuban output 40K BPD vs demand 90-110K BPD",
   "change": "AUGUST 27-28 — No new grid collapse and no new figure published. Six nationwide collapses in 2026; blackouts >20 hours/day; up to 105 consecutive days without steady power; Havana neighbourhood protests after outages beyond 48 hours. Venezuela, the supplier, is itself rationing at four-to-five-hour daily cuts with street protest. || AUGUST 25-27 — No new grid collapse. Six nationwide collapses in 2026; blackouts >20 hours/day; up to 105 consecutive days without steady power in some communities. NEW: neighbourhood protests in Havana municipalities after outages exceeding 48 hours. The crisis has crossed to the supplier — pot-banging protests, road blockades and burning tyres in Turmero, Aragua, Venezuela. || AUGUST 24 — No new grid event. Consolidated: six nationwide collapses in 2026, blackouts >20 hours/day, some communities up to 105 consecutive days without steady power; FM Bruno Rodríguez reports 'no progress.'",
   "change_color": "var(--danger, #d64545)",
