  {
   "name": "Saudi Oil Capacity Impact",
   "value": "AUGUST 27 — RIYADH IS THE LEAST-AFFECTED GULF EXPORTER AND ITS BYPASS IS NOW UNDER FIRE. Al Jazeera's August 27 dataset puts Saudi daily port calls DOWN ONLY 15%, against Kuwait at -86%, the UAE at -69% and Qatar, Iraq and Bahrain at -66 to -68%, because Saudi Arabia alone has pipeline capacity and a Red Sea coast. THE RED SEA TERMINUS WAS ATTACKED THE SAME DAY: an air-defence system operated by GREEK MILITARY PERSONNEL in Saudi Arabia engaged a DRONE SWARM in the wider YANBU oil-refining region, launching THREE MISSILES and intercepting THREE HOUTHI UAVs in full coordination with Saudi air defences — the THIRD COMBAT USE of the Greek-operated system since the war began. Yanbu is the Red Sea terminus of the East-West pipeline, the principal Saudi route around Hormuz, so the insulation measured in the port-call data is itself now a target set. Riyadh continues to run SHIP-TO-SHIP TRANSFERS with GOVERNMENT-BACKED INSURANCE alongside the Franco-Saudi Hormuz bypass programme advanced in Paris on August 24. Suez freight runs $10-11 a barrel against roughly $5 via Yanbu before the Houthi campaign. (Al Jazeera, August 27, 2026; Jerusalem Post and Greek City Times, August 27, 2026.) || AUGUST 25-27 — RIYADH IS UNDERWRITING ITS OWN WAR RISK AND MOVING BARRELS OUTSIDE THE CHOKEPOINT. Saudi Arabia is running SHIP-TO-SHIP TRANSFERS with GOVERNMENT-BACKED INSURANCE, alongside the Hormuz bypass programme advanced at the Franco-Saudi Strategic Partnership Council in Paris on AUGUST 24 (Omani ports, doubled pipeline capacity, rail). TANKERTRACKERS counted MORE THAN FIFTEEN SHIP-TO-SHIP TRANSFER OPERATIONS involving ABOUT 25 MILLION BARRELS of crude in the Gulf of Oman, originating from 'EVERY COUNTRY IN THE REGION, MINUS IRAN.' The Red Sea flank remains contested after the AUGUST 24 Houthi ballistic-missile and drone strike on the Bahri VLCC AMZAN about 63 nautical miles west of Yanbu, declared a 'siege for siege' operation; no new Saudi-flagged casualty was reported in this cycle. The freight economics of the detour are now quantified: Suez routing costs roughly $10-11 A BARREL against about $5 via YANBU before the Houthi campaign. Riyadh continues to call for de-escalation and to promote the Pakistan-brokered track while asserting a right to self-defence, and has not endorsed Operation Economic Outcast. (Fox News Digital live updates, August 25, 2026; The National, 'Chart of the week: China's Iranian oil imports nearly halve since war,' August 26, 2026; everycrsreport.com R48971, August 2026; Al Jazeera, August 24-26, 2026.) || AUGUST 24-25 — RIYADH IS BUILDING THE BYPASS, DECLINING TO ENDORSE THE SANCTIONS, AND TAKING FIRE AT SEA. The Franco-Saudi Strategic Partnership Council met in Paris on AUGUST 24 with Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman and President Macron, with Hormuz alternatives as the headline file — increased trade through OMANI PORTS outside the Gulf, expanded and possibly DOUBLED PIPELINE CAPACITY, and new regional RAIL LINKS — alongside a standing France-Saudi joint task force on energy and logistics meeting at ministerial level. Riyadh and Paris urged a negotiated solution and a resumption of Iranian cooperation with the IAEA. YET THE GULF STATES THAT HAVE INVESTED BILLIONS IN BYPASS PIPELINES HAVE NOT COMMENTED PUBLICLY ON OPERATION ECONOMIC OUTCAST — a hedge the audit records. AND THE COST OF THAT POSITION ARRIVED THE SAME DAY: Yemen's Houthis struck the Saudi VLCC AMZAN, operated by the national shipping company BAHRI, about 63 NAUTICAL MILES WEST OF YANBU in the Red Sea with ballistic missiles and drones, starting a fire on the main deck and causing nearby vessels to flee; all crew are safe and no environmental impact was reported. Houthi military spokesman YAHYA SAREE described the strike as part of a 'SIEGE FOR SIEGE' operation targeting the Saudis, an explicit linkage between the Iran blockade and attacks on Saudi shipping. || AUGUST 23-24 — RIYADH IS IN PARIS BUILDING THE BYPASS AND TEHRAN HAS PUT THE BYPASS ON ITS TARGET LIST. Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman is in France for his first visit since 2023, meeting President Macron on AUGUST 24 at the Franco-Saudi Strategic Partnership Council with EXPANDED OIL PIPELINES AS AN ALTERNATIVE TO THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ as the principal file. French presidency officials describe the proposals as increasing trade through OMANI PORTS OUTSIDE THE PERSIAN GULF, EXPANDING AND POTENTIALLY DOUBLING PIPELINE CAPACITY in Saudi Arabia and other countries, and developing NEW RAIL LINKS; a FRANCE-SAUDI JOINT TASK FORCE on Middle East-Europe energy and logistics connections meets at MINISTERIAL LEVEL the same day, with agreements prepared on transport, energy, technology and health. On AUGUST 23 SNSC Secretary MOHSEN REZAEI said Iran would TARGET THOSE ALTERNATIVE OIL-SHIPPING ROUTES if regional states join the economic war, and warned via IRNA that Tehran would not allow 'a single drop of oil' to leave the Gulf if pressure escalates. IMF April-vintage projections are unchanged at −1.4% for Saudi Arabia in 2026, with Gulf energy-facility repairs estimated at about $58bn. (The National, August 22-23, 2026; Bloomberg, August 21, 2026; Energy Connects, August 2026; The Washington Post/AP, August 22-23, 2026.) || AUGUST 22 — SAUDI ARABIA IS PROJECTED AT −1.4% FOR 2026 (IMF, April vintage), the mildest contraction among the heavily exposed Gulf producers, insulated by non-oil sectors and bypass pipelines. THE PRESSURE THIS CYCLE IS FROM THE SOUTH AND FROM DIPLOMACY RATHER THAN FROM HORMUZ: the Houthis claimed drone strikes on AUGUST 20 against NAJRAN AIRPORT — described in reporting as hosting Patriot and other air-defence systems — and an ARAMCO FACILITY, following a claimed strike on the JAZAN REFINERY on AUGUST 18 and the JIZAN REFINERY FIRE of AUGUST 9. Riyadh is reported to be weighing a ground escalation in Yemen, and Yemeni government forces engaged Houthi positions northwest and south of Marib on AUGUST 22. Meanwhile CROWN PRINCE MOHAMMED BIN SALMAN TRAVELLED TO PARIS for an AUGUST 24 FRANCO-SAUDI STRATEGIC PARTNERSHIP COUNCIL devoted to HORMUZ ALTERNATIVES — maritime diversification, pipelines and regional rail. THE IEA AND RYSTAD PUT REPAIR COSTS FOR 80-PLUS DAMAGED GULF ENERGY FACILITIES AT ABOUT $58 BILLION. (Shafaq News, August 20, 2026; Al-Monitor, August 2026; Bloomberg, August 21, 2026; The National, August 22, 2026; Stimson Center, 2026.) || AUGUST 2026 — SAUDI ARABIA REMAINS AMONG THE BETTER-INSULATED GULF ECONOMIES, expected to keep growing at a slower pace, protected by a relatively large non-oil sector and by pipelines that bypass the Strait. THE THREAT HAS MOVED TO THE RED SEA FLANK: Houthi forces declared A NAVAL BLOCKADE OF SAUDI ARABIA IN THE RED SEA ON JULY 20, breaking a years-long civil-war truce and calling it retaliation for a Saudi 'siege' of Yemen, which Riyadh denies. On AUGUST 11-12 a Houthi missile attack on the Egyptian-owned cargo ship TIHAMAH in the BAB AL-MANDEB killed six — one Indonesian and three Pakistani crew, plus two Yemeni service personnel struck by a second missile during the rescue — the first shipping deaths attributed to the group since the war began; Houthi-aligned media said the vessel carried Saudi military equipment while Yemen's Transport Ministry said it carried food. On AUGUST 20 a Houthi military spokesman claimed drone attacks on NAJRAN AIRPORT and an ARAMCO FACILITY. Across the war roughly 80 Gulf energy facilities have been struck for an estimated $58 billion in damage, more than a third of them severely. (Al Jazeera, August 12, 2026; UPI; PBS NewsHour; Reuters via Just Security, August 21, 2026; The Conversation; AGBI.) || JULY 31 — RIYADH BUILDS AN ALLIANCE AROUND ITS ONLY REMAINING EXPORT ROUTE. Saudi Arabia's Defence Ministry convened MILITARY REPRESENTATIVES OF 43 COUNTRIES IN RIYADH ON JULY 30 to stand up a multinational MARITIME DEFENCE COALITION headquartered in the kingdom and focused on the BAB AL-MANDEB, the RED SEA and the GULF OF ADEN, with joint operations, intelligence sharing, training and exercises; THIRTEEN STATES publicly backed it — Kuwait, Bahrain, Qatar, Pakistan, Turkey, Egypt, Jordan, Yemen, Bangladesh, Nigeria, Sudan, Djibouti and Somalia. The driver is structural: YANBU, the outlet of the East-West Pipeline, handled roughly 92% OF SAUDI SEABORNE CRUDE EXPORTS IN JUNE 2026 after the closure of Hormuz, and every cargo loading there must transit the Bab al-Mandeb — inside the maritime blockade the Houthis declared on the kingdom on July 20. The contrast with Washington's Hormuz escort coalition, which still cannot obtain a British or French co-lead, is the cycle's clearest measure of who can raise a naval coalition and where. (The National; Al Jazeera; Bloomberg; The Hill; MarineLink; Dawn; POLITICO, July 30-31, 2026.) || JULY 29 — SAUDI ARABIA BECAME A SHOOTER ON BOTH FLANKS OF ITS OWN ENERGY COMPLEX. Maj. Gen. Turki Al-Maliki said Saudi air defences had 'intercepted and destroyed multiple drones launched from Iraqi territory aimed at vital petroleum facilities in the Kingdom's Eastern Province and Riyadh region,' and confirmed Saudi coordination with CENTCOM in joint precision strikes on militia logistics hubs and weapons storage sites in Iraq on July 28 — Riyadh's first claimed strikes inside Iraq since the war began, justified under Article 51 of the UN Charter, with the Kingdom stating it 'does not seek escalation but will respond to any aggression it faces.' On the Red Sea flank the Houthis fired ballistic missiles at the Saudi state-owned product tanker NCC Ghazal, forcing it to reverse course; UKMTO confirmed an explosion with the crew safe. Roughly 80 regional energy facilities have been struck and about $58bn of damage recorded, with the World Bank's 2026 GCC growth forecast still cut from 4.4% to 1.3%; Saudi Arabia continues shifting Red Sea export volumes toward Suez as Bab el-Mandeb exports run near zero. (CENTCOM public release; The Week; Al Jazeera; The Maritime Executive; The Conversation, July 28-29, 2026.) || JULY 28 — THE HOUTHIS CLAIMED THE NCC GHAZAL. Houthi military spokesman YAHYA SAREE said the group 'carried out a military operation against the Saudi oil tanker NCC Ghazal, due to violation of the maritime embargo imposed on the Saudi enemy and ignoring warning calls.' The 50,000 dwt Saudi-flagged, state-owned product tanker, built 2014, was struck by anti-ship missiles, turned around and returned to port; she had dropped off AIS tracking on July 23 after last being seen at Yanbu. Saudi air defences separately continued intercepting Iraq-launched drones aimed at petroleum facilities. Bab el-Mandeb transits fell to eleven vessels on July 26, the lowest monthly level recorded, and Riyadh is shifting export routing toward Suez. Yanbu — the East-West Pipeline outlet — handled ~92% of Saudi seaborne crude exports in June with Hormuz shut; the Jizan refinery processes 400,000 bpd. The Kingdom remains interdicted on both flanks. (The Maritime Executive; ABC News live blog; The National, July 26-28, 2026.) || JULY 27 — Riyadh began shifting crude export routing toward Suez as Houthi pressure drove Bab al-Mandeb oil exports toward zero; Bab el-Mandeb traffic fell to eleven vessels on Sunday July 26, its lowest monthly level. Saudi air defences separately intercepted drones launched from Iraq at petroleum facilities, attributed to Iran-backed militias. Yanbu — the East-West Pipeline outlet that handled ~92% of Saudi seaborne crude exports in June with Hormuz shut — and the 400,000 bpd Jizan refinery remain the July 25 Houthi strike targets. The Kingdom is now interdicted on both flanks. (The National; Just Security; CBS News, July 26-27, 2026.) || JULY 26 — The scale of the Red Sea exposure became explicit: YANBU, the outlet of the East-West Pipeline, handled approximately 92% of Saudi Arabia's seaborne crude exports in June 2026 — having become the Kingdom's only functioning crude-export route after Hormuz closed — and was struck by Houthi missiles and drones on July 25 alongside the Jizan refinery, which was set ablaze. With the Houthi maritime blockade of Saudi Arabia declared July 20 still in force, the Kingdom faces simultaneous interdiction on both flanks for the first time in its history, with no third corridor and no pipeline to an unthreatened port. (TechTimes; Al Jazeera; Bloomberg, July 20-26, 2026.) || JULY 25 — The Red Sea export 'relief valve' came under direct attack. Houthi military spokesman Brig. Gen. Yahya Saree said the group fired 'dozens of ballistic and cruise missiles and drones' at Aramco facilities in Jizan and Yanbu and that the operations 'successfully achieved their objectives'; CNN-geolocated video showed large fires in Jizan's industrial area, home to a major Aramco refinery. Saudi civil defence confirmed repeated alerts early Saturday and air defences intercepted further ballistic missiles later in the day; as of early Saturday neither Aramco nor the Saudi government had issued a damage assessment. Yanbu is Saudi Arabia's Red Sea export terminus and its principal remaining crude corridor with Hormuz functionally shut. This follows the July 20 Houthi declaration of a maritime blockade of Saudi Arabia and the July 23 attacks on two Saudi crude tankers. Standing prior damage: Iranian attacks on Manifa and Khurais cut ~600,000 bpd of production capacity and reduced East-West (Petroline) throughput by ~700,000 bpd. (Fortune; The Washington Post; Türkiye Today; CNN, July 20-25, 2026.) || JULY 22 — New RED SEA exposure: the Houthis declared a Bab el-Mandeb blockade and two Saudi crude tankers loaded at Yanbu for China/India U-turned toward Suez, threatening Saudi Arabia's Red Sea export 'relief valve' that had cushioned markets while Hormuz is shut. || -600,000 bpd production capacity; -700,000 bpd East-West pipeline",
   "change": "AUGUST 27 — Saudi port calls down only 15%, against Kuwait -86%, UAE -69% and Qatar/Iraq/Bahrain -66-68%, on pipeline and Red Sea access. But the bypass terminus was attacked: a Greek-operated Patriot battery in Saudi Arabia launched three missiles and downed three Houthi UAVs over the Yanbu refining region on August 27, the third combat use of the system since the war began. Ship-to-ship transfers with government-backed insurance continue; Suez freight $10-11/bbl against ~$5 via Yanbu pre-campaign. || AUGUST 25-27 — Saudi Arabia running ship-to-ship transfers with government-backed insurance alongside the Franco-Saudi Hormuz bypass programme. TankerTrackers: 15+ STS operations moving ~25m barrels in the Gulf of Oman, from 'every country in the region, minus Iran.' Suez freight ~$10-11/bbl against ~$5 via Yanbu pre-Houthi. No new Saudi-flagged casualty this cycle after the August 24 Amzan strike. Riyadh has not endorsed Operation Economic Outcast. || AUGUST 24-25 — Franco-Saudi council meets in Paris on Hormuz bypass (Omani ports, doubled pipeline capacity, rail) while Gulf bypass investors decline to comment on the sanctions. Houthis struck the Saudi VLCC Amzan off Yanbu with ballistic missiles and drones — 'siege for siege,' per Yahya Saree; crew safe.",
   "change_color": "var(--warning, #d68c45)",
   "as_of": "2026-08-28"
  },
  {
   "name": "Bahrain & Jordan Strikes (June 10-11)",
   "value": "JULY 25 — The Gulf host-states' role became contested. The Wall Street Journal ('Bahrain, Kuwait Warplanes Struck Iran in Rare Gulf Retaliation,' July 24) reported that Bahraini and Kuwaiti warplanes secretly struck Iranian missile and drone storage depots and other military installations earlier in July — the first direct Gulf Arab military action against Iran and, per the WSJ, a significant departure from the region's decades-long proxy-war playbook — with the UAE providing targeting intelligence and defensive air cover. Kuwait's ambassador to the United States, Sheikha Zain Al-Sabah, sent a formal letter to the paper's editorial board calling the report 'entirely false' and stating that Kuwait has not participated in any military operations against Iran nor allowed its territory, airspace or territorial waters to be used for offensive operations against any neighbouring country. Bahrain and the UAE have neither confirmed nor denied. Both states had absorbed months of Iranian missile and drone attacks tied to their hosting of US forces. CONTESTED-UNRESOLVED (open-question: gulf-states-direct-strikes-iran-july-2026). (WSJ via TASS/ANI/JPost; Arab Times, July 24-25, 2026.) || JULY 21 (Day 145) — Bahrain (home to the US Navy's Fifth Fleet) took fresh Iranian strikes: air-raid sirens and explosions in Manama, with 3 KILLED and 51 WOUNDED reported cumulatively. Jordan remained the site of the July 17 base strike that killed 2 US service members. (NPR; Casualties of the 2026 Iran war, July 20, 2026.) || JULY 13 — In Iran's July 12 multi-state retaliation, Iran again struck at US-partner states — Bahrain (air-raid sirens activated), Kuwait, Jordan, Qatar (3 wounded, incl. a child, from interceptor debris) and, for the first time in this cycle, Oman — most intercepted; the UAE said it engaged incoming missiles/UAVs. (Al Jazeera; Khaleej Times, July 12, 2026.) || JULY 9 — Iran hit Bahrain (fuel tanks) and Jordan (a US C2 node and the Al-Azraq air base; 10 ballistic missiles) as part of its four-state retaliation; Bahrain 'intercepted and destroyed several' aerial attacks, and Jordan shot down 8 missiles (debris fell, no casualties or property damage). (RFE/RL; The National; Al Jazeera, July 9, 2026.) ||  || JUNE 28-29 — Iran's IRGC struck the US Fifth Fleet headquarters (Port Salman) in Bahrain with ballistic missiles and drones (alongside the Ali Al Salem strike in Kuwait), the first Iranian attack on US Gulf bases since June 10-11; a US official said all Iranian weapons were intercepted or failed with no US injuries. Bahrain said the attacks 'undermined opportunities for de-escalation.' Separately, one Qatari civilian was killed by shrapnel from the operations (Qatar Interior Ministry). (Al Jazeera; RFE/RL; CBS News, June 28, 2026.) || JUNE 10-11 — In retaliation for the renewed US strikes, Iran hit US forces at BAHRAIN's Sheikh Isa airbase (US Fifth Fleet HQ) and JORDAN's Al-Azraq airbase (12 ballistic missiles); Tehran's Tasnim claimed 'eighteen important targets' destroyed across Bahrain, Kuwait and Jordan. Bahrain intercepted the attack but an 11-year-old girl was injured and homes/vehicles were damaged by debris; Jordan claimed interception of ~20 missiles with no casualties. First direct Iranian targeting of US forces in Jordan in this cycle.",
   "change": "JULY 25 — WSJ reports BAHRAINI AND KUWAITI WARPLANES struck Iranian missile/drone storage depots earlier in July with UAE targeting intelligence and air cover — the first direct Gulf Arab strikes on Iran. KUWAIT'S ambassador to the US formally calls the report 'entirely false.' CONTESTED-UNRESOLVED.",
