  {
   "name": "US Gasoline (AAA national avg, $/gal)",
   "as_of": "2026-08-28",
   "value": "AUGUST 27-28 — NO NEW AAA PRINT WAS PUBLISHED IN THIS CYCLE AND THE FIGURE OF RECORD REMAINS ABOUT $4.0969 FOR REGULAR, EFFECTIVELY FLAT, WITH AUGUST 2026 ON COURSE TO BE THE HIGHEST AUGUST ON RECORD. The month-to-date average is $4.06, surpassing 2022's August average of $3.97; the national average first crossed $4.03 on August 13; the price is up nearly a dollar year on year; and diesel has set successive records, around $5.40 in mid-August with $5.6134 logged on August 24. July PCE inflation, reported August 26, held at 3.7% year on year. THE INTERNATIONAL COMPARISON PUBLISHED THIS CYCLE IS THE CLEANEST MEASURE OF INCIDENCE THE AUDIT HAS: between FEBRUARY AND MAY 2026 EUROPEAN NATURAL GAS PRICES ROSE 44% AND ASIAN PRICES ROSE 66%, WHILE US PRICES DECLINED 6%. The American cost of this war is arriving as a price index; the acute cost is arriving elsewhere as rationing. || AUGUST 26 — THE AAA NATIONAL AVERAGE FOR REGULAR STOOD AT ABOUT $4.0969, EFFECTIVELY FLAT, AND AUGUST 2026 IS ON COURSE TO BE THE HIGHEST AUGUST ON RECORD. The month-to-date average is $4.06, surpassing 2022's August average of $3.97. The national average first crossed $4.03 on AUGUST 13, the highest ever recorded that late in a calendar year, and reached $4.10 on AUGUST 20, also a record for the date. Prices are up nearly a dollar on a year ago. DIESEL has set successive records, averaging about $5.40 in mid-August — itself a mid-August record — with the audit's prior print of $5.6134 on August 24 standing as the most recent daily figure logged. THE MACRO CONTEXT ARRIVED THE SAME DAY: JULY PCE INFLATION HELD AT 3.7% YEAR-ON-YEAR, reported AUGUST 26, well above the Federal Reserve's 2% target, with the Commerce Department release framed explicitly against the Iran war and the trade disputes. Two-thirds of Democratic campaign advertisements mentioning gasoline prices cite the war as the cause. (AAA Fuel Prices, August 26, 2026; Quartz, 'Gas and diesel hit record seasonal highs in mid-August 2026'; Transport Topics, August 2026; U.S. News and The Washington Times on the July PCE release, August 26, 2026; Fox News Digital live updates, August 25, 2026.) || AUGUST 24 — AAA NATIONAL AVERAGE $4.0991 FOR REGULAR, EFFECTIVELY FLAT, WHILE DIESEL SET A FURTHER RECORD. Regular stood at $4.0991 against $4.0986 the previous day, $4.0636 a week earlier, $4.1054 a month earlier and $3.1533 a year ago — 95 cents above last year. Diesel reached $5.6134 against $5.6074 the previous day, $5.4454 a week earlier, $5.2401 a month earlier and $3.6862 a year ago — $1.93 above last year and 37 cents above a month ago, a further record August level. The pump is now decoupled from the crude tape on a daily basis: Brent fell on August 24 and retail was unchanged, which is the normal lag rather than a signal. The straits.live board projects a US pump price of $4.75 a gallon if the Strait is fully closed. (AAA Fuel Prices national average, August 24, 2026.) || AUGUST 23 — AAA NATIONAL AVERAGE $4.0986 FOR REGULAR, EASING FRACTIONALLY WHILE DIESEL SETS A RECORD AUGUST LEVEL. Regular stood at $4.0986 against $4.1024 the previous day, $4.0656 a week earlier, $4.0911 a month earlier and $3.1497 a year earlier — ninety cents above the year-ago level and still above $4, which the national average first exceeded in April 2026 for the first time since August 2022. THE FREIGHT SIDE IS WORSE AND IS THE ONE THAT TRANSMITS INTO GOODS PRICES: DIESEL REACHED $5.6074, against $5.5947 the previous day, $5.4460 a week earlier, $5.2089 a month earlier and $3.6893 a year earlier — a rise of $1.92 year-on-year and 40 cents in a month, and a record August level. (AAA Fuel Prices national average, August 23, 2026; Transport Topics, 'Diesel, gasoline prices hit record August highs,' August 2026.) || AUGUST 22 — AAA NATIONAL AVERAGE $4.1024, holding above $4 and close to a dollar above the year-earlier level. The national average first exceeded $4 a gallon in April 2026, for the first time since August 2022, and the August monthly average of $4.06 has already surpassed August 2022's $3.97. (AAA Fuel Prices national average, August 22, 2026; CNN live updates, August 21, 2026.) || AUGUST 20 — THE NATIONAL AVERAGE REACHED $4.10 A GALLON FOR REGULAR, THE HIGHEST EVER RECORDED ON THIS DATE. The August monthly average stands at $4.06, already surpassing August 2022's $3.97 to make this the highest August on record. Drivers paid $4.08 on AUGUST 5, 28 cents above the $3.80 national average of JULY 6 — a 7.5% monthly increase. AAA attributes the rise to crude costs amid continued instability in the Strait of Hormuz, noting that gasoline demand is down as is typical for the season and that crude is pushing the average up regardless. (AAA Newsroom, 'High Crude Oil Prices Push Up National Average,' August 2026; AAA Fuel Prices, August 2026; gasprices.aaa.com monthly archive.) || AUGUST 1 — NO NEW AAA PRINT PUBLISHED FOR THE WEEKEND CYCLE. The last recorded figures stand at regular $4.1060 and diesel $5.3530, the latter another war high and within roughly 46 cents of the June 2022 record, with diesel having risen every day for a week regardless of crude direction. With Brent restated $2.30 lower at $87.93 and futures markets closed, the crude-to-pump divergence is unchanged rather than narrowed. (AAA via prior cycle; TradingEconomics, July 31 – August 1, 2026.) || JULY 31 — BOTH GRADES ROSE AGAIN AND DIESEL SET ANOTHER WAR HIGH. AAA's national average for regular gasoline was $4.1060, up from $4.0980 a day earlier, against $4.1050 a week ago, $3.8470 a month ago and $3.1470 a year ago. Diesel reached $5.3530, up from $5.3390, against $5.2400 a week ago, $4.8530 a month ago and $3.7410 a year ago — the highest of the war and now within about 46 cents of the all-time record of $5.816 set on June 19, 2022. Diesel has risen every day of the past week irrespective of the direction of crude. (AAA Gas Prices, July 31, 2026.) || JULY 30 — BOTH GRADES ROSE AND DIESEL SET ANOTHER WAR HIGH. AAA's national average was $4.0980 for regular, up from $4.0910 and level with the week-ago figure, against $3.8470 a month ago and $3.1400 a year ago. Diesel rose to $5.3390 from $5.3290 — a fresh wartime record — against $5.2090 a week ago, $4.8530 a month ago and $3.7400 a year ago. Diesel has now risen on every day of the past week regardless of which direction crude moved, confirming that distillate tightness rather than crude is setting the print. AAA's own summary was \"Little Change to National Average, Pump Prices Remain High.\" (AAA Fuel Prices; AAA Newsroom, July 30, 2026.) || JULY 29 — REGULAR SLIPPED AGAIN WHILE DIESEL SET ANOTHER WAR HIGH. The AAA national average was $4.0910 for regular, down fractionally from $4.099 the day before, and $5.3290 for diesel, up from $5.321 — a fresh conflict peak, and the eighth consecutive daily increase for diesel. Comparisons: a week earlier roughly $4.03 regular / $5.17 diesel; a month earlier $3.867 / $4.887; a year earlier $3.143 / $3.731. The decoupling is now visible in both directions: diesel rose on every day of a three-session, ~$16 Brent decline, and regular fell on the day Brent rebounded 3.4% back above $90. Distillate tightness, not crude, is setting the diesel print. (AAA Fuel Prices national averages, July 29, 2026.) || JULY 28 — Regular eased marginally while DIESEL SET ANOTHER WAR HIGH. The AAA national average was $4.099 for regular, down from $4.110 the day before, and $5.321 for diesel, up from $5.296. Comparisons: a week earlier $4.019 regular / $5.142 diesel; a month earlier $3.867 / $4.887; a year earlier $3.143 / $3.731. Retail continues to lag crude in both directions — Brent has fallen roughly $14 from the July 23 settle while diesel has risen every day of that fall. (AAA Fuel Prices national averages, July 28, 2026.) || JULY 27 — Pump prices did not follow crude down. The AAA national average held flat at $4.110 for regular and rose to $5.296 for diesel, against $4.003/$5.108 a week earlier, $3.878/$4.905 a month earlier and $3.145/$3.735 a year earlier. Retail continues to lag a two-session Brent collapse, leaving the consumer-facing cost of the war at its wartime high. (AAA Fuel Prices, July 27, 2026.) || JULY 26 — The AAA national average reached $4.111/gal for regular and $5.295/gal for DIESEL — a war high for both. Comparisons: a week earlier $3.998 regular / $5.101 diesel; a month earlier $3.901 / $4.934; a year earlier $3.151 / $3.738. Regular is up about 13 cents on the week and 96 cents year-on-year; diesel is up about 19 cents on the week and $1.56 year-on-year. Pump prices continued rising even as Brent fell more than 5% on the strike pause, reflecting the usual lag from crude to retail. (AAA Fuel Prices, July 26, 2026.) || JULY 25 — The AAA US national average reached $4.111/gal, up from $3.95 on July 16 (about +15 cents in nine days; CNN put the Saturday move at about a penny to $4.11 and +11 cents on the week) and roughly 95 cents above the year-earlier $3.15. California highest at $5.59; Indiana lowest at $3.53. US gasoline futures eased below $3.45/gal from a two-month high above $3.50 on July 23 as reports of a Pakistan-brokered, China-encouraged US-Iran talks revival pressured crude. The domestic pump price is the clearest transmission of the Hormuz closure into US politics. (AAA Fuel Prices; Clarksville Online; CNN; TradingEconomics, July 23-25, 2026.)",
   "change": "AUGUST 27-28 — No new AAA print; figure of record ~$4.0969 for regular, effectively flat, with August month-to-date at $4.06, the highest August on record against 2022's $3.97. Diesel at successive records (~$5.6134 logged August 24). July PCE 3.7% y/y. Incidence comparison: February-May European gas +44% and Asian gas +66% against US gas -6%. || AUGUST 26 — AAA regular ~$4.0969, effectively flat; August month-to-date average $4.06, the highest August on record against 2022's $3.97; up nearly a dollar year-on-year. Diesel at successive records (~$5.40 mid-August; $5.6134 logged August 24). July PCE inflation 3.7% y/y reported August 26, above the Fed's 2% target. || AUGUST 24 — Regular $4.0991 (prev. $4.0986; week ago $4.0636; year ago $3.1533). Diesel $5.6134, a further record, $1.93 above a year ago and 37 cents above a month ago.",
