   "source": "ABC News live updates, 'Iran live updates: Tanker attacked in Strait of Hormuz, UKMTO says,' entries of August 27, 2026 (UKMTO Warning 121-26 at 6:35 GMT; White House Press Secretary Karoline Leavitt at 13:46 GMT); Gulf News, \"'Unknown projectile' hits oil tanker off Oman: UKMTO,\" August 27, 2026; Fox News Digital live updates, 'Iran attacks another commercial ship in Strait of Hormuz as Trump sends new warship to Middle East,' August 27, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Iran, Qatar hold Hormuz talks amid int'l hopes dialogue with US will resume,' August 27, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Is Iran war diplomacy ramping up, as Qatar, Oman, Pakistan officials visit?,' August 27, 2026 (Ali Vaez, International Crisis Group); Al Jazeera, 'How a 95 percent drop in Hormuz traffic changed global shipping,' August 27, 2026 (Richard Matthews, Gibson Shipbrokers); Al Arabiya English, 'Qatar PM pursues mediation in Tehran as US ramps up ‘economic war’,' August 27, 2026; RTE, 'Qatari PM heads to Iran to ‘de-escalate tensions’,' August 27, 2026; Al Bawaba, 'Qatar PM visits Tehran as Iran insists on rights and calls for talks,' August 27, 2026; Reuters via CNN, 'US says not talking to Iran as Strait of Hormuz diplomacy intensifies,' August 27, 2026 (senior Iranian source: agreement with Oman 'has not been finalized'); Bloomberg, 'US Aims to Revive Civil War-Era Court to Claim Iran Oil as Prize,' August 26, 2026 (US Attorney Aaron Reitz, Southern District of Texas); Fox Business, 'DOJ plans to revive maritime prize courts to seize Iranian oil tankers as war prizes: report,' August 26-27, 2026; Newsmax, 'DOJ Eyes Prize Courts for Seized Iranian Tankers,' August 26, 2026; Just Security, 'Early Edition: August 27, 2026' (Elisabeth Jennings and Ammanuel Desta); The Times of Israel liveblog, August 27, 2026 (former Mossad chief Barnea; White House 'no talks or conversations'; USS Theodore Roosevelt deployment; Chinese banks under consideration for designation); Iran International, 'Iran weighs gasoline rationing as fuel deficit widens' and live coverage of August 26-27, 2026 (Executive Vice-President Mohammad Jafar Ghaempanah; Tehran and Mashhad fuel queues; Pezeshkian at the 40th International Islamic Unity Conference, August 27); TradingEconomics Brent crude and WTI price pages and news blurbs, August 26-27, 2026; The Jerusalem Post, 'US military made secret path through Hormuz, 10 million oil barrels pass daily,' August 2026; gCaptain, 'Trump Administration Says Gulf Oil Flows Recover to 15 Million Barrels a Day,' August 11, 2026 (Energy Secretary Chris Wright; Joint Maritime Information Center counts); Haaretz, 'IDF Strikes in Lebanon Kill One, Wound Six, Including Child, Woman,' August 27, 2026; JNS, 'IDF launches strikes on Hezbollah targets in Southern Lebanon,' August 27, 2026.",
   "summary": "THE ORGANISING FACT OF THIS CYCLE IS THAT THE UNITED STATES ANSWERED AN ATTACK ON THE STRAIT BY STANDING UP A COURT.\n\nTHE ATTACK. At 6:35 GMT on AUGUST 27 the United Kingdom Maritime Trade Operations centre issued WARNING 121-26: a tanker transiting the Strait of Hormuz was HIT BY AN UNKNOWN PROJECTILE, causing a fire that has since been extinguished. No casualties and no environmental damage were reported, the crew was reported safe, and vessels were advised to transit with caution. It was the SECOND VESSEL STRUCK IN THE WATERWAY THIS WEEK, coming thirty-six hours after President Trump declared every mine cleared from the Strait's international waters and announced a 'ZERO TOLERANCE' rule under which 'any ship or boat placing new mines will be immediately and systematically destroyed.' The audit records the distinction that matters: the weapon was a PROJECTILE, NOT A MINE. The demining claim is therefore not falsified by this attack, and the attack is instead a direct confirmation of ALEX ALMEIDA of Ambrey, quoted two days earlier, that mines 'HAVE NEVER BEEN THE PRIMARY THREAT.' Clearing the mines did not make the Strait safe; it removed one of several instruments.\n\nTHE AMERICAN INSTRUMENT OF THE CYCLE IS A COURT. Bloomberg reported on AUGUST 26 that the Justice Department is preparing to activate MARITIME PRIZE COURTS — dormant since the Second World War and of negligible use since the Spanish-American War of 1898 — to give federal prosecutors a faster route to condemn oil and other cargo taken from enemy or neutral vessels as UNITED STATES PROPERTY, to be sold with the proceeds transferred to the Treasury. AARON REITZ, the Houston-based US attorney whose office is partnering with department headquarters, confirmed that DOJ is 'NOW REVIVING' what he called an 'ANCIENT BODY OF MARITIME LAW.' The reported purposes are to STRENGTHEN THE BLOCKADE and to OFFSET THE COST OF THE CONFLICT. The reported trigger is procedural: the civil forfeiture route used against a seized Venezuelan supertanker has bogged down under interventions from the shipping company and from families of Iranian terrorism victims. The plans are described as unfinalised. FOR THIS AUDIT THE SIGNIFICANCE IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN KINETIC: a state that expects to stop seizing ships shortly does not rebuild the nineteenth-century legal machinery for condemning them. This is institutional evidence that the maritime campaign is being built to last.\n\nDIPLOMACY MOVED, AND THEN ONE GOVERNMENT CONTRADICTED ITSELF. Qatar's Prime Minister and Foreign Minister SHEIKH MOHAMMED BIN ABDULRAHMAN BIN JASSIM AL THANI spent AUGUST 27 in Tehran, meeting Foreign Minister ABBAS ARAGHCHI, President MASOUD PEZESHKIAN, Parliament Speaker MOHAMMAD BAGHER GHALIBAF and security chief MOHSEN REZAI. The published agenda was the proposed Hormuz framework — a temporary shipping corridor and a joint mine-clearing operation — and the conditions for renewed US-Iran dialogue. Sheikh Mohammed said 'DIALOGUE AND DIPLOMATIC SOLUTIONS REMAIN THE OPTIMAL PATH FOR ADDRESSING DISPUTES,' stressing respect for freedom of navigation in accordance with international law. REZAI restated the Iranian precondition without softening it: the United States 'MUST FIRST TAKE PRACTICAL STEPS TO FULFIL IRAN'S CONDITIONS' — lift the naval blockade, unfreeze assets, remove oil sanctions. It is the third mediator in Tehran in four days, after Pakistan's army chief ASIM MUNIR on August 24 and Oman's BADR ALBUSAIDI on August 25.\n\nAND THEN THE DEAL ITSELF WAS PUT BACK IN DOUBT BY TEHRAN. A SENIOR IRANIAN SOURCE told Reuters on AUGUST 27 that the agreement with Oman over the Strait of Hormuz 'HAS NOT BEEN FINALIZED' — one day after IRGC spokesman Brigadier General HOSSEIN MOHEBBI said the two countries had agreed each country's share of the strait's waters and of its revenues, and two days after the two foreign ministries announced a phased framework. The contradiction is INTERNAL TO THE IRANIAN STATE and is logged as such rather than resolved: the IRGC and the diplomatic apparatus are describing the same instrument at different stages of completion.\n\nWASHINGTON CLOSED THE DOOR. The White House said on AUGUST 27 that there are 'NO TALKS OR CONVERSATIONS' going on or scheduled with Iran. President TRUMP said: 'WE DON'T WANT TO SPEAK TO THEM. WE'RE NOT LOOKING TO MEET OR ANYTHING.' Press Secretary KAROLINE LEAVITT said the administration is 'CONTINUING TO PULL EVERY ECONOMIC LEVER' and that 'THE NAVAL BLOCKADE CONTINUES TO BE IN EFFECT AS WELL, WHICH HAS PROVEN HUGELY SUCCESSFUL,' adding that 'NO NEGOTIATIONS ARE HAPPENING RIGHT NOW, AND THIS WILL CONTINUE UNTIL THE PRESIDENT FEELS THAT MAYBE THEY COME TO THE TABLE IN A MEANINGFUL WAY.' She described Iran's condition in figures the Iranian government does not publish: 'INFLATION IS OVER 100%. THEY CAN'T PAY THEIR POLICE. THEY CAN'T PAY THEIR MILITARY.' Reporting the same day indicated the administration is NO LONGER INTERESTED IN REVIVING THE JUNE 17 MEMORANDUM OF UNDERSTANDING, having told mediating states it will not accept the previous terms.\n\nTHE TRAFFIC NUMBER LANDED AGAINST THE PRESIDENT'S OWN NUMBER. Al Jazeera published a dataset on AUGUST 27 putting transits at ABOUT FIVE VESSELS A DAY between JULY 15 and AUGUST 23 against a pre-war baseline of about 100 a day — a fall of NEARLY 95%. Gulf crude exports are down 47%, from roughly 17 million barrels a day to about 9 million; direct crude through the strait averages 2.2 million barrels a day; analysts estimate 5-7 million barrels a day disrupted; combined Gulf shipments fell from about 400 million barrels in February to about 200 million in July. RICHARD MATTHEWS of Gibson Shipbrokers: 'THERE IS NO ALTERNATIVE MARITIME ROUTE. THERE ARE SOME PIPELINES, BUT THERE'S NO ALTERNATIVE.' This sits directly against TRUMP'S claim, made on the Glenn Beck programme on AUGUST 26, that TEN MILLION BARRELS passed through the strait on Tuesday and that the waterway is 'functioning' apart from the occasional 'drone or a rocket or something shot.' The audit does not adjudicate the two figures; it records that the divergence is now wide enough to be a fact about the information environment rather than about the strait.\n\nOIL DIVERGED WITHIN THE SAME SESSION AND THE DIVERGENCE IS LOGGED, NOT RESOLVED. TradingEconomics carried Brent at $89.48 on AUGUST 27, up $1.86 or 2.12%, breaking the three-session slide; the same service's news commentary described Brent 'slipping toward $87 on Thursday, extending its decline to a fourth straight session' on signs of diplomatic progress. Fox News carried crude 'near $82 Thursday, maintaining losses for the third consecutive day.' What every print agrees on is direction of cause: the market is pricing diplomacy and a sanctions package judged milder than feared, not supply loss.\n\nINSIDE IRAN THE FUEL POSITION WAS DESCRIBED AS A RED ALERT. Executive Vice-President MOHAMMAD JAFAR GHAEMPANAH said domestic gasoline production cannot meet consumption and the American naval blockade prevents imports; strategic reserves are described internally as at RED ALERT with remaining stocks potentially exhausted within weeks. Car queues blocked streets in parts of TEHRAN and MASHHAD on Tuesday and Wednesday. A price increase and a rationing scheme have been signalled within weeks, against an existing tier structure whose lowest band is 15,000 rials a litre with further bands at 30,000 and 50,000. The rial holds at its record of roughly 2.06 million to the dollar on the open market against an official rate near 1.5 million, with inflation at almost 90% by the government's own Statistical Center. Iran International reported DISAGREEMENT BETWEEN PEZESHKIAN AND THE REVOLUTIONARY GUARD over drawing down the fuel reserve. Pezeshkian addressed the 40th International Islamic Unity Conference in Tehran on AUGUST 27.\n\nTHE WAR AIM WAS STATED IN ITS MAXIMAL FORM BY A THIRD PARTY. Former Mossad chief BARNEA said on AUGUST 27 that there is 'NO CHOICE' but to keep striking Iran 'UNTIL THE REGIME FALLS — AND IT WILL FALL.' ALI VAEZ of the International Crisis Group put the open question the other way round, asking whether Washington is running 'a zero-sum campaign aimed at collapsing the regime, or leverage towards an agreement.'\n\nLEBANON. On AUGUST 27 the IDF conducted a wave of strikes on Hezbollah targets in southern Lebanon after confirming that two explosive drones were launched at Israeli soldiers operating inside the security zone in south-eastern Lebanon. The Lebanese Health Ministry reported ONE WOMAN KILLED and SIX WOUNDED, including a child.\n\nTHE GROUND RUNG DID NOT MOVE. A twenty-seventh consecutive night passed with no confirmed US or Israeli strike inside Iranian territory since August 1. No landing, no move on Kharg Island, no draft order.",
   "details": "UKMTO WARNING 121-26 (August 27, 06:35 GMT): tanker in the Strait of Hormuz hit by an unknown projectile; fire caused and since extinguished; crew safe; no casualties; no environmental damage; vessels advised to transit with caution; second vessel struck in the waterway this week. PRIZE COURTS (Bloomberg, August 26): DOJ preparing to activate maritime prize courts to condemn seized Iranian tankers and cargo as US property for sale, proceeds to the Treasury; dormant since WWII, negligible use since 1898; stated purposes — strengthen the blockade, offset the cost of the conflict; US Attorney AARON REITZ (Houston) confirms DOJ is 'now reviving' an 'ancient body of maritime law'; plans unfinalised; trigger is the bogged-down civil forfeiture of a seized Venezuelan supertanker with third-party interveners. QATAR (August 27): PM/FM SHEIKH MOHAMMED BIN ABDULRAHMAN BIN JASSIM AL THANI in Tehran; met ARAGHCHI, PEZESHKIAN, GHALIBAF, security chief MOHSEN REZAI; agenda — temporary shipping corridor, joint mine-clearing, conditions for renewed US-Iran dialogue; Sheikh Mohammed: 'Dialogue and diplomatic solutions remain the optimal path for addressing disputes'; REZAI: the US 'must first take practical steps to fulfil Iran's conditions' — lift the naval blockade, unfreeze assets, remove oil sanctions. Third mediator in four days after ASIM MUNIR (August 24) and BADR ALBUSAIDI (August 25). OMAN DEAL STATUS (Reuters, August 27): a senior Iranian source says the agreement with Oman over the Strait 'has not been finalized,' one day after IRGC spokesman HOSSEIN MOHEBBI said shares of the waterway and its revenues had been agreed. US POSITION (August 27): White House — 'no talks or conversations' going on or scheduled; TRUMP — 'We don't want to speak to them. We're not looking to meet or anything'; LEAVITT — 'continuing to pull every economic lever,' 'the naval blockade continues to be in effect as well, which has proven hugely successful,' 'No negotiations are happening right now, and this will continue until the president feels that maybe they come to the table in a meaningful way,' 'Inflation is over 100%. They can't pay their police. They can't pay their military'; administration reported no longer interested in reviving the June 17 MOU. TRAFFIC (Al Jazeera, August 27): ~5 vessels/day July 15-August 23 vs ~100/day pre-war = ~95% drop; Gulf crude exports -47% (~17m bpd to ~9m bpd); direct crude via strait 2.2m bpd; 5-7m bpd estimated disrupted; combined Gulf shipments ~400m barrels (February) to ~200m (July); strait carries 33% of global seaborne crude and 29% of LPG; RICHARD MATTHEWS (Gibson Shipbrokers): 'there is no alternative maritime route. There are some pipelines, but there's no alternative'; prices ~20% above pre-war; April peak >$130. AGAINST: TRUMP (Glenn Beck, August 26) — 10 million barrels through Hormuz on Tuesday; strait 'functioning'; 'every once in a while there will be a drone or a rocket or something shot.' Prior administration claim: Energy Secretary CHRIS WRIGHT, ~15m bpd out of the Gulf and a 7-day Hormuz average near 9m bpd (August 11), against an IEA 2025 baseline of 19.87m bpd and JMIC counts of 42 US-facilitated transits over August 9-10 vs ~138/day historically. OIL (August 27): TradingEconomics Brent $89.48, +$1.86/+2.12%; same service's commentary — Brent 'slipped toward $87... fourth straight session'; Fox — crude 'near $82... third consecutive day' of losses. IRAN DOMESTIC: GHAEMPANAH — domestic gasoline production insufficient, blockade prevents imports; strategic reserves at internal 'red alert,' stocks potentially exhausted within weeks; fuel queues in Tehran and Mashhad Tuesday-Wednesday; price rise and rationing signalled within weeks against tiers of 15,000 / 30,000 / 50,000 rials per litre; rial ~2.06m/USD open market vs ~1.5m official; inflation ~90% (Statistical Center of Iran) vs 'over 100%' (White House); Iran International reports Pezeshkian-IRGC disagreement over reserve drawdown; Pezeshkian addressed the 40th International Islamic Unity Conference, August 27. WAR AIMS: former Mossad chief BARNEA — 'no choice' but to keep hitting Iran 'until the regime falls — and it will fall'; ALI VAEZ (International Crisis Group) — is this 'a zero-sum campaign aimed at collapsing the regime, or leverage towards an agreement?' LEBANON (August 27): IDF wave of strikes on Hezbollah targets in southern Lebanon after two explosive drones were launched at Israeli troops in the south-eastern security zone; Lebanese Health Ministry — one woman killed, six wounded including a child. CASUALTIES: Pentagon total unchanged at 776 — 18 killed, 758 wounded. GROUND RUNG: 27th consecutive night with no confirmed US or Israeli strike inside Iran; no landing; no Kharg seizure; no draft order.",
   "prediction_impact": "The predicted US GROUND SEIZURE of the Strait of Hormuz or Kharg Island remains DISCONFIRMED at Day 183, and this cycle adds a category of negative evidence the audit has not previously been able to log: INSTITUTIONAL. The Justice Department's revival of maritime prize courts is the construction of durable legal machinery for condemning seized ships and cargo — an instrument that only makes sense if the United States expects to be interdicting Iranian shipping for a long time and wants the proceeds. Governments that intend to resolve a chokepoint by taking it do not spend the interval rebuilding nineteenth-century admiralty procedure. Read alongside the Axios report of a strike pause expected to hold past the midterms, and the carrier ROTATION rather than surge logged in the Ground Invasion Tracker, the American posture is being engineered for endurance on the maritime and financial rungs. Predictions that the war would end in a NEGOTIATED SETTLEMENT are WEAKENED on the American side and DESTABILISED on the Iranian side in the same cycle. The White House states there are no talks or conversations under way or scheduled; Trump says 'we don't want to speak to them'; the administration is reported to have abandoned the June 17 MOU as a basis. Simultaneously the Iran-Oman corridor — logged in the previous cycle as the first bilateral instrument of the war to address the Strait's future administration — was described by a senior Iranian source as NOT FINALIZED, one day after the IRGC described it as settled including revenue shares. The audit records the contradiction as internal to the Iranian state and does not resolve it. Predictions that ESCALATION WOULD PRICE OIL UPWARD remain DISCONFIRMED in this window: a tanker was struck in the Strait and the market moved on diplomacy rather than on the attack, with prints diverging between about $87 and $89.48 for Brent and about $81-82 for WTI, all attributed to de-escalation expectations. Predictions that IRAN RETAINS EFFECTIVE CONTROL OF THE STRAIT are neither confirmed nor refuted, but the American claim to have restored flow is now under measured contradiction: Al Jazeera's dataset of about five transits a day against a 100-a-day baseline sits against the President's claim of ten million barrels moved on a single Tuesday. The gap between official American throughput figures and independent transit counts is now a standing feature of the record and is carried in the open-questions register rather than adjudicated here. Predictions that SANCTIONS PRESSURE WOULD PRODUCE VISIBLE IRANIAN DOMESTIC BREAKDOWN are further PARTIALLY CONFIRMED as to symptom — strategic fuel reserves at internal red alert, queues in two major cities, rationing signalled, a record rial — and remain UNTESTED as to political effect: no institutional fracture, no loss of territorial control, and a reported policy disagreement between the President and the IRGC that is evidence of a functioning dispute rather than of collapse. Predictions of REGIME CHANGE AS THE DECLARED WAR AIM are now supported by the rhetoric of two governments and questioned by a non-fringe analyst in the same cycle; the question of whether collapse is the objective or the leverage is entered as a new open question and not resolved.",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "The revival of maritime prize courts is read as the legal formalisation of an existing blockade — giving prosecutors a faster and cleaner route to dispose of cargo already lawfully seized under a declared campaign — and, by the opposing reading, as the conversion of the war into a self-financing revenue operation, in which the United States acquires a standing institutional interest in continuing to seize ships because the proceeds accrue to its own Treasury.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented: Bloomberg reported on August 26 that the Justice Department is preparing to activate maritime prize courts, dormant since the Second World War and of negligible use since 1898. Documented: US Attorney Aaron Reitz, whose Houston office is partnering with department headquarters, confirmed DOJ is 'now reviving' an 'ancient body of maritime law.' Documented: the reported mechanism is a faster path for federal prosecutors to claim oil and other cargo taken from enemy or neutral vessels as US property, to be sold with proceeds transferred to the Treasury. Documented: the reported purposes are to strengthen the blockade of Iran and to offset the cost of the conflict. Documented: the reported trigger is that the civil-forfeiture route used against a seized Venezuelan supertanker has bogged down under interventions by the shipping company and by families of Iranian terrorism victims. Documented: CENTCOM's last published tally is 71 commercial vessels redirected, 3 disabled and 2 boarded since the July 14 blockade resumption. Not documented: whether the plans will be finalised, which court or courts would sit, how many vessels or what volume of cargo is contemplated, what revenue is projected, or whether any condemnation proceeding has been filed.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "US Attorney Aaron Reitz, confirming the revival and framing it as a procedural fix to a bogged-down forfeiture process",
      "Bloomberg and Bloomberg Law, reporting the initiative as intended to strengthen the blockade and offset the cost of the conflict",
      "Fox Business, Newsmax and WorldNetDaily, reporting it as a wartime enforcement measure",
      "Legal commentators and maritime-law writers characterising the move as reviving prize money as a state revenue mechanism"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "The two readings turn on institutional intent and on projected revenue, neither of which is in the open record. The plans are described as unfinalised, no condemnation proceeding has been reported filed, and no figure for expected proceeds has been published. Until a court sits and a docket exists, the distinction between 'faster disposal of what we already seize' and 'a standing incentive to seize more' cannot be adjudicated from open sources."
    },
    {
     "reading": "The senior Iranian source's statement that the Oman agreement 'has not been finalized' is read as a routine clarification that a framework is not yet a signed instrument — and, by the opposing reading, as evidence that the IRGC announced a settlement of waters and revenues that the Iranian diplomatic apparatus has not agreed to, making the corridor a contested object inside the Iranian state rather than a national position.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented: on August 25 the Iranian and Omani foreign ministries announced a 'phased framework' for a joint temporary navigational corridor and a joint mine-clearing project. Documented: on August 26 IRGC spokesman Brigadier General Hossein Mohebbi said the two countries had agreed 'each country's share of the strait's waters and Iran and Oman's share of its revenues' and that 'the Strait of Hormuz belongs to Iran and the Sultanate of Oman.' Documented: on August 26 Deputy Foreign Minister Kazem Gharibabadi gave operational terms on state television — seven miles wide, entry beginning in Iranian territorial waters, Iranian permission and surveillance, military vessels barred, a permanent arrangement in 30-60 days — while calling the route temporary. Documented: on August 27 a senior Iranian source told Reuters the agreement with Oman 'has not been finalized.' Not documented: which Iranian institution speaks authoritatively for the arrangement, whether any text has been initialled, route coordinates, a start date, or Omani confirmation of the revenue-sharing description.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "IRGC spokesman Brigadier General Hossein Mohebbi, describing shares of the waterway and its revenues as agreed",
      "An unnamed senior Iranian source speaking to Reuters, stating the agreement is not finalised",
      "Iranian Deputy Foreign Minister Kazem Gharibabadi, describing terms while insisting the route is temporary and the strait is not reopened",
      "Omani Foreign Minister Badr Albusaidi and Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi, presenting a phased framework rather than a concluded agreement"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "No text has been published by either government, Oman has not publicly confirmed or denied the revenue-sharing characterisation, and the dissenting Iranian account is anonymous. Whether the gap is a stage-of-completion difference or an institutional dispute cannot be determined without a published instrument or an on-record Omani statement.",
     "open_question_ref": "iran-oman-hormuz-corridor-concession-or-consolidation-august-2026"
    },
    {
     "reading": "The gap between the President's claim that ten million barrels moved through the Strait on Tuesday and Al Jazeera's finding of about five transits a day is read as a difference between barrels moved by all routes, including US-escorted convoys and bypass pipelines, and vessels counted transiting the strait itself — and, by the opposing reading, as an official throughput figure that cannot be reconciled with any independent count and is being published to shape the oil price.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented: on August 26 Trump said on the Glenn Beck programme that ten million barrels passed through the Strait of Hormuz on Tuesday and that the waterway is 'functioning.' Documented: Al Jazeera's August 27 analysis puts transits at about five vessels a day between July 15 and August 23 against a pre-war baseline of about 100 a day, a fall of nearly 95%, with Gulf crude exports down 47% from about 17m bpd to about 9m bpd. Documented: Energy Secretary Chris Wright said on August 11 that flows out of the Persian Gulf had reached about 15m bpd with a seven-day Hormuz average near 9m bpd. Documented: the IEA's 2025 baseline for Hormuz is 19.87m bpd. Documented: JMIC recorded 42 US-facilitated transits over August 9-10 against a historical average near 138 a day, and 73 transits over August 10-16 against 91 the previous week. Documented: CENTCOM has redirected 71 commercial vessels since July 14. Not documented: the methodology, vessel list or measurement window behind any administration figure.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "President Trump and Energy Secretary Chris Wright, publishing throughput figures far above independent transit counts",
      "Al Jazeera and Gibson Shipbrokers' Richard Matthews, publishing the transit and export collapse",
      "Reason, in 'The Trump administration's Hormuz oil numbers don't add up,' disputing the reconciliation",
      "Joint Maritime Information Center and Kpler, publishing counts materially below the official figures"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "The administration has not published the methodology, measurement window or vessel list behind its throughput figures, and the independent counts measure transits rather than barrels. Without a common denominator the two series cannot be reconciled or falsified from open sources.",
     "open_question_ref": "hormuz-transit-volume-dispute-august-2026"
    }
   ]
