   "source": "The Times of Israel liveblog, August 27, 2026 (USS Theodore Roosevelt Carrier Strike Group to deploy within weeks; USS George Washington relieving USS Abraham Lincoln; continuous carrier presence in the Arabian Sea since late February); Forbes, 'Another US Navy Supercarrier Set To Deploy To The Middle East,' August 27, 2026; USNI News, 'Carrier USS George Washington Now in the Middle East,' August 20, 2026; USNI News, 'USS George Washington Heading West, Lincoln Deployment Nears 9 Months,' August 13, 2026; Stars and Stripes, 'USS George Washington arrives in Middle East to relieve USS Abraham Lincoln,' August 20, 2026; USNI News Fleet and Marine Tracker, August 17 and August 24, 2026 (USS Boxer (LHD-4), USS Portland (LPD-27), USS Comstock (LSD-45) and the 11th MEU in the Arabian Sea; USS Tripoli (LHA-7) in port at Sasebo, Japan); Bloomberg, 'US Aims to Revive Civil War-Era Court to Claim Iran Oil as Prize,' August 26, 2026; ABC News live updates, August 27, 2026 (White House Press Secretary Karoline Leavitt on the naval blockade); Foreign Policy, 'How a U.S. Ground Operation in Iran Could Unfold,' July 24, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Could the US take control of Iran's southern islands?,' July 15, 2026; The National, 'Iran reroutes oil flow as loading resumes at Kharg Island terminal,' August 16, 2026; BlackVoiceNews, 'National Automatic Draft Registration Set to Begin in December 2026,' August 4, 2026 (effective December 18, 2026); CNN Politics, 'Automatic military draft registration takes effect in the US in December 2026,' April 9, 2026.",
   "summary": "STATUS: NO_GROUND_TROOPS, 183RD CONSECUTIVE DAY. No US ground forces are inside Iran. No amphibious operation, no lodgement, no seizure of Iranian territory or offshore infrastructure has been reported by any party, including by Iran, which has every incentive to report one.\n\nTHE CYCLE'S DECISIVE DATAPOINT IS A ROTATION. Reporting on AUGUST 27 confirmed that the USS THEODORE ROOSEVELT CARRIER STRIKE GROUP will deploy from San Diego to the Middle East WITHIN WEEKS, on a deployment expected to run AT LEAST SEVEN MONTHS with crews told to plan for EIGHT, to RELIEVE the USS GEORGE WASHINGTON. The George Washington entered the Fifth Fleet area in the week of AUGUST 20 after deploying from Japan to relieve the USS ABRAHAM LINCOLN, which left the CENTCOM area of responsibility on AUGUST 22 ending a deployment of more than 200 days. The United States has maintained a continuous carrier presence in the Arabian Sea since the war opened in late February.\n\nTHE AUDIT READS THIS AS A NEGATIVE INDICATOR AND STATES WHY. A one-for-one relief sequence sustains ONE carrier in theatre. That is DOWN from the two carriers CENTCOM cited publicly in July and down from the three-carrier posture logged in April as a candidate invasion-staging signature. Massing precedes a landing; rotating sustains a campaign. The eight-month planning horizon given to Theodore Roosevelt's crews carries the American posture in this theatre into 2027, which is consistent with the Axios report of the previous cycle that the non-kinetic posture is expected to hold until after the midterm elections, and inconsistent with imminent execution.\n\nTHE SECOND STRUCTURAL DATAPOINT IS NEW IN KIND. The Justice Department is reviving MARITIME PRIZE COURTS — dormant since the Second World War — to condemn seized Iranian tankers and their cargo as United States property for sale, with proceeds to the Treasury, for the stated purposes of strengthening the blockade and offsetting the cost of the conflict. US Attorney AARON REITZ confirmed DOJ is 'now reviving' an 'ancient body of maritime law.' A government that expects to resolve a chokepoint by taking it does not rebuild nineteenth-century admiralty procedure in the interval. This is institutional investment in the maritime rung.\n\nTHE WHITE HOUSE NAMED ITS OWN INSTRUMENT SET AND A GROUND FORCE WAS NOT IN IT. Press Secretary KAROLINE LEAVITT said on AUGUST 27 that the administration is 'CONTINUING TO PULL EVERY ECONOMIC LEVER' and that 'THE NAVAL BLOCKADE CONTINUES TO BE IN EFFECT AS WELL, WHICH HAS PROVEN HUGELY SUCCESSFUL.' Two instruments named in an official briefing on the war's conduct; neither is a landing force.\n\nCOUNTER-INDICATORS, LOGGED IN FULL AND WITHOUT DISCOUNT. (1) A THIRD carrier strike group is being generated for this theatre; the sequence is a relief, but the capability being sustained is a strike capability and the United States is choosing to sustain it rather than let it lapse. (2) The BOXER AMPHIBIOUS READY GROUP — USS Boxer (LHD-4), USS Portland (LPD-27) and USS Comstock (LSD-45) with approximately 2,200 Camp Pendleton Marines of the 11th Marine Expeditionary Unit — remains in the Arabian Sea on its 59TH DAY in theatre, still with no publicly reported tasking. An untasked ARG is a preserved option. (3) Former Mossad chief BARNEA said publicly on AUGUST 27 that there is 'NO CHOICE' but to keep striking Iran 'UNTIL THE REGIME FALLS — AND IT WILL FALL,' and Treasury Secretary Bessent's declared objective of collapsing the regime stands from the previous cycle. A declared regime-collapse objective is the class of war aim that has historically preceded ground commitment when economic instruments fail.\n\nKHARG ISLAND: NOT SEIZED, NOT TARGETED, NOT MENTIONED IN THIS CYCLE. Loading remains episodic rather than recovered — the western terminal was reactivated on AUGUST 12 after a 25-day shutdown, with a VLCC berthed for the first time since JULY 18, and no sustained export recovery has been confirmed. No US strike on the island has been reported, no American or Iranian statement about its status was made in this cycle, and the last publicly reported American consideration of seizing it remains the March Washington Post reporting on prepared raid options and Trump's March 30 remark that the island 'lacks defences.'\n\nUSS TRIPOLI: IN PORT AT SASEBO, JAPAN. The tracker records this explicitly because Tripoli is a standing item on the indicator list: the second big-deck amphibious ship most often named in invasion speculation is not in CENTCOM at all, it is forward-deployed in the Western Pacific.\n\nDRAFT RUNG: UNCHANGED, WITH ONE POINT OF NEW PRECISION. Automatic Selective Service registration of male citizens and most male residents aged 18-26 is now reported to take effect on DECEMBER 18, 2026, implementing the National Defense Authorization Act provision. This is a change in how the draft POOL is assembled, not an activation of conscription: automatic registration orders nobody into service, and no lottery, no induction authority and no call-up has been sought or enacted. No member of Congress has introduced an induction measure and no administration official has raised conscription in connection with Iran.\n\nTROOP COUNT: MORE THAN 50,000 US personnel in the Middle East, unchanged this cycle and unchanged since the Operation Epic Fury build-up. CENTCOM's last public figure, given in July, was more than 50,000 troops, around 200 combat aircraft and two aircraft carriers; more than 330 US military aircraft are positioned in the region, principally at Al Udeid in Qatar, Muwaffaq Salti in Jordan and Prince Sultan in Saudi Arabia. NONE OF IT IS INSIDE IRAN.\n\nEVERY EXECUTION INDICATOR REMAINS AT ZERO ON A 183RD DAY: no mobilisation order, no reserve call-up, no hospital-ship movement, no medical or casualty-reception surge, no forward fuel or ammunition prepositioning reported, no evacuation of dependants beyond existing levels, no ROE change enabling ground action, no congressional authorisation sought, and no ground-operation statement from the Pentagon.",
   "details": "STATUS: no_ground_troops, day 183. TROOPS IN THEATRE: >50,000 (unchanged); >330 aircraft; CENTCOM July public figure — >50,000 troops, ~200 combat aircraft, two carriers. NONE INSIDE IRAN. CARRIERS (August 27 reporting): USS THEODORE ROOSEVELT CSG to deploy from San Diego to the Middle East 'within weeks'; deployment expected 7+ months, crews told to plan for 8; relieves USS GEORGE WASHINGTON. USS GEORGE WASHINGTON entered 5th Fleet week of August 20, relieving USS ABRAHAM LINCOLN, which departed CENTCOM August 22 after a 200+ day deployment. Continuous carrier presence in the Arabian Sea since late February. NET: one-for-one relief sustaining ONE carrier in theatre, against two cited by CENTCOM in July and the three-carrier posture of April. AMPHIBIOUS: BOXER ARG — USS Boxer (LHD-4), USS Portland (LPD-27), USS Comstock (LSD-45) with ~2,200 Camp Pendleton Marines of the 11th MEU; in the Arabian Sea; 59th day in theatre (entered June 30); no publicly reported tasking; embarked F-35B, MV-22, helicopters and surface-connector capability. USS TRIPOLI (LHA-7): IN PORT, SASEBO, JAPAN — outside CENTCOM. INSTITUTIONAL: DOJ reviving maritime prize courts to condemn seized Iranian tankers and cargo as US property for sale, proceeds to the Treasury; stated purposes — strengthen the blockade, offset the cost of the conflict; US Attorney Aaron Reitz: DOJ 'now reviving' an 'ancient body of maritime law'; plans unfinalised. WHITE HOUSE (August 27): Leavitt — 'continuing to pull every economic lever'; 'the naval blockade continues to be in effect as well, which has proven hugely successful.' No ground reference. COUNTER-INDICATORS: (1) a third CSG being generated for the theatre and an 8-month planning horizon carrying the posture into 2027; (2) Boxer ARG untasked in theatre on day 59 — a preserved option; (3) former Mossad chief Barnea, August 27 — 'no choice' but to keep hitting Iran 'until the regime falls — and it will fall'; Bessent's declared objective of collapsing the regime stands. KHARG ISLAND: not seized, not targeted, not mentioned this cycle. Western terminal reactivated August 12 after a 25-day shutdown; first VLCC berthed since July 18; no sustained export recovery confirmed; terminals otherwise idle under the blockade with tankers queued offshore; island concentrates ~90-95% of Iranian crude exports. DRAFT: automatic Selective Service registration for males 18-26 now reported effective DECEMBER 18, 2026 under the NDAA provision; pool-building, not activation; no lottery, no induction authority, no call-up sought; no congressional induction measure introduced; no administration official has linked conscription to Iran. EXECUTION INDICATORS AT ZERO: no mobilisation order; no reserve call-up; no hospital-ship movement; no casualty-reception surge; no reported forward fuel/ammunition prepositioning; no dependant evacuation beyond existing levels; no ROE change enabling ground action; no congressional authorisation sought; no ground-operation statement from the Pentagon. SPECIAL OPERATIONS: no new reported activity. The standing record is the March Washington Post reporting that the Pentagon prepared for weeks of limited ground operations including possible special-operations and infantry raids on Kharg Island — prepared, never executed — and the July Foreign Policy assessment that former US officials and experts do not see the United States moving toward a major ground operation.",
   "prediction_impact": "Corpus predictions of a US GROUND INVASION OF IRAN, a MARINE LANDING, or a SEIZURE OF KHARG ISLAND OR THE STRAIT remain DISCONFIRMED at Day 183. This cycle strengthens the disconfirmation on force-generation grounds, which is the strongest evidentiary basis the tracker has available short of a statement of policy: the United States is ROTATING its carrier force one-for-one rather than massing it, sustaining a single carrier in theatre against two in July and three in April, and has told the incoming strike group's crews to plan for eight months. Amphibious and ground-assault operations are preceded by concentration, not by relief-in-place. The parallel revival of maritime prize courts is institutional evidence pointing the same way: the United States is building durable legal machinery for condemning seized shipping, which is an investment in the maritime rung's longevity. The counter-indicators are recorded and not discounted. A third carrier strike group is being generated for this theatre; the Boxer ARG with approximately 2,200 Marines of the 11th MEU has now spent 59 days in the Arabian Sea without publicly reported tasking, which preserves rather than forecloses an amphibious option; and a former Mossad chief stated publicly that there is no choice but to strike until the Iranian regime falls, alongside a US Treasury Secretary who has already declared regime collapse the objective. Declared regime-collapse aims are the class of war aim that has historically preceded ground commitment when economic instruments fail to deliver, and the tracker holds that as the principal live pathway to a status change. The USS Tripoli's location in port at Sasebo, Japan is logged explicitly as a negative datapoint on a standing indicator. Predictions of a US DRAFT or CONSCRIPTION remain DISCONFIRMED. The one scheduled change is automatic Selective Service registration effective December 18, 2026, which enlarges the registration pool and confers no induction authority.",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "The Theodore Roosevelt deployment is read as routine force sustainment — a one-for-one relief keeping a single carrier on station and evidence that Washington is settling in for a long air-and-naval campaign — and, by the opposing reading, as the front end of a renewed build-up, on the grounds that generating a third strike group for one theatre inside seven months and telling its crews to plan for eight is how a sustained offensive capability is kept available for a decision that has not yet been made.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented: the USS Theodore Roosevelt Carrier Strike Group will deploy from San Diego to the Middle East within weeks, on a deployment expected to last at least seven months with crews told to plan for eight, relieving the USS George Washington. Documented: the George Washington entered the Fifth Fleet area in the week of August 20, relieving the USS Abraham Lincoln, which departed CENTCOM on August 22 after more than 200 days. Documented: the United States has maintained a continuous carrier presence in the Arabian Sea since late February. Documented: CENTCOM's last public figure, given in July, cited two aircraft carriers; the April posture logged by this project was three. Documented: Axios reported on August 25 that US officials expect no new strikes on Iran 'for the time being,' with a second official putting the horizon past the midterm elections. Not documented: whether the Theodore Roosevelt's arrival will overlap with the George Washington's departure, any tasking order, or any change in the number of carriers intended to be on station.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "US Navy officials cited in reporting of the deployment timeline and the eight-month planning guidance",
      "Forbes and The Times of Israel, reporting the deployment as another supercarrier being sent to the Middle East",
      "USNI News and Stars and Stripes, reporting the sequence as a relief-in-place",
      "Commentators reading continuous carrier presence into 2027 as evidence of an open-ended campaign"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "The distinction between sustainment and build-up turns on whether the strike groups will overlap on station and on force-planning intent, neither of which is in the open record. Until arrival and departure dates are published, a relief and a reinforcement are indistinguishable from the announcement alone.",
     "open_question_ref": "three-carrier-posture-2026"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-08-28",
   "theatre": "Iran / Strait of Hormuz",
   "tag": "Gulf States",
   "headline": "GULF STATES — DOHA TOOK ITS TURN IN TEHRAN AND A GREEK PATRIOT BATTERY DEFENDED SAUDI OIL. QATAR'S PRIME MINISTER AND FOREIGN MINISTER SHEIKH MOHAMMED BIN ABDULRAHMAN BIN JASSIM AL THANI SPENT AUGUST 27 IN TEHRAN, MEETING ARAGHCHI, PEZESHKIAN, GHALIBAF AND SECURITY CHIEF MOHSEN REZAI ON THE TEMPORARY CORRIDOR, THE JOINT MINE-CLEARING PROPOSAL AND THE CONDITIONS FOR RENEWED US-IRAN DIALOGUE — THE THIRD MEDIATOR THROUGH TEHRAN IN FOUR DAYS AFTER PAKISTAN'S ASIM MUNIR AND OMAN'S BADR ALBUSAIDI. SHEIKH MOHAMMED: 'DIALOGUE AND DIPLOMATIC SOLUTIONS REMAIN THE OPTIMAL PATH FOR ADDRESSING DISPUTES.' REZAI GAVE THE UNMOVED IRANIAN PRICE: WASHINGTON 'MUST FIRST TAKE PRACTICAL STEPS' — LIFT THE BLOCKADE, UNFREEZE ASSETS, REMOVE OIL SANCTIONS. ON THE SAME DAY AN AIR-DEFENCE BATTERY OPERATED BY GREEK MILITARY PERSONNEL IN SAUDI ARABIA ENGAGED A DRONE SWARM OVER THE YANBU OIL-REFINING REGION, LAUNCHING THREE MISSILES AND DOWNING THREE HOUTHI UAVS IN FULL COORDINATION WITH SAUDI AIR DEFENCES — THE THIRD COMBAT USE OF THE GREEK SYSTEM SINCE THE WAR BEGAN, AND A REMINDER THAT THE RED SEA FLANK OF THE BYPASS ROUTE IS ITSELF UNDER FIRE. THE COST OF THE WAR TO THE GULF WAS QUANTIFIED IN PORT CALLS ON AUGUST 27: KUWAIT DOWN 86%, THE UAE DOWN 69% FROM 78 DAILY CALLS TO 24, QATAR, IRAQ AND BAHRAIN DOWN 66-68%, AND SAUDI ARABIA DOWN ONLY 15% BECAUSE IT HAS PIPELINES AND A RED SEA COAST. THE ASYMMETRY IS THE STORY: THE STATES WITHOUT AN ALTERNATIVE COASTLINE ARE CARRYING THE BLOCKADE.",
   "tags": [
    "Gulf States",
    "Qatar Mediation",
    "Sheikh Mohammed bin Abdulrahman Al Thani",
    "Tehran Visit",
    "Mohsen Rezai",
    "Practical Steps First",
    "Third Mediator In Four Days",
    "Asim Munir",
    "Badr Albusaidi",
    "Yanbu",
    "Greek Patriot Battery",
    "Three Missiles Three UAVs",
    "Third Combat Use",
    "Houthi Drones",
    "Saudi Air Defence",
    "Port Calls Collapse",
    "Kuwait -86%",
    "UAE -69%",
    "Saudi -15%",
    "Bypass Asymmetry",
    "UKMTO 121-26"
   ],
   "source": "Al Jazeera, 'Iran, Qatar hold Hormuz talks amid int'l hopes dialogue with US will resume,' August 27, 2026; Al Arabiya English, 'Qatar PM pursues mediation in Tehran as US ramps up ‘economic war’,' August 27, 2026; The Jerusalem Post, 'Greek Patriot system engages drone swarm near Saudi Yanbu oil refinery,' August 27, 2026; Greek City Times, 'Greek Forces Successfully Defend Saudi Arabia Against Drone Attacks,' August 27, 2026; Reuters via Just Security 'Early Edition: August 27, 2026'; Al Jazeera, 'How a 95 percent drop in Hormuz traffic changed global shipping,' August 27, 2026 (port-call data by country); ABC News live updates, August 27, 2026 (UKMTO Warning 121-26).",
   "summary": "DOHA TOOK ITS TURN. Qatar's Prime Minister and Foreign Minister SHEIKH MOHAMMED BIN ABDULRAHMAN BIN JASSIM AL THANI spent AUGUST 27 in Tehran, meeting Foreign Minister ABBAS ARAGHCHI, President MASOUD PEZESHKIAN, Parliament Speaker MOHAMMAD BAGHER GHALIBAF and security chief MOHSEN REZAI. Iranian Foreign Ministry spokesman ESMAEIL BAGHAEI said the talks covered the continuation of Qatar's mediation and wider regional developments. The published agenda was the proposed Hormuz framework — a TEMPORARY SHIPPING CORRIDOR and a JOINT MINE-CLEARING OPERATION — together with 'efforts being made to reduce escalation' and the creation of conditions for renewed US-Iran dialogue. Sheikh Mohammed said 'DIALOGUE AND DIPLOMATIC SOLUTIONS REMAIN THE OPTIMAL PATH FOR ADDRESSING DISPUTES,' stressing respect for freedom of navigation in accordance with international law.\n\nTHE IRANIAN PRICE DID NOT MOVE FOR THE THIRD MEDIATOR RUNNING. REZAI told the Qatari delegation that the United States 'MUST FIRST TAKE PRACTICAL STEPS TO FULFIL IRAN'S CONDITIONS' before the strait reopens: lift the naval blockade, unfreeze assets, remove oil sanctions. This is the third mediator in Tehran in four days — Pakistan's army chief ASIM MUNIR on AUGUST 24, Oman's BADR ALBUSAIDI on AUGUST 25, Qatar on AUGUST 27 — and Qatar in particular carries standing, having brokered the June arrangement that briefly halted hostilities. Three mediators, one unchanged price, and an American position that there are no talks or conversations scheduled.\n\nA EUROPEAN BATTERY DEFENDED SAUDI OIL. On AUGUST 27 an air-defence system operated by GREEK MILITARY PERSONNEL in Saudi Arabia engaged a swarm of drones in the wider YANBU region, which contains major oil refining facilities. The Greek PATRIOT battery launched THREE MISSILES and intercepted THREE HOUTHI UNMANNED AERIAL VEHICLES, operating in full coordination with the air-defence systems of the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia. It was the THIRD COMBAT USE of the Greek-operated system since the Iran war began. The audit records two things about this. First, Yanbu is the Red Sea terminus of the East-West pipeline — the principal Saudi route around Hormuz — so the bypass that has kept Saudi port calls down only 15% is itself now a target set. Second, the interceptor was European and the target was Houthi: the war's air defence of Gulf energy infrastructure has become a multinational undertaking without a multinational command.\n\nTHE COST TO THE GULF WAS QUANTIFIED. Al Jazeera's AUGUST 27 analysis put the change in daily port calls by country: KUWAIT DOWN 86%; the UAE DOWN 69%, from 78 daily calls to 24; QATAR, IRAQ and BAHRAIN DOWN 66-68%; and SAUDI ARABIA DOWN ONLY 15%, because it has pipeline capacity and a Red Sea coast. Gulf crude exports overall are down 47%, from about 17 million barrels a day to about 9 million. RICHARD MATTHEWS of Gibson Shipbrokers stated the structural fact plainly: 'THERE IS NO ALTERNATIVE MARITIME ROUTE. THERE ARE SOME PIPELINES, BUT THERE'S NO ALTERNATIVE.' The asymmetry is the finding: the blockade's cost is distributed across the Gulf in inverse proportion to how much non-Hormuz coastline a state possesses, which is why Riyadh can underwrite workarounds while Kuwait and Qatar cannot.\n\nTHE WATERWAY REMAINS DANGEROUS FOR GULF-BOUND TRADE. UKMTO Warning 121-26 recorded a tanker struck by an unknown projectile in the Strait on AUGUST 27, the second vessel hit this week, with fire extinguished and crew safe. The commercial workarounds logged in the previous cycle continue: ADNOC running a shuttle service with transponders off, Saudi Arabia running ship-to-ship transfers under government-backed insurance, and TankerTrackers counting more than fifteen ship-to-ship operations moving about 25 million barrels in the Gulf of Oman from 'every country in the region, minus Iran.'",
   "details": "QATAR (August 27): PM/FM Sheikh Mohammed bin Abdulrahman bin Jassim Al Thani in Tehran; met Araghchi, Pezeshkian, Ghalibaf, security chief Mohsen Rezai; Iranian MFA spokesman Esmaeil Baghaei confirmed the mediation agenda; topics — temporary shipping corridor, joint mine-clearing, de-escalation, conditions for renewed US-Iran dialogue. Sheikh Mohammed: 'Dialogue and diplomatic solutions remain the optimal path for addressing disputes'; freedom of navigation to be respected in accordance with international law. Rezai: the US 'must first take practical steps to fulfil Iran's conditions' — lift the naval blockade, unfreeze assets, remove oil sanctions. Sequence of mediators: Asim Munir (Pakistan) August 24; Badr Albusaidi (Oman) August 25; Qatar August 27. Qatar brokered the June arrangement. YANBU (August 27): air-defence system operated by Greek military personnel in Saudi Arabia engaged a drone swarm in the wider Yanbu oil-refining region; Greek PATRIOT battery launched three missiles and intercepted three Houthi UAVs; full coordination with Saudi air defences; third combat use of the Greek-operated system since the war began. Yanbu is the Red Sea terminus of the East-West pipeline. PORT CALLS (Al Jazeera, August 27): Kuwait -86%; UAE -69% (78 to 24 daily calls); Qatar, Iraq, Bahrain -66-68%; Saudi Arabia -15% (pipeline and Red Sea alternatives). Gulf crude exports -47% (~17m bpd to ~9m bpd). Richard Matthews (Gibson Shipbrokers): 'there is no alternative maritime route. There are some pipelines, but there's no alternative.' SHIPPING RISK: UKMTO Warning 121-26 — tanker hit by unknown projectile in the Strait of Hormuz, August 27, fire extinguished, crew safe, second vessel struck this week. Workarounds continuing: ADNOC shuttle with transponders off; Saudi ship-to-ship transfers under government-backed insurance; TankerTrackers — 15+ STS operations moving ~25m barrels in the Gulf of Oman, from 'every country in the region, minus Iran.' QATAR ECONOMY: unchanged — IMF April vintage projects -14.7% for 2026, ~17% of LNG export capacity lost, ~$20bn of annual revenue foregone since the March Ras Laffan strike.",
   "prediction_impact": "Predictions that the Gulf monarchies would be DRAWN INTO THE WAR AS BELLIGERENTS remain DISCONFIRMED: Qatar, Oman and Pakistan are acting as mediators, and the one act of force by a Gulf-based system in this cycle was DEFENSIVE and executed by GREEK personnel against HOUTHI drones. Predictions that the Gulf states would SPLIT FROM WASHINGTON are PARTIALLY CONFIRMED in form and unresolved in substance: three US-aligned states have now carried Iranian terms to Tehran within four days while the White House says there are no talks scheduled, which is diplomatic divergence without policy rupture. Predictions that the blockade would impose SEVERE COSTS ON GULF ENERGY EXPORTERS are CONFIRMED and now quantified by country: port calls down 86% in Kuwait, 69% in the UAE and 66-68% in Qatar, Iraq and Bahrain, against 15% in Saudi Arabia. The distribution confirms a specific corpus claim about chokepoint geography — that possession of non-Hormuz coastline, not alignment or wealth, determines exposure. Predictions that the BYPASS ROUTES WOULD PROVIDE A DURABLE ALTERNATIVE are WEAKENED: the Yanbu engagement places the Red Sea terminus of the principal Saudi bypass under direct attack, and Gibson Shipbrokers' assessment that 'there is no alternative maritime route' is the clearest expert statement yet against the substitution thesis."
  },
