    "No New Figures"
   ],
   "source": "CNN, 'Storms disrupt Cuba's power restoration, operator says, less than a day after sixth nationwide blackout,' August 3, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Cuba's power grid collapses again despite restoration efforts,' August 3, 2026; CBC News, 'More misery for Cubans as power grid fails again amid summertime heat,' August 2026; CNN Politics, 'Venezuela's Nicolas Maduro gets a June trial date,' July 22, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'US judge sets 2027 trial date for Venezuela's Maduro and wife,' July 22, 2026; Axios, 'US eases Venezuela bank sanctions amid South American nation's economic woes,' April 14, 2026; CNN, 'Her boss is in US prison. How the woman running Venezuela got Trump on her side,' June 14, 2026.",
   "summary": "NO NEW PUBLICATION, NO IMPROVEMENT. Neither Havana nor Caracas published a new grid figure or a new rationing measure on AUGUST 27-28, and the audit therefore carries the figures of record forward rather than inventing movement.\n\nCUBA. The national electricity system has collapsed SIX TIMES IN 2026 — twice in March, three times within ten days in July, and once on AUGUST 2-3, when thunderstorms disrupted the restoration attempt and produced two nationwide outages within hours. Blackouts EXCEED TWENTY HOURS A DAY and some communities have gone up to 105 CONSECUTIVE DAYS without steady power. The grid remains fragile after repeated collapses, worsened by fuel shortages tied to US sanctions and to the collapse of Venezuelan supply. Neighbourhood protests have been recorded in Havana municipalities after outages exceeding forty-eight hours.\n\nVENEZUELA. Pot-banging protests continue across the country over recurring blackouts and appliance damage from voltage fluctuation, with road blockades and burning tyres recorded in TURMERO, ARAGUA STATE, about 80 kilometres from Caracas, against daily cuts of four to five hours and government-imposed rationing. Acting President DELCY RODRÍGUEZ has activated a national electricity and water savings plan, attributing the shortfall to the 'SUPER NIÑO' climate phenomenon and a recent earthquake; the measures have been openly rejected. The political track opened on AUGUST 6 between JORGE RODRÍGUEZ for the government and DINORAH FIGUERA for the opposition continues with NO PUBLISHED ELECTORAL TIMETABLE.\n\nTHE MADURO CASE. NICOLÁS MADURO and CILIA FLORES remain in US custody. A federal judge set trial for JUNE 1, 2027. Maduro faces four counts including narco-terrorism conspiracy and cocaine importation conspiracy and could face life imprisonment; both have pleaded not guilty while denying the legitimacy of the proceedings.\n\nCOLOMBIA. The de la Espriella government continues its hardline programme and the severance of relations with Cuba and Nicaragua. No new development was reported this cycle.\n\nTHE STRUCTURAL POINT IS UNCHANGED. The energy crisis that began as a Cuban supply problem has crossed to its supplier: Venezuela, whose oil sector Washington has been reintegrating through Chevron expansion and sanctions relief, is now itself rationing power and absorbing street protest. The government carrying that protest is the one the United States acquired by default when Maduro was removed.",
   "details": "CUBA: six nationwide grid collapses in 2026 (two in March, three within ten days in July, one on August 2-3); blackouts >20 hours/day; up to 105 consecutive days without steady power in some communities; thunderstorms disrupted the August restoration attempt producing two nationwide outages within hours; neighbourhood protests in Havana municipalities after outages beyond 48 hours; fuel shortages tied to US sanctions and Venezuelan supply collapse. No new collapse reported August 27-28. VENEZUELA: pot-banging protests over recurring blackouts and appliance damage from voltage fluctuation; road blockades and burning tyres in Turmero, Aragua (~80 km from Caracas); daily cuts of four to five hours; Delcy Rodríguez's national electricity and water savings plan blamed on 'Super Niño' and an earthquake, openly rejected. Caracas talks opened August 6 under Jorge Rodríguez and Dinorah Figuera; no electoral timetable published. MADURO: Maduro and Cilia Flores in US custody; trial set June 1, 2027; four counts including narco-terrorism conspiracy and cocaine importation conspiracy; life imprisonment possible; both pleaded not guilty and deny the legitimacy of the proceedings. US-VENEZUELA: Chevron expansion under two agreements with the interim government; bank sanctions eased in April; partial opening of state industries; power concentrating around the acting president. COLOMBIA: de la Espriella government's hardline programme and severance of ties with Cuba and Nicaragua continue; no new development this cycle.",
   "prediction_impact": "Predictions of REGIME COLLAPSE IN CUBA remain UNTESTED: six nationwide grid collapses, blackouts beyond twenty hours a day and localised protest have not produced institutional failure, and the audit continues to record the distinction between severe deprivation and political rupture. Predictions that US INTERVENTION IN VENEZUELA WOULD PRODUCE A STABLE CLIENT GOVERNMENT are PARTIALLY DISCONFIRMED and further weakened by silence: the Rodríguez government has met Washington's principal demands and is nonetheless rationing electricity, facing street protest, and running a political negotiation that has published no electoral timetable three weeks after opening. Predictions that MADURO'S REMOVAL WOULD BE FOLLOWED BY RAPID NORMALISATION are DISCONFIRMED as to timeline: the trial is set for June 1, 2027, more than seventeen months after his capture. No corpus prediction is advanced or refuted by a cycle in which nothing was published; the entry exists so that the absence of movement is recorded rather than read as improvement."
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-08-28",
   "theatre": "US-China Trade",
   "tag": "US-China",
   "headline": "US-CHINA — THE CONFRONTATION MOVED FROM OIL TO BANKS, NETWORKS AND THE INDO-PACIFIC IN A SINGLE CYCLE. REPORTING ON AUGUST 27 INDICATED THE ADMINISTRATION IS CONSIDERING SANCTIONS AGAINST CHINESE BANKS CONDUCTING BUSINESS WITH IRAN — THE ESCALATION BEIJING WAS WARNED ABOUT WHEN OPERATION ECONOMIC OUTCAST DEFERRED SECONDARY SANCTIONS BEHIND A CURE PERIOD, AND THE ONE MEASURE THAT WOULD TEST LIN JIAN'S PLEDGE TO 'TAKE ALL NECESSARY MEASURES.' ON THE SAME DAY THE JUSTICE DEPARTMENT ANNOUNCED THAT CHINESE HACKERS HAD CARRIED OUT A STRING OF INTRUSIONS AGAINST FEDERAL AGENCIES AND HOSPITALS OVER THE PAST YEAR, MOSTLY LINKED TO THE STATE-SPONSORED GROUP QTFY — MOVING THE DISPUTE ONTO THE CYBER RUNG WHILE THE TARIFF AND OIL TRACKS RUN. AND PENTAGON OFFICIALS WERE REPORTED WORRIED THAT A PROLONGED IRAN CONFLICT COULD EMBOLDEN CHINA IN THE INDO-PACIFIC — THE OVERSTRETCH THESIS, STATED INSIDE THE BUILDING RATHER THAN BY ITS CRITICS, AND THE MOST DIRECT PIECE OF EVIDENCE THE AUDIT HAS YET LOGGED FOR THE CORPUS CLAIM THAT THE IRAN WAR IS A STRATEGIC GIFT TO BEIJING. THE UNDERLYING VOLUMES CONTINUE TO FALL WITHOUT ANY DESIGNATION HAVING BITTEN: CHINESE IMPORTS OF IRANIAN CRUDE VIA HORMUZ RUN AT ABOUT 530,000 BARRELS A DAY, 48% BELOW PRE-WAR AND 72% BELOW THE OCTOBER 2024 PEAK. THE SEPARATE TARIFF TRACK IS UNCHANGED AND STILL POINTED AT THE SEPTEMBER 24 XI-TRUMP SUMMIT, WITH THE TRUCE EXPIRING NOVEMBER 10 AND THE JULY 23 FORCED-LABOUR TARIFF OF 12.5% IN FORCE SINCE JULY 24. TAIWAN: NO ESCALATION; THE 15% US TARIFF RATE AGREED IN JANUARY STANDS AND PLA SORTIE RATES REMAIN AT THE PRE-LAI BASELINE.",
   "tags": [
    "US-China",
    "Chinese Banks Sanctions Considered",
    "Secondary Sanctions",
    "Cure Period",
    "Lin Jian All Necessary Measures",
    "QTFY",
    "Justice Department",
    "Federal Agencies And Hospitals",
    "Cyber Rung",
    "Pentagon Indo-Pacific Concern",
    "Overstretch Thesis",
    "Kpler 530000 bpd",
    "48 Percent Below Pre-War",
    "Section 301",
    "September 24 Summit",
    "Truce Expires November 10",
    "Forced Labour Tariff 12.5%",
    "Taiwan 15 Percent",
    "No Escalation"
   ],
   "source": "Just Security, 'Early Edition: August 27, 2026' (Justice Department announcement on state-linked intrusion set QTFY; Pentagon concern that a prolonged Iran conflict could embolden China in the Indo-Pacific); The Times of Israel liveblog, August 27, 2026 (administration weighing designations against Chinese banks); Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the PRC, 'Foreign Ministry Spokesperson Lin Jian's Regular Press Conference,' August 2026; Al Jazeera, 'China says will safeguard its own interests as US expands Iran sanctions,' August 25, 2026; Global Times, 'China vows to do ‘what is necessary’ to safeguard rights,' August 2026; The China-Global South Project, 'China, U.S., Iran sanctions and international law,' August 26, 2026; Congressional Research Service, 'U.S.-Taiwan Trade and Economic Relations' (IF10256); Trade Compliance Resource Hub, 'Trump 2.0 tariff tracker,' July 27, 2026.",
   "summary": "THE ESCALATION BEIJING WAS WARNED ABOUT IS NOW REPORTEDLY UNDER CONSIDERATION. Reporting on AUGUST 27 indicated that the administration is weighing SANCTIONS AGAINST CHINESE BANKS conducting business with Iran. This is the specific measure that Operation Economic Outcast, unveiled on AUGUST 24, held back: five sectoral determinations and nearly 60 designations were issued, but most secondary sanctions were deferred behind a 'cure period' and no partner country was named. Designating a Chinese financial institution would convert a deferred threat into an act, and it is the measure against which LIN JIAN's pledge that 'CHINA WILL TAKE ALL NECESSARY MEASURES TO FIRMLY SAFEGUARD ITS OWN RIGHTS AND INTERESTS' would actually be tested.\n\nTHE DISPUTE OPENED A CYBER FRONT ON THE SAME DAY. The Justice Department announced that CHINESE HACKERS carried out a string of intrusions targeting FEDERAL AGENCIES AND HOSPITALS over the past year, mostly linked to the state-sponsored group QTFY. The audit records this as a widening of the confrontation's surface rather than as a response to the Iran file: the intrusions are described as running over a year, and no linkage to the sanctions dispute was asserted in the announcement.\n\nTHE OVERSTRETCH THESIS WAS STATED FROM INSIDE THE PENTAGON. Officials were reported on AUGUST 27 to be concerned that a PROLONGED IRAN CONFLICT COULD EMBOLDEN CHINA IN THE INDO-PACIFIC. This is significant for the audit because the corpus contains a recurring claim — that American entanglement in the Middle East transfers strategic initiative to Beijing — which the project has previously been able to log only as commentary by outside analysts. It is now attributed to serving officials in the department running the war. It is a concern, not a policy, and is logged at that weight.\n\nTHE VOLUMES KEEP FALLING WITHOUT A DESIGNATION HAVING BITTEN. Kpler data published AUGUST 26 puts Chinese imports of Iranian crude through Hormuz at about 530,000 BARRELS A DAY — 48% BELOW PRE-WAR levels and 72% BELOW the October 2024 peak of 1.9 million — with the Red Sea route at about 1.02 million barrels a day in August against an April peak of 2.62 million. Shandong independent refiners continue substituting, with Brazilian Lapa and Iraqi Basrah grades reported. The blockade and the freight cost, not the sanctions list, are doing the work: Suez freight runs $10-11 a barrel against roughly $5 via Yanbu before the Houthi campaign, and the Cape route takes about 50 days against 25-27.\n\nTHE TARIFF TRACK IS UNCHANGED. The 7.5% Section 301 overcapacity tariff remains prepared ahead of the SEPTEMBER 24 XI-TRUMP SUMMIT; the November 2025 truce that cut bilateral tariffs from 125% to 10% expires NOVEMBER 10, 2026; the USTR's 12.5% forced-labour tariff on all Chinese goods, imposed JULY 23, took effect JULY 24 and drew condemnation from Beijing without announced countermeasures. The Commerce Department's BIS closed comments on AUGUST 27 on expanding the Section 232 product list for steel, aluminium and copper.\n\nTAIWAN. No escalation. The 15% US tariff rate agreed with Taipei in January stands, reduced from the 32% imposed in 2025, with electronics, semiconductors and Section 232 goods excepted. PLA sortie rates around the island remain at the pre-Lai baseline.",
   "details": "BANKS (August 27 reporting): administration considering sanctions against Chinese banks conducting business with Iran; Operation Economic Outcast (August 24) issued five sectoral determinations and ~60 designations but deferred most secondary sanctions behind a 'cure period' without naming partner countries. CYBER (August 27): Justice Department announced Chinese hackers carried out a string of intrusions targeting federal agencies and hospitals over the past year, mostly linked to state-sponsored group QTFY. INDO-PACIFIC (August 27): Pentagon officials reported concerned that a prolonged Iran conflict could embolden China in the Indo-Pacific. BEIJING'S POSITION: Lin Jian — new sanctions 'will only further intensify tensions and conflicts'; China's cooperation with Iran 'has always been conducted within the framework of international law and should not be disrupted or undermined'; 'China is closely following relevant developments and will take all necessary measures to firmly safeguard its own rights and interests'; measures characterised as 'illicit unilateral sanctions that have no basis in international law or the authorization of the UN Security Council'; 'the most urgent task is to de-escalate tensions and return to negotiations.' VOLUMES (Kpler, published August 26): Chinese imports of Iranian crude via Hormuz ~530,000 bpd, -48% vs pre-war and -72% vs the October 2024 peak of 1.9m bpd; Red Sea route ~1.02m bpd in August vs an April peak of 2.62m; Shandong teapots substituting Brazilian Lapa and Iraqi Basrah; Suez freight $10-11/bbl vs ~$5 via Yanbu pre-Houthi campaign; Cape route ~50 days vs 25-27. TARIFFS: 7.5% Section 301 overcapacity tariff prepared ahead of the September 24 Xi-Trump summit; November 2025 truce (125% to 10%) expires November 10, 2026; USTR 12.5% forced-labour tariff on all Chinese goods imposed July 23, effective July 24, condemned by Beijing without announced countermeasures; BIS Section 232 expansion comment period on steel, aluminium and copper closed August 27. TAIWAN: 15% US tariff rate agreed January 2026 (down from 32% in 2025), excepting electronics, semiconductors and Section 232 goods; PLA sortie rates at the pre-Lai baseline; no escalation this cycle.",
   "prediction_impact": "Predictions of a US-CHINA MILITARY CONFRONTATION or a TAIWAN CRISIS remain UNTESTED and are not advanced: PLA activity around Taiwan is at baseline and the tariff calendar is intact. Predictions that the IRAN WAR WOULD PULL CHINA INTO CONFRONTATION WITH WASHINGTON are PARTIALLY CONFIRMED and moved a rung this cycle: the reported consideration of sanctions on Chinese banks would be the first measure to touch the Chinese financial system directly, and Beijing has pre-committed to 'all necessary measures' in response. The audit notes that this remains a REPORTED CONSIDERATION and not an action. Predictions that AMERICAN ENTANGLEMENT IN THE MIDDLE EAST WOULD TRANSFER STRATEGIC INITIATIVE TO BEIJING are strengthened by the best available class of evidence: the concern is now attributed to Pentagon officials rather than to outside commentators. Predictions that SANCTIONS WOULD SEVER CHINA-IRAN OIL TRADE are PARTIALLY CONFIRMED as to outcome and DISCONFIRMED as to mechanism — Chinese imports of Iranian crude via Hormuz are down 48% from pre-war and 72% from the 2024 peak, but the causes documented in the record are the naval blockade, freight costs and route length, not any designation yet enforced against a Chinese entity."
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-08-28",
   "theatre": "East Asia — DPRK / Korean Peninsula",
   "tag": "North Korea",
   "headline": "NORTH KOREA — AN EIGHTH CONSECUTIVE DAY WITHOUT A LAUNCH AND A SEVENTH WITHOUT A WORD ABOUT WASHINGTON. NO DPRK MISSILE ACTIVITY HAS BEEN REPORTED SINCE THE ROUGHLY TEN KN-25 SHORT-RANGE BALLISTIC MISSILES FIRED ON AUGUST 20, AND PYONGYANG HAS STILL NOT PUBLICISED THAT LAUNCH DOMESTICALLY. THE AUGUST 25 ENLARGED POLITBURO MEETING OF THE WORKERS' PARTY CENTRAL COMMITTEE, REPORTED ON AUGUST 26, REVIEWED MAJOR ECONOMIC SECTORS AND 2026 POLICY GOALS AND MADE NO REFERENCE TO PRESIDENT TRUMP'S RENEWED OVERTURE OR TO A POSSIBLE SUMMIT — THE MOST SENIOR PARTY FORUM SINCE THE EXERCISE CONCESSION, PASSING IN SILENCE ON THE ONE SUBJECT WASHINGTON IS WAITING ON. AUGUST 27 WAS THE DATE ON WHICH ULCHI FREEDOM SHIELD'S COUNTEROFFENSIVE PHASE WAS ORIGINALLY SCHEDULED TO END; IT NEVER TOOK PLACE, THE ELEVEN-DAY TWO-PHASE EXERCISE HAVING BEEN CUT TO FIVE DAYS AS A GOODWILL GESTURE AND ENDED ON AUGUST 21. THAT DATE HAS NOW PASSED WITHOUT A DPRK ACKNOWLEDGEMENT, A LAUNCH, OR A SUMMIT DATE. THE CONCESSION WAS MADE, THE PROVOCATION WAS WITHHELD, AND NOTHING HAS BEEN BUILT ON EITHER.",
   "tags": [
    "North Korea",
    "No Launch Since August 20",
    "KN-25",
    "Unpublicised Domestically",
    "Enlarged Politburo August 25",
    "No Mention Of Trump",
    "No Summit Date",
    "Ulchi Freedom Shield",
    "Counteroffensive Phase Never Held",
    "Cut From Eleven Days To Five",
    "Kim Yo Jong",
    "Quiet Cycle"
   ],
   "source": "Al Jazeera, 'North Korea launches ballistic missiles as US-South Korea drills end,' August 20, 2026; US News/Reuters, 'North Korea Launches 10 Ballistic Missiles as US-South Korea Drills Near End,' August 20, 2026; Stars and Stripes, 'North Korea fires first ballistic missile since April,' August 6, 2026; KCNA via Reuters reporting of the August 25 enlarged Politburo meeting, published August 26, 2026.",
   "summary": "AN EIGHTH DAY WITHOUT A LAUNCH. No North Korean missile activity has been reported since roughly TEN KN-25 SHORT-RANGE BALLISTIC MISSILES were fired on AUGUST 20 during the closing phase of the US-South Korea exercise. South Korea's presidential office condemned that launch and ordered full military readiness; the launch followed KIM YO JONG's condemnation of the drills the previous day, which YANG MOO-JIN of the University of North Korean Studies read as an effort to show her warning was 'NOT MERE RHETORIC.' Pyongyang has still not publicised the launch to its own population.\n\nTHE MOST SENIOR PARTY FORUM SINCE THE CONCESSION SAID NOTHING ABOUT THE UNITED STATES. KIM JONG UN presided on AUGUST 25 over an ENLARGED MEETING OF THE POLITICAL BUREAU of the Workers' Party Central Committee, reported by state media on AUGUST 26. The published account reviewed progress in 'MAJOR ECONOMIC SECTORS' and implementation of 2026 policy goals, with Kim calling for 'EXTRAORDINARY RESPONSIBILITY AND HIGH PRUDENCE' in major state projects and citing 'A SERIES OF DEVIATIONS' caused by 'the immature operation and leadership of the economic guidance organs.' It carried NO REFERENCE to President Trump's renewed overture or to a possible summit.\n\nTHE DATE THAT PASSED. AUGUST 27 was the day on which the COUNTEROFFENSIVE PHASE of ULCHI FREEDOM SHIELD was originally scheduled to conclude. It never took place. The eleven-day, two-phase exercise was CUT TO FIVE DAYS as a goodwill gesture and ended on AUGUST 21, deleting the half of the programme Pyongyang denounces as an invasion rehearsal. Trump cut the drills short and said he wanted to revive diplomacy with Kim Jong Un. The date has now passed without a DPRK acknowledgement of the concession, without a launch marking it, and without a summit date or venue.\n\nTHE AUDIT'S READING. A concession was made and a provocation was withheld, and neither side has built on either. The Korean track is the one theatre in this briefing where the absence of events is itself the development: eight days of quiet after a launch that was never announced domestically, and a senior party meeting that declined to mention the American opening at all.",
   "details": "LAUNCH RECORD: ~10 KN-25 short-range ballistic missiles fired August 20 during the closing phase of the US-South Korea exercise; South Korea's presidential office condemned the launch and ordered full readiness; preceded by Kim Yo Jong's August 19 condemnation of the drills; Yang Moo-jin (University of North Korean Studies) read the launch as showing her warning was 'not mere rhetoric'; still unpublicised inside the DPRK. No launch reported since. Earlier 2026 activity: first ballistic missile since April fired from the Wonsan area in early August. POLITBURO (August 25, reported August 26): Kim Jong Un presided over an enlarged meeting of the Political Bureau of the WPK Central Committee; reviewed major economic sectors and 2026 policy goals; called for 'extraordinary responsibility and high prudence'; cited 'a series of deviations' from 'the immature operation and leadership of the economic guidance organs'; no reference to Trump's overture or to a summit. EXERCISE: Ulchi Freedom Shield cut from eleven days and two phases to five days; ended August 21; the counteroffensive phase originally scheduled through August 27 never took place; Trump shortened the drills and said he wanted to revive diplomacy with Kim. DIPLOMACY: no summit date, no venue, no DPRK acknowledgement of the exercise concession.",
   "prediction_impact": "Predictions of a KOREAN PENINSULA CRISIS or a DPRK NUCLEAR TEST remain UNTESTED and are not advanced: eight days have passed without a launch, the August 20 salvo was short-range and remains unannounced inside North Korea, and no test preparation has been reported. Predictions of a RENEWED TRUMP-KIM SUMMIT remain UNTESTED and are weakened by silence rather than by refusal: the most senior party forum held since the exercise concession made no reference to the American overture, which is a datapoint about Pyongyang's public positioning and not necessarily about its private channel. Predictions that AMERICAN CONCESSIONS WOULD PRODUCE RECIPROCITY are DISCONFIRMED for this cycle: the halving of Ulchi Freedom Shield, including deletion of the phase Pyongyang objects to most, has produced no acknowledgement, no reciprocal restraint that can be distinguished from ordinary quiet, and no negotiating date."
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-08-28",
   "theatre": "Iran / Strait of Hormuz",
   "tag": "Hormuz-Dependent Economies",
   "headline": "HORMUZ-DEPENDENT ECONOMIES — THE SIX-MONTH TOLL WAS PUT INTO NUMBERS, AND THE NUMBER IS NEARLY 95%. AL JAZEERA'S AUGUST 27 ANALYSIS PUTS TRANSITS AT ABOUT FIVE VESSELS A DAY BETWEEN JULY 15 AND AUGUST 23 AGAINST A PRE-WAR BASELINE OF ABOUT 100 — A FALL OF NEARLY 95% — WITH GULF CRUDE EXPORTS DOWN 47% FROM ROUGHLY 17 MILLION BARRELS A DAY TO ABOUT 9 MILLION, COMBINED GULF SHIPMENTS DOWN FROM ABOUT 400 MILLION BARRELS IN FEBRUARY TO ABOUT 200 MILLION IN JULY, AND ANALYSTS PUTTING 5-7 MILLION BARRELS A DAY OF SUPPLY DISRUPTED. THE STRAIT CARRIES 33% OF GLOBAL SEABORNE CRUDE AND 29% OF LPG FLOWS AND THERE IS NO SUBSTITUTE: RICHARD MATTHEWS OF GIBSON SHIPBROKERS — 'THERE IS NO ALTERNATIVE MARITIME ROUTE. THERE ARE SOME PIPELINES, BUT THERE'S NO ALTERNATIVE.' RATIONING IS NOW THE NORMAL CONDITION ACROSS SOUTH AND SOUTHEAST ASIA: FOUR-DAY WEEKS AND WORK-FROM-HOME MANDATES IN PAKISTAN, BANGLADESH, THE PHILIPPINES, VIETNAM AND SRI LANKA; INDONESIA AND SRI LANKA CAPPING FUEL PURCHASES AT 50 AND 15 LITRES A DAY; INDIA RESTRICTING FOREIGN TRAVEL AND ENCOURAGING WORK FROM HOME; PAKISTAN RESTRICTING BUSINESS HOURS; MALAYSIA CAPPING COOLING. BANGLADESH, INDIA AND PAKISTAN TOOK ALMOST TWO-THIRDS OF THEIR LNG THROUGH HORMUZ IN 2025, AND GAS IS 50% OF BANGLADESH'S AND 25% OF PAKISTAN'S POWER MIX. NORTH ASIA IS DRAWING DOWN RATHER THAN RATIONING: JAPAN HAS BEEN TAKING FROM STRATEGIC RESERVES. EUROPEAN AND ASIAN GAS PRICES ROSE 44% AND 66% BETWEEN FEBRUARY AND MAY WHILE US PRICES FELL 6% — THE CLEANEST SINGLE MEASURE OF WHO IS PAYING FOR THIS WAR.",
   "tags": [
    "Hormuz-Dependent Economies",
    "95 Percent Traffic Drop",
    "Five Vessels A Day",
    "Gulf Crude -47%",
    "5-7 Million Barrels Disrupted",
    "33 Percent Of Seaborne Crude",
    "29 Percent Of LPG",
    "Gibson Shipbrokers",
    "No Alternative Route",
    "Four-Day Weeks",
    "Fuel Caps",
    "Indonesia 50 Litres",
    "Sri Lanka 15 Litres",
    "India LPG",
    "Bangladesh 95 Percent Imported",
    "Pakistan 85 Percent",
    "Japan Strategic Reserves",
    "South Korea 206 Days",
    "Gas Prices +44% Europe +66% Asia",
    "US Prices -6%",
    "Distributional Finding"
   ],
   "source": "Al Jazeera, 'How a 95 percent drop in Hormuz traffic changed global shipping,' August 27, 2026 (Richard Matthews, Gibson Shipbrokers; port-call data for Kuwait, UAE, Qatar, Iraq, Bahrain and Saudi Arabia); International Energy Agency, 'Strait of Hormuz' oil-security page, 2026; Gulf International Forum, 'Hormuz Disruptions and Asia's Energy Resilience,' 2026; ORF Middle East, 'Asia's Energy Stress: Regional Impact of Hormuz Crisis,' 2026; Credendo, 'Global energy crisis triggered by Strait of Hormuz blockade and energy infrastructure damages,' 2026; Global Voices, 'Bangladesh's energy crisis worsens as US's war on Iran drags on,' April 12, 2026; gCaptain, 'Trump Administration Says Gulf Oil Flows Recover to 15 Million Barrels a Day,' August 11, 2026; Reason, 'The Trump administration's Hormuz oil numbers don't add up,' August 12, 2026.",
   "summary": "THE SIX-MONTH TOLL WAS PUT INTO NUMBERS. Al Jazeera published a consolidated dataset on AUGUST 27 measuring what the closure has done to global shipping. TRANSITS: about FIVE VESSELS A DAY between JULY 15 and AUGUST 23, against a pre-war baseline of about 100 a day — a fall of NEARLY 95%. CRUDE: Gulf crude exports down 47%, from roughly 17 million barrels a day to about 9 million; direct crude through the strait averaging 2.2 million barrels a day; analysts estimating 5-7 MILLION BARRELS A DAY of supply disrupted; combined Gulf shipments down from about 400 million barrels in February to about 200 million in July. STRUCTURE: the strait carries 33% OF GLOBAL SEABORNE CRUDE OIL and 29% OF LPG FLOWS, and 80% of world trade by volume moves by sea. PRICE: current levels about 20% above pre-war, against April peaks above $130.\n\nAND THE STRUCTURAL FINDING WAS STATED WITHOUT HEDGE. RICHARD MATTHEWS, director at Gibson Shipbrokers: 'THERE IS NO ALTERNATIVE MARITIME ROUTE. THERE ARE SOME PIPELINES, BUT THERE'S NO ALTERNATIVE.' The audit records this as the most direct expert statement yet against the substitution thesis that has underwritten several corpus predictions about chokepoint resilience.\n\nTHE DISTRIBUTION OF PAIN IS THE FINDING. Port calls by country: KUWAIT DOWN 86%; the UAE DOWN 69%, from 78 daily calls to 24; QATAR, IRAQ and BAHRAIN DOWN 66-68%; SAUDI ARABIA DOWN ONLY 15%, because it has pipeline capacity and a Red Sea coast. Exposure tracks geography, not alignment.\n\nRATIONING IS THE NORMAL CONDITION ACROSS SOUTH AND SOUTHEAST ASIA. Demand-side measures now in force include FOUR-DAY WORKING WEEKS, work-from-home mandates and school closures in PAKISTAN, BANGLADESH, THE PHILIPPINES, VIETNAM and SRI LANKA; INDIA encouraging work from home and restricting foreign travel; BANGLADESH promoting public transport; PAKISTAN restricting business hours; MALAYSIA encouraging limits on cooling temperatures; and hard purchase caps in INDONESIA (50 litres a day) and SRI LANKA (15 litres a day).\n\nTHE STRUCTURAL EXPOSURE BEHIND THOSE MEASURES. BANGLADESH, INDIA AND PAKISTAN imported almost TWO-THIRDS of their total LNG supplies via the Strait of Hormuz in 2025. Natural gas dominates the power sector of Bangladesh and Pakistan, at 50% AND 25% of the electricity supply mix respectively in 2024. Pakistan imports roughly 85% of its supply by tanker; Bangladesh imports about 95% of its petroleum needs. India, which takes 60% of its LPG through the strait, is seeing black-marketing of domestic cylinders and restaurant closures.\n\nNORTH ASIA IS DRAWING DOWN RATHER THAN RATIONING. JAPAN sends the majority of its Middle Eastern crude through the strait — more than 90% of its crude comes from the Middle East, about 75% via Hormuz — and has been DRAWING ON STRATEGIC RESERVES, holding about 146 days. SOUTH KOREA draws about 70% of its crude and 18% of its LNG through Hormuz and holds about 206 days. Korean petrochemicals are already impaired, with Lotte Chemical, LG Chem and Hanwha Solutions having issued potential force majeure notices and Yeochun NCC declaring supply disruption. LNG carrier rates are around $300,000 a day, a 650% increase, and crude tanker rates are at historic highs.\n\nTHE CLEANEST SINGLE MEASURE OF INCIDENCE. Between FEBRUARY and MAY 2026, European natural gas prices rose 44% and Asian prices rose 66%, while US prices DECLINED 6%. The war's energy cost is being paid by importers on the far side of the chokepoint and not by the belligerent that closed the account.\n\nIN THE UNITED STATES the war is a price index rather than a shortage: the AAA national average for regular stands near $4.10 with August 2026 on course to be the highest August on record at a $4.06 month-to-date average against 2022's $3.97, diesel at successive records, and July PCE inflation at 3.7% year on year.",
   "details": "TRANSITS (Al Jazeera, August 27): ~5 vessels/day July 15-August 23 vs ~100/day pre-war = ~95% drop. CRUDE: Gulf crude exports -47% (~17m bpd to ~9m bpd); direct crude via strait 2.2m bpd average; 5-7m bpd estimated disrupted; combined Gulf shipments ~400m barrels (February) to ~200m (July). STRUCTURE: strait carries 33% of global seaborne crude and 29% of LPG flows; 80% of world trade by volume moves by sea. PRICE: ~20% above pre-war; April peaks >$130. PORT CALLS: Kuwait -86%; UAE -69% (78 to 24); Qatar/Iraq/Bahrain -66-68%; Saudi Arabia -15%. QUOTE: Richard Matthews, Gibson Shipbrokers — 'there is no alternative maritime route. There are some pipelines, but there's no alternative.' DEMAND MEASURES: four-day weeks / WFH mandates / school closures in Pakistan, Bangladesh, the Philippines, Vietnam, Sri Lanka; India — WFH encouragement and foreign-travel restrictions; Bangladesh — public transport promotion; Pakistan — restricted business hours; Malaysia — cooling-temperature limits; Indonesia — 50 litres/day fuel cap; Sri Lanka — 15 litres/day fuel cap. EXPOSURE: Bangladesh, India and Pakistan imported almost two-thirds of total LNG via Hormuz in 2025; gas is 50% of Bangladesh's and 25% of Pakistan's electricity mix (2024); Pakistan imports ~85% of supply by tanker; Bangladesh ~95% of petroleum needs; India takes 60% of LPG through the strait, with black-marketing of domestic cylinders and restaurant closures. NORTH ASIA: Japan — >90% of crude from the Middle East, ~75% via Hormuz, ~146 days of reserves, drawing on strategic stocks; South Korea — ~70% of crude and 18% of LNG via Hormuz, ~206 days of reserves; Lotte Chemical, LG Chem and Hanwha Solutions potential force majeure notices; Yeochun NCC supply disruption declared. FREIGHT: LNG carrier rates ~$300,000/day (+650%); crude tanker rates at historic highs. PRICES: European gas +44% and Asian gas +66% February-May 2026; US gas -6%. UNITED STATES: AAA regular ~$4.10; August month-to-date $4.06, highest August on record vs 2022's $3.97; diesel at successive records; July PCE inflation 3.7% y/y.",
   "prediction_impact": "Predictions that A HORMUZ CLOSURE WOULD PRODUCE A GLOBAL ENERGY CRISIS are CONFIRMED and now quantified: transits down nearly 95%, Gulf crude exports down 47%, and 5-7 million barrels a day disrupted at the six-month mark. Predictions that MARKETS WOULD ROUTE AROUND THE CHOKEPOINT are DISCONFIRMED in the terms the corpus used: the industry's own assessment, from Gibson Shipbrokers, is that no alternative maritime route exists and that pipelines are not a substitute, and the port-call data show only Saudi Arabia — the one Gulf state with a Red Sea coast — substantially insulated. Predictions that THE COST WOULD FALL ON THE UNITED STATES are DISCONFIRMED as to incidence and PARTIALLY CONFIRMED as to politics: European and Asian gas prices rose 44% and 66% while US prices fell 6%, but the American cost is arriving as the highest August gasoline average on record and 3.7% PCE inflation. Predictions that SOUTH AND SOUTHEAST ASIAN STATES WOULD BE THE MARGINAL LOSERS are CONFIRMED: hard purchase caps in Indonesia and Sri Lanka, four-day weeks across five countries, and LNG dependence through the strait of almost two-thirds for Bangladesh, India and Pakistan. Predictions that STRATEGIC RESERVES WOULD BRIDGE THE DISRUPTION remain UNTESTED but are entering their decisive phase: Japan is drawing down against about 146 days of cover and South Korea holds about 206, which are cushions measured in months at a disruption now six months old."
  },
