   ],
   "source": "Axios, 'Rubio tells allies US not expected to launch new strikes on Iran,' August 25, 2026 (via Reuters, TASS, Middle East Eye and MarketScreener syndication); Axios, 'Trump announces all mines have been removed from the Strait of Hormuz,' August 25, 2026; Marine Insight, 'Trump Says U.S. Navy Has Cleared All Mines From Strait Of Hormuz Shipping Lane, Warns Iran,' August 25, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Iran, Oman agree on temporary Hormuz route: What we know,' August 26, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Iran, Oman discuss temporary shipping lane through Strait of Hormuz,' August 25, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Why Hormuz remains high risk for ships despite US claims of mine-clearing,' August 26, 2026 (Mark Hilborne, King's College London; Alex Almeida, Ambrey; war-risk insurance at 7.5-10% of hull value against 1-3% pre-war); Al Jazeera, 'Trump tells Al Jazeera “not in a hurry” for Iran to return to talks,' August 26, 2026 (interview with Tanya Noury); Al Jazeera live blog, 'Iran war live: Trump says US “not in a hurry” over talks with Tehran,' August 26, 2026; CBS News live updates, 'Iran War Updates,' entries of August 25-26, 2026 (Glenn Beck podcast quotes; Lin Jian; Araghchi letter to Guterres; Eslami; Shekarchi; Ghalibaf; Akraminia; July PCE at 3.7%; Dubai airport traffic); Fox News Digital live updates, 'Oil tanker attacked in Strait of Hormuz as Iran claims plan to shoulder Trump sanctions,' August 25, 2026 (TankerTrackers ship-to-ship data; bilateral trade-exposure figures; Economy Minister Ali Madanizadeh's two-year plan); The Intercept, 'Sixty More U.S. Soldiers Reported Injured in Iran War,' August 25, 2026 (776 total — 18 killed, 758 wounded; DCAS undercount dispute; WHO figure of 37,181); Reuters via Yahoo Finance, 'Oil prices fall to one-week low as Iran sanctions disappoint,' August 25, 2026 (Ole Hansen, Saxo Bank; BBH strategists; Tim Waterer, KCM); CNBC, 'U.S. crude hovers around $80 as oil prices sink on Hormuz deal hopes,' August 26, 2026; CNBC, 'Oil hits one-week low as investors shrug off latest U.S. sanctions on Iran,' August 25, 2026; Iran International live blog, entries of August 26-27, 2026 (IRGC spokesman Brig. Gen. Hossein Mohebbi; Executive Vice-President Ghaempanah; Energy Secretary Chris Wright; White House Deputy Press Secretary Kelly; Qatari PM visit; Indian tanker warning); TASS, 'Iran detains Indian tanker on southern route in Strait of Hormuz — agency,' August 26, 2026 (Mehr, citing Iran's shipping monitoring agency: tanker HAANA warned to stop; 'no vessel traffic has been observed on the southern route of the Strait of Hormuz over the past 24 hours'); The Irish Times, 'Long queues form at petrol stations in Iran as US blockade triggers fuel shortages,' August 25, 2026; The Washington Post, 'Iran's gas lines are growing, but there's no sign so far that its leaders will bow to US sanctions,' August 25, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Pakistan says significant progress made in talks on Iran-US war,' August 25, 2026; Bloomberg, 'Pakistan Army Chief Ends Tehran Visit in US-Iran Mediation Push,' August 25, 2026; Qatar Tribune, 'PM to visit Tehran for talks on de-escalation, Strait of Hormuz,' August 26, 2026; Middle East Monitor, 'Qatari Prime Minister set to visit Tehran in bid to reduce regional tensions,' August 26, 2026; alanchand.com and Iran International open-market rial quotations, August 25-26, 2026; straits.live status board, 26 August 2026 2300Z; IMF PortWatch via straits.live (3 transits recorded for 23 August 2026).",
   "summary": "THIS CYCLE COVERS TWO DAYS, AND ITS ORGANISING FACT IS THAT THE UNITED STATES SPENT THEM CLEARING THE STRAIT RATHER THAN CLAIMING IT.\n\nTHE POSTURE ACQUIRED A DURATION. On AUGUST 25 Axios reported, citing US officials, that Secretary of State MARCO RUBIO has told several allied foreign ministers in recent days that 'FOR THE TIME BEING' the United States is not expected to launch new strikes against Iran and will instead focus on other means of pressure. Rubio did not rule out strikes if Iran attacks first. A SECOND OFFICIAL told Axios the posture is expected to remain in place UNTIL AFTER THE MIDTERM ELECTIONS, when another military campaign could again be considered. US officials said Washington will concentrate on sanctions, the naval blockade, and 'moving ever-increasing amounts of oil out of the Strait of Hormuz.' For an audit tracking a predicted ground seizure, this is the first time the pause has been given a length, and the length is measured in months rather than days. It is a report from anonymous officials about a future decision and is logged as such, not as a commitment.\n\nTHE MINES. On AUGUST 25 President Trump announced on Truth Social that the US Navy has disposed of ALL MINES in the international waters of the Strait of Hormuz, declaring a 'ZERO TOLERANCE' policy: 'ANY SHIP OR BOAT PLACING NEW MINES WILL BE IMMEDIATELY AND SYSTEMATICALLY DESTROYED,' with the Space Force 'watching every square inch' of the waterway. US officials confirmed the substance: over recent months US Navy UNDERWATER DRONES scanned the Traffic Separation Scheme and located MORE THAN 100 OBJECTS SUSPECTED TO BE MINES, and the US military CONTRACTED WITH PRIVATE COMPANIES to remove or detonate them. CENTCOM began the major clearance operation in APRIL. Trump also said the US had destroyed all 28 Iranian mine-laying boats. THE POINT FOR THIS AUDIT IS THE INSTRUMENT: the capability the United States applied to the chokepoint in this cycle was an unmanned underwater vehicle and a commercial contractor, not an amphibious force.\n\nTHE CLAIM IS DISPUTED, AND THE DISPUTE IS RECORDED. Deputy Foreign Minister KAZEM GHARIBABADI said 'NO ONE EXCEPT IRAN KNOWS THE LOCATION' of the mines and that the announcement was 'ONLY AIMED AT CALMING MARKETS.' Independent specialists split the difference: MARK HILBORNE of King's College London said 'the claim that the mines have been cleared came from CENTCOM initially, and in this we can have strong confidence' for the main shipping lanes, while cautioning 'there may be risks beyond the lanes'; ALEX ALMEIDA of Ambrey said mines 'HAVE NEVER BEEN THE PRIMARY THREAT,' with drones and anti-ship cruise and ballistic missiles the greater danger. Iran's mine stockpile is estimated at 2,000-6,000, both moored contact and bottom-sensor types. WAR-RISK INSURANCE remains at 7.5-10% of hull value against 1-3% before the war.\n\nTHE CORRIDOR. Oman's Foreign Minister BADR ALBUSAIDI arrived in Tehran on AUGUST 25 and met ABBAS ARAGHCHI. A joint press statement from the two foreign ministries described a 'PHASED FRAMEWORK' comprising a JOINT TEMPORARY NAVIGATIONAL CORRIDOR through the Strait of Hormuz and 'an agreement to implement a joint project to clear the Strait of mines,' with technical talks to continue toward a PERMANENT corridor, 'the future administration of the Strait,' and mechanisms for information exchange, traffic management and maritime and security services. ABC News records the framework as proceeding under ARTICLE 5 OF THE ISLAMABAD MEMORANDUM. Albusaidi posted that he was 'hopeful' the two would 'soon announce a temporary corridor,' adding that 'future management of the Strait and a permanent solution will follow in due course.' Araghchi posted that the proposal showed 'Iran's commitment to peace and stability is matched by steadfast diplomacy with our neighbours.'\n\nTHE TERMS, AS TEHRAN STATES THEM. On AUGUST 26 GHARIBABADI told state television that Iran and Oman have agreed a TEMPORARY maritime route: SEVEN MILES (11.3 km) WIDE; ENTRY BEGINNING IN IRANIAN TERRITORIAL WATERS with part of the exit route also passing through them; vessels requiring IRANIAN PERMISSION AND SURVEILLANCE before entry; MILITARY VESSELS NOT PERMITTED; and the arrangement holding while a PERMANENT one is negotiated over 30 TO 60 DAYS. 'THE AGREED-UPON TRANSIT ROUTE WITH OMAN IS A TEMPORARY ROUTE.' He set out the conditions for full reopening: the United States must fulfil its JUNE 17 MOU commitments, provide SANCTIONS RELIEF, RELEASE FROZEN IRANIAN ASSETS, END THE WAR, and Iran demands WAR-DAMAGE COMPENSATION. He added that 'Washington was mistaken about its ability to enforce sanctions.' IRGC spokesman Brig. Gen. HOSSEIN MOHEBBI said on AUGUST 26 that negotiations with Oman began about a month ago and had produced agreement on 'EACH COUNTRY'S SHARE OF THE STRAIT'S WATERS AND IRAN AND OMAN'S SHARE OF ITS REVENUES,' declaring that 'THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ BELONGS TO IRAN AND THE SULTANATE OF OMAN' and that the United States is obstructing progress. WHAT REMAINS UNPUBLISHED IS AS IMPORTANT AS WHAT WAS ANNOUNCED: precise route coordinates, a safe-passage start date, revenue-sharing mechanics, which vessels and flags qualify, and any mine-clearing timeline.\n\nTHE ENFORCEMENT RECORD CUT AGAINST THE DIPLOMACY WITHIN A DAY. Iran's shipping monitoring agency, via MEHR, said the Indian tanker HAANA planned to transit the SOUTHERN ROUTE — the Oman Corridor — 'but received a warning that it had to stop,' and turned back. The same agency said 'NO VESSEL TRAFFIC HAS BEEN OBSERVED ON THE SOUTHERN ROUTE OF THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ OVER THE PAST 24 HOURS.' This sits directly against the CNN Business/Kpler dataset logged in the previous cycle putting MORE THAN 80% of liquids transits over two weeks on the Omani route. The audit does not resolve the contradiction; it records both, and notes that they are measurements of different things — Kpler counts hulls that completed transits, the Iranian agency counts what its own forces permitted.\n\nTHE MARKET FELL FOR THREE SESSIONS. On AUGUST 25 Brent declined more than 3% to about $89, its lowest since AUGUST 19, with WTI near $82, the softest since AUGUST 17; traders judged the sanctions less disruptive than a war because Bessent gave partners time to comply without naming target countries or an implementation timeline. OLE HANSEN of Saxo Bank said the shift from military confrontation to economic sanctions 'had helped calm nerves across energy markets'; BBH strategists called the announcement 'MORE OF A WARNING SHOT THAN A DECISIVE BLOW'; TIM WATERER of KCM cautioned that 'prices had not fully deflated' because Iran retains the ability to disrupt shipping. On AUGUST 26 Brent fell about 3% again to roughly $86 and WTI about 2.8% to about $80, a third consecutive daily decline, explicitly attributed to the Iran-Oman corridor framework and the mine-clearing announcement. THE DIRECTION IS THE FINDING: on the two days after the largest sanctions package of the war, and on the day Washington declared the chokepoint demined, the price of the commodity at issue went DOWN.\n\nTHE PRESIDENT'S OWN FRAMING REMOVED THE CLOCK. Asked by AL JAZEERA'S TANYA NOURY on AUGUST 26 how much time he was giving Iran to return to talks, Trump said: 'I HAVE NO TIME SCHEDULE, NONE. I'M NOT IN A HURRY. I HAVE NO TIME SCHEDULE AT ALL.' He said 'WE ARE WINNING VERY BIG. THEY'VE GOT MASSIVE INFLATION, THEIR ECONOMY IS FALLING APART, THEY'RE TREATING THEIR PEOPLE VERY BADLY, THEY ARE KILLING MANY, MANY PROTESTERS,' and that economic and military measures 'ARE BOTH EFFECTIVE.' Speaking to GLENN BECK the same day he called the waterway 'A VERY FUNCTIONING STRAIT' — 'yes, every once in a while there will be a drone or a rocket or something shot' — and said of Iran's Supreme Leader MOJTABA KHAMENEI, absent from public view for about six months, 'I DON'T THINK HE'S DEAD. HE WAS VERY SERIOUSLY WOUNDED,' describing wounds to 'the left side of his body, the arm, the leg, the whole thing.'\n\nDIPLOMACY IS NOW A QUEUE OF MEDIATORS. Pakistan's Field Marshal ASIM MUNIR and Interior Minister MOHSIN NAQVI completed their day-long Tehran visit, meeting President PEZESHKIAN, Araghchi, Parliament Speaker GHALIBAF and Supreme National Security Council Secretary MOHSEN REZAEI; on AUGUST 25 Pakistani officials reported 'SIGNIFICANT PROGRESS' toward reviving the ISLAMABAD MEMORANDUM, with talks focused on preventing further escalation, expediting the end of the war and reopening the Strait. Qatar's Prime Minister and Foreign Minister SHEIKH MOHAMMED BIN ABDULRAHMAN AL THANI is due in Tehran on THURSDAY AUGUST 27 for de-escalation talks centred on freedom of navigation, with Doha pressing to restore conditions to the PRE-FEBRUARY 28 STATUS QUO. Against all of that, the White House Deputy Press Secretary said on AUGUST 26 that NO CONVERSATIONS ARE UNDER WAY WITH TEHRAN.\n\nTHE NUCLEAR FILE HARDENED RHETORICALLY. Energy Secretary CHRIS WRIGHT said 'IRAN HAS A NUCLEAR WEAPONS PROGRAM AND OUR GOAL IS TO STOP IT,' and that the United States could destroy Iranian nuclear facilities 'IF NECESSARY.' Atomic Energy Organisation head MOHAMMAD ESLAMI said Iranian sites remain unsecured for inspection under wartime conditions and criticised the IAEA for failing to protest strikes on registered facilities: 'THE FACILITIES THAT HAVE NOW BEEN SUBJECTED TO MILITARY STRIKES WERE ALL REGISTERED WITH THE IAEA.'\n\nTHE HOME FRONT INSIDE IRAN IS THE SHARPEST DETERIORATION OF THE CYCLE. Long queues formed at petrol stations across Tehran and stations closed after running out; an Iranian state body warned of a 'CRITICAL' petrol shortfall; a 20-LITRE-PER-CAR limit forced repeat visits. The government said quotas would be reduced and non-subsidised fuel prices raised while subsidised prices hold. Executive Vice-President GHAEMPANAH said 'WITH THE US NAVAL BLOCKADE, WE CANNOT IMPORT GASOLINE.' President PEZESHKIAN said Iran faces 'THE WORST CONDITIONS OF AN ECONOMIC WAR.' The rial hit a RECORD 2,060,000 to the dollar on AUGUST 25, easing to about 2,005,000-2,007,000 on AUGUST 26 against an official rate near 1.5 million. Economy Minister ALI MADANIZADEH said the government has a 'TWO-YEAR PLAN' to withstand sanctions.\n\nTHE LEDGER. The Pentagon's published toll now stands at 776 US CASUALTIES — 18 KILLED AND 758 WOUNDED — after 60 wounded were added on AUGUST 21, with more than 90% of the newest additions Army soldiers. About 345 of the casualties have occurred since JULY 7. The Intercept's AUGUST 25 accounting again documents the undercount dispute: more than 200 sailors treated for smoke inhalation and lacerations after the USS Gerald R. Ford fire of MARCH 12 are excluded from the counts; at least one recognised death is omitted; and on APRIL 21 the wounded-in-action figure fell by 15 without explanation. A government official called the approach an 'ACCOUNTING TRICK' — 'They wanted the heat on them to slow down after the really devastating attacks.' On the Iranian side, the WHO estimate carried in the same reporting is 37,181 casualties including 3,375 deaths, against the Iranian government's own 3,528 killed and 27,170 injured; the audit records the divergence rather than choosing.\n\nTHE STRIKE PAUSE HELD FOR A TWENTY-FIFTH AND TWENTY-SIXTH CONSECUTIVE NIGHT. No US or Israeli strike inside Iranian territory has been confirmed since the President cancelled the planned multi-day energy campaign late on AUGUST 1.",
   "details": "AXIOS/RUBIO (August 25): US not expected to launch new strikes 'for the time being'; strikes not ruled out if Iran attacks first; a second official expects the posture to hold until after the midterm elections; focus on sanctions, the naval blockade and 'moving ever-increasing amounts of oil out of the Strait of Hormuz.' MINE CLEARANCE (August 25): Trump — all mines removed from international waters; 'Zero Tolerance'; 'Any ship or boat placing new mines will be immediately and systematically destroyed'; Space Force 'watching every square inch'; all 28 Iranian mine-laying boats destroyed. US officials — underwater drones identified 100+ suspected mines over recent months; private contractors removed or detonated them; CENTCOM operation began in April. GHARIBABADI: 'No one except Iran knows the location'; the claim is 'only aimed at calming markets.' HILBORNE (KCL): 'The claim that the mines have been cleared came from CENTCOM initially, and in this we can have strong confidence' — but 'there may be risks beyond the lanes.' ALMEIDA (Ambrey): mines 'have never been the primary threat.' Iranian mine stockpile estimated 2,000-6,000. War-risk insurance 7.5-10% of hull value vs 1-3% pre-war. IRAN-OMAN CORRIDOR: Albusaidi in Tehran August 25; joint FM statement describes a 'phased framework' — joint temporary navigational corridor plus a joint mine-clearing project; technical talks to continue toward a permanent corridor, 'the future administration of the Strait,' information exchange, traffic management, maritime and security services; proceeding under Article 5 of the Islamabad Memorandum. Albusaidi: 'I am hopeful we will soon announce a temporary corridor'; 'Future management of the Strait and a permanent solution will follow in due course.' Araghchi: 'Iran's commitment to peace and stability is matched by steadfast diplomacy with our neighbours.' GHARIBABADI (August 26, state TV): route 7 miles / 11.3 km wide; entry begins in Iranian territorial waters; part of the exit also through Iranian waters; Iranian permission and surveillance required; military vessels barred; permanent arrangement to be negotiated in 30-60 days; 'The agreed-upon transit route with Oman is a temporary route'; conditions for full reopening — US compliance with the June 17 MoU, sanctions relief, release of frozen assets, end of the war, war-damage compensation; 'Washington was mistaken about its ability to enforce sanctions.' MOHEBBI (IRGC, August 26): talks began about a month ago; agreement reached on 'each country's share of the strait's waters and Iran and Oman's share of its revenues'; 'The Strait of Hormuz belongs to Iran and the Sultanate of Oman'; the US is obstructing. UNRESOLVED: coordinates, start date, revenue mechanics, eligible flags, mine-clearing timeline. ENFORCEMENT CONTRADICTION: Indian tanker HAANA warned to stop and turned back on the southern (Oman Corridor) route; Iran's shipping monitoring agency via Mehr — 'no vessel traffic has been observed on the southern route of the Strait of Hormuz over the past 24 hours.' Against CNN Business/Kpler: >80% of liquids transits over two weeks on the Omani route. OIL: August 25 — Brent -3%+ to ~$89 (lowest since August 19), WTI ~$82 (softest since August 17); some prints -2.67% to $89.44. August 26 — Brent -3% to ~$86 (Investing.com 85.52 against a 85.75 previous close), WTI -2.8% to ~$80 (one print -$1.37/-1.7% to $80.99). Third consecutive session lower. straits.live carried $87.39 at 26 August 2300Z, +0.38% over 24h, +21.4% against a ~$72 pre-crisis baseline — divergent from futures prints and logged as such. Analysts: Hansen (Saxo), BBH ('more of a warning shot than a decisive blow'), Waterer (KCM). TRUMP QUOTES: 'I have no time schedule, none. I'm not in a hurry. I have no time schedule at all'; 'We are winning very big. They've got massive inflation, their economy is falling apart, they're treating their people very badly, they are killing many, many protesters'; economic and military measures 'are both effective'; 'It is a very functioning strait'; 'I don't think he's dead. He was very seriously wounded'; 'If Donald Trump wasn't president, there would be no Israel right now… and that's a guarantee.' IRANIAN STATEMENTS: Pezeshkian — 'the worst conditions of an economic war'; 'Our foundation is understanding and resolving issues through interaction and negotiation.' Ghalibaf — 'Negotiation … is neither a replacement for … resistance nor a sign of retreat from it'; thanked China for declining to comply with US sanctions. Araghchi — asked UN Secretary-General Guterres to 'condemn and reject' US actions, calling it a 'crucial moment' for international law. UN ambassador Gholamhossein Darzi urged the Security Council to condemn unilateral coercive measures. Armed forces spokesman Brig. Gen. Abolfazl Shekarchi — BBC Persian, Iran International and Radio Farda are military targets: 'Hostile media outlets are soldiers of the Zionists and America.' Army spokesman Mohammad Akraminia — all damaged systems 'rebuilt' and new equipment imported, suppliers unnamed. Ebrahim Zolfaghari — Iran will 'soon unveil the largest missile city on the planet.' US STATEMENTS: Energy Secretary Chris Wright — 'Iran has a nuclear weapons program and our goal is to stop it'; the US could destroy Iranian nuclear facilities 'if necessary.' Hegseth — strikes remain 'on the table.' White House Deputy Press Secretary Kelly (August 26) — no conversations under way with Tehran. Netanyahu — 'An agreement cannot be reached with that group there, with those savages.' DOMESTIC IRAN: petrol station closures and queues across Tehran; state body warns of a 'critical' shortfall; 20-litre per-car limit; quotas to be cut and non-subsidised prices raised; Executive VP Ghaempanah — 'with the US naval blockade, we cannot import gasoline'; rial record 2,060,000/USD on August 25, ~2,005,000-2,007,000 on August 26 against an official ~1.5m; Economy Minister Ali Madanizadeh — a 'two-year plan' to withstand sanctions. CASUALTIES: DoD 776 total — 18 killed, 758 wounded; 60 wounded added August 21; ~345 casualties since July 7; >90% of the newest additions Army. Intercept undercount record: 200+ USS Gerald R. Ford fire casualties (March 12) excluded; a recognised death omitted; April 21 WIA count fell by 15 unexplained; official quote — 'accounting trick,' 'They wanted the heat on them to slow down after the really devastating attacks.' WHO estimate for Iran: 37,181 casualties including 3,375 deaths, against the Iranian government's 3,528 killed / 27,170 injured. BLOCKADE: CENTCOM published no new tally this cycle; the figure of record remains 71 commercial vessels redirected, 3 disabled and 2 boarded as of August 24, plus more than 40 humanitarian aid vessels permitted through. TankerTrackers reported 15+ ship-to-ship transfer operations moving about 25 million barrels in the Gulf of Oman, from 'every country in the region, minus Iran.' TRAFFIC: IMF PortWatch via straits.live recorded 3 transits for August 23 against an ~85/day baseline (4%); Reuters reported only two vessels transited on Monday August 24, the fewest since early May; the straits.live board carried Crisis Pressure 91 (Extreme) and an Escalation Forecast of 52 (Elevated, cooling) at 26 August 2300Z, with market odds of a reopening by August 31 below 1% and by December 31 at 35%. STRIKE PAUSE: a 25th and 26th consecutive night with no confirmed US or Israeli strike inside Iranian territory since the August 1 cancellation.",
   "prediction_impact": "The predicted US ground seizure of the Strait of Hormuz or Kharg Island remains DISCONFIRMED at Day 182, and this cycle supplies the most specific negative evidence yet: an Axios report that the non-kinetic posture is expected to hold UNTIL AFTER THE MIDTERM ELECTIONS. That is a reported expectation from anonymous officials about a future decision, not a commitment, and it is logged with that qualification — but it is the first time the pause has been given a duration at all, and the duration is months. Corpus predictions that the United States would ultimately take physical control of the Strait are under further pressure from the instrument actually used: the capability applied to the chokepoint this cycle was UNDERWATER DRONES AND PRIVATE CONTRACTORS clearing more than 100 suspected mines, followed by a declaratory 'zero tolerance' rule enforced from orbit. That is coercive control by robot and by rule, not by lodgement. Predictions that the war would end in a negotiated settlement are STRENGTHENED IN FORM AND UNRESOLVED IN SUBSTANCE: Iran and Oman announced a phased framework for a temporary navigational corridor and a joint mine-clearing project, with a permanent arrangement to be negotiated in 30-60 days — the first bilateral instrument of the war to address the STRAIT'S FUTURE ADMINISTRATION. But Tehran states the terms in a form that keeps entry and part of the exit inside Iranian territorial waters, requires Iranian permission and surveillance, bars military vessels and divides revenue with Oman; and Iranian forces turned back the Indian tanker HAANA on that same southern route within a day. Whether the corridor is a concession or a consolidation cannot be resolved from open sources and is entered as a new open question. Predictions that Iran retains operational control of the Strait are now supported by one dataset and contradicted by another in the same 48 hours: Kpler's >80% Omani-route share against Iran's own agency reporting zero traffic on that route in 24 hours. The audit records both. Predictions that escalation would price oil upward are DISCONFIRMED for a second consecutive instance: Brent fell on the day of the sanctions announcement and then fell twice more, on the demining announcement and on the corridor framework. Predictions that sanctions pressure would produce visible Iranian domestic breakdown are PARTIALLY CONFIRMED as to symptom and UNTESTED as to political effect: petrol rationing, station closures, a record rial and an executive vice-president stating that Iran cannot import gasoline are all documented, while the government's public position hardened rather than softened. The Hegseth non-foreclosure and Energy Secretary Wright's statement that the United States could destroy Iranian nuclear facilities 'if necessary' are logged as live counter-indicators and remain UNTESTED.",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "The Iran-Oman temporary corridor is read as the first concrete de-escalation of the war and the mechanism by which the Strait reopens — and, by the opposing reading, as Iranian consolidation of legal and physical control over the chokepoint, dressed as a concession: a route whose entry and part of whose exit lie inside Iranian territorial waters, which vessels may use only with Iranian permission and under Iranian surveillance, whose revenues Tehran and Muscat divide between them, and from which warships are barred.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented: the Iranian and Omani foreign ministries announced on August 25 a 'phased framework' for a joint temporary navigational corridor and a joint mine-clearing project, with talks to continue toward a permanent corridor and 'the future administration of the Strait.' Documented: Gharibabadi's August 26 terms — seven miles wide, entry beginning in Iranian territorial waters, part of the exit through them, Iranian permission and surveillance required, military vessels barred, permanent arrangement in 30-60 days. Documented: IRGC spokesman Mohebbi said agreement covers 'each country's share of the strait's waters and Iran and Oman's share of its revenues' and that 'the Strait of Hormuz belongs to Iran and the Sultanate of Oman.' Documented: Iran says the strait is not reopened and conditions full opening on US compliance with the June 17 MoU, sanctions relief, release of frozen assets, an end to the war and war-damage compensation. Documented: on August 26 Iranian forces warned the Indian tanker HAANA to stop on the southern Omani route and it turned back, and Iran's shipping monitoring agency reported no traffic on that route in 24 hours. Documented: Trump threatened on August 17 to bomb Oman if it 'gets in the way.' Not documented: route coordinates, a safe-passage start date, revenue-sharing mechanics, which flags qualify, any mine-clearing timeline, or whether the United States will recognise the corridor at all.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "Omani Foreign Minister Badr Albusaidi and Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi, presenting the framework as diplomacy that will restore navigation",
      "Iranian Deputy Foreign Minister Kazem Gharibabadi, who states explicitly that the route is temporary and the strait is not reopened",
      "IRGC spokesman Brig. Gen. Hossein Mohebbi, framing the arrangement as an Iranian-Omani condominium over the waterway and its revenues",
      "Oil-market participants, who sold crude on the announcement as supply relief",
      "The White House, which says no conversations are under way with Tehran and has not endorsed the corridor"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "The instrument exists only as a joint press statement and a televised description of its terms. No coordinates, start date, flag eligibility or revenue mechanism has been published, and the United States — whose navy enforces the blockade the corridor would have to coexist with — has neither endorsed nor rejected it. Resolution would require a published corridor definition with coordinates, an observed sustained rise in transits along it, and a documented US position on whether blockaded vessels may use it.",
     "open_question_ref": "iran-oman-hormuz-corridor-concession-or-consolidation-august-2026"
    },
    {
     "reading": "Trump's August 25 announcement that all mines have been cleared from the Strait's international waters is read as the removal of the principal physical obstacle to reopening and evidence that the United States now controls the waterway — and, by the opposing reading, as an unverifiable declaratory act aimed at oil prices, in a waterway where mines were never the main threat and where the party that laid them says only it knows where they are.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented: Trump announced on August 25 that the US Navy had disposed of all mines in the Strait's international waters, declared a 'zero tolerance' policy and said the Space Force is monitoring the waterway. Documented: US officials said underwater drones had identified more than 100 suspected mines over recent months and that private contractors removed or detonated them, with the CENTCOM operation beginning in April. Documented: Gharibabadi said 'no one except Iran knows the location' of the mines and that the announcement was 'only aimed at calming markets.' Documented: Mark Hilborne of King's College London said the CENTCOM-sourced claim warrants 'strong confidence' for the main lanes while 'there may be risks beyond the lanes'; Alex Almeida of Ambrey said mines 'have never been the primary threat,' with drones and anti-ship missiles the greater danger. Documented: Iran's mine stockpile is estimated at 2,000-6,000 and war-risk insurance remains at 7.5-10% of hull value. Documented: transits stood at 3 on August 23 and two on August 24 — the fewest since early May — after the clearance was complete. Not documented: any independent survey of the Traffic Separation Scheme, any accounting of mines outside the lanes, or any sustained rise in traffic attributable to the clearance.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "President Trump and US officials, presenting the clearance as complete and the strait as 'very functioning'",
      "Iranian Deputy Foreign Minister Kazem Gharibabadi, calling the claim a market-management exercise",
      "Independent naval and maritime-risk analysts, who credit the clearance of the lanes while disputing that mines were ever the binding constraint",
      "Marine insurers, whose war-risk rates have not moved from 7.5-10% of hull value"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "Mine clearance is verifiable only by transiting the water or by an independent survey, and neither has occurred at scale — traffic remained at or near war-time lows in the days after the announcement. The competing claims are about a seabed no neutral party has published data on. Resolution would require an independent hydrographic survey, a sustained recovery in transit counts, or a fall in war-risk premiums.",
     "open_question_ref": "hormuz-mine-clearance-completeness-august-2026"
    },
    {
     "reading": "The Axios report that the United States will not launch new strikes until after the midterm elections is read as confirmation that the war has settled into a durable economic-and-naval phase — and, by the opposing reading, as an explicitly electoral pause that schedules, rather than forecloses, the resumption of a military campaign, with the ground option reopening in November.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented: Axios reported on August 25, citing US officials, that Rubio told several allied foreign ministers the United States is not expected to launch new strikes 'for the time being' and will focus on other means of pressure, without ruling out strikes if Iran attacks first. Documented: a second official told Axios the posture is expected to remain in place until after the midterm elections, when another military campaign could again be considered. Documented: no confirmed US or Israeli strike inside Iran since August 1, now a 26th consecutive night. Documented: Hegseth says strikes remain 'on the table' and Energy Secretary Wright says the US could destroy Iranian nuclear facilities 'if necessary.' Documented: Trump said on August 26 he has 'no time schedule, none.' Not documented: any order, any planning artefact, or any on-record official statement tying the posture to the electoral calendar.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "Axios, reporting two unnamed US officials",
      "Secretary of State Marco Rubio, as characterised to allied foreign ministers in that reporting",
      "Secretary of War Pete Hegseth and Energy Secretary Chris Wright, both of whom continue to decline to foreclose military action",
      "Democratic campaign operations, which are running the war and its fuel prices as a midterm issue"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "The claim is about an unannounced future decision attributed to anonymous officials, and both readings are consistent with every observable fact in the record — a strike pause is what each would look like today. Resolution requires the passage of the election itself, or an on-record statement or order that ties the posture to a date.",
     "open_question_ref": "us-strike-pause-midterm-timeline-august-2026"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-08-27",
   "theatre": "Iran / Strait of Hormuz",
   "tag": "Ground Invasion Tracker",
   "headline": "GROUND INVASION TRACKER — DAY 182: STATUS HOLDS NO_GROUND_TROOPS, AND THIS DOUBLE CYCLE PRODUCED THE FIRST REPORTED DURATION FOR THE NON-KINETIC POSTURE. AXIOS REPORTED ON AUGUST 25, CITING US OFFICIALS, THAT SECRETARY OF STATE MARCO RUBIO HAS TOLD ALLIED FOREIGN MINISTERS THE UNITED STATES IS NOT EXPECTED TO LAUNCH NEW STRIKES ON IRAN 'FOR THE TIME BEING' AND WILL FOCUS ON OTHER MEANS OF PRESSURE — AND THAT A SECOND OFFICIAL EXPECTS THAT POSTURE TO HOLD UNTIL AFTER THE MIDTERM ELECTIONS, WHEN ANOTHER MILITARY CAMPAIGN COULD AGAIN BE CONSIDERED. THE STATED PRIORITIES IN THE SAME REPORT ARE SANCTIONS, THE NAVAL BLOCKADE AND 'MOVING EVER-INCREASING AMOUNTS OF OIL OUT OF THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ' — THREE NON-GROUND INSTRUMENTS, NAMED IN ORDER. THE CYCLE'S OTHER EXECUTION-RELEVANT FACT POINTS THE SAME WAY: THE CAPABILITY THE UNITED STATES APPLIED TO THE CHOKEPOINT WAS UNDERWATER DRONES AND PRIVATE CONTRACTORS CLEARING MORE THAN 100 SUSPECTED MINES, FOLLOWED BY A DECLARATORY 'ZERO TOLERANCE' RULE ENFORCED BY SPACE FORCE SURVEILLANCE. COERCIVE CONTROL BY ROBOT AND BY RULE, NOT BY LODGEMENT. THE COUNTER-INDICATORS ARE LOGGED IN FULL AND WITHOUT DISCOUNT: SECRETARY OF WAR HEGSETH SAYS STRIKES REMAIN 'ON THE TABLE,' AND ENERGY SECRETARY CHRIS WRIGHT SAID THE UNITED STATES COULD DESTROY IRANIAN NUCLEAR FACILITIES 'IF NECESSARY' AND THAT 'IRAN HAS A NUCLEAR WEAPONS PROGRAM AND OUR GOAL IS TO STOP IT.' NO NEW USNI FLEET TRACKER WAS PUBLISHED IN THIS CYCLE — USNI PUBLISHES MONDAYS AND THE EDITION OF RECORD REMAINS AUGUST 24, WITH CARRIER PRESENCE AT TWO AND USS ABRAHAM LINCOLN DEPARTED FOR 7TH FLEET. BOXER ARG WITH THE 11TH MEU REMAINS THE SOLE DEPLOYED ARG IN THEATRE, WITH NO TASKING, NO EMBARKATION AND NO REHEARSAL. USS TRIPOLI REMAINS IN PORT AT SASEBO. KHARG UNCHANGED — TERMINALS IDLE UNDER BLOCKADE, NO US TARGETING, NO NAVAL MOVE. DRAFT RUNG UNCHANGED — AUTOMATIC SELECTIVE SERVICE REGISTRATION STILL DUE BY DECEMBER 2026, NO CALL-UP, NO ORDER. FORCE LEVEL HOLDS ABOVE 50,000 WITH NONE OF IT INSIDE IRAN. NO LANDING, NO SEIZURE, NO DRAFT ORDER — DISCONFIRMED FOR A 182ND DAY.",
