   "tag": "Gulf States",
   "headline": "GULF STATES — MUSCAT DELIVERED AN INSTRUMENT AND DOHA IS NEXT THROUGH THE DOOR. OMAN'S FOREIGN MINISTER BADR ALBUSAIDI MET ARAGHCHI IN TEHRAN ON AUGUST 25 AND THE TWO MINISTRIES ANNOUNCED A 'PHASED FRAMEWORK' FOR A JOINT TEMPORARY NAVIGATIONAL CORRIDOR THROUGH THE STRAIT AND A JOINT MINE-CLEARING PROJECT, WITH TECHNICAL TALKS TO CONTINUE TOWARD A PERMANENT CORRIDOR AND 'THE FUTURE ADMINISTRATION OF THE STRAIT.' QATAR'S PRIME MINISTER AND FOREIGN MINISTER SHEIKH MOHAMMED BIN ABDULRAHMAN AL THANI ARRIVES IN TEHRAN ON THURSDAY AUGUST 27 FOR DE-ESCALATION TALKS CENTRED ON FREEDOM OF NAVIGATION, WITH DOHA PRESSING TO RESTORE CONDITIONS TO THE PRE-FEBRUARY 28 STATUS QUO. THE UAE'S ANWAR GARGASH SAID ON AUGUST 25 THAT THE CONFLICT HAS MOVED 'TO A NEW PHASE' MARKED BY 'ESCALATING ECONOMIC PRESSURE.' THE COMMERCIAL WORKAROUNDS ARE NOW THE REGION'S OPERATING MODEL: ADNOC IS RUNNING A SHUTTLE SERVICE WITH TRANSPONDERS OFF, SAUDI ARABIA IS USING SHIP-TO-SHIP TRANSFERS WITH GOVERNMENT-BACKED INSURANCE, AND TANKERTRACKERS COUNTED MORE THAN FIFTEEN SHIP-TO-SHIP OPERATIONS MOVING ABOUT 25 MILLION BARRELS IN THE GULF OF OMAN — FROM 'EVERY COUNTRY IN THE REGION, MINUS IRAN.' THE COST OF THE WAR TO THE GULF'S CIVIL ECONOMY IS NOW MEASURABLE IN PASSENGERS: DUBAI INTERNATIONAL HANDLED 13 MILLION IN THE SECOND QUARTER AGAINST 31.5 MILLION IN THE FIRST, A FALL OF 31.3%. GULF EXPOSURE TO THE SANCTIONS ARCHITECTURE REMAINS THE UNRESOLVED QUESTION — IRAN'S LARGEST TRADE COUNTERPARTIES ARE CHINA AT $14.58BN, IRAQ AT $11.7BN, THE UAE AT $7.16BN AND TÜRKIYE AT $6.1BN, AND ABU DHABI SUSPENDED ALL TRADE WITH TEHRAN ON AUGUST 19.",
