   "source": "Qatar Tribune, 'PM to visit Tehran for talks on de-escalation, Strait of Hormuz,' August 26, 2026; Middle East Monitor, 'Qatari Prime Minister set to visit Tehran in bid to reduce regional tensions,' August 26, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Iran, Oman discuss temporary shipping lane through Strait of Hormuz,' August 25, 2026; Muscat Daily, 'Oman, Iran propose temporary Hormuz corridor, mine-clearing,' August 25, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Iran, Oman agree on temporary Hormuz route: What we know,' August 26, 2026 (including Trump's August 17 threat to Oman); Al Jazeera, 'Why Hormuz remains high risk for ships despite US claims of mine-clearing,' August 26, 2026 (ADNOC transponder-off shuttle service; Saudi ship-to-ship transfers and government-backed insurance); CBS News live updates, August 26, 2026 (Dubai International Airport Q2 traffic; CEO Paul Griffiths); Everycrsreport.com, 'The Arab Gulf States, the Iran Conflict, and U.S. Relations: In Brief' (R48971), August 2026 (Anwar Gargash, August 25: conflict has moved 'to a new phase' of 'escalating economic pressure'); Fox News Digital live updates, August 25, 2026 (TankerTrackers: 15+ ship-to-ship operations moving about 25 million barrels in the Gulf of Oman, from 'every country in the region, minus Iran'; bilateral trade exposure — China $14.58bn, Iraq $11.7bn, UAE $7.16bn, Türkiye $6.1bn); Solace Global, 'Gulf States: Situation & Travel Update, 21 August 2026.'",
   "summary": "THE GULF SPENT THIS CYCLE DOING THE TWO THINGS IT HAS DONE ALL WAR — MEDIATING AND ROUTING AROUND — AND ONE OF THEM PRODUCED AN ACTUAL INSTRUMENT.\n\nOMAN. Foreign Minister BADR ALBUSAIDI arrived in Tehran on AUGUST 25 and held consultations with ABBAS ARAGHCHI. The joint press statement from the two foreign ministries described a 'PHASED FRAMEWORK' comprising a JOINT TEMPORARY NAVIGATIONAL CORRIDOR through the Strait of Hormuz and 'an agreement to implement a joint project to CLEAR THE STRAIT OF MINES,' with officials to continue talks 'with the aim of reaching agreement on a permanent shipping corridor and THE FUTURE ADMINISTRATION OF THE STRAIT,' plus mechanisms for information exchange, traffic management and the provision of maritime and security services. Albusaidi posted afterwards that he was 'HOPEFUL' the two would 'SOON ANNOUNCE A TEMPORARY CORRIDOR,' adding that 'FUTURE MANAGEMENT OF THE STRAIT AND A PERMANENT SOLUTION WILL FOLLOW IN DUE COURSE.' THE SIGNIFICANCE FOR THIS AUDIT IS THAT THE FIRST DOCUMENT OF THE WAR TO ADDRESS WHO WILL ADMINISTER THE STRAIT AFTERWARDS WAS SIGNED BY IRAN AND OMAN, NOT BY THE UNITED STATES. Muscat is doing this eight days after President Trump said on AUGUST 17 that 'if Oman gets in the way, we'll bomb the s*** out of them.'\n\nQATAR. Prime Minister and Foreign Minister SHEIKH MOHAMMED BIN ABDULRAHMAN BIN JASSIM AL THANI arrives in Tehran on THURSDAY AUGUST 27 for talks with senior Iranian officials on de-escalation and on creating conditions for dialogue. The Qatari foreign ministry says the talks will focus on FREEDOM OF NAVIGATION IN THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ and that Doha's position is that navigation should be restored to the STATUS QUO THAT EXISTED BEFORE FEBRUARY 28. That is a more expansive objective than the Iranian-Omani corridor, and the two tracks have not been reconciled.\n\nTHE UAE. Presidential adviser ANWAR GARGASH said on AUGUST 25 that the US-Iran conflict has moved 'TO A NEW PHASE' marked by 'ESCALATING ECONOMIC PRESSURE.' Abu Dhabi's AUGUST 19 suspension of all trade, commercial exchange and financial transactions with Iran stands, and UAE-domiciled entities were among the roughly 60 designated under Operation Economic Outcast on AUGUST 24. ADNOC is reported to be offering a shuttle service to customers with TRANSPONDERS SWITCHED OFF — a state oil company operating its own dark fleet inside its own waters.\n\nSAUDI ARABIA. Riyadh is running SHIP-TO-SHIP TRANSFERS with GOVERNMENT-BACKED INSURANCE to keep barrels moving outside the chokepoint, alongside the Hormuz bypass programme with France logged in the previous cycle. TANKERTRACKERS counted MORE THAN FIFTEEN SHIP-TO-SHIP TRANSFER OPERATIONS involving ABOUT 25 MILLION BARRELS of crude in the Gulf of Oman, originating from 'EVERY COUNTRY IN THE REGION, MINUS IRAN' — a measure both of how much oil is still moving and of how completely Iranian barrels have been excluded from the flow.\n\nTHE CIVIL COST IS NOW IN THE AVIATION NUMBERS. DUBAI INTERNATIONAL AIRPORT reported 13 MILLION PASSENGERS in the second quarter against 31.5 MILLION in the first — a fall of 31.3%. Chief executive PAUL GRIFFITHS said: 'I'm very confident we'll be back to full operation in a very short space of time.' Gulf air routes remain disrupted.\n\nTHE EXPOSURE THAT MAKES THE SANCTIONS REGIME A GULF PROBLEM RATHER THAN AN IRANIAN ONE IS UNCHANGED: Iran's principal trade counterparties are CHINA at $14.58BN, IRAQ at $11.7BN, the UAE at $7.16BN and TÜRKIYE at $6.1BN. Every one of those relationships is now inside the cure period Treasury announced on AUGUST 24, and none of the Gulf states has publicly said what it will do when the period expires.",
   "details": "OMAN (August 25): Albusaidi in Tehran with Araghchi; joint FM statement — 'phased framework,' joint temporary navigational corridor, joint project to clear the Strait of mines; talks to continue toward a permanent corridor, 'the future administration of the Strait,' information exchange, traffic management, maritime and security services. Albusaidi on X: 'I am hopeful we will soon announce a temporary corridor for the Strait of Hormuz'; 'Future management of the Strait and a permanent solution will follow in due course.' Context: Trump, August 17 — 'If Oman gets in the way, we'll bomb the s*** out of them.' QATAR: PM and FM Sheikh Mohammed bin Abdulrahman bin Jassim Al Thani arrives Tehran Thursday August 27 for de-escalation talks; focus on freedom of navigation in the Strait; Doha's stated objective is restoring navigation to the pre-February 28 status quo; the visit is framed by the foreign ministry as reflecting Qatar's position that diplomacy remains the best means of resolving differences. UAE: Anwar Gargash (August 25) — the conflict has moved 'to a new phase' marked by 'escalating economic pressure.' August 19 suspension of all trade, commercial exchange and financial transactions with Iran stands; UAE-domiciled entities among the ~60 designated August 24. ADNOC offering a shuttle service with transponders off. SAUDI ARABIA: ship-to-ship transfers with government-backed insurance; Hormuz bypass programme with France (Omani ports, doubled pipeline capacity, rail) continuing from the previous cycle. TANKERTRACKERS: 15+ ship-to-ship transfer operations involving about 25 million barrels of crude in the Gulf of Oman, from 'every country in the region, minus Iran.' DUBAI INTERNATIONAL AIRPORT: 13 million passengers in Q2 against 31.5 million in Q1, -31.3%. CEO Paul Griffiths — 'I'm very confident we'll be back to full operation in a very short space of time.' IRAN TRADE EXPOSURE (recipients of Iranian exports): China $14.58bn; Iraq $11.7bn; UAE $7.16bn; Türkiye $6.1bn (Türkiye also facing potential loss of Iranian natural gas supply).",
   "prediction_impact": "Corpus predictions that a US-Iran war would fracture the Gulf coalition are PARTIALLY CONFIRMED and now measurable in a specific direction: the first bilateral instrument of the war addressing the STRAIT'S FUTURE ADMINISTRATION was signed between Iran and Oman, a US security partner, eight days after the President threatened to bomb Oman if it 'gets in the way,' and Qatar arrives in Tehran the next day with a broader objective than the corridor Muscat negotiated. Predictions that the Gulf states would bear the economic cost of a Hormuz closure are CONFIRMED and the datasets keep arriving: Dubai International's passenger traffic fell 31.3% quarter-on-quarter, ADNOC is running transponder-off shuttles inside its own waters, and Saudi Arabia is underwriting its own war-risk insurance. Predictions that secondary sanctions would put Washington into direct conflict with its own Gulf partners remain UNTESTED pending the expiry of the cure period announced on August 24, with the UAE at $7.16bn of Iranian trade the most exposed of the GCC states."
  },
