   ],
   "source": "For Our Climate, '[Press Release] Hormuz blockade lays bare structural limits of fossil fuel reliance in South Korea, Japan,' 2026; Zero Carbon Analytics, 'Asian countries most at risk from oil and gas supply disruptions in Strait of Hormuz'; ORF Middle East, 'Asia's Energy Stress: Regional Impact of Hormuz Crisis'; The Globe and Mail, 'War in Iran causes energy crisis in South Asia, disrupting life for millions'; WHEELS Global Foundation, 'Hormuz Closure and India's LPG Crisis'; Gulf International Forum, 'Hormuz Disruptions and Asia's Energy Resilience'; AAA Fuel Prices national average, August 26, 2026; Quartz, 'Gas and diesel hit record seasonal highs in mid-August 2026'; Transport Topics, 'Diesel, gasoline prices hit record August highs,' August 2026; US Bureau of Economic Analysis July PCE release via CBS News and U.S. News, 'Key Inflation Gauge Remains Elevated During Iran War and Ongoing US Trade Fights,' August 26, 2026; CBS News live updates, August 26, 2026 (Dubai International Airport Q2 passenger figures).",
   "summary": "THE STRAIT HAS BEEN EFFECTIVELY CLOSED SINCE LATE FEBRUARY AND THE ECONOMIES THAT DEPEND ON IT HAVE STOPPED TREATING RATIONING AS AN EMERGENCY MEASURE.\n\nSOUTH AND SOUTHEAST ASIA. PAKISTAN, BANGLADESH, THE PHILIPPINES, VIETNAM AND SRI LANKA have all imposed FOUR-DAY WORKING WEEKS, WORK-FROM-HOME MANDATES OR SCHOOL CLOSURES to conserve fuel. Pakistan relies on foreign tankers for roughly 85% OF ITS SUPPLY; Bangladesh imports about 95% OF ITS PETROLEUM NEEDS and faces the steepest adjustment of any affected state. Reporting places Indonesia, the Philippines and Pakistan at five to six weeks of petrol supply. Long queues have been recorded at filling stations in both Pakistan and Bangladesh.\n\nINDIA. Sixty per cent of India's LPG imports pass through the Strait, and Qatar and the UAE together supply 53% of its LNG. The shortage has produced BLACK-MARKETING OF DOMESTIC CYLINDERS and price rises in major cities, forcing hotels and restaurants to close. Inflationary pressure on food and essential goods is being driven by energy-related logistics costs.\n\nTHE STRUCTURAL POINT ABOUT REFINED PRODUCTS. Crude has strategic buffers; DIESEL AND JET FUEL DO NOT, because there is no economic substitution of routes or vessels for specialised product tankers. Asian export volumes of jet fuel, diesel and gasoline have fallen to their lowest levels in recent years.\n\nNORTH ASIA. SOUTH KOREA sources approximately 70% of its crude and 18% of its LNG through Hormuz and holds about 206 DAYS of emergency reserves; JAPAN takes more than 90% of its crude from the Middle East, about 75% of it via Hormuz, and holds about 146 DAYS. The reserves are why neither has a fuel-queue problem and the exposure is why both have an industrial one: LOTTE CHEMICAL, LG CHEM and HANWHA SOLUTIONS have issued potential FORCE MAJEURE notices and YEOCHUN NCC, Korea's largest ethylene producer, has declared supply disruption. Analysts warn that a prolonged closure produces a widening supply gap in both economies.\n\nSHIPPING ECONOMICS. LNG CARRIER RATES have surged to around $300,000 PER DAY, a 650% increase, and crude tanker rates are at historic highs. War-risk insurance in the Strait itself remains at 7.5-10% OF HULL VALUE against 1-3% pre-war. Freight from the Gulf via SUEZ now costs roughly $10-11 A BARREL against about $5 via Yanbu before the Houthi campaign; the CAPE OF GOOD HOPE routing adds about 25 days.\n\nAVIATION. DUBAI INTERNATIONAL AIRPORT handled 13 MILLION PASSENGERS in the second quarter against 31.5 MILLION in the first, a fall of 31.3%. Chief executive PAUL GRIFFITHS said he is 'very confident we'll be back to full operation in a very short space of time.'\n\nTHE UNITED STATES. July PCE inflation held at 3.7% YEAR-ON-YEAR, reported on AUGUST 26, well above the Federal Reserve's 2% target, with the Commerce Department release explicitly framed against the Iran war and the trade disputes. The AAA national average for regular gasoline stands at about $4.10 A GALLON — roughly a dollar above a year ago — and AUGUST 2026 is on course to be THE HIGHEST AUGUST ON RECORD at a $4.06 monthly average, surpassing 2022's $3.97. Diesel has set successive records. Two-thirds of Democratic campaign advertisements mentioning gasoline prices cite the war as the cause, and the price level is shaping up as a central midterm issue — which is the domestic context in which the reported decision to hold off further strikes until after the elections has to be read.",
   "details": "SOUTH AND SOUTHEAST ASIA: Pakistan, Bangladesh, the Philippines, Vietnam and Sri Lanka have imposed four-day working weeks, work-from-home mandates or school closures to conserve fuel. Pakistan imports ~85% of supply by tanker; Bangladesh ~95% of petroleum needs. Indonesia, the Philippines and Pakistan reported at five to six weeks of petrol supply. Queues recorded at filling stations in Pakistan and Bangladesh. INDIA: 60% of LPG imports transit Hormuz; Qatar and the UAE supply 53% of LNG imports; black-marketing of domestic cylinders and price rises in major cities; hotels and restaurants closing; inflationary pressure on food and essentials from energy logistics costs. REFINED PRODUCTS: crude has strategic buffers, diesel and jet fuel do not — no economic route or vessel substitution for specialised product tankers; Asian export volumes of jet fuel, diesel and gasoline at multi-year lows. SOUTH KOREA: ~70% of crude and 18% of LNG via Hormuz; ~206 days of emergency reserves; Lotte Chemical, LG Chem and Hanwha Solutions have issued potential force majeure notices; Yeochun NCC, the largest domestic ethylene producer, has declared supply disruption. JAPAN: >90% of crude from the Middle East, ~75% via Hormuz; ~146 days of emergency reserves. SHIPPING: LNG carrier rates ~$300,000/day (+650%); crude tanker rates at historic highs; Hormuz war-risk insurance 7.5-10% of hull value against 1-3% pre-war; Suez freight ~$10-11/bbl against ~$5 via Yanbu pre-Houthi; Cape of Good Hope routing ~50 days against 25-27. AVIATION: Dubai International Airport 13m passengers in Q2 against 31.5m in Q1, -31.3%; CEO Paul Griffiths — 'I'm very confident we'll be back to full operation in a very short space of time.' UNITED STATES: July PCE inflation 3.7% year-on-year, reported August 26, against the Fed's 2% target; AAA national average for regular ~$4.10/gal, about a dollar above a year ago; August 2026 monthly average $4.06, the highest August on record, surpassing 2022's $3.97; diesel at successive records; two-thirds of Democratic campaign advertisements mentioning gas prices cite the war.",
   "prediction_impact": "Corpus predictions that a Hormuz closure would transmit as a political rather than merely economic shock across Asia are CONFIRMED: five states have altered their working week or school calendar to conserve fuel, and India's LPG shortage has produced a black market in domestic cylinders. Predictions that strategic reserves would insulate the advanced North Asian economies are PARTIALLY CONFIRMED: Japan and South Korea have avoided fuel queues on 146 and 206 days of cover respectively, but their petrochemical sectors are already issuing force majeure notices, so the insulation is holding for households and failing for industry. Predictions that oil would reach $130 a barrel on a sustained ground commitment remain UNTESTED and increasingly remote — Brent closed this cycle near $86 with no ground commitment made. Predictions that the war would become a domestic American electoral issue are CONFIRMED: July PCE at 3.7%, a record August gasoline average and two-thirds of Democratic ads on gas prices citing the war establish the mechanism, and the same electoral calendar now appears in reporting on when the United States might resume strikes."
  },
