   ],
   "source": "Al Jazeera, 'US launches “Operation Economic Outcast” to cut Iran's economic lifeline,' August 24, 2026; Gulf News, 'US-Iran standoff: What UAE residents need to know today (Aug. 24, 2026)'; Al Jazeera, 'US threatens toughest sanctions yet against Iran: What we know,' August 24, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Yemen's Houthis report attack on Saudi oil tanker in Red Sea,' August 24, 2026; gCaptain, 'Houthis Escalate Saudi Shipping Campaign With VLCC Strike,' August 24, 2026; Marine Log, 'Saudi tanker Amzan struck by Houthi missile,' August 24, 2026; Arab News, 'Oil tanker struck by unidentified projectile off Oman,' August 25, 2026; Press TV, 'Omani foreign minister to visit Iran to discuss Strait of Hormuz shipping,' August 24, 2026; Iran International live blog, entries of August 24, 2026; Solace Global, 'Gulf States: Situation & Travel Update, 21 August 2026.'",
   "summary": "THE ECONOMIC WAR IS NOW BEING FOUGHT THROUGH GULF JURISDICTIONS, AND THE FIRST LIST SAYS SO EXPLICITLY.\n\nTHE DESIGNATIONS NAME THE NEIGHBOURHOOD. Of the nearly 60 people, entities and vessels designated under Operation Economic Outcast on August 24, the locations reported are the UNITED ARAB EMIRATES, HONG KONG, CHINA, SINGAPORE and SWITZERLAND. The UAE is the only Gulf state on that list, and it is also the Gulf state that has moved furthest: Abu Dhabi suspended all trade, commercial exchange and financial transactions with Iran on August 19, and is reported to be preparing PHASED reductions in trade and financial engagement in parallel with the American measures, beginning with restrictions on cargo and trade flows. Emirati air defences remain on high alert.\n\nTHE STATES BUILDING THE BYPASS SAID NOTHING. The Gulf countries that have invested billions in pipelines designed to route oil around the Strait have not commented publicly on the sanctions package. That silence sits directly against the previous cycle's record, in which Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman met President Macron in Paris on August 24 with Hormuz alternatives as the headline file — Omani ports outside the Gulf, expanded and possibly doubled pipeline capacity, new rail links, and a standing France-Saudi joint task force on energy and logistics meeting at ministerial level. Riyadh and Paris separately urged a negotiated solution and a resumption of Iranian cooperation with the IAEA. A government that is building a workaround and declining to endorse the sanctions that would make the workaround necessary is hedging, and the audit records the hedge.\n\nTEHRAN AIMED AT THE HOSTS RATHER THAN THE ISSUER. Foreign Ministry spokesman Esmail Baghaei said Iran has the right to target any location used to launch attacks against it, and warned countries against providing territory or facilities to the United States. Taken with SNSC Secretary Mohsen Rezaei's August 23 warning that any state joining the sanctions is 'considered an enemy' and that not a drop of oil would leave the Gulf — including by the alternative routes — the Iranian deterrent is now pointed at the American basing structure in Gulf states rather than at American forces at sea.\n\nTWO HULLS WERE HIT AND ONLY ONE HAS AN OWNER. Yemen's Houthis struck the Saudi VLCC AMZAN, operated by the national shipping company Bahri, about 63 nautical miles west of YANBU in the Red Sea with ballistic missiles and drones on August 24, starting a fire on the main deck and causing nearby vessels to flee the area; all crew are safe and no environmental impact was reported. Houthi military spokesman Yahya Saree described it as part of a 'SIEGE FOR SIEGE' operation targeting the Saudis — an explicit linkage between the Iran blockade and attacks on Saudi shipping. Separately, at 0131 UTC on August 25, UKMTO reported a tanker struck by an unidentified projectile about nine nautical miles northeast of ASH SHISHAH, OMAN, with the engine room damaged and the vessel disabled; the crew are safe and no party has claimed it.\n\nOMAN KEEPS ITS TRACK OPEN. Foreign Minister Sayyid Badr Albusaidi arrives in Tehran TODAY, August 25, to meet Abbas Araghchi on the Strait, bilateral relations, maritime safety and maritime governance, following their August 21 telephone call. Muscat and Tehran have been negotiating a safe shipping route for nearly three months and have agreed route coordinates; this visit is described as settling remaining operational detail.\n\nCIVIL AVIATION REMAINS DEGRADED. Emirates cancelled Dubai-Bahrain services EK835 and EK837 while EK839 and Dubai-Kuwait services were scheduled to operate; flydubai cancelled FZ8504 to Bahrain while continuing Kuwait, Bahrain and Saudi routes; Etihad's Abu Dhabi-Bahrain and Abu Dhabi-Kuwait services largely operated; Air Arabia carried cancellations and pending departures across UAE-Kuwait-Bahrain routes.",
   "details": "DESIGNATIONS: ~60 persons, entities and vessels across the UAE, Hong Kong, China, Singapore and Switzerland. UAE: all trade, commercial exchange and financial transactions with Iran suspended August 19; phased cargo and trade restrictions reported in preparation alongside the US measures; air defences on high alert. BYPASS STATES: Gulf countries with Hormuz-bypass pipeline investment have not commented on the package. Saudi Arabia and France urged a negotiated solution and resumed IAEA cooperation; the Franco-Saudi Strategic Partnership Council met in Paris August 24 with Omani ports, doubled pipeline capacity and new rail as the headline file, plus a ministerial-level joint task force on energy and logistics. IRANIAN THREAT TO HOSTS: Baghaei — Iran has the right to target any location used to launch attacks against it; warning to countries against providing territory or facilities to the US. Rezaei (August 23) — states joining the sanctions are 'considered an enemy'; 'not a single drop of oil' would leave the Gulf including via alternative routes. SHIPPING: Saudi VLCC Amzan (Bahri) struck by Houthi ballistic missiles and drones ~63nm west of Yanbu, Red Sea, August 24 — fire on main deck, nearby vessels fled, crew safe, no environmental impact; Yahya Saree: 'siege for siege' operation against the Saudis. Tanker struck by unidentified projectile ~9nm NE of Ash Shishah, Oman, 0131 UTC August 25 — engine room damaged, vessel disabled, crew safe, unclaimed. OMAN: FM Sayyid Badr Albusaidi arrives Tehran August 25 to meet Araghchi on the Strait, maritime safety and governance; follows an August 21 call and ~3 months of route negotiation with agreed coordinates. AVIATION: Emirates cancelled EK835/EK837 (Dubai-Bahrain), EK839 scheduled; flydubai cancelled FZ8504 (Bahrain); Etihad Abu Dhabi-Bahrain and Abu Dhabi-Kuwait largely operating; Air Arabia cancellations across UAE-Kuwait-Bahrain.",
   "prediction_impact": "Corpus predictions that the Gulf monarchies would align behind Washington in a confrontation with Iran are PARTIALLY CONFIRMED and PARTIALLY DISCONFIRMED in the same cycle: the UAE has cut trade outright and is phasing further restrictions, while the states that have built the bypass infrastructure declined to comment on the sanctions that make it strategically central. Predictions that Gulf states would face direct Iranian retaliation for participating are UNTESTED but now explicitly threatened — Baghaei's claim of a right to strike any launch location is aimed at host states, not at the US Navy. Predictions that the Houthi front would be reactivated by escalation against Iran are CONFIRMED IN DIRECTION: the Amzan strike was framed by its perpetrators as retaliation-by-proxy for a siege, and it landed on Saudi rather than American shipping. Predictions that Oman would be the surviving channel between Washington and Tehran are SUPPORTED — Muscat's foreign minister is in Tehran today while the American instrument is a designation list."
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-08-25",
   "theatre": "Russia-Ukraine War",
   "tag": "Russia-Ukraine",
   "headline": "RUSSIA-UKRAINE — UKRAINE MARKED ITS 35TH INDEPENDENCE DAY UNDER A 143-DRONE, EIGHT-MISSILE OVERNIGHT BARRAGE THAT KILLED EIGHT AND INJURED 43 ACROSS SIX OBLASTS. RUSSIA LAUNCHED TWO ONIKS ANTI-SHIP MISSILES, SIX KH-31P AND KH-59 GUIDED AIR-LAUNCHED MISSILES AND 143 DRONES, ROUGHLY A THIRD OF THEM JET-POWERED; AIR DEFENCES DOWNED 109 DRONES, ONE ONIKS, TWO KH-31P/KH-59 AND TWO BANDEROL MUNITIONS, WITH STRIKES RECORDED AT 22 LOCATIONS. EUROPEAN LEADERS CAME TO KYIV AND WASHINGTON DID NOT SEND A REPRESENTATIVE. BRITAIN'S PRIME MINISTER ANDY BURNHAM, ON HIS FIRST FOREIGN TRIP, PLEDGED THAT MBDA WOULD RELEASE CLASSIFIED DATA ON UK COMPONENTS FOR THE SCALP MISSILE TO ENABLE UKRAINIAN LOCAL ASSEMBLY; NORWAY'S JONAS GAHR STORE COMMITTED $9 BILLION FOR 2027. ZELENSKY PRESSED THE ~34-NATION COALITION OF THE WILLING FOR AIR DEFENCE AND THE EU FOR ITS PROMISED €90 BILLION LOAN AGAINST A $27 BILLION MILITARY BUDGET DEFICIT. ISW REPORTS RUSSIAN ADVANCES EAST OF SLOVYANSK AND A ZELENSKY UKRAINE-US-EUROPE PROPOSAL OF AUGUST 23 OFFERING SIGNIFICANT CONCESSIONS THAT THE KREMLIN HAS NOT RECIPROCATED. NO NATO TROOP COMMITMENT WAS ANNOUNCED.",
   "tags": [
