   ],
   "source": "CNBC, 'Trump admin unveils anti-Iran global sanctions plan, signals China not exempt,' August 24, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'US launches “Operation Economic Outcast” to cut Iran's economic lifeline,' August 24, 2026; Quartz, 'Trump Treasury launches Iran sanctions campaign targeting China,' August 24, 2026; RFE/RL, 'What If China Doesn't Play Ball? US Sanctions Campaign Faces Its Biggest Test,' August 2026; Asia Financial, 'China Rejects US Call to Support Economic Sanctions on Iran,' August 2026; Bloomberg, 'US Eyes China Overcapacity Tariffs of 7.5% Before Xi-Trump Talks,' August 24, 2026; Reuters via TradingView and Yahoo Finance, 'US eyes China overcapacity tariffs of 7.5% before Xi-Trump talks, Bloomberg News reports,' August 24, 2026; Investing.com, 'US plans 7.5% China overcapacity tariff before Sept. 24 Xi-Trump summit,' August 24, 2026; China Ministry of Commerce statement of July 27, 2026; Kpler estimates via CNN and Reuters, August 2026.",
   "summary": "TWO SEPARATE AMERICAN INSTRUMENTS WERE POINTED AT CHINA ON THE SAME DAY, AND THEY PULL IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS.\n\nTHE FIRST IS THE IRAN SANCTIONS. Operation Economic Outcast's opening designations, announced August 24, include persons, entities and vessels located in CHINA and HONG KONG among roughly 60 across five jurisdictions, and the administration signalled that China is NOT EXEMPT from the secondary-sanctions architecture. Bessent framed the choice for Iran's trading partners as commercial ties with Tehran or access to the American financial system: 'No one is above the reach of US sanctions.' Every country is to receive a defined wind-down timeline, with a cure period before enforcement and a major financial institution expected to be designated by the end of the week.\n\nBEIJING'S POSITION HAS NOT MOVED AND ITS COUNTERMEASURES PRE-DATE THE ANNOUNCEMENT. The Foreign Ministry restated that sanctions do not 'fit the interest of any party' and that 'sanctions and pressure tactics are not the solution,' urging resolution through political and diplomatic dialogue rather than economic coercion and warning that pressure will only heighten regional tensions. Beijing has previously ordered Chinese companies to disregard US restrictions on independent refineries importing Iranian crude, and has invoked its 2021 BLOCKING STATUTE prohibiting Chinese firms from complying with foreign sanctions it deems illegitimate. The confrontation the sanctions architecture requires — a US designation against a large Chinese counterparty, met by a Chinese legal prohibition on compliance — has not yet been triggered.\n\nTHE SECOND INSTRUMENT IS TARIFF POLICY, AND IT IS SCHEDULED. The United States is preparing to impose a 7.5% TARIFF on Chinese goods under a Section 301 investigation into excess manufacturing capacity, ahead of a XI-TRUMP SUMMIT IN WASHINGTON ON SEPTEMBER 24. The arithmetic is precise and is the point: the current replacement rate is 12.5%, Beijing's Commerce Ministry said on July 27 that Washington had committed to capping replacement tariffs at 20%, and 7.5 percentage points is exactly the headroom to that ceiling. Publishing the overcapacity report before the summit would allow the announcement to be timed around the meeting. The bilateral trade truce expires NOVEMBER 10 and an extension is under negotiation.\n\nTHE TENSION IS STRUCTURAL AND THE AUDIT RECORDS IT RATHER THAN RESOLVING IT: Washington is simultaneously asking Beijing to help strangle Iran's economy and preparing to raise tariffs on Chinese goods to the agreed ceiling four weeks before a leaders' summit.\n\nAND THE VOLUME THAT MATTERS WAS ACHIEVED WITHOUT EITHER INSTRUMENT. China's imports of Iranian crude remain around 534,000 barrels a day in August against an average of about 1.4 million a day in 2025, a fall of roughly 60% year-on-year, with Beijing having bought an estimated 80-90% of Iran's exported crude. The reduction was produced by naval interdiction, not by a designation list — which remains the inverse of the announced strategy, and is the single most useful fact this theatre has produced about which instrument actually works.",
   "details": "SANCTIONS: Operation Economic Outcast designations (August 24) include entities and vessels in China and Hong Kong among ~60 across the UAE, Hong Kong, China, Singapore and Switzerland; administration signals China is not exempt from secondary sanctions; defined wind-down timelines and a cure period; major financial institution designation signalled for end of week. CHINA RESPONSE: Foreign Ministry — sanctions do not 'fit the interest of any party'; 'sanctions and pressure tactics are not the solution'; calls for political and diplomatic resolution; warns pressure heightens regional tensions. Beijing has ordered companies to ignore US restrictions on Iranian-crude imports by independent refiners and has invoked its 2021 blocking statute. TARIFFS: US preparing a 7.5% Section 301 excess-manufacturing-capacity tariff on Chinese goods; would take second-term duties to ~20%; current replacement rate 12.5%; China's Commerce Ministry said July 27 that Washington committed to a 20% cap, leaving exactly 7.5 points of headroom; Xi-Trump summit in Washington September 24; bilateral trade truce expires November 10 with an extension under negotiation. VOLUMES: Chinese imports of Iranian crude ~534,000 bpd in August vs ~1.4m bpd 2025 average (~-60% y/y); China buys an estimated 80-90% of Iran's exported crude; Iran supplies roughly 13% of Chinese oil imports on Kpler estimates.",
   "prediction_impact": "Corpus predictions that Washington would be forced to choose between confronting China and enlisting it against Iran are CONFIRMED as a live tension and UNRESOLVED as an outcome: both instruments were pointed at Beijing on the same day. Predictions that China would defy US secondary sanctions on Iran remain SUPPORTED but UNTESTED at scale — the blocking statute and the instruction to ignore restrictions are on the record, but no designation against a large Chinese financial counterparty has yet been issued. Predictions that a US-China trade rupture would occur in 2026 remain DISCONFIRMED: the tariff being prepared runs precisely to an agreed ceiling rather than through it, and a leaders' summit is scheduled for September 24. Predictions that sanctions rather than force would reduce Iranian oil exports are DISCONFIRMED on the evidence to date — the 60% collapse in Chinese purchases was produced by a naval blockade before any new designation existed."
  },
