   "date": "2026-08-25",
   "theatre": "East Asia — DPRK / Korean Peninsula",
   "tag": "North Korea",
   "headline": "KOREAN PENINSULA — THE EXERCISE ENDED EARLY AND THE SECOND PHASE NEVER HAPPENED. ULCHI FREEDOM SHIELD, ALREADY CUT FROM ELEVEN DAYS TO FIVE, WRAPPED UP ON FRIDAY AUGUST 21 — MEANING THE COUNTEROFFENSIVE-OPERATIONS PHASE ORIGINALLY SCHEDULED TO RUN THROUGH AUGUST 27 DID NOT TAKE PLACE AT ALL. THE SEQUENCE ON THE RECORD IS NOW: A SUMMIT OFFER AND AN EXERCISE REDUCTION ON AUGUST 18-19, KIM YO JONG'S DISMISSAL AND HER DENIAL THAT ANY TRUMP-KIM CONTACT OCCURRED ON AUGUST 19, ABOUT TEN KN-25 SHORT-RANGE BALLISTIC MISSILES FIRED ROUGHLY 300 KILOMETRES ON AUGUST 20, AND THE DRILLS TERMINATED A DAY LATER. PYONGYANG HAS REMAINED SILENT ON ITS OWN LAUNCHES SINCE. NO NEW LAUNCH, NO SUMMIT DATE AND NO VENUE HAVE BEEN ANNOUNCED.",
   "tags": [
    "North Korea",
    "Korean Peninsula",
    "Ulchi Freedom Shield",
    "Exercise Curtailed",
    "Second Phase Cancelled",
    "KN-25",
    "Kim Yo Jong",
    "Denial Of Contact",
    "Trump Kim Summit",
    "No Date No Venue",
    "Pyongyang Silence"
   ],
   "source": "The Korea Times, 'S. Korea, US wrap up drills early, day after NK missile barrage,' August 22, 2026; PBS News/AP, 'U.S.-South Korea military drill wraps up early, a day after North Korea's missile barrage,' August 21, 2026; U.S. News/AP, 'US, South Korean Militaries Wrap up Drill Early, a Day After North Korea's Missile Barrage,' August 21, 2026; The Korea Times, 'N. Korea remains silent over latest missile launches,' August 21, 2026; CBS News, 'North Korea launches ballistic missiles as Trump seeks to rekindle nuclear talks with Kim Jong Un,' August 20, 2026; Eastern Herald, 'North Korea Fires 10 Missiles After Trump Curtails Ulchi Freedom Shield, Announces Kim Summit,' August 22, 2026.",
   "summary": "THE CONCESSION WAS LARGER THAN THE PREVIOUS CYCLE RECORDED, AND THE AUDIT ENTERS THE CORRECTION.\n\nULCHI FREEDOM SHIELD, the annual US-South Korean command-post exercise, was originally scheduled as an ELEVEN-DAY EVENT IN TWO PHASES: defensive operations through Friday August 21, then COUNTEROFFENSIVE OPERATIONS THROUGH AUGUST 27. It was curtailed to five days as a goodwill gesture, and South Korea's military confirmed it ENDED ON FRIDAY AUGUST 21. The second phase — the counteroffensive half, which is the half Pyongyang objects to most loudly as an invasion rehearsal — DID NOT TAKE PLACE. The concession was therefore not a shortening of a single exercise but the deletion of its offensive component.\n\nTHE SEQUENCE, ASSEMBLED. On AUGUST 18-19 the exercise reduction was announced and President Trump confirmed he expects to meet Kim Jong Un this year — 'Yeah, I will be,' and of Kim, 'I get along with him great' — putting Pyongyang's arsenal at '57 very powerful nuclear weapons.' On AUGUST 19 KIM YO JONG dismissed the reduction, saying it does not change the drills' 'provocative, aggressive nature,' and DENIED that Kim Jong Un had responded to any request from Trump for a conversation. On AUGUST 20 North Korea fired ABOUT TEN KN-25 SHORT-RANGE BALLISTIC MISSILES from the Pyongyang area roughly 185 miles — some 300 kilometres — toward the sea. On AUGUST 21 the drills ended.\n\nSINCE THEN, SILENCE. North Korea has issued no comment on its own launches, which is itself a departure from its usual practice of publicising tests. No further launch has been reported. No date and no venue for a Trump-Kim meeting has been announced. The August 12 and early-August short-range launches remain the preceding pattern.\n\nThe state of this theatre is a concession made, refused in words, answered in ordnance, and then quietly completed anyway — with the offering side having given up the exercise phase it originally intended to run and received nothing on the record in return.",
   "details": "ULCHI FREEDOM SHIELD: originally 11 days in two phases — defensive operations to August 21, counteroffensive operations to August 27; curtailed to five days as a goodwill gesture; South Korea's military confirmed the exercise ended Friday August 21; the counteroffensive phase did not take place. SEQUENCE: August 18-19 — reduction announced; Trump confirms he expects to meet Kim Jong Un this year ('Yeah, I will be'; 'I get along with him great'; '57 very powerful nuclear weapons'). August 19 — Kim Yo Jong dismisses the reduction as leaving the drills' 'provocative, aggressive nature' unchanged and denies any Trump-Kim contact occurred. August 20 — about ten KN-25 short-range ballistic missiles fired from the Pyongyang area roughly 185 miles (~300 km) seaward. August 21 — drills end. SINCE: North Korea silent on its own launches; no new launch reported; no summit date or venue announced. PRIOR PATTERN: SRBM launches on August 12 and in early August.",
   "prediction_impact": "Corpus predictions that unilateral exercise reductions would draw Pyongyang toward diplomacy are DISCONFIRMED, and this cycle strengthens the finding rather than repeating it: the concession was larger than first recorded — an entire counteroffensive exercise phase was deleted, not merely shortened — and it was refused in words within 24 hours and answered with ten ballistic missiles within 48. Predictions of a fourth Trump-Kim summit in 2026 remain UNTESTED: the offer is on the record, Kim Yo Jong has denied the contact behind it, and no date or venue exists. Predictions that the Iran war would create an opening for DPRK opportunism are PARTIALLY CONFIRMED in the sense that the launches came while American attention and munitions are committed elsewhere, but no escalation beyond short-range testing has occurred."
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-08-25",
   "theatre": "Global Economy — Hormuz-Dependent States",
   "tag": "Hormuz-Dependent Economies",
   "headline": "HORMUZ-DEPENDENT ECONOMIES — THE DIVERGENCE BETWEEN POLICY RESPONSES IS NOW THE CLEAREST NATURAL EXPERIMENT OF THE CRISIS. SOUTH KOREA RUNS LICENCE-PLATE RATIONING RESTRICTING VEHICLE USE TO ALTERNATE DAYS PLUS TEMPORARY FUEL PRICE CAPS, AND IS THE ONLY AFFECTED STATE RESPONDING WITH CONCRETE RENEWABLE-EXPANSION PLANS. JAPAN, WHICH SOURCES 90% OF ITS OIL FROM THE MIDDLE EAST, IS THE MOST EXPOSED AND HAS NOT MATCHED THAT. INDIA AVOIDED FORMAL RATIONING ENTIRELY AND ABSORBED THE SHOCK FISCALLY — AN EXCISE CUT OF ROUGHLY ₹10 PER LITRE ON PETROL AND DIESEL, FORGOING ABOUT ₹1.7 LAKH CRORE IN REVENUE — WITH RETAIL FUEL PRICES UP ONLY ABOUT 7% AGAINST A GLOBAL AVERAGE OF 25-30%. ACROSS ASIA THE STANDING MEASURES ARE WORK-FROM-HOME AND FOREIGN-TRAVEL RESTRICTIONS IN INDIA, PUBLIC-TRANSPORT PROMOTION IN BANGLADESH, SHORTER WORKING WEEKS IN SRI LANKA, RESTRICTED BUSINESS HOURS IN PAKISTAN AND COOLING-TEMPERATURE LIMITS IN MALAYSIA. EIGHT OF NINE MAJOR CONTAINER CARRIERS ARE ROUTING VIA THE CAPE OF GOOD HOPE.",
   "tags": [
