    "Pakistan",
    "Malaysia",
    "Cape of Good Hope",
    "Container Carriers",
    "Qatar LNG",
    "Energy Policy Divergence"
   ],
   "source": "Table.Briefings, 'Energy crisis: How Japan and South Korea are responding differently to the Strait of Hormuz blockade,' 2026; CSIS, 'The Hormuz Implications for Energy Policymaking: Japan and South Korea'; Fox Business, 'Iran war fuels Asia energy crunch as India, Japan, others feel strain'; ORF Middle East, 'Asia's Energy Stress: Regional Impact of Hormuz Crisis'; IMPRI, 'India's Response To The Strait Of Hormuz Crisis'; India Narrative, 'The Strait That Didn't Break India: How a Decade of Preparation Defied the Hormuz Crisis'; Statista, 'Emergency policy responses to the Iran war fuel crisis across countries'; Fortune, 'Iran war fuel crisis: Asia work from home, closed schools, price caps,' March 11, 2026; straits.live Strait of Hormuz status board, 24 August 2026.",
   "summary": "THE CRISIS HAS RUN LONG ENOUGH TO SEPARATE POLICY EFFECTS FROM EXPOSURE EFFECTS, AND THE SEPARATION IS STARK.\n\nEXPOSURE DID NOT DETERMINE OUTCOME. Roughly a fifth of the world's oil moved through the Strait before February 28, some 80% of it to Asia. JAPAN sources 90% of its oil from the Middle East and is among the most exposed economies on earth. INDIA is also among the most Hormuz-exposed large importers. Yet India avoided formal fuel rationing altogether and saw retail fuel prices rise by only about 7% across the crisis period against a global average of 25-30%, while SOUTH KOREA, BANGLADESH, THAILAND, VIETNAM, INDONESIA, SRI LANKA and PAKISTAN all moved to standing rationing regimes.\n\nWHAT INDIA DID INSTEAD. It absorbed the shock through the budget: an excise-duty cut of roughly ₹10 PER LITRE on petrol and diesel, forgoing about ₹1.7 LAKH CRORE in revenue, layered on a decade of sourcing diversification. The non-price measures were behavioural rather than restrictive — encouraging work-from-home and restricting foreign travel.\n\nWHAT SOUTH KOREA DID. LICENCE-PLATE RATIONING restricting vehicle use to alternate days, plus temporary fuel price caps to blunt the immediate cost pass-through. Seoul is also the only affected state responding to the crisis with concrete plans to expand renewable generation — a structural answer to a chokepoint problem, and the only one of its kind in the dataset.\n\nTHE REST OF THE ASIAN MEASURE SET. Public-transport promotion in BANGLADESH, shorter working weeks in SRI LANKA, restricted business hours in PAKISTAN, and limits on cooling temperatures in MALAYSIA. School closures and work-from-home mandates were used across the region in the acute March phase.\n\nSHIPPING HAS RE-ROUTED STRUCTURALLY RATHER THAN WAITED. Eight of nine major container carriers are routing via the CAPE OF GOOD HOPE. Market odds of a Strait reopening by December 31 stand at 34%, and under 1% for August 31 — which is a market saying the disruption is now a fixture to be engineered around rather than an interruption to be waited out. Qatar's LNG position remains impaired, with roughly 17% of export capacity lost and South Korea and Japan facing critical LNG deficits from Qatari and Emirati supply.",
   "details": "PRE-WAR BASELINE: ~20% of world oil and ~19% of LNG through the Strait; ~80% of the oil bound for Asia. JAPAN: 90% of oil from the Middle East; no concrete renewable-expansion response recorded. SOUTH KOREA: licence-plate rationing (alternate-day vehicle use); temporary fuel price caps; the only affected state with concrete renewable-expansion plans. INDIA: no formal rationing; excise-duty cut of ~₹10/litre on petrol and diesel, ~₹1.7 lakh crore revenue forgone; retail fuel prices up ~7% against a 25-30% global average; work-from-home encouragement and foreign-travel restrictions. STANDING RATIONING REGIMES: South Korea, Bangladesh, Thailand, Vietnam, Indonesia, Sri Lanka, Pakistan. OTHER MEASURES: public-transport promotion (Bangladesh), shorter working weeks (Sri Lanka), restricted business hours (Pakistan), cooling-temperature limits (Malaysia). SHIPPING: 8 of 9 major container carriers routing via the Cape of Good Hope; market odds of reopening by August 31 <1%, by December 31 34%. QATAR: ~17% of LNG export capacity lost, ~$20bn annual revenue forgone, IMF April vintage projecting -14.7% for 2026; South Korea and Japan facing critical LNG deficits.",
   "prediction_impact": "Corpus predictions that Hormuz exposure would mechanically determine domestic fuel outcomes are DISCONFIRMED: India is among the most exposed large importers and had the mildest retail outcome, at roughly 7% against a 25-30% global average, because it paid the difference out of the fisc and had diversified sourcing for a decade. Predictions that a prolonged closure would force structural energy transition are PARTIALLY CONFIRMED and only in one case — South Korea is the sole affected state responding with concrete renewable expansion, which makes the transition thesis a policy variable rather than a physical consequence. Predictions that shipping would adapt around the chokepoint rather than wait for it are CONFIRMED: eight of nine major container carriers are on the Cape route and the market prices a December reopening at 34%."
  },
