   ],
   "source": "CNBC, 'Treasury Secretary to announce Iran sanctions as Tehran dismisses economic warfare threat,' August 23, 2026; Scott Bessent, opinion, Financial Times, August 23, 2026 ('At dawn begins an economic D-Day'); CNN live updates, 'US-Iran war latest: Iran's hardline new security chief issues stark threat to Gulf states ahead of fresh US sanctions,' August 23, 2026; Al Jazeera live blog, 'Iran war live: Iranian parliament advances plans for Hormuz service fees,' August 23, 2026; Middle East Eye, 'Iranian parliamentary committee approves provision to charge ships in Hormuz,' August 23, 2026; The Tribune (India)/Reuters, 'Iran approves service fees for “authorised” ships transiting Strait of Hormuz,' August 23, 2026; The Washington Post/AP, 'Iran security chief threatens neighbors against economic measures, and other Mideast developments,' August 22-23, 2026; TASS, '70 vessels redirected by US military since resumption of blockade of Iran — CENTCOM,' August 23, 2026; U.S. Central Command via X, blockade tally as of August 23, 2026; straits.live Strait of Hormuz status board, Day 176, 23 August 2026 2300Z; The Times of Israel, 'Declaring “economic D-Day,” Trump says any country trading with Iran will be sanctioned,' August 20-21, 2026; OPB/NPR, 'Trump warns of “economic D-Day” against Iran, but Tehran is well acquainted with sanctions,' August 21, 2026; Arab News, 'Egypt and Iran foreign ministers discuss reviving US negotiations,' August 22-23, 2026; RFE/RL via GlobalSecurity.org, 'Egypt Stresses Need For Diplomacy In Call With Iran Foreign Minister,' August 23, 2026; Egypt Today, 'Egypt stresses freedom of navigation, Gulf security in talks with Iran,' August 23, 2026; AAA Fuel Prices national average, August 23, 2026; The Washington Post, 'More than 750 U.S. service members wounded in Iran war,' August 21, 2026; Stars and Stripes, 'Pentagon adds 60 wounded to Iran war casualty count,' August 21, 2026; Investing.com/Analysis, 'Inside Iran's Economic Meltdown,' August 2026; The Jerusalem Post, 'Israel strikes in Gaza's Khan Yunis' live updates, August 23, 2026.",
   "summary": "THE CYCLE'S DEFINING FACT IS THAT BOTH BELLIGERENTS SPENT IT DRAFTING DOCUMENTS. Washington's next move is a Treasury news conference; Tehran's is a bill in committee. Neither instrument requires a soldier, and neither produced one.\n\nWASHINGTON PUT THE OFFENSIVE IN WRITING BEFORE DELIVERING IT. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent published an opinion piece in the Financial Times on Sunday August 23 opening: 'At dawn begins an economic D-Day — the single greatest financial offensive ever marshalled against an adversary.' His news conference detailing the package is set for 2 p.m. ET on Monday August 24, and at the time this cycle closed it had not yet been held; what follows is the announced shape rather than the announced content. The declared targets, from the President's August 20 Truth Social post, are 'oil smuggling, swap lines, cash transfers, exchange houses, ship registries, front companies'; the declared mechanism is secondary sanctions against 'ANY country that allows its financial institutions, businesses, airports or government entities to provide any type of lifeline to Iran.' Bessent has separately promised 'the toughest sanctions in history,' 'the greatest coordinated economic isolation in the history of the world,' and — the line that matters most to this audit — that 'if we are doing the maximum economic pressure, then that means that likely there will not be a large-scale kinetic restart.' The administration's own stated theory of victory now runs through the banking system rather than through a beach.\n\nTEHRAN'S ANSWER WAS ALSO A DOCUMENT, AND IT IS THE MOST CONCRETE MOVE IRAN HAS MADE TOWARD MONETISING THE CHOKEPOINT. On August 23 the Iranian parliament's National Security and Foreign Policy Commission approved Article 3 of its plan titled 'Strategic Action to Ensure the Security and Progress of the Strait of Hormuz.' Ships of countries permitted to transit will pay for services 'including maritime, environmental, fueling under special conditions, insurance, safety, and other services provided in the Strait of Hormuz,' and 'these fees will be charged in Rials or any other currency desired by the Islamic Republic of Iran.' The commission spokesman is named as Hassan Qashqavi by Middle East Eye and Al Jazeera and as Ebrahim Rezaei by Reuters and the Tribune; the audit records both attributions rather than choosing between them. The measure still requires a full parliamentary vote on its provisions and on overall passage, and then Guardian Council review for constitutional and Islamic compliance — it is a committee stage, not a law. The straits.live board adds that violating vessels would face fines, detention or confiscation. Passage was free before the war. This is the toll regime the United States rejected in July and that the June MOU was reported to exclude, now advancing through Iran's own legislature rather than through a negotiation.\n\nTHE SECURITY CHIEF ESCALATED FROM SELECTIVITY TO TOTALITY, AND FOR THE FIRST TIME NAMED THE BYPASS ROUTES. Mohsen Rezaei, secretary of the Supreme National Security Council, warned through IRNA that Tehran 'will not allow a single drop of oil to leave the Persian Gulf' if US-led economic pressure escalates — a claim carried by the straits.live board as reported and unconfirmed. He said that if Trump 'wants to do something, we will retaliate in a seismic manner,' repeated that any state joining the sanctions is 'considered an enemy,' and — the genuinely new element — said Iran would target the oil-shipping routes out of the Persian Gulf that are being built as alternatives to the Strait. He also cautioned against any additional US military deployment to the region, and said the transit fees would be pressed through the Oman channel. The threat has therefore widened from the chokepoint to the workarounds, on the same weekend that Saudi Arabia and France convened in Paris specifically to build those workarounds.\n\nTHE MOST CONSEQUENTIAL IRANIAN STATEMENT OF THE CYCLE WAS NOT A THREAT. In two appearances in Tehran on Sunday August 23, President Masoud Pezeshkian said Iran 'cannot continue with war forever' and that 'one day, a decision must be made to save the country from the state of “neither war nor peace”.' He defended the June memorandum of understanding — reporting states he did so despite the purported misgivings of Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei — saying that everyone directly involved in the negotiations believed the memorandum was the best way forward and that 'by implementing this memorandum, we can resolve the problems with logic and dignity.' This is the first cycle of the war in which an open elite split over the settlement has been reported on the record on the Iranian side, and it runs in the opposite direction from Rezaei's escalation published the same day. The audit records the divergence and does not resolve it.\n\nTHE STRIKE PAUSE HELD FOR A TWENTY-THIRD NIGHT. No US or Israeli strike inside Iranian territory has been confirmed since the President cancelled the planned multi-day energy campaign late on August 1. The kinetic activity that continued was elsewhere: an Israeli drone strike in the south-western Syrian village of Beit Jin caused injuries, and Israeli operations continued in Gaza's Khan Yunis and in Lebanon.\n\nTHE BLOCKADE TIGHTENED BY TWO HULLS AND THE TRAFFIC PICTURE REMAINS IRRECONCILABLE. CENTCOM said that as of August 23 US forces had redirected 70 commercial vessels, disabled three and boarded two since the blockade resumed on July 14 — up from 68 as of August 21. Against that, CNN reported the number of ships using the Strait at 20% of the pre-war average; the straits.live board carried a single transit on its most recent daily datapoint against a 73-a-day baseline; and an American official quoted in reporting on the D-Day announcement said US forces are facilitating 15-20 transits a night carrying roughly ten million barrels of oil a day, about half the pre-war volume, adding: 'The Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps can be a nuisance, but they don't control the strait. We do.' These figures cannot all be true, they are not reconcilable from open sources, and the audit logs the spread rather than picking a number.\n\nDIPLOMACY RAN THROUGH CAIRO. Egyptian Foreign Minister Badr Abdelatty and Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi held a telephone call on Saturday August 22 on creating conditions for de-escalation and a return to diplomacy, covering the Oman-Iran negotiations and implementation of the June 18 US-Iran memorandum. Abdelatty stressed international freedom of navigation and respect for the security and stability of Gulf countries. Egypt is not an official mediator — Pakistan and Qatar hold that role — which makes the call an addition to the mediation surface rather than a change in it.\n\nTHE LEDGER. The Pentagon's published toll held at 775 US casualties — 18 killed and 757 wounded — with no addition since the single-day increase of 60 on August 21. Brent stood at $94.39 on the straits.live board at 2300Z on August 23, unchanged over 24 hours with futures markets closed for the weekend and roughly 31% above the ~$72 pre-crisis baseline. War-risk cover runs about $10.0m per VLCC passage, forty times pre-crisis, with six P&I clubs having withdrawn coverage. The AAA national average for regular gasoline was $4.0986 on August 23, down fractionally from $4.1024 the day before but 90 cents above a year earlier; diesel reached $5.6074, a record August level and nearly two dollars above last year. Iran's own economy is where the sanctions land: the IMF projects a 2026 contraction of about 6.1% with inflation near 68.9%, the rial has been reported at roughly two million to the dollar, and the official minimum monthly wage of 166.255m rials covers about 37% of an estimated basic living basket.",
   "details": "SANCTIONS: Bessent FT op-ed published Sunday August 23 — 'At dawn begins an economic D-Day — the single greatest financial offensive ever marshalled against an adversary.' News conference 2 p.m. ET Monday August 24, not yet held at cycle close. Declared targets (Trump, Truth Social, August 20): oil smuggling, swap lines, cash transfers, exchange houses, ship registries, front companies. Mechanism: secondary sanctions on third-country banks, businesses, airports and government entities. IRAN FEE LAW: National Security and Foreign Policy Commission approved Article 3 of 'Strategic Action to Ensure the Security and Progress of the Strait of Hormuz,' August 23 — fees for maritime, environmental, conditional fueling, insurance, safety and other services; payable in rials 'or any other currency desired by the Islamic Republic'; spokesman named as Hassan Qashqavi (MEE, Al Jazeera) and Ebrahim Rezaei (Reuters, Tribune); next steps full parliamentary vote then Guardian Council; violators reported to face fines, detention or confiscation. REZAEI: 'will not allow a single drop of oil to leave the Persian Gulf' if pressure escalates (IRNA, reported/unconfirmed); 'if [Trump] wants to do something, we will retaliate in a seismic manner'; any state joining sanctions 'considered an enemy'; would target Gulf bypass routes; cautioned against additional US deployments. PEZESHKIAN (two Tehran appearances, Sunday August 23): Iran 'cannot continue with war forever'; 'one day, a decision must be made to save the country from the state of “neither war nor peace”'; defended the June MOU despite reported misgivings of Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei; 'by implementing this memorandum, we can resolve the problems with logic and dignity.' STRIKE PAUSE: no confirmed US or Israeli strike inside Iran since August 1 — a twenty-third consecutive night. Adjacent kinetic activity: Israeli drone strike at Beit Jin, south-western Syria (injuries); Israeli operations in Khan Yunis and Lebanon. BLOCKADE: CENTCOM 70 vessels redirected / 3 disabled / 2 boarded as of August 23 (68 on August 21; 67 on August 20; 64 on August 17; 44 on August 3; 35 on August 2), since the July 14 resumption; more than 20 warships enforcing. TRAFFIC (IRRECONCILABLE): CNN — 20% of pre-war average; straits.live — 1 transit on the latest daily datapoint vs 73/day baseline (1%); US official — 15-20 transits nightly, ~10m bbl/day, about half pre-war, 'The IRGC can be a nuisance, but they don't control the strait. We do.'; Al Jazeera route shares August 1-19 — 236 hulls, 83 openly Iranian route, 3 openly Omani, 148 dark. WAR RISK: ~$10.0m per VLCC transit, 40× pre-crisis; six P&I clubs have withdrawn coverage. OIL: Brent $94.39 at straits.live 2300Z August 23, +0.00% over 24 hours with futures markets closed; ~31% above the ~$72 pre-crisis baseline. PUMP: AAA national average regular $4.0986 on August 23 (yesterday $4.1024; week ago $4.0656; month ago $4.0911; year ago $3.1497). Diesel $5.6074 (yesterday $5.5947; week ago $5.4460; month ago $5.2089; year ago $3.6893). US CASUALTIES: 775 total — 18 killed, 757 wounded; unchanged since the August 21 addition of 60. DIPLOMACY: Abdelatty-Araghchi phone call Saturday August 22 on de-escalation, the Oman-Iran track and implementation of the June 18 MOU; Egypt stressed freedom of navigation and Gulf security; Egypt is not an official mediator (Pakistan and Qatar are). IRAN ECONOMY: IMF 2026 contraction ~6.1%, inflation ~68.9%; rial reported around two million to the dollar; official minimum monthly wage 166.255m rials against an estimated 450m rial basic basket (~37%).",
   "prediction_impact": "The predicted US ground seizure of the Strait of Hormuz or Kharg Island remains DISCONFIRMED at Day 179, and this cycle is an unusually clean instance of why. Both belligerents took their most consequential action of the cycle in writing: the United States through a Treasury news conference and an op-ed, Iran through a committee vote on a fee statute. Corpus predictions that the chokepoint would be resolved by force of arms are DISCONFIRMED for this instance; predictions that Iran would seek to convert the Strait into a rent-collecting asset are CONFIRMED IN DIRECTION and now have a legislative instrument behind them, though the bill has passed a committee and not a parliament. Predictions that sanctions escalation would precede rather than accompany a ground phase are SUPPORTED by the administration's own framing: Bessent has said that maximum economic pressure means 'likely there will not be a large-scale kinetic restart.' The Pezeshkian statements are the first on-record evidence of an Iranian elite split over the settlement, which cuts against corpus models of a monolithic regime and toward models that predicted the war would fracture Tehran's leadership — those are PARTIALLY CONFIRMED, with the caveat that a president's public dissent is not a policy change. Rezaei's threat against the bypass routes is the first Iranian escalation aimed at the workarounds rather than the chokepoint, and it tests corpus predictions that closure would prove circumventable: if the alternatives are themselves targetable, circumvention is slower and more expensive than those predictions assumed.",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "The Iranian parliament's approval of Hormuz service fees is read as Tehran institutionalising permanent control of the waterway — and, by the opposing reading, as an admission that Iran can no longer close the Strait and is monetising what it can no longer deny, converting a blockade it cannot sustain into a revenue stream it can.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented: on August 23 the National Security and Foreign Policy Commission approved Article 3 of the 'Strategic Action to Ensure the Security and Progress of the Strait of Hormuz' plan, charging 'authorised' ships for maritime, environmental, conditional fueling, insurance and safety services, payable in rials or another currency Iran accepts. Documented: the measure requires a full parliamentary vote and Guardian Council review and is not law. Documented: on the same day Rezaei said the Strait 'is closed and will not reopen' absent a change in US behaviour and that Iran would allow no oil out of the Gulf if pressure escalates. Documented: Iran granted Iraqi tankers a transit exemption on August 22. Not documented: whether any vessel has paid or would pay such a fee, whether underwriters would price a fee-paying transit as insurable, or how a fee regime is enforced against the 62% of August traffic that sailed dark.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "Iran's parliamentary National Security and Foreign Policy Commission and its spokesman, presenting the fees as a sovereign service charge",
      "SNSC Secretary Mohsen Rezaei, pressing the fee regime through the Oman channel while maintaining the Strait is closed",
      "The United States, which rejected an Iranian toll or transit-authorisation regime in July and now asserts the waterway as 'an American territory'",
      "Maritime analysts arguing that a state which can charge for passage is a state that expects passage to resume"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "A fee schedule for traffic that is not moving is untestable. No open source reports a fee having been levied or paid, and the dominant transit mode is dark by definition, which is the mode least likely to present itself for invoicing. Resolution would require either a documented fee payment and a corresponding insurance quotation, or an Iranian enforcement action against a non-paying transit.",
     "open_question_ref": "iran-hormuz-administration-tolls-dispute-june-2026"
    },
    {
     "reading": "Pezeshkian's 'we cannot continue with war forever' is read as evidence of a real split in Tehran between a presidency seeking an exit and a Supreme Leader refusing one — and, by the opposing reading, as a sanctioned division of labour in which the president speaks to a domestic audience and to Western capitals while the security council speaks to Washington, with no actual disagreement about policy.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented: in two Tehran appearances on Sunday August 23 Pezeshkian said Iran 'cannot continue with war forever,' that 'one day, a decision must be made to save the country from the state of “neither war nor peace”,' and that 'by implementing this memorandum, we can resolve the problems with logic and dignity.' Documented: reporting states he defended the June MOU despite the purported misgivings of Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei. Documented: on the same day Rezaei threatened to stop all oil leaving the Gulf and to retaliate 'in a seismic manner.' Not documented: any statement by Mojtaba Khamenei on the MOU, any decision of the Supreme National Security Council reversing or endorsing Pezeshkian's position, or any account of the misgivings other than through sourcing described as purported.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "President Masoud Pezeshkian, publicly defending the memorandum and the negotiating team",
      "CNN and other outlets reporting 'signs of growing differences within the Iranian leadership over negotiations with the United States'",
      "Iranian hardline commentary treating the president's remarks as speaking only for himself",
      "US officials who have argued throughout the war that Iranian public divisions are stage-managed"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "The Islamic Republic does not publish the deliberations of the Supreme National Security Council or the Leader's office, and a genuine split and a coordinated dual message are observationally identical from outside. Resolution would require an attributable statement from the Leader's office on the memorandum, a personnel change, or an Iranian negotiating move that contradicts one of the two public lines.",
     "open_question_ref": "iran-succession-mojtaba-khamenei-2026"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-08-24",
   "headline": "DAY 179 GROUND-INVASION TRACKER — STATUS HOLDS no_ground_troops FOR A 179TH CONSECUTIVE DAY, AND THIS CYCLE CARRIES ONE CORRECTION AND ONE MATERIAL BACKFILL. (1) CORRECTION: NET CARRIER PRESENCE IN CENTCOM IS TWO, NOT ONE. USS GEORGE H.W. BUSH (CVN-77) HAS BEEN IN THEATRE SINCE APRIL 23 AND USS GEORGE WASHINGTON (CVN-73) ARRIVED AUGUST 19 RELIEVING USS ABRAHAM LINCOLN, WHICH SAILED FOR SAN DIEGO AFTER ROUGHLY 200 CONSECUTIVE DAYS AT SEA WITHOUT A LIBERTY PORT CALL. THE PRIOR CYCLE'S 'PRESENCE IS ONE' IS WITHDRAWN; THE SUBSTANTIVE POINT IS UNCHANGED, BECAUSE TWO CARRIERS IS THE SAME NUMBER CENTCOM PUBLISHED IN JULY — A ROTATION, NOT A SURGE. (2) BACKFILL: THE CLOSEST THE WAR HAS COME TO AN ACTUAL GROUND ORDER IS OLDER AND MORE CONCRETE THAN THIS BRIEFING HAD RECORDED. CNN REPORTED ON JUNE 12 THAT CHAIRMAN OF THE JOINT CHIEFS GEN. DAN CAINE MADE A SECRET, RUSHED TRIP FROM A NATO MEETING IN BRUSSELS TO CENTCOM HEADQUARTERS IN TAMPA ON MAY 19 TO BE BRIEFED ON PLANS TO SEND GROUND TROOPS INTO IRAN TO SEIZE ITS HIGHLY ENRICHED URANIUM — AND THAT TRUMP DECLINED THE OPERATION AFTER WARNINGS OF SIGNIFICANT US CASUALTIES. ON JUNE 13 CNN REPORTED IRAN HAD COLLAPSED TUNNELS AND BOOBY-TRAPPED ENTRANCES WITH MINES TO SEAL THE CACHE. (3) EVERY EXECUTION INDICATOR REMAINED AT ZERO: NO LANDING, NO EMBARKATION, NO MEU TASKING, NO AIRBORNE INSERTION, NO RELIEF ARG, NO NEW DEPLOYMENT ORDER, NO MOBILISATION, NO DRAFT ORDER, NO PREPOSITIONING CONSISTENT WITH A LODGEMENT. (4) THE AMPHIBIOUS RUNG IS STRUCTURALLY THIN AND THE NAVY SAYS SO: ONE ARG DEPLOYED WORLDWIDE, WITH COMBATANT COMMANDERS REQUESTING 'WELL NORTH OF THREE' ARG-MEU PAIRINGS. (5) THE ADMINISTRATION'S OWN FORWARD PATH IS FINANCIAL, AND BESSENT SAYS MAXIMUM ECONOMIC PRESSURE MEANS 'LIKELY THERE WILL NOT BE A LARGE-SCALE KINETIC RESTART.' (6) IRAN'S OWN THREAT MODEL STILL ANTICIPATES A FORCE FLOW THAT HAS NOT COME: REZAEI CAUTIONED AGAINST ADDITIONAL US MILITARY DEPLOYMENT TO THE REGION.",
   "theatre": "Iran / Strait of Hormuz",
   "tag": "Ground Invasion Tracker",
   "tags": [
    "Ground Invasion Tracker",
