   ],
   "source": "The National, 'France's Macron and Saudi Crown Prince to discuss oil pipelines to by-pass Strait of Hormuz,' August 23, 2026; The National, 'Saudi Crown Prince visits France to sign deals and discuss Strait of Hormuz,' August 22, 2026; Bloomberg, 'Macron and MBS to Focus on Hormuz, Deals in Paris Talks,' August 21, 2026; Energy Connects, 'Macron and MBS to Focus on Hormuz, Deals in Paris Talks,' August 2026; Gulf News, 'Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman meets France's Macron,' August 2026; The Washington Post/AP, 'Iran security chief threatens neighbors against economic measures,' August 22-23, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Iran warns of retaliation against countries joining US “economic war”,' August 22, 2026; straits.live status board, 23 August 2026 2300Z (IRNA ‘not a single drop’ line, recorded as reported); Egypt Today, 'Egypt stresses freedom of navigation, Gulf security in talks with Iran,' August 23, 2026; Fortune, 'UAE halts all trade with Iran after new missile fire in Gulf,' August 19, 2026.",
   "summary": "THE CHOKEPOINT IS NOW BEING ENGINEERED AROUND AT HEAD-OF-STATE LEVEL, AND IRAN HAS RESPONDED BY PUTTING THE ENGINEERING ON ITS TARGET LIST.\n\nCrown Prince Mohammed bin Salman arrived in France for his first visit since 2023, and on Monday August 24 meets President Emmanuel Macron with expanded oil pipelines as an alternative to the Strait of Hormuz as the principal file. French presidency officials described the proposals as increasing trade through Omani ports located outside the Persian Gulf, expanding and potentially doubling pipeline capacity in Saudi Arabia and other countries, and developing new rail links. The two governments have established a joint task force on energy and logistics connections between the Middle East and Europe, which meets at ministerial level on the same day. Agreements covering transport, energy, technology and health are being prepared alongside. This is the first time in the war that Hormuz substitution has been the headline agenda item of a bilateral summit between a Gulf producer and a European power rather than a line in an analyst's briefing note.\n\nIRAN NAMED THE SAME INFRASTRUCTURE AS A TARGET THE DAY BEFORE. Mohsen Rezaei, secretary of the Supreme National Security Council, said on August 23 that Iran would target the oil-shipping routes out of the Persian Gulf that serve as alternatives to the Strait of Hormuz if regional states join what he called the economic war, restating that any country participating in the sanctions is 'considered an enemy' and that their interests would be harmed. Through IRNA he went further, warning that Tehran 'will not allow a single drop of oil to leave the Persian Gulf' if US-led economic pressure escalates — a line the audit records as reported and unconfirmed. The threat is a recognition of what the Paris agenda represents: if the Gulf can route around the chokepoint, the chokepoint stops being leverage, and the only way to preserve the leverage is to make the alternatives unsafe as well.\n\nTHE LITTORAL IS STILL NOT MOVING AS A BLOC. The UAE's indefinite suspension of all trade, commercial exchange and financial transactions with Iran, announced on August 19 after its air defences detected two Iranian ballistic missiles, removes a partner that supplied more than 30% of Iranian imports worth some $21bn and took nearly 13% of Iranian exports worth some $7bn; Tehran denies the launches and warns of 'false flag' operations. Iraq holds a transit exemption granted by Tehran on August 22. Oman is negotiating the transit arrangement and the fee regime. Saudi Arabia is in Paris designing the bypass. Egypt, from outside the Gulf, used its August 22 foreign-ministers' call with Tehran to stress international freedom of navigation and respect for the security and stability of Gulf countries. Four different postures, one coastline.\n\nTHE STANDING DAMAGE PICTURE IS UNCHANGED. ADNOC counts 16 vessels attacked since February 28, one crew member killed and 20 injured. Qatar is projected to contract 14.7% in 2026 and Saudi Arabia 1.4% on IMF April-vintage figures, with Gulf energy-facility repairs estimated at about $58bn. The August 7 Mecca Joint Defence Agreement binding Saudi Arabia, Türkiye and Pakistan remains in force and remains the largest structural fact bearing on any future US ground option in the region.",
   "details": "PARIS, AUGUST 24: Macron-MBS meeting at the Franco-Saudi Strategic Partnership Council; principal file is expanding oil pipelines as an alternative to Hormuz. French presidency proposals: increased trade through Omani ports outside the Persian Gulf; expanding and potentially doubling pipeline capacity in Saudi Arabia and other countries; new regional rail links. France-Saudi joint task force on Middle East-Europe energy and logistics connections meets at ministerial level the same day. Agreements prepared on transport, energy, technology and health. MBS's first France visit since 2023. IRAN, AUGUST 23: Rezaei said Iran would target Persian Gulf oil-shipping routes that are alternatives to the Strait if neighbours join the economic war; any participating state 'considered an enemy' with its interests harmed; via IRNA, Tehran 'will not allow a single drop of oil to leave the Persian Gulf' if pressure escalates (reported, unconfirmed); cautioned against additional US military deployments; would press transit fees through the Oman channel. GULF POSTURES: UAE — indefinite suspension of all trade, commercial exchange and financial transactions with Iran from August 19 after detecting two Iranian ballistic missiles (first outside territorial waters, second inside); pre-war UAE supplied >30% of Iranian imports (~$21bn) and took ~13% of Iranian exports (~$7bn); Iran denies the launches and alleges false-flag operations. Iraq — tanker transit exemption granted by Tehran August 22. Oman — negotiating transit arrangement and fee regime. Saudi Arabia — in Paris designing the bypass. Egypt — August 22 Abdelatty-Araghchi call stressing freedom of navigation and Gulf security. DAMAGE AND MACRO: ADNOC 16 vessels attacked since February 28, one killed, 20 injured. IMF April vintage: Qatar −14.7% for 2026, Saudi Arabia −1.4%. Gulf energy-facility repairs estimated ~$58bn. ALLIANCE: August 7 Mecca Joint Defence Agreement (Saudi Arabia, Türkiye, Pakistan) remains in force.",
   "prediction_impact": "Corpus predictions that a Hormuz closure would be absorbed by rerouting are CONFIRMED IN DIRECTION and now have state-level sponsorship: a Gulf producer and a European power are convening at head-of-state level specifically to build maritime, pipeline and rail alternatives, with a standing ministerial task force. Predictions that the Gulf monarchies would respond as a bloc remain DISCONFIRMED — the UAE has cut trade entirely, Iraq holds an Iranian exemption, Oman is negotiating a fee regime with Tehran, and Riyadh is in Paris. Rezaei's threat against the bypass routes is a genuine test of the substitution thesis the audit has tracked since March: predictions that circumvention would be straightforward are now QUALIFIED, because the alternatives are being declared targets before they are built. Predictions of a US ground operation launched from Gulf territory remain DISCONFIRMED and are further constrained by the Mecca pact binding the three states whose basing and overflight such an operation would most require."
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-08-24",
   "headline": "RUSSIA-UKRAINE — THE LINE HOLDS WHILE THE DIPLOMATIC LANGUAGE MOVES A NOTCH AND THE STRIKE WAR CONTINUES ON BOTH SIDES. ON AUGUST 23 RUSSIAN GUIDED BOMBS STRUCK IZYUM, WOUNDING TWO PEOPLE AND DAMAGING AT LEAST TWENTY BUILDINGS, CARS AND POWER LINES; OVERNIGHT UKRAINIAN FORCES STRUCK A RADAR STATION AND A SITE USED TO STORE AND LAUNCH UNMANNED SURFACE VESSELS IN OCCUPIED CRIMEA. ON AUGUST 21 DEPUTY FOREIGN MINISTER SERGEY RYABKOV SAID MOSCOW IS READY TO LISTEN TO 'NEW IDEAS' FOR ENDING THE WAR PROVIDED THEY ALIGN WITH PUTIN'S POSITION AND WITH 'REALITIES ON THE GROUND' — AN OPENING PHRASED AS A PRECONDITION. PRESIDENT ZELENSKY SAID UKRAINE HAS DEVELOPED A PLAN WITH ITS US AND EUROPEAN PARTNERS ENVISAGING A CEASEFIRE, A FREE ECONOMIC ZONE AND A WITHDRAWAL OF TROOPS. UKRAINE'S GENERAL STAFF PUTS CUMULATIVE RUSSIAN PERSONNEL LOSSES AT ABOUT 1,476,580 AS OF AUGUST 23. NET RUSSIAN TERRITORIAL GAINS FOR 2026 REMAIN AT 81.1 SQUARE KILOMETRES.",
   "theatre": "Russia-Ukraine War",
   "tag": "Russia-Ukraine",
   "tags": [
