   ],
   "source": "CNN Politics, 'Venezuela's Nicolás Maduro gets a June trial date,' July 22, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'US judge sets 2027 trial date for Venezuela's Maduro and wife,' July 22, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Cuba's power grid collapses again despite restoration efforts,' August 3, 2026; CNN, 'Storms disrupt Cuba's power restoration,' August 3, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'De la Espriella sworn in as Colombia's leader, vows crackdown on armed groups,' August 8, 2026; The Washington Post, 'Colombia's new president signals energy policy shift, backs oil and gas,' August 7, 2026; NPR, 'Colombia's political pendulum swings right as de la Espriella is sworn in,' August 7, 2026.",
   "summary": "NO MATERIAL DEVELOPMENT WAS REPORTED IN THIS THEATRE OVER THE WEEKEND, AND THE ENTRY IS LOGGED TO PRESERVE CONTINUITY RATHER THAN TO RECORD MOVEMENT.\n\nVENEZUELA. The government-opposition talks that opened in Caracas on August 6 under Jorge Rodríguez for the government and Dinorah Figuera for the opposition continue without a published electoral timetable. Delcy Rodríguez remains acting president, having been sworn in after Maduro's capture; she has made significant changes to the armed forces and has met many of Washington's demands, and the United States lifted the sanctions imposed on her in 2018 in April. Nicolás Maduro and Cilia Flores remain in US custody. Their criminal trial is set for June 1, 2027 before Judge Alvin Hellerstein in the Southern District of New York, on four counts including narco-terrorism conspiracy and cocaine importation conspiracy, with a possible life sentence; the date was jointly proposed by prosecutors and defence counsel in late July and may shift over classified-information handling. Maduro has endorsed the talks from jail.\n\nCUBA. The island is still recovering from the sixth nationwide blackout of 2026 on August 2-3, when the national electricity system collapsed and thunderstorms then disrupted restoration. The structural cause is unchanged: Cuba has been cut off from its main fuel source since January, when the United States captured Maduro and the acting Venezuelan government stopped shipping oil, on top of chronic shortages of fuel and replacement parts.\n\nCOLOMBIA. Abelardo de la Espriella has been president since August 7. He has signalled an energy-policy shift toward full reactivation of the hydrocarbon sector and has vowed a crackdown on armed groups; Gustavo Petro continues to dispute the June result. De la Espriella has separately expressed support for regime change in Cuba.",
   "details": "VENEZUELA: Caracas government-opposition talks opened August 6, led by Jorge Rodríguez (government) and Dinorah Figuera (opposition); no published electoral timetable. Delcy Rodríguez acting president; US sanctions on her lifted in April; significant changes made to the armed forces. Maduro and Cilia Flores in US custody; trial June 1, 2027 before Judge Alvin Hellerstein, SDNY; four counts including narco-terrorism conspiracy and cocaine importation conspiracy; possible life sentence; date jointly proposed late July, may shift on classified-information handling. Maduro has endorsed the talks from jail. CUBA: still recovering from the sixth nationwide blackout of 2026 on August 2-3; storms disrupted restoration; cut off from its main fuel source since January when the acting Venezuelan government stopped shipping oil; chronic fuel and spare-parts shortages. COLOMBIA: Abelardo de la Espriella in office since August 7; energy-policy shift toward full hydrocarbon-sector reactivation; vowed crackdown on armed groups; Petro disputes the June result; de la Espriella has voiced support for regime change in Cuba.",
   "prediction_impact": "Corpus predictions of a rapid, US-installed electoral transition in Venezuela remain PARTIALLY CONFIRMED AT BEST: talks are running, an acting president has met many US demands and had her sanctions lifted, and no electoral timetable has been published eighteen days into the process. Predictions of a Cuban systemic collapse remain UNTESTED but continue to accumulate supporting indicators — six nationwide blackouts in a single year with the fuel supply severed at source. Predictions of a rightward regional realignment following US action in Venezuela are CONFIRMED IN DIRECTION by Colombia's transfer of power on August 7 and the incoming government's alignment with Washington on both energy and Cuba."
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-08-24",
   "headline": "US-CHINA — THE ENFORCEMENT COLLISION ARRIVES TODAY, AND THE VOLUMES SHOW THE BLOCKADE IS ALREADY DOING WHAT THE SANCTIONS ARE MEANT TO DO. CHINA'S IRANIAN CRUDE IMPORTS HAVE FALLEN TO ABOUT 534,000 BARRELS A DAY SO FAR IN AUGUST AGAINST AN AVERAGE OF 1.4 MILLION A DAY LAST YEAR — A COLLAPSE ACHIEVED BY NAVAL INTERDICTION BEFORE A SINGLE NEW DESIGNATION HAS BEEN ISSUED. BEIJING'S POSITION IS UNCHANGED AND EXPLICIT: FOREIGN MINISTRY SPOKESMAN LIN JIAN SAYS 'SANCTIONS AND PRESSURE TACTICS ARE NOT THE SOLUTION' AND THAT SUCH MEASURES DO NOT 'FIT THE INTEREST OF ANY PARTY'; CHINA HAS ALREADY INVOKED ITS 2021 BLOCKING RULE TO DIRECT FIRMS NOT TO COMPLY WITH US DESIGNATIONS OF REFINERIES BUYING IRANIAN CRUDE. BESSENT HAS TOLD THIRD COUNTRIES THEY ARE 'EITHER WITH US OR AGAINST US.' AND THE COLLISION IS SCHEDULED AGAINST A CALENDAR: XI JINPING IS DUE IN WASHINGTON ON SEPTEMBER 24, A VISIT HE HAS REPORTEDLY TRIMMED TO THE TRUMP MEETING ALONE, SKIPPING THE UN GENERAL ASSEMBLY.",
   "theatre": "US-China Trade",
   "tag": "US-China",
   "tags": [
    "US-China",
    "Iranian Crude Imports Collapse",
    "534,000 bpd",
    "Blockade Effect",
    "Lin Jian",
    "Sanctions Not The Solution",
    "MOFCOM Blocking Rule",
    "Teapot Refineries",
    "Secondary Sanctions",
    "Either With Us Or Against Us",
    "Xi Washington Visit September 24",
    "Skips UNGA",
    "Taiwan Han Kuang",
    "Enforcement Collision"
   ],
   "source": "CNBC, 'Treasury Secretary to announce Iran sanctions as Tehran dismisses economic warfare threat,' August 23, 2026; RFE/RL via GlobalSecurity.org, 'Report: US Blockade Squeezes Iranian Oil Supplies To China,' August 21, 2026; The China-Global South Project, 'Iranian Oil Exports to China Face Major Challenges,' August 21, 2026; Newsweek, 'China Fights Back Against US' Iran Oil Sanctions,' 2026 (MOFCOM blocking-rule order); Fox News, 'China orders firms to ignore US Iran sanctions, daring US to enforce crackdown,' 2026; RBC-Ukraine, 'Trump announces Xi Jinping's US visit and reveals date,' July 2026; Press TV, 'Xi Jinping to limit September US visit to meeting with Trump, skip UN General Assembly,' August 18, 2026; ABC News, 'Taiwan's military drills suggest deeper alignment with the U.S.,' August 2026.",
   "summary": "THE MOST IMPORTANT US-CHINA FACT OF THIS CYCLE IS A VOLUME, NOT A STATEMENT. China's imports of Iranian crude have fallen to roughly 534,000 barrels a day so far in August against an average of about 1.4 million barrels a day last year. Beijing has been buying the overwhelming majority of Iran's exported crude — estimates in the 80-90% range — so a fall of that magnitude is the blockade working on the demand side before the sanctions package that is meant to accomplish it has even been announced. The instrument that has actually reduced Chinese purchases of Iranian oil is a naval cordon, not a designation list, which is a distinction the audit records because the announced strategy is the reverse.\n\nBEIJING'S DECLARED POSITION IS UNMOVED. Foreign ministry spokesman Lin Jian has restated that 'sanctions and pressure tactics are not the solution' and that such measures do not 'fit the interest of any party,' consistent with China's standing line that it opposes unilateral sanctions lacking a basis in international law and a UN Security Council mandate. Behind the statement sits a concrete legal instrument: China's Ministry of Commerce has invoked the sanctions-blocking mechanism introduced in 2021 to direct Chinese companies not to comply with US designations of independent refineries importing Iranian crude — the first time Beijing has explicitly ordered its firms to defy such measures. Five Shandong- and Hebei-based refiners plus Hengli have been designated by OFAC since March 2025. Chinese embassy spokesman Liu Pengyu's formulation is the durable one: 'The normal economic and trade cooperation conducted by Chinese enterprises with relevant countries should not be subject to interference or disruption.'\n\nTHE AMERICAN DEMAND IS BINARY AND THE CALENDAR IS SHORT. Treasury Secretary Bessent has told third countries they are 'either with us or against us' and that 'any remaining tie to Tehran will hasten a nation's economic oblivion,' and his news conference today is expected to lean heavily on secondary sanctions against foreign banks, businesses and governments. That collision is scheduled against a summit: Xi Jinping is due in Washington on or around September 24, arriving September 23 and departing September 25 for a single day of White House meetings, and reporting from August 18 says he will skip the UN General Assembly in New York and limit the trip to the Trump meeting. A sanctions architecture that names Chinese entities in late August will be on the table in the Oval Office four weeks later.\n\nTAIWAN WAS QUIET. Taiwan's annual Han Kuang exercises ran in early August through August 14, structured around resilience and dispersal rather than platform display, with analysts reading the design as closer alignment with US military practice. No new PLA exercise activity around the island was reported in this cycle.",
   "details": "VOLUMES: Chinese imports of Iranian crude ~534,000 bpd so far in August 2026 against ~1.4m bpd average in 2025; China takes the large majority of Iran's exported crude (estimates 80-90%+); Iran supplies roughly 13% of China's oil imports on Kpler estimates. The reduction has been achieved by naval interdiction ahead of the announced sanctions package. BEIJING'S POSITION: Lin Jian — 'sanctions and pressure tactics are not the solution'; measures do not 'fit the interest of any party'; standing opposition to unilateral sanctions lacking international-law basis and UNSC mandate. MOFCOM has invoked the 2021 sanctions-blocking mechanism directing Chinese firms not to comply with US designations of refineries importing Iranian crude — the first explicit such order. OFAC-designated refiners since March 2025 include Shandong Jincheng Petrochemical, Hebei Xinhai Chemical, Shandong Shengxing Chemical, Shandong Shouguang Luqing Petrochemical and Hengli Petrochemical Refining. Liu Pengyu (Chinese embassy): 'The normal economic and trade cooperation conducted by Chinese enterprises with relevant countries should not be subject to interference or disruption.' US POSITION: Bessent — third countries are 'either with us or against us'; 'any remaining tie to Tehran will hasten a nation's economic oblivion'; August 24 news conference expected to centre on secondary sanctions. CALENDAR: Xi Jinping due in Washington around September 24, arriving September 23 and departing September 25, one day of White House meetings; reported August 18 to be skipping the UN General Assembly and limiting the trip to the Trump meeting. TAIWAN: Han Kuang exercises ran early August through August 14, emphasising resilience and dispersal over platform display; analysts read closer alignment with US military practice. No new PLA exercise activity reported this cycle.",
   "prediction_impact": "Corpus predictions that China would defy US secondary sanctions over Iranian oil are CONFIRMED as to declared intent — Beijing has issued a formal blocking-rule order — but the volume data complicates the picture in a way the predictions did not anticipate: Chinese purchases have already fallen by roughly 60% year-on-year, and the cause is a naval blockade rather than a legal instrument. Predictions that economic coercion would fracture the US-China relationship before any military confrontation are SUPPORTED. Predictions of a US-China military confrontation over Taiwan in 2026 remain DISCONFIRMED for the period to date. The September 24 Washington summit is the scheduled test: predictions that sanctions enforcement and summit diplomacy cannot coexist become falsifiable within four weeks."
  },
