   "source": "The National, 'France's Macron and Saudi Crown Prince to discuss oil pipelines to by-pass Strait of Hormuz,' August 23, 2026; Bloomberg, 'Macron and MBS to Focus on Hormuz, Deals in Paris Talks,' August 21, 2026; CSIS, 'The Hormuz Energy Crisis Generates a Tailwind for Nuclear Energy in Northeast Asia,' 2026; The Conversation, 'Some countries in Asia are rationing energy — why they've been hit hardest by the crisis in the Gulf,' 2026; Zero Carbon Analytics, 'Asian countries most at risk from oil and gas supply disruptions in Strait of Hormuz,' 2026; IMPRI, 'India's Response To The Strait Of Hormuz Crisis,' 2026; AAA Fuel Prices national average, August 23, 2026; Transport Topics, 'Diesel, gasoline prices hit record August highs,' August 2026; straits.live status board, 23 August 2026 2300Z.",
   "summary": "THE ADAPTATION HAS PASSED FROM EMERGENCY MEASURE TO INSTITUTIONAL DESIGN, WHICH IS WHAT A CHOKEPOINT CLOSURE LOOKS LIKE WHEN IT STOPS BEING TREATED AS TEMPORARY.\n\nFrance and Saudi Arabia have established a joint task force focused on energy and logistics connections between the Middle East and Europe, meeting at ministerial level on August 24, alongside the Macron-MBS summit whose principal file is Hormuz substitution: increased trade through Omani ports outside the Persian Gulf, expanded and potentially doubled pipeline capacity in Saudi Arabia and other states, and new regional rail links. A standing bilateral body with a ministerial cadence is a different thing from a chartered vessel or a rerouted cargo. It is a commitment of capital on the assumption that the Strait will not be reliable for years.\n\nIRAN HAS RESPONDED BY PUTTING THE ALTERNATIVES ON ITS TARGET LIST. Mohsen Rezaei said on August 23 that Iran would target the oil-shipping routes out of the Persian Gulf that serve as alternatives to the Strait if regional states join the economic war, and warned via IRNA that Tehran would not allow 'a single drop of oil' to leave the Gulf if pressure escalates. The bypass is therefore being built and threatened in the same week.\n\nTHE DEMAND SIDE HAS SETTLED INTO MANAGED SCARCITY. Asian energy prices since March have run roughly 50-100% above February 2026 levels. South Korea and Japan face critical LNG deficits from UAE and Qatari supply; South Korea has run licence-plate rationing and has sent a first trial container ship through the Arctic route; Bangladesh, Thailand and Vietnam ration energy; Indonesia caps purchases at 50 litres and Sri Lanka at 15, with Sri Lanka also on a shortened working week; Pakistan restricts business hours; India has imposed work-from-home and travel restrictions. India is the notable outlier on price: it avoided formal fuel rationing altogether and retail fuel prices have risen by only about 7% over the crisis period against a global average of 25-30%.\n\nTHE AMERICAN CONSUMER IS PAYING ON A LAG AND THE FREIGHT SIDE IS PAYING MORE. The AAA national average for regular gasoline stood at $4.0986 on August 23, ninety cents above a year earlier. Diesel reached $5.6074, a record August level, $1.92 above the year-earlier $3.6893 and 40 cents above the level of one month ago — a steeper and more persistent climb than gasoline, and the one that transmits into goods prices rather than into household sentiment.\n\nAND THE CRISIS IS CHANGING GENERATION POLICY, NOT ONLY PROCUREMENT. CSIS records that the Hormuz energy crisis has generated a tailwind for nuclear energy across Northeast Asia — the clearest sign yet that the importing economies are treating the chokepoint's unreliability as a structural planning assumption rather than a transient shock.",
   "details": "INSTITUTIONS: France-Saudi joint task force on Middle East-Europe energy and logistics connections, meeting at ministerial level August 24; Macron-MBS Paris summit the same day with Hormuz alternatives as the principal file — Omani ports outside the Gulf, expanded and potentially doubled pipeline capacity, new regional rail links. IRANIAN THREAT: Rezaei, August 23 — Iran would target Persian Gulf oil-shipping routes serving as alternatives to the Strait if neighbours join the economic war; via IRNA, no 'single drop of oil' would leave the Gulf if pressure escalates (reported, unconfirmed). ASIA: energy prices roughly 50-100% above February 2026 since March. South Korea and Japan — critical LNG deficits from UAE and Qatari supply; South Korea licence-plate rationing and a first trial container ship via the Arctic route. Bangladesh, Thailand, Vietnam — energy rationing. Indonesia — 50-litre caps. Sri Lanka — 15-litre caps and a shortened working week. Pakistan — restricted business hours. India — work-from-home and travel restrictions, but no formal fuel rationing and retail fuel prices up only ~7% against a global average of 25-30%. UNITED STATES: AAA national average regular $4.0986 on August 23 (year ago $3.1497). Diesel $5.6074 (week ago $5.4460; month ago $5.2089; year ago $3.6893) — a record August level. STRUCTURAL: CSIS records the Hormuz energy crisis generating a tailwind for nuclear energy in Northeast Asia. Six EU states have urged a windfall tax on oil companies profiting from the crisis. WAR RISK: ~$10.0m per VLCC transit, 40× pre-crisis; six P&I clubs withdrawn.",
   "prediction_impact": "Corpus predictions that a Hormuz closure would produce cascading energy crises across import-dependent Asia are CONFIRMED, and the rationing regimes have now persisted long enough to count as policy rather than as emergency response. Predictions that the disruption would prove temporary are DISCONFIRMED: standing bilateral institutions, capital commitments to pipelines and rail, and a generation-policy shift toward nuclear in Northeast Asia are all decisions taken on the assumption of multi-year unreliability. Predictions that substitution would be straightforward are QUALIFIED by Rezaei's threat against the alternative routes. India's outcome — no formal rationing and a ~7% retail rise against a 25-30% global average — is a DISCONFIRMATION of corpus predictions that treated import dependence as uniformly determinative of domestic price outcomes; policy and sourcing diversification changed the result."
  },
