   "source": "UPI, 'Trump says Strait of Hormuz is ‘an American territory’,' August 22, 2026; CNN Politics, 'Trump: ‘I view the Strait of Hormuz as an American territory’,' August 22, 2026; Fox News video, 'Trump again says he views Strait of Hormuz as an ‘American territory’,' August 22, 2026; CNN live updates, 'US-Iran war latest: Iranian military leader vows Tehran ‘will not submit’ as US prepares further economic squeeze,' August 22, 2026; Al Jazeera live blog, 'Iran war live: Tehran says US sanctions ‘declaration of war’ on all nations,' August 22, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Iran says new US sanctions violate sovereignty of other states,' August 22, 2026; CNBC, 'Ahead of tough new U.S. sanctions, Iran criticizes ‘extraterritorial sovereignty’,' August 22, 2026; Middle East Eye, 'US sanctions ‘declaration of war on all nations’, Iran says,' August 22, 2026; France 24 live blog, 'Iran warns states joining US sanctions will be considered enemies,' August 22, 2026; The Times of Israel, 'Iran says any country joining new US sanctions would be ‘considered an enemy’,' August 22, 2026; Iran International live blog, August 22, 2026 (Rezaei on the Strait, the Mecca agreement and ‘earthquake-like’ retaliation; Rosatom at Bushehr; Truth Social territory map; UK power-plant cyber incident); Reuters via Investing.com, 'Iran grants permission for a number of Iraqi oil tankers to pass through Hormuz,' August 22, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Iran grants permission for some Iraqi oil tankers to pass through Hormuz,' August 22, 2026; The National, 'Iran gives permission for Iraqi oil tankers to pass through Strait of Hormuz,' August 22, 2026; Oman Observer, 'Iran allowed Iraqi tankers to pass through Hormuz,' August 22, 2026; CBS News live updates, 'Iran declares it has won the war as U.S. turns to economic warfare,' August 21-22, 2026; U.S. Central Command via X, blockade tally as of August 21, 2026; ANI, 'US forces redirect 68 commercial vessels, disable 3, board 2 amid Iran blockade: CENTCOM,' August 22, 2026; Middle East Eye, 'Centcom says 68 vessels redirected so far in Iran naval blockade,' August 22, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Are ships passing Hormuz more willing to defy Iran or US? What data shows,' August 20, 2026; straits.live Strait of Hormuz status board, Day 175, 22 August 2026 2256Z; Eastern Herald, 'Oil Prices Today, August 22, 2026: Brent Near $94 and Frozen Until Monday'; TradingEconomics Brent crude series, August 21-22, 2026; AAA Fuel Prices national average, August 22, 2026; The National, 'Bessent vows to isolate Iran’s economy as US increases pressure on allies,' August 20, 2026; CNBC, 'Bessent says U.S. likely won’t restart large-scale Iran combat as it steps up economic pressure,' August 20, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'US Treasury secretary says new economic measures will ‘collapse’ Iran,' August 20, 2026; Fortune, 'What Bessent’s economic isolation of Iran could look like,' August 15, 2026; Wikipedia, '2026 Strait of Hormuz crisis' (vessel-attack chronology, Persian Gulf Strait Authority), retrieved August 23, 2026; Seatrade Maritime, 'Seafarer killed in Hormuz cargo ship attack,' August 2026; Marine Insight, 'Third Engineer Goes Missing After Strike On Bulker In Strait Of Hormuz Leads To Blackout & Fire,' August 2026; Al Jazeera, 'UAE accuses Iran of attacks on two ADNOC vessels in Strait of Hormuz,' August 14, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'UAE says Iran targeted ADNOC tanker in Strait of Hormuz, no casualties,' August 8, 2026; Middle East Eye, 'Oil tanker loading resumes at Iran’s Kharg Island, TankerTrackers says,' August 2026; IndexBox, 'Iran resumes crude loading at Kharg Island after 25-day halt,' August 2026; Euronews Business, 'Iran’s inflation spiral deepens as rial slides and tensions rise,' 2026; The Washington Post, 'More than 750 U.S. service members wounded in Iran war,' August 21, 2026.",
   "summary": "THE CYCLE'S DEFINING FACT IS A COLLISION OF TWO CLAIMS TO THE SAME WATER, MADE ON THE SAME DAY, NEITHER OF THEM BACKED BY A LANDING. On August 22 President Trump told supporters in South Carolina: 'We don't even know if we won, because I view the Strait of Hormuz as an American territory right now.' He re-posted on Truth Social the image he had first published on August 18 showing the waterway labelled 'NEW U.S. TERRITORY.' On the same day Iran's state news agency IRNA announced that Tehran had granted a number of Iraqi oil tankers permission to transit the Strait, after repeated requests from Baghdad through several channels including parliament speaker Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf's visit to Iraq. It is the first named state-level transit exemption of the war. Iraq, which produced roughly four million barrels a day before the conflict, has no national tanker fleet and depends on foreign hulls; it is simultaneously developing alternatives through Ceyhan in Turkey, Baniyas in Syria and Aqaba in Jordan. Iraqi President Nizar Amidi said 'everyone is keen to prevent Iraq from being dragged into the ongoing war in the region' and that Iraqi territory 'will not be used to launch attacks against any other country.' An American claim of sovereignty over the water and an Iranian grant of passage through it were issued within hours of each other, and the audit records both without adjudicating between them.\n\nTHE STRIKE PAUSE HELD FOR A TWENTY-SECOND NIGHT. No US or Israeli strike inside Iranian territory has been confirmed since the President cancelled the planned multi-day energy campaign late on August 1. The pause is now three weeks old and has outlasted the expiry of the negotiating window it was meant to protect.\n\nTEHRAN ANSWERED THE COMING SANCTIONS WITH ITS BROADEST THREAT OF THE WAR, AIMED NOT AT WASHINGTON BUT AT THIRD PARTIES. Foreign ministry spokesman Esmaeil Baghaei said the package due on Monday amounts to a 'declaration of war' on all countries and goes beyond 'an illegal economic war against a single nation,' describing it as 'an attempt by the United States to exert extraterritorial sovereignty over all independent UN member states.' Combining sanctions with a naval blockade, he said, would end in 'the complete erosion of sovereignty as the foundational basis of the UN-based inter-State system, and a recipe for an abysmal return to full-scale classic colonialism.' Mohsen Rezaei, secretary of the Supreme National Security Council, went further on state television: 'Any country participating in the imposition of economic restrictions against us is considered an enemy.' States that will not 'distance themselves' from Washington will have their interests 'harmed,' he said, naming the disruption of Gulf oil shipments as the instrument. He also said 'the Strait of Hormuz is closed and will not reopen' unless American behaviour changes, that 'if Trump wants to take action, we will retaliate in an earthquake-like manner,' and — a notable limiting clause — that Iran had 'not yet attacked any American economic interests.' A close ally of the Supreme Leader said Trump's rhetoric came from 'desperation' and vowed Tehran 'will not submit.'\n\nWASHINGTON'S SIDE OF THE LEDGER STAYED FINANCIAL. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent's news conference detailing the package is set for 2 p.m. ET on Monday August 24, after his pledge of 'the toughest sanctions in history,' 'the greatest coordinated economic isolation in the history of the world,' and a 'one-two punch' of blockade plus designations that 'is going to work in Iran and we are going to collapse this regime.' He told third countries they are 'either with us or against us' and that 'any remaining tie to Tehran will hasten a nation's economic oblivion, whether that tie be purposefully constructed or willfully ignored.' Bessent has also said the United States likely would not restart large-scale combat operations. Trump, asked about a deal on August 22, said: 'Iran would love to make a deal, but they're not ready to make the right deal in my opinion.'\n\nTHE TRAFFIC DATA NOW CUTS AGAINST THE AMERICAN CLAIM RATHER THAN FOR IT. Al Jazeera's August 20 analysis of the period August 1-19 found 236 vessels of all cargo types crossing the Strait: 83 openly used Iran's prescribed northern route, three openly used the US-backed Omani route, two used the pre-war central route and 148 — 62% — went dark or unclassified. Of 112 oil and gas vessels, 21 openly took the Iranian route, two the Omani route and 89 — more than 80% — went dark. Al Jazeera's conclusion: 'the fear of offending Tehran appears to outweigh Trump's threats,' and Iran 'still retains significant capacity' to influence traffic. Iran International reported on August 22 that nearly 200 ships transited in the past week, up from about 150 the week before, with over 80% using Omani routes or going dark. The straits.live board at 2256Z on August 22 carried Day 175, one transit on August 16 as PortWatch's most recent published day against a 73-per-day pre-crisis baseline, 331 hulls holding position away from berth on a seven-day range of 201-530, war-risk cover at 40 times pre-crisis levels or roughly $10.0 million to insure a single VLCC, and market-implied odds of a reopening by September 1 below 1%. UKMTO data cited by CBS put the week ending August 7 at 21 inbound and 18 outbound transits, roughly 4% of pre-conflict levels, and UKMTO still assesses the risk to mariners as severe.\n\nTHE BLOCKADE TIGHTENED BY ONE HULL. CENTCOM said that as of August 21 US forces had redirected 68 commercial vessels, disabled three and boarded two — up from 67 as of August 20 — and published imagery of an MH-60R Sea Hawk landing aboard USS Ross (DDG-71), described as one of more than 20 warships supporting the blockade.\n\nTHE SHOOTING THAT IS STILL HAPPENING IS HAPPENING TO MERCHANT CREWS, AND THIS BRIEFING HAD NOT NAMED IT. The August record inside the Strait includes the bulk carrier Minoan Pioneer, struck by an unidentified projectile in her engine room about 20 nautical miles northeast of Khasab late on August 3, causing a complete blackout and a fire in the accommodation block, with the third engineer reported missing and no claim of responsibility; an ADNOC tanker hit by a missile on August 8, which Abu Dhabi called an act of piracy by the IRGC; the ADNOC vessels Navig8 Messi and Tarif struck by drones on August 13; and the Greek-owned, Liberian-flagged bulk carrier Minoan Dignity attacked on August 17-18, killing her chief engineer. ADNOC says 16 of its vessels have been attacked since February 28, with one crew member killed and 20 injured. These are civilian maritime casualties inside the chokepoint, distinct from the Bab al-Mandeb deaths already logged.\n\nKHARG WAS THROTTLED, THEN PARTLY RESTARTED — AND STILL NOT TAKEN. TankerTrackers recorded a National Iranian Tanker Company VLCC loading about two million barrels at the Azarpad jetty on August 14, the first loading at the island since late July, after the western terminal sat empty for 22 consecutive days from July 18, the LPG terminal for 13 days from July 27 and the eastern terminal for 10 days from July 30. This refines the prior cycle's log of a dark VLCC berthing on August 12: loading resumed in the August 12-14 window after a roughly 25-day halt. No US naval or ground move against the island accompanied it. The denial of Iran's principal export outlet continues to be produced by blockade and insurance rather than by seizure.\n\nTWO SECOND-ORDER FACTS WORTH LOGGING. Iran International reported on August 22 that Russia's Rosatom has returned 42 specialists to the Bushehr nuclear plant with staffing planned to rise to 100 by autumn, and that Iran-linked hackers shut down a small British power plant for four days in what officials described as a first-of-its-kind attack; the cyber report is single-sourced and is recorded here as reported rather than as established.\n\nAND TEHRAN CONTINUED TO NARRATE THE WAR AS WON. President Masoud Pezeshkian said the world had 'approved' Iran's victory and that America was 'hated around the world,' repeating that 'it is better to end the war today, while we are strong and honored.' Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi dismissed the sanctions threat as 'same bull, different bullies.' Ghalibaf said the United States and Israel had recognised they could not overcome Iran through conventional military warfare and had turned to 'cognitive and economic warfare,' and that neighbouring states were seeking to 'reshape new security arrangements' with Tehran. Iran finalised a preferential trade agreement with Oman, pending parliamentary approval, and Iranian officials floated expanded Omani port trade, new pipelines and rail links to bypass the Strait — a set of proposals that, if realised, would reduce the value of the chokepoint Tehran is currently holding shut.",
   "details": "STRIKE PAUSE: no confirmed US or Israeli strike inside Iran since August 1 — a twenty-second consecutive night. BLOCKADE: CENTCOM 68 vessels redirected / 3 disabled / 2 boarded as of August 21 (67 on August 20; 64 on August 17; 44 on August 3; 35 on August 2); more than 20 warships supporting, USS Ross (DDG-71) cited. HORMUZ ROUTE SHARES (Al Jazeera, August 1-19): 236 vessels total — 83 openly Iranian route, 3 openly Omani route, 2 pre-war central route, 148 dark (62%); oil and gas 112 total — 21 Iranian, 2 Omani, 89 dark (>80%). WEEKLY VOLUME: ~200 transits last week vs ~150 the week before (Iran International, August 22); 21 inbound / 18 outbound week ending August 7 and ~4% of pre-conflict levels (UKMTO via CBS). DAILY: 1 transit on August 16 (PortWatch) vs 73/day baseline. HOLDING: 331 hulls off berth at 2256Z August 22, seven-day range 201-530 (straits.live Day 175). WAR RISK: 40× pre-crisis, ~$10.0m per VLCC transit; reopening by September 1 priced below 1%. OIL: Brent futures $93.93 on August 22 (previous close $93.78; range $92.76-$94.82); straits.live carried $94.39 with markets closed; second consecutive weekly gain of roughly 6%. PUMP: AAA national average $4.1024 on August 22. US CASUALTIES: 774-775 total — 18 killed and 756-757 wounded depending on outlet, after a single-day addition of 59-60 on August 21; Epic Fury 14 killed / 418 wounded since February 28, overseas operations since July 7 four killed / 339 wounded. MERCHANT CASUALTIES IN HORMUZ (AUGUST): Minoan Pioneer third engineer missing (August 3); ADNOC tanker missile strike (August 8); Navig8 Messi and Tarif drone strikes (August 13); Minoan Dignity chief engineer killed (August 17-18); ADNOC cumulative 16 vessels attacked since February 28, one killed and 20 injured. KHARG: NITC VLCC loaded ~2m bbl at Azarpad on August 14, first since late July, after a ~25-day halt; western terminal empty 22 days from July 18, LPG 13 days from July 27, eastern 10 days from July 30. IRAN ECONOMY: IMF projects 2026 inflation averaging about 68.9% and a real GDP contraction in the 5.4%-6.1% range depending on vintage; rial around 1.32 million to the dollar; official minimum monthly wage covers roughly 37% of a basic living basket. SANCTIONS: Bessent news conference 2 p.m. ET Monday August 24.",
   "prediction_impact": "The corpus-predicted US GROUND seizure of Hormuz or Kharg Island remains DISCONFIRMED at Day 178. The cycle adds a specific and unusual data point: the President asserted a territorial claim over the Strait — 'I view the Strait of Hormuz as an American territory right now' — on the same day that Iran exercised a sovereign allocation of transit rights by granting Iraqi tankers passage. Predictions that American control of the chokepoint would be established by force of arms are DISCONFIRMED in their execution and only rhetorically satisfied: the claim is being made verbally and by naval interdiction, not by occupation, and the traffic data cuts against it. Al Jazeera's route analysis is the strongest open-source evidence yet AGAINST the proposition that Washington controls the waterway — 83 hulls openly took Iran's route in nineteen days against three that openly took the US-backed Omani route — and correspondingly WEAKENS corpus claims that American naval supremacy translates directly into commercial control. Predictions of a negotiated Hormuz reopening remain LIVE BUT WEAKENING: Rezaei stated flatly that the Strait 'is closed and will not reopen' absent a change in US behaviour, and markets price reopening by September 1 below 1%. Predictions that economic coercion would fracture Iran's position are UNTESTED PENDING the August 24 package, but Tehran's counter-move is now visible and is aimed at third parties rather than at the United States: Rezaei's declaration that any participating country is 'considered an enemy,' with Gulf oil shipments named as the retaliation vector. Predictions that Iran would be isolated from its neighbours are COMPLICATED rather than confirmed — Iran granted Iraq an exemption, finalised a preferential trade agreement with Oman, and its parliament speaker describes neighbours as seeking new security arrangements with Tehran, while the UAE has suspended all trade. Corpus predictions of escalation-by-ratchet continue to be DISCONFIRMED for a twenty-second consecutive night without a strike inside Iran.",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "Iran's grant of transit to Iraqi tankers is read as proof that Tehran, not Washington, allocates passage through Hormuz — and, by the opposing reading, as a narrow client-state favour with no bearing on control, since the traffic that matters is either dark or stopped by the US blockade.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented: IRNA announced on August 22 that Iran granted a number of Iraqi oil tankers permission to transit after repeated Baghdad requests, including during Ghalibaf's visit to Iraq. Documented: on the same day Trump said 'I view the Strait of Hormuz as an American territory right now' and re-posted a map labelling it 'NEW U.S. TERRITORY.' Documented: Al Jazeera's analysis of August 1-19 found 83 of 236 hulls openly using Iran's route against three openly using the Omani route, with 148 dark. Documented: CENTCOM has redirected 68 commercial vessels, disabled three and boarded two, with more than 20 warships enforcing. Not documented: whether any Iraqi tanker has actually completed a transit under the grant, whether the US blockade would interdict one, or what fraction of the dark 148 moved with Iranian permission, without it, or in defiance of both parties.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "Iran's IRNA and parliament speaker Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf, presenting the grant as an exercise of Iranian authority over transit",
      "President Trump and CENTCOM, asserting American control of the Strait by blockade and now by territorial claim",
      "Al Jazeera's August 20 route analysis, concluding that 'the fear of offending Tehran appears to outweigh Trump's threats' and that Iran 'still retains significant capacity' to influence traffic",
      "Analysts quoted by Al Jazeera arguing Iran favours Iraq because it has 'always stood up to the United States' while punishing Gulf states hosting US bases"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "A permission granted and a permission that would have been unnecessary look identical from outside. No public dataset separates hulls that moved with Iranian consent from hulls that moved despite it or that were stopped by American interdiction, and 62% of August traffic is dark by definition. Resolution would require either a documented US interdiction of an Iranian-permitted Iraqi cargo, or underwriters' own attribution of why the other hulls are not sailing.",
     "open_question_ref": "hormuz-control-attribution-august-2026"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-08-23",
   "headline": "DAY 178 GROUND-INVASION TRACKER — STATUS HOLDS no_ground_troops FOR A 178TH CONSECUTIVE DAY, AND THIS IS THE CYCLE IN WHICH THE AMERICAN CLAIM ON THE STRAIT BECAME EXPLICITLY TERRITORIAL WHILE REMAINING ENTIRELY UNOCCUPIED. (1) TRUMP SAID ON AUGUST 22 'I VIEW THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ AS AN AMERICAN TERRITORY RIGHT NOW' AND RE-POSTED A MAP LABELLING IT 'NEW U.S. TERRITORY' — A SOVEREIGNTY CLAIM OVER WATER, ASSERTED WITH ZERO US GROUND FORCES ON IRANIAN SOIL. (2) EVERY EXECUTION INDICATOR REMAINED AT ZERO: NO LANDING, NO EMBARKATION, NO MEU TASKING, NO AIRBORNE INSERTION, NO RELIEF ARG, NO NEW DEPLOYMENT ORDER, NO MOBILISATION, NO DRAFT ORDER. (3) THE AMPHIBIOUS PICTURE IS NOW STRUCTURALLY THIN IN A WAY WORTH NAMING: THE BOXER ARG WITH THE 11TH MEU REMAINS THE ONLY DEPLOYED ARG IN CENTCOM, THE NAVY'S OTHER DEPLOYED ARG — IWO JIMA WITH THE 22ND MEU — IS COMMITTED TO THE CARIBBEAN UNDER SOUTHCOM, AND THE 24TH MEU IS DEPLOYED WITHOUT AN ARG AT ALL AS 'LITTORAL COMBAT FORCE 24,' A DISTRIBUTED CONSTRUCT THAT IS THE OPPOSITE OF ASSAULT MASSING. (4) THE ONE GENUINE GROUND-PLANNING INDICATOR IN THE RECORD IS THE JULY 26 NEW YORK POST REPORT OF A URANIUM-SEIZURE OPERATION REQUIRING 'THOUSANDS OF GROUND TROOPS' — SINGLE-SOURCED, UNCONFIRMED, UNDECIDED, AND THE SAME CONCEPT TRUMP PUBLICLY DECLINED IN JUNE. (5) THE DRAFT RUNG MOVED ON PAPER AND ONLY ON PAPER: A DEFENSE AUTHORIZATION ACT PROVISION AUTOMATING SELECTIVE SERVICE REGISTRATION IS REPORTED TO TAKE EFFECT DECEMBER 18, 2026, WHICH BUILDS THE POOL AND DOES NOT INDUCT ANYONE. (6) THE MECCA JOINT DEFENCE AGREEMENT OF AUGUST 7 BINDS THE THREE STATES A US GROUND CAMPAIGN WOULD MOST NEED — SAUDI ARABIA, TÜRKIYE AND PAKISTAN — INTO A MUTUAL-DEFENCE PACT OF THEIR OWN.",
   "theatre": "Iran / Strait of Hormuz",
   "tag": "Ground Invasion Tracker",
   "tags": [
    "Ground Invasion Tracker",
    "no_ground_troops",
    "Day 178",
    "American Territory Claim",
    "Zero Execution Indicators",
