   "source": "The Conversation, 'Some countries in Asia are rationing energy – why they’ve been hit hardest by the crisis in the Gulf,' 2026; Council on Foreign Relations, 'The Iran War Turned Asia’s Fragile Energy Dependence Into an Emergency,' 2026; Gulf International Forum, 'Hormuz Disruptions and Asia’s Energy Resilience,' 2026; ORF Middle East, 'Asia’s Energy Stress: Regional Impact of Hormuz Crisis,' 2026; Fox Business, 'Iran war squeezes Asia energy supply as India, Japan feel strain,' 2026; CBS News live updates, 'Iran declares it has won the war as U.S. turns to economic warfare,' August 21-22, 2026; straits.live Strait of Hormuz status board, Day 175, 22 August 2026 2256Z; Al Jazeera, 'Are ships passing Hormuz more willing to defy Iran or US? What data shows,' August 20, 2026; AAA Fuel Prices national average, August 22, 2026; World Bank Lebanon Economic Monitor, August 2026.",
   "summary": "THE IMPORTANT CHANGE IN THE DEPENDENT ECONOMIES IS NO LONGER THE DEPTH OF THE SHORTAGE BUT THE FACT THAT CAPITAL IS NOW BEING SPENT ON ROUTES THAT ASSUME THE STRAIT STAYS SHUT. South Korea has sent its first trial container ship through the Arctic route to avoid the Middle East entirely — a voyage that only makes commercial sense if the operator expects the disruption to outlast a season. On Monday August 24 Saudi Arabia's crown prince and the French president will convene the Franco-Saudi Strategic Partnership Council in Paris with an agenda devoted to diversifying transport routes for energy and logistics, including alternative maritime routes, pipeline projects and regional rail links between the Middle East and Europe. And Iran itself is floating expanded Omani port trade, new pipelines and new rail links to bypass Hormuz — which means the state currently holding the chokepoint closed is investing in reducing the value of the asset it is holding. Three parties with opposed interests are building the same workaround.\n\nTHE DEMAND-SIDE MEASURES HAVE HARDENED FROM EMERGENCY RESPONSE INTO STANDING POLICY. South Korea is operating licence-plate vehicle rationing, restricting use to alternate days. Bangladesh, South Korea, Thailand and Vietnam have imposed energy rationing. Indonesia is capping fuel purchases at 50 litres a day and Sri Lanka at 15, with Sri Lanka also running shorter working weeks. Pakistan has restricted business hours, Malaysia is encouraging limits on cooling temperatures, Bangladesh is promoting public transport, and India is encouraging work from home and restricting foreign travel. India — which imports nearly 90% of its crude and about half its natural gas, and is the world's third-largest oil importer and consumer — is additionally managing an LPG supply crisis caused directly by the disruption. Japan and South Korea continue drawing on joint emergency stockpiles.\n\nTHE PRICE SIGNAL REMAINS ELEVATED AND POLITICALLY LIVE. Brent traded near $94 on August 22 for a second consecutive weekly gain of roughly 6%, and the AAA national average US pump price stood at $4.1024, close to a dollar above the year-earlier level. Six EU member states have urged a windfall tax on oil companies profiting from the crisis. Beyond Asia, the World Bank projects Lebanon's economy will contract 6.4% with inflation reaching 17.5%.\n\nAND THE TRAFFIC ITSELF DID NOT RECOVER. UKMTO recorded 21 inbound and 18 outbound transits in the week ending August 7 — about 4% of pre-conflict levels — and still assesses the risk to mariners as severe. PortWatch's most recent published day, August 16, logged one transit against a 73-per-day pre-crisis baseline. straits.live counted 331 hulls holding position away from berth at 2256Z on August 22 and prices war-risk cover at 40 times pre-crisis levels, roughly $10 million for a single VLCC passage, with the market-implied probability of a reopening by September 1 below 1%.",
   "details": "BYPASS BUILDING: South Korea's first trial container ship via the Arctic route; Franco-Saudi Strategic Partnership Council in Paris on August 24 with an agenda on maritime diversification, pipelines and regional rail; Iranian proposals for expanded Omani port trade, new pipelines and rail links to bypass Hormuz. RATIONING: South Korea licence-plate vehicle rationing; energy rationing in Bangladesh, South Korea, Thailand and Vietnam; Indonesia 50 l/day and Sri Lanka 15 l/day fuel caps; shorter working weeks in Sri Lanka; restricted business hours in Pakistan; cooling-temperature limits encouraged in Malaysia; public-transport promotion in Bangladesh; work-from-home and foreign-travel restrictions in India; Japan-South Korea joint emergency stockpile drawdown. INDIA: imports ~90% of crude and ~half its natural gas; third-largest oil importer and consumer; LPG supply crisis. PRICES: Brent ~$94 on August 22, second consecutive weekly gain of ~6%; AAA US national average $4.1024 on August 22, nearly $1 above a year earlier. EU: six member states urge a windfall tax on oil companies profiting from the crisis. LEBANON: World Bank projects −6.4% GDP and 17.5% inflation. TRAFFIC: 21 inbound / 18 outbound in the week ending August 7 (UKMTO), ~4% of pre-conflict levels, mariner risk assessed severe; 1 transit on August 16 (PortWatch) vs 73/day baseline; 331 hulls holding off berth at 2256Z August 22; war-risk 40× pre-crisis (~$10m per VLCC); reopening by September 1 priced below 1%.",
   "prediction_impact": "Corpus predictions of a global energy shock following a Hormuz closure are CONFIRMED in the affected states and CONTINUE TO BE OVERSTATED in magnitude: Brent near $94 is roughly 30% above the pre-war baseline and well below the $126 April wartime peak and the $200-plus several corpus lectures projected for a sustained closure. Predictions that importing states would be forced into demand rationing are CONFIRMED across at least nine countries. Predictions that the disruption would prove temporary are DISCONFIRMED by the behaviour rather than the rhetoric: an Arctic trial voyage, a Franco-Saudi summit devoted to alternative routes, and Iranian pipeline and rail proposals are all capital commitments that price in a long closure. Predictions that the closure would fracture the importing bloc into competing bilateral deals are PARTIALLY CONFIRMED — Japan and South Korea are pooling stockpiles while Saudi Arabia negotiates bypass routes with France and Iran negotiates trade terms with Oman."
  },
