   "source": "Axios, 'Trump cancels Iran attack, citing progress in negotiations,' August 2, 2026; NPR, August 2, 2026; GlobalSecurity.org, 'Iran War 2026 — Day 173 Update,' August 19, 2026; CNBC, 'Bessent says U.S. likely won't restart large-scale Iran combat as it steps up economic pressure,' August 20, 2026; CNBC, 'JD Vance says economic pressure is the best way to achieve Washington's objectives in Iran,' August 21, 2026; USNI News Fleet and Marine Tracker, August 3, August 10 and August 17, 2026; USNI News, 'Carrier USS George Washington Now in the Middle East,' August 20, 2026; Stars and Stripes, 'USS George Washington arrives in Middle East to relieve USS Abraham Lincoln,' August 20, 2026; U.S. Pacific Fleet, 'Tripoli ARG, 31st MEU return from U.S. 5th, 7th Fleet deployment,' July 2026; DefenseScoop and U.S. Central Command, 'CENTCOM Launches First-Ever Multinational Attack Drone Task Force,' August 13, 2026; Military Times, 'Selective Service System wants to wargame the draft,' July 24, 2026; Washington Technology, 'Selective Service System seeks software to aid the draft,' July 2026; The National, 'Iran reroutes oil flow as loading resumes at Kharg Island terminal,' August 16, 2026; Anadolu Agency, 'US ramps up deployments to CENTCOM despite ongoing talks with Iran,' 2026; INSS, 'Interactive Live Map: U.S. Forces Posture in the Middle East (CENTCOM),' August 2026; Defense News and RTX, Tomahawk contract, August 17, 2026; Stars and Stripes, 'Iran offers up to $60K bounty on US troops,' August 17, 2026; Council on Foreign Relations Global Conflict Tracker.",
   "summary": "STATUS: no_ground_troops, DAY 177. No US ground force has entered Iranian territory at any point in this war, and this cycle produced the strongest evidence yet that none is intended in the near term — not from accumulated absence, but from a chain of positive, on-record decisions.\n\nTHE LADDER WAS DISMANTLED FROM THE TOP. The rung the corpus treats as immediately preceding a ground phase is a large-scale campaign against Iranian infrastructure. That campaign was cancelled by the President on August 1 — 'I am canceling the attack' — with the cancellation conditioned on rapid movement toward a deal covering the Strait and the nuclear programme. Through August 19 no US or Israeli strike inside Iran was confirmed by CENTCOM, a twentieth consecutive night. Twenty nights of no strikes is not a pause inside an escalation; it is the absence of the precondition.\n\nAND THE ADMINISTRATION NAMED ITS SUCCESSOR PHASE, WHICH IS NOT GROUND. On August 20 Vice President Vance said the war had entered a 'new phase' of economic pressure, calling it 'the best way to ultimately accomplish the final objective here.' Treasury Secretary Bessent promised 'the toughest sanctions in history' and 'the greatest coordinated economic isolation in the history of the world' — and, in the same interview, said the US likely would not restart large-scale combat operations. This is the first cycle of the war in which senior officials have publicly described the war's forward path in terms that exclude a major kinetic escalation, and it comes from the Treasury Secretary and Vice President rather than from anonymous planners.\n\nFORCE FLOW: A ROTATION, NOT A BUILD-UP. USS Abraham Lincoln departed the theatre after roughly nine months and 252 days without a port call, with reporting describing serious morale strain aboard. USS George Washington (CVN-73) arrived in CENTCOM on August 19 with USS Robert Smalls (CG-62) and USS Shoup (DDG-86). Net carrier presence is unchanged at one. No second strike group was ordered, no additional brigade was alerted, no new deployment order was announced.\n\nAMPHIBIOUS PICTURE UNCHANGED AND THIN. The USNI Fleet and Marine Tracker of August 17 continues to show the Boxer Amphibious Ready Group — USS Boxer (LHD-4), USS Comstock (LSD-45), USS Portland (LPD-27) — with the embarked 11th Marine Expeditionary Unit in the region, still the sole deployed Amphibious Ready Group fleet-wide. CH-53E Super Stallions are embarked aboard Portland as the MEU's routine aviation combat element, not as evidence of a lift build-up. No tasking, no embarkation, no landing rehearsal, no mine-countermeasures preparation of an approach and no repositioning toward Kharg has been reported. USS Tripoli has been home since concluding its deployment on July 22, with roughly 2,500 Marines of the 31st MEU ashore at Okinawa and no relief ARG announced — the amphibious rung has now been down for nearly a month.\n\nKHARG: THROTTLED, NOT TAKEN — AND THE SUBSTITUTION IS NOW EXPLICIT. The island was not struck in this cycle and has never been seized. Satellite imagery through early August showed its loading terminals empty with 16-17 tankers waiting offshore and no tanker loaded for a week; on August 12 a dark VLCC berthed and loaded for the first time since July 18, while the eastern oil terminal and the LPG facility stayed idle. Iran's principal export outlet is being denied by blockade, insurance and buyer risk aversion — Chinese purchases fell from 823,000 bpd in July to 534,000 bpd in August — rather than by the 1,200-plus troops a seizure would require. Trump's own framing of the mechanism on August 17 was financial and naval: 'We control it with the blockade,' a 'wall of steel.'\n\nTHE CAPABILITY INVESTMENT POINTS AWAY FROM LAND. On August 13 CENTCOM announced Task Force Falcon Strike, the first multi-domain, multinational one-way attack drone task force, building on Task Force Scorpion Strike; and on August 17 the Navy awarded Raytheon a seven-year, $22.9 billion Tomahawk contract to lift annual production above 1,000 missiles after roughly a third of the stockpile was expended. Both are investments in striking from a distance.\n\nDRAFT RUNG: QUIET. The Selective Service System's vendor window for its government-owned 'Readiness Simulation' — a model for testing staffing, processing and mobilisation across multiple draft scenarios — closed on August 14 with no award announced. The automatic-registration rule withdrawn on July 23 has still not been re-proposed. The statutory start of automatic registration for men aged 18 to 26 on December 19, 2026 is unchanged and is not a draft; conscription still requires both Congress and the President during a declared national emergency. No draft has been called, no lottery scheduled and no classification or induction machinery activated.\n\nCOUNTERVAILING, LOGGED NOT DISMISSED. The July 24 reporting that the Pentagon had readied options for weeks of limited ground operations — Kharg plus coastal raids — pending presidential approval has never been retracted. Roughly 2,000 soldiers of the 82nd Airborne Division with divisional headquarters remain in the region since late March, never committed. Hegseth's non-foreclosure on 'boots on the ground' stands unretracted and was not renewed this cycle. Iran's August 16 bounty of $30,000 — $60,000 if collected by a woman — for killing or capturing US service members presupposes American ground forces that do not exist inside Iran.",
   "details": "TROOPS: more than 50,000 US personnel in the Middle East, unchanged; 330-plus US military aircraft across Al Udeid (Qatar), Muwaffaq Salti (Jordan) and Prince Sultan (Saudi Arabia); roughly 310 logistics flights into the region since early January. NAVAL: USS George Washington (CVN-73) arrived CENTCOM August 19 with USS Robert Smalls (CG-62) and USS Shoup (DDG-86); USS Abraham Lincoln departed after 252 days without a port call; carrier presence unchanged at one. AMPHIBIOUS: Boxer ARG (LHD-4, LSD-45, LPD-27) with 11th MEU, sole deployed ARG fleet-wide as of the August 17 USNI tracker; USS Tripoli home since July 22 with the 31st MEU ashore at Okinawa; no relief ARG announced. GROUND: ~2,000 soldiers of the 82nd Airborne with divisional HQ in theatre since late March, never committed; no alert, N-hour sequence or movement toward Iranian territory at any point in the war. KHARG: not seized, not struck this cycle; terminals idle with 16-17 tankers waiting offshore, one dark VLCC loading August 12 — the first since July 18. STRIKES: none inside Iran confirmed by CENTCOM through 0500 ET August 19, twentieth consecutive night. BLOCKADE: 67 vessels redirected, 3 disabled, 2 boarded as of August 20. DRAFT: Selective Service 'Readiness Simulation' vendor submissions closed August 14, no award announced; withdrawn auto-registration rule not re-proposed; statutory automatic registration begins December 19, 2026. EXECUTION INDICATORS AT ZERO: no landing, no embarkation, no MEU tasking, no airborne insertion, no relief ARG, no new deployment order, no draft order, no mobilisation, no medical or fuel prepositioning reported for a lodgement, no special-operations ground activity inside Iran reported. PUBLIC OPINION: roughly 20% of Americans support deploying troops; net support for the war −19.6 in mid-August.",
   "prediction_impact": "The predicted US GROUND seizure of Kharg Island or the Hormuz littoral is DISCONFIRMED at Day 177 and is now further from realisation than at any point since planning was first reported on March 25, 2026. Three positive facts drive this, none of them mere absence: the President publicly cancelled the campaign that would have been its precondition; the Vice President and Treasury Secretary publicly named the war's next phase as economic, with Bessent adding that large-scale combat likely would not restart; and the theatre's two capability investments this cycle — a multinational one-way attack drone task force and a $22.9 billion Tomahawk production contract — are investments in standoff strike, which is what a force substitutes for a landing. Predictions that the blockade would prove insufficient and force a physical seizure of Kharg are DISCONFIRMED for a further cycle: the blockade is achieving export denial, with Chinese purchases of Iranian crude down from 823,000 to 534,000 bpd and terminals idle for weeks at a time. Predictions of conscription or mobilisation in the United States remain DISCONFIRMED: the Selective Service procurement window closed without an announced award, the withdrawn registration rule has not returned, and December 19, 2026 remains the next scheduled test of the registration machinery — not of a draft.",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "Whether the announced pivot to economic pressure represents an abandonment of the ground option or a sequencing of it — sanctions first, force later if they fail — is being asserted both ways.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented: Vance said the war has entered a 'new phase' of economic pressure and that this is 'the best way to ultimately accomplish the final objective'; Bessent said the US likely would not restart large-scale combat. Documented: Trump said on August 17 that he had 'no time schedule,' was not 'in a hurry,' and demanded Iran 'put up the white flag of surrender' — language of prolonged coercion rather than of settlement. Documented: the July 24 reporting of Pentagon options for weeks of limited ground operations has never been retracted, and Hegseth's non-foreclosure on 'boots on the ground' stands. Documented: roughly 2,000 soldiers of the 82nd Airborne remain in theatre. Not documented: any statement ruling out ground operations, any withdrawal of the standing plans, any change to the 82nd Airborne's tasking.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "The White House and Treasury, presenting economic isolation as the operative strategy",
      "Reporting that treats 'no time schedule' and the surrender demand as evidence of an open-ended coercive campaign with force held in reserve",
      "Foreign Policy's earlier survey of former commanders finding the US 'does not appear to be moving toward a major ground operation in Iran'"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "An abandoned option and a deferred option look identical while nothing happens. Plans that are never executed are also never formally cancelled, and no administration announces the removal of an option it wants an adversary to fear. Only execution, a formal withdrawal of forces, or an on-record renunciation would separate them.",
     "open_question_ref": "hegseth-non-foreclosure-2026"
    }
