    "War Risk Premiums 300-400%",
    "Energy Coalition"
   ],
   "source": "Table.Briefings, 'Energy crisis: How Japan and South Korea are responding differently to the Strait of Hormuz blockade,' 2026; Gulf International Forum, \"Hormuz Disruptions and Asia's Energy Resilience,\" 2026; ORF Middle East, \"Asia's Energy Stress: Regional Impact of Hormuz Crisis,\" 2026; Zero Carbon Analytics, 'Asian countries most at risk from oil and gas supply disruptions in Strait of Hormuz,' 2026; Wood Mackenzie, 'Strait of Hormuz closure threatens South Asia LNG supply,' 2026; IEEFA, \"Pakistan's LNG surplus crisis,\" 2026; Outlook Business, 'How Pakistan Went From Excess LNG to Emergency Buying in Just One Year,' 2026; Global Voices, \"Bangladesh's energy crisis worsens as US's war on Iran drags on,\" 2026; LightCastle Partners, \"Strait of Hormuz Disruption and Bangladesh's Energy Security,\" 2026; CNBC, 'The Strait of Hormuz is facing a blockade. These countries will be most impacted,' 2026; straits.live Strait of Hormuz Status briefs, August 18 and August 21, 2026.",
   "summary": "THE SECOND-ORDER COSTS OF THE STRAIT ARE NOW THE LARGER STORY, AND THEY ARE BORNE ALMOST ENTIRELY BY STATES WITH NO PART IN THE WAR. Transits are running between one and thirteen vessels a day against a pre-crisis baseline of 73, and the responses have hardened from emergency measures into standing policy.\n\nNORTHEAST ASIA. Japan imports more than 95% of its Middle Eastern crude through the waterway and South Korea more than 70% of its total imported crude; South Korea also takes 14% of its LNG from Qatar and the UAE against Japan's 6%. Seoul is running licence-plate rationing, restricting vehicle use to alternate days. Tokyo and Seoul are releasing oil from their joint stockpile to moderate prices that have run above $100 a barrel at points in the crisis.\n\nSOUTH ASIA IS WORSE, BECAUSE IT IS LNG RATHER THAN OIL. Qatar and the UAE account for 99% of Pakistan's LNG imports, 72% of Bangladesh's and 53% of India's. Pakistan has moved from an LNG surplus to record spot purchases within a year and is running an emergency gas management plan that prioritises domestic and residential supply while suspending roughly 78 million cubic feet a day to the fertiliser sector. Bangladesh was scheduled to receive 115 LNG cargoes in 2026 and expects to lose about forty; four of its five state-run urea fertiliser plants have shut for at least fifteen days, and gas to the power sector has been cut from 870 to 820 million cubic feet a day, with officials warning of increased load-shedding and possible blackouts. India is absorbing inflationary pressure on food and essential goods as energy-linked logistics costs rise.\n\nSOUTHEAST ASIA IS CAPPING CONSUMPTION DIRECTLY. Indonesia has limited fuel purchases to 50 litres a day and Sri Lanka to 15.\n\nAND THE FINANCIAL TRANSMISSION IS SEPARATE FROM THE PHYSICAL ONE. With the Persian Gulf designated a 'Listed Area' by global insurers, maritime premiums across the region have surged 300-400%, raising freight costs directly — for Bangladesh, on the ready-made garment exports that carry its economy. A coalition spanning South Korea, Japan, India, China, Vietnam, Thailand and more than thirty other states has organised around the shortages. None of these states is a party to the war, and none has a mechanism to end it.",
   "details": "TRANSITS: 1-13 vessels a day against a 73/day pre-crisis baseline; 386 hulls holding position away from berth on August 21. JAPAN: >95% of Middle Eastern crude via Hormuz; 6% of LNG from Qatar and the UAE; drawing on the joint stockpile with South Korea. SOUTH KOREA: >70% of imported crude via Hormuz; 14% of LNG from Qatar and the UAE; licence-plate vehicle rationing on alternate days. PAKISTAN: 99% of LNG from Qatar and the UAE; from surplus to record spot purchases; emergency gas management plan prioritising domestic and residential supply; ~78 mmcfd of LNG to the fertiliser sector suspended. BANGLADESH: 72% of LNG from Qatar and the UAE; ~40 of 115 scheduled 2026 cargoes expected to be lost; four of five state urea plants shut for at least fifteen days; power-sector gas cut from 870 to 820 mmcfd. INDIA: 53% of LNG from Qatar and the UAE; food and logistics inflation. INDONESIA: fuel purchases capped at 50 litres a day. SRI LANKA: capped at 15 litres a day. INSURANCE: Persian Gulf designated a 'Listed Area'; maritime premiums up 300-400%; Hormuz transit war-risk cover near 10% of hull value. COALITION: 30-plus states organised around the shortages.",
   "prediction_impact": "Predictions that a Hormuz closure would impose its heaviest costs on Asian importers rather than on the belligerents are CONFIRMED and now measurable in rationing regimes: licence-plate restrictions in South Korea, 50-litre and 15-litre daily fuel caps in Indonesia and Sri Lanka, suspended fertiliser-sector gas in Pakistan, and forty lost LNG cargoes with four of five urea plants shut in Bangladesh. Predictions that global oil markets would price a supply rupture are still DISCONFIRMED: Brent is near $94, far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130-plus a sustained US ground commitment would imply, because the disruption is being absorbed through rationing, stockpile releases and demand destruction in importing states rather than through price alone. Predictions that the affected states would be able to force a diplomatic resolution remain DISCONFIRMED: a coalition of more than thirty states has organised around the shortages and has no mechanism to end the war."
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-08-02",
   "headline": "DAY 157 — THE WEEKEND THE CAMPAIGN WAS TO BEGIN PASSED WITHOUT IT. THROUGH AUGUST 1 NO NEW US STRIKE WAVE ON IRANIAN TERRITORY WAS REPORTED, AND THE REPORTING VERB REVERTED FROM 'HAS ORDERED' TO 'IS WEIGHING': AL JAZEERA RECORDED THAT TRUMP 'HAD NOT YET AUTHORIZED THE POTENTIAL TWO-WEEK OPERATION TARGETING TEHRAN'S ELECTRICITY GRID,' AND ABC NEWS REPORTED THAT CENTCOM HAD PREPARED A TWO-WEEK AIR CAMPAIGN WHILE SOME ADMINISTRATION OFFICIALS FAVOURED 'A SHORTER, MORE SEVERE SALVO' TO ADVANCE NEGOTIATIONS. THE CYCLE'S LARGEST NEW FACT IS A CEILING, NOT A STRIKE: CSIS PUTS REMAINING US STOCKS AT FEWER THAN 827 PATRIOT INTERCEPTORS (FROM ~2,200 PRE-WAR) AND FEWER THAN 278 THAAD (FROM 452) — MORE THAN 60% EXPENDED — WITH THREE SOURCES TELLING CNN THE ESTIMATES ARE CLOSE TO INTERNAL GOVERNMENT FIGURES, REBUILDING ASSESSED AT THREE-PLUS YEARS, AND GEN. DAN CAINE REPORTEDLY WARNING PRIVATELY ABOUT SHORTAGES. WASHINGTON MOVED PEOPLE INSTEAD OF MUNITIONS: US EMBASSIES IN AT LEAST TEN MIDDLE EASTERN COUNTRIES TOLD AMERICANS TO 'CONSIDER DEPARTING' OR 'BE PREPARED TO DEPART,' CITING 'THE POTENTIAL FOR UNFORESEEN ESCALATION.' TWO TANKERS WERE HIT OR NEAR-MISSED OFF OMAN; HORMUZ TRANSITS FELL 77% IN A DAY (22 TO 5). IRAN DECLARED A 'COMPREHENSIVE PLAN' NAMING US ENERGY INFRASTRUCTURE IN THE REGION AND ISRAELI CRITICAL INFRASTRUCTURE AS TARGETS. THE SENATE REJECTED A WAR POWERS RESOLUTION 49-50. BRENT SETTLED $87.93. STILL AIR AND MARITIME ONLY — NO LANDING, NO KHARG SEIZURE, NO DRAFT. PREDICTED US GROUND SEIZURE REMAINS DISCONFIRMED.",
   "theatre": "Iran / Strait of Hormuz",
   "tag": "Iran War",
   "tags": [
