   ],
   "source": "Al Jazeera, 'Iran warns against ‘flames of war’ amid US strike threats on energy sites,' August 1, 2026; ABC News live updates, 'Trump weighs strikes on Iranian energy sites, US officials say,' July 31 (7:34 PM EDT) – August 1, 2026; CBS News live updates, 'Iran accuses U.S. of escalating tensions as Washington prepares new strikes,' August 1, 2026; CNN live blog, 'US-Iran war news; State Department warns citizens across the Middle East,' August 1, 2026; CSIS, 'Rebuilding U.S. Missile Inventory: A Multiyear Project,' 2026; CNN Politics, 'US weapons stockpiles continue to dwindle with permanent end to Iran war nowhere in sight,' July 29, 2026; ABC News, 'US Patriot interceptor stockpile diminished by some 65% during Iran war, think tank estimates,' 2026; Military Times, 'Iran war depleted US Patriot missile stockpiles, creating readiness challenges, experts say,' July 30, 2026; EA WorldView, 'UPDATES: US War on Iran, Day 156 — American Attacks Resume,' August 1, 2026; U.S. Embassy Amman, Security Alert, August 1, 2026; U.S. Embassy Jerusalem, Security Alert, August 1, 2026; U.S. Embassy Muscat, Security Alert, August 1, 2026; The Week, \"'Be prepared to depart': US embassies in Middle East warn citizens to beware an 'unforeseen escalation',\" August 1, 2026; UKMTO advisories via Gulf News, 'Tanker Explosion Off Oman as Strait of Hormuz Shipping Plunges 77%,' August 1, 2026; South China Morning Post, 'Tankers targeted in twin incidents near Strait of Hormuz off Oman,' August 1, 2026; Tasnim News Agency via Pravda USA and Just The News on Iran's 'comprehensive plan,' July 31 – August 1, 2026; TechTimes, \"Iran's Proven Energy Retaliation Playbook Has Cost $58B,\" August 1, 2026; Democracy Now!, 'Senate Votes Down Iran War Powers Resolution After Fetterman Votes with GOP Majority,' July 31, 2026; The Hill, 'Senate Republicans defeat Iran war powers resolution,' July 30, 2026; Roll Call, 'Iran war powers resolution blocked in Senate as conflict heats up,' July 30, 2026; The Washington Post, 'U.S. spy agencies suspect Iran launched cyberattack on Minnesota water facilities,' July 30, 2026; CBS News, 'U.S. investigating if Iran was behind cyberattack on water systems in 7 states,' July 30, 2026; TradingEconomics Brent crude, July 31, 2026; straits.live Strait of Hormuz board, August 1, 2026 (22:59 UTC); Stars and Stripes, 'US troop casualties in Iran war surpass 600 as fighting pauses,' July 27, 2026; CNN Politics, 'Iran war casualty count eclipses 600 as Pentagon quietly changes accounting,' July 26, 2026.",
   "summary": "THE CAMPAIGN DID NOT BEGIN. The single most testable claim in the record from the previous cycle was that a multi-day campaign against Iran's energy and civil infrastructure could start 'as soon as this weekend.' The weekend arrived. Through the end of August 1 no new US strike wave against Iranian territory was reported by any outlet, and the reporting verb moved backwards. Al Jazeera recorded on August 1 that President Trump 'had not yet authorized the potential two-week operation targeting Tehran's electricity grid.' ABC News, updating at 7:34 PM EDT on July 31, described Trump as 'considering' strikes that could begin over the weekend, with two US officials cautioning that 'those plans could still change' — and added a detail that reframes the whole package: CENTCOM had prepared a two-week air campaign, but some administration officials favoured 'a shorter, more severe salvo' intended to advance negotiations rather than to force capitulation. That is a dispute about the shape of an air campaign, and it is being had inside the administration in public.\n\nTHE CEILING. The cycle's largest new fact is not an event but a constraint. CSIS researchers estimate that fewer than 827 Patriot interceptors and fewer than 278 THAAD interceptors remain in US stocks, against pre-war estimates of roughly 2,200 Patriots of the two most-modernised variants and 452 THAAD — more than 60% of both expended in five months of this war. Three sources familiar with the matter told CNN the CSIS figures are close to internal government numbers. The recovery arithmetic is worse than the inventory: the United States produces 183 THAAD interceptors a year and takes roughly three and a half years from contract signature to delivery, and CSIS assesses three or more years to return to pre-war levels even against the $18.2 billion allocated for Patriot, THAAD and Tomahawk within a $67 billion FY2026 emergency funding proposal. General Dan Caine, chairman of the Joint Chiefs, is reported to have warned privately about the shortages, and that warning is being cited as a reason for administration restraint on broader strikes. CSIS's own framing is that the strategic risk is not sustaining this war but responding to another high-intensity contingency before the inventory is rebuilt.\n\nWASHINGTON MOVED PEOPLE INSTEAD. On August 1 US embassies across at least ten Middle Eastern countries — the UAE, Qatar, Oman, Iraq, Jordan, Israel, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia, Bahrain and Egypt — issued near-simultaneous security alerts telling Americans to consider departing, or to be prepared to depart, citing a security environment with 'the potential for unforeseen escalation' and warning of flight cancellations, periodic airspace closures and travel disruption. The stated rationale named Iran's expansion of attacks to countries not previously targeted, and a drone strike on a US gas tanker in the Mediterranean.\n\nTHE STRAIT. UKMTO logged two incidents on August 1: a tanker reported a large splash followed by an explosion about 21 nautical miles northeast of Khasab, Oman, with the master reporting no damage; and a second tanker was struck by an unknown projectile about 11 nautical miles northeast of Limah, Oman, sustaining engine-room damage and reporting itself not under command. No casualties, no environmental impact and no attribution. Ship-tracking data showed transits through the strait falling 77% in a single day, from 22 vessels to five. The straits.live board at 22:59 UTC on August 1 held to its 11%-of-normal framing (latest published volume 10 transits against an 88/day pre-crisis baseline), with 371 vessels holding position away from berth, 36 tankers running AIS-dark in 24 hours, war-risk insurance at 8.0 times pre-crisis rates (roughly $2.5 million per VLCC), eight of the nine largest container lines routing via the Cape of Good Hope, and market-implied odds of the strait reopening by August 15 at 2%. CENTCOM's running tally of vessels redirected since the blockade restart rose to 24.\n\nIRAN'S DECLARED ANSWER. A senior Iranian security official told Tasnim that Iran has prepared a comprehensive plan to respond to any American attack on its energy infrastructure, naming US energy infrastructure in the region and Israeli critical infrastructure as explicit targets. Major General Ali Abdollahi, head of Iran's wartime military command, said at 4:27 AM on August 1 that 'the United States is rapidly moving down the path of escalating tensions,' and warned that countries acting as 'a defensive shield for criminal, aggressor America will burn in the fire of war.' Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi warned against 'adventurous action' in calls with Turkish and Pakistani counterparts, stating Tehran's readiness for a 'decisive and proportionate response.' President Masoud Pezeshkian said 'the future of Iran will be shaped by the people of Iran.' The threat is not hypothetical: Iran executed this playbook in March 2026, striking Saudi Arabia's SAMREF refinery and Jubail petrochemical complex, the UAE's Al Hosn gas field and Qatar's Ras Laffan LNG and Mesaieed petrochemical facilities within 24 hours, with combined Gulf energy damage put at up to $58 billion across more than 80 facilities, over a third severely damaged.\n\nWASHINGTON'S OWN CONSTRAINTS. The Senate rejected 49-50 a resolution to discharge from committee a measure directing the President to halt hostilities against Iran absent congressional authorisation. Republicans Susan Collins, Lisa Murkowski and Rand Paul crossed over; Senator John Fetterman was the only Democrat voting to block it. It was the second war powers measure to fail in the Senate in two weeks. Separately, the FBI and CISA are investigating cyberattacks on municipal water systems in at least seven states; US intelligence assessed Iran likely responsible for a coordinated attack on more than 30 Minnesota municipal systems, with Michigan also affected. Programmable logic controllers were the common target; no water supply has been reported compromised.\n\nMARKETS. Brent settled at $87.93, up $1.05 or 1.21%, finishing July up 22.86% and 26.21% year-on-year; the straits.live board carried the same $87.93 at 22:59 UTC on August 1, +2.38% over 24 hours against a roughly $72 pre-crisis baseline. This reconciles downward the $90.24 intraday print logged in the previous cycle — the direction and the monthly magnitude are unchanged, the level is about $2.30 lower. Futures markets were closed for the weekend, so no August 1 or August 2 settle exists. Exxon Mobil's second-quarter profit doubled to $14.53 billion and Chevron's nearly quadrupled to $12.07 billion.\n\nA CASUALTY-LEDGER DIVERGENCE WORTH FLAGGING. This project's running tally stands at 685 wounded and 18 killed in action as of July 31. The Pentagon's published figure, after the July reclassification that split the war into 'Operation Epic Fury' and a new 'Overseas Operations' category from July 7, is 18 killed and 624 wounded. Wikipedia's infobox carries 19 military dead plus one contractor and 624 wounded. The gap is an accounting-basis difference, not a contradiction, and it is recorded here rather than silently reconciled.",
   "details": "WHAT DID NOT HAPPEN IS THE FINDING. A prediction-audit project has to be as disciplined about non-events as about events, and this cycle is a non-event of the first order. On July 31 four outlets reported a multi-day bombing campaign against Iranian power plants, refineries, Tehran's electricity grid and dual-use bridges, described by the Wall Street Journal as ordered and aimed at forcing surrender, and able to begin as soon as the weekend. CBS reported the weekend window explicitly and noted internal discussion of concluding before markets opened Monday. Through the end of Saturday August 1 no such campaign had begun, and no outlet reported American munitions landing on Iranian soil during the cycle.\n\nThe reporting did not merely fail to confirm the campaign; it moved in the opposite direction. Al Jazeera's August 1 account states that Trump 'had not yet authorized the potential two-week operation targeting Tehran's electricity grid,' which aligns with CBS's July 31 position that final authorisation had not been given and against the WSJ's framing that the order had been issued. ABC News introduced a further distinction: CENTCOM had prepared a two-week air campaign, but some administration officials preferred 'a shorter, more severe salvo' to advance negotiations. Two things follow. First, the internal dispute is over the duration and severity of an air campaign, and no reporting in this cycle placed a ground option back in the discussion. Second, the objective is contested inside the administration — capitulation versus leverage in talks — which is a materially different reading of the same package from the one that circulated on July 31.\n\nTHE MUNITIONS CEILING AND WHY IT MATTERS TO THE GROUND QUESTION. CSIS's estimates — fewer than 827 Patriot interceptors from roughly 2,200 pre-war, fewer than 278 THAAD from 452, above 60% of both expended — are the most consequential numbers logged in weeks, and three sources told CNN they track internal government figures closely. Production and lead times make this a multi-year condition rather than a procurement problem: 183 THAAD interceptors a year, roughly three and a half years from contract to delivery, three or more years to pre-war levels. The $18.2 billion earmarked within the $67 billion FY2026 emergency request buys the schedule, not relief inside it.\n\nThe ground-rung implication is direct and is set out in this cycle's Ground Invasion Tracker entry: an amphibious or airborne lodgement on Kharg Island would have to be covered against Iranian ballistic and cruise missiles for as long as it was held, and the interceptors that would do the covering are the same depleted inventory. Specialists have identified holding the island rather than taking it as the binding problem since March; the munitions data supplies an arithmetic reason why that judgement has not changed. General Caine's reported private warning about shortages, cited as a restraint on broader strikes, is the same constraint operating one rung lower.\n\nTHE DEPARTURE ADVISORIES POINT THE SAME WAY. Mass advisories to depart are an escalation indicator and were correctly read as one across the press. They are not, however, a ground-operation indicator. Force flow ahead of a landing moves people and materiel toward a theatre; this cycle moved American civilians out of ten countries in it. The advisories are consistent with an imminent air campaign and Iranian retaliation against Gulf hosts, which is precisely what both sides have said is coming.\n\nTHE STRAIT TIGHTENED WITHOUT A DECISION. The 77% single-day collapse in transits (22 to five) and the two tanker incidents off Oman occurred with no strike wave and no Iranian declaration — the threat alone was sufficient. That is worth recording because it bears on a recurring corpus claim that chokepoint closure requires physical seizure: the strait is functioning at roughly a tenth of normal, held there by insurance pricing, AIS-dark running and 371 vessels waiting, without either side taking ground.\n\nSOURCE-DISCIPLINE NOTES. (1) The Brent figure is revised down from the previous cycle's $90.24 to a $87.93 settle on TradingEconomics, corroborated by the straits.live board; the monthly gain is restated at 22.86% against the roughly 24% logged earlier. Both derive from the same two trackers on different snapshots, and the lower figure is the settle. (2) The US casualty divergence between this project's 685-wounded running tally and the Pentagon's 624 is an accounting-basis artefact of the July reclassification and is flagged, not resolved. (3) Kuwait's air defences intercepted and destroyed Iranian drones early on Saturday August 1, with Iran's army attributing the strikes to 'the recent aggression by the terrorist U.S. military against our country and its brutal attack on a residential home on Qeshm Island'; this is logged in full in the Gulf States entry for this cycle.",
   "prediction_impact": "The corpus-predicted US GROUND seizure of Hormuz or Kharg Island remains DISCONFIRMED at Day 157, and this cycle adds a mechanism to the verdict rather than another day of absence. For five months the audit has recorded that the ground option was available, staffed and never taken. The CSIS interceptor data supplies a material reason: with more than 60% of Patriot and THAAD stocks expended and three-plus years to rebuild, the air-defence umbrella required to hold a lodgement against Iranian missile fire does not exist in sufficient depth, and the Joint Chiefs chairman is reported to have said so privately about air operations generally. Predictions that a resumption of large-scale strikes would be the prelude to a ground phase remain DISCONFIRMED: the resumption itself did not occur in this cycle, and the internal argument that did occur was between a two-week air campaign and a shorter, more severe air salvo. Predictions of an imminent US collapse of Iranian resistance through bombing are UNTESTED and were not advanced this cycle. Predictions of a negotiated Hormuz reopening remain LIVE BUT UNMOVED, and the strait tightened by 77% in a day without any decision by either government — evidence against corpus claims that chokepoint control requires physical occupation. Predictions of a supply-driven oil shock above $130 remain DISCONFIRMED at an $87.93 settle, below the previous cycle's logged level, even with a surrender-forcing energy campaign publicly reported as imminent. Predictions that congressional constraint would halt the war remain DISCONFIRMED: the second war powers measure in two weeks failed 49-50, with three Republicans crossing over and one Democrat crossing back. Predictions that Iranian retaliation would be confined to military targets remain DISCONFIRMED, with the cyber campaign against municipal water systems in at least seven US states now assessed by US intelligence as likely Iranian.",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "Why the reported weekend campaign did not begin is being read three incompatible ways in public, all drawing on the same documented facts.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented: the WSJ reported the campaign as ordered and able to begin as soon as the weekend; CBS reported no final authorisation; CNN reported it could be called off; Al Jazeera reported on August 1 that Trump 'had not yet authorized the potential two-week operation targeting Tehran's electricity grid.' Documented: ABC News reported CENTCOM had prepared a two-week air campaign while some administration officials favoured 'a shorter, more severe salvo' to advance negotiations. Documented: CSIS estimates more than 60% of Patriot and THAAD interceptor stocks expended, with three sources telling CNN the figures track internal government numbers, and Gen. Dan Caine reported to have warned privately about shortages — cited in reporting as a restraint on broader strikes. Documented: no US strike wave on Iranian territory was reported through August 1. Not documented: any statement by Trump, Hegseth or CENTCOM explaining the non-start; any cancellation order; any record of the decision chain.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "CBS News and CNN, whose reporting implies the campaign never carried final authorisation — the non-start being simply the absence of an order",
      "The Wall Street Journal (via Bloomberg and The Times of Israel), whose reporting of an issued order implies the campaign was held or deferred rather than never authorised",
      "EA WorldView and CNN reporting attributing administration restraint on broader strikes to Gen. Caine's private munitions warnings",
      "ABC News, reporting an unresolved internal preference for a shorter salvo aimed at negotiations rather than the two-week capitulation campaign"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "A campaign that was never authorised, a campaign authorised and withheld as leverage, and a campaign deferred on materiel advice produce an identical public signature: no strikes, no announcement, no explanation. All three readings are advanced by mainstream outlets citing US officials with access to different points in the decision chain. Only an execution, a formal cancellation, a War Powers notification, or an on-record account would separate them.",
     "open_question_ref": "iran-weekend-campaign-non-start-august-2026"
    },
    {
     "reading": "Whether the President ORDERED the multi-day energy campaign or is still weighing it remains contested, and this cycle strengthened the 'not authorised' side without settling it.",
