   "source": "CSIS, 'Rebuilding U.S. Missile Inventory: A Multiyear Project,' 2026; CNN Politics, 'US weapons stockpiles continue to dwindle with permanent end to Iran war nowhere in sight,' July 29, 2026; ABC News, 'US Patriot interceptor stockpile diminished by some 65% during Iran war, think tank estimates,' 2026; Military Times, 'Iran war depleted US Patriot missile stockpiles, creating readiness challenges, experts say,' July 30, 2026; EA WorldView, 'UPDATES: US War on Iran, Day 156,' August 1, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Iran warns against ‘flames of war’ amid US strike threats on energy sites,' August 1, 2026; ABC News live updates, July 31 – August 1, 2026; Military Times, 'Selective Service System wants to wargame the draft,' July 24, 2026; [your]NEWS, 'Selective Service Seeks Draft Mobilization Simulator as Automatic Registration Nears,' July 28, 2026; Antiwar.com, 'Selective Service Withdraws Proposed Rules for ‘Automatic’ Draft Registration,' July 24, 2026; USNI News Fleet and Marine Tracker, July 27, 2026 (most recent edition); CNN Politics, 'Iran building up defenses of Kharg Island to protect against potential US ground attack, sources say,' March 25, 2026; Al Jazeera, \"Pentagon readies 'for weeks of US ground operations' in Iran,\" March 29, 2026; Britannica, 'Why did the U.S. strike Kharg Island in March 2026?'; The Jerusalem Post, 'US strikes Kharg Island military targets, American official confirms,' March 2026; CBS News on the 82nd Airborne deployment, March 2026; U.S. Embassy security alerts (Amman, Jerusalem, Muscat), August 1, 2026; The Week and Legal Insurrection on the regional departure advisories, August 1, 2026.",
   "summary": "Day 157 holds at no_ground_troops. Two things happened this cycle that bear on the ground question, and neither is a troop movement.\n\nTHE TEST THE PREVIOUS CYCLE SET WAS FAILED BY THE CAMPAIGN, NOT BY THE TRACKER. On July 31 four outlets reported a multi-day bombing campaign able to begin 'as soon as this weekend.' Through August 1 it did not begin. No strike wave, and — the point for this tracker — no landing, no embarkation order, no Marine Expeditionary Unit tasking, no airborne alert, no Kharg operation, and no relief Amphibious Ready Group. The internal argument that was reported in its place, per ABC News, is between CENTCOM's prepared two-week air campaign and some officials' preference for 'a shorter, more severe salvo' to advance negotiations. Both options are air options.\n\nTHE CONSTRAINT BECAME MEASURABLE. For five months this tracker has recorded that the Kharg seizure option is available, staffed, repeatedly reported and never taken, with specialists identifying HOLDING the island rather than taking it as the binding problem. This cycle supplies arithmetic for that judgement. CSIS estimates fewer than 827 Patriot interceptors remain against roughly 2,200 pre-war, and fewer than 278 THAAD against 452 — more than 60% of both expended in five months. Three sources told CNN the estimates are close to internal government figures. Production runs at 183 THAAD a year with roughly three and a half years from contract to delivery; CSIS assesses three or more years to pre-war levels even against $18.2 billion allocated within a $67 billion FY2026 emergency request. General Dan Caine is reported to have warned privately about the shortages, and that warning is being cited as a restraint on broader strikes.\n\nWhy this is a ground-rung fact and not merely an air-defence fact: an amphibious or airborne lodgement on a mined island 20 miles off the Iranian coast would have to be defended against Iranian ballistic and cruise missile fire for the entire period it was held, and the interceptors that would defend it are the same inventory. The force requirement (roughly 1,200-plus troops — a Marine battalion landing team, a reinforced Army airborne battalion, or a combination) has not changed since March. What this cycle adds is that the covering asset is now scarce and cannot be replenished inside three years.\n\nTHE PEOPLE MOVED THE WRONG WAY. US embassies in at least ten Middle Eastern countries told Americans on August 1 to consider departing or be prepared to depart, citing 'the potential for unforeseen escalation.' This is a genuine escalation indicator and is logged as one. It is not a ground indicator. The signature that precedes a landing is inbound: force flow, prepositioning, medical and fuel staging, embarkation. This cycle's movement was outbound civilians.\n\nTHE DRAFT RUNG SPLIT. For the first time the two draft sub-indicators moved in opposite directions. The regulatory sub-rung stays DOWN: the automatic-registration rule withdrawn on July 23 after the White House held it for nearly four months has still not been re-proposed and no explanation has been given. The administrative sub-rung ROSE: the Selective Service System published a federal contracting request for a government-owned 'Readiness Simulation' capable of testing staffing, processing and mobilisation scenarios across multiple draft scenarios, with vendor submissions due August 14, 2026, ahead of the December 19, 2026 statutory start of automatic registration. The agency's own framing is explicit that this does not announce a draft; conscription still requires authorisation from both Congress and the President during a national emergency. The tracker records the split rather than netting it to zero.\n\nEVERY EXECUTION INDICATOR REMAINS AT ZERO FOR A 157TH CONSECUTIVE DAY.",
   "details": "STATUS: no_ground_troops (unchanged, Day 157).\n\nEXECUTION INDICATORS — ALL ZERO:\n• US ground forces inside Iranian territory: NONE reported, 157 consecutive days.\n• Kharg Island landing or seizure: NONE. Kharg was not named as a target in any version of the reported energy campaign and has never been seized. The island's only kinetic event of the war remains the March 13, 2026 USAF raid on military sites — air defences, naval-mine and missile storage — which reporting states deliberately avoided the oil and gas infrastructure.\n• Amphibious embarkation or landing: NONE.\n• New MEU tasking, relief ARG, or deployment order: NONE.\n• Draft or mobilisation order: NONE.\n• New force-flow or troop-increase announcement: NONE.\n\nAMPHIBIOUS PICTURE (unchanged, eleventh day). USS Boxer (LHD-4) with USS Comstock (LSD-45), USS Portland (LPD-27) and the 11th Marine Expeditionary Unit in the Arabian Sea, alongside expeditionary sea base USS Miguel Keith (ESB-5) — still the sole deployed Amphibious Ready Group fleet-wide. USS Tripoli remains home at Sasebo after offloading roughly 2,500 Marines of the 31st MEU at Okinawa on July 26 with no relief ARG announced. The USNI News Fleet and Marine Tracker edition of July 27 remains the most recent published; the next edition is due Monday August 3 and is the scheduled next test of this indicator.\n\nFORCE POSTURE. Approximately 50,000 US personnel in the Middle East, roughly 10,000 above the standing baseline, unchanged since the Operation Epic Fury build-up. Includes thousands of Marines and sailors and approximately 2,000 soldiers of the 82nd Airborne Division with its divisional headquarters, in theatre since late March 2026 for contingency purposes and never committed to Iranian territory. The Pentagon's earlier consideration of sending up to 10,000 additional troops has not been reported as executed.\n\nTHE MUNITIONS ARITHMETIC IN FULL.\n• Patriot: fewer than 827 remaining, against a pre-war estimate of roughly 2,200 of the platform's two most-modernised variants — a reduction of about 65%.\n• THAAD: fewer than 278 remaining, against 452 pre-war.\n• CSIS assessment: more than 60% of key Patriot and THAAD interceptors fired during the war with Iran.\n• Corroboration: three sources familiar with the matter told CNN the CSIS estimates are close to internal government figures.\n• Production: 183 THAAD interceptors a year; roughly three and a half years from contract signature to delivery.\n• Recovery: three or more years to pre-war inventory levels.\n• Funding: $18.2 billion for Patriot, THAAD and Tomahawk within a $67 billion FY2026 emergency funding proposal — which buys the schedule, not relief inside it.\n• Command signal: Gen. Dan Caine reported to have warned privately about the shortages, cited in reporting as a factor in administration restraint on broader Iranian strikes.\n• CSIS's own framing: the greater strategic risk is not sustaining this conflict but responding to another high-intensity contingency before inventories can be rebuilt.\n\nCOUNTERVAILING SIGNALS, LOGGED NOT DISMISSED. The Pentagon was reported on July 24 to be readying options for weeks of limited ground operations — a Kharg seizure plus coastal raids by special operations and conventional infantry — pending presidential approval, and that reporting has never been retracted. Roughly 2,000 soldiers of the 82nd Airborne with divisional headquarters remain in theatre. Secretary Hegseth's March non-foreclosure ('Our adversary right now thinks there are 15 different ways we could come at them with boots on the ground. And guess what: There are') stands unretracted, as does Gen. Caine's statement that the range of options is 'not just limited to... forces on the ground.' Neither was renewed this cycle. The Selective Service readiness-simulation procurement is a preparatory signal on the mobilisation rung and is logged as such.\n\nASSESSMENT OF WHAT WOULD CHANGE THE STATUS. A relief ARG announcement or a second ARG ordered to the theatre; an MEU embarkation or amphibious rehearsal; an 82nd Airborne alert or N-hour sequence; medical, fuel or ammunition prepositioning consistent with sustained ground operations; a re-proposed automatic-registration rule or any Selective Service action touching classification, lottery or induction rather than registration; or a Kharg operation named in reporting as tasked rather than considered. None occurred in this cycle.",
   "prediction_impact": "Corpus predictions of a US GROUND INVASION of Iran, a MARINE SEIZURE of Kharg Island, or US TROOPS TAKING THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ remain DISCONFIRMED at Day 157. The disconfirmation this cycle rests on a stronger footing than accumulated absence. First, the reported coercive campaign that was to begin this weekend did not begin, and the internal dispute reported in its place is between two air options. Second, the constraint that specialists have identified since March — that holding Kharg, not taking it, is the binding problem — now has published arithmetic behind it: an air-defence inventory more than 60% expended, three-plus years from replenishment, and a Joint Chiefs chairman reported to be warning privately about it. A lodgement that cannot be covered is a lodgement that will not be attempted, and the audit records that as evidence rather than inference only because the numbers are now sourced. Third, the mass departure advisories, correctly read as an escalation indicator, are the inverse of the force-flow signature a ground operation produces. Predictions tying a US ground war to conscription remain DISCONFIRMED on the execution test while the underlying indicator SPLIT for the first time: the withdrawn registration rule has not been re-proposed, but the Selective Service is procuring a mobilisation simulator with vendor submissions due August 14 and a statutory automatic-registration start of December 19, 2026. The project treats the simulator as preparatory capability on the registration rung, not as movement toward a draft, and flags December 19 as the next scheduled test."
  },
