   ],
   "source": "U.S. Embassy Amman, 'Security Alert: U.S. Embassy Amman – August 1, 2026'; U.S. Embassy Jerusalem, 'Security Alert – August 1, 2026'; U.S. Embassy Muscat, 'Security Alert – August 1, 2026'; The Week, \"'Be prepared to depart': US embassies in Middle East warn citizens to beware an 'unforeseen escalation',\" August 1, 2026; Legal Insurrection, \"U.S. Warns its Citizens to Leave Middle East Citing 'Unforeseen Escalation' With Iran,\" August 1, 2026; Daily Pakistan, \"'Leave Middle East or Prepare for Possible Evacuation', US Embassies warn all Americans,\" August 1, 2026; CBS News live updates, 'Iran accuses U.S. of escalating tensions as Washington prepares new strikes,' August 1, 2026; CNN live blog, 'State Department warns citizens across the Middle East,' August 1, 2026; NBC News, 'Iran and Kuwait report attacks on water and power infrastructure as strikes escalate,' July 31 – August 1, 2026; Gulf News, 'Tanker Explosion Off Oman as Strait of Hormuz Shipping Plunges 77%,' August 1, 2026; South China Morning Post, 'Tankers targeted in twin incidents near Strait of Hormuz off Oman,' August 1, 2026; UKMTO advisories via Middle East Eye, August 1, 2026; The Maritime Executive, 'Houthi Blockade of Saudi Shipping Begins to Bite,' 2026; The National, 'Tankers carrying Saudi oil make U-turn in Red Sea after Houthi threats,' July 21, 2026; EA WorldView, 'UPDATES: US War on Iran, Day 156,' August 1, 2026; TechTimes, \"Iran's Proven Energy Retaliation Playbook Has Cost $58B: Power Grid Strikes Risk Round Two,\" August 1, 2026; Tasnim News Agency via Pravda USA and Just The News, July 31 – August 1, 2026; The National, \"Saudi Arabia proposes 43-nation 'maritime defence coalition' in Red Sea,\" July 30, 2026; Haaretz live blog on southern Lebanon demolitions, July 31 – August 1, 2026; The Jerusalem Post, 'Gulf countries continue to face Iran attacks as critical energy infrastructure at risk,' 2026.",
   "summary": "THE ADVISORY IS THE STORY. On August 1 the United States told its citizens across the Gulf and the Levant to leave. Embassies in at least ten countries — the UAE, Qatar, Oman, Iraq, Jordan, Israel, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia, Bahrain and Egypt — issued near-simultaneous security alerts advising Americans to consider departing or to be prepared to depart, describing a security environment with 'the potential for unforeseen escalation' and warning of flight cancellations, periodic airspace closures and travel disruption. The stated rationale was Iran's demonstrated willingness to strike without warning and to expand attacks to countries not previously targeted — Egypt named explicitly — together with a drone strike on a US gas tanker in the Mediterranean. Advisories on this scale had not been issued simultaneously across the region at any prior point in the war.\n\nKUWAIT AGAIN. Kuwait's military said early on Saturday August 1 that its air defences had intercepted and destroyed Iranian drones. Iran's army stated the strikes came 'in response to the recent aggression by the terrorist U.S. military against our country and its brutal attack on a residential home on Qeshm Island' — the July 30 strike in which IRNA reported three killed, identified as a couple and their two-year-old child. No Kuwaiti casualties were reported. Kuwait has now absorbed strikes or attempted strikes on Ahmad al-Jaber and Ali al-Salem air bases, an offshore drilling platform, border posts, a desalination-and-power plant and Kuwait International Airport across the course of the war.\n\nOMANI WATERS. Two tanker incidents were logged by UKMTO on August 1: an explosion close to a tanker about 21 nautical miles northeast of Khasab, with the master reporting no damage; and a tanker struck by an unknown projectile about 11 nautical miles northeast of Limah, sustaining engine-room damage and declaring itself not under command. No casualties, no pollution, no claim of responsibility and no attribution by any reporting outlet.\n\nTHE SECOND CHOKEPOINT TIGHTENS ON RIYADH. The Houthi maritime embargo against Saudi shipping moved from declaration to measurable effect. The Houthis claimed eight Saudi oil tankers were forced to change course. Ship-tracking identified the Singapore-flagged VLCC Xin Long Yang and the Liberia-flagged tanker Rodos, both loaded with crude at Yanbu, abandoning the Bab al-Mandeb route and turning toward the Suez Canal. Saudi Arabia's 43-nation maritime defence coalition, convened in Riyadh on July 30 with a headquarters in the kingdom and thirteen public backers, is the institutional answer and is not yet operational.\n\nWHAT IRAN HAS PROMISED THE GULF. A senior Iranian security official told Tasnim that Tehran has prepared a comprehensive plan against any American strike on its energy infrastructure, naming US energy infrastructure in the region — which in practice means facilities on Gulf state territory — and Israeli critical infrastructure. The precedent is documented: in March 2026 Iran struck Saudi Arabia's SAMREF refinery and Jubail petrochemical complex, the UAE's Al Hosn gas field, and Qatar's Ras Laffan LNG and Mesaieed petrochemical facilities within 24 hours, with combined damage across Gulf energy assets put at up to $58 billion covering more than 80 facilities, over a third severely damaged. Bahrain's state energy company Bapco declared force majeure after earlier waves. Alternative routing continues to develop around the chokepoints: an Iraq-Turkey pipeline arrangement provides a 750,000-barrel-per-day path that avoids Hormuz.\n\nLEBANON. Israeli forces conducted large controlled detonations across southern Lebanon, including near al-Mansouri, Majdal Zoun and Haddatha, following the demolition of a multi-level Hezbollah tunnel complex beneath the Beaufort ridge with roughly 700 tonnes of explosives. Trilateral Israel-Lebanon-US talks remain scheduled for Rome, August 4-6.",
   "details": "THE ADVISORY LANGUAGE, PRECISELY. The alerts told Americans in the region to 'exercise caution and heightened vigilance,' to 'be prepared for flight cancellations, periodic airspace closures, and potential travel disruptions,' and — the operative instruction — to consider departing or be prepared to depart should there be escalation. The named rationale was that 'the Iranian regime is unpredictable, as seen by its recent decisions to attack areas in the region without warning or provocations as well as to expand attacks to areas not previously targeted.' Egypt was cited as the example of that expansion, a reference to the July 29 Damietta drone attack whose attribution remains contested — Araghchi has denied Iranian involvement and Cairo has publicly rejected reporting that it blames Tehran.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS FOR THE GULF STATES' POSITION. Every state on the advisory list except Israel has spent the war trying to remain a host rather than a belligerent. A simultaneous American instruction to its own nationals to leave those countries is an assessment, delivered publicly, that host status will not protect them from what is coming. It sits alongside the Saudi decision to build its own coalition rather than join the American one, Baghdad's public denial of prior knowledge of the July 28 US-Saudi strikes on its territory, and Kuwait's repeated absorption of Iranian ordnance aimed at American assets on Kuwaiti soil.\n\nDIRECTIONALITY NOTE FOR THE GROUND QUESTION. The advisories are logged in this project's Ground Invasion Tracker as an escalation indicator that is NOT a ground indicator: a landing is preceded by inbound force flow, and this cycle produced outbound civilian movement. The distinction is recorded to prevent the two being conflated in scoring.\n\nSHIPPING AND INSURANCE CONTEXT. Transits through Hormuz fell 77% in a single day, from 22 vessels to five. The straits.live board at 22:59 UTC on August 1 held its 11%-of-normal framing against an 88/day pre-crisis baseline, with 371 vessels holding position away from berth, 36 tankers running AIS-dark over 24 hours, war-risk insurance at 8.0 times pre-crisis rates (approximately $2.5 million per VLCC) and eight of the nine largest container lines routing via the Cape of Good Hope. CENTCOM's tally of commercial vessels redirected since the blockade restart rose to 24.",
   "prediction_impact": "Predictions that the Gulf monarchies would be drawn into the war as belligerents remain PARTIALLY CONFIRMED and unmoved: Kuwait and Bahrain continue to absorb strikes aimed at US assets on their territory without declaring themselves parties, and Saudi Arabia's one direct combat contribution remains the July 28 joint strikes in Iraq. Predictions that the United States would organise the region's maritime defence remain DISCONFIRMED — Riyadh is building the Red Sea coalition and headquartering it, while Britain and France continue to decline the Hormuz lead. Predictions of catastrophic damage to Gulf energy infrastructure are CONFIRMED for the March 2026 wave, with up to $58 billion across more than 80 facilities, and Iran has now publicly named regional US energy infrastructure as its retaliation target should its own energy sites be struck — making the next round a stated, not inferred, risk. Predictions that closure of a single chokepoint could be routed around remain PARTIALLY CONFIRMED: the Iraq-Turkey pipeline provides 750,000 bpd around Hormuz, but the simultaneous Houthi pressure on Bab al-Mandeb removed the Red Sea alternative for Saudi westbound crude, with the Xin Long Yang and Rodos diversions documenting the effect. Predictions that a US ground campaign would be preceded by regional force generation remain DISCONFIRMED; the only mass movement organised this cycle was the departure of American civilians.",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "The near-simultaneous departure advisories across ten countries are being read both as pre-strike deconfliction and as generalised threat warning, and the two imply different timelines.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented: US embassies in at least ten Middle Eastern countries issued security alerts on August 1 advising Americans to consider departing or be prepared to depart, citing 'the potential for unforeseen escalation.' Documented: the alerts followed by one day reporting that a multi-day campaign against Iranian energy infrastructure could begin over the weekend, and Iran's declared plan to retaliate against US energy infrastructure in the region. Documented: no US strike wave began during the cycle. Not documented: any statement linking the advisories to a specific planned operation, or any ordered departure of official personnel or dependents, which is a distinct and more escalatory category than the advisory issued.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "The Week and Legal Insurrection, reporting the advisories against the imminent-bombardment framing",
      "PJ Media, framing them as embassies telling Americans to come home 'as Iran bombardment looms'",
      "The State Department's own alert text, which cites Iranian unpredictability and the expansion of attacks to new countries rather than any US operation"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "Pre-strike civilian deconfliction and generalised threat warning produce identical public documents. The distinguishing act — an ordered departure of embassy staff and dependents — was not taken in this cycle, which is evidence against the tightest pre-strike reading but does not exclude it."
    }
   ]
