   "source": "CNN via affiliates, 'Zelensky warns air defenses are being overwhelmed as Russia pounds Ukraine, killing 9,' August 1, 2026; The Washington Post, 'Zelensky says Ukraine has run out of interceptors as Russia hits Kyiv,' August 1, 2026; UPI, 'Russian strikes on Kyiv kill 9; Ukraine sinks Russian container ship,' August 1, 2026; Institute for the Study of War, 'Russian Offensive Campaign Assessment, July 31, 2026' (via Kyiv Post); Al Jazeera, 'Poland protests to Russian ambassador over missile incursion,' July 31, 2026; CDM, 'Russian Missile Strikes NATO Soil In Poland As Moscow Launches Widest Ukraine Assault Yet,' July 30, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Zelenskyy and Trump discuss Patriot deal and reviving Russian peace talks,' July 28, 2026; CSIS, 'Rebuilding U.S. Missile Inventory: A Multiyear Project,' 2026; CNN Politics, 'US weapons stockpiles continue to dwindle with permanent end to Iran war nowhere in sight,' July 29, 2026; Military Times, 'Iran war depleted US Patriot missile stockpiles, creating readiness challenges, experts say,' July 30, 2026.",
   "summary": "THE NUMBER THAT MATTERS IS ONE OF TWENTY-SEVEN. Russia's overnight barrage into August 1 killed at least nine people and wounded thirty across Kyiv and surrounding regions, with four children among the casualties. Ukrainian air defences shot down one of twenty-seven ballistic missiles. It was the second major aerial assault in two days and the second ballistic attack on Kyiv within a week, and Ukrainian officials read the pattern as a deliberate Russian effort to expose the shortage rather than to take ground — the Kremlin probing the one category of threat Ukraine cannot answer while its offensive stalls.\n\nZELENSKY SAID THE SHORTAGE OUT LOUD. The Ukrainian president warned that the country's air defences are being overwhelmed and said Ukraine has run out of interceptors, renewing his plea for Western air-defence supply. This is a harder statement than the appeals of previous cycles, which asked for more; this one describes exhaustion.\n\nAND HERE THE TWO THEATRES CONVERGE. The interceptor Ukraine lacks against ballistic missiles is Patriot. CSIS estimates that fewer than 827 Patriot interceptors remain in US stocks against roughly 2,200 pre-war — a reduction of about 65% — with more than 60% of both Patriot and THAAD expended in the war with Iran, and three or more years required to return to pre-war levels at a production rate that CSIS describes as a multiyear project. Three sources familiar with the matter told CNN the estimates are close to internal government figures. On July 28 Zelensky met Trump at the White House and discussed licences to produce Patriot interceptors in Ukraine, following Trump's earlier agreement to allow joint production. That request now sits against a supplier whose own inventory is the constraint. The audit records this as the first cycle in which the Iran war's munitions consumption is a documented, quantified limit on Ukrainian air defence rather than an inferred one.\n\nUKRAINE ANSWERED WHERE IT CAN. Ukrainian forces sank a Russian container ship in the Black Sea, continuing the asymmetric campaign — deep strikes on refineries and logistics, maritime attrition — that has substituted for territorial progress.\n\nTHE LINE STILL DOES NOT MOVE, AND ISW SAYS MOSCOW KNOWS IT. In its July 31 assessment the Institute for the Study of War judged that Russia's Northern Grouping of Forces and senior command are likely misreporting gains in northern Ukraine to the Kremlin, in order to sustain the narrative of advance across the entire theatre including a sector largely static for nearly two years. Available battlefield evidence does not corroborate Russian claims of advances in northern Sumy and Kharkiv oblasts, and Ukrainian forces are counterattacking in places. ISW places this within its standing assessment that Russia's spring-summer 2026 offensive has failed to achieve operationally significant gains, with the June 2026 rate of advance a fraction of June 2025's.\n\nNATO. Poland formally protested to the Russian ambassador over the July 30 Kh-101 cruise missile incursion that detonated in a field near Tarnawa-Kolonia outside Lublin, leaving a crater roughly ten metres wide. Warsaw asserts the missile was recently manufactured — a claim that cuts against the argument that Russia's nightly barrages are drawing down an unreplenishable arsenal. Poland did not invoke Article 4.",
   "details": "CASUALTY AND INTERCEPT DETAIL. At least nine killed and thirty wounded in the overnight attack into August 1, with four children among the casualties; Kyiv and surrounding regions were the main targets. One of twenty-seven ballistic missiles intercepted. This follows the July 30 barrage of more than 70 missiles and roughly 280 drones in which Ukraine downed 55 of the missiles and 265 of the drones but only one of nine ballistic missiles. Across two consecutive barrages the ballistic intercept rate is two of thirty-six.\n\nWHY THE BALLISTIC GAP IS THE PATRIOT GAP. Ukraine's layered defence performs well against cruise missiles and Shahed-type drones — intercept rates in the high eighties to mid nineties across both barrages — and poorly against ballistic missiles, which in Ukrainian service only the Patriot system engages effectively. The intercept ratio is therefore a direct read-out of Patriot interceptor availability, and it has now collapsed twice in three days.\n\nTHE SUPPLY-SIDE ARITHMETIC. CSIS: fewer than 827 Patriot interceptors remaining against roughly 2,200 pre-war of the two most-modernised variants; fewer than 278 THAAD against 452; more than 60% of key interceptors fired during the Iran war; three or more years to pre-war levels; $18.2 billion allocated for Patriot, THAAD and Tomahawk within a $67 billion FY2026 emergency request. CSIS's stated strategic concern is the ability to respond to another high-intensity contingency before inventories are rebuilt. Ukraine is, in effect, that contingency already in progress.\n\nISW ASSESSMENT IN FULL. The Northern Grouping of Forces and high military command are likely misrepresenting the extent of gains in northern Ukraine to the Kremlin, furthering a narrative of theatre-wide advance including in a sector largely stagnant for nearly two years. ISW characterises the exaggeration as part of a cognitive-warfare effort intended to persuade Ukraine's partners to abandon support. Battlefield evidence does not corroborate claimed advances in northern Sumy and Kharkiv oblasts; Ukrainian forces are counterattacking in some areas.\n\nSTANDING TERRITORIAL CONTEXT (unchanged this cycle). The Economist recorded zero square miles of Russian gain over the thirty days to July 28; DeepState recorded a net ten square miles over four weeks against 31 in the prior period. Russia's claimed capture of Kostiantynivka on July 3, announced to Putin by Chief of the General Staff Valery Gerasimov, remains contested, with CNN reporting on July 16 that the claim was false and Ukraine denying it.",
   "prediction_impact": "Predictions of a Russian operational breakthrough in Donbas remain DISCONFIRMED, and ISW's July 31 assessment strengthens the verdict from an unusual direction: not only is Russia not advancing at scale, its own field command is assessed to be misreporting advances upward to the Kremlin. Predictions of a negotiated settlement in 2026 remain UNTESTED; the July 28 Trump-Zelensky White House meeting produced discussion of Patriot production licences and reviving talks with Moscow, and nothing further moved this cycle. Predictions that NATO would be drawn into direct conflict by a spillover incident remain DISCONFIRMED for a second consecutive cycle: Poland protested to the Russian ambassador over the Kh-101 detonation and did not invoke Article 4. Predictions of Ukrainian air-defence collapse under sustained bombardment are moving from UNTESTED toward PARTIALLY CONFIRMED on the ballistic axis specifically: two of thirty-six ballistic missiles intercepted across two barrages, and a presidential statement that interceptors have run out. The cross-theatre finding is the one the corpus most consistently misses — corpus predictions treat the Iran and Ukraine theatres as independent, and this cycle documents a hard coupling: more than 60% of US Patriot and THAAD stocks were expended defending against Iran, and the resulting three-year replenishment horizon is now a measurable constraint on what Washington can supply Kyiv."
  },
