   "source": "The Wall Street Journal via The Times of Israel liveblog, 'Trump orders fresh strikes with aim of forcing Iran to surrender,' July 31, 2026; CBS News, 'U.S., Israel preparing to bombard energy-related targets in Iran, sources say,' July 31, 2026; Bloomberg, 'Trump Orders Iran Attack as Soon as This Weekend, WSJ Says,' July 31, 2026; CNN live blog, 'US-Iran war; Trump admin plans fresh strikes as soon as this weekend, officials say,' July 31, 2026; Axios, 'Trump considering strikes on Iranian energy targets within days,' July 31, 2026; Haaretz live blog, 'Report: U.S., Israel Preparing Extensive Strikes on Iranian Energy Sites as Soon as This Weekend,' July 31, 2026; ABC News live updates, 'Trump says he is losing faith with Iran, but does not rule out deal,' July 31, 2026; CNBC, 'Iran targets strategic U.S. assets in Kuwait, Bahrain after Egypt drone attack,' July 31, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'IRGC strikes US targets in Kuwait a day after US hits Iran: Latest events,' July 31, 2026; Middle East Monitor, \"Iranian army says drones target key US facilities at Bahrain's Sheikh Isa Air Base,\" July 30-31, 2026; IranWire, 'Iran Claims Drone Attacks on US Bases in Bahrain and Jordan,' July 31, 2026; Middle East Monitor, \"Iran's Araghchi: Egypt is an important friend, its security is of utmost importance, warns of 'Israeli conspiracy',\" July 31, 2026; Press TV, \"Egypt rejects reports linking Iran to Damietta drone attack,\" July 31, 2026; The Defense Post, 'Egypt Analyzing Drone Fragments from Port Strike, Warns of Escalation,' July 31, 2026; Democracy Now!, 'Iran Claims Drone Attack on Egypt May Have Been False Flag,' July 31, 2026; TradingEconomics Brent crude, July 31, 2026; straits.live Strait of Hormuz status board, 31 JUL 2026 2259Z; S&P Global via webindia123, 'Hormuz vessel transits hit two-week high as LNG tanker traffic resumes,' July 31, 2026; AAA Gas Prices national averages, July 31, 2026.",
   "summary": "Day 156 is the cycle in which the United States settled on an instrument. After five months in which the coercive options debated in public included seizing Kharg Island with Marines and paratroopers, the reported plan for forcing Iran to accept American terms is a multi-day air campaign against the country's energy and civil infrastructure.\n\nTHE PLAN. The Wall Street Journal reported that President Trump has ordered another wave of strikes against Iran with the aim of forcing the Islamic Republic's surrender, that the bombing is expected to last several days, and that it could begin as soon as this weekend. CBS News reported that the United States and Israel are preparing to bombard energy-related targets, naming power plants and refineries as likely, with electricity infrastructure across Tehran under high-level discussion and bridges of dual military-civilian use also on the list. The plan was raised at Trump's Friday cabinet meeting at Camp David, where he said: \"We'll be hitting them very hard. At some point, they're going to say, 'We just can't take it anymore.'\" CBS reported strikes were possible throughout the weekend, that an end point was not locked in, and that there was discussion of concluding before financial markets opened Monday. White House aides focused on politics were, per one source, \"strongly opposed.\" CNN, citing two US officials, reported fresh strikes as soon as this weekend while noting the scope and targets were not clear and that the strikes could be called off.\n\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY CONTESTED IS WHETHER IT HAS BEEN ORDERED. The WSJ framing is that Trump has ordered it. CBS reported that the president has not yet given final authorisation. An Israeli official said: \"Israel is unaware of a decision to restart full military operations, nor has been requested of Israel to join any military actions against Iran\" — this against multiple US sources saying Israel had been notified and was coordinating. The project records the reporting, not a resolution.\n\nIRAN'S ANSWER CAME BY DRONE, ACROSS THE GULF, AGAINST BASES. Iran's army said it struck US facilities at Sheikh Isa Air Base in southern Bahrain — which it called \"one of the most important US military installations in the Gulf region\" — with one-way attack drones aimed at power generators, navigation systems and administrative and support buildings, framing the operation as retaliation for the killing of an Iranian Air Force Su-24 pilot at the start of the war in February. The IRGC and Iran's army separately claimed attacks on Ahmad al-Jaber and Ali al-Salem air bases in Kuwait — targeting aircraft shelters, satellite communication systems and equipment storage facilities — and on Al Azraq in Jordan. Iran claimed its recent attacks had inflicted \"significant damage\"; there was no immediate comment from Bahraini or US authorities, and no casualties were reported.\n\nTHE HUMAN LEDGER MOVED ON BOTH SIDES. The running US tally reached 685 wounded and 18 killed in action, a rise of 32 wounded from the 653 reported the previous evening. On the Iranian side, US Central Command acknowledged that it is investigating reports that an Iranian two-year-old and the child's parents were killed in the July 30 strike on Qeshm, a CENTCOM spokesman saying \"The U.S. military never targets civilians, unlike Iranian forces.\"\n\nDAMIETTA MOVED AWAY FROM TEHRAN, NOT TOWARD IT. Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi denied Iranian involvement in the July 29 drone attack on Egypt's Damietta port, calling Egypt \"an important friend and partner in the region\" and warning: \"We must all be vigilant against Israeli plots and false-flag operations designed to undermine regional peace.\" Egypt's Minister of State for Information Diaa Rashwan publicly dismissed a Wall Street Journal report that Cairo believed Iran responsible; Prime Minister Mostafa Madbouly said security agencies were examining recovered drone debris. Cairo has condemned the strike, warned against escalation, and declined to name anyone.\n\nTHE MARKET PRICED THE THREAT, NOT A RUPTURE. Brent settled at $90.24, up $3.36 or 3.87% on the day, closing July with a gain of roughly 24% — its strongest month since March — with analysts projecting $101.25 by quarter-end. AAA's national average for regular reached $4.1060 and diesel $5.3530, another war high and the seventh consecutive daily rise in diesel. On the water the picture improved marginally: S&P Global recorded 28 Hormuz transits on July 29, the highest since July 14, as LNG tanker traffic resumed after a two-week gap, with Iran-linked vessels falling to 60% of movements from a 68% second-half-of-July average. The straits.live board still assessed commercial transit at 11% of the pre-crisis norm, with 85 AIS-dark tankers against a 42.4 seven-day baseline and war-risk insurance at 8.0x peacetime rates.",
   "details": [
    "TRUMP ORDERS A MULTI-DAY CAMPAIGN AIMED AT IRANIAN SURRENDER (July 31, 2026): The Wall Street Journal, citing US officials, reported that President Trump has ordered another wave of strikes against Iran with the aim of forcing the Islamic Republic's surrender; the bombing is expected to last several days and could start as soon as this weekend. (The Wall Street Journal via The Times of Israel liveblog; Bloomberg, July 31, 2026.)",
    "THE TARGET CLASSES ARE ENERGY AND CIVIL INFRASTRUCTURE (July 31, 2026): CBS News reported that the United States and Israel are preparing to bombard energy-related targets in Iran, with power plants and refineries the likely target set, electricity infrastructure across Tehran under high-level discussion but no decision as of Friday, and bridges of dual military-civilian purpose also under consideration. Strikes were described as possible throughout the weekend, with an end point not locked in and discussion of concluding before financial markets opened Monday. (CBS News; Axios; Haaretz, July 31, 2026.)",
    "THE PLAN WAS RAISED AT A CAMP DAVID CABINET MEETING (July 31, 2026): Trump raised the strike plan at his Friday cabinet meeting at Camp David, saying: \"We'll be hitting them very hard. At some point, they're going to say, 'We just can't take it anymore.'\" One source said White House aides focused on politics \"were strongly opposed.\" (CBS News, July 31, 2026.)",
    "THE AUTHORISATION STATUS IS DISPUTED ACROSS OUTLETS (July 31, 2026): The WSJ reported the strikes as ordered. CBS News reported that Trump had not yet given final authorisation and that the plan remained pending his decision. CNN, citing two US officials, reported fresh strikes as soon as this weekend while cautioning that the precise scope and targets were unclear and that the strikes could be called off. An Israeli official said: \"Israel is unaware of a decision to restart full military operations, nor has been requested of Israel to join any military actions against Iran,\" against multiple US sources saying Israel had been notified and was coordinating. (The Wall Street Journal; CBS News; CNN; Bloomberg, July 31, 2026.)",
    "TRUMP SAYS HE IS 'LOSING FAITH' BUT DOES NOT RULE OUT A DEAL (July 31, 2026): Asked whether a deal remained possible, Trump said: \"Yes, you can. I mean, I'm losing faith in them because they do lie,\" and separately that \"Iran is doing very poorly... they're doing very, very poorly.\" (ABC News live updates, July 31, 2026, 5:19 PM GMT.)",
    "IRAN RETALIATES BY DRONE AGAINST US FACILITIES IN BAHRAIN (July 31, 2026): Iran's army said its one-way attack drones struck US assets at Sheikh Isa Air Base in southern Bahrain — described by Iran's military as \"one of the most important US military installations in the Gulf region\" — targeting power generators, navigation systems, and administrative and support buildings. Iran stated the operation was carried out \"in retaliation for the killing of an Iranian Air Force Su-24 pilot at the start of the US-Israeli war on Iran in February,\" and claimed its recent attacks had caused \"significant damage\" and \"serious damage to the base's combat and logistical support capabilities.\" There was no immediate comment from Bahraini or US authorities. (Middle East Monitor; IranWire; CNBC, July 30-31, 2026.)",
    "IRAN RETALIATES BY DRONE AGAINST US FACILITIES IN KUWAIT AND JORDAN (July 31, 2026): Iran's army said it targeted aircraft shelters, satellite communication systems and equipment storage facilities used by the US military at Kuwait's Ahmad al-Jaber Air Base in a drone strike; the IRGC separately claimed attacks on US assets at Ali al-Salem Air Base in Kuwait and Al Azraq Air Base in Jordan, all framed as responses to the US strikes of the night of July 29-30. Damage and casualty details were not established. (CNBC; Al Jazeera; IranWire; Siasat, July 31, 2026.)",
    "US CASUALTY TALLY RISES TO 685 WOUNDED, 18 KILLED (July 31, 2026): The running US casualty tally reached 685 wounded and 18 killed in action, up 32 wounded from the 653 reported at 9:06 PM GMT on July 30, which had itself been 11 higher than Tuesday's figure. (ABC News live updates, July 30-31, 2026.)",
    "CENTCOM CONFIRMS AN INVESTIGATION INTO THE QESHM CIVILIAN DEATHS (July 31, 2026): US Central Command acknowledged that it is investigating reports that an Iranian two-year-old and the child's parents were killed in the July 30 strike on Qeshm Island. A CENTCOM spokesman said: \"The U.S. military never targets civilians, unlike Iranian forces.\" (ABC News live updates, July 31, 2026, 5:35 PM GMT.)",
    "IRAN DENIES DAMIETTA AND ALLEGES AN ISRAELI FALSE FLAG (July 30-31, 2026): Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi denied Iranian involvement in the drone attack on Egypt's Damietta port, writing that \"Egypt is an important friend and partner in the region\" and that Cairo's security is of utmost importance to Tehran, and warning: \"We must all be vigilant against Israeli plots and false-flag operations designed to undermine regional peace.\" (Middle East Monitor; Press TV; Tasnim; Democracy Now!, July 30-31, 2026.)",
    "EGYPT PUBLICLY REJECTS REPORTING THAT CAIRO BLAMES IRAN (July 31, 2026): Egypt's Minister of State for Information Diaa Rashwan dismissed a Wall Street Journal report claiming the Egyptian government believed Iran was responsible for the Damietta attack. Egyptian authorities said no group had claimed responsibility, that drone wreckage had been recovered for forensic analysis, and that the investigation was ongoing; Prime Minister Mostafa Madbouly said security agencies were examining the debris to determine its origin. Cairo condemned the strike and warned against further escalation without assigning responsibility. (Press TV; The Defense Post; Al Arabiya via Cryptobriefing, July 31, 2026.)",
    "BRENT CLOSES JULY UP ROUGHLY 24% (July 31, 2026): Brent settled at $90.24 per barrel, up $3.36 or 3.87% on the day, recording a gain of nearly 24% for July — its strongest monthly increase since March. TradingEconomics noted analyst projections of $101.25 by quarter-end. The straits.live board recorded $90.24 with a 24-hour change of +2.36% at 20:52 UTC, against a pre-crisis baseline of about $72 (+25.3%). (TradingEconomics; straits.live, July 31, 2026.)",
    "PUMP PRICES: DIESEL SETS ANOTHER WAR HIGH (July 31, 2026): AAA's national average for regular gasoline was $4.1060, up from $4.0980 the previous day, against $4.1050 a week earlier, $3.8470 a month earlier and $3.1470 a year earlier. Diesel reached $5.3530, up from $5.3390, against $5.2400 a week earlier, $4.8530 a month earlier and $3.7410 a year earlier — another war high and still below the June 2022 record of $5.816. (AAA Gas Prices, July 31, 2026.)",
    "HORMUZ TRANSITS HIT A TWO-WEEK HIGH AS LNG TRAFFIC RESUMES (July 29-31, 2026): S&P Global recorded 28 vessel transits through the Strait of Hormuz on July 29, the highest since July 14, driven by resumed LNG tanker traffic after a two-week gap along with several dark transits; Iran-linked vessels made up 60% of movements, down from a 68% average in the second half of July. Bab el-Mandeb crossings rose to 39, while VLCCs increasingly gathered at Egypt's Sidi Kerir terminal, complicating voyage logistics. Separately, Iran's Fars news agency said the Revolutionary Guards stopped two tankers from passing through the waterway while four others altered course. (S&P Global via webindia123; TradingEconomics, July 31, 2026.)",
    "THE STRAITS.LIVE BOARD REVERTS TO ITS PRIOR FRAMING AFTER THE FLAGGED DISCONTINUITY (July 31, 2026, 2259Z): The tracker showed Brent $90.24; latest recorded daily transit volume of 10 vessels on July 23 against a typical pre-crisis 88 per day, or 11% of normal throughput; 1,364 vessels stranded or anchored in the zone (29-hour range 1,105-1,397, up 109 on 29 hours earlier); and 85 AIS-dark tankers in the last 24 hours against a 7-day baseline of 42.4, a 100% increase. War-risk insurance for supertankers was reported at 8.0x peacetime rates, and prediction markets priced a 1% probability of normalised traffic by August 1, 2026. The reversion to the 10-transits/88-baseline framing supports the prior cycle's assessment that the July 30 readings reflected a measurement change rather than a real surge. (straits.live, July 31, 2026.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "The corpus-predicted US GROUND seizure of Hormuz or Kharg Island remains DISCONFIRMED at Day 156, and this cycle supplies the clearest evidence yet on the question of instrument. For five months the publicly debated route to forcing Iranian capitulation included an amphibious and airborne seizure of Kharg — reported from March 2026 as a Pentagon option involving two Marine Expeditionary Units and roughly 1,000-2,000 soldiers of the 82nd Airborne. The plan now reported for that same objective, across four independent outlets, is a multi-day AIR campaign against power plants, refineries, Tehran's electricity grid and dual-use bridges. No ground element appears in any version of the reporting; no landing force, embarkation order, MEU tasking or ARG repositioning was named. Kharg Island was not identified as a target and has never been seized. This does not foreclose a ground phase, but it is the first cycle in which the reported coercive plan is stated in full and contains no ground component. Predictions of a negotiated Hormuz transit regime remain LIVE BUT UNMOVED: Iran's rejection of the Omani 50-50 plan stands, its counterproposal for full control of the inbound lane stands, and the reported American answer is escalation rather than concession. Predictions that the war would remain confined to the Gulf littoral remain DISCONFIRMED, though this cycle pulled marginally the other way on Egypt: Tehran denied Damietta and Cairo publicly rejected reporting that it blames Iran, leaving the Mediterranean incident unattributed rather than annexed to the Iranian campaign. Predictions of a supply-driven oil shock above $130 remain DISCONFIRMED at $90.24, even with a surrender-forcing energy campaign reported as imminent — the market is pricing the announced destruction of Iranian refining capacity as a bounded event.",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "Whether the President has ORDERED the multi-day energy campaign or is still weighing it is reported differently by outlets working the same story on the same day, and the discrepancy is not a matter of emphasis.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented: the Wall Street Journal reported, citing US officials, that Trump has ordered another wave of strikes aimed at forcing Iran's surrender, expected to last several days and able to start as soon as this weekend. Documented: CBS News, citing multiple US sources, reported that the president has not yet given final authorisation and that the plan remains pending his decision. Documented: CNN, citing two US officials, reported planning for strikes as soon as this weekend while stating the scope and targets were unclear and the strikes could be called off. Documented: an Israeli official said Israel 'is unaware of a decision to restart full military operations, nor has been requested of Israel to join any military actions against Iran,' while multiple US sources told CBS that Israel had been notified and was coordinating. Not documented: any execute order, tasking message, ATO, deployment order or on-record statement by Trump, Hegseth or CENTCOM confirming or denying an order.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "The Wall Street Journal, reporting the campaign as ordered, relayed by Bloomberg and The Times of Israel",
      "CBS News, reporting that final authorisation has not been given",
      "CNN, reporting planning without a decision and noting the strikes could be called off",
      "An unnamed Israeli official, stating Israel is unaware of a decision",
      "President Trump, who described intent ('We'll be hitting them very hard') without confirming an order"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "'Ordered' and 'planned but not authorised' produce an identical public signature in the interval before execution: the same planning traffic, the same allied notification, the same presidential rhetoric. Sourcing on both sides is anonymous US officials with access to different points in the decision chain. Only execution, a formal announcement, a War Powers notification to Congress, or an on-record denial would separate them.",
     "open_question_ref": "iran-energy-strike-campaign-order-status-july-2026"
    },
    {
     "reading": "Iran's claim of 'significant damage' to US bases in Kuwait, Bahrain and Jordan is met with silence rather than denial, repeating the evidentiary structure of the Al Azraq F-35 claim.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented: Iran's army and the IRGC issued specific target descriptions for Sheikh Isa (power generators, navigation systems, administrative and support buildings), Ahmad al-Jaber and Ali al-Salem (aircraft shelters, satellite communication systems, equipment storage) and Al Azraq. Documented: Iran asserted the attacks caused 'significant damage' and 'serious damage to the base's combat and logistical support capabilities.' Documented: there was no immediate comment from Bahraini or US authorities. Not documented: any battle-damage assessment, imagery, casualty report or denial from CENTCOM or the host governments for this cycle. Precedent from the prior cycle: CENTCOM categorically denied Iran's claim of three F-35s destroyed at Al Azraq while remaining silent on the claimed personnel casualties.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "Iran's army public relations office and the IRGC, asserting significant damage",
      "Middle East Monitor, IranWire and CNBC, reporting the claims while noting the absence of US or host-nation comment",
      "CENTCOM, which in the prior cycle denied aircraft losses while not addressing personnel claims"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "Base damage on host-nation soil is not independently observable; Gulf host governments have consistently declined to detail damage from Iranian attacks, and the United States has answered only the specific claims it chose to. Absence of denial is not corroboration, and absence of imagery is not refutation. Only released imagery, a formal BDA, or host-government disclosure would resolve it.",
     "open_question_ref": "irgc-al-azraq-f35-casualty-claim-july-2026"
    },
    {
     "reading": "The Damietta attack is now denied by Iran, unattributed by Egypt, and publicly rejected by Cairo as an Iranian operation — against a Wall Street Journal report that the Egyptian government privately believes Iran did it.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented: Araghchi denied Iranian involvement, called Egypt 'an important friend and partner in the region,' and alleged possible Israeli false-flag activity. Documented: Egypt's Minister of State for Information Diaa Rashwan publicly dismissed a WSJ report that Cairo believed Iran responsible. Documented: Egypt recovered drone wreckage for forensic analysis, Prime Minister Madbouly said agencies were examining the debris, no party has claimed responsibility, and Egypt has condemned the strike without naming anyone. Documented from the prior cycle: President Trump blamed Iran without presenting evidence. Not documented: any published debris analysis, launch track, radar assessment or on-record intelligence finding by any government.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi, denying involvement and alleging an Israeli false flag",
      "Egypt's Minister of State for Information Diaa Rashwan, rejecting the WSJ characterisation",
      "The Wall Street Journal, reporting that the Egyptian government believed Iran responsible",
      "President Trump, attributing the attack to Iran without presenting evidence",
      "The Foundation for Defense of Democracies, reading the strike as part of an expanding Iranian pressure campaign"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "A single unclaimed one-way drone against a fixed port target leaves an identical public signature regardless of launch origin, and every actor with an interest has now stated a position that its own incentives predict. Cairo gains from non-attribution, Tehran from denial, Washington from attribution. Only published forensic or track data, or a claim of responsibility, would resolve it.",
     "open_question_ref": "damietta-drone-attribution-july-2026"
    }
   ]
