   "source": "The National, \"Saudi Arabia proposes 43-nation 'maritime defence coalition' in Red Sea,\" July 30, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Saudi Arabia announces maritime defence alliance to secure vital waterways,' July 30, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Why is Saudi Arabia forming a coalition to protect the Red Sea?', July 30, 2026; Bloomberg, 'Saudi Arabia Seeks to Lead Maritime Coalition to Protect Red Sea,' July 30, 2026; The Hill, 'Saudi Arabia forms Red Sea defense coalition to protect against Houthi attacks,' July 30, 2026; MarineLink, 'Saudi Arabia Launches Multinational Maritime Defense Coalition Plans,' July 30, 2026; Dawn, 'Riyadh unveils maritime alliance as war spreads,' July 31, 2026; Middle East Monitor, \"Iraq denies Trump's claim of prior knowledge of US-Saudi strikes,\" July 31, 2026; Al Jazeera, \"Iraq calls Saudi-US attacks a 'flagrant violation of sovereignty',\" July 29, 2026; The Washington Times, \"U.S.-Saudi strikes on Iraq's pro-Iran militias underscore Baghdad's fragile grip on country,\" July 29, 2026; Rudaw, \"Iraq's Iran-aligned factions condemn Saudi-US airstrikes on PMF bases,\" July 29, 2026; CNBC, 'Iran targets strategic U.S. assets in Kuwait, Bahrain after Egypt drone attack,' July 31, 2026; Press TV, 'Egypt rejects reports linking Iran to Damietta drone attack,' July 31, 2026; The Defense Post, 'Egypt Analyzing Drone Fragments from Port Strike, Warns of Escalation,' July 31, 2026; TechTimes, 'Houthi Strike Burns Jizan Aramco Refinery; Yanbu Attack Seals Saudi Arabia's Oil Trap,' July 25, 2026; POLITICO on the Hormuz naval coalition, July 28-29, 2026.",
   "summary": "The most consequential Gulf development of Day 156 is organisational rather than kinetic, and it inverts the coalition story of the past week.\n\nRIYADH RAISES THE COALITION WASHINGTON COULD NOT. Saudi Arabia's Defence Ministry convened military representatives of 43 countries in Riyadh on Thursday, July 30, to stand up a multinational maritime defence coalition. Its declared purpose is to enhance maritime security, safeguard freedom of navigation, secure international trade routes and energy supply lines, and counter threats to maritime navigation and global trade, with a geographic focus on the Bab al-Mandeb strait, the Red Sea and the Gulf of Aden. Proposed activities are joint maritime operations, intelligence sharing, training and joint exercises. The headquarters would sit in the kingdom. Thirteen countries publicly backed the initiative in a joint statement — Kuwait, Bahrain, Qatar, Pakistan, Turkey, Egypt, Jordan, Yemen, Bangladesh, Nigeria, Sudan, Djibouti and Somalia — a list that spans the Gulf, South Asia, the Levant, the Horn of Africa and West Africa.\n\nTHE CONTRAST IS THE POINT. Two days earlier, POLITICO reported that Secretary Hegseth was urging Britain and France to help lead the American naval coalition for the Strait of Hormuz and that European allies were refusing until they saw an enduring ceasefire in the Iran war, with no movement expected soon. A US-led escort coalition for the Gulf cannot find a European co-lead; a Saudi-led coalition for the Red Sea drew 43 delegations in a week. The driver is that the threat is now Saudi Arabia's own: the Houthis declared a maritime blockade of the kingdom on July 20, and Yanbu — the outlet of the East-West Pipeline and the only functioning Saudi crude-export route since Hormuz closed — handled roughly 92% of Saudi seaborne crude exports in June 2026. Every barrel leaving Yanbu must transit the Bab al-Mandeb, inside the declared blockade zone.\n\nTHE HOST STATES TOOK ANOTHER NIGHT OF IRANIAN FIRE. Iran's army and the IRGC claimed one-way attack drones against US facilities at Kuwait's Ahmad al-Jaber and Ali al-Salem air bases and Bahrain's Sheikh Isa Air Base, targeting aircraft shelters, satellite communications, power generators, navigation systems and equipment storage. Neither Manama nor Washington commented on damage. The pattern of the war holds: the Gulf states absorb the retaliation earned by American strikes launched from or supported by their territory, and decline to publicise what it costs them.\n\nBAGHDAD CONTRADICTS THE PRESIDENT. Iraq said it had no prior knowledge of the July 28 US-Saudi strikes on Popular Mobilisation Forces positions, directly contradicting Trump's statement that the operation had been coordinated with Baghdad. Iraq's National Security Council, chaired by Prime Minister Ali al-Zaidi, called the attacks on PMF bases across seven provinces \"a flagrant violation of Iraq's sovereignty and the sanctity of its lands\" and \"an aggression\" contrary to international law and the UN Charter. Baghdad suspended al-Zaidi's planned visit to Saudi Arabia. The prime minister has separately committed to disarming the armed groups and bringing weapons under state control by September 30, the date on which US forces are scheduled to withdraw.\n\nEGYPT DECLINES THE ATTRIBUTION OFFERED TO IT. Cairo publicly rejected reporting that the Egyptian government believes Iran carried out the July 29 Damietta drone attack, recovered wreckage for forensic analysis, and warned against escalation while naming no one.",
   "details": [
    "SAUDI ARABIA CONVENES 43 NATIONS FOR A MARITIME DEFENCE COALITION (July 30, 2026): Saudi Arabia's Defence Ministry hosted military representatives of 43 countries in Riyadh to discuss standing up a multinational maritime defence coalition, aiming to 'enhance maritime security, protect international maritime corridors, and counter threats targeting maritime navigation and global trade.' The alliance would be headquartered in Saudi Arabia and focus on the Bab al-Mandeb strait, the Red Sea and the Gulf of Aden, with joint maritime operations, intelligence sharing, training programmes and joint exercises. (The National; Al Jazeera; Bloomberg; MarineLink; The Hill, July 30, 2026.)",
    "THIRTEEN COUNTRIES PUBLICLY BACKED THE INITIATIVE (July 30, 2026): Kuwait, Bahrain, Qatar, Pakistan, Turkey, Egypt, Jordan, Yemen, Bangladesh, Nigeria, Sudan, Djibouti and Somalia expressed support, with a joint statement issued by the backing states. (Al Jazeera; The National, July 30, 2026.)",
    "THE HORMUZ COALITION STILL CANNOT FIND A EUROPEAN CO-LEAD (July 28-31, 2026): POLITICO reported that Secretary of Defense Hegseth is urging the United Kingdom and France to help lead the American naval coalition for the Strait of Hormuz, with UK Defence Minister Wes Streeting assisting, and that European allies are refusing until they see an enduring ceasefire in the Iran war; four British officials and one US official said no movement was expected soon. No change was reported this cycle. (POLITICO, July 28-29, 2026.)",
    "THE DRIVER IS THE HOUTHI BLOCKADE OF SAUDI ARABIA (July 20-31, 2026): The Houthis declared a maritime blockade of the kingdom on July 20, accusing Saudi leaders of imposing 'an unjust and oppressive siege' on Yemen for nearly twelve years, and have since claimed attacks on the Saudi tankers Encelia, Layla and NCC Ghazal. Yanbu, the outlet of the East-West Pipeline, handled roughly 92% of Saudi seaborne crude exports in June 2026 after the closure of the Strait of Hormuz, and every cargo loading there must transit the Bab al-Mandeb. (Al Jazeera; The Hill; TechTimes, July 20-30, 2026.)",
    "IRANIAN DRONES STRIKE US FACILITIES IN KUWAIT AND BAHRAIN (July 31, 2026): Iran's army said it targeted aircraft shelters, satellite communication systems and equipment storage facilities used by the US military at Kuwait's Ahmad al-Jaber Air Base; the IRGC claimed attacks on US assets at Ali al-Salem Air Base in Kuwait; and Iran's army claimed drone strikes on power generators, navigation systems and administrative and support buildings at Bahrain's Sheikh Isa Air Base. No comment on damage came from Bahraini or US authorities. (CNBC; Al Jazeera; Middle East Monitor; IranWire, July 31, 2026.)",
    "IRAQ DENIES TRUMP'S CLAIM OF PRIOR KNOWLEDGE (July 31, 2026): The Iraqi government said it had no prior knowledge of the July 28 US and Saudi strikes on Popular Mobilisation Forces positions, contradicting President Trump's statement that the operation had been coordinated with Baghdad. (Middle East Monitor, July 31, 2026.)",
    "IRAQ'S NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL CALLS THE STRIKES AN AGGRESSION (July 29-31, 2026): Iraq's National Security Council, chaired by Prime Minister Ali al-Zaidi, called the attacks on PMF bases in seven Iraqi provinces 'a flagrant violation of Iraq's sovereignty and the sanctity of its lands' and 'an aggression' in contravention of international law and the UN Charter. Baghdad suspended al-Zaidi's planned visit to Saudi Arabia. The prime minister has committed to disarming armed groups and restricting weapons to state control by September 30, coinciding with the scheduled withdrawal of US forces from Iraq. (Al Jazeera; The Washington Times; Rudaw; Foreign Policy, July 29-31, 2026.)",
    "EGYPT REJECTS ATTRIBUTION OF DAMIETTA TO IRAN (July 31, 2026): Egypt's Minister of State for Information Diaa Rashwan dismissed a Wall Street Journal report claiming the Egyptian government believed Iran was responsible for the Damietta drone attack. Egyptian authorities said no group had claimed responsibility, recovered drone wreckage for forensic analysis, and warned against further regional escalation; Prime Minister Mostafa Madbouly said security agencies were examining the debris to determine its origin. (Press TV; The Defense Post; Al Arabiya, July 31, 2026.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "Predictions of Gulf-state realignment away from the United States remain PARTIALLY CONFIRMED and gained their clearest institutional evidence this cycle. Saudi Arabia raised 43 delegations for a coalition it will headquarter and lead, covering the Red Sea and Bab al-Mandeb, in the same week that the American-led Hormuz escort coalition could not obtain a British or French co-lead. That is not a break with Washington — Riyadh flew strikes alongside CENTCOM three days earlier and is lobbying for F-35s — but it is the assumption of a security-provider role rather than a security-consumer one, in the theatre where Saudi interests are now most exposed. Predictions of Gulf-state acquiescence in US operations from their territory remain CONFIRMED in practice and CONTESTED in politics: Kuwait and Bahrain again absorbed Iranian retaliation for American strikes and again declined to detail the damage. Predictions that the war would fracture the US-Iraq relationship gained ground: Baghdad publicly contradicted the President of the United States on whether it had been consulted about strikes on its own territory, suspended a bilateral visit to Riyadh, and reaffirmed a September 30 deadline to disarm Iran-aligned militias that coincides with the scheduled US withdrawal.",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "Whether Baghdad was consulted before the July 28 US-Saudi strikes is asserted flatly and incompatibly by the two governments involved.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented: President Trump stated the operation had been coordinated with Baghdad. Documented: the Iraqi government stated it had no prior knowledge of the strikes. Documented: Iraq's National Security Council, chaired by PM Ali al-Zaidi, called the strikes 'a flagrant violation of Iraq's sovereignty' and 'an aggression'; Baghdad summoned both the US charge d'affaires and the Iranian ambassador and suspended al-Zaidi's planned visit to Saudi Arabia. Not documented: any notification record, readout, call transcript or third-party account establishing whether, when and at what level Iraq was informed.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "President Trump, stating the strikes were coordinated with Baghdad",
      "The Government of Iraq, denying prior knowledge",
      "Iraq's National Security Council under PM Ali al-Zaidi, characterising the strikes as an aggression and a sovereignty violation",
      "Iran-aligned Iraqi factions, condemning the strikes as a 'dangerous escalation'"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "Both claims can be simultaneously defensible depending on the level and timing of any notification: a warning to one official minutes before execution can be described in Washington as coordination and in Baghdad as no prior knowledge, and a government under domestic pressure from the targeted militias has a strong incentive to deny consultation whether or not it occurred. No documentary record has been published by either side.",
     "open_question_ref": "iraq-prior-knowledge-us-saudi-strikes-july-2026"
    }
   ]
