   ],
   "source": "The Washington Post, 'Russian missile landed in Poland amid strikes on Ukraine, NATO says,' July 30, 2026; Stars and Stripes, 'Russia missile lands in Poland as NATO scrambles in response,' July 30, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'NATO jets scramble as Russian missile detonates in Poland,' July 30, 2026; The Irish Times, \"Nato says it will 'defend territory' after Russian missile crashes in Poland,\" July 30, 2026; Foreign Policy, 'NATO Says Russian Missile Violated Polish Airspace,' July 30, 2026; CNN, 'Poland blames Russia for firing missile into its territory as Moscow unleashes deadly attack on Ukraine,' July 29-30, 2026; NPR, 'Russian strikes across Ukraine kill 10 and a missile falls in Poland,' July 30, 2026; TIME, 'Russian Bombardment Kills at Least Eight in Ukraine as Zelensky Renews Plea for Air Defense Supplies,' July 30, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'At least 8 killed as Russia strikes Ukraine a day after Zelenskyy warning,' July 30, 2026; Mezha/Bukvy, 'Ukraine strikes Russian logistics and oil infrastructure in long-range sanction raids,' July 31, 2026; Charter'97, 'Zelenskyy Announced More Diplomatic Strikes Against Russia,' July 30, 2026; Institute for the Study of War, 'Russian Offensive Campaign Assessment,' July 30, 2026; Russia Matters, 'The Russia-Ukraine War Report Card, July 29, 2026'; NPR, 'Senior Ukraine negotiator says Russia is running out of options,' July 28, 2026.",
   "summary": "The war crossed a NATO border this cycle, by ordnance rather than by decision.\n\nTHE MISSILE IN POLAND. During one of Russia's largest overnight missile and drone attacks on Ukraine, a projectile entered Polish airspace early Thursday and detonated in a field near the village of Tarnawa-Kolonia, close to Lublin, leaving a crater about ten metres across. Experts assessed the projectile as a Russian Kh-101 cruise missile. NATO's military headquarters said the alliance had activated a wide range of air defences in response to the intrusion and scrambled fighter aircraft; the alliance said it would 'defend territory.' Polish authorities blamed Russia. No casualties were reported on Polish soil. The incident joins a growing series of NATO airspace violations that has raised concern the war could escalate beyond Ukraine's borders.\n\nTHE BARRAGE ITSELF. Zelensky said Russia launched more than 70 missiles and about 280 drones overnight, with Kyiv and Lviv the principal targets. Reported deaths began at eight, including three children, with dozens injured, and rose to nine and then ten in later counts. Zelensky renewed his plea for air-defence supplies and ordered what he described as asymmetric defensive measures.\n\nUKRAINE'S ANSWER RAN DEEP INTO RUSSIA. On July 31 a fresh wave of Ukrainian long-range strikes hit multiple supply hubs and energy sites, with oil refineries and Wildberries logistics facilities reported struck in Russia's Volgograd, Tatarstan and Krasnodar regions overnight. Zelensky had said in a video address on July 30 that Ukraine's defence forces would intensify their strikes on Russian territory. Ukraine's negotiators have made the deep-strike campaign the centrepiece of their leverage argument: senior negotiator Sergiy Kyslytsya told NPR on July 28 that Kyiv now has the leverage to force Russia into serious talks because long-range drones are repeatedly hitting Russian oil and gas facilities, causing fuel shortages and weakening the Kremlin's capacity to sustain the war.\n\nTHE LINE DID NOT MOVE. Ukraine's General Staff recorded 226 combat clashes by 22:00 on July 30, with the heaviest activity on the Kostiantynivka and Pokrovsk axes. The Economist's estimate showed Russia gaining zero square miles over the thirty days to July 28; DeepState OSINT data put the net gain at ten square miles over the four weeks from June 30 to July 28, against thirty-one square miles in the preceding four-week period. On the Ukrainian home front, 66% of Ukrainians told pollsters the government should seek to negotiate an end to the war as soon as possible.",
   "details": [
    "A RUSSIAN MISSILE DETONATES ON NATO TERRITORY (July 30, 2026): A missile entered Polish airspace early Thursday during Russia's overnight barrage on Ukraine and exploded in a field near the village of Tarnawa-Kolonia, close to Lublin in eastern Poland, leaving a crater about ten metres wide. Experts assessed the projectile as a Russian Kh-101 cruise missile. (The Washington Post; Al Jazeera; Foreign Policy; The Irish Times; Stars and Stripes, July 30, 2026.)",
    "NATO SCRAMBLES AND SAYS IT WILL 'DEFEND TERRITORY' (July 30, 2026): A spokesman for NATO's military headquarters said the alliance activated a wide range of air defences in response to the intrusion, and NATO fighter aircraft were scrambled. NATO said it would 'defend territory.' Polish authorities blamed Russia for firing the missile into Polish territory. No casualties were reported in Poland. (Al Jazeera; The Irish Times; Stars and Stripes; CNN, July 30, 2026.)",
    "THE OVERNIGHT BARRAGE: 70+ MISSILES, ~280 DRONES, AT LEAST EIGHT DEAD (July 30, 2026): President Zelensky said Russia launched more than 70 missiles and about 280 drones in an overnight attack that killed at least eight people, including three children, and injured dozens, with Kyiv and Lviv the main targets. Later reports raised the toll to nine and then ten. Zelensky renewed his plea for air-defence supplies. (TIME; Al Jazeera; NPR; Mezha/Bukvy, July 30, 2026.)",
    "UKRAINE STRIKES REFINERIES AND LOGISTICS DEEP INSIDE RUSSIA (July 31, 2026): A new wave of Ukrainian long-range strikes hit multiple supply hubs across Russia, raising pressure on the logistics and fuel chains supporting front-line operations; multiple Wildberries facilities and oil refineries were reported hit in Russia's Volgograd, Tatarstan and Krasnodar regions overnight. (Mezha/Bukvy, July 31, 2026.)",
    "ZELENSKY PROMISES TO INTENSIFY STRIKES ON RUSSIAN TERRITORY (July 30, 2026): In a video address, Zelensky said Ukraine's defence forces would intensify their strikes on Russian territory, and ordered asymmetric defensive measures following the mass missile attack. (Charter'97; Mezha/Bukvy, July 30, 2026.)",
    "THE FRONT: 226 CLASHES, HEAVIEST AT KOSTIANTYNIVKA AND POKROVSK (July 30, 2026): Ukraine's General Staff recorded 226 combat clashes by 22:00 on July 30, with the heaviest activity on the Kostiantynivka and Pokrovsk axes. (Ukraine General Staff via ISW/Critical Threats, July 30, 2026.)",
    "TERRITORIAL CHANGE REMAINS NEAR ZERO (through July 28, 2026): The Economist estimated Russia gained zero square miles of Ukrainian territory over the preceding thirty days. DeepState OSINT data showed a Russian net gain of ten square miles over the four weeks from June 30 to July 28, against thirty-one square miles in the prior four-week period. (Russia Matters, 'The Russia-Ukraine War Report Card, July 29, 2026.')",
    "UKRAINE'S NEGOTIATING ARGUMENT RESTS ON THE DEEP-STRIKE CAMPAIGN (July 28, 2026): Senior Ukrainian negotiator Sergiy Kyslytsya said Kyiv believes it now has the leverage to force Russia into serious negotiations because Ukraine's long-range drones are repeatedly hitting Russian oil and gas facilities, causing fuel shortages and weakening the Kremlin's ability to wage war. Polling showed 66% of Ukrainians believe the government should seek to negotiate an end to the war as soon as possible. (NPR, July 28, 2026; Russia Matters, July 29, 2026.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "Predictions of direct NATO-Russia armed conflict remain DISCONFIRMED, and this cycle tests the boundary rather than crossing it: a Russian cruise missile detonated on the territory of a NATO member, the alliance scrambled aircraft and activated air defences, and the response was a statement of intent to defend territory rather than an Article 4 or Article 5 process being reported. Predictions of a Russian operational breakthrough in Donbas remain DISCONFIRMED: two independent estimates put Russia's territorial gain over the past month at between zero and ten square miles, the lowest sustained rate of the war's current phase, against 226 clashes a day. Predictions that Ukraine would be forced to negotiate from collapse remain DISCONFIRMED in the form predicted; what is developing instead is a negotiation argument built on Ukrainian deep strikes against Russian refining and logistics, with 66% of Ukrainians favouring a negotiated end. Predictions of NATO fragmentation under wartime pressure remain CONTESTED: the alliance responded collectively to the Polish incursion in the same week its two principal European navies declined to lead the American coalition in the Gulf."
