   "source": "U.S. Central Command public release, 'U.S. Strikes IRGC Targets After Attempted Iranian Attacks,' July 30, 2026; CENTCOM via CNBC, \"Iran says it will 'punish the aggressor today' after 'heavy wave' of U.S. strikes,\" July 30, 2026; Al Jazeera, \"US launches 'heavy wave' of new attacks on Iran,\" July 30, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'US hits multiple targets in Iran as IRGC pledges retaliation: What we know,' July 30, 2026; NBC News, \"Iran vows to 'punish the aggressor today' after U.S. unleashes 'heavy wave' of strikes,\" July 30, 2026; The Hill, 'Iran threatens to punish US after retaliatory strikes,' July 30, 2026; The Jerusalem Post, \"Latest US strikes on Iran roughly twice the size of previous bombardment, source tells 'Post',\" July 30, 2026; The Washington Times, 'CENTCOM rejects IRGC claims of destroyed jets in Jordan strikes,' July 30, 2026; U.S. News & World Report, \"US Military Says It Hit Dozens of Iran's IRGC Targets,\" July 30, 2026; Fox News Digital live blog, 'American forces hit dozens of Iranian targets following attempted attack on US troops,' July 30, 2026; IRNA via CNBC (Qeshm casualties), July 30, 2026; Kuwait Ministry of Defence spokesman Saud Al-Atwan via Al Arabiya English, 'Iranian attack hits Chinese company building in Kuwait, killing one worker,' July 30, 2026; Business Recorder, 'Kuwait says Iranian strike hits Chinese company building, kills 1,' July 30, 2026; The National, 'Egypt says drone attack caused fire on two ships in Damietta port,' July 30, 2026; Seatrade Maritime, \"Drone strike hits Energos FSRU and a LNG carrier in Egypt's Damietta port,\" July 30, 2026; Euronews, 'Drone hits US LNG vessel at Damietta in first attack on Egyptian soil,' July 30, 2026; The Maritime Executive, \"Two LNG Carriers Hit in Apparent Drone Attack on Egypt's Port of Damietta,\" July 30, 2026; Just Security, 'Early Edition: July 30, 2026'; Al Arabiya English, 'Prince Khalid holds White House meeting hours after joint US-Saudi strikes,' July 30, 2026; Semafor, 'Saudi defense minister visits Washington to lobby for F-35s,' July 29, 2026; Al Jazeera, \"Iraq calls Saudi-US attacks a 'flagrant violation of sovereignty',\" July 29, 2026; TradingEconomics, Brent crude, July 30, 2026; straits.live Strait of Hormuz tracker, July 30, 2026 22:59Z; AAA Fuel Prices national averages, July 30, 2026.",
   "summary": "Day 155 is the cycle in which the United States went back to bombing Iran itself. After six days in which no American munition had touched Iranian territory — and one in which the retaliation for an Iranian ballistic strike had been routed to Iraq instead — US Central Command announced overnight that it had \"successfully completed a heavy wave of strikes against Iran.\"\n\nTHE STRIKE. CENTCOM said its forces \"struck dozens of Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) targets in Iran, including military command centers, missile and drone facilities, coastal surveillance and defense sites, and maritime capabilities.\" The operation ran roughly two hours. A US official told The Jerusalem Post the bombardment was \"about twice the size of the previous\" one. The stated predicate was the IRGC's July 28 attempted ballistic attack on US forces, which CENTCOM said was fully intercepted, and Trump's framing after it — that it was now \"our turn.\"\n\nWHERE IT LANDED. The target map is the most geographically legible of the war, and it is a coastline. Explosions were reported at Bandar Abbas in Hormozgan, on Qeshm Island, on Kish Island, at Abu Musa, across three cities of Bushehr province, at Kazerun and Farashband in Fars, and at Abadan and Ahvaz in Khuzestan — where seven locations were hit and power went out. Nearly every named site sits on the Persian Gulf littoral or the approaches to the Strait. Kharg Island was not among the reported targets.\n\nTHE COST ON THE GROUND. Iran's IRNA said three people were killed and two wounded in a strike on Qeshm. Al Jazeera reported the dead as a couple and their two-year-old child, with children aged seven and nine wounded and two more people trapped under rubble; three IRGC personnel were also reported killed.\n\nIRAN'S ANSWER, PROMISED AND PARTLY DELIVERED. The IRGC said early Thursday that \"with the help of God Almighty, the aggressor will be punished today,\" warned that countries assisting the United States would \"receive a harsh response,\" and asserted that it retains \"full control\" of the Strait of Hormuz, where \"a stranger who has come from thousands of kilometres away will not be allowed to interfere.\" The Guard separately claimed it had destroyed three F-35 fighter jets at Al Azraq — the Jordanian base also known as Muwaffaq Salti — and killed US personnel there. CENTCOM rejected the aircraft claim categorically, saying no US aircraft were destroyed or damaged and that all missiles and drones were intercepted; it did not address the personnel claim, and The Washington Times reported requesting clarification.\n\nTHE WAR WIDENS TO TWO NEW PLACES. Kuwait's Ministry of Defence said an Iranian strike hit a building belonging to a Chinese company in the country's north, killing one worker and causing extensive material damage; spokesman Saud Al-Atwan called it \"Iranian aggression,\" and the Kuwaiti Armed Forces said they would take \"all necessary measures to protect the nation's sovereignty.\" And a drone struck the US-owned floating storage and regasification unit Energos Winter at Egypt's Mediterranean port of Damietta at about 14:20 UTC on July 29, with fire spreading to the Bermuda-flagged LNG carrier Gaslog Salem. Crews were evacuated, the fire was contained and no injuries were reported. Egypt's cabinet confirmed a drone caused it. No party claimed responsibility; Trump blamed Iran without presenting evidence. It is the first attack on Egyptian soil since the war began on February 28, and it struck a gas IMPORT terminal — extending the energy war from the Gulf's export chokepoint to the Mediterranean's receiving end.\n\nDIPLOMACY, RUNNING UNDERNEATH. Saudi Defence Minister Prince Khalid bin Salman met Trump and Vice President JD Vance at the White House on July 29, hours after the joint US-Saudi strikes in Iraq, carrying a message from Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman: Riyadh joined those strikes in self-defence but wants de-escalation, judging diplomacy the more fruitful path, and assesses that Iran will keep seeking leverage through Iraqi militias and the Houthis. He also lobbied for F-35s. Reporting on the Trump-Netanyahu session described three options canvassed — diplomacy, intensified economic pressure and blockade, or renewed military action — with Netanyahu expressing no preference. Oman and Pakistan indicated mediation continues.\n\nMARKETS AND THE STRAIT. Brent surged 7.9% on July 29 and then eased back below $90 on July 30, holding most of the prior session's gain: the straits.live tracker showed $89.45, down 1.11% (−$1.00), at 22:59Z. Pump prices rose again — AAA's national average was $4.0980 for regular and $5.3390 for diesel, another war high. The straits.live tracker's July 30 readings are not comparable to the prior cycle's and appear to reflect a change in how the zone is counted: 35 tankers transiting against 10-13 a day earlier, 1,357 vessels anchored or stopped against 339, and 9 dark tankers against 68. War-risk insurance held at 8.0× pre-crisis, about $2.5m for a VLCC.\n\nFORCE POSTURE. No landing, no Kharg seizure, no new amphibious order and no draft. Every ground-execution indicator stayed at zero for a 155th consecutive day.",
   "details": [
    "US STRIKES ON IRANIAN TERRITORY RESUME AFTER SIX DAYS (overnight July 29-30, 2026): US Central Command announced it had \"successfully completed a heavy wave of strikes against Iran,\" saying its forces \"struck dozens of Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) targets in Iran, including military command centers, missile and drone facilities, coastal surveillance and defense sites, and maritime capabilities.\" The operation ran approximately two hours. A US official told The Jerusalem Post the bombardment was \"about twice the size of the previous\" one. This is the first US strike on Iranian soil since July 24; the previous cycle's retaliation for Iran's July 28 ballistic attack had been delivered in Iraq, jointly with Saudi aircraft. (CENTCOM public release; CNBC; Al Jazeera; The Jerusalem Post; U.S. News & World Report, July 30, 2026.)",
    "THE TARGET SET IS COASTAL AND MARITIME (July 30, 2026): Explosions were reported at Bandar Abbas (Hormozgan), Qeshm Island, Kish Island, Abu Musa, three cities in Bushehr province, Kazerun and Farashband (Fars), and Abadan and Ahvaz (Khuzestan), where seven locations were struck and power outages followed. CENTCOM's own categories — \"coastal surveillance and defense sites\" and \"maritime capabilities\" — match the geography. Kharg Island was NOT among the reported targets this cycle. (Al Jazeera; The Jerusalem Post; Fox News Digital, July 30, 2026.)",
    "IRANIAN CASUALTIES ON QESHM (July 30, 2026): Iran's official IRNA news agency said three people were killed and two wounded in a strike on the island of Qeshm. Al Jazeera reported the dead as a couple and their two-year-old child, with two children aged seven and nine wounded and two further people trapped under rubble; three IRGC personnel were separately reported killed. No independent verification of the totals was available at the cycle close. (IRNA via CNBC; Al Jazeera, July 30, 2026.)",
    "IRGC PROMISES SAME-DAY RETALIATION AND CLAIMS THE STRAIT (July 30, 2026): The IRGC said \"with the help of God Almighty, the aggressor will be punished today\" (via Fars), warned that countries aiding the United States would \"receive a harsh response,\" and asserted it retains \"full control\" of the Strait of Hormuz, where \"a stranger who has come from thousands of kilometres away will not be allowed to interfere.\" No further Iranian strike had been confirmed by the cycle close. (Sputnik; CNBC; The Hill; Al Jazeera, July 30, 2026.)",
    "IRGC CLAIMS THREE F-35s DESTROYED IN JORDAN; CENTCOM REJECTS IT (July 30, 2026): The IRGC claimed its forces destroyed three F-35 fighter jets at Al Azraq Air Base in Jordan — the installation also identified as Muwaffaq Salti — and killed US personnel there. CENTCOM rejected the aircraft claim categorically, stating that no US aircraft were destroyed or damaged and that all missiles and drones were intercepted. CENTCOM did not formally address the claim of US casualties, and The Washington Times reported that it had requested clarification. (The Washington Times, July 30, 2026.)",
    "IRAN STRIKES A CHINESE COMPANY'S BUILDING IN KUWAIT, KILLING A WORKER (July 30, 2026): Kuwait's Ministry of Defence said an Iranian strike hit a building belonging to a Chinese company in northern Kuwait, killing one worker and causing extensive material damage. Spokesman Saud Al-Atwan described it as \"Iranian aggression\"; the Kuwaiti Armed Forces said they would continue \"taking all necessary measures to protect the nation's sovereignty and safeguard its security and stability.\" The worker's nationality was not stated in the initial official account. (Al Arabiya English; Business Recorder; The Washington Post; Shafaq News; The Times of Israel, July 30, 2026.)",
    "FIRST ATTACK ON EGYPTIAN SOIL OF THE WAR — DRONE HITS TWO LNG VESSELS AT DAMIETTA (July 29-30, 2026): A drone struck the US-owned Marshall Islands-flagged floating storage and regasification unit Energos Winter at Egypt's Mediterranean port of Damietta at approximately 14:20 UTC on July 29, with fire spreading to the Bermuda-flagged LNG carrier Gaslog Salem. Both crews were evacuated, the fire was brought under control and no injuries were reported. Egypt's cabinet confirmed on July 30 that preliminary investigations found the incident was caused by a drone. No party claimed responsibility; Trump blamed Iran without presenting evidence. Damietta is one of Egypt's principal gas import terminals and operations there were disrupted. (The National; Seatrade Maritime; Euronews; The Maritime Executive; Al Jazeera; CNBC; UPI, July 29-30, 2026.)",
    "SAUDI DEFENCE MINISTER CARRIES A DE-ESCALATION MESSAGE TO THE WHITE HOUSE (July 29, 2026): Prince Khalid bin Salman met President Trump and Vice President JD Vance hours after the joint US-Saudi strikes in Iraq, delivering a message from Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman. He said the Kingdom joined the strikes to defend itself from Iranian-backed attacks but favours de-escalation as the more fruitful path, characterised Netanyahu as pushing for escalation, and assessed that Iran will continue seeking leverage through pro-Iranian militias in Iraq and the Houthis in Yemen. He separately lobbied for F-35 sales. His trip had been postponed from the previous week amid an uptick in militia attacks. (Al Arabiya English; Axios via CGTN; Semafor; TASS; WION, July 29-30, 2026.)",
    "TRUMP AND NETANYAHU CANVASSED THREE OPTIONS; ISRAEL EXPRESSED NO PREFERENCE (July 29-30, 2026): Reporting compiled in Just Security's July 30 Early Edition recorded that the two leaders discussed three approaches to Iran — diplomacy, intensified economic pressure and blockade, or renewed military action — and that Netanyahu expressed no preference among them. (Just Security, 'Early Edition: July 30, 2026'.)",
    "OPERATIONAL SECURITY ORDER FOR US TROOPS IN JORDAN (July 30, 2026): US troops in Jordan were warned that their phones may be confiscated in coming days over \"operational security concerns,\" with the CENTCOM commander cautioning that Iran gains real-time visibility of strikes from service members' social-media posts. (Just Security, 'Early Edition: July 30, 2026'.)",
    "IRAQ ESCALATES ITS SOVEREIGNTY OBJECTION (July 29-30, 2026): Iraq's National Security Council, chaired by Prime Minister Ali al-Zaidi, called the strikes on PMF sites across seven provinces \"a flagrant violation of Iraq's sovereignty and the sanctity of its lands\" and \"an aggression in contravention of the principles of international law and the United Nations Charter.\" The presidency called it \"an unacceptable attack and a blatant violation of Iraq's sovereignty, targeting its official security institutions.\" The foreign ministry summoned the US chargé d'affaires and, separately, the Iranian ambassador. Baghdad asserted a \"right to respond and self-defense\" using available means. (Al Jazeera; Rudaw; Fox News; NPR; The Jerusalem Post, July 29-30, 2026.)",
    "MARKETS (July 30, 2026): Brent surged 7.9% on July 29 on the renewed fighting and then eased back below $90 on July 30 while holding most of that gain; the straits.live tracker showed $89.45, down 1.11% (−$1.00), at 22:59Z. TradingEconomics recorded Brent easing below $91 and then below $90 intraday. AAA's national average rose to $4.0980 for regular from $4.0910 and to $5.3390 for diesel from $5.3290 — another war high, against $3.140 and $3.740 a year earlier. (TradingEconomics; straits.live; Bloomberg; AAA Fuel Prices, July 30, 2026.)",
    "STRAIT METRICS SHOW AN APPARENT MEASUREMENT DISCONTINUITY (July 30, 2026): The straits.live tracker at 22:59Z reported 35 tankers transiting, 1,357 vessels anchored or stopped in the zone (5-hour range 1,255-1,390) and 9 tankers running AIS-dark over 24 hours. The prior cycle's readings were 10-13 transiting, 339 stranded and 68 dark. All three metrics moved by factors of roughly 3-8x in directions that do not reconcile with one another as a single physical event, and the tracker published no methodology note. This project records the figures as printed and flags the discontinuity as probably a change in the counted zone rather than a fourfold surge in strandings; IMF PortWatch's most recent independent count remains 10 commercial transits on July 23 against an 88-per-day pre-crisis baseline. War-risk insurance held at 8.0× pre-crisis (~$2.5m for a VLCC); the tracker's Crisis Pressure index read 92 (extreme) and its Escalation Forecast composite 54 (elevated). (straits.live, July 30, 2026 22:59Z; IMF PortWatch.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "The corpus-predicted US GROUND seizure of Hormuz/Kharg remains DISCONFIRMED at Day 155, but this is a less one-sided cycle than Day 154 and the reason is worth stating precisely. The United States resumed striking Iranian territory after six days, in a wave CENTCOM called \"heavy\" and a US official put at roughly twice the size of the previous one — and the declared target categories were \"coastal surveillance and defense sites\" and \"maritime capabilities,\" struck along the Gulf littoral at Bandar Abbas, Qeshm, Kish, Abu Musa, Bushehr, Abadan and Ahvaz. That is the same geography the Pentagon's reported limited-raid options would require shaped. It is also exactly what an air-and-naval interdiction campaign against anti-shipping capability looks like, and the two are not distinguishable from the strike list alone. What did NOT happen still governs the verdict: no landing, no Kharg strike or seizure this cycle, no new amphibious or MEU order, no change to the ~50,000 troop level, no draft, and no ground formation named by the President, the Pentagon or CENTCOM. Predictions of a negotiated Hormuz transit regime remain live but unmoved — Iran reasserted \"full control\" of the Strait and its rejection of the Omani plan stands. Predictions that the war would stay confined to the Gulf are now DISCONFIRMED in a new direction: the first attack on Egyptian soil struck an LNG IMPORT terminal on the Mediterranean, extending the energy war from the export chokepoint to the receiving end. Predictions that Gulf states would be drawn into direct belligerency remain PARTIALLY CONFIRMED and are pulling in two directions at once — Riyadh flew strikes in Iraq on July 28 and carried a de-escalation message to the White House on July 29.",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "The coastal and maritime character of the July 30 strike wave is read either as interdiction of Iran's anti-shipping capability or as air preparation of the littoral for the limited ground raids the Pentagon is reported to have planned.",
     "evidentiary_status": "The strike list is documented and the ambiguity is intrinsic to it. CENTCOM stated it hit \"coastal surveillance and defense sites\" and \"maritime capabilities\" among dozens of IRGC targets, and the reported locations — Bandar Abbas, Qeshm, Kish, Abu Musa, Bushehr, Abadan, Ahvaz — are almost entirely littoral. Separately documented since March 2026: the Pentagon has prepared options for weeks of limited ground operations comprising Special Operations and conventional raids on Kharg Island and coastal weapons sites, explicitly short of invasion. Not documented: any order, embarkation, rehearsal, ARG repositioning, MEU tasking or landing. Kharg itself was not struck this cycle, and the Boxer ARG's disposition is unchanged since the July 27 USNI tracker.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
