      "Commentary outlets reading the wave as the opening of a sustained infrastructure campaign (e.g. HotAir, \"Will This (Finally) Be Infrastructure Week?\", July 30, 2026)",
      "Quincy Institute and National Security Journal analysts arguing publicly against the raid thesis on feasibility grounds"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "Suppression of coastal radar, anti-ship missiles and surveillance is a prerequisite for an amphibious raid AND a complete objective in its own right for a navy protecting a blockade and escorting convoys. The two purposes generate an identical target list. Only an embarkation order, an ARG movement toward the northern Gulf, a Kharg strike package, or an on-record statement of intent would separate them.",
     "open_question_ref": "us-coastal-strike-set-shaping-or-interdiction-july-2026"
    },
    {
     "reading": "The Damietta drone strike is attributed to Iran by the President of the United States and to no one at all by the government on whose territory it happened.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented: a drone struck the US-owned FSRU Energos Winter at Damietta at about 14:20 UTC on July 29, fire spread to the LNG carrier Gaslog Salem, crews were evacuated, no injuries resulted, and Egypt's cabinet confirmed on July 30 that its preliminary investigation found a drone caused the fire. Documented: Trump publicly blamed Iran. Not documented: any claim of responsibility, any debris or forensic attribution released by Egypt, any launch-point assessment, and any corroborating US intelligence statement. Egypt did not name a perpetrator.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "President Trump, attributing the attack to Iran without presenting evidence (reported in Just Security's July 30 Early Edition)",
      "Egypt's Cabinet, confirming a drone caused the fire while stating no party had claimed responsibility",
      "Maritime trade press (Seatrade Maritime, The Maritime Executive) reporting the incident as unattributed"
