     "why_unresolvable": "A single unclaimed one-way drone against a fixed port target leaves the same open-source signature whoever launched it. Attribution would require debris analysis, a launch-track, an intercepted communication or a claim — none of which has been published. Egypt has an evident interest in not attributing an attack it would then have to answer.",
     "open_question_ref": "damietta-drone-attribution-july-2026"
    },
    {
     "reading": "The IRGC says it destroyed three F-35s and killed US personnel at Al Azraq; CENTCOM denies the aircraft and does not address the personnel.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented as competing claims. The IRGC asserted on July 30 that it destroyed three F-35 fighter jets at Al Azraq Air Base in Jordan and inflicted US casualties. CENTCOM stated categorically that no US aircraft were destroyed or damaged and that all missiles and drones were intercepted. CENTCOM did not formally address the casualty claim, and The Washington Times reported requesting clarification and not receiving it by publication. Not documented: imagery of damaged or intact aircraft at the base, an independent count of airframes present, or a Jordanian statement on damage.",
