    "Boxer Sole Deployed ARG",
    "11th MEU Arabian Sea",
    "Tripoli Home In Sasebo",
    "No Relief ARG",
    "~50,000 Personnel",
    "Draft Rule Still Withdrawn",
    "Dec 19 Deadline Unchanged",
    "Air Not Ground",
    "Saudi De-escalation Message",
    "UK France Refuse Coalition",
    "Jordan OPSEC Phone Order",
    "Disconfirmed"
   ],
   "source": "U.S. Central Command public release, 'U.S. Strikes IRGC Targets After Attempted Iranian Attacks,' July 30, 2026; The Jerusalem Post, 'Latest US strikes on Iran roughly twice the size of previous bombardment,' July 30, 2026; Al Jazeera, \"US launches 'heavy wave' of new attacks on Iran,\" July 30, 2026; USNI News Fleet and Marine Tracker, July 27, 2026; The War Zone, 'Where Are The Aircraft Carriers: July 28, 2026'; The Washington Post via Al Jazeera, \"Pentagon readies 'for weeks of US ground operations' in Iran,\" March 29, 2026; Foreign Policy, 'How a U.S. Ground Operation in Iran Could Unfold,' July 24, 2026; Quincy Institute for Responsible Statecraft, 'Limited US Ground Operations in Iran Will Not Shift the War's Balance'; National Security Journal, 'The Kharg Island Invasion Myth'; CNN, 'Iran building up defenses of Kharg Island to protect against potential US ground attack,' March 25, 2026; Antiwar.com, 'Selective Service Withdraws Proposed Rules for Automatic Draft Registration,' July 24, 2026; CNN Politics, 'Automatic military draft registration takes effect in the US in December 2026,' April 9, 2026; Just Security, 'Early Edition: July 30, 2026' (Jordan phone-confiscation OPSEC order); POLITICO / E&E News, 'Allies shrug as US pushes for Strait of Hormuz patrol,' July 29, 2026; Al Arabiya English, 'Prince Khalid holds White House meeting hours after joint US-Saudi strikes,' July 30, 2026; LiveNOW from FOX, 'US military assets in the Middle East: Ships, planes, and 50,000 American troops.'",
   "summary": "Day 155 holds at no_ground_troops for a 155th consecutive day, and it is the first cycle in a week in which an indicator moved TOWARD rather than away from a ground phase — though only on the shaping rung, and ambiguously.\n\nWHAT MOVED. The United States resumed striking Iranian territory after a six-day interval, and it did so with a wave CENTCOM called \"heavy,\" running roughly two hours against \"dozens\" of IRGC targets, which a US official put at about twice the size of the previous bombardment. The declared categories included \"coastal surveillance and defense sites\" and \"maritime capabilities,\" and the reported strike map is almost entirely littoral: Bandar Abbas, Qeshm Island, Kish Island, Abu Musa, three cities of Bushehr province, Kazerun and Farashband, Abadan and Ahvaz. Suppressing coastal radar, anti-ship missiles and surveillance along that arc is a precondition for the limited Special Operations and conventional raids on Kharg and coastal weapons sites that the Pentagon has been reported since March to have prepared as options. It is also a complete objective in itself for a navy running a blockade and escorting convoys through a contested strait. The strike list cannot distinguish the two, and this tracker records it as an ambiguous shaping signal rather than as evidence of intent.\n\nWHAT DID NOT MOVE — WHICH REMAINS THE VERDICT. No US or allied ground force landed anywhere in Iran. Kharg Island was not among the reported targets this cycle and has still never been seized. No new amphibious ready group, Marine Expeditionary Unit or ground formation was ordered, tasked or repositioned; USS Boxer (LHD-4) remains in the Arabian Sea with USS Comstock, USS Portland and the 11th MEU, still the sole deployed ARG fleet-wide as of the most recent USNI and War Zone counts, with USS Tripoli home in Sasebo and no relief ARG announced nine days after its departure. Troop strength in theatre remains approximately 50,000. No draft exists, none is planned, and the Selective Service's proposed automatic-registration rule remains withdrawn since July 23 with the December 19, 2026 statutory deadline unchanged and no implementing rule pending.\n\nEVERY STATED ESCALATION OPTION REMAINS AERIAL. Neither the President, the Pentagon nor CENTCOM named a ground formation, an amphibious operation or a Kharg landing this cycle. The announced act was an air wave; the announced justification was retaliation for an intercepted missile attack; the announced follow-on was nothing at all.\n\nTHE ALLIED AND DIPLOMATIC DIRECTION RUNS AGAINST A GROUND PHASE. Saudi Arabia — which flew strikes alongside CENTCOM in Iraq on July 28 — sent its defence minister to the White House the next day to argue for de-escalation, telling Vance that Netanyahu is pushing for escalation while Riyadh judges diplomacy more fruitful. The only force generation actually under way in this war remains an allied naval escort coalition for Hormuz, which Britain and France have declined to lead absent an enduring ceasefire.\n\nONE NEW FORCE-PROTECTION DATUM. US troops in Jordan were told their phones may be confiscated over operational-security concerns, with the CENTCOM commander warning that service members' social-media posts give Iran real-time visibility of strikes. This is a defensive measure at an air base under repeated ballistic and drone attack, not a staging indicator — but it is the kind of tightening that precedes any operation, and it is recorded here for that reason.\n\nEvery execution indicator — US troops in Iran, a landing, a Kharg seizure, a new ARG or MEU order, a draft — stayed at zero for a 155th consecutive day.",
   "details": [
    "THE US RETURNED TO STRIKING IRANIAN TERRITORY — BY AIR (overnight July 29-30, 2026): CENTCOM \"successfully completed a heavy wave of strikes against Iran,\" hitting \"dozens\" of IRGC targets over roughly two hours, at about twice the size of the previous bombardment per a US official. No ground element was involved, committed or announced. The domain of the entire American escalation this cycle was air power. (CENTCOM public release; The Jerusalem Post; Al Jazeera, July 30, 2026.)",
    "AMBIGUOUS SHAPING SIGNAL — THE TARGET SET IS THE LITTORAL (July 30, 2026): CENTCOM's declared categories included \"coastal surveillance and defense sites\" and \"maritime capabilities,\" and the reported strike locations run along the Gulf coast and the approaches to the Strait: Bandar Abbas, Qeshm Island, Kish Island, Abu Musa, three cities in Bushehr province, Kazerun and Farashband in Fars, and Abadan and Ahvaz in Khuzestan. Since March 2026 the Pentagon has been reported to hold options for weeks of limited ground operations — Special Operations and conventional raids on Kharg Island and coastal weapons sites, explicitly short of full-scale invasion — which would require exactly this suppression first. The same strikes are equally consistent with pure anti-shipping interdiction in support of the blockade and convoy escort. This tracker records the ambiguity and does not resolve it. (CENTCOM; The Washington Post via Al Jazeera, March 29, 2026; Foreign Policy, July 24, 2026.)",
    "KHARG ISLAND — NOT STRUCK THIS CYCLE, NEVER SEIZED (July 30, 2026): Kharg did not appear in any reported target list for the July 29-30 wave. No US or allied ground force has landed on or seized the island at any point in the war. Iran's fortification of it against a potential US ground attack — anti-personnel and anti-armour mines including on the shoreline where an amphibious landing would stage — stands as last reported. Prior reporting that seizure plans were repeatedly shelved as too risky and would require a significant number of ground troops has not been superseded. (CNN, March 25, 2026; National Security Journal; Quincy Institute.)",
    "AMPHIBIOUS PICTURE UNCHANGED, NINE DAYS ON (as of the July 27-28 trackers): USS Boxer (LHD-4) remains in the Arabian Sea with the Boxer ARG — USS Comstock (LSD-45), USS Portland (LPD-27) and the 11th Marine Expeditionary Unit, roughly 2,200 Camp Pendleton Marines — alongside expeditionary sea base USS Miguel Keith (ESB-5). The War Zone's most recent count confirms it as the sole deployed amphibious ready group fleet-wide. USS Tripoli (LHA-7) remains home in Sasebo after disembarking 31st MEU elements at Okinawa on July 22, and no relief ARG has been announced nine days on. Carriers Abraham Lincoln and George H.W. Bush remain in the CENTCOM AOR; George Washington is in the South China Sea; Theodore Roosevelt is with RIMPAC 2026. No new ARG, MEU or ground formation was ordered this cycle. (USNI News Fleet and Marine Tracker, July 27, 2026; The War Zone, July 28, 2026.)",
    "TROOP STRENGTH UNCHANGED (July 30, 2026): Approximately 50,000 US personnel in the Middle East, roughly 10,000 above the normal baseline, including thousands of Marines and sailors and about 2,000 soldiers of the 82nd Airborne Division. No new deployment order was announced this cycle. Foreign Policy's assessment that a major ground operation would require far more troops than are currently in the region stands unrevised. (LiveNOW from FOX; Foreign Policy, July 24, 2026.)",
    "DRAFT INDICATOR — STILL WITHDRAWN, NO NEW MOVEMENT (as of July 30, 2026): The Selective Service System's proposed automatic-registration rule, withdrawn on July 23 after the White House held it for nearly four months without acting, has not been re-proposed and neither the agency nor the White House has explained the withdrawal. The statutory mandate is unchanged: automatic registration must begin December 19, 2026 under the December 2025 NDAA provision. Automatic registration is not a draft; Congress would have to authorise a draft, and none has been called, planned or proposed. There has been no draft since 1973. (Antiwar.com, July 24, 2026; CNN Politics, April 9, 2026; Military.com.)",
    "ALLIED FORCE GENERATION STILL REFUSED, AND RIYADH ARGUES FOR DE-ESCALATION (July 29-30, 2026): Britain and France continue to decline leading the Hormuz naval coalition until they see an enduring ceasefire. Saudi Defence Minister Prince Khalid bin Salman told Trump and Vance that the Kingdom joined the July 28 Iraq strikes in self-defence but wants de-escalation as the more fruitful path, and characterised Netanyahu as the party pushing for escalation. The only force generation under way in this war remains a naval escort mission that cannot find its leadership. (POLITICO / E&E News, July 29, 2026; Al Arabiya English; Axios via CGTN; Semafor, July 29-30, 2026.)",
    "FORCE-PROTECTION TIGHTENING IN JORDAN (July 30, 2026): US troops in Jordan were warned that their phones may be confiscated in coming days over \"operational security concerns,\" with the CENTCOM commander warning that Iran derives real-time visibility of strikes from service members' social-media posts. Recorded as a defensive measure at a base under repeated attack, not as a staging indicator. (Just Security, 'Early Edition: July 30, 2026'.)",
    "ZERO ON EVERY EXECUTION INDICATOR FOR A 155TH DAY (July 30, 2026): No US troops in Iran. No amphibious landing. No Kharg seizure. No new ARG or MEU order. No mobilisation, call-up or draft. No Pentagon or CENTCOM statement naming a ground operation. The predicted US ground seizure of the Strait of Hormuz or Kharg Island remains DISCONFIRMED."
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "The ground-seizure prediction remains DISCONFIRMED at Day 155, and the honest reading of this cycle is that it is the first in a week to contain anything pointing the other way. The resumed strikes on Iranian territory were concentrated on the coastal and maritime target set that a limited raid on Kharg or the coastal weapons sites would require suppressed first — which is what the Pentagon's reported options have described since March. That is a shaping-consistent pattern, and this tracker records it as such. It is not evidence of intent, because the identical strikes are what a blockading navy does to protect convoys, and because every execution indicator remains at zero: no landing, no Kharg strike this cycle, no new amphibious or MEU order, no troop increase beyond ~50,000, no draft, and no ground formation named by any US official. The structural indicators still run the other way — the draft rule is withdrawn, the allied coalition is naval and leaderless, and the closest Gulf partner spent the cycle arguing in the Oval Office for de-escalation.",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "The concentrated strikes on Iran's coastal surveillance, coastal defence and maritime capability are read either as the interdiction campaign a blockading navy runs, or as the suppression phase a coastal raid would require.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented: CENTCOM's own target categories and a littoral strike map spanning Bandar Abbas, Qeshm, Kish, Abu Musa, Bushehr, Kazerun, Farashband, Abadan and Ahvaz; and, separately, reporting since March 2026 that the Pentagon prepared options for weeks of limited Special Operations and conventional raids on Kharg Island and coastal weapons sites. Not documented: any embarkation, rehearsal, ARG movement toward the northern Gulf, MEU tasking, Kharg strike this cycle, or statement of ground intent. Kharg was not struck; the Boxer ARG has not moved on the record since July 27.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "Former Defense Secretary Mark Esper, who has publicly read this war's operational pauses as preparation for \"something much bigger\"",
      "Commentary outlets reading the wave as the start of a sustained infrastructure campaign (HotAir, July 30, 2026)",
      "Quincy Institute for Responsible Statecraft and National Security Journal, arguing publicly that a Kharg ground operation is neither imminent nor militarily sound"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "Suppression of anti-ship and coastal-surveillance systems is simultaneously the whole objective of maritime interdiction and the first step of an amphibious raid. The target list is identical under both hypotheses. Only an embarkation order, an ARG repositioning, a Kharg strike package or an explicit statement would separate them, and none exists in the open record.",
     "open_question_ref": "us-coastal-strike-set-shaping-or-interdiction-july-2026"
    }
   ]
