  {
   "date": "2026-07-31",
   "headline": "DAY 155 GULF STATES — THE WAR TOUCHES EGYPT FOR THE FIRST TIME AND KUWAIT TAKES ANOTHER FATAL STRIKE, WHILE RIYADH SUES FOR DE-ESCALATION ONE DAY AFTER FLYING STRIKES. AN UNCLAIMED DRONE HIT THE US-OWNED FSRU ENERGOS WINTER AT DAMIETTA ON JULY 29, SPREADING FIRE TO THE LNG CARRIER GASLOG SALEM — CREWS EVACUATED, NO INJURIES, EGYPT'S CABINET CONFIRMING A DRONE AND NAMING NO ONE, TRUMP BLAMING IRAN WITHOUT EVIDENCE. IT IS THE FIRST ATTACK ON EGYPTIAN SOIL OF THE WAR AND THE FIRST ON AN ENERGY IMPORT TERMINAL. KUWAIT'S DEFENCE MINISTRY SAID AN IRANIAN STRIKE HIT A CHINESE COMPANY'S BUILDING IN THE NORTH, KILLING ONE WORKER. PRINCE KHALID BIN SALMAN MET TRUMP AND VANCE CARRYING MBS'S MESSAGE — SELF-DEFENCE IN IRAQ, BUT DIPLOMACY IS 'A MORE FRUITFUL PATH,' AND NETANYAHU IS THE ONE PUSHING ESCALATION — WHILE LOBBYING FOR F-35s. BAGHDAD CALLED THE JULY 28 STRIKES 'A FLAGRANT VIOLATION OF IRAQ'S SOVEREIGNTY' AND SUMMONED BOTH THE US CHARGÉ D'AFFAIRES AND THE IRANIAN AMBASSADOR. GCC GROWTH FORECAST STILL CUT 4.4%→1.3%; ~80 ENERGY FACILITIES HIT.",
   "theatre": "Iran / Strait of Hormuz",
   "tag": "Gulf States",
   "tags": [
    "Gulf States",
    "Egypt",
    "Damietta",
    "Energos Winter",
    "Gaslog Salem",
    "First Attack On Egyptian Soil",
    "LNG Import Terminal",
    "Unclaimed Drone",
    "Kuwait Strike",
    "Chinese Company Building",
    "Worker Killed",
    "Prince Khalid Bin Salman",
    "White House Meeting",
    "Saudi De-escalation",
    "F-35 Lobbying",
    "Iraq Sovereignty",
    "Both Ambassadors Summoned",
    "GCC Recession Risk",
    "Qatar Ras Laffan",
    "Bab el-Mandeb"
   ],
   "source": "Kuwait Ministry of Defence spokesman Saud Al-Atwan via Al Arabiya English and Business Recorder, July 30, 2026; The Washington Post, 'An Iranian strike has hit a Chinese firm's building, killing 1, hours after Jordan downs missiles,' July 30, 2026; Al Arabiya English, 'Prince Khalid holds White House meeting hours after joint US-Saudi strikes,' July 30, 2026; Axios via CGTN, July 30, 2026; Semafor, 'Saudi defense minister visits Washington to lobby for F-35s,' July 29, 2026; Al Jazeera, \"Iraq calls Saudi-US attacks a 'flagrant violation of sovereignty',\" July 29, 2026; Fox News, 'US strikes against Iran-backed militias in Iraq reportedly continue as Baghdad warns of right to respond,' July 29-30, 2026; Rudaw, \"Iraq's Iran-aligned factions condemn Saudi-US airstrikes on PMF bases,\" July 29, 2026; The National, 'Egypt says drone attack caused fire on two ships in Damietta port,' July 30, 2026; Euronews, 'Drone hits US LNG vessel at Damietta in first attack on Egyptian soil,' July 30, 2026; The Conversation, 'How severe has the economic impact of the Iran war been for the Gulf states?'; Stimson Center, 'Iran Conflict Hits Foundations of Gulf Economies'; The Hill, 'Red Sea shipping slows after Houthi attacks on Saudi Arabia: Data,' July 27, 2026.",
   "summary": "The Gulf and its periphery absorbed two new blows this cycle, one of them outside the Gulf entirely, while the region's most consequential government argued in Washington for the war to stop.\n\nKUWAIT TAKES ANOTHER FATAL STRIKE. Kuwait's Ministry of Defence said an Iranian strike hit a building belonging to a Chinese company in the country's north, killing one worker and causing extensive material damage. Spokesman Saud Al-Atwan called it \"Iranian aggression\"; the armed forces said they would take \"all necessary measures to protect the nation's sovereignty and safeguard its security and stability.\" Kuwait has now been struck repeatedly across this war — the June 3 airport strike, the July utilities attacks, and now this.\n\nEGYPT IS DRAWN IN FOR THE FIRST TIME. A drone struck the US-owned FSRU Energos Winter at the Mediterranean port of Damietta at about 14:20 UTC on July 29, and fire spread to the Bermuda-flagged LNG carrier Gaslog Salem. Crews were evacuated, the fire was contained, no one was injured, and Egypt's cabinet confirmed a drone caused it. No party claimed responsibility; Trump blamed Iran without evidence. This is the first attack on Egyptian soil since the war began on February 28, and its significance is structural rather than kinetic: Damietta is an IMPORT terminal. For five months the energy war has been fought over the export chokepoint at Hormuz. It has now touched the receiving end of the chain, on the Mediterranean, roughly 2,000 km from the Strait.\n\nRIYADH FIGHTS AND SUES FOR DE-ESCALATION IN THE SAME WEEK. Prince Khalid bin Salman met Trump and Vance at the White House on July 29, hours after Saudi jets flew alongside CENTCOM against militia targets in Iraq. He carried a message from the Crown Prince: the Kingdom struck in self-defence and will defend itself, but favours diplomacy as the more fruitful path; Netanyahu is pushing for escalation; and Iran will keep generating leverage through Iraqi militias and the Houthis. He also lobbied for F-35s. The two facts belong together — Saudi Arabia's first claimed strikes inside Iraq and its most explicit de-escalation appeal of the war fell in consecutive days.\n\nBAGHDAD HARDENS. Iraq's National Security Council under Prime Minister Ali al-Zaidi called the strikes on PMF sites \"a flagrant violation of Iraq's sovereignty and the sanctity of its lands\" and an \"aggression in contravention of the principles of international law and the United Nations Charter\"; the presidency called them an attack on Iraq's \"official security institutions.\" The foreign ministry summoned the US chargé d'affaires and, separately, the Iranian ambassador — a two-sided protest that mirrors the government's structural position. Baghdad asserted a right to respond and to self-defence using available means.\n\nTHE ECONOMIC BASELINE. The World Bank's 2026 regional growth forecast remains cut from 4.4% to 1.3%, with Oxford Economics projecting some GCC economies to enter recession in the second half. Roughly 80 energy facilities have been struck since February. Qatar's Ras Laffan damage from March 18 still stands at 17-20% of production capacity with a three-to-five-year restoration and about $20bn a year in lost revenue; S&P projects a 5% contraction in Qatari real GDP. The UAE continues to route up to 1.8m bpd around the Strait via the Habshan-Fujairah pipeline. Bab el-Mandeb traffic remains depressed after the Houthi attacks on Saudi Red Sea shipping, with a recent low of 11 cargo vessels transiting in a day.",
   "details": [
    "KUWAIT — IRANIAN STRIKE KILLS A WORKER AT A CHINESE COMPANY'S BUILDING (July 30, 2026): Kuwait's Ministry of Defence said the strike hit a building belonging to a Chinese company in northern Kuwait, killing one worker and causing extensive material damage. Spokesman Saud Al-Atwan attributed it to \"Iranian aggression.\" The Kuwaiti Armed Forces said they would continue \"taking all necessary measures to protect the nation's sovereignty and safeguard its security and stability.\" The worker's nationality was not stated in the initial official account. (Al Arabiya English; Business Recorder; The Washington Post; Shafaq News; WION; The Times of Israel, July 30, 2026.)",
    "EGYPT — FIRST ATTACK ON EGYPTIAN SOIL OF THE WAR (July 29-30, 2026): A drone struck the US-owned, Marshall Islands-flagged floating storage and regasification unit Energos Winter at Damietta at about 14:20 UTC on July 29; fire spread to the Bermuda-flagged LNG carrier Gaslog Salem. Both crews were evacuated, the fire was brought under control and no injuries were reported. Egypt's Cabinet confirmed on July 30 that preliminary investigations found a drone caused the incident; no party claimed responsibility. Trump blamed Iran without presenting evidence. Damietta is one of Egypt's principal gas import terminals and port operations were disrupted. (The National; Seatrade Maritime; The Maritime Executive; Euronews; Al Jazeera; CNBC; UPI; The Jerusalem Post, July 29-30, 2026.)",
    "SAUDI ARABIA — DEFENCE MINISTER DELIVERS A DE-ESCALATION MESSAGE ONE DAY AFTER FLYING STRIKES (July 29, 2026): Prince Khalid bin Salman met President Trump and Vice President JD Vance at the White House hours after the joint US-Saudi strikes in Iraq, conveying a message from Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman. He said Saudi Arabia joined the strikes to defend itself against Iranian-backed attacks that had crossed Riyadh's red line, but that the Crown Prince favours diplomacy over a wider regional war; that Netanyahu is pushing for escalation while Saudi Arabia wants de-escalation as \"a more fruitful path\"; and that Iran will keep seeking leverage through pro-Iranian militias in Iraq and the Houthis in Yemen. He separately lobbied for F-35 sales. The visit had been postponed from the previous week after an uptick in militia attacks. (Al Arabiya English; Axios via CGTN; Semafor; TASS; WION, July 29-30, 2026.)",
    "IRAQ — GOVERNMENT ESCALATES ITS SOVEREIGNTY OBJECTION AND SUMMONS BOTH AMBASSADORS (July 29-30, 2026): The National Security Council chaired by Prime Minister Ali al-Zaidi called the strikes on PMF sites across seven provinces \"a flagrant violation of Iraq's sovereignty and the sanctity of its lands\" and \"an aggression in contravention of the principles of international law and the United Nations Charter.\" The presidency called them \"an unacceptable attack and a blatant violation of Iraq's sovereignty, targeting its official security institutions.\" The foreign ministry summoned the US chargé d'affaires and, separately, the Iranian ambassador. Baghdad asserted a right to respond and to self-defence using available means. Iran-aligned factions condemned a \"dangerous escalation.\" (Al Jazeera; Rudaw; Fox News; NPR; The Jerusalem Post, July 29-30, 2026.)",
    "GCC ECONOMIC BASELINE (as of late July 2026): The World Bank's 2026 regional growth forecast remains cut from 4.4% to 1.3%; Oxford Economics projects some GCC economies entering recession in the second half of the year. Around 80 energy facilities — production plants, refineries and pipelines — have been targeted since February. Strikes on Qatar's Ras Laffan LNG infrastructure on March 18 damaged 17-20% of production capacity, with restoration estimated at three to five years and roughly $20bn a year in lost revenue; S&P Global projects a 5% contraction in Qatar's real GDP. The UAE continues to export up to 1.8m bpd via the Habshan-Fujairah pipeline to the Gulf of Oman, bypassing the Strait. (The Conversation; Stimson Center; Al Jazeera; IMF Regional Economic Outlook, April 2026.)",
    "RED SEA — TRAFFIC STILL DEPRESSED AFTER HOUTHI ATTACKS ON SAUDI SHIPPING (late July 2026): Following Houthi attacks on Saudi Red Sea oil depots and shipping, Bab el-Mandeb transits fell to 11 cargo vessels in a day, the lowest in months. The Houthi maritime embargo of Saudi Arabia declared on July 20 remains in force, with missiles and drones deployed near the strait. Roughly 8% of the world's seaborne oil passes through Bab el-Mandeb. (The Hill, July 27, 2026; Al Jazeera, July 20, 2026; CNBC, July 22, 2026.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "Predictions that the Gulf monarchies would be drawn into direct belligerency remain PARTIALLY CONFIRMED and are now pulling visibly in two directions inside the same government: Saudi Arabia flew its first claimed strikes inside Iraq on July 28 and sent its defence minister to the Oval Office on July 29 to argue for de-escalation and against Israeli escalation. Predictions that the war would remain geographically confined to the Gulf and its immediate approaches are DISCONFIRMED by the Damietta strike — the first attack on Egyptian soil, on the Mediterranean, against a gas IMPORT terminal. Predictions of catastrophic Gulf economic damage remain CONFIRMED and stable: growth forecast cut from 4.4% to 1.3%, ~80 energy facilities struck, Qatar facing a 5% real GDP contraction and a three-to-five-year Ras Laffan restoration.",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "The Damietta drone strike is attributed to Iran by the US President and left unattributed by Egypt, which investigated it and named no one.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented: the strike, the two vessels, the timing, the evacuation, the absence of injuries, and Egypt's cabinet confirmation that a drone caused the fire. Documented: Trump's public attribution to Iran. Not documented: any claim of responsibility, forensic or debris attribution, launch-point assessment, or corroborating intelligence statement from any government. Egypt named no perpetrator.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "President Trump, blaming Iran without presenting evidence",
      "Egypt's Cabinet, confirming a drone caused the fire while noting no party had claimed responsibility",
      "Maritime trade press reporting the attack as unattributed"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "An unclaimed one-way drone against a fixed port target produces the same public record regardless of origin, and the host government has an evident interest in not attributing an attack it would then be obliged to answer. Only debris analysis, a launch track, an intercept or a claim would resolve it.",
     "open_question_ref": "damietta-drone-attribution-july-2026"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-07-31",
   "headline": "DAY 155 RUSSIA-UKRAINE — RUSSIA FIRES 70+ MISSILES AND ~280 DRONES OVERNIGHT, KILLING AT LEAST EIGHT (LATER REPORTS: NINE AND TEN), WITH KYIV AND LVIV THE MAIN TARGETS — AND A RUSSIAN CRUISE MISSILE CROSSES INTO POLAND. NATO CONFIRMS THE INCURSION, SCRAMBLES JETS, AND SAYS THE MISSILE LANDED IN A RURAL AREA; TUSK TELLS AN EMERGENCY MEETING 'ALL THE INDICATIONS ARE THAT IT WAS A RUSSIAN KH-101... BUT WE WANT TO BE 100% CERTAIN ABOUT THE TYPE OF MISSILE AND WHO LAUNCHED IT,' WITH NO DIRECT THREAT. THE DECISIVE NUMBER IS THE INTERCEPT RATIO: UKRAINE DOWNED 55 OF 70+ MISSILES AND 265 OF ~280 DRONES BUT ONLY ONE OF NINE BALLISTIC MISSILES — THE EXACT GAP PATRIOT FILLS, TWO DAYS AFTER ZELENSKY WENT TO THE OVAL OFFICE TO BEG FOR THEM AND WHILE CENTCOM RUNS WAVES TWICE THE SIZE OF THEIR PREDECESSORS OUT OF THE SAME MAGAZINE.",
   "theatre": "Russia-Ukraine War",
   "tag": "Russia-Ukraine",
   "tags": [
    "Russia-Ukraine",
    "70+ Missiles",
    "280 Drones",
    "Kyiv",
    "Lviv",
    "At Least 8 Killed",
    "Kh-101 In Poland",
    "NATO Scrambles Jets",
    "Tusk",
    "Article 4 Pressure",
    "1 Of 9 Ballistic Intercepted",
    "Patriot Shortage",
    "Two Wars One Magazine",
    "Graham Sanctions Act 86-12",
    "Untested"
   ],
   "source": "Just Security, 'Early Edition: July 30, 2026'; NPR / Associated Press, 'Russian strikes across Ukraine kill 10 and a missile falls in Poland,' July 30, 2026; The Philadelphia Inquirer / AP, 'Russian strikes across Ukraine kill 10 and NATO scrambles jets after a missile falls in Poland,' July 30, 2026; Daily Herald / AP, July 30, 2026; Polish Prime Minister Donald Tusk, emergency meeting remarks, July 30, 2026; Ukrainian Foreign Minister Andrii Sybiha, July 30, 2026; Ukrainian Air Force interception data via AP, July 30, 2026.",
   "summary": "Russia ran one of its largest combined strikes of the summer overnight into July 30 and put a cruise missile into NATO territory in the process.\n\nTHE STRIKE. Russia attacked Ukraine with more than 70 missiles and about 280 drones, killing at least eight people by President Zelensky's count, with later reports putting the toll at nine and ten. Kyiv and the western Lviv region — on the Polish border — were the main focus. Ukraine's air force said it shot down 55 missiles and 265 drones but only one of nine ballistic missiles. That single ratio is the story of the cycle: Ukrainian air defence remains highly effective against cruise missiles and drones and nearly helpless against ballistic ones, which is precisely the gap Patriot interceptors fill, and precisely the inventory the Iran war is consuming.\n\nTHE MISSILE IN POLAND. Ukrainian Foreign Minister Andrii Sybiha said a Russian cruise missile crossed into Poland during the strike. NATO confirmed a Russian missile entered Polish airspace and landed in a rural area; NATO scrambled jets. Polish Prime Minister Donald Tusk told an emergency meeting that \"all the indications are that it was a Russian Kh-101 ballistic missile, but we want to be 100% certain about the type of missile and who launched it,\" adding there was no direct threat because it fell in an uninhabited area. Tusk's characterisation of the Kh-101 as \"ballistic\" is a misdescription — it is an air-launched cruise missile — and the qualification he attached to it (\"we want to be 100% certain\") is the operative part.\n\nTHE CONTEXT. The attack came two days after Zelensky met Trump and US defence company representatives in Washington, pleading for faster and larger Patriot deliveries specifically to stop Russian ballistic missiles. It also came two days after the Senate advanced the Lindsey O. Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act of 2026 by 86-12, which would authorise tariffs of up to 200% on the largest purchasers of Russian oil and gas. The competition between two US-supported wars for one depleted interceptor magazine is now the central material fact of both.",
   "details": [
    "LARGE COMBINED STRIKE ON UKRAINE (overnight July 29-30, 2026): Russia attacked with more than 70 missiles and approximately 280 drones. President Zelensky put the death toll at at least eight; subsequent Associated Press-sourced reports gave nine and then ten killed, with dozens injured. Kyiv and the western Lviv region were the main focus. (Just Security, 'Early Edition: July 30, 2026'; NPR/AP; The Philadelphia Inquirer/AP; Daily Herald/AP, July 30, 2026.)",
    "THE INTERCEPTION RATIO IS THE STORY (July 30, 2026): Ukraine's air force said it shot down 55 of the 70+ missiles and 265 of the ~280 drones, but only ONE of NINE ballistic missiles. Cruise and drone defence is holding; ballistic defence is not. That gap is what Patriot interceptors close, and Patriot and THAAD stocks are separately reported to be three or more years from restoration after more than 11,000 munitions were expended in the first sixteen days of the resumed Iran phase. (AP via NPR; The Intercept, July 28, 2026.)",
    "RUSSIAN MISSILE CROSSES INTO POLAND; NATO SCRAMBLES JETS (July 30, 2026): Ukrainian Foreign Minister Andrii Sybiha said a Russian cruise missile crossed into Poland during the strike. NATO confirmed a Russian missile entered Polish airspace and landed in a rural, uninhabited area, and scrambled jets. Polish Prime Minister Donald Tusk told an emergency meeting: \"all the indications are that it was a Russian Kh-101 ballistic missile, but we want to be 100% certain about the type of missile and who launched it,\" adding there was no direct threat. Tusk ordered an investigation. (Note: the Kh-101 is an air-launched CRUISE missile, not a ballistic missile; the Prime Minister's on-the-record characterisation is recorded as given.) (Just Security; NPR/AP; The Philadelphia Inquirer/AP, July 30, 2026.)",
    "CONTEXT — ZELENSKY'S PATRIOT APPEAL AND THE SANCTIONS BILL (July 28-30, 2026): The strike came two days after Zelensky met Trump and representatives of US defence companies in Washington to press for swifter and larger Patriot deliveries, and two days after the Senate advanced the Lindsey O. Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act of 2026 by 86-12 — a bill authorising tariffs of up to 200% on the largest purchasers of Russian oil and natural gas, including China and India, and expanding sanctions on Russian financial institutions. (AP; Just Security, July 28-30, 2026.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "Predictions of NATO-Russia direct escalation remain UNTESTED but were pressed again: a Russian cruise missile landed on NATO territory and the alliance scrambled jets, yet Warsaw's response was an investigation and an explicit refusal to attribute before certainty — the same de-escalatory handling as prior incursions, and evidence against predictions of automatic Article 5 cascade. Predictions of a negotiated settlement remain UNTESTED and unmoved; no negotiation development was reported this cycle. Predictions that US munitions capacity would become the binding constraint across both theatres are increasingly CONFIRMED: Ukraine intercepted one of nine ballistic missiles on the same day CENTCOM ran a wave twice the size of its predecessor, with Patriot and THAAD stocks three or more years from restoration."
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-07-31",
   "headline": "DAY 155 MULTI-THEATRE — THE DAMIETTA STRIKE REDRAWS THE ENERGY-RISK MAP FOR THE HORMUZ-DEPENDENT ECONOMIES: THE IMPORT END IS NOW A TARGET TOO. JAPAN ~75% OF OIL, SOUTH KOREA ~60% OF CRUDE, INDIA ~50% OF CRUDE AND 60% OF GAS THROUGH THE STRAIT; QATAR AND THE UAE SUPPLY 99% OF PAKISTAN'S LNG AND 72% OF BANGLADESH'S. US-CHINA PULLS BOTH WAYS IN ONE CYCLE — THE UNVERIFIED REUTERS MANPADS REPORT (BEIJING: 'COMPLETELY GROUNDLESS') AGAINST AN IRANIAN MISSILE KILLING A WORKER IN A CHINESE COMPANY'S BUILDING IN KUWAIT — WHILE XI'S ~SEPT 24 WHITE HOUSE VISIT AND THE NOV 10 TARIFF-EXCLUSION DEADLINE STAY ON THE CALENDAR. NORTH KOREA: KIM'S 92 PUBLIC ACTIVITIES IN H1, 30 MILITARY — HIS HIGHEST FIRST-HALF MILITARY COUNT SINCE 2012 — WITH KANG KON CRUISE-MISSILE TESTS, A SEPTEMBER COMMISSIONING TARGET AND SOHAE SATELLITE PREPARATIONS. LATIN AMERICA: VENEZUELA'S TRANSITION TALKS SET FOR AUGUST 1 WITH MACHADO OUTSIDE THE ROOM; CUBA ANSWERS ITS FOURTH GRID COLLAPSE WITH THE BIGGEST PRIVATE-SECTOR LIBERALISATION OF THE CRISIS; COLOMBIA'S DE LA ESPRIELLA TAKES OFFICE AUGUST 7 PLEDGING TO CUT TIES WITH HAVANA.",
   "theatre": "Multi-theatre",
   "tag": "Multi-theatre",
   "tags": [
    "Multi-theatre",
    "Hormuz-Dependent Economies",
    "Japan",
    "South Korea",
    "India",
    "Pakistan LNG",
    "Bangladesh LNG",
    "Import Terminals Now Targets",
    "US-China",
    "China MANPADS Contested",
    "Chinese Worker Killed In Kuwait",
    "Xi White House Visit",
    "Tariff Exclusions Nov 10",
    "North Korea",
    "Kim 30 Military Activities",
    "Kang Kon Destroyer",
    "Sohae Satellite Prep",
    "Venezuela August 1 Talks",
    "Delcy Rodriguez",
    "Maduro Trial June 2027",
    "Cuba Liberalisation",
    "Cuba Fourth Grid Collapse",
    "Colombia De La Espriella"
   ],
   "source": "Just Security, 'Early Edition: July 30, 2026'; AEI, 'Korean Peninsula Update, July 28, 2026'; Wikipedia, '2026 in North Korea'; ABC News, 'Ousted Venezuelan leader Nicolas Maduro will stand trial June 1, 2027'; NPR, \"U.S. lifts sanctions on Venezuela's acting President Delcy Rodriguez,\" April 2, 2026; Associated Press, \"Colombia's leader to visit Venezuela for key talks with acting President Delcy Rodriguez\"; CNBC, 'Xi warns Trump: Mishandling Taiwan will put U.S.-China relationship in great jeopardy,' May 14, 2026; CSIS, 'Trump-Xi 2026 Summit'; Al Jazeera, 'Trump-Xi summit: China, US disagree on what they agreed on,' May 15, 2026; CNBC, 'The Strait of Hormuz is facing a blockade. These countries will be most impacted'; Zero Carbon Analytics, 'Asian countries most at risk from oil and gas supply disruptions in Strait of Hormuz'; Gulf International Forum, \"Hormuz Disruptions and Asia's Energy Resilience\"; Seatrade Maritime, 'The Strait of Hormuz crisis and its devastating impact on Asia-Gulf trade.'",
   "summary": "The theatres outside the Iran war moved little this cycle, but the linkages between them tightened.\n\nBLOCKADE-AFFECTED ECONOMIES. The Damietta strike changes the shape of the energy-import risk map. Until July 29 the exposure ran through one chokepoint: Japan draws close to three-quarters of its oil through Hormuz, South Korea roughly 60% of its crude, India about half its crude and 60% of its natural gas, and Qatar and the UAE together supply 99% of Pakistan's LNG and 72% of Bangladesh's. South Asia remains the most acutely exposed because Pakistan and Bangladesh have limited storage and little procurement flexibility. What Damietta adds is that the receiving infrastructure is now also a target: an unclaimed drone disabled operations at one of Egypt's main gas import terminals, on the Mediterranean.\n\nUS-CHINA. The relationship is being pulled in two directions simultaneously. The Reuters report that Iran will receive up to 400 Chinese QW-12 and FN-16 MANPADS within weeks — denied as \"completely groundless\" by Beijing and \"absolutely concocted and false\" by Islamabad on the transit claim — remains unresolved and unverified. In the same cycle an Iranian missile destroyed a Chinese company's building in Kuwait and killed a worker there, giving Beijing a direct grievance against Tehran. Neither fact has produced a stated change in Chinese policy. The diplomatic track is still scheduled: Xi's White House visit is expected around September 24 and USTR tariff exclusions on 178 Chinese products run to November 10, against the May 14-15 Beijing summit's unresolved Taiwan warning — Xi telling Trump that mishandling Taiwan would put the relationship in \"great jeopardy.\"\n\nNORTH KOREA. Kim Jong Un recorded 92 public activities in the first half of 2026, 30 of them military — his highest first-half military count since 2012. Weapons tests aboard the guided-missile destroyer Kang Kon on July 3 included evaluation of \"strategic\" cruise missile launch capability, with commissioning targeted for September; Kim has set a build rate of two Choe Hyon-class destroyers a year through 2030 and floated two 10,000-ton cruisers a year. Preparations continue at the Sohae Satellite Launching Grounds for the first military reconnaissance satellite launch since 2024, and Russia's July 22 statement that it no longer supports Korean denuclearisation stands.\n\nLATIN AMERICA. Venezuela's formal transition talks were set for August 1, with acting President Delcy Rodríguez leading one delegation and María Corina Machado outside the room. Maduro's US trial is set for June 1, 2027. Cuba, four island-wide grid collapses into 2026 and starved of Venezuelan fuel since the February tanker interdictions, announced major economic reforms easing restrictions on private business and approving private imports of goods and medicines — the most substantial liberalisation of the crisis. Colombia's president-elect Abelardo de la Espriella takes office on August 7 and has said he will sever relations with Havana and close 14 embassies and 15 consulates.",
   "details": [
    "HORMUZ-EXPOSED ECONOMIES — THE IMPORT END IS NOW ALSO A TARGET (July 29-30, 2026): Japan relies on the Strait for close to three-quarters of its oil imports; South Korea for roughly 60% of its crude; India for about half its crude imports and 60% of its natural gas. Qatar and the UAE account for 99% of Pakistan's LNG imports and 72% of Bangladesh's, and both countries have limited storage and procurement flexibility, making South Asia the most acutely exposed region. The July 29 drone strike on the Energos Winter FSRU and the LNG carrier Gaslog Salem at Damietta disrupted operations at one of Egypt's principal gas IMPORT terminals — the first attack of this war on receiving rather than exporting infrastructure. (CNBC; Zero Carbon Analytics; Gulf International Forum; Seatrade Maritime; The National, July 2026.)",
    "US-CHINA — TWO CONTRADICTORY DATA POINTS IN ONE CYCLE (July 28-30, 2026): The Reuters report that Iran will receive within weeks the first of up to 400 Chinese QW-12 and FN-16 MANPADS worth $60-70m, routed from Urumqi through Pakistan via the Hong Kong intermediary Zhongqing Baoshang International Investment, remains unverified and categorically denied — Beijing calling it \"completely groundless,\" Pakistan's military calling the transit claim \"absolutely concocted and false.\" In the same cycle an Iranian strike destroyed a Chinese company's building in northern Kuwait and killed a worker. Neither event produced a stated change in Chinese policy. (Reuters via U.S. News & World Report, July 28, 2026; Al Arabiya English, July 30, 2026.)",
    "US-CHINA DIPLOMATIC TRACK UNCHANGED (as of July 30, 2026): Xi Jinping's White House visit remains expected around September 24 and USTR's extension of tariff exclusions on 178 Chinese products runs to November 10. The May 14-15 Beijing summit produced divergent US and Chinese readouts and an explicit Taiwan warning from Xi — that mishandling the issue would put the relationship in \"great jeopardy\" — alongside agreement to discuss a reciprocal tariff-reduction framework covering $30bn or more of products and a Chinese halt to rare-earth export restrictions. No new development was reported this cycle. (CNBC; CSIS; Al Jazeera; NPR, May 2026.)",
    "NORTH KOREA — RECORD MILITARY ACTIVITY AND A NAVAL BUILD-OUT (July 2026): Kim Jong Un made 92 public appearances in the first half of 2026, 30 of them military-related — his highest first-half military count since taking power in 2012. Weapons tests aboard the guided-missile destroyer Kang Kon on July 3 included evaluation of \"strategic\" cruise missile launch capability, with Kim calling for testing to complete in time for commissioning in September. He has set a target of two Choe Hyon-class guided-missile destroyers a year through 2030 and suggested two 10,000-ton cruisers a year. Satellite imagery from July 21 shows renovated facilities at the Sohae Satellite Launching Grounds ahead of the first military reconnaissance satellite launch since 2024; Russia's July 22 statement that it no longer supports Korean denuclearisation stands. (AEI, 'Korean Peninsula Update, July 28, 2026'; AEI July 7 and 14 updates; Wikipedia, '2026 in North Korea'.)",
    "VENEZUELA — TRANSITION TALKS SET FOR AUGUST 1 (July 28-30, 2026): Formal transition talks between a delegation led by acting President Delcy Rodríguez and an opposition group that does not include María Corina Machado were set for August 1, following the agreement between representatives of the 2015 National Assembly and the interim government. Rival marches crossed Caracas on July 28. The US lifted sanctions on Rodríguez in April 2026 and has restored diplomatic relations; Treasury's March authorisation permits PDVSA to sell Venezuelan oil directly to US companies and on global markets. Nicolás Maduro and Cilia Flores are set to stand trial in New York on June 1, 2027. (ABC News; NPR; Associated Press; CBS News, 2026.)",
    "CUBA — THE MOST SUBSTANTIAL LIBERALISATION OF THE CRISIS (July 30, 2026): Cuba announced major economic reforms easing restrictions on private business and approving private imports of goods and medicines. The measures follow the fourth island-wide grid collapse of 2026 on July 27, which left all ten million inhabitants without power, and a fuel shortage critical since January driven by the US interdiction of Venezuelan oil shipments that began in February 2026. (Just Security, 'Early Edition: July 30, 2026'; Wikipedia, '2026 Cuban crisis'.)",
    "COLOMBIA — INCOMING GOVERNMENT WILL SEVER TIES WITH HAVANA (July 26-30, 2026): President-elect Abelardo de la Espriella takes office on August 7 and has announced he will close 14 embassies and 15 consulates and sever relations with Cuba. (Reporting compiled July 26-29, 2026.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "Predictions of Chinese material support for Iran remain CONTESTED-UNRESOLVED: the Reuters MANPADS report stands denied by both named governments with no interdiction, imagery or designation published, while an Iranian missile killed a worker in a Chinese company's building in Kuwait in the same cycle. Predictions of a US-China rupture over the Iran war remain UNTESTED — the September summit and the November tariff-exclusion deadline are both still on the calendar. Predictions of DPRK escalation remain PARTIALLY CONFIRMED on capability and unconfirmed on use: record military activity, a naval build-out and satellite-launch preparations, but no test that crosses a stated red line. Predictions of Venezuelan regime consolidation under a US-aligned transition remain PARTIALLY CONFIRMED with the August 1 talks; predictions of Cuban regime collapse remain DISCONFIRMED — the response to four grid failures and a fuel famine has been economic liberalisation, not collapse."
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-07-30",
   "headline": "DAY 154 — THE WAR RESUMES ON THREE RUNGS AT ONCE, AND THE US RETALIATION LANDS IN IRAQ WITH SAUDI JETS ALONGSIDE. IRAN CLAIMS THE JORDAN ATTACK IT WOULD NOT ACKNOWLEDGE A DAY EARLIER: the IRGC says its air force targeted MUWAFFAQ SALTI AIR BASE and a CENTCOM centre, and Jordan's air defences intercepted FIVE missiles. THE IRGC THEN STRUCK AND STOPPED THREE TANKERS IN HORMUZ — its first claimed shipping strikes since the pause. TRUMP: IRAN IS 'GOING TO GET A BEATING' and 'WE'LL BE HITTING THEM HARD.' BUT THE ACTUAL US KINETIC RESPONSE WENT TO IRAQ, NOT IRAN: CENTCOM AND THE SAUDI ARABIAN ARMED FORCES flew JOINT PRECISION STRIKES on militia logistics and weapons sites after 30+ IRGC-directed drone attacks in 72 hours — RIYADH'S FIRST CLAIMED STRIKES INSIDE IRAQ OF THE WAR, invoking ARTICLE 51. PMF: 20 KILLED, 32 WOUNDED. IRAN REJECTS OMAN'S HORMUZ PLAN and counterproposes ONE LANE ENTIRELY IN IRANIAN WATERS plus a reported $1M-PER-SHIP FEE; GHARIBABADI WARNS MINE-CLEARING VESSELS WILL BE 'LEGITIMATE TARGETS.' BRITAIN AND FRANCE REFUSE TO LEAD THE US NAVAL COALITION absent an enduring ceasefire. REUTERS: CHINA TO SHIP UP TO 400 MANPADS TO IRAN WITHIN WEEKS (Beijing: 'completely groundless'). BRENT JUMPS BACK ABOVE $90 (+3.4%). BACKLOG EASES A SECOND DAY (405→339). STILL AIR/MARITIME ONLY — NO LANDING, NO KHARG SEIZURE, NO RELIEF ARG, AND THE DRAFT RULE WAS WITHDRAWN. PREDICTED US GROUND SEIZURE REMAINS DISCONFIRMED.",
   "theatre": "Iran / Strait of Hormuz",
   "tag": "Iran War",
   "tags": [
    "Iran War",
    "Strait of Hormuz",
    "IRGC Claims Jordan Attack",
    "Muwaffaq Salti Air Base",
    "Five Missiles Intercepted",
    "Three Tankers Struck",
    "'Going To Get A Beating'",
    "'We'll Be Hitting Them Hard'",
    "US-Saudi Joint Strikes In Iraq",
    "Article 51",
    "PMF 20 Killed",
    "Iraqi Sovereignty Protest",
    "Oman Plan Rejected",
    "$1m Per Ship Fee",
    "Mine-Clearers 'Legitimate Targets'",
    "UK France Refuse Coalition",
    "China MANPADS To Iran",
    "Brent Back Above $90",
    "Backlog 405→339",
    "No Ground Seizure",
    "Disconfirmed"
   ],
   "source": "U.S. Central Command statement via CNBC, 'Trump says U.S. will give Iran a ‘beating’ after surprise attack,' July 29, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Iran attacks US forces in Jordan as Trump meets Netanyahu,' July 29, 2026; IranWire, 'Iran Fires Ballistic Missiles at US Base in Jordan, CENTCOM Says All Intercepted,' July 29, 2026; Bloomberg, 'Trump Says US Will Strike Iran Hard as War Escalates Again,' July 29, 2026; CBS News live blog, 'U.S. thwarts Iranian missile attack on U.S. troops, strikes Iranian proxies in Iraq with Saudi forces,' July 29, 2026; U.S. Central Command public release, 'U.S., Saudi Forces Strike Iran-Backed Terrorist Sites in Iraq,' July 28, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Iraqi armed groups condemn ‘dangerous escalation’ after US-Saudi strikes,' July 29, 2026; The Week, 'US, Saudi forces launch joint precision strikes in Iraq targeting ‘Iran-aligned terrorists’,' July 29, 2026; Tasnim News Agency, 'Three Trespassing Vessels Stopped in Strait of Hormuz: IRGC,' July 29, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Iran and Oman swap proposals to manage Strait of Hormuz: What we know,' July 29, 2026; IranWire, 'Iran Rejects Oman’s Strait of Hormuz Plan,' July 29, 2026; Muscat Daily, 'Iran rejects joint Hormuz oversight plan, submits counterplan,' July 29, 2026; Democracy Now, 'U.S.-Iran Negotiations Stall as Iran Attacks U.S. Base in Jordan,' July 29, 2026; Just Security, 'Early Edition: July 29, 2026'; POLITICO / E&E News, 'Allies shrug as US pushes for Strait of Hormuz patrol,' July 29, 2026; Reuters via U.S. News & World Report, 'Exclusive: Iran to Get Chinese Shoulder-Launched Missile Systems in Weeks, Sources Say,' July 28, 2026; The Times of Israel, 'Iran set to receive up to 400 Chinese shoulder-fired air-defense systems within weeks,' July 29, 2026; TradingEconomics, Brent crude, July 29, 2026; straits.live Strait of Hormuz tracker, July 29, 2026 23:03Z; AAA Fuel Prices national averages, July 29, 2026.",
   "summary": "Day 154 restarted the war on three rungs simultaneously — ballistic, maritime and air — and the single most informative fact of the cycle is where the American retaliation actually landed. It landed in Iraq, delivered by fighter aircraft, with Saudi jets flying alongside.\n\nFirst, Iran claimed what it had left unclaimed. A day after CENTCOM announced an 'attempted surprise attack' that Tehran neither acknowledged nor explained, the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps stated that its air force had targeted Muwaffaq Salti Air Base and a CENTCOM centre in Jordan. Jordan's air defences intercepted five missiles. The IRGC framed it as open-ended: 'As long as threats against the Islamic Republic of Iran continue and illegal and vicious actions by American forces against our interests continue, the resistance will continue.' No US casualties were reported. This is a material change from the previous cycle's record, in which the launch was documented only by the United States; the attribution is now bilateral and on the record.\n\nSecond, the IRGC returned to the shipping lanes. It said it had struck and stopped three oil tankers in the Strait of Hormuz, claiming the vessels 'continued to move along an unsafe and illegal route, ignoring our warnings.' Tasnim carried it as 'Three Trespassing Vessels Stopped in Strait of Hormuz.' These are the first Iranian strikes on Hormuz shipping claimed since the two-sided halt began on July 24-26. No casualties were reported and no independent confirmation of damage was available through the cycle close. The strikes also formally trigger the doctrine Trump announced on July 22 — one Iranian bridge or power plant destroyed for every ship Iran shoots at in the Strait — which had not been invoked as of the cycle close.\n\nThird, Trump promised heavy retaliation in explicitly non-specific terms. He said Iran was 'going to get a beating' and told Fox News 'we'll be hitting them hard,' repeating that the US had 'destroyed most of' Iran and that he would 'finish the job.' No ground formation, landing or Kharg operation was named.\n\nWhat the United States actually did was strike Iraq. US Central Command announced that it 'and the Saudi Arabian Armed Forces conducted precision strikes in Iraq, July 28, against Iran-aligned terrorists that the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) directed to attack U.S. forces and Saudi energy infrastructure.' The targets were multiple logistics hubs and weapons storage sites. CENTCOM cited more than 30 IRGC-directed drone attacks in the preceding 72 hours, none of which it said succeeded, and more than 600 attempted attacks on US personnel and facilities between February and April. The Saudi participation is the milestone: it is the first time Riyadh has claimed responsibility for launching strikes inside Iraq since the war began. Maj. Gen. Turki Al-Maliki confirmed coordination with CENTCOM and said Saudi air defences had 'intercepted and destroyed multiple drones launched from Iraqi territory aimed at vital petroleum facilities in the Kingdom's Eastern Province and Riyadh region.' The Saudi defence ministry invoked Article 51 of the UN Charter, said the Kingdom 'does not seek escalation but will respond to any aggression it faces,' and reserved the right to respond 'at the appropriate time and place.' Iraq's Popular Mobilisation Forces said at least 20 of its members were killed and 32 wounded, across sites reported in Baghdad, Wasit, Nineveh, Basra, Kirkuk, Karbala and Diyala provinces, and called the strikes 'a highly dangerous escalation' and a violation of Iraqi sovereignty and of official state security institutions. The Iraqi presidency 'categorically rejected any attacks in Iraqi territory' while also opposing 'the use of Iraqi territory as a launching point'; Prime Minister Ali al-Zaidi convened an emergency security meeting. Iran's foreign ministry condemned 'aggressive attacks' violating Iraq's 'national sovereignty and territorial integrity.'\n\nThe Hormuz governance track went backwards. Iran rejected the Gulf-backed Omani proposal outright. Deputy Foreign Minister Kazem Gharibabadi described what was offered: 'Oman proposed to us the creation of a route in the Strait of Hormuz, with 50 percent of it located in our territorial waters and 50 percent in Omani territorial waters' — ships entering through Iranian waters and exiting through Omani, funded by voluntary industry contributions on the Malacca model, which generates roughly $70 million a year. Iran's counterproposal inverts it: 'Our proposal is for one route to lie entirely within the territorial waters of the Islamic Republic of Iran, with part of the other route also passing through Iranian territorial waters,' giving Tehran oversight of both inbound and outbound traffic, with a reported $1 million per ship 'service fee' earmarked for reconstruction. Gharibabadi tied it to war aims — 'If the Strait of Hormuz returns to its previous state, our success in this war is not complete' — argued the June 17 MOU obliged Iran to provide free safe passage 'for only 60 days,' and denied Iran had sought talks: 'there was no begging from the Iranian delegation to continue the negotiations,' with Tehran saying it had not requested direct US negotiations in 15 days.\n\nHe also drew the sharpest maritime tripwire of the war, and it was aimed squarely at the coalition Washington is trying to build: 'We told them explicitly that under no circumstances are they permitted to do so... if they bring equipment or any vessel... for mine-clearing operations near the Strait of Hormuz, they will be considered legitimate targets.' Demining, Iran says, is Iran's responsibility, and US warships are not permitted near the Strait.\n\nThat coalition is not forming. POLITICO reported that Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth is urging the United Kingdom and France to help lead the naval mission, with UK Defence Minister Wes Streeting assisting, and that European allies are refusing to support it 'until they see an enduring ceasefire in the Iran war.' Four British officials and one US official, all anonymous, said no movement was expected soon. The planned London conference is intended to assign operational roles and identify which nations would contribute warships for mine-clearing and shipping protection; the two governments Washington wants at the head of it have declined for now.\n\nA separate supply-side development cuts across the China file. Reuters reported that Iran is expected to receive within weeks the first shipment of up to 400 Chinese-made shoulder-fired air-defence launchers — 300 to 400 QW-12 and FN-16 MANPADS worth $60-70 million, contracted through a Hong Kong intermediary, Zhongqing Baoshang International Investment, and routed from Urumqi through Pakistan. It would be Tehran's largest known effort to rebuild short-range air defence since the war exposed those gaps. China's foreign ministry called the report 'completely groundless,' saying China 'has consistently played a role in promoting peace and ending the conflict'; Pakistan's military called the claims 'absolutely concocted and false.'\n\nMarkets reversed. Brent jumped back above $90 — the straits.live tracker showed $90.40, up 3.43% (+$3.00) over 24 hours at 23:03Z on July 29, against a reported July 28 close of $84.09 (-4.8%) — ending a three-session slide that had taken roughly $14 off the July 23 settle of $100.69. Pump prices barely moved: AAA's national average was $4.091 for regular and $5.329 for diesel, another war high for diesel. The Strait itself eased for a second day from a still-closed baseline: 339 vessels anchored or stopped (down from 405), 68 tankers running dark (down from 77), with IMF PortWatch's most recent count still 10 transits on July 23 against an 88-per-day pre-crisis baseline — about 11% of normal.\n\nCasualties. The Pentagon confirmed that 'Operation Epic Fury has concluded' as the stated rationale for its new 'Overseas Operations' casualty category, while declining to give the operation a formal end date; the total wounded across both categories now stands at 642. On the Iranian side the standing HRANA cumulative baseline is 3,636 deaths (1,221 military, 1,701 civilian, 714 unclassified) against the Foundation of Martyrs' 3,468, with US and Israeli estimates at 6,000 or more and injured estimates ranging from 15,000 to the Iranian Health Ministry's 26,500. Political executions since February 28 reached at least 50 with public hangings in Isfahan on July 28 — a toll distinct from war casualties.\n\nEvery ground execution indicator stayed at zero for a 154th consecutive day.",
   "details": [
    "IRAN CLAIMS THE JORDAN ATTACK (July 29, 2026): The IRGC said its air force had targeted Muwaffaq Salti Air Base and a CENTCOM centre in Jordan, reversing a day of silence in which the launch was documented only by CENTCOM. Jordan's air defences intercepted five missiles. IRGC statement: 'As long as threats against the Islamic Republic of Iran continue and illegal and vicious actions by American forces against our interests continue, the resistance will continue.' No US casualties reported. Analysts quoted by Al Jazeera read it as Iran 'sending a message to Trump that they need to be involved in all discussions' and attempting to 'retake the initiative and momentum.' (Al Jazeera; IranWire, July 29, 2026.)",
    "IRGC STRIKES AND STOPS THREE TANKERS IN HORMUZ (July 29, 2026): The IRGC said it had struck and halted three oil tankers, claiming they 'continued to move along an unsafe and illegal route, ignoring our warnings.' Tasnim carried the item as 'Three Trespassing Vessels Stopped in Strait of Hormuz.' These are the first Iranian strikes on Hormuz shipping claimed since the two-sided halt began. No casualties were reported and no independent confirmation of damage was available through the cycle close. The strikes meet the stated trigger for Trump's July 22 doctrine of destroying one Iranian bridge or power plant per ship attacked, which had not been invoked as of the cycle close. (Tasnim News Agency; Al Jazeera; CBS News, July 29, 2026.)",
    "TRUMP THREATENS A 'BEATING' (July 29, 2026): Trump said Iran was 'going to get a beating' for the attempted overnight missile attack on US forces in Jordan and told Fox News 'we'll be hitting them hard.' He repeated that the US had 'destroyed most of' Iran and would 'finish the job' by resuming strikes absent a deal. No ground formation, amphibious operation or Kharg landing was named. (Bloomberg; CNBC; CBS News; Al Jazeera, July 29, 2026.)",
    "US AND SAUDI FORCES FLY JOINT PRECISION STRIKES IN IRAQ — RIYADH'S FIRST CLAIMED STRIKES INSIDE IRAQ OF THE WAR (July 28, 2026): CENTCOM: 'U.S. Central Command and the Saudi Arabian Armed Forces conducted precision strikes in Iraq, July 28, against Iran-aligned terrorists that the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) directed to attack U.S. forces and Saudi energy infrastructure.' Targets were multiple logistics hubs and weapons storage sites. CENTCOM cited more than 30 IRGC-directed aerial drone attacks in the previous 72 hours, none successful, and more than 600 attempted attacks on US personnel and facilities between February and April 2026. Saudi Maj. Gen. Turki Al-Maliki confirmed coordination with CENTCOM and said Saudi air defences 'intercepted and destroyed multiple drones launched from Iraqi territory aimed at vital petroleum facilities in the Kingdom's Eastern Province and Riyadh region.' Riyadh invoked Article 51 of the UN Charter, said the Kingdom 'does not seek escalation but will respond to any aggression it faces,' and reserved the right to respond 'at the appropriate time and place.' (U.S. Central Command public release; The Week; The Jerusalem Post; Al Jazeera, July 28-29, 2026.)",
    "IRAQI CASUALTIES AND SOVEREIGNTY PROTEST (July 29, 2026): Iraq's Popular Mobilisation Forces said at least 20 of its members were killed and 32 wounded, with strike sites reported across Baghdad, Wasit, Nineveh, Basra, Kirkuk, Karbala and Diyala provinces, and called the operation 'a highly dangerous escalation' and a violation of Iraqi sovereignty and of official state security institutions. The Iraqi presidency 'categorically rejected any attacks in Iraqi territory' while also opposing 'the use of Iraqi territory as a launching point.' Prime Minister Ali al-Zaidi convened an emergency security meeting. Iran's foreign ministry condemned 'aggressive attacks' violating Iraq's 'national sovereignty and territorial integrity.' (Al Jazeera; CBS News, July 29, 2026.)",
    "IRAN REJECTS OMAN'S HORMUZ PLAN AND SUBMITS A COUNTERPLAN (July 29, 2026): Deputy Foreign Minister Kazem Gharibabadi on the Omani offer: 'Oman proposed to us the creation of a route in the Strait of Hormuz, with 50 percent of it located in our territorial waters and 50 percent in Omani territorial waters' — ships entering through Iranian waters and exiting through Omani, funded by voluntary industry contributions on the Strait of Malacca model, which generates roughly $70 million a year. Iran's counterproposal: 'Our proposal is for one route to lie entirely within the territorial waters of the Islamic Republic of Iran, with part of the other route also passing through Iranian territorial waters,' giving Tehran oversight of both inbound and outbound traffic, with a reported $1 million per ship 'service fee' earmarked for reconstruction. Gharibabadi: 'If the Strait of Hormuz returns to its previous state, our success in this war is not complete.' He said the June 17 MOU obliged Iran to provide free safe passage 'for only 60 days.' (Al Jazeera; IranWire; Muscat Daily; Container News; Business Standard, July 29, 2026.)",
    "IRAN DECLARES MINE-CLEARING VESSELS 'LEGITIMATE TARGETS' (July 29, 2026): Gharibabadi: 'We told them explicitly that under no circumstances are they permitted to do so... if they bring equipment or any vessel... for mine-clearing operations near the Strait of Hormuz, they will be considered legitimate targets.' He said demining is Iran's own responsibility, that Iran would not permit third-country mine-clearing, and that US warships were not allowed near the Strait. He also denied Iran had sought talks: 'there was no begging from the Iranian delegation to continue the negotiations,' with Tehran stating it had not requested direct negotiations with Washington in 15 days. (Democracy Now; Just Security Early Edition; CBS News, July 29, 2026.)",
    "BRITAIN AND FRANCE DECLINE TO LEAD THE US NAVAL COALITION (July 29, 2026): POLITICO reported that Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth is urging the UK and France to help lead the Hormuz naval mission, with UK Defence Minister Wes Streeting assisting, and that 'European allies are refusing to support a U.S. push to create a joint Strait of Hormuz patrol until they see an enduring ceasefire in the Iran war.' Four British officials and one US official, all anonymous, said no movement was expected soon. The planned London conference is intended to establish operational roles and determine which nations would contribute warships for mine-clearing and civilian shipping protection. The report characterises the effort as the most concerted US attempt to seek allied assistance since the war began. (POLITICO / E&E News; Ynetnews, July 29, 2026.)",
    "REUTERS: CHINA TO DELIVER UP TO 400 MANPADS TO IRAN WITHIN WEEKS (July 28-29, 2026): Three sources familiar with the deal said Iran is expected to receive within weeks the first shipment of 300-400 Chinese-made man-portable air-defence systems — QW-12 and FN-16 infrared-guided launchers — valued at $60-70 million, contracted through the Hong Kong-based intermediary Zhongqing Baoshang International Investment and routed from Urumqi in western China through Pakistan. It would be Tehran's largest known effort to rebuild short-range air defence since the war exposed gaps in its ability to protect military sites and strategic infrastructure. China's foreign ministry: 'The relevant reports are completely groundless. China has consistently played a role in promoting peace and ending the conflict.' Pakistan's military called the claims 'absolutely concocted and false.' (Reuters via U.S. News & World Report; The Times of Israel; Iran International; Japan Times, July 28-29, 2026.)",
    "MARKETS REVERSE — BRENT BACK ABOVE $90 (July 29, 2026): The straits.live tracker showed Brent at $90.40, up 3.43% (+$3.00) over 24 hours at 23:03Z, with a 7-day range of $101.47 high to $82.51 low; CNBC headlined 'Brent oil jumps back above $90 after Trump threatens to hit Iran hard,' after reporting a July 28 close of $84.09, down 4.8%. The move ends a three-session slide that had taken roughly $14 off the July 23 settle of $100.69. Still far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (straits.live; CNBC, July 29, 2026.)",
    "PUMP PRICES AND THE STRAIT (July 29, 2026): AAA's national average was $4.0910 for regular and $5.3290 for diesel, another war high for diesel. The straits.live tracker recorded 339 vessels anchored or stopped in the Hormuz zone (down from 405), a 7-day range of 297-540, and 68 tankers running dark in 24 hours (down from 77). IMF PortWatch's most recent count remains 10 commercial transits on July 23 against an 88-per-day pre-crisis baseline — about 11% of normal throughput. The tracker's day count for the closure stood at 151. (AAA Fuel Prices; straits.live, July 29, 2026.)",
    "CASUALTIES (July 28-29, 2026): A Pentagon official said that 'because Operation Epic Fury has concluded, the military has decided to classify casualties tied to the latest strikes in the new category,' but would not give a formal end date for the operation; total wounded across the Operation Epic Fury and 'Overseas Operations' categories now stands at 642. The four reclassified deaths are the three soldiers killed when Iranian ballistic missiles and drones struck a base in Jordan on July 17 and one killed in Iraq on July 18 during a controlled detonation of an Iranian drone. On the Iranian side, the standing HRANA cumulative baseline is 3,636 deaths (1,221 military, 1,701 civilian, 714 unclassified) against the Foundation of Martyrs' 3,468; US and Israeli estimates put total Iranian deaths at 6,000 or more, with injured estimates ranging from 15,000 (US/Israeli) to 26,500 (Iranian Health Ministry). Political executions since February 28 reached at least 50 with public hangings in Isfahan on July 28 — a toll distinct from war casualties. (Associated Press via KSAT/US News/Washington Times, July 28, 2026; casualty trackers, July 29, 2026.)",
    "PUBLIC OPINION (July 27-29, 2026): The Reuters/Ipsos survey remains the most recent: 33% of Americans approve of US attacks on Iran and 62% disapprove; Trump's overall approval is 37%, up three points from the prior month; nearly 70% say Trump has not clearly explained the goals of US military involvement in Iran; Republicans approve 71% to just over a quarter disapproving. War approval has stayed below 40% since the February 28 strikes — against roughly 70% for the Iraq War's early months and about 90% for Afghanistan's. (Reuters/Ipsos via The Hill, Detroit News, Al-Monitor, July 27, 2026.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "The corpus-predicted US GROUND seizure of Hormuz/Kharg remains DISCONFIRMED, and Day 154 is among the strongest negative cycles of the war for it, because escalation resumed on every rung EXCEPT the ground one. Iran escalated ballistically (a claimed strike on Muwaffaq Salti Air Base) and maritimely (three tankers struck). The United States escalated by AIR and OUTSIDE IRAN, striking militia logistics in Iraq with Saudi aircraft alongside. Trump's threats remained categorically unspecific and aerial — 'a beating,' 'hitting them hard,' 'finish the job.' No ground formation, landing or Kharg operation was named by any party. Two structural indicators moved AGAINST a ground phase in the same cycle: the Selective Service withdrew its proposed automatic-registration rule on July 23 after the White House sat on it for nearly four months, the first backward movement on the draft rung of the war; and the only force generation actually under way — an allied naval escort coalition — was refused by both governments Washington asked to lead it. Predictions of a negotiated Hormuz transit regime remain live but moved sharply AWAY from settlement: Iran rejected the Omani 50-50 plan, counterproposed sole control of the inbound lane plus a reported $1m-per-ship fee, and declared third-party mine-clearing vessels legitimate targets. Predictions of monotonic escalation to Iranian capitulation remain disconfirmed — the United States has still not struck Iranian territory since July 24. Predictions of Chinese material support to Iran gain their first concrete, if denied, sourcing. (CONTESTED-UNRESOLVED — open-questions: china-manpads-transfer-iran-july-2026; us-saudi-iraq-strikes-pmf-status-july-2026; selective-service-rule-withdrawal-july-2026; iran-hormuz-administration-tolls-dispute-june-2026; us-iran-direct-talks-existence-july-2026; iran-strike-pause-reload-or-climbdown-july-2026; pentagon-casualty-reclassification-july-2026; kharg-seizure-total-control-threat-june-2026.)",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "Whether China is arming Iran with hundreds of shoulder-fired air-defence launchers is asserted by Reuters on three sources and flatly denied by both governments named in the transit chain.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented as reporting, denied as fact. Reuters, citing three sources familiar with the deal, reported a signed contract for 300-400 QW-12 and FN-16 MANPADS worth $60-70 million, a named Hong Kong intermediary (Zhongqing Baoshang International Investment), a first shipment expected within weeks, and a planned route from Urumqi through Pakistan. China's foreign ministry called the reports 'completely groundless'; Pakistan's military called them 'absolutely concocted and false'; the intermediary's parent group did not respond to requests for comment. Not documented: any interdiction, manifest, flight record, imagery, or delivery. No Western government has publicly confirmed or contradicted the report.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "Reuters, citing three sources familiar with the deal, in an exclusive carried by U.S. News & World Report, The Times of Israel, Japan Times and Iran International",
      "China's Ministry of Foreign Affairs, denying the report as 'completely groundless'",
      "Pakistan's military, denying any involvement as 'absolutely concocted and false'"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "A covert arms transfer routed through a commercial intermediary and a third-country air corridor produces the same open-source signature whether it is real or not: anonymous sourcing, a corporate cut-out that does not comment, and categorical state denials. Only interdiction, imagery of the systems in Iranian service, a sanctions designation naming the transaction, or an on-record intelligence assessment would resolve it.",
     "open_question_ref": "china-manpads-transfer-iran-july-2026"
    },
    {
     "reading": "The US-Saudi strikes in Iraq are read either as precision strikes on IRGC-directed militia logistics, or as an attack on Iraq's own official state security apparatus — and the Popular Mobilisation Forces are formally both.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented on both sides, with no independent adjudication of the targets. CENTCOM described 'precision strikes... against Iran-aligned terrorists that the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) directed to attack U.S. forces and Saudi energy infrastructure,' hitting 'multiple logistics hubs and weapons storage sites,' and cited 30+ IRGC-directed drone attacks in 72 hours. The PMF — a body legally incorporated into the Iraqi state security structure — said at least 20 of its members were killed and 32 wounded and called the strikes a violation of Iraqi sovereignty and of 'official security institutions.' The Iraqi presidency rejected both the attacks and the use of Iraqi territory as a launching point, a formulation that concedes part of the American premise while rejecting the action. Not documented: independent battle-damage assessment, target lists, or verification of which specific units or facilities were struck in each of the seven named provinces.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "U.S. Central Command, in its July 28 public release describing the targets as IRGC-directed terrorist logistics and weapons sites",
      "Saudi Arabia's defence ministry and Maj. Gen. Turki Al-Maliki, invoking Article 51 self-defence against drones aimed at Eastern Province and Riyadh petroleum facilities",
      "Iraq's Popular Mobilisation Forces, describing the dead as its own members and the strikes as an attack on official Iraqi security institutions",
      "The Iraqi presidency and Prime Minister Ali al-Zaidi, rejecting attacks on Iraqi territory while opposing its use as a launching point",
      "Iran's foreign ministry, condemning violations of Iraq's national sovereignty and territorial integrity"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "The PMF's dual legal status — an Iraqi state security institution containing factions that operate under Iranian direction — means both characterisations can be simultaneously accurate about different elements at the same sites, and neither government has published target lists or damage assessments. Only an independent Iraqi parliamentary or UN inquiry naming the struck units and facilities would resolve it.",
     "open_question_ref": "us-saudi-iraq-strikes-pmf-status-july-2026"
    },
    {
     "reading": "The Selective Service's withdrawal of its automatic-registration rule is read either as a political retreat from anything resembling a draft, or as a procedural reset before a re-proposal that still has to meet a December statutory deadline.",
     "evidentiary_status": "The procedural record is documented; the intent is not. The Selective Service System submitted proposed rules to the White House on March 30, 2026 to implement the automatic-registration mandate Congress enacted in December 2025, and withdrew them on July 23, 2026 after the White House held them for nearly four months without acting. The statutory deadline for automatic registration to begin — December 19, 2026 — is unchanged, and separate reporting notes the agency is procuring a draft mobilisation simulator. Not documented: any White House statement on the withdrawal, any explanation from the agency, or any re-proposal. No draft has been called and no formal plans to reinstate one exist.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "Antiwar.com, characterising the withdrawal as 'a sign that the White House has recognized that moving toward a draft will be politically costly' while noting 'automatic draft registration is not yet a done deal'",
      "[your]NEWS, reporting the agency's parallel procurement of a draft mobilisation simulator as automatic registration nears",
      "Fast Company and CNN, both continuing to report that no draft is planned and that automatic registration does not activate one"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "A rule withdrawn without explanation, from an agency facing an unchanged statutory deadline, is consistent with both political retreat and administrative reset; the White House issued no statement either way. Only a re-proposed rule, a request to Congress to postpone or rescind the December 19 deadline, or an on-record explanation would resolve it.",
     "open_question_ref": "selective-service-rule-withdrawal-july-2026"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-07-30",
   "headline": "DAY 154 GROUND-INVASION TRACKER — STATUS HOLDS no_ground_troops, AND TWO STRUCTURAL INDICATORS MOVED BACKWARD IN THE SAME CYCLE. (1) THE DRAFT RUNG FELL FOR THE FIRST TIME IN THE WAR: the SELECTIVE SERVICE WITHDREW its proposed automatic-registration rules on JULY 23 after the White House held them nearly FOUR MONTHS without acting; the December 19, 2026 statutory deadline is unchanged but no rule is pending. (2) ALLIED FORCE GENERATION WAS REFUSED: BRITAIN AND FRANCE declined to lead Hegseth's Hormuz naval coalition absent an enduring ceasefire. MEANWHILE THE US KINETIC RESPONSE TO IRAN'S ESCALATION WENT TO IRAQ, BY AIR, WITH SAUDI JETS — not to Iranian soil and not by ground. TRUMP'S THREATS STAYED CATEGORICAL AND AERIAL: 'a beating,' 'we'll be hitting them hard,' 'finish the job.' IRAN'S OWN TRIPWIRE IS MARITIME: mine-clearing vessels are 'LEGITIMATE TARGETS.' AMPHIBIOUS PICTURE UNCHANGED SEVEN DAYS ON: BOXER ARG (Boxer LHD-4, Comstock, Portland, 11th MEU) in the Arabian Sea and still the SOLE DEPLOYED ARG fleet-wide; TRIPOLI home in Sasebo; NO RELIEF ARG. Kharg fortified and struck from the air, not seized. ~50,000 personnel unchanged.",
   "theatre": "Iran / Strait of Hormuz",
   "tag": "Ground Invasion Tracker",
   "tags": [
    "Ground Invasion Tracker",
    "no_ground_troops",
    "Draft Rule Withdrawn",
    "Selective Service",
    "Dec 19 Deadline Unchanged",
    "UK France Refuse Coalition",
    "Retaliation Went To Iraq",
    "Air Not Ground",
    "Saudi Jets Alongside",
    "'A Beating'",
    "Mine-Clearers 'Legitimate Targets'",
    "Boxer Sole Deployed ARG",
    "11th MEU Arabian Sea",
    "No Relief ARG",
    "Kharg Fortified Not Seized",
    "~50,000 Personnel",
    "Disconfirmed"
   ],
   "source": "Antiwar.com, 'Selective Service Withdraws Proposed Rules for ‘Automatic’ Draft Registration,' July 24, 2026; [your]NEWS, 'Selective Service Seeks Draft Mobilization Simulator as Automatic Registration Nears,' July 28, 2026; CNBC, 'Automatic U.S. military draft registration by December 2026 planned,' April 9, 2026; CNN Politics, 'Automatic military draft registration takes effect in the US in December 2026. Here’s how it would work,' April 9, 2026; POLITICO / E&E News, 'Allies shrug as US pushes for Strait of Hormuz patrol,' July 29, 2026; USNI News Fleet and Marine Tracker, July 27, 2026; The War Zone, 'Where Are The Aircraft Carriers: July 28, 2026'; U.S. Central Command public release, 'U.S., Saudi Forces Strike Iran-Backed Terrorist Sites in Iraq,' July 28, 2026; Democracy Now, 'U.S.-Iran Negotiations Stall as Iran Attacks U.S. Base in Jordan,' July 29, 2026 (Gharibabadi on mine-clearing vessels as legitimate targets); Foreign Policy, 'How a U.S. Ground Operation in Iran Could Unfold,' July 24, 2026; CNN, 'Iran building up defenses of Kharg Island to protect against potential US ground attack,' March 25, 2026; Associated Press via KSAT, 'Pentagon ties new casualty category for renewed US-Iran fighting to the end of ‘Operation Epic Fury’,' July 28, 2026; Reuters/Ipsos poll via The Hill and Detroit News, July 27, 2026.",
   "summary": "Day 154 is the cycle in which escalation resumed on every rung except the ground one, and two of the structural preconditions for a ground phase moved backward at the same time.\n\nTHE DRAFT RUNG FELL. On July 23, 2026 the Selective Service System withdrew the proposed rules for automatic registration of young men for a military draft that it had submitted to the White House on March 30. The White House had held the proposal for nearly four months without acting on it, and issued no statement on the withdrawal. This is the first backward movement on the draft indicator since the project began tracking it: every prior data point — the December 2025 NDAA mandate, the March submission, the reported December 2026 implementation date — pointed one direction. The statutory deadline itself is unchanged: automatic registration is still required to begin on December 19, 2026 under the law Congress enacted in December 2025, and the agency is separately reported to be procuring a draft mobilisation simulator. But no implementing rule is now pending, the agency faces a compressed timeline to complete multiple administrative proceedings before the deadline, and the alternative is for Congress to postpone or rescind the requirement. Critically, automatic registration was never a draft — it automates the register should a draft be called — and no draft has been called or planned.\n\nALLIED FORCE GENERATION WAS REFUSED. The only force generation actually under way in this war remains maritime and multinational, and this cycle both governments Washington asked to lead it said no. POLITICO reported that Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth is urging the United Kingdom and France to help lead the Hormuz naval mission, with UK Defence Minister Wes Streeting assisting, and that European allies are refusing 'until they see an enduring ceasefire in the Iran war.' Four British officials and one US official said no movement was expected soon. The London conference is still intended to assign operational roles and identify which nations would contribute warships for mine-clearing and shipping protection — a naval escort mission, not a ground commitment — and it cannot presently find its leadership.\n\nTHE US RESPONSE TO IRANIAN ESCALATION WAS AIR, AND IT LANDED OUTSIDE IRAN. Iran escalated on two rungs this cycle: it claimed the ballistic strike on Muwaffaq Salti Air Base in Jordan, and the IRGC struck and stopped three tankers in the Strait. The American answer was a joint CENTCOM-Saudi air operation against militia logistics hubs and weapons storage sites in Iraq, in response to 30+ IRGC-directed drone attacks in 72 hours. That is escalation by fighter aircraft, against a proxy, on the territory of a third state, with a partner air force flying alongside — the opposite of a decision to commit ground forces to Iranian soil. Trump's stated escalation path stayed categorical and aerial: Iran is 'going to get a beating,' 'we'll be hitting them hard,' 'finish the job.' No ground formation, amphibious operation or Kharg landing was named by the President, the Pentagon or CENTCOM.\n\nIRAN'S DECLARED TRIPWIRE REMAINS MARITIME, NOT TERRITORIAL. Deputy Foreign Minister Kazem Gharibabadi's sharpest threat of the cycle was aimed at ships, not troops: mine-clearing vessels and equipment brought near the Strait 'will be considered legitimate targets,' demining is Iran's own responsibility, and US warships are not permitted near the Strait. Iran's stated war aim at the chokepoint is lane control and transit fees — one route entirely in Iranian waters, a reported $1 million per ship — not defence against a landing.\n\nTHE AMPHIBIOUS PICTURE IS UNCHANGED SEVEN DAYS ON. USNI News's July 27 tracker places USS Boxer (LHD-4) in the Arabian Sea with the Boxer Amphibious Ready Group — USS Comstock (LSD-45), USS Portland (LPD-27) and the 11th Marine Expeditionary Unit — alongside expeditionary sea base USS Miguel Keith (ESB-5). The War Zone's July 28 count confirms it as the sole deployed amphibious ready group fleet-wide. USS Tripoli (LHA-7) remains home in Sasebo after disembarking 31st MEU elements at Okinawa on July 22, and seven days on no relief ARG has been announced. Carriers Abraham Lincoln and George H.W. Bush remain in the CENTCOM area of responsibility; George Washington is in the South China Sea; Theodore Roosevelt is leading RIMPAC 2026 near Hawaii.\n\nKharg Island remains fortified and repeatedly struck from the air, and unseized. Troop strength in theatre remains roughly 50,000, unchanged. Every execution indicator — US troops in Iran, a landing, a Kharg seizure, a new ARG or MEU order, a draft — stayed at zero for a 154th consecutive day.",
   "details": [
    "DRAFT INDICATOR MOVES BACKWARD FOR THE FIRST TIME (July 23, 2026): The Selective Service System withdrew the proposed rules for 'automatic' registration of young men for a military draft that it had submitted to the White House for review on March 30, 2026. The White House held the proposal for nearly four months without acting on it and issued no public statement on the withdrawal or on implementation plans. The underlying mandate is unchanged: legislation Congress enacted in December 2025 requires the SSS to identify, locate and register potential draftees automatically using federal agency data, with a statutory start date of December 19, 2026. Antiwar.com characterised the withdrawal as 'a sign that the White House has recognized that moving toward a draft will be politically costly' while cautioning that 'automatic draft registration is not yet a done deal,' and noted the agency must complete multiple administrative proceedings before the deadline or Congress must postpone or rescind it. (Antiwar.com, July 24, 2026; NaturalNews, July 28, 2026.)",
    "SELECTIVE SERVICE PROCURING A DRAFT MOBILISATION SIMULATOR (July 28, 2026): Separate reporting states the agency is seeking a draft mobilisation simulator as automatic registration nears — a preparatory capability, not an activation. No draft has been called; automatic registration automates the register should one be called and does not itself activate a draft; and there are no formal plans to reinstate conscription. ([your]NEWS, July 28, 2026; CNN Politics, April 9, 2026; Fast Company, 'U.S. military draft 2026, Iran war: What you need to know about rumors.')",
    "BRITAIN AND FRANCE DECLINE TO LEAD THE HORMUZ NAVAL COALITION (July 29, 2026): POLITICO reported Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth is urging the UK and France to help lead the naval mission, with UK Defence Minister Wes Streeting assisting, and that 'European allies are refusing to support a U.S. push to create a joint Strait of Hormuz patrol until they see an enduring ceasefire in the Iran war.' Sourced to four British officials and one US official, all anonymous; the sources 'didn't expect any movement soon.' The London conference is intended to establish operational roles and determine which nations would contribute warships for mine-clearing and civilian shipping protection. This is force generation for allied naval escort, not for US ground forces on Iranian territory — and it is currently stalled. (POLITICO / E&E News; Ynetnews, July 29, 2026.)",
    "THE US KINETIC RESPONSE WENT TO IRAQ, BY AIR, WITH A PARTNER AIR FORCE (July 28, 2026): CENTCOM and the Saudi Arabian Armed Forces conducted joint precision strikes in Iraq against 'Iran-aligned terrorists that the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) directed to attack U.S. forces and Saudi energy infrastructure,' hitting multiple logistics hubs and weapons storage sites after 30+ IRGC-directed drone attacks in 72 hours. Riyadh's first claimed strikes inside Iraq of the war, invoked under Article 51. No ground formation was committed and no US or Saudi ground element entered Iran. (U.S. Central Command public release; The Week; Al Jazeera, July 28-29, 2026.)",
    "EVERY STATED US ESCALATION OPTION THIS CYCLE WAS AERIAL AND UNSPECIFIC (July 29, 2026): Trump said Iran was 'going to get a beating' and told Fox News 'we'll be hitting them hard,' repeating that the US had 'destroyed most of' Iran and would 'finish the job.' No ground formation, amphibious operation or Kharg landing was named by the President, the Pentagon or CENTCOM. (Bloomberg; CNBC; CBS News; Al Jazeera, July 29, 2026.)",
    "IRAN'S DECLARED TRIPWIRE IS MARITIME (July 29, 2026): Deputy Foreign Minister Kazem Gharibabadi: 'We told them explicitly that under no circumstances are they permitted to do so... if they bring equipment or any vessel... for mine-clearing operations near the Strait of Hormuz, they will be considered legitimate targets.' Demining is Iran's own responsibility; US warships are not permitted near the Strait. Iran's stated aim at the chokepoint is lane control and transit fees — one route entirely in Iranian territorial waters plus part of the other, with a reported $1 million per ship 'service fee' — not defence against a landing. (Democracy Now; Al Jazeera; Muscat Daily, July 29, 2026.)",
    "AMPHIBIOUS AND CARRIER PICTURE — UNCHANGED SEVEN DAYS ON (July 27-28, 2026): USNI News's July 27 Fleet and Marine Tracker places USS Boxer (LHD-4) in the Arabian Sea with the Boxer ARG — USS Comstock (LSD-45), USS Portland (LPD-27) and the 11th Marine Expeditionary Unit — with expeditionary sea base USS Miguel Keith (ESB-5) also in the Arabian Sea and independently deployed destroyers USS Milius (DDG-69), USS Michael Murphy (DDG-112) and USS John Finn (DDG-113) in the region. The War Zone's July 28 count confirms the Boxer ARG as the sole deployed amphibious ready group fleet-wide. USS Tripoli (LHA-7) remains in Sasebo after disembarking 31st MEU elements at Okinawa on July 22; no relief ARG has been announced seven days on. Carriers Abraham Lincoln and George H.W. Bush remain in the CENTCOM AOR; George Washington (CVN-73) is in the South China Sea; Theodore Roosevelt (CVN-71) is leading RIMPAC 2026 near Hawaii. (USNI News Fleet and Marine Tracker, July 27, 2026; The War Zone, 'Where Are The Aircraft Carriers: July 28, 2026'; Forbes, July 28, 2026.)",
    "KHARG ISLAND — FORTIFIED, STRUCK FROM THE AIR, UNSEIZED (July 29, 2026): No US or allied ground force has landed on or seized Kharg Island. Iran continues to build up its defences there against a potential US ground attack, and the island has been struck repeatedly from the air during the resumed phase, most notably on July 7 and July 15. Prior reporting that seizure plans were 'continuously shelved' as 'too risky' and would 'require a significant number of ground troops' has not been superseded. This cycle produced no new Kharg targeting, naval positioning or seizure signal. (CNN, March 25 and April 7, 2026; Al Jazeera, July 8, 2026; RFE/RL, July 8, 2026.)",
    "TROOP STRENGTH AND ATTRITION CONTEXT (July 27-29, 2026): Force levels in the region remain roughly 50,000, unchanged. Foreign Policy's July 24 survey of former US military officials found the United States 'does not appear to be moving toward a major ground operation in Iran,' which would require far more troops than are currently deployed; retired Gen. Joseph Votel framed the realistic case narrowly — 'What we might see is the use of ground forces in limited raids that are going after key locations.' The Pentagon's total wounded across both casualty categories now stands at 642, and reported interceptor depletion — more than 11,000 munitions expended in the first 16 days, with Patriot and THAAD stocks three or more years from restoration — continues to argue against concentrating fixed ground forces inside Iranian missile range. (Foreign Policy, July 24, 2026; The Intercept, July 28, 2026; Associated Press via KSAT, July 28, 2026.)",
    "DOMESTIC PERMISSION STRUCTURE (July 27, 2026): Reuters/Ipsos found 33% of Americans approve of US attacks on Iran and 62% disapprove, with nearly 70% saying Trump has not clearly explained the goals of US military involvement and overall approval at 37%. War approval has stayed below 40% since February 28. A ground commitment requiring mobilisation would have to be launched from the weakest domestic base of the conflict, and in the same week the draft rule was withdrawn. (Reuters/Ipsos via The Hill, Detroit News and Al-Monitor, July 27, 2026.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "The predicted US GROUND seizure of Hormuz/Kharg remains DISCONFIRMED for a 154th consecutive day, and this cycle supplies the clearest structural evidence yet against it. Escalation resumed on the ballistic, maritime and air rungs while the ground rung not only failed to move but had two of its preconditions regress: the draft-registration rule was withdrawn without explanation after four months of White House inaction, and the allied naval coalition Washington is trying to raise was refused by both prospective lead nations. The American answer to Iranian escalation was an air strike on a proxy in a third country, flown jointly with Saudi aircraft — a coalition-building move in the air domain, not a ground mobilisation. Predictions that ground forces would follow a stalled air campaign remain disconfirmed: the air campaign against Iranian territory has now been halted for five days while the war escalated by other means. (CONTESTED-UNRESOLVED — open-questions: selective-service-rule-withdrawal-july-2026; us-saudi-iraq-strikes-pmf-status-july-2026; kharg-seizure-total-control-threat-june-2026; iran-strike-pause-reload-or-climbdown-july-2026.)"
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-07-30",
   "headline": "GULF STATES — SAUDI ARABIA CROSSES FROM DEFENCE TO OFFENCE: RIYADH'S FIRST CLAIMED STRIKES INSIDE IRAQ OF THE WAR, FLOWN JOINTLY WITH CENTCOM AND JUSTIFIED UNDER ARTICLE 51. Maj. Gen. TURKI AL-MALIKI confirmed coordination and said Saudi air defences 'intercepted and destroyed multiple drones launched from Iraqi territory aimed at vital petroleum facilities in the Kingdom's EASTERN PROVINCE and RIYADH region.' The Kingdom 'does not seek escalation but will respond to any aggression it faces,' reserving the right to act 'at the appropriate time and place.' PMF: 20 KILLED, 32 WOUNDED across seven provinces; Baghdad calls it a sovereignty violation. ON THE OTHER FLANK THE HOUTHIS HIT THE SAUDI TANKER NCC GHAZAL with ballistic missiles — Saree citing the 'maritime embargo imposed on the Saudi enemy'; UKMTO logged the explosion, crew safe, vessel reversed course. 'BAHRAIN SHIELD' ran July 28-30 with all six GCC armed forces. Riyadh is now interdicted on both flanks and shooting on both.",
   "theatre": "Gulf States",
   "tag": "Gulf States",
   "tags": [
    "Gulf States",
    "Saudi First Strikes In Iraq",
    "Article 51",
    "Turki Al-Maliki",
    "Eastern Province Drones",
    "Riyadh Region",
    "PMF 20 Killed",
    "Iraqi Sovereignty",
    "NCC Ghazal",
    "Houthi Ballistic Missiles",
    "Yahya Saree",
    "UKMTO",
    "Maritime Embargo",
    "Bahrain Shield",
    "All Six GCC States",
    "World Bank 1.3%",
    "$58bn Damage"
   ],
   "source": "U.S. Central Command public release, 'U.S., Saudi Forces Strike Iran-Backed Terrorist Sites in Iraq,' July 28, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Iraqi armed groups condemn ‘dangerous escalation’ after US-Saudi strikes,' July 29, 2026; The Jerusalem Post, 'US-Saudi joint strikes on Iran-backed militias are a milestone in cooperation - analysis,' July 29, 2026; The Week, 'US, Saudi forces launch joint precision strikes in Iraq,' July 29, 2026 (Maj. Gen. Turki Al-Maliki; Article 51); Al Jazeera, 'Yemen’s Houthis claim missile attack on Saudi Arabia oil tanker,' July 28, 2026; The Maritime Executive, 'Saudi Tanker Hit by Houthi Missiles in the Red Sea'; HNGN, 'Houthis Fire Ballistic Missiles At Saudi Oil Tanker In Escalating Red Sea Blockade,' July 29, 2026; Gulf Daily News / Asharq Al-Awsat English, 'Gulf Forces to Kick off ‘Bahrain Shield’ Exercise,' July 27-28, 2026; The National, 'Bahrain hosts joint GCC military exercises as regional tensions persist,' July 28, 2026; The Conversation, 'How severe has the economic impact of the Iran war been for the Gulf states?'; Zero Carbon Analytics, 'Asian countries most at risk from oil and gas supply disruptions in Strait of Hormuz.'",
   "summary": "The Gulf's posture changed category this cycle. Saudi Arabia stopped being a target and a host and became a belligerent with its own claimed offensive operations on foreign soil.\n\nUS Central Command announced that it 'and the Saudi Arabian Armed Forces conducted precision strikes in Iraq, July 28, against Iran-aligned terrorists that the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) directed to attack U.S. forces and Saudi energy infrastructure,' hitting multiple logistics hubs and weapons storage sites. It is the first time Riyadh has claimed responsibility for launching strikes inside Iraq since the war began — a threshold that Gulf analysts described as a milestone in US-Saudi military cooperation. Maj. Gen. Turki Al-Maliki confirmed coordination with CENTCOM, said the strikes targeted militia positions directly linked to cross-border drone attacks, and reported that Saudi air defences had 'intercepted and destroyed multiple drones launched from Iraqi territory aimed at vital petroleum facilities in the Kingdom's Eastern Province and Riyadh region.' The defence ministry invoked Article 51 of the UN Charter, said Saudi Arabia 'does not seek escalation but will respond to any aggression it faces,' and reserved the right to respond 'at the appropriate time and place.'\n\nThe cost was borne in Iraq and the political blowback was immediate. Iraq's Popular Mobilisation Forces said at least 20 of its members were killed and 32 wounded, with strike sites reported in Baghdad, Wasit, Nineveh, Basra, Kirkuk, Karbala and Diyala provinces, and condemned 'a highly dangerous escalation' violating Iraqi sovereignty and official state security institutions. The Iraqi presidency 'categorically rejected any attacks in Iraqi territory' while also opposing 'the use of Iraqi territory as a launching point' — a formulation that rejects the strike while conceding the grievance behind it. Prime Minister Ali al-Zaidi convened an emergency security meeting. Iran's foreign ministry condemned 'aggressive attacks' violating Iraq's 'national sovereignty and territorial integrity.'\n\nOn the Red Sea flank the pressure Riyadh cannot answer with airpower continued. The Houthis fired ballistic missiles at the Saudi state-owned product tanker NCC Ghazal, with military spokesman Yahya Saree saying the group 'carried out a military operation against the Saudi oil tanker NCC Ghazal, due to violation of the maritime embargo imposed on the Saudi enemy and ignoring warning calls,' and that the strike forced the vessel to reverse course. The UK Maritime Trade Operations centre confirmed a tanker had reported an explosion while transiting the Red Sea, with the crew safe and no environmental damage. The attack enforces the maritime blockade the Houthis declared on July 20, accusing Saudi leaders of imposing 'an unjust and oppressive siege' on Yemen for nearly twelve years, and follows the July 25 strikes on Aramco installations at Jizan and Yanbu. Saudi Arabia is now interdicted at both chokepoints — Hormuz to the east, Bab el-Mandeb to the south — and is exchanging fire on both.\n\nThe collective-defence track continued in parallel. The Bahrain Defence Force's joint 'Bahrain Shield' exercise, which opened Tuesday July 28 with the armed forces of all six Gulf Cooperation Council states — Saudi Arabia, the UAE, Kuwait, Oman, Qatar and Bahrain — ran through July 30, aimed at strengthening joint military cooperation and assessing shared operational systems. The economic baseline is unchanged: the World Bank's 2026 GCC growth forecast remains cut from 4.4% to 1.3%, with roughly 80 energy facilities hit and about $58 billion of damage, and insurance premiums at levels not seen since the 1980s tanker wars.",
   "details": [
    "SAUDI ARABIA'S FIRST CLAIMED STRIKES INSIDE IRAQ OF THE WAR (July 28, 2026): CENTCOM: 'U.S. Central Command and the Saudi Arabian Armed Forces conducted precision strikes in Iraq, July 28, against Iran-aligned terrorists that the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) directed to attack U.S. forces and Saudi energy infrastructure.' Targets: multiple logistics hubs and weapons storage sites. Trigger: more than 30 IRGC-directed aerial drone attacks in the previous 72 hours, none of which CENTCOM said succeeded; CENTCOM also cited more than 600 attempted attacks on US personnel and facilities between February and April 2026. Analysts described it as a milestone in US-Saudi military cooperation and the end of Riyadh's practice of keeping its military outside the US campaign. (U.S. Central Command public release; The Jerusalem Post; The Week; The Tribune, July 28-29, 2026.)",
    "SAUDI JUSTIFICATION AND AIR-DEFENCE CLAIM (July 28-29, 2026): Maj. Gen. Turki Al-Maliki confirmed Saudi coordination with CENTCOM and said the strikes targeted militia positions directly linked to cross-border drone attacks, with Saudi air defences having 'intercepted and destroyed multiple drones launched from Iraqi territory aimed at vital petroleum facilities in the Kingdom's Eastern Province and Riyadh region.' The Ministry of Defence invoked Article 51 of the UN Charter, asserted the Kingdom's 'right to self-defence,' said it 'does not seek escalation but will respond to any aggression it faces,' and reserved the right to respond 'at the appropriate time and place.' (The Week; Al Jazeera, July 28-29, 2026.)",
    "IRAQI CASUALTIES AND POLITICAL BLOWBACK (July 29, 2026): Iraq's Popular Mobilisation Forces said at least 20 members were killed and 32 wounded, with strike sites reported across Baghdad, Wasit, Nineveh, Basra, Kirkuk, Karbala and Diyala provinces, and called the operation 'a highly dangerous escalation' and a violation of Iraqi sovereignty and of official security institutions. The Iraqi presidency 'categorically rejected any attacks in Iraqi territory' while opposing 'the use of Iraqi territory as a launching point'; Prime Minister Ali al-Zaidi convened an emergency security meeting. Iran's foreign ministry condemned 'aggressive attacks' violating Iraq's 'national sovereignty and territorial integrity.' (Al Jazeera; CBS News, July 29, 2026.)",
    "HOUTHIS HIT THE SAUDI TANKER NCC GHAZAL WITH BALLISTIC MISSILES (July 28, 2026): Houthi military spokesman Yahya Saree said the group 'carried out a military operation against the Saudi oil tanker NCC Ghazal, due to violation of the maritime embargo imposed on the Saudi enemy and ignoring warning calls,' and that the attack forced the tanker to reverse course and return the way it had come. The NCC Ghazal is a 50,000 dwt Saudi state-owned product tanker built in 2014, last seen at Yanbu before dropping off AIS tracking on July 23. The UK Maritime Trade Operations centre said a tanker reported an explosion while transiting the Red Sea, with the crew safe and no environmental damage reported. The strike enforces the maritime blockade the Houthis declared on July 20, citing 'an unjust and oppressive siege' on Yemen for nearly twelve years, and follows the July 25 attacks on Aramco installations at Jizan and Yanbu. (Al Jazeera; The Maritime Executive; Detroit News; HNGN; Middle East Monitor, July 28-29, 2026.)",
    "'BAHRAIN SHIELD' RUNS WITH ALL SIX GCC ARMED FORCES (July 28-30, 2026): The Bahrain Defence Force's joint exercise opened Tuesday July 28 and ran through July 30 with participation from the armed forces of all six Gulf Cooperation Council states — Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates, Kuwait, Oman, Qatar and Bahrain — with stated aims of strengthening joint military cooperation, exchanging expertise and assessing the effectiveness of joint operational systems. (Gulf Daily News / Asharq Al-Awsat English; The National; Daily News Egypt, July 27-28, 2026.)",
    "ECONOMIC BASELINE UNCHANGED (July 2026): The World Bank's 2026 GCC growth forecast remains cut from 4.4% to 1.3%, with roughly 80 energy facilities struck and about $58 billion of damage across the Gulf states. Strikes on the Hidd desalination complex in Bahrain and the Fujairah storage hub in the UAE — a critical Hormuz bypass — have curtailed the region's ability to export even through alternative routes, and marine insurance premiums stand at levels not seen since the 1980s tanker wars. Saudi Arabia continues shifting Red Sea export volumes toward Suez as Bab el-Mandeb oil exports run near zero. (The Conversation; Zero Carbon Analytics; The National, July 2026.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "Predictions that the Gulf monarchies would be drawn from hosting into active belligerency are now CONFIRMED on the record for Saudi Arabia: Riyadh has claimed its own offensive strikes on foreign soil, jointly with CENTCOM, under an Article 51 justification. Predictions of a general Gulf collapse or regime instability remain disconfirmed — the GCC states responded with a six-nation joint exercise and continued economic adaptation, not fragmentation. Predictions of a US ground campaign remain disconfirmed: the Gulf contribution to escalation was airpower and air defence, not troops. (CONTESTED-UNRESOLVED — open-questions: us-saudi-iraq-strikes-pmf-status-july-2026; gulf-states-direct-strikes-iran-july-2026.)",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "Saudi Arabia's participation is read either as a genuine shift to co-belligerency with the United States, or as a narrowly bounded self-defence action against drone launch sites that Riyadh will not repeat.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented action, undocumented intent. Confirmed: CENTCOM's joint attribution, Maj. Gen. Turki Al-Maliki's confirmation of coordination, the Article 51 invocation, and the explicit disclaimer that the Kingdom 'does not seek escalation.' Also documented: Riyadh had kept its military outside the US campaign for five months, and the stated trigger was drones aimed at Saudi petroleum facilities in its own Eastern Province and Riyadh region — a self-defence framing rather than a war-aims framing. Not documented: any Saudi commitment to further operations, any change in basing or overflight permissions, or any Saudi statement on participation in strikes against Iran itself. The July 26 WSJ report that Bahraini and Kuwaiti warplanes struck Iranian depots earlier in July, which Kuwait called 'entirely false,' remains separately unresolved.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "U.S. Central Command, framing the strikes as a joint US-Saudi operation against IRGC-directed targets",
      "Saudi Arabia's Ministry of Defence and Maj. Gen. Turki Al-Maliki, framing them as Article 51 self-defence that 'does not seek escalation'",
      "The Jerusalem Post and Gulf analysts, describing the operation as a milestone in US-Saudi military cooperation and the end of Saudi military separation from the campaign",
      "Iraq's Popular Mobilisation Forces and Iran's foreign ministry, framing it as Saudi participation in aggression"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "A single joint operation with an explicit non-escalation disclaimer is consistent with both a durable shift in Saudi posture and a one-off response to a direct threat to Saudi oil infrastructure; only subsequent operations, or their absence, will distinguish them, and no Saudi commitment either way has been published.",
     "open_question_ref": "us-saudi-iraq-strikes-pmf-status-july-2026"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-07-30",
   "headline": "RUSSIA-UKRAINE — THE SENATE ADVANCES THE GRAHAM RUSSIA-AND-IRAN SANCTIONS BILL 86-12 WITH ZELENSKY WATCHING FROM THE GALLERY, HOURS AFTER GRAHAM'S FUNERAL. The Ukrainian president made a closed-door appeal to senators before the procedural vote on the LINDSEY O. GRAHAM SANCTIONING RUSSIA AND IRAN ACT OF 2026, which authorises TARIFFS OF UP TO 200% on the largest purchasers of Russian oil and gas and expands sanctions on Russian banks, oligarchs, political elites and the SHADOW FLEET. AT THE WHITE HOUSE Zelensky offered UKRAINIAN DRONE-COUNTERMEASURE EXPERTISE in exchange for PATRIOT MISSILES — the same interceptors CENTCOM is burning through in the Gulf. ON THE GROUND: 226 CLASHES July 28, POKROVSK hottest with 29 assaults, 6,125 kamikaze drones, 59 airstrikes and 190 guided bombs. ZELENSKY SAYS RUSSIA IS PREPARING VORONEZH BASES FOR ~30,000 MORE NORTH KOREAN TROOPS atop ~15,000 already deployed.",
   "theatre": "Russia-Ukraine War",
   "tag": "Russia-Ukraine",
   "tags": [
    "Russia-Ukraine",
    "Senate 86-12",
    "Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act",
    "Zelensky Capitol Appeal",
    "200% Secondary Tariffs",
    "Shadow Fleet",
    "Patriots For Drone Expertise",
    "Interceptor Competition",
    "226 Clashes",
    "Pokrovsk 29 Assaults",
    "6,125 Kamikaze Drones",
    "30,000 DPRK Troops",
    "Voronezh Oblast"
   ],
   "source": "Kyiv Post, 'US Senate Delivers Overwhelming 86-12 Bipartisan Backing for Russia Sanctions as Zelensky Watches,' July 29, 2026; RFE/RL, 'US Senate Advances Landmark Russia Sanctions Bill After Zelenskyy’s Capitol Appeal,' July 29, 2026; Axios, '86 senators vote to move forward with Graham Russia sanctions bill,' July 29, 2026; ABC News, 'Senate takes first steps on Russian sanctions after meeting with Zelenskyy,' July 29, 2026; Just Security, 'Early Edition: July 29, 2026'; Ukrinform, 'War update: 226 clashes on front line as Pokrovsk sector remains hottest combat zone,' July 29, 2026; Kyiv Post, 'Ukraine Repels 226 Clashes in a Single Day, Kills 32 Russian Soldiers Near Pokrovsk Alone,' July 29, 2026; AEI, 'Korean Peninsula Update, July 28, 2026' (Zelensky on 30,000 additional DPRK troops; Voronezh Oblast basing).",
   "summary": "Ukraine converted a funeral into legislative momentum, and in doing so exposed the resource collision between its war and the one in the Gulf.\n\nThe Senate voted 86-12 on July 28 to advance the Lindsey O. Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act of 2026, hours after the funeral of the South Carolina senator who was the bill's chief architect and whose name it now carries. President Volodymyr Zelensky made a personal appeal to senators behind closed doors before the vote, then watched it from the Senate chamber. The bill — renamed at Trump's request to add Iran — authorises the President to impose tariffs of up to 200% on the world's largest purchasers of Russian oil and natural gas, a category that includes China and India, and expands sanctions targeting Russia's financial institutions, political elites, oligarchs and the 'shadow fleet.' A procedural vote of that margin in a chamber that has repeatedly stalled on Russia sanctions is the most substantial legislative movement of the war.\n\nAt the White House the day before, Zelensky's ask was narrower and more revealing. He offered Ukrainian expertise in drone countermeasures — accumulated across three and a half years of mass drone attack — in exchange for Patriot missiles. That is a direct trade of knowledge for interceptors, and it lands at the precise moment CENTCOM is reported to have expended more than 11,000 munitions in the first sixteen days of the resumed Iran phase, with Patriot and THAAD stocks three or more years from restoration. Two American-supported wars are now bidding for the same finite magazine, and Kyiv is offering intellectual property to compete for it.\n\nThe front line did not soften. Ukraine's General Staff recorded 226 combat engagements on July 28, with the Pokrovsk sector again the epicentre: 29 Russian assaults, with attempts to advance near Vasylivka, Kotlyne, Udachne, Novomykolaivka, Molodetske and Novooleksandrivka and toward Toretske, Novopavlivka, Kucheriv Yar, Vilne, Svitle, Shevchenko, Matiasheve, Novyi Donbas and Novohryshyne. Ukrainian forces reported killing 32 Russian soldiers and wounding nine in that sector alone. Across the front Russia carried out 59 airstrikes using 190 guided aerial bombs, launched 6,125 kamikaze drones and conducted 2,047 attacks on Ukrainian positions and populated areas; the Northern Slobozhanshchyna and Kursk sectors absorbed 54 attacks including one multiple-launch rocket system strike.\n\nThe North Korean dimension expanded. Zelensky said on July 27 that Russia is preparing bases in Voronezh Oblast to receive approximately 30,000 additional North Korean soldiers, on top of an estimated 15,000 already deployed who supported operations in Kursk Oblast and now perform rear-area functions; deployment specifics and timelines remain unclear. South Korean intelligence assessed in March that North Korea earned $620 million from four separate troop deployments, and Pyongyang has supplied more than 100 KN-23 and KN-24 ballistic missiles to Russia, of which at least 80 have been used.\n\nThe Iran and Ukraine theatres also touched directly. Iran considered attacking Ukrainian infrastructure after a Ukrainian strike on an Iranian ship in the Caspian, but diplomacy 'calmed the situation for now.' Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov's denial that any aerial-ceasefire talks are under way, and his warning that intensified Ukrainian strikes inside Russia will prolong the war, stand unretracted.",
   "details": [
    "SENATE ADVANCES THE GRAHAM SANCTIONS BILL 86-12 (July 28, 2026): The Senate voted 86-12 on a procedural motion to advance the Lindsey O. Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act of 2026, hours after the funeral of the bill's chief architect. President Zelensky appealed to senators behind closed doors before the vote and watched it from the Senate chamber. The legislation authorises the President to impose tariffs of up to 200% on the world's largest purchasers of Russian oil and natural gas — including China and India — and expands sanctions on Russian financial institutions, political elites, oligarchs and the 'shadow fleet.' The bill was renamed at Trump's request to add Iran. (Kyiv Post; RFE/RL; Axios; ABC News; Spectrum News, July 28-29, 2026.)",
    "ZELENSKY TRADES DRONE EXPERTISE FOR PATRIOTS (July 28, 2026): At his Oval Office meeting with Trump, Zelensky offered Ukrainian expertise in drone countermeasures while requesting Patriot missiles. The ask collides with the Gulf: CENTCOM is reported to have expended more than 11,000 munitions in the first sixteen days of the resumed Iran phase, with Patriot and THAAD stocks three or more years from pre-war restoration. Two US-supported wars are competing for the same interceptor inventory. (Just Security, 'Early Edition: July 29, 2026'; The Intercept, July 28, 2026.)",
    "FRONT LINE — 226 CLASHES, POKROVSK HOTTEST (July 28, 2026): Ukraine's General Staff recorded 226 combat engagements, with 29 Russian assaults in the Pokrovsk sector — attempts to advance near Vasylivka, Kotlyne, Udachne, Novomykolaivka, Molodetske and Novooleksandrivka, and toward Toretske, Novopavlivka, Kucheriv Yar, Vilne, Svitle, Shevchenko, Matiasheve, Novyi Donbas and Novohryshyne. Ukrainian forces reported 32 Russian soldiers killed and nine wounded in the Pokrovsk sector. Across the front: 59 airstrikes with 190 guided aerial bombs, 6,125 kamikaze drones launched, and 2,047 attacks on positions and populated areas; 54 attacks in the Northern Slobozhanshchyna and Kursk sectors including one MLRS strike. (Ukrinform; Kyiv Post; Mezha, July 29, 2026.)",
    "ZELENSKY: RUSSIA PREPARING VORONEZH BASES FOR ~30,000 MORE DPRK TROOPS (July 27, 2026): Zelensky said Russia is preparing bases in Voronezh Oblast to receive approximately 30,000 additional North Korean soldiers, atop an estimated 15,000 already deployed who supported operations in Kursk Oblast and now perform rear-area functions; deployment specifics and timelines remain unclear. South Korean intelligence assessed in March 2026 that North Korea earned $620 million from four separate troop deployments, and Pyongyang has supplied more than 100 KN-23 and KN-24 ballistic missiles to Russia with at least 80 used. (AEI, 'Korean Peninsula Update, July 28, 2026.')",
    "THE IRAN AND UKRAINE THEATRES TOUCH DIRECTLY (July 28-29, 2026): Iran considered attacking Ukrainian infrastructure after a Ukrainian strike on an Iranian ship, but diplomacy 'calmed the situation for now.' The Ukrainian strikes on Iran-linked cargo vessels in the Caspian, which killed a sailor, remain the proximate cause. (New York Times via Just Security 'Early Edition: July 29, 2026.')",
    "MOSCOW'S POSITION UNRETRACTED (July 28-29, 2026): Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov's denial that any aerial-ceasefire talks are under way, and his warning that intensified Ukrainian strikes inside Russia will prolong the war, stand unretracted against the Ukrainian and US officials reported to have been working on an air-war ceasefire proposal. (Al Jazeera; Institute for the Study of War, July 2026.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "Predictions of a negotiated end to the Russia-Ukraine war remain UNTESTED and further from resolution: the air-ceasefire proposal Kyiv brought to Washington is denied by Moscow to exist, and the legislative movement this cycle was coercive rather than diplomatic. Predictions of Western sanctions escalation are CONFIRMED in direction and now legislatively concrete — an 86-12 Senate margin authorising up to 200% secondary tariffs on Chinese and Indian purchases of Russian energy, which if exercised would couple this theatre directly to the US-China trade file. Predictions of Russian collapse or breakthrough remain disconfirmed: Pokrovsk is contested at 29 assaults a day without resolution. Predictions of deepening DPRK-Russia military integration are CONFIRMED and escalating, with a claimed further 30,000 troops in preparation. The interceptor competition between the Ukraine and Iran theatres is newly documented and argues against any US commitment requiring large fixed air-defence coverage. (CONTESTED-UNRESOLVED — open-questions: kostiantynivka-control-contested-july-2026.)"
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-07-30",
   "headline": "MULTI-THEATRE — VENEZUELA'S TRANSITION TALKS OPEN AUGUST 1 WITH MACHADO OUTSIDE THE ROOM AND CARACAS IN THE STREETS: rival marches on July 28 ahead of the Rodríguez-led delegation's talks with a MACHADO-LESS opposition group; leftists say Rodríguez negotiates 'WITH A GUN TO HER HEAD,' while MACHADO declares the transition already under way and demands elections. CUBA SUFFERS ITS FOURTH ISLAND-WIDE GRID COLLAPSE OF 2026 — all 10 million without power — and calls COLOMBIA'S severing of ties an 'ACT OF SUBORDINATION' to Washington; DE LA ESPRIELLA will close 14 EMBASSIES and 15 CONSULATES and cut Cuba and Nicaragua loose when he takes office AUGUST 7. US-CHINA: REUTERS reports up to 400 CHINESE MANPADS bound for Iran within weeks (Beijing: 'completely groundless'), even as XI'S WHITE HOUSE VISIT holds for ~SEPTEMBER 24 and USTR extends 178 tariff exclusions to NOVEMBER 10. DPRK: SOHAE PREPARES ITS FIRST RECONNAISSANCE-SATELLITE LAUNCH SINCE 2024, and LAVROV says Russia NO LONGER SUPPORTS Korean denuclearisation.",
   "theatre": "Multi-theatre",
   "tag": "Multi-theatre",
   "tags": [
    "Multi-theatre",
    "Venezuela Transition Aug 1",
    "Machado Excluded",
    "'Gun To Her Head'",
