    "Hormuz-Affected Economies"
   ],
   "source": "Jamaica Observer, 'Venezuela’s political camps protest ahead of transition talks,' July 28, 2026; Caracas Chronicles, 'Is Rubio’s “Transition Plan” for Venezuela Going to Kick In?,' July 27, 2026; MercoPress, 'Rubio says talks on Venezuela’s democratic transition will begin in early August,' July 20, 2026; Cubaheadlines, 'Maria Corina Machado Declares Venezuela’s Transition is Underway'; Press TV, '‘Act of subordination’ to US: Cuba slams Colombia’s plan to break diplomatic ties,' July 29, 2026; Xinhua, 'Cuba says Colombian president-elect’s plan to cut ties follows U.S. policy,' July 29, 2026; The City Paper Bogotá, 'Colombia’s De la Espriella to Close 14 Embassies, Cut Ties With Cuba and Nicaragua'; ColombiaOne, 'Colombia to Close Embassies and Consulates Under De la Espriella,' July 27, 2026; Latin America Daily Briefing / AOL, Cuba island-wide grid collapse, July 2026; Reuters via U.S. News & World Report, 'Exclusive: Iran to Get Chinese Shoulder-Launched Missile Systems in Weeks, Sources Say,' July 28, 2026; Iran International, 'Iran set to receive Chinese shoulder-fired air defense systems - Reuters,' July 29, 2026; CSIS, 'Trump-Xi 2026 Summit'; USTR, Presidential Tariff Actions (178-product exclusion extension to November 10, 2026); AEI, 'Korean Peninsula Update, July 28, 2026'; Zero Carbon Analytics, 'Asian countries most at risk from oil and gas supply disruptions in Strait of Hormuz'; Table.Briefings, 'Energy crisis: How Japan and South Korea are responding differently to the Strait of Hormuz blockade'; UN News, 'Stranded seafarers remain trapped as Hormuz shipping stalls,' July 2026.",
   "summary": "LATIN AMERICA. Venezuela's transition moved from announcement to a dated process with a contested guest list. Formal talks open August 1 between a delegation led by acting president Delcy Rodríguez and an opposition group that does not include María Corina Machado, following the agreement between representatives of the 2015 National Assembly and the interim government that Secretary of State Marco Rubio announced in July. Rival marches crossed Caracas on July 28 ahead of the opening: leftist demonstrators insisted Rodríguez is negotiating 'with a gun to her head,' having governed under Washington's supervision since US forces removed Nicolás Maduro in January; Machado's supporters called for elections to make the transition genuine, and Machado herself has declared the transition already under way. Rodríguez is reported to have accepted the American agenda while reserving conditions. The parallel US legal track is unchanged: the Justice Department has asked a Florida federal court to grant Rodríguez head-of-state immunity against the civil suit that produced a $314 million judgment on July 14, while Maduro's own trial remains set for June 1, 2027.\n\nCuba's grid failed again. The national electrical system collapsed at midday Monday, leaving all ten million inhabitants without power — the fourth total grid failure of 2026, on infrastructure that has been deteriorating for decades and against a fuel shortage that has been critical since the start of the year. Havana simultaneously absorbed a diplomatic loss: Colombian President-elect Abelardo de la Espriella announced on July 26 that his government will sever relations with Cuba and Nicaragua and close 14 embassies and 15 consulates, redirecting the savings to security and public investment, when he takes office on August 7. Cuba called the decision an 'act of subordination' to the United States and said it follows Washington's longstanding policy of confrontation with the island.\n\nUS-CHINA. The most consequential development is a reported arms transfer, not a trade measure. Reuters reported that Iran is expected to receive within weeks the first shipment of up to 400 Chinese-made shoulder-fired air-defence launchers — 300 to 400 QW-12 and FN-16 MANPADS worth $60-70 million, contracted through the Hong Kong-based intermediary Zhongqing Baoshang International Investment and routed from Urumqi through Pakistan. China's foreign ministry called the report 'completely groundless' and said China 'has consistently played a role in promoting peace and ending the conflict'; Pakistan's military called the claims 'absolutely concocted and false.' If accurate it would be the first concrete Chinese military resupply of Iran during the war and would sit awkwardly beside the diplomatic calendar, which is intact: Xi Jinping's White House visit remains expected around September 24, aligned with the UN General Assembly, and USTR has extended tariff exclusions on 178 Chinese products to November 10, 2026. The July 24 Section 301 forced-labour duties are fully operative with the in-transit exemption window closed since July 28. Beijing's tariff détente has meanwhile bought space for intensified military coercion of Taiwan, with PLA joint combat readiness patrols continuing.\n\nKOREAN PENINSULA. North Korea is preparing its first military reconnaissance satellite launch since 2024. Satellite imagery from July 21 shows renovated launch facilities at the Sohae Satellite Launching Grounds, following a rocket engine test in early July; Kim Jong Un designated the capability a priority at the February Party Congress, and it would support an emerging precision-strike complex that currently lacks robust targeting infrastructure. Naval expansion continues at Rason and Nampo to support larger surface combatants, with worker camps under construction at Rajin Shipyard and the destroyer Kang Kon towed to Rason for repairs. The most significant diplomatic shift came from Moscow: Foreign Minister Sergei Lavrov said on July 22 that Russia no longer supports denuclearisation of the Korean Peninsula. PRC-DPRK bilateral trade reached $1.5 billion in the first half of 2026, the highest since 2017, with the new Yalu River Bridge expected to open by year-end; South Korea detained the cargo ship Prada on suspicion of exporting North Korean coal and iron ore in violation of UN sanctions. In Seoul, President Lee Jae Myung's approval hit its second-lowest level since his June 2025 inauguration, with disapproval exceeding approval for the first time; Unification Minister Chung Dong-young announced 'Seven Major Reforms' on July 23 proposing dialogue and reduced drone flights near the border, which Pyongyang has rejected, with Kim Yo Jong reaffirming that denuclearisation lacks legal grounds.\n\nHORMUZ-AFFECTED ECONOMIES. The exposure baseline is unchanged and the closure is in its 151st day by the straits.live count. Japan draws close to three-quarters of its oil imports through the Strait and faces the most direct disruption risk; South Korea sources roughly 60% of its crude and is the only one of the pair responding with concrete renewable expansion plans; India moves almost half its crude imports and about 60% of its natural gas through the chokepoint. Qatar and the UAE account for 99% of Pakistan's LNG imports, 72% of Bangladesh's and 53% of India's, leaving Pakistan and Bangladesh especially vulnerable given limited storage and procurement flexibility. UN News reports stranded seafarers still trapped as Hormuz shipping stalls.",
   "details": [
    "VENEZUELA — TRANSITION TALKS OPEN AUGUST 1 WITHOUT MACHADO (July 28-29, 2026): Formal talks begin August 1 between a delegation led by acting president Delcy Rodríguez and an opposition group that does not include María Corina Machado, following the agreement between representatives of the 2015 National Assembly and the interim government announced by Secretary of State Marco Rubio. Rival marches crossed Caracas on July 28: leftist demonstrators said Rodríguez is negotiating 'with a gun to her head,' having governed under US supervision since American forces removed Nicolás Maduro in January; Machado's supporters called for elections to ensure a genuine transition, and Machado has declared the transition already under way. Rodríguez is reported to have accepted the US agenda while reserving conditions. (Jamaica Observer; Caracas Chronicles; MercoPress; Cubaheadlines; South Shore Journal, July 27-29, 2026.)",
    "VENEZUELA — US LEGAL TRACK UNCHANGED (July 2026): The Justice Department has asked a Florida federal court to grant Delcy Rodríguez head-of-state immunity in the civil suit that produced a $314 million judgment on July 14, while Nicolás Maduro's trial remains set for June 1, 2027. (PolitiFact; Latin Times; Al Jazeera, July 2026.)",
    "CUBA — FOURTH ISLAND-WIDE GRID COLLAPSE OF 2026 (July 2026): Cuba's national electrical grid collapsed at midday Monday, leaving all ten million inhabitants without power. It is the fourth total grid failure of the year, on infrastructure deteriorating for decades and against a fuel shortage critical since the start of 2026. (Latin America Daily Briefing; AOL; Sociedad Media, July 2026.)",
    "COLOMBIA — DE LA ESPRIELLA TO CUT TIES WITH CUBA AND NICARAGUA AND CLOSE 14 EMBASSIES (July 26-29, 2026): President-elect Abelardo de la Espriella, who takes office August 7, announced a sweeping overhaul of Colombia's diplomatic network: closing 14 embassies and 15 consulates, severing relations with Cuba and Nicaragua, and redirecting the savings to security and public investment. Cuba condemned the decision as an 'act of subordination' to the United States and said it aligns with Washington's longstanding policy of confrontation against the island. (Press TV; Xinhua; The City Paper Bogotá; ColombiaOne; CiberCuba, July 27-29, 2026.)",
    "US-CHINA — REUTERS REPORTS UP TO 400 CHINESE MANPADS BOUND FOR IRAN (July 28-29, 2026): Three sources familiar with the deal said Iran is expected to receive within weeks the first shipment of 300-400 Chinese-made man-portable air-defence systems — QW-12 and FN-16 infrared-guided launchers — valued at $60-70 million, contracted through the Hong Kong-based intermediary Zhongqing Baoshang International Investment and routed from Urumqi in western China through Pakistan. It would be Tehran's largest known effort to rebuild short-range air defence since the war exposed those gaps. China's foreign ministry: 'The relevant reports are completely groundless. China has consistently played a role in promoting peace and ending the conflict.' Pakistan's military called the claims 'absolutely concocted and false.' The intermediary's parent group did not respond to requests for comment. (Reuters via U.S. News & World Report; The Times of Israel; Iran International; Japan Times; Business Recorder, July 28-29, 2026.)",
    "US-CHINA — DIPLOMATIC AND TARIFF CALENDAR INTACT (July 2026): Xi Jinping's White House visit remains expected around September 24, 2026, aligned with the UN General Assembly, as Trump confirmed on July 7. USTR has extended tariff exclusions on 178 Chinese products to November 10, 2026. The July 24 Section 301 forced-labour duties are fully operative and the in-transit exemption window closed July 28. Xi has warned that Taiwan is the 'most important' issue in the relationship, and the tariff détente has bought Beijing space to intensify military coercion of Taiwan, with PLA joint combat readiness patrols continuing. (CSIS, 'Trump-Xi 2026 Summit'; USTR Presidential Tariff Actions; China Briefing; Model Diplomat, July 2026.)",
    "DPRK — FIRST RECONNAISSANCE-SATELLITE LAUNCH PREPARATION SINCE 2024 (July 21-28, 2026): Satellite imagery from July 21 shows renovated launching facilities at the Sohae Satellite Launching Grounds, following a rocket engine test in early July. Kim Jong Un designated the capability a priority at the February Party Congress; it would support North Korea's emerging precision-strike complex, which currently lacks robust targeting infrastructure. Naval expansion continues at Rason and Nampo for larger surface combatants, with worker camps under construction at Rajin Shipyard and the guided-missile destroyer Kang Kon towed to Rason for repairs after its failed 2025 launch. Kim has set goals of two Choe Hyon-class destroyers annually through 2030 plus potentially two 10,000-ton cruisers a year, targets current infrastructure cannot support. (AEI, 'Korean Peninsula Update, July 28, 2026.')",
    "DPRK — LAVROV: RUSSIA NO LONGER SUPPORTS KOREAN DENUCLEARISATION (July 22, 2026): Russian Foreign Minister Sergei Lavrov said Russia no longer supports denuclearisation of the Korean Peninsula, a significant policy shift. North Korean Foreign Minister Choe Son Hui had visited Moscow on June 18, meeting Putin and Lavrov and confirming Pyongyang's commitment to supporting Russian military operations in Ukraine. (AEI, 'Korean Peninsula Update, July 28, 2026.')",
    "DPRK — TRADE, SANCTIONS EVASION AND SEOUL POLITICS (July 2026): PRC-North Korea bilateral trade reached $1.5 billion in January-June 2026, the highest since 2017, with the new Yalu River Bridge expected to open by end-2026. South Korea detained the Tanzania-registered, likely China-operated cargo ship Prada on suspicion of exporting North Korean coal and iron ore in violation of UN sanctions; the Citizens' Alliance for North Korean Human Rights reported DPRK coal and mineral exports have surged nearly fivefold since 2019. President Lee Jae Myung's approval hit its second-lowest level since his June 2025 inauguration, with Realmeter showing 47-48.3% approval against 49.5% disapproval by late July and Gallup showing a decline from 54% to 51% — disapproval exceeding approval for the first time. Unification Minister Chung Dong-young announced 'Seven Major Reforms' on July 23 proposing dialogue, peaceful coexistence and reduced drone flights near the border; North Korea has rejected such overtures, with Kim Yo Jong stating denuclearisation lacks legal grounds. (AEI, 'Korean Peninsula Update, July 28, 2026.')",
    "HORMUZ-AFFECTED ECONOMIES — EXPOSURE BASELINE UNCHANGED (July 2026): Japan relies on the Strait of Hormuz for close to three-quarters of its oil imports and faces the most direct disruption risk; South Korea sources roughly 60% of its crude through the route and is the only one of the pair responding with concrete renewable-expansion plans; India moves almost half its crude imports and about 60% of its natural gas through the chokepoint. Qatar and the UAE account for 99% of Pakistan's LNG imports, 72% of Bangladesh's and 53% of India's; Pakistan and Bangladesh are especially vulnerable given limited storage and procurement flexibility. UN News reports stranded seafarers still trapped as Hormuz shipping stalls. (Zero Carbon Analytics; Table.Briefings; Gulf International Forum; ORF Middle East; UN News, July 2026.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "Venezuela predictions of a US-supervised transition are CONFIRMED in direction and now dated — talks open August 1 — while predictions of a Machado presidency remain DISCONFIRMED for now: she is outside the negotiating room and calling for elections she has not been granted. Cuba-collapse predictions remain PARTIALLY CONFIRMED at the infrastructure level (a fourth national grid failure) without political rupture. Predictions of hemispheric realignment against Havana are CONFIRMED by Colombia's incoming severance of ties. US-China predictions of decoupling remain PARTIALLY CONFIRMED and bifurcated: the diplomatic and tariff calendar is intact while a reported Chinese arms transfer to Iran, if accurate, would mark a material shift from commercial competition to military support of a US adversary in an active war — the single most consequential new datum in this theatre, and one both Beijing and Islamabad deny. DPRK predictions of accelerating capability development and Russian alignment are CONFIRMED and strengthened by the Sohae launch preparations and Lavrov's abandonment of denuclearisation as a Russian objective. (CONTESTED-UNRESOLVED — open-questions: china-manpads-transfer-iran-july-2026.)",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "Whether China is supplying Iran with hundreds of shoulder-fired air-defence launchers is asserted by Reuters on three sources and denied outright by both China and Pakistan.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented as reporting, denied as fact. Reuters described a signed contract for 300-400 QW-12 and FN-16 MANPADS worth $60-70 million, a named Hong Kong intermediary, a first shipment within weeks, and a route from Urumqi through Pakistan. China's foreign ministry called it 'completely groundless'; Pakistan's military called it 'absolutely concocted and false'; the intermediary's parent group did not comment. Not documented: any interdiction, imagery, manifest, delivery, sanctions designation, or on-record Western intelligence assessment either way.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "Reuters, citing three sources familiar with the deal, carried by U.S. News & World Report, The Times of Israel, Japan Times, Business Recorder and Iran International",
      "China's Ministry of Foreign Affairs, denying the report as 'completely groundless'",
      "Pakistan's military, denying involvement as 'absolutely concocted and false'"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "A covert transfer through a commercial cut-out and a third-country air corridor produces an identical open-source signature whether real or fabricated: anonymous sourcing, a non-responsive intermediary and categorical state denials. Only interdiction, imagery of the systems in Iranian service, a sanctions designation naming the transaction, or an on-record intelligence assessment would resolve it.",
     "open_question_ref": "china-manpads-transfer-iran-july-2026"
    }
   ]
