   ],
   "source": "U.S. Central Command statement via CBS News live blog, 'Iran War Updates: Trump meets Israel's Netanyahu as his threat to resume bombing Iran looms over diplomacy,' July 28, 2026; CNN live blog, 'Live updates: US says it intercepted Iranian ballistic missile attack on American forces in the Middle East,' July 28, 2026; ABC News live blog, 'Iran live updates: US intercepts Iranian missiles in “attempted surprise attack,” CENTCOM says,' July 28, 2026; Fox News Digital live blog, 'Trump, Netanyahu reaffirm “ironclad” commitment to keeping nuclear weapons from Iran,' July 28, 2026; The Times of Israel liveblog, July 28, 2026; The Times of Israel, 'Trump, ahead of meet with Netanyahu: “I don’t need Bibi” for intel on Pickaxe Mountain,' July 28, 2026; Ynetnews, 'After Trump jab, Netanyahu arrives at White House with Pickaxe Mountain intelligence,' July 28, 2026; Jewish Insider, 'Trump expresses frustration with Netanyahu’s reported warnings about Iranian nuclear activity,' July 28, 2026; Al Jazeera liveblog, 'Iran war live: Tehran vows Hormuz control, Trump-Netanyahu talks “positive”,' July 28, 2026; AGBI, 'US reviews Oman plan for Hormuz transit,' July 2026; NBC News, 'Iran and Oman propose fee plan for Strait of Hormuz, sources say,' July 2026; IranWire, 'Iran Gives Two Different Death Tolls for U.S. Strikes on Same Day,' July 28, 2026; The Intercept, 'Iran Has Figured Out How to Overwhelm U.S. Air Defenses,' July 28, 2026; TradingEconomics, Brent crude, July 28, 2026; straits.live Strait of Hormuz tracker, July 28, 2026 22:59Z; AAA Fuel Prices national averages, July 28, 2026.",
   "summary": "Day 153 ended the war's longest reciprocal quiet, and it was Tehran that ended it. At 5:45 p.m. ET on July 28, US Central Command said Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps forces 'launched multiple ballistic missiles from Iran in an attempted surprise attack on U.S. forces' in the Middle East, and that 'all Iranian missiles were successfully intercepted,' adding that 'U.S. forces remain vigilant and at a high state of readiness.' CENTCOM did not name the target or the number fired; initial estimates put it at no more than four missiles, and reporting placed the trajectory toward a US military base in Jordan — the same axis Iran struck on July 9 and July 17. No US casualties were reported. The launch came roughly twenty-four hours after Trump had said Iran wanted to talk and four days into a halt that Iran's own army spokesman had publicly reciprocated on July 26, and it reverses the direction of the kinetic indicator that had been falling since July 24. Crucially, it moves that indicator on the BALLISTIC/AIR rung: no ground contact, no landing, no territorial move by either side. The day's other event was diplomatic and intra-alliance. Benjamin Netanyahu arrived at the White House just before 11 a.m. for his first meeting with Trump since the war began on February 28, joined by Vice President JD Vance, Secretary of State Marco Rubio and Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent; the session ran a little over an hour behind closed doors, with reporters excluded, and press secretary Karoline Leavitt called it 'positive and productive.' A senior Israeli source said the two 'held an excellent and comprehensive discussion on the key issues of the day, first and foremost Iran' and that they 'reaffirmed their ironclad commitment to ensuring that Iran never has nuclear weapons'; Netanyahu called it 'one of the best we've ever had.' The warmth followed a public rebuke. Netanyahu had been reported to be bringing updated Israeli intelligence on Pickaxe Mountain, the fortified site near Natanz where Israeli assessments say Iran has moved centrifuges and nuclear equipment, and Trump pre-empted it on Fox: 'I don't need Bibi to tell me that... I know exactly what's going on there,' adding that 'Bibi's telling me that because he wants me to stay involved' and asking 'why did you have to announce it to the world?' He said the US could take out Pickaxe 'very easily' and that Space Force cameras are trained on it. After the meeting he framed it as an air task conditional on diplomacy: 'We took out their nuclear sites and we'll have to take out Pickaxe. If we don't make a deal, we don't make a deal. We'll take it very easily.' On the war's terms he was blunt — 'If they don't make a deal then I go back and I finish the job' — and on the Strait he claimed a maritime, not territorial, victory: 'We control the Strait. Our navy is incredible. Not one ship has gotten to Iran during any blockade.' CENTCOM's running blockade tally rose to 18 commercial ships stopped from entering or leaving Iranian ports, with two disabled and two boarded, enforced by more than 20 warships. The Hormuz governance track advanced and hardened at the same time. Oman has put a Gulf-backed proposal for joint regional management of the Strait to Tehran and carried it to Washington; it draws on the Malacca/Singapore model of VOLUNTARY industry contributions to fund safe navigation, and a person familiar with the US position said the proposals as delivered contain no mandatory transit tolls. White House spokesperson Anna Kelly said 'President Trump has been clear that Iran cannot toll the Strait, which is an international waterway,' and Rubio told reporters 'no country is allowed to charge tolls or fees on an international waterway. That's existing international law.' Iran's deputy foreign minister Kazem Gharibabadi said Tehran would take 'any action' to maintain control of the Strait and repeated that Iran has not requested negotiations with the United States. Markets read the day as de-escalatory until the launch: Brent slipped a third consecutive session to about $86.58, down 1.54%, with the straits.live tracker at $87.66 late in the day — a far shallower fall than the two prior sessions. The pump did not follow: AAA regular eased a tenth of a cent to $4.099 while diesel set another war high at $5.321. For the first time in the resumed phase the shipping backlog measurably eased — 405 vessels stranded against 426 the day before, and 77 tankers running dark against 97 — though only 13 tankers were transiting and IMF PortWatch's most recent count was 10 transits on July 23 against an 88-per-day baseline, about 11% of pre-crisis throughput.",
   "details": [
    "IRAN BREAKS THE PAUSE — IRGC BALLISTIC MISSILES AT US FORCES (July 28, 2026, 5:45 p.m. ET): US Central Command said 'Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps forces launched multiple ballistic missiles from Iran in an attempted surprise attack on U.S. forces' based in the Middle East, that 'all Iranian missiles were successfully intercepted,' and that 'U.S. forces remain vigilant and at a high state of readiness.' CENTCOM did not specify the number or the targeted location; initial estimates were of no more than four ballistic missiles, with reporting indicating a trajectory toward a US military base in Jordan. No US casualties were reported. The launch came roughly a day after Trump said the US had halted strikes and that Iran wanted to talk, and two days after Iran's army spokesman Mohammad Akraminia publicly declared Iran's own retaliatory operations halted. (CENTCOM via CBS News, CNN, ABC News and Fox News Digital live blogs, July 28, 2026.)",
    "TRUMP AND NETANYAHU MEET FOR THE FIRST TIME SINCE THE WAR BEGAN (July 28, 2026): Netanyahu arrived at the White House just before 11 a.m. and the closed-door meeting — reporters excluded — ran a little over an hour, concluding around 12:25 p.m. and formally wrapping at 1:08 p.m. EDT. Vice President JD Vance, Secretary of State Marco Rubio and Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent attended. Press secretary Karoline Leavitt called it 'positive and productive.' A senior Israeli source said the leaders 'held an excellent and comprehensive discussion on the key issues of the day, first and foremost Iran' and 'reaffirmed their ironclad commitment to ensuring that Iran never has nuclear weapons.' Netanyahu said the meeting 'was one of the best we've ever had.' (Fox News Digital; ABC News; The Times of Israel; The Jerusalem Post, July 28, 2026.)",
    "TRUMP PUBLICLY REBUFFS NETANYAHU'S PICKAXE MOUNTAIN INTELLIGENCE (July 28, 2026): Ahead of the meeting Trump told Fox, 'I don't need Bibi to tell me that... I know exactly what's going on there,' and 'Bibi's telling me that because he wants me to stay involved,' asking 'Why didn't you just tell it to me, why did you have to announce it to the world?' On 'Fox & Friends' he said the US could take out Pickaxe Mountain 'very easily' and that Space Force has state-of-the-art cameras trained on the site, describing it as 'not a big problem.' Israeli assessments hold that Iran has transferred centrifuges and nuclear equipment into the fortified facility near Natanz. US officials called the Israeli collection 'amazing' but said Jerusalem 'sees what it wants to see' and was 'not assessing the intelligence objectively,' and described Netanyahu as 'seeking another escalation against Iran, even if he avoids saying so explicitly because he does not want to appear to be pushing for war.' (The Times of Israel; Ynetnews; Jewish Insider; The Washington Times, July 28, 2026.)",
    "TRUMP AFTER THE MEETING — AN AIR THREAT, CONDITIONAL ON A DEAL (July 28, 2026): 'We took out their nuclear sites and we'll have to take out Pickaxe. If we don't make a deal, we don't make a deal. We'll take it very easily.' And: 'If they don't make a deal then I go back and I finish the job.' On the split inside his own party: 'Some Republicans said, “why don't we hit them harder?” ... Then you have a group that says, this is actually a good time to make a deal.' No ground formation, landing or Kharg operation was named in any of it. (Fox News Digital live blog; CBS News live blog, July 28, 2026.)",
    "THE BLOCKADE TALLY RISES AND TRUMP CLAIMS THE STRAIT (July 28, 2026): 'We control the Strait. Our navy is incredible. Not one ship has gotten to Iran during any blockade.' CENTCOM's figures as of July 28: more than 20 US Navy warships enforcing the blockade, 18 commercial vessels stopped from entering or exiting Iranian ports (up from 12 redirected on July 27), 2 vessels disabled and 2 boarded. The blockade has run unpaused through the strike halt. (Fox News Digital live blog; CBS News live blog; CENTCOM, July 28, 2026.)",
    "THE OMAN HORMUZ PLAN: VOLUNTARY FEES, NOT IRANIAN TOLLS (July 28, 2026): Oman has presented Iran with a Gulf-backed proposal for joint regional management of the Strait and carried it to Washington. It draws in part on the Malacca Strait and Singapore model, in which voluntary industry contributions fund safe navigation, and contrasts with Iran's stated preference for unilateral control and mandatory tolls; a person familiar with the US position said the proposals as delivered contain no mandatory transit tolls. White House spokesperson Anna Kelly: 'President Trump has been clear that Iran cannot toll the Strait, which is an international waterway.' Rubio: 'No country is allowed to charge tolls or fees on an international waterway. That's existing international law.' US negotiators have 'concerns' but say differences can be resolved technically; mediators say Iran and Oman remain far apart on fees. (AGBI; NBC News; Forbes, July 28, 2026.)",
    "IRAN: 'ANY ACTION' TO KEEP THE STRAIT, AND STILL NO US NEGOTIATIONS (July 28, 2026): Deputy foreign minister Kazem Gharibabadi said Iran will take 'any action' to maintain control of the Strait of Hormuz and that Tehran has not requested negotiations with the United States and has no current plans to negotiate directly with Washington — sustaining the flat public split with Trump's 'very deep talks' characterisation from July 27. Iran and Oman continued talks on a transit mechanism. (Al Jazeera liveblog; CBS News live blog, July 28, 2026.)",
    "IRAN'S CASUALTY ACCOUNTING SPLITS AGAIN (July 28, 2026): Government spokesperson Fatemeh Mohajerani told a Tuesday press conference that more than 350 government employees had been killed in the line of duty — telecommunications engineers, power-line workers, doctors, nurses and other service personnel — and that Bushehr Airport had been 'completely knocked out of service' with the aircraft stationed there destroyed, while the Bushehr Meteorological Centre, 'which is completely of a civilian nature, has almost nothing left of it.' The same day Health Minister Mohammad-Reza Zafarghandi put the toll from recent US strikes in the southern provinces at 60. IranWire noted the two figures were issued on the same day and are not reconcilable from the public record. Iranian-sourced figures remain unverified independently. (IranWire; Tehran Times; ABC News, July 28, 2026.)",
    "THE INTERCEPT ON AIR-DEFENCE ATTRITION (July 28, 2026): A report published the same day found Iran has fired between 8,000 and 9,000 drones and missiles since the war began in February, that at least nine US outposts across the Middle East have been struck since July 9 with several sustaining 'significant' damage, and that US Patriot and THAAD interceptor stocks are severely depleted — more than 11,000 munitions expended in the first 16 days of the conflict, with experts putting restoration of pre-war inventory at 'three or more years.' It reported that the US now 'allows missiles through its defenses only in cases where a strike is judged not to imperil U.S. lives,' and asserted the Pentagon drastically understates casualty figures — a claim that bears directly on the July 26 casualty reclassification. CONTESTED-UNRESOLVED. (The Intercept, 'Iran Has Figured Out How to Overwhelm U.S. Air Defenses,' July 28, 2026.)",
    "MARKETS AND THE STRAIT (July 28, 2026): Brent fell a third consecutive session to about $86.58, down 1.54% on the day, with the straits.live tracker showing $87.66 (−0.11% over 24h) at 22:59Z — a much shallower move than the July 26-27 collapse. AAA's national average eased to $4.099 for regular from $4.110 while diesel rose again to $5.321, a fresh war high (a year earlier: $3.143 and $3.731). The straits.live tracker recorded 405 vessels stranded (down from 426), 77 tankers running dark (down from 97), 13 tankers transiting and 107 transiting vessels overall; IMF PortWatch's latest count was 10 transits on July 23 against an 88-per-day pre-crisis baseline — about 11% of normal throughput. The tracker's Crisis Pressure Index stood at 90 (Extreme), its escalation forecast at 55 (Elevated), VLCC war-risk insurance at 8.0× pre-crisis, and the market-implied probability of a normal reopening by August 31 at 11%. (TradingEconomics; straits.live; AAA Fuel Prices; IMF PortWatch, July 28, 2026.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "The corpus-predicted US GROUND seizure of Hormuz/Kharg remains DISCONFIRMED, and Day 153 held the ground rung at zero for a 153rd consecutive day. The day's escalation was real but it ran in the opposite direction from the predicted one: an IRANIAN ballistic-missile launch at US forces, fully intercepted, with no territorial component on either side. Trump's own stated escalation path remains explicitly aerial — 'we'll have to take out Pickaxe,' 'I go back and I finish the job' — and his claim of victory at the chokepoint was maritime: 'We control the Strait... Not one ship has gotten to Iran during any blockade.' Predictions of a negotiated Hormuz transit regime remain live and untested and moved a rung closer to testable form: Oman's Gulf-backed plan offers voluntary navigation-fund contributions on the Malacca model, while Iran continues to seek control and mandatory tolls, and Washington rejects tolls as unlawful. Predictions that the campaign would escalate monotonically to Iranian capitulation remain disconfirmed — the US has now gone five days without striking. (CONTESTED-UNRESOLVED — open-questions: netanyahu-pickaxe-intelligence-escalation-july-2026; iran-strike-pause-reload-or-climbdown-july-2026; us-iran-direct-talks-existence-july-2026; iran-hormuz-administration-tolls-dispute-june-2026; pentagon-casualty-reclassification-july-2026; kharg-seizure-total-control-threat-june-2026.)",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "Whether Netanyahu brought Pickaxe Mountain intelligence to the White House at all — and whether Israel is working to pull the United States back into escalation — is asserted and denied on the record by the same government.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented on both sides. Pre-meeting: Ynetnews, Jewish Insider, The Times of Israel and Reuters all reported that Netanyahu would present updated Israeli intelligence on Pickaxe Mountain, the fortified site near Natanz to which Israeli assessments say Iran has moved centrifuges and enriched-uranium stockpile; Trump responded publicly, 'I don't need Bibi to tell me that... Bibi's telling me that because he wants me to stay involved.' Post-meeting: Tzipi Hotovely, head of Israel's National Public Diplomacy Directorate, said Pickaxe Mountain did not come up in the meeting and that 'the idea that we have some intelligence here is a sort of fake news that ran throughout the day.' Yet Trump himself said afterwards, 'we'll have to take out Pickaxe.' US officials separately described the Israeli collection as 'amazing' while saying Jerusalem 'sees what it wants to see,' was 'not assessing the intelligence objectively,' and that Netanyahu is 'seeking another escalation against Iran, even if he avoids saying so explicitly.' Not documented: the meeting record. Reporters were excluded and no transcript, readout of substance or intelligence product has been published.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "Ynetnews, Jewish Insider, The Times of Israel and Reuters, reporting that Netanyahu would present Pickaxe Mountain intelligence",
      "Tzipi Hotovely, head of Israel's National Public Diplomacy Directorate, calling the intelligence story 'a sort of fake news' and saying Pickaxe did not come up",
      "President Trump, publicly attributing motive to Netanyahu ('he wants me to stay involved') and then saying 'we'll have to take out Pickaxe'",
      "unnamed senior US administration officials, saying Israel 'sees what it wants to see' and that Netanyahu is 'seeking another escalation'",
      "Iran's foreign ministry, which has said since July 26 that Israel is pushing Washington to resume strikes"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "The meeting was closed to press and no substantive readout was issued by either government; both a genuine warning about nuclear reconstitution and a lobbying effort for renewed strikes would produce the same public artifacts — leaked pre-briefs, a warm joint characterisation, and a subsequent denial that the sensitive item was discussed. Only publication of the Israeli intelligence assessment, an IAEA finding on Pickaxe Mountain, or a meeting record would settle it.",
     "open_question_ref": "netanyahu-pickaxe-intelligence-escalation-july-2026"
    },
    {
     "reading": "Iran's July 28 missile launch is read either as Tehran terminating the pause and resuming its retaliatory campaign, or as a bounded signalling shot fired precisely because it could be intercepted — one that leaves the diplomatic track intact.",
     "evidentiary_status": "The documented facts support both. For termination: CENTCOM's own characterisation was an 'attempted surprise attack' by IRGC forces on US personnel, it came two days after Iran's army spokesman Mohammad Akraminia publicly declared Iran's retaliatory operations halted, and it followed a stated Iranian condition that its halt would hold only as long as Washington's did — which Washington had not broken. For bounded signalling: the salvo was small by the standards of this war (initial estimates of no more than four missiles against Iranian volleys of dozens earlier in July), every missile was intercepted, no US casualties resulted, and Iran did not announce or claim the strike, unlike its publicised July 9 and July 17 attacks on Jordan. Not documented: any IRGC or Iranian government statement explaining or claiming the launch, and any US decision on how to respond.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "U.S. Central Command, characterising it as an 'attempted surprise attack' on U.S. forces",
      "Iranian army spokesman Mohammad Akraminia (July 26), whose declared halt the launch appears to contradict",
      "Iranian deputy foreign minister Kazem Gharibabadi, maintaining the same day that Iran seeks no negotiations with Washington and will take 'any action' over the Strait"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "Iran has issued no claim or explanation, and an intercepted salvo produces the same observable record whether it was intended to kill or to signal. Only an Iranian claim of responsibility with stated intent, a subsequent pattern of launches, or a US retaliatory decision would disambiguate.",
     "open_question_ref": "iran-strike-pause-reload-or-climbdown-july-2026"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-07-29",
   "headline": "DAY 153 GROUND-INVASION TRACKER — STATUS HOLDS no_ground_troops. THE PAUSE BROKE, BUT IT BROKE ON THE BALLISTIC RUNG AND FROM THE IRANIAN SIDE: the IRGC fired missiles at US forces and CENTCOM intercepted all of them. EVERY US ESCALATION OPTION NAMED BY THE PRESIDENT ON THE RECORD THIS CYCLE WAS AN AIR OPTION — 'we'll have to take out Pickaxe... we'll take it very easily' and 'I go back and I finish the job' — and his claim at the chokepoint was NAVAL: 'We control the Strait. Our navy is incredible. Not one ship has gotten to Iran during any blockade.' THE AMPHIBIOUS PICTURE CONTRACTED FURTHER ON THE RECORD: The War Zone's July 28 count confirms the BOXER ARG is the SOLE DEPLOYED ARG fleet-wide, with USS TRIPOLI now back in SASEBO after disembarking the 31st MEU at Okinawa and NO RELIEF ARG announced six days on. Carriers Abraham Lincoln and George H.W. Bush remain in 5th Fleet; George Washington is in the South China Sea. No landing, no Kharg seizure, no new ARG/MEU order, no draft; ~50,000 personnel unchanged.",
   "theatre": "Iran / Strait of Hormuz",
   "tag": "Ground Invasion Tracker",
   "tags": [
    "Ground Invasion Tracker",
    "no_ground_troops",
    "Pause Broken On Ballistic Rung",
    "Iranian Missiles Intercepted",
    "Air Options Only",
    "'Take Out Pickaxe'",
    "'We Control The Strait'",
    "Boxer Sole Deployed ARG",
    "Tripoli Home To Sasebo",
    "No Relief ARG",
    "Kharg Fortified Not Seized",
    "No Draft",
    "~50,000 Personnel",
    "Disconfirmed"
   ],
   "source": "The War Zone, 'Where Are The Aircraft Carriers: July 28, 2026'; USNI News Fleet and Marine Tracker, July 27, 2026; U.S. Central Command statement via CBS News and ABC News live blogs, July 28, 2026; Fox News Digital live blog, July 28, 2026 (CENTCOM blockade tally; Trump 'We control the Strait'); Foreign Policy, 'How a U.S. Ground Operation in Iran Could Unfold,' July 24, 2026; Axios, 'Scoop: U.S., U.K. planning international conference on Strait of Hormuz,' July 24, 2026; Stars and Stripes / USNI News / Marine Corps Times, USS Tripoli and 31st MEU return, July 22, 2026; Stars and Stripes, 'Another amphibious assault ship joins US naval buildup amid Iran talks,' July 1, 2026; Times of San Diego, 'USS Boxer amphibious group with Pendleton Marines arrives in Middle East,' July 5, 2026; CNN, 'Iran building up defenses of Kharg Island to protect against potential US ground attack,' March 25, 2026; CNBC, 'Automatic U.S. military draft registration by December 2026 planned,' April 9, 2026; Fast Company, 'U.S. military draft 2026, Iran war: What you need to know about rumors'; The Intercept, 'Iran Has Figured Out How to Overwhelm U.S. Air Defenses,' July 28, 2026.",
   "summary": "The strike pause ended on Day 153, and the way it ended is itself the ground reading. The kinetic act was Iranian, ballistic and fully intercepted: CENTCOM said IRGC forces 'launched multiple ballistic missiles from Iran in an attempted surprise attack on U.S. forces' at 5:45 p.m. ET, that all were intercepted, and that US forces 'remain vigilant and at a high state of readiness.' Nothing in it involved ground contact, a landing or a territorial move by either party. On the American side, every escalation option the President stated on the record this cycle was an air option. Asked about Iran's Pickaxe Mountain enrichment site he said the US could take it out 'very easily' and, after meeting Netanyahu, 'we took out their nuclear sites and we'll have to take out Pickaxe. If we don't make a deal, we don't make a deal.' On the war's terms: 'If they don't make a deal then I go back and I finish the job.' No ground formation, amphibious operation or Kharg landing was named. His claim of control at the chokepoint was explicitly naval — 'We control the Strait. Our navy is incredible. Not one ship has gotten to Iran during any blockade' — and CENTCOM's supporting tally was maritime: more than 20 warships, 18 commercial vessels stopped from entering or leaving Iranian ports, two disabled, two boarded. Coercion at Hormuz continues to be delivered by hulls, not by infantry. The amphibious picture, the single most load-bearing indicator for a Kharg operation, contracted further on the record. The War Zone's July 28 fleet count confirms the USS Boxer (LHA-4) is the sole deployed amphibious ready group in the entire fleet, operating in the Middle East region; USS Tripoli (LHA-7) has returned to Sasebo, Japan after a six-month CENTCOM/PACOM deployment, having disembarked 31st Marine Expeditionary Unit elements at Okinawa on July 22. Six days after that subtraction no relief ARG has been announced. Fleet-wide the count is one ARG deployed, five training or in port and three in maintenance; carriers Abraham Lincoln (CVN-72) and George H.W. Bush (CVN-77) remain in CENTCOM/5th Fleet, with George Washington (CVN-73) in 7th Fleet after transiting the Luzon Strait into the South China Sea. The assault force named in the March ground-options reporting is at its home port; the seizure capability fell while the reported options stayed reported. Kharg Island remains fortified and unseized. Iran has laid anti-personnel and anti-armour mines around the island including on the shoreline where an amphibious landing would stage, and has moved additional personnel and air defences there. The domestic mobilisation picture is unchanged: automatic Selective Service registration begins December 18, 2026 under the NDAA requirement passed in December, which automates registration should a draft be called but does not activate one; reinstating conscription would require an act of Congress, and the administration continues to say there is no current plan for a draft or for ground troops. Estimated US strength in the region holds around 50,000. Every execution indicator stayed at zero for a 153rd consecutive day.",
   "details": [
    "THE KINETIC INDICATOR ROSE — ON THE BALLISTIC RUNG, FROM THE IRANIAN SIDE (July 28, 2026): CENTCOM said IRGC forces launched multiple ballistic missiles from Iran at 5:45 p.m. ET in an 'attempted surprise attack' on US forces in the Middle East and that all were intercepted, with US forces 'vigilant and at a high state of readiness.' Initial estimates were of no more than four missiles, with reporting indicating a trajectory toward a US base in Jordan. No ground contact, no landing, no territorial movement by either side. (CENTCOM via CBS News, CNN, ABC News, July 28, 2026.)",
    "EVERY US ESCALATION OPTION NAMED ON THE RECORD WAS AERIAL (July 28, 2026): Trump on Pickaxe Mountain — 'We took out their nuclear sites and we'll have to take out Pickaxe. If we don't make a deal, we don't make a deal. We'll take it very easily'; earlier on 'Fox & Friends' that the US could take it out 'very easily' with Space Force cameras trained on the site. On the war generally — 'If they don't make a deal then I go back and I finish the job.' Neither statement named a ground formation, an amphibious operation or a Kharg landing. (Fox News Digital live blog; CBS News live blog, July 28, 2026.)",
    "THE CHOKEPOINT CLAIM IS NAVAL, NOT TERRITORIAL (July 28, 2026): 'We control the Strait. Our navy is incredible. Not one ship has gotten to Iran during any blockade.' CENTCOM's supporting figures: more than 20 US Navy warships enforcing the blockade, 18 commercial vessels stopped from entering or exiting Iranian ports, 2 disabled, 2 boarded. The instrument of coercion at Hormuz remains hulls and aircraft. (Fox News Digital live blog; CENTCOM, July 28, 2026.)",
    "AMPHIBIOUS SUBTRACTION CONFIRMED AND UNREVERSED (July 28, 2026): The War Zone's July 28 fleet count records USS Boxer (LHA-4) as the flagship of the SOLE DEPLOYED amphibious ready group fleet-wide, in the Middle East region; USS Tripoli (LHA-7) returned to Sasebo, Japan after a six-month CENTCOM and PACOM deployment, having disembarked 31st MEU elements at Okinawa on July 22. Fleet-wide: one ARG deployed, five training or in port, three in maintenance. No relief ARG has been announced six days after the Tripoli group's departure from theatre. (The War Zone, 'Where Are The Aircraft Carriers: July 28, 2026'; USNI News Fleet and Marine Tracker, July 27, 2026.)",
    "CARRIER POSTURE UNCHANGED (July 28, 2026): USS Abraham Lincoln (CVN-72) and USS George H.W. Bush (CVN-77) remain in CENTCOM/5th Fleet conducting maritime security operations; USS George Washington (CVN-73) is in 7th Fleet, having transited the Luzon Strait westbound into the South China Sea on July 22. Three carriers deployed, three training or in port, four in maintenance, one in a training role. Two carriers in theatre is the standing wartime posture, not a surge. (The War Zone, July 28, 2026.)",
    "KHARG ISLAND — FORTIFIED, NOT SEIZED (unchanged): Iran has laid anti-personnel and anti-armour mines around Kharg, including on the shoreline where US troops could stage an amphibious landing, and has moved additional military personnel and air defences onto the island. No US landing, raid or seizure has occurred; no US force has set foot on Iranian territory. Kharg is Iran's principal oil-export terminal, with about 90% of the crude loaded there historically bound for China. (CNN, March 25, 2026; Gulf News; Reuters.)",
    "EXPERT BASELINE UNCHANGED (July 24, 2026, still standing): Foreign Policy's survey of former US military officials found the United States 'does not appear to be moving toward a major ground operation in Iran,' which would require far more troops than the roughly 50,000 currently in the region. Retired Gen. Joseph Votel framed the realistic case narrowly: 'What we might see is the use of ground forces in limited raids that are going after key locations.' Senior officials continue to say no final decision on ground deployments has been made. Nothing on July 28 disturbed this. (Foreign Policy, July 24, 2026.)",
    "FORCE GENERATION REMAINS MARITIME AND ALLIED (July 24-28, 2026): The US and UK are convening a high-level London conference on an international coalition to protect Hormuz shipping, potentially gathering defence ministers and senior commanders from Western and regional states, with Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth and Gen. Dan Caine possibly attending and participating governments expected to be asked for military equipment AND PERSONNEL. Reopening the Strait is framed as central to a US exit strategy. This is allied naval escort force generation, not US ground forces committed to Iranian territory. (Axios, July 24, 2026.)",
    "DRAFT INDICATORS — UNCHANGED (July 2026): Automatic Selective Service registration of eligible men aged 18-26 begins December 18, 2026, implementing a requirement Congress passed in the December NDAA. It automates registration should a draft be called; it does not activate a draft. Reinstating conscription would require an act of Congress to amend federal law. The administration says there is no current plan for a draft or for ground troops, while declining to foreclose either. No mobilisation order, no Selective Service activation, no reserve call-up for Iran. (CNBC, April 9, 2026; Fast Company; Newsweek, July 2026.)",
    "THE ATTRITION ARGUMENT CUTS AGAINST, NOT TOWARD, A GROUND PHASE (July 28, 2026): The Intercept reported Iran has fired 8,000-9,000 drones and missiles since February, that at least nine US outposts have been struck since July 9 with several sustaining significant damage, and that Patriot and THAAD interceptor stocks are severely depleted — more than 11,000 munitions expended in the first 16 days, with three or more years needed to restore pre-war inventory. Thin air-defence stocks argue against, not for, concentrating fixed-position ground forces inside Iranian missile range. (The Intercept, July 28, 2026.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "The predicted US GROUND seizure of Hormuz/Kharg stays DISCONFIRMED at 153 days. Day 153 is the cleanest directional test the tracker has had: the pause broke, and it broke upward on the BALLISTIC rung and from the IRANIAN side, while the only US escalation options stated on the record were air options against a nuclear site. The amphibious capability required to execute the reported Kharg options is at its lowest point of the resumed phase — one ARG deployed fleet-wide, the 31st MEU home at Okinawa, no relief announced — and force generation is being solicited from allies for a NAVAL coalition. Predictions of escalation continue to resolve onto air and maritime rungs."
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-07-29",
   "headline": "GULF STATES — 'BAHRAIN SHIELD' OPENS WITH ALL SIX GCC ARMED FORCES AS THE HOUTHIS CLAIM A SAUDI TANKER: the joint exercise began Tuesday July 28 and runs to July 30 with forces from Saudi Arabia, the UAE, Kuwait, Oman, Qatar and Bahrain, aimed at strengthening joint military cooperation and assessing shared operational systems. The Houthis claimed the missile attack on the Saudi state-owned product tanker NCC GHAZAL in the Red Sea — spokesman Yahya Saree citing 'violation of the maritime embargo imposed on the Saudi enemy' — with the vessel turning back to port. Riyadh stays interdicted on both flanks. The World Bank's 2026 GCC growth forecast remains cut from 4.4% to 1.3%, with ~80 energy facilities hit and about $58bn of damage.",
   "theatre": "Gulf States",
   "tag": "Gulf States",
   "tags": [
    "Gulf States",
    "Bahrain Shield",
    "GCC Joint Exercise",
    "All Six GCC States",
    "NCC Ghazal",
    "Houthi Claim",
    "Yahya Saree",
    "Maritime Embargo",
    "Bab el-Mandeb",
