   ],
   "source": "Gulf Daily News, 'The Bahrain Defence Force is conducting the joint exercise “Bahrain Shield” with the participation of the armed forces of the Gulf Cooperation Council countries,' July 27, 2026; Anadolu, 'Bahrain announces joint Gulf military exercise amid regional tensions,' July 27, 2026; CBS News live blog, July 27, 2026; Just Security, 'Early Edition: July 27, 2026'; The National, 'Saudi Arabia shifts to Suez as Houthis drive Bab Al Mandeb oil exports to near zero,' July 26, 2026; The Washington Post, 'Houthis claim attack on Saudi oil refinery as Iran war widens to Red Sea,' July 25, 2026; The Conversation, 'How severe has the economic impact of the Iran war been for the Gulf states?'; Stimson Center, 'Iran Conflict Hits Foundations of Gulf Economies'; Al Jazeera, 'New front in US-Iran war escalates as Houthis fire at Saudi oil facilities,' July 26, 2026.",
   "summary": "The Gulf's response to the strike pause was to drill rather than to stand down. Bahrain's Defence Force announced it would conduct the joint 'Bahrain Shield' exercise with the participation of the armed forces of all Gulf Cooperation Council states from Tuesday July 28 to Thursday July 30 — some reporting placed the window at July 29-31 — aimed at enhancing joint military action between GCC countries, exchanging expertise and evaluating shared military systems. It is a collective-defence signal from the six states most exposed to both Iranian retaliation and a US resumption, staged during the first sustained lull of the resumed phase. The kinetic pressure on Saudi Arabia meanwhile continued from two directions that the Iran pause does not cover. Saudi air defences intercepted drones launched from Iraq at petroleum facilities, attributed to Iran-backed militias, and the Red Sea front opened by the Houthis on July 25 — ballistic missiles and drones against Aramco installations at Jizan and Yanbu, announced by Houthi military spokesman Yahya Saree — continued to bite. Jizan processes 400,000 barrels per day; Yanbu is the outlet of the East-West Pipeline and, with Hormuz shut, handled roughly 92% of Saudi seaborne crude exports in June per Kpler data. Bab el-Mandeb transits fell to eleven vessels on Sunday July 26, the lowest monthly level recorded, and Riyadh has begun shifting export routing toward Suez. The cumulative economic reckoning is now substantial: since the war began in February the World Bank has cut its 2026 regional GDP growth forecast from 4.4% to 1.3%, with Oxford Economics projecting recession for some GCC economies in the second half; around 80 energy facilities from production plants to refineries and pipelines have been hit by Iranian missile and drone attacks, with damage estimated at about $58 billion; Qatar stands to lose up to $20 billion a year in export revenue; and IMF projections from April showed contractions for Bahrain, Kuwait and Qatar against positive growth for Saudi Arabia, the UAE and Oman. Jet fuel costs have risen roughly 95% following damage to Qatar's Pearl GTL plant, Kuwait's Mina Al-Ahmadi and Mina Abdullah, and the SATORP refinery.",
   "details": [
    "'BAHRAIN SHIELD' — A GCC-WIDE EXERCISE DURING THE LULL (July 27-30, 2026): The Bahrain Defence Force announced it will conduct the joint 'Bahrain Shield' exercise with the participation of the armed forces of the Gulf Cooperation Council states — Bahrain, Saudi Arabia, the UAE, Kuwait, Oman and Qatar — from Tuesday July 28 to Thursday July 30, with the stated aims of enhancing joint military action between GCC countries, exchanging expertise, and evaluating joint military-action systems. CBS reported the window as July 29-31. The drill comes amid what Anadolu described as a cautious calm since Friday. (Gulf Daily News; Anadolu; CBS News live blog, July 27, 2026.)",
    "SAUDI ARABIA INTERCEPTS IRAQ-LAUNCHED DRONES (July 26-27, 2026): Saudi air defences intercepted drones launched from Iraq targeting petroleum facilities, attributed to Iran-backed militias. The Iran-US strike pause does not extend to the militia and Houthi vectors. (CBS News live blog, July 27, 2026.)",
    "BAB EL-MANDEB AT ELEVEN VESSELS — THE LOWEST MONTHLY LEVEL (July 26-27, 2026): Following the July 25 Houthi attacks on Aramco facilities at Jizan and Yanbu, shipping through the Bab el-Mandeb Strait dropped to its lowest monthly level, with eleven vessels transiting on Sunday July 26. With Hormuz effectively closed and Bab el-Mandeb interdicted, Saudi Arabia is squeezed on both flanks. (Just Security, Early Edition July 27, 2026; The National, July 26, 2026.)",
    "RIYADH SHIFTS TOWARD SUEZ (July 26, 2026): Saudi Arabia has begun shifting crude export routing toward Suez as Houthi pressure drives Bab al-Mandeb oil exports toward zero. Yanbu is the exit point of the East-West Pipeline and, per Kpler data reported by AFP, handled approximately 92% of the Kingdom's seaborne crude exports in June 2026 — the sole functioning route after the closure of Hormuz. Jizan processes 400,000 barrels per day and was set ablaze in the July 25 strike. (The National; TechTimes; Türkiye Today, July 25-26, 2026.)",
    "THE CUMULATIVE ECONOMIC RECKONING (February-July 2026): Since the war began the World Bank has downgraded its 2026 GDP growth forecast for the region from 4.4% to 1.3%, and Oxford Economics projects some GCC economies entering recession in the second half of the year. Around 80 energy facilities — production plants, refineries and pipelines — have been targeted by Iranian missile and drone attacks, with damage estimated at about US$58 billion. Qatar stands to lose up to $20 billion per year in export revenue. IMF projections from April 2026 showed positive growth for Saudi Arabia (+3.1%), the UAE (+3.1%) and Oman (+3.5%), with contractions for Bahrain, Kuwait and Qatar. Saudi Aramco nonetheless reported a 26% jump in first-quarter profits. Damage to Qatar's Pearl GTL plant, Kuwait's Mina Al-Ahmadi and Mina Abdullah, and the SATORP refinery has raised jet fuel costs by roughly 95%. (The Conversation; Stimson Center; Juan Cole; Middle East Council on Global Affairs, 2026.)",
    "THE MARITIME COALITION QUESTION LANDS ON GULF CAPITALS (July 24-27, 2026): The US/UK London conference on a Hormuz shipping-protection coalition is expected to ask participating governments — including regional states — for military equipment and personnel. Gulf participation would formalise what has so far been intelligence-sharing and airspace decisions into a standing multinational naval operation, and sits against the still-contested WSJ reporting that Bahraini and Kuwaiti warplanes struck Iranian depots earlier in July, which Kuwait's ambassador to the US calls 'entirely false.' (Axios, July 24, 2026; prior calibration entries.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "Gulf-state behaviour continues to track the corpus's expectation of hedging under pressure rather than alignment with either belligerent: a GCC-wide defensive exercise, continued diplomatic pressure for de-escalation, and no public commitment to the US/UK maritime coalition. Predictions of Gulf economic collapse remain partially confirmed — a 4.4%-to-1.3% regional growth downgrade and ~$58bn of energy-infrastructure damage are severe, but Saudi, UAE and Omani growth remain positive. (CONTESTED-UNRESOLVED — open-questions: gulf-states-direct-strikes-iran-july-2026; uae-opec-exit-2026.)"
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-07-28",
   "headline": "RUSSIA-UKRAINE — ZELENSKY TO THE WHITE HOUSE TUESDAY WITH AN AIR-CEASEFIRE PROPOSAL WHILE RUSSIA ADVANCES BETWEEN POKROVSK AND KOSTIANTYNIVKA: Ukraine's Khortytsia group confirms Russian gains on the wedge between the two Donetsk towns on July 27. Zelensky meets Trump July 28 — the same day as Netanyahu — to press a proposal for a ceasefire in the air war, and warns Russia is preparing to receive 30,000 MORE North Korean troops with facilities readied in Voronezh since June. Kyiv is asking for the European 'Freyja' anti-ballistic prototype by mid-2027. Iran's parliamentary security chief Ebrahim Azizi threatens retaliation for Ukraine's Caspian strike on an Iranian vessel.",
   "theatre": "Russia-Ukraine War",
   "tag": "Russia-Ukraine",
   "tags": [
    "Russia-Ukraine",
    "Zelensky White House July 28",
    "Air Ceasefire Proposal",
    "Pokrovsk",
    "Kostiantynivka",
    "Khortytsia Group",
    "30,000 More DPRK Troops",
    "Voronezh Staging",
    "Freyja Anti-Ballistic",
    "Caspian Retaliation Threat",
    "Lindsey Graham Funeral"
   ],
   "source": "Kyiv Post, 'Trump to Meet Zelensky in Washington Tuesday, White House Says,' July 27, 2026; HNGN, 'Zelensky To Meet Trump At White House Tuesday As Ukraine Pushes New Ceasefire Proposal,' July 27, 2026; Bloomberg via Liga.net, 'Zelenskyy and Trump Will Meet on July 28'; Ukrainska Pravda, July 24, 2026; Axios, 'Trump to meet Zelensky at White House, as MAGA voices shift on Ukraine,' July 26, 2026; Yahoo/Ukrinform, 'Russia advances between key towns of Pokrovsk, Kostiantynivka in Donetsk Oblast, Ukrainian military says,' July 27, 2026; Just Security, 'Early Edition: July 27, 2026'; Institute for the Study of War / Critical Threats, Russian Offensive Campaign Assessment, July 2026; CBS News live blog, July 27, 2026 (Ebrahim Azizi).",
   "summary": "The Ukraine file moved onto the same Tuesday as the Iran file. President Volodymyr Zelensky travels to Washington to meet Trump at the White House on July 28 — hours apart from Netanyahu, and alongside the funeral of Senator Lindsey Graham — carrying a new proposal: Ukrainian and US officials have been working on an air ceasefire to put to Russian officials as part of a renewed round of peace proposals. An air-only truce is a narrower ask than the comprehensive settlement that has repeatedly stalled over Russian demands that Ukraine withdraw from territory it still holds, and it is designed around the part of the war that is hurting Ukrainian cities most. On the ground the Russian summer campaign continued to grind. A spokesperson for Ukraine's Khortytsia group of forces confirmed on July 27 that Russian troops had advanced in the area between Pokrovsk and Kostiantynivka in Donetsk Oblast, extending the partial wedge Moscow has been driving between the two towns; the Pokrovsk axis remains the main effort, with Russia attempting to break through toward Dnipropetrovsk Oblast and DeepState and front-line accounts reporting Russian forces inside Pokrovsk with a 'search and destruction' operation under way. ISW's assessments through late June and early July judged that Russian forces lack the density to sustain simultaneous offensives against both Pokrovsk and Kostiantynivka, and that gains at Kostiantynivka remained small-group infiltration rather than consolidated control; The Economist estimated Russian gains of about 31 square kilometres over the thirty days to June 30. Zelensky also carried a manpower warning: Russia is preparing to receive an additional 30,000 North Korean troops, with facilities readied in Voronezh oblast since June, on top of the roughly 11,000 DPRK personnel in Kursk Oblast at the start of 2026 and Pyongyang's estimated 25-40% share of Russian artillery ammunition supply. Kyiv is separately requesting a prototype European anti-ballistic system, 'Freyja,' operational by mid-2027. The Iran-Ukraine vector opened by the July 25 Caspian strikes hardened: Ebrahim Azizi, head of Iran's parliamentary national security committee, threatened retaliation for the attack on the Iranian commercial vessel in which a sailor was killed.",
   "details": [
    "ZELENSKY AT THE WHITE HOUSE TUESDAY WITH AN AIR-CEASEFIRE PROPOSAL (July 27, 2026): The White House confirmed Zelensky will meet Trump on Tuesday July 28. Ukrainian and US officials have been working on a proposal for a ceasefire in the air war to present to Russian officials as part of a renewed round of peace proposals. Zelensky will also attend the funeral of Senator Lindsey Graham. Trump hosts Netanyahu the same day in separate meetings, making July 28 a single-day convergence of the Iran and Ukraine tracks. (Kyiv Post; HNGN; Bloomberg via Liga.net; Axios, July 26-27, 2026.)",
    "RUSSIA ADVANCES BETWEEN POKROVSK AND KOSTIANTYNIVKA (July 27, 2026): A spokesperson for Ukraine's Khortytsia group of forces confirmed that Russian troops advanced in the area between Pokrovsk and Kostiantynivka in Donetsk Oblast as the Russian summer offensive continues. The Pokrovsk direction remains the key vector, with most Russian troops concentrated there and an attempt under way to break through toward Dnipropetrovsk Oblast; DeepState and front-line accounts report Russian forces having entered Pokrovsk with a 'search and destruction' operation ongoing. (Yahoo/Ukrinform, July 27, 2026.)",
    "ISW'S STANDING ASSESSMENT (late June-July 2026): ISW judged that Russian forces lack the numerical density to sustain simultaneous offensives against both Pokrovsk and Kostiantynivka, and that gains at Kostiantynivka remain confined to small-group infiltration rather than consolidated control. The Economist estimated Russian gains of 31 square kilometres (12 square miles) over the thirty days to June 30. Ukraine's occupied share holds at roughly 20% with no confirmed net change. (Critical Threats/ISW; Russia Matters War Report Card, July 1, 2026.)",
    "30,000 MORE NORTH KOREAN TROOPS IN PREPARATION (July 25-27, 2026): Zelensky warned that Russia plans to deploy an additional 30,000 North Korean troops, with preparations under way in Voronezh oblast since June, and that Putin is 'preparing the conditions for expanding mobilization.' Roughly 11,000 DPRK personnel (10,000 combat, 1,000 engineers) were in Kursk Oblast at the start of 2026, with Pyongyang supplying an estimated 25-40% of Russian artillery ammunition. (Just Security, Early Edition July 27, 2026; prior calibration entries.)",
    "KYIV REQUESTS THE 'FREYJA' ANTI-BALLISTIC PROTOTYPE (July 27, 2026): Ukraine is requesting a prototype European anti-ballistic system, Freyja, operational by mid-2027 — a European rather than US capability request, consistent with the diversification of Ukrainian air-defence sourcing. (Just Security, Early Edition July 27, 2026.)",
    "IRAN THREATENS RETALIATION FOR THE CASPIAN STRIKE (July 26-27, 2026): Ebrahim Azizi, head of Iran's parliamentary national security committee, threatened retaliation for Ukraine's July 25 long-range strike on an Iranian commercial vessel in the Caspian Sea, in which one sailor was killed. Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi had called the strike 'hostile and criminal,' said it was carried out 'at Israel's behest,' and said it 'cannot go unanswered.' The Ukraine and Iran theatres now have a direct kinetic connection beyond arms transfers. (CBS News live blog, July 27, 2026; prior entries.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "Corpus predictions of a negotiated Russia-Ukraine settlement remain untested: a narrower air-ceasefire proposal reaching the Oval Office on July 28 is the most concrete diplomatic vehicle in months, but Russia has not accepted it and the territorial positions remain irreconcilable. Predictions of a decisive Russian breakthrough remain unconfirmed — the Pokrovsk-Kostiantynivka wedge is real but incremental at roughly 31 km² a month. Predictions of deepening Russia-DPRK military integration are further confirmed by the reported 30,000-troop preparation. (CONTESTED-UNRESOLVED — open-questions: kostiantynivka-control-contested-july-2026.)"
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-07-28",
   "headline": "MULTI-THEATRE ROUNDUP — US-CHINA/TAIWAN: the July 24 Section 301 forced-labour tariffs (10-12.5% across ~60 economies including Taiwan) are the operative regime, while Beijing and Washington are 'agreed in principle' to fold agriculture into a reciprocal reduction framework and Chinese July crude imports rebound toward ~7.8 mb/d. DPRK: Kim orders the Kang Kon destroyer commissioned within two months after July 3 cruise-missile tests, with 30,000 more troops reportedly bound for Russia. LATIN AMERICA: Maduro's trial is set for June 1, 2027; Delcy Rodríguez's government continues normalising with Washington; Cuba's grid has collapsed repeatedly through July under the US fuel blockade. ALSO: Bangladesh's president resigns; ICC members vote 82-of-125 to dismiss Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan.",
   "theatre": "Multi-theatre",
   "tag": "Multi-theatre",
   "tags": [
    "US-China Trade",
    "Section 301 Forced Labour",
    "Taiwan Tariffs",
    "China Crude Rebound",
    "North Korea",
    "Kang Kon Destroyer",
    "DPRK Troops To Russia",
    "Venezuela",
    "Maduro Trial June 2027",
    "Delcy Rodríguez",
    "Cuba Grid Collapse",
    "Colombia Handover Aug 7",
    "Bangladesh President Resigns",
    "ICC Prosecutor Dismissed"
   ],
   "source": "Trade Compliance Resource Hub, 'Trump 2.0 tariff tracker,' July 21, 2026; TaiwanPlus News, 'US Imposes New Tariffs On Trade Partners Including Taiwan,' July 24, 2026; China Briefing, 'US-China Tariff Rates'; PIIE, 'Why the US-China trade war lacks the guardrails of the past'; Bloomberg via Yahoo Finance, 'China’s Crude Imports in July Poised to Rebound From Decade Low'; AEI, 'Korean Peninsula Update, July 14, 2026'; NK News, 'Kim Jong Un oversees cruise missile test from new destroyer ahead of deployment'; CNN, 'Venezuela’s Nicolás Maduro gets a June trial date,' July 22, 2026; ABC News, July 22, 2026; Democracy Now!, 'Cuba’s National Electric Grid Collapses Again Amid U.S. Oil Blockade,' July 15, 2026; Americas Quarterly, 'Maduro’s Fall May Shape Colombia’s Election'; CRS, 'Colombia’s 2026 Presidential Election'; Just Security, 'Early Edition: July 27, 2026.'",
   "summary": "US-China. The tariff regime that took effect July 24 remains operative: the 10% Section 122 global tariff, ruled unlawful by the Court of International Trade in May 2026 but kept in force by a Federal Circuit stay, hit its 150-day statutory limit on July 24 and was replaced the same day by Section 301 forced-labour duties of 10-12.5% covering roughly 60 economies including Taiwan; China remains the most heavily taxed at about 35% above MFN on most goods and well over 100% on certain strategic products. Against that, China's Ministry of Commerce confirmed the two sides have 'agreed in principle' to include agricultural products in a framework for reciprocal tariff reductions and to set indicative targets for expanding two-way agricultural trade — negotiation running alongside escalation. On the energy side, Chinese seaborne crude imports are expected to average about 7.8 million barrels a day in July, rebounding from a more-than-decade low as Gulf flows partially resumed after the June interim agreement, before the July escalation snarled Hormuz again; China takes some 5.4 mb/d through the Strait and receives roughly 80-90% of Iranian exports, and has been in talks with Tehran on safe passage for its own cargoes. On the military file, Secretary of State Rubio has voiced concern over China's increasing regional military activity while Defense Secretary Hegseth has described US-China relations as 'better than they've been in many years,' making no mention of Taiwan. Korea. Kim Jong Un oversaw weapons tests aboard the guided-missile destroyer Kang Kon on July 3, including evaluation of its 'strategic' cruise-missile launch capability, and ordered remaining sea trials completed and the ship commissioned within two months — before September 2026. He has called for twelve Choe Hyon-class destroyers by 2030 and turned attention to a 10,000-tonne guided-missile cruiser. Kim made 92 public appearances in the first half of 2026, 30 of them military — his highest first-half military total since 2012. Zelensky's warning of 30,000 additional DPRK troops bound for Russia is the largest single reported tranche of the war. Latin America. A US judge set June 1, 2027 as the trial date for Nicolás Maduro and Cilia Flores, who pleaded not guilty to drug-trafficking and firearms charges after being seized in the January raid; acting president Delcy Rodríguez continues to meet Washington's demands, with US sanctions on her lifted in April, and has called for an end to remaining sanctions. Cuba's grid has collapsed repeatedly through July — twice in five days in early July and again mid-month — under the combination of the halted Venezuelan crude supply and Executive Order 14380 of January 29, which authorises tariffs on any country supplying Cuba with fuel; the blocked shipments covered about 60% of the roughly 100,000 barrels a day the island's power system needs. Colombia's president-elect takes office August 7 following the June 21 runoff. Elsewhere. Bangladesh's President Mohammed Shahabuddin resigned citing health, amid political pressure over former prime minister Sheikh Hasina's potential return; ICC member states voted 82 of 125 to dismiss Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan over sexual-misconduct allegations; Belgian authorities arrested a Canadian NATO intern on espionage suspicion; and Brazil denied visas to two State Department officials planning to question electoral integrity before October elections.",
   "details": [
    "US-CHINA TARIFF REGIME (July 24-27, 2026): The Section 122 10% global tariff reached its 150-day statutory limit on July 24 and was replaced the same day by Section 301 forced-labour duties of 10-12.5% covering about 60 economies including Taiwan. China remains most heavily taxed — roughly 35% above MFN on most goods and well over 100% on certain strategic products. The USTR proposed the 12.5% China rate following a Section 301 forced-labour investigation, one of two launched to reinstate tariffs after the Supreme Court struck down the IEEPA tariffs. (Trade Compliance Resource Hub; TaiwanPlus News; China Briefing; PIIE, July 2026.)",
    "AGRICULTURE FOLDED INTO THE REDUCTION FRAMEWORK (July 2026): China's Ministry of Commerce confirmed that the US and China have 'agreed in principle' to include agricultural products in a framework for reciprocal tariff reductions and to set indicative targets for expanding two-way agricultural trade — a negotiating track running in parallel with the tariff escalation. (China Briefing; SCMP, July 2026.)",
    "CHINESE CRUDE IMPORTS REBOUND (July 2026): China's seaborne crude imports are expected to average about 7.8 million barrels a day in July, rebounding from the lowest level in more than a decade after Persian Gulf flows ramped up following the June interim agreement — before the July escalation snarled Hormuz traffic again. China moves roughly 5.4 mb/d through the Strait, at least double its Russian imports, and takes an estimated 80-90% of Iranian exports; Beijing has been in talks with Tehran on safe passage for its cargoes. (Bloomberg via Yahoo Finance; Bruegel; Reuters via AOL, July 2026.)",
    "DPRK NAVAL PROGRAMME ON A DEADLINE (July 3-27, 2026): Kim Jong Un oversaw weapons tests aboard the guided-missile destroyer Kang Kon on July 3 including evaluation of its 'strategic' cruise-missile launch capability, and ordered military officials to complete all remaining sea trials and commission the ship within two months — indicating operational status before September 2026. He has called for twelve Choe Hyon-class destroyers by the end of 2030 and has turned to developing a 10,000-tonne guided-missile cruiser, twice the Choe Hyon displacement, to extend naval nuclear deterrence. Kim made 92 public appearances in the first half of 2026, 30 of them military-related — his highest first-half military total since taking power in 2012. (AEI Korean Peninsula Update, July 7 and July 14, 2026; NK News; Defence Security Asia.)",
    "MADURO TRIAL DATE SET (July 22, 2026): A US judge set June 1, 2027 as the trial date for Nicolás Maduro and his wife Cilia Flores, who pleaded not guilty to drug-trafficking and firearms charges after being seized by US troops in a military raid in early January 2026. (CNN; ABC News; GlobalSecurity, July 22-23, 2026.)",
    "VENEZUELA UNDER RODRÍGUEZ (April-July 2026): Delcy Rodríguez, sworn in as acting president after Maduro's capture, has made significant changes to the armed forces and met many of Washington's demands; the US lifted sanctions on her in April. She has publicly called for an end to remaining US sanctions and has discussed security cooperation with Colombian ministers in Caracas. (Atlantic Council; Reuters via AOL; Americas Quarterly, 2026.)",
    "CUBA'S GRID KEEPS FAILING (July 2026): Cuba suffered its second nationwide blackout in five days on July 10 and a further national grid collapse mid-month — the island's repeated total outages of 2026 — driven by the halt of subsidised Venezuelan crude after Maduro's capture and by Executive Order 14380 of January 29, which declared Cuba 'an unusual and extraordinary threat' and authorised tariffs on any country directly or indirectly supplying it with fuel. The blocked shipments had covered about 60% of the roughly 100,000 barrels a day the power system requires; the grid's overcentralised design means a single plant failure can destabilise the whole system. (Democracy Now!; TechTimes; OilPrice; Bloomberg, July 2026.)",
    "COLOMBIA'S HANDOVER APPROACHES (June-August 2026): Colombia certified a May 31 first round in which right-wing outsider Abelardo de la Espriella took 44% against Historic Pact senator Iván Cepeda's 41%, with a June 21 runoff and the winner taking office on August 7. Trump endorsed de la Espriella, which President Petro rejected as election interference; de la Espriella has vowed to join Trump's Americas Counter Cartel initiative. Caracas is watching the handover closely given the candidates' divergent postures toward Venezuela. (CRS, 'Colombia’s 2026 Presidential Election'; Americas Quarterly; Latin America Reports.)",
    "ELSEWHERE (July 26-27, 2026): Bangladesh's President Mohammed Shahabuddin resigned citing health concerns amid political pressure over former prime minister Sheikh Hasina's potential return. ICC member states voted to dismiss Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan, with 82 of 125 supporting removal over sexual-misconduct allegations. Belgian authorities arrested a Canadian NATO intern of Chinese origin on espionage suspicion. Brazil denied visas to two State Department officials planning to question electoral integrity ahead of October elections. Israel's security cabinet approved frameworks allowing a multinational force of about 200 personnel, from nations including Uganda and Morocco, to enter Gaza under Trump's ceasefire plan. (Just Security, Early Edition July 27, 2026.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "US-China decoupling predictions remain partially confirmed — the tariff floor persists through successive legal authorities even after the IEEPA tariffs were struck down — while predictions of an imminent Taiwan military crisis remain untested; Taiwan's exposure this cycle is tariff policy, not force. Predictions of deepening Russia-DPRK integration are further confirmed. Predictions of US-forced regime change in Venezuela remain partially confirmed with the Rodríguez government normalising with Washington and Maduro's trial now docketed for June 2027; predictions of Cuban systemic collapse under fuel denial remain the most starkly confirmed item in the Latin America theatre. (CONTESTED-UNRESOLVED — open-questions: trump-xi-summit-iran-readout-divergence-2026.)"
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-07-27",
   "headline": "DAY 151 — THE PAUSE HOLDS A SECOND NIGHT AND IRAN RECIPROCATES: 'WE HAVE ALSO HALTED OUR RETALIATORY OPERATIONS' (ARMY SPOKESMAN MOHAMMAD AKRAMINIA), AND A DEFENSE DEPARTMENT SOURCE TELLS CNN US OPERATIONS ARE 'ON A HOLD.' AXIOS REPORTS CENTCOM COMMANDER BRAD COOPER TOLD THE WHITE HOUSE THE STRIKES HAD 'SIGNIFICANTLY DEGRADED' IRAN'S ANTI-SHIPPING CAPABILITY AND THE TARGET SET WAS 'MOSTLY EXHAUSTED'; VANCE AND GEN. DAN CAINE RAISED MUNITIONS AND AIR-DEFENCE-INTERCEPTOR CONCERNS AT THE JULY 24 WHITE HOUSE MEETING. IRAN-OMAN DEPUTY-FM TALKS IN TEHRAN MAKE 'PROGRESS' ON HORMUZ TRANSIT MECHANISMS; WALTZ SAYS TRUMP IS 'GIVING DIPLOMACY SOME SPACE' WHILE 'THERE'S MORE MOVING INTO THEATER.' BRENT PLUNGES ~5-7% TO ~$91/BBL. THE PENTAGON CUTS ITS OFFICIAL IRAN-WAR DEATH TOLL FROM 18 TO 14 AND ITS WOUNDED FROM 482 TO 420 BY MOVING FOUR JULY DEATHS AND 207 WOUNDED INTO A NEW 'OVERSEAS OPERATIONS' CATEGORY. UKRAINE STRIKES IRAN-LINKED CARGO VESSELS IN THE CASPIAN, KILLING A SAILOR. HORMUZ AT A NEAR-TOTAL STOP — ONE VESSEL, ZERO TANKERS, IN 24 HOURS — WITH THE BLOCKADE 'IN FULL EFFECT.' STILL AIR/MARITIME: NO LANDING, NO KHARG SEIZURE, NO DRAFT — AND THE TRIPOLI ARG / 31ST MEU HAS LEFT THE THEATRE. PREDICTED US GROUND SEIZURE REMAINS DISCONFIRMED.",
   "theatre": "Iran / Strait of Hormuz",
   "tag": "Iran War",
   "tags": [
    "Iran War",
    "Strait of Hormuz",
