   "source": "Fortune, 'U.S. military suddenly goes silent on Iran airstrikes while Houthi rebels and Saudi Arabia exchange attacks in battle over Red Sea shipping,' July 25, 2026; The Washington Post, 'Iran reports no new U.S. strikes as tensions remain high,' July 25, 2026; Fox News Digital live blog, 'Iran reports first night without new US strikes as Houthis attack Saudi Arabian city,' July 25, 2026; CNN live blog, 'There's an apparent pause in US-Iran strikes, even as a new front emerges on the Red Sea,' July 25, 2026; Al Jazeera live blog, 'Trump says US locked and loaded as it seeks Iran talks,' July 25, 2026; RFE/RL, 'Trump Says Iran \"Far More Serious\" In Talks, Even As Attacks Intensify, Widen In Region,' July 25, 2026; Reuters via Pakistan Today/BOE Report, 'Pakistan, Iran explore path towards new talks with US in a China-initiated push,' July 24, 2026; Middle East Eye / Anadolu / IRNA, 'Omani delegation arrives in Tehran to discuss managing traffic in Strait of Hormuz,' July 24, 2026; AP via Türkiye Today, 'US military disables another tanker for breaching Iran blockade,' July 25, 2026; AAA Fuel Prices / Clarksville Online, 'National Average Gas Price Jumps to $4.09,' July 25, 2026; IMF PortWatch Strait of Hormuz transit data (July 19, 2026).",
   "summary": "On Day 150 the war's defining pattern broke: after thirteen consecutive nights, Iran reported no US strikes overnight into Saturday July 25. Iranian Health Ministry spokesman Hossein Kermanpour posted that 'Iran had a peaceful night'; US Central Command announced no new strikes and declined to comment when contacted. President Trump framed the pause as leverage rather than retreat, saying the US remains 'locked and loaded' for a major strike while the threatened 'massive' attack 'may be unnecessary' because talks are continuing — Iranian negotiators, he said, 'are being by far the most serious that we've seen them,' though 'that doesn't mean we get there,' and Washington could still escalate 'to a much higher level.' His stated bottom line was unchanged: 'They cannot have a nuclear weapon.' The diplomatic track thickened on two channels: an Omani delegation that arrived in Tehran July 24 discussed 'appropriate mechanisms for managing ship traffic in the Strait of Hormuz' (IRNA), a formula the US and Gulf states have rejected because it concedes Iranian co-administration; and Pakistan — encouraged by China, and with momentum from Iranian Interior Minister Eskandar Momeni's second Islamabad visit in under two weeks — explored a route back to US-Iran talks, with Beijing's foreign ministry confirming its backing. The kinetic energy of the war migrated rather than stopped: the Houthis struck Saudi Aramco facilities at Jizan and Yanbu, Saudi Arabia struck Hodeidah, and US blockade enforcement continued — CENTCOM disabled the M/T Lavine in the Gulf of Oman after at least four attempts to run the blockade (firing into the engine room) and completed a verification boarding of the Comoros-flagged M/T Charminar in the Arabian Sea, which was cleared onward; since the blockade was re-imposed the running tally is 12 vessels redirected and 2 disabled. Trump said damage to ships 'will be paid for by Iranian money that the United States has in its possession and controls.' Hormuz stayed functionally shut — IMF PortWatch recorded 15 transits on July 19 against an 88-per-day pre-crisis baseline, with roughly 85 tankers running dark and the IMO still calling the waterway too dangerous for commercial transit. Brent ended the week in the $96-98 range after the July 23 spike to a $100.69 settle (up more than 12% on the week and about 30% on the month), and the AAA US pump average hit $4.111 a gallon, about 95 cents above a year earlier. Every ground-execution indicator stayed at zero: no US troops in Iran, no Kharg landing or seizure, no new ARG/MEU order, no draft; 50,000+ US personnel unchanged. The corpus-predicted US GROUND seizure of Hormuz/Kharg remains disconfirmed.",
   "details": [
    "STRIKE STREAK BROKEN (July 25, 2026): Iran reported no US strikes overnight into Saturday — the first such night after 13 consecutive nights of attacks. Health Ministry spokesman Hossein Kermanpour: 'Iran had a peaceful night.' CENTCOM announced no new strikes and declined immediate comment. A cessation of the AIR campaign, not a substitution of it by anything on the ground. (Fortune; The Washington Post; Fox News, July 25, 2026.)",
    "'LOCKED AND LOADED' BUT 'MAY BE UNNECESSARY' (July 25, 2026): Trump said dialogue with Tehran continues and the threatened 'massive' attack 'may be unnecessary,' while the US remains 'locked and loaded' and could escalate 'to a much higher level.' He called Iranian negotiators 'by far the most serious that we've seen them,' adding 'that doesn't mean we get there,' and repeated that Iran 'cannot have a nuclear weapon.' (Al Jazeera live blog; The Epoch Times; RFE/RL, July 25, 2026.)",
    "OMANI DELEGATION IN TEHRAN (July 24-25, 2026): An Omani diplomatic delegation arrived in Tehran to discuss 'appropriate mechanisms for managing ship traffic in the Strait of Hormuz' (IRNA). Tehran has consistently offered co-management with Oman, the Strait's only other littoral state; the US and Gulf states have rejected any Iranian-led transit regime. (Middle East Eye; Anadolu; Al Jazeera, July 24-25, 2026.)",
    "PAKISTAN/CHINA TALKS PUSH (July 24-25, 2026): Pakistan explored a route to reviving US-Iran negotiations in a China-initiated push, with momentum from Iranian Interior Minister Eskandar Momeni's second Islamabad visit in under two weeks; China's foreign ministry confirmed support for Pakistani and other mediation. Sources cautioned the obstacles remain high. (Reuters via Pakistan Today/BOE Report; China-Global South Project, July 24-25, 2026.)",
    "BLOCKADE ENFORCEMENT CONTINUES (July 25, 2026): CENTCOM disabled the M/T Lavine in the Gulf of Oman after the crew ignored warnings and attempted to breach the blockade at least four times, firing into the ship's engine room; US forces also completed a verification boarding of the Comoros-flagged M/T Charminar in the Arabian Sea, which continued its voyage. Running total since the blockade was re-imposed: 12 vessels redirected, 2 disabled. Interdiction and visit-board-search — not a port seizure or landing. (AP via Türkiye Today; NewsNation; CENTCOM, July 25, 2026.)",
    "HORMUZ STILL FUNCTIONALLY SHUT (July 25, 2026): IMF PortWatch recorded 15 transits on July 19 against an 88-per-day pre-crisis baseline; roughly 85 tankers were running dark (no AIS), and the IMO continued to assess the waterway as too dangerous for commercial vessels. Late-June/early-July averages had been ~25 ships/day versus ~100 a year earlier. (IMF PortWatch; Statista; Windward, July 2026.)",
    "OIL AND PUMP PRICES (July 24-25, 2026): Brent ended the week in the $96-98 range — reports differ on the precise Friday settle (~$97 per CNBC/Yahoo; $98.38 per TradingEconomics) — after the July 23 spike that closed at $100.69, leaving Brent up more than 12% on the week and roughly 30% on the month; one outlet reported an early-Saturday indication back above $100 on the Jizan/Yanbu strikes, and another reported a retreat to ~$96 on the Pakistan/China talks reports (weekend quotes are indicative; futures markets were closed). The AAA US national average pump price reached $4.111/gal on July 25, up from $3.95 on July 16 and about 95 cents above the year-earlier $3.15; California highest at $5.59, Indiana lowest at $3.53. Still below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (CNBC; TradingEconomics; TechTimes; Fox News; AAA/Clarksville Online, July 24-25, 2026.)",
    "CUMULATIVE CASUALTY PICTURE (July 25, 2026): Reported running tolls — more than 3,400 killed in Iran (Iran Health Ministry); 18 US service members; 24 Israeli civilians and 38 Israeli troops; more than 4,000 in Lebanon. Iranian officials put the July 8-25 phase at more than 100 US air attacks across Iran, at least 50 killed and more than 500 wounded. Figures are largely Iranian-sourced and not independently verified. (Fortune; Al Jazeera, July 18-25, 2026.)",
    "US INTELLIGENCE ON THE NEW SUPREME LEADER (July 25, 2026): A New York Times-reported US assessment holds that Mojtaba Khamenei is more willing than his father to pursue nuclear weapons — seeking a warhead that could be miniaturised for a missile — where the elder Khamenei's fatwa had been treated as a genuine obstacle. Officials cautioned that most of the underlying intelligence predates the war and that the new leader has been in hiding, with limited communication, since being injured in the Feb 28 strike. (NYT via Ynet; The Jerusalem Post; ANI, July 25, 2026.)",
    "GROUND RUNG UNCHANGED (July 25, 2026): No US troops in Iran, no Kharg landing or seizure, no new ARG/MEU order, no draft; 50,000+ US personnel in the CENTCOM AOR unchanged. The day's movement was a REDUCTION in kinetic activity on the US-Iran axis plus a widening of a proxy/allied fight in the Red Sea — neither a ground move. (Fortune; Fox News; CENTCOM, July 19-25, 2026.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "The corpus-predicted US GROUND seizure of Hormuz/Kharg remains disconfirmed. Day 150 broke the 13-night air campaign rather than escalating it: no strikes were announced, Trump paired 'locked and loaded' with 'may be unnecessary,' and two mediation channels (Oman on Hormuz traffic mechanisms; Pakistan/China on a talks restart) opened — while every ground-execution indicator stayed at zero and 50,000+ personnel held. Predictions of a negotiated Hormuz transit regime remain live and untested; the Iranian-co-administration formula Oman is carrying is precisely the arrangement Washington has refused. (CONTESTED-UNRESOLVED — open-questions: kharg-seizure-total-control-threat-june-2026; iran-hormuz-administration-tolls-dispute-june-2026.)",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "The halt of the 13-night strike campaign is read either as the operational pause before a larger escalation — repositioning, reloading and reposturing for 'something much bigger' — or as the first concrete de-escalation of the resumed phase, produced by the Omani and Pakistani/Chinese mediation tracks and signalled by Trump's own 'may be unnecessary.'",
     "evidentiary_status": "Both readings rest on the same record: CENTCOM announced no strikes and declined comment (it did not declare a pause, a ceasefire, or a resumption); Trump simultaneously said the US is 'locked and loaded,' that a 'massive' attack 'may be unnecessary,' and that Iranian negotiators are 'by far the most serious' he has seen; an Omani delegation was in Tehran on Hormuz traffic mechanisms; and the Pentagon's reported ground-operation options from July 24 remain undecided. Nothing in the open record distinguishes a reload from a climb-down.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "former Defense Secretary Mark Esper, who said the administration may have stopped strikes to 'reposition, prepare, reload, et cetera, for something much bigger that looks like the beginning of the war,' possibly 'a 24- or 48-hour respite'",
      "outlets and analysts reading the same silence alongside the Omani and Pakistani/Chinese tracks as a negotiated de-escalation in progress"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "A pause of unannounced duration is observationally identical to pre-escalation preparation until either strikes resume at a higher intensity or a pause/agreement is formalised. US officials have characterised neither.",
     "open_question_ref": "kharg-seizure-total-control-threat-june-2026"
    }
   ]
