   ],
   "source": "Fortune, July 25, 2026; Fox News Digital live blog, July 25, 2026; The Jerusalem Post, \"'Finger on trigger': Trump has not decided on large-scale attacks against Iran, sources tell 'Post',\" July 25, 2026; MEAWW/Fox News, \"'Something much bigger': Former Pentagon chief predicts US could expand Iran attacks soon,\" July 25, 2026; Foreign Policy, 'How a U.S. Ground Operation in Iran Could Unfold,' July 24, 2026; Sputnik/GlobalSecurity, 'More Than 50,000 US Troops Deployed Across Middle East - US CENTCOM,' July 19, 2026; CNBC, 'Automatic U.S. military draft registration by December 2026 planned,' April 9, 2026; Washington Examiner, \"Trump says 'I would' invade Iran's Kharg Island if 'we degrade them' enough,\" July 2026.",
   "details": [
    "STATUS: no_ground_troops (Day 150). The US-Iran axis went KINETICALLY QUIET — no strikes announced, none reported by Iran. A reduction in air activity is the opposite of a ground-escalation indicator. (Fortune; The Washington Post, July 25, 2026.)",
    "TROOP COUNT: 50,000+ US personnel across the CENTCOM AOR — the level CENTCOM publicly confirmed on July 19 and described as the largest US footprint in the region since the 2003 invasion of Iraq — broadly unchanged. No new deployment order accompanied the pause. (Sputnik/GlobalSecurity, July 19, 2026.)",
    "PENTAGON OPTIONS STILL OPTIONS: The July 24 reporting that the Pentagon is preparing options for potential US ground operations lasting weeks (limited SpecOps/conventional raids on Kharg Island and coastal weapons sites, explicitly NOT a full-scale invasion) remains live, but The Jerusalem Post reported July 25 that Trump has NOT decided on large-scale attacks — 'finger on trigger.' Undecided options are planning, not execution. (Foreign Policy, July 24; The Jerusalem Post, July 25, 2026.)",
    "THE PAUSE CUTS BOTH WAYS: Former Defense Secretary Mark Esper said the halt could be repositioning to 'reposition, prepare, reload, et cetera, for something much bigger that looks like the beginning of the war' — possibly 'a 24- or 48-hour respite.' This is an INTENT reading of a REDUCTION in observed activity, and Esper framed the prospective escalation as an expanded war, not specifically a ground campaign. (MEAWW/Fox News, July 25, 2026.)",
    "KHARG: no US move. No landing, no seizure, no amphibious task force ordered against it. Trump's standing position remains conditional and outsourced — he has said he 'would' invade Kharg if US forces 'degrade them' enough and that 'sometimes you need a ground campaign,' while adding there are 'other people who would do the campaign for us.' (Washington Examiner; Benzinga, July 2026.)",
    "DRAFT: no change. No Selective Service activation, stop-loss, or Guard/Reserve call-up. The only standing item remains the pre-existing statutory plan for automatic Selective Service registration of men 18-26 beginning December 18, 2026 — an administrative automation mandated by the NDAA, not a draft activation; only Congress can authorise induction. White House press secretary Karoline Leavitt has said Trump 'keeps his options on the table'; polling shows 77% of Americans oppose conscription or are unsure. (CNBC; Fast Company; Responsible Statecraft, 2026.)",
    "MARINE POSTURE: no new amphibious order. The Boxer ARG / 11th MEU (~2,200 Marines) and the Tripoli ARG / 31st MEU held routine/contingency posture in the Arabian Sea/Indian Ocean; Marines were used, if at all, only for at-sea boardings (the M/T Charminar verification boarding). The 82nd Airborne headquarters element and brigade combat team deployed to the region in the spring remain in place with no forcible-entry movement reported. (Foreign Policy, July 24; CENTCOM, July 25, 2026.)",
    "NEW DIRECT-STRIKE ACTORS WERE AIR, AND NOT AMERICAN: The Wall Street Journal reported that Bahraini and Kuwaiti warplanes struck Iranian missile and drone storage depots earlier in July with UAE targeting intelligence and air cover — the first direct Gulf Arab military action against Iran (Kuwait's ambassador to the US formally denies it). Even taken at face value this is allied AIR action, not a US ground operation, and it cuts toward Trump's 'other people will do the ground campaign for us' outsourcing posture. (WSJ, July 24; Arab Times, July 25, 2026.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "Ground-invasion indicators remain at zero on the US side, and on Day 150 the adjacent KINETIC indicator fell for the first time since the resumed phase began: the 13-night air campaign stopped without a successor operation. Pentagon ground-operation options remain reported-but-undecided; the only new direct-strike actors were allied air forces. The specific US-ground-seizure-of-Hormuz/Kharg prediction stays disconfirmed. (CONTESTED-UNRESOLVED — open-question: kharg-seizure-total-control-threat-june-2026.)"
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-07-26",
   "headline": "GULF STATES — THE WAR'S KINETIC CENTRE MOVES TO THE RED SEA AND, PER WSJ, GULF ARABS STRUCK IRAN DIRECTLY. The Houthis fired 'dozens of ballistic and cruise missiles and drones' at Saudi Aramco facilities in JIZAN (refinery ablaze; CNN-geolocated fires) and YANBU — the Kingdom's Red Sea export relief valve while Hormuz is shut — in retaliation for Saudi strikes on Hodeidah and Kamaran Island; Riyadh called its strikes a 'firm, resolute and proportionate military response' and the Houthi political wing vowed 'escalation for escalation.' Separately the WSJ reported BAHRAINI AND KUWAITI WARPLANES struck Iranian missile/drone depots earlier in July with UAE targeting intelligence and air cover — the first direct Gulf Arab strikes on Iran — which KUWAIT FORMALLY DENIES as 'entirely false.' Iran's FM Araghchi said there is 'no military solution' in Yemen. Qatar restored normal maritime navigation in its coastal zones.",
   "theatre": "Iran / Strait of Hormuz",
   "tag": "Gulf States",
   "tags": [
    "Gulf States",
    "Red Sea Front",
    "Houthi Strikes on Aramco",
    "Jizan Refinery Fire",
    "Yanbu",
    "Saudi Strikes on Hodeidah",
    "'Escalation for Escalation'",
    "WSJ: Bahrain/Kuwait Struck Iran",
    "Kuwait Denial",
    "UAE Targeting Intelligence",
    "Araghchi 'No Military Solution'",
    "Qatar Reopens Navigation"
   ],
   "source": "The Wall Street Journal, 'Bahrain, Kuwait Warplanes Struck Iran in Rare Gulf Retaliation,' July 24, 2026 (via TASS, ANI, The Jerusalem Post, Defence Security Asia); Arab Times, 'Kuwait Denies Participation in Military Operations Against Iran, Calls WSJ Report False,' July 25, 2026; Fortune, July 25, 2026; Türkiye Today, 'Houthis claim strikes on Aramco sites in Saudi Arabia's Jizan, Yanbu,' July 25, 2026; The Washington Post, 'Houthis claim attack on Saudi oil refinery as Iran war widens to Red Sea,' July 25, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Saudi Arabia strikes Houthi-held Hodeidah, Yemeni group says,' July 24-25, 2026; The National, 'Houthis attack Saudi Aramco sites - with Yemen at risk of renewed war,' July 25, 2026; Türkiye Today / Free Malaysia Today, \"Iran's Araghchi says 'no military solution' to Yemen conflict,\" July 25, 2026; Qatar Ministry of Transport via Qatar Tribune / PortNews, July 2026.",
   "summary": "The Gulf theatre absorbed the day's kinetic activity as the US-Iran axis went quiet. Houthi military spokesman Brig. Gen. Yahya Saree announced in a prerecorded statement that the group had fired 'dozens of ballistic and cruise missiles and drones' at Saudi Aramco-affiliated facilities in the Red Sea towns of Jizan and Yanbu, saying the operations 'successfully achieved their objectives.' Video geolocated by CNN showed large fires in Jizan's industrial area, home to a major Aramco refinery; Saudi civil defence confirmed alerts sounded repeatedly early Saturday, and Saudi air defences intercepted further ballistic missiles from Yemen later in the day. As of early Saturday neither Aramco nor the Saudi government had issued a damage assessment. The strikes were framed as retaliation for a Saudi-led coalition attack on Hodeidah port and telecommunications infrastructure and on Kamaran Island — which coalition spokesman Maj. Gen. Turki al-Malki called a 'firm, resolute and proportionate military response' to Houthi attacks on commercial vessels, and which Houthi-run al-Masirah said wounded a woman on Kamaran (Fortune reported two injured). The Houthi political wing vowed to meet 'escalation with escalation.' The targeting matters economically: Yanbu is Saudi Arabia's Red Sea export outlet and, with Hormuz functionally shut, its principal remaining crude corridor. Separately, The Wall Street Journal reported July 24 that Bahraini and Kuwaiti warplanes had secretly struck Iranian missile and drone storage depots and other military sites earlier in July — the first direct Gulf Arab military action against Iran, breaking decades of proxy-war convention — with the UAE providing targeting intelligence and defensive air cover. Kuwait's ambassador to the United States, Sheikha Zain Al-Sabah, sent a formal letter to the WSJ's editorial board rejecting the report as 'entirely false,' saying Kuwait has not participated in any military operation against Iran and has not permitted its territory, airspace or territorial waters to be used to launch offensive operations against any neighbouring country. Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi told reporters there is 'no military solution to the issue of Yemen, Bab al-Mandab, and other regional issues,' called for dialogue, and said events around Bab al-Mandab were 'rooted in old disputes' between Yemen, Saudi Arabia and regional states and 'cannot be attributed to Iran.' Qatar's Ministry of Transport restored normal maritime navigation for all vessel types within its roughly seven-nautical-mile coastal zones.",
   "details": [
    "HOUTHI STRIKES ON ARAMCO (July 25, 2026): Brig. Gen. Yahya Saree said the Houthis fired 'dozens of ballistic and cruise missiles and drones' at Aramco-affiliated facilities in Jizan and Yanbu and that the operations 'successfully achieved their objectives.' CNN-geolocated video showed large fires in Jizan's industrial area; no official Aramco or Saudi damage assessment had been issued as of early Saturday. (Fortune; The Washington Post; Türkiye Today; CNN, July 25, 2026.)",
    "SAUDI STRIKES ON HODEIDAH AND KAMARAN (July 24-25, 2026): The Saudi-led coalition struck Houthi military sites, Hodeidah port and telecommunications infrastructure, and Kamaran Island. Coalition spokesman Maj. Gen. Turki al-Malki described a 'firm, resolute and proportionate military response' against sites used to 'threaten commercial vessels'; al-Masirah reported a woman wounded on Kamaran (Fortune: two injured). (Al Jazeera; The National; RT; Fox News, July 24-25, 2026.)",
    "'ESCALATION FOR ESCALATION' (July 25, 2026): The Houthi political wing condemned the Saudi strikes and vowed to meet 'escalation with escalation.' Saudi air defences intercepted further ballistic missiles from Yemen on Saturday with no immediate damage reported. (Anadolu; Fox News, July 25, 2026.)",
    "WHY YANBU MATTERS (July 25, 2026): Yanbu is the terminus of Saudi Arabia's Red Sea export route — the 'relief valve' that has cushioned markets while Hormuz is functionally shut — and the Houthis had declared a maritime blockade of Saudi Arabia on July 20 and struck two Saudi crude tankers on July 23. Roughly 12% of world trade, including a quarter of container traffic, transits Bab el-Mandeb. (Fortune; CNN, July 20-25, 2026.)",
    "WSJ: BAHRAIN AND KUWAIT STRUCK IRAN (July 24, 2026): The Wall Street Journal ('Bahrain, Kuwait Warplanes Struck Iran in Rare Gulf Retaliation') reported that Bahraini and Kuwaiti warplanes secretly struck Iranian missile and drone storage depots and other military installations earlier in July — their first direct military action against Iran — with the UAE providing targeting intelligence and defensive air cover. Both states host US forces and had absorbed months of Iranian missile and drone attacks. The WSJ described the strikes as a significant departure from the region's decades-long proxy-war playbook. (WSJ via TASS, ANI, The Jerusalem Post, Defence Security Asia, July 24-25, 2026.)",
    "KUWAIT'S FORMAL DENIAL (July 25, 2026): Kuwait's ambassador to the United States, Sheikha Zain Al-Sabah, sent a formal letter to the WSJ editorial board calling the report 'entirely false,' stating that Kuwait has not participated in any military operations against Iran and has not allowed the use of its territory, airspace or territorial waters to launch offensive operations against any neighbouring country. Bahrain and the UAE did not publicly confirm the account. (Arab Times, July 25, 2026.)",
    "IRAN'S RESPONSE (July 25, 2026): FM Abbas Araghchi said 'we believe that there is no military solution to the issue of Yemen, Bab al-Mandab, and other regional issues,' called for dialogue, and said the Yemen situation 'cannot be attributed to Iran,' being 'rooted in old disputes and issues between Yemen, Saudi Arabia and the countries of the region.' He has separately warned that attacks on Iranian infrastructure would compel 'a powerful and decisive response.' (Türkiye Today; Free Malaysia Today, July 25, 2026.)",
    "QATAR REOPENS COASTAL NAVIGATION (July 2026): Qatar's Ministry of Transport restored normal maritime navigation for all vessel types within its roughly seven-nautical-mile coastal zones — from Lusail City and its ports east to Hamad Port, and from Umm Heesh west to Doha Salwa — after precautionary conflict-related restrictions. (Qatar Tribune; PortNews; Fox News, July 2026.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "Bears on corpus predictions of Gulf-state alignment and regional coalition behaviour. The documented record shows Gulf states moving from passive hosting and interception toward active belligerency — Saudi Arabia in an open exchange with the Houthis, and (per WSJ, denied by Kuwait) Bahrain and Kuwait striking Iran directly with UAE support. This runs against earlier calibration entries in which Gulf states refused airspace and condemned strikes on Iran, but the specific Bahrain/Kuwait claim cannot be confirmed from open sources against an official denial. Status: partially confirmed on Saudi-Houthi belligerency; contested-unresolved on direct Gulf strikes into Iran. (CONTESTED-UNRESOLVED — open-question: gulf-states-direct-strikes-iran-july-2026.)",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "The WSJ account of Bahraini and Kuwaiti warplanes striking Iranian depots with UAE support is read either as an accurate disclosure of a covert first — Gulf Arab states crossing from host-and-intercept into direct belligerency — or as an account Kuwait says is simply untrue, with the reporting reflecting sourcing that overstates Kuwait's role (or conflates coalition air activity with national participation).",
     "evidentiary_status": "Documented on both sides: a named WSJ report with specific targets (missile and drone storage depots) and a described UAE intelligence/air-cover role; and a formal, on-the-record letter from Kuwait's ambassador to the United States to the paper's editorial board calling it 'entirely false' and denying any use of Kuwaiti territory, airspace or waters for offensive operations. Bahrain and the UAE have neither confirmed nor denied. No imagery, flight-tracking, BDA or Iranian attribution of the strikes to Gulf aircraft has been published.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "The Wall Street Journal and outlets syndicating its report (TASS, ANI, The Jerusalem Post, Defence Security Asia, voz.us)",
