   "source": "NPR, 'Trump threatens a major escalation in Iran after 13 straight nights of strikes,' July 24, 2026; CNN live blog, 'Trump mulls escalation but says Tehran is getting more serious,' July 24, 2026; Foreign Policy, 'How a U.S. Ground Operation in Iran Could Unfold,' July 24, 2026; Just Security, 'Early Edition: July 24, 2026'; GlobalSecurity, 'Iran War 2026 -- Day 147 Update -- 24 July 2026'; Yahoo Finance/CNBC, 'Oil set for weekly rise amid Red Sea shipping attacks, Kazakhstan output cuts,' July 24, 2026; Fox News, 'Iran responds to reports US weighing ground operations: we never accept humiliation,' July 24, 2026.",
   "summary": "On Day 149 the US ran a 13th consecutive night of strikes on Iran while President Trump publicly weighed a step-change in the campaign and Iran rejected the latest ceasefire overture. Trump told Axios he was considering restarting major combat operations 'bigger than' the strikes carried out during 'Operation Epic Fury,' while stressing he had not yet made a decision; he also said talks were continuing and that Tehran was 'getting more serious,' even as he insisted 'they need more of the same' and declined to negotiate a new pause. Iran rejected a US ceasefire proposal delivered Thursday by the Iraqi prime minister, with Iranian officials saying Tehran was not interested in a temporary deal that left the question of control of the Strait of Hormuz unresolved. Oil pulled back from its spike: after Brent briefly topped $100 and settled at $100.69 (+7%) on July 23 on the Houthi attack on two Saudi tankers (the Incilia and Layla), Brent fell roughly 4% on Friday July 24 to settle near $97 — its biggest one-day drop since late June — as traders judged that crude was still traversing Middle East routes despite the escalation, with Kazakhstan output cuts also in the mix. On the ground rung, the Pentagon was reported to be preparing options for potential US ground operations in Iran that could last weeks if Trump approves — framed as limited raids by special operations and conventional infantry rather than a full-scale invasion, with possible targets including Kharg Island and coastal weapons sites near the Strait of Hormuz. Iran's Foreign Ministry spokesman Esmaeil Baghaei warned of 'repercussions' if the US moves against Kharg, and Tehran said it would 'never accept humiliation.' Despite the intensified rhetoric and planning, every ground-execution indicator stayed at zero: no US troops in Iran, no Kharg landing or seizure, no new ARG/MEU order, and no draft; ~50,000 US personnel unchanged. The corpus-predicted US GROUND seizure of Hormuz/Kharg remains disconfirmed.",
   "details": [
    "13TH CONSECUTIVE NIGHT (July 24, 2026): The US ran a 13th straight night of strikes on Iranian military command centers, drone/coastal and maritime targets tied to the Hormuz threat; Trump signaled he could go 'bigger.' Continuation of the AIR campaign, not a landing. (NPR; GlobalSecurity, July 24, 2026.)",
    "'BIGGER THAN OPERATION EPIC FURY' (July 24, 2026): Trump told Axios he was considering restarting major combat operations larger than those in 'Operation Epic Fury,' but stressed no decision had been made — a threatened AIR/standoff escalation, not an announced ground assault. (CNN; NPR, July 24, 2026.)",
    "IRAN REJECTS IRAQI-BROKERED CEASEFIRE (July 24, 2026): Iran rejected a US ceasefire proposal delivered by the Iraqi prime minister, saying it was not interested in a temporary deal that left control of the Strait of Hormuz unresolved. Trump said talks continue and Tehran is 'getting more serious' but 'need[s] more of the same.' (CNN, July 24, 2026.)",
    "OIL RETREATS TO ~$97 (July 24, 2026): After Brent briefly topped $100 and settled at $100.69 (+7%) on July 23 on the Houthi tanker attack, it fell ~4% Friday to settle near $97 — its biggest one-day drop since late June — as crude kept traversing Middle East routes despite the escalation; Kazakhstan output cuts added supply. Still below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (Yahoo Finance; CNBC, July 24, 2026.)",
    "PENTAGON READIES GROUND-OPS OPTIONS (July 24, 2026): The Pentagon was reported to be preparing options for potential US ground operations in Iran that could last weeks if Trump approves — limited raids by special operations and conventional infantry, not a full-scale invasion, with possible targets including Kharg Island and coastal weapons sites near Hormuz. Planning/options, not an executed movement of troops. (Foreign Policy; Just Security, July 24, 2026.)",
    "IRAN: 'REPERCUSSIONS' / 'NEVER ACCEPT HUMILIATION' (July 24, 2026): FM spokesman Esmaeil Baghaei warned of 'repercussions' if the US moves against Kharg Island; Iran said it would 'never accept humiliation' in response to the ground-operations reports. (Fox News, July 24, 2026.)",
    "GROUND RUNG UNCHANGED (July 24, 2026): No US troops in Iran, no Kharg landing or seizure, no new ARG/MEU order, no draft; ~50,000 US personnel unchanged. The weighed 'bigger' attack is framed as an air/standoff escalation; Pentagon 'options' are planning, not execution. (NPR; Foreign Policy, July 24, 2026.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "The corpus-predicted US GROUND seizure of Hormuz/Kharg remains disconfirmed. Day 149 ran a 13th straight night, saw Trump threaten a 'bigger' escalation and Iran reject an Iraqi-brokered ceasefire over Hormuz control, and drew reporting that the Pentagon is readying options for weeks of limited ground operations — but produced no landing, no Kharg assault, no new ARG/MEU order, and no draft; ~50,000 personnel unchanged and the weighed escalation framed as air/standoff. (CONTESTED-UNRESOLVED — open-question: kharg-seizure-total-control-threat-june-2026.)",
