   "summary": "The US imposed a fresh wave of tariffs late Thursday on about 60 nations — including China, Japan, South Korea, India and EU member states — hours before a temporary 10% blanket levy was due to expire. The new duties range from 10% to 12.5% and are the outcome of a Section 301 investigation into forced labour, with USTR Jamieson Greer casting the action as correcting 'both a human rights abuse and distortive trade practice.' China draws a 12.5% added rate (leaving it at roughly a 35%+ effective rate); Japan, South Korea, Switzerland and the EU fall in the 10-12.5% band, while Canada, Mexico, India, Indonesia and the UK sit at 10%. Analysts read the forced-labour rationale as a pretext for the broader America-first agenda. A separate Section 301 probe into excess industrial capacity across 16 economies (China, Taiwan, India, Japan, South Korea, Mexico, the EU and others) remains open. The move firms up rates rather than rupturing the Trump-Xi truce, which otherwise runs toward November 2026; Taiwan is unchanged at ~10% plus Section 232 metals duties. No new Taiwan military escalation was reported.",
