   "summary": "On Day 147 the US ran an 11TH consecutive night of strikes on Iran — a roughly 75-minute wave that CENTCOM said targeted 'Iranian military operations centres, maritime capabilities, aircraft hangars, drone storage facilities, and military logistics infrastructure' — while Washington visibly cooled on the diplomatic track floated a day earlier. Secretary of State Marco Rubio said Tehran is 'not serious' about talks; President Trump dismissed calls for a ceasefire, insisting Iran must first halt attacks on US forces and shipping, and posted that 'Any time the Islamic Republic of Iran shoots at a ship in the Strait of Hormuz, whether it be by Missile, Rocket, Drone, or any other device or weapon, the United States will bomb and destroy ONE BRIDGE OR POWER PLANT.' The 10-day ceasefire proposal from Qatar and Pakistan (which Iran confirmed receiving) was left hanging as the White House signaled it could keep striking. Markets repriced the deepening war and the SECOND-chokepoint threat: Brent crude rose almost 5% to more than $95/bbl for the first time in nearly six weeks, extending gains as the Iran-aligned Houthis' newly declared Bab el-Mandeb blockade turned back two tankers carrying Saudi crude to Asia — CNN framing oil as facing 'no way out' with both the Hormuz and Red Sea export routes now under threat. Hormuz stayed functionally shut: shipping-analytics firm Kpler said only 3 commodity vessels crossed the Strait on Tuesday, down from 4 on Monday. On casualties, the Pentagon identified a 4TH US service member killed in recent days; Middle East Eye reported the death of a third/fourth soldier raised the cumulative US toll since the war began on February 28 to 18 (outlets still differ on the running count). On cost and scale, Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth told lawmakers the Iran war has cost about $37.5 billion, and Trump is seeking roughly $70 billion more for the military. Iran sustained its Gulf and maritime retaliation and its leadership kept warning of a wider war. Across every new development the instruments stayed AIR and MARITIME: no US troops entered Iran, no Kharg landing or seizure, no new ARG/MEU order, and no draft; ~50,000 US personnel unchanged, with Trump reiterating that 'we have other people that will do the ground campaign for us.' The corpus-predicted US GROUND seizure of Hormuz/Kharg remains disconfirmed.",
   "details": [
