   "source": "U.S. News/Reuters, 'Venezuela's Maduro Due Back in US Court in Drug Trafficking Case,' July 22, 2026; PBS NewsHour, 'Venezuela's Nicolas Maduro back to court for pretrial hearing in his U.S. drug trafficking case,' July 22, 2026; NBC News, 'Venezuela's Nicolas Maduro trial date set for next June,' July 22, 2026; The Washington Times, July 22, 2026.",
   "summary": "Ousted Venezuelan President Nicolas Maduro and his wife Cilia Flores appeared in Manhattan federal court on July 22 for a pretrial hearing before US District Judge Alvin K. Hellerstein, who set their drug-trafficking trial for June 1, 2027 at the request of lawyers on both sides. Under the approved schedule, defense attorney Barry Pollack will begin filing motions in early September challenging the indictment — including an argument that Maduro is immune from prosecution as the former leader of a sovereign nation — with the couple returning to court November 17 for oral arguments. Both remain held in Brooklyn and deny the charges, which accuse them of conspiring with drug cartels to move thousands of tons of cocaine into the US. Venezuela has been governed since Maduro's January capture by his former Vice President Delcy Rodriguez; the constitutional-succession question stays unresolved. The hearing resolves the long-tracked 'July 22 Maduro hearing' watch item by producing a firm trial date without altering the underlying partial-confirmation of the corpus's Latin-America regime-change theme.",
