   ],
   "source": "CBS News live blog, 'American service member killed in Iraq; U.S. strikes Iran for 9th straight night,' July 20, 2026; NPR, 'U.S. and Iran launch new strikes after 3 U.S. service members are killed,' July 20, 2026; CNN live updates, 'US striking Iran again after Trump warned of strong retaliation for the deaths of US service members,' July 20, 2026; The Intercept, 'U.S. Official: Iran Attacks Injured \"Far More\" Troops Than Pentagon Admits,' July 20, 2026; Newsweek, 'U.S. launches fresh Iran strikes as Trump floats a \"ground campaign\",' July 2026; TradingEconomics/CNBC (Brent), July 20, 2026; Al Jazeera Iran-war live blog, July 20, 2026; CENTCOM public releases.",
   "summary": "On Day 145 the US-Iran war extended its consecutive-night air campaign — CBS reported the US completed a NINTH straight night (ending ~10pm ET July 19) and multiple outlets described a TENTH consecutive night into July 20-21 — and, for the second time in three days, struck a nuclear-adjacent site: US projectiles hit multiple locations in the southern port city of BUSHEHR, home to Iran's only civilian nuclear power plant, per Bushehr governor Mohammad Mozaffari ('several locations in Bushehr city were hit by American enemy's projectiles'). CENTCOM characterized the wave as targeting Iranian military command centers, air-defense, coastal-surveillance and missile/drone-launch sites 'to further degrade Iran's ability to threaten commercial shipping.' Iran's IRGC said it struck and immobilized a SECOND oil tanker at the Strait of Hormuz and claimed to have intercepted four vessels transiting the Strait over the weekend. Iran sustained its Gulf retaliation: a Kuwaiti power-and-water plant caught fire for a third consecutive day (cumulative Kuwait toll from Iranian strikes reported at 4 soldiers and 7 civilians killed, 82 soldiers and 105 civilians wounded), and Bahrain — home to the US Navy's Fifth Fleet — took fresh strikes (air-raid sirens and explosions in Manama; 3 killed, 51 wounded). US combat losses rose: after the July 17 Jordan deaths and a July 19 Iraq death, reporting put cumulative US KIA in the theatre at ~17, and The Intercept quoted a US official saying Iranian attacks had injured 'far more' troops than the Pentagon admits — 'dozens' treated after recent strikes, with cumulative US casualties (killed and wounded) 'at least 446,' a ~15% rise since the April 8 ceasefire; The Intercept named Pvt. Isabella Gonzales (19) and Lt. Tyler James Feehan (25) among the dead and said a third missing soldier's remains had been identified. Oil pushed higher — Brent crossed ~$90/bbl Monday July 20 (up roughly 30% from its early-July lows, its highest in over a month) — while Hormuz traffic collapsed to only ~8 transits Thursday, the lowest in three weeks. Trump said 'We're going to hit them very hard tonight, and we're going to hit them hard tomorrow' and, asked about seizing Kharg Island, declined to rule it out ('I can't say that to you because if I did, it would be foolish'). Despite the escalation — a tenth night, a second nuclear-adjacent strike, a second immobilized tanker, and coy Kharg talk — the US instrument stayed AIR and MARITIME: no US troops in Iran, no Kharg landing or port seizure, no new ARG/MEU order, no draft; ~50,000 personnel unchanged. The corpus-predicted US GROUND seizure of Hormuz/Kharg remains disconfirmed.",
   "details": [
    "NINTH AND TENTH STRAIGHT NIGHT; BUSHEHR STRUCK (July 19-21, 2026): CBS reported the US completed its NINTH consecutive night of strikes (ending ~10pm ET July 19), with CENTCOM citing Iranian military command centers, air-defense, coastal-surveillance and missile/drone-launch sites; a TENTH consecutive night was reported into July 20-21. US projectiles hit multiple locations in the southern port city of BUSHEHR — home to Iran's only civilian nuclear power plant — per Bushehr governor Mohammad Mozaffari. This is the second nuclear-adjacent target in three days after the July 18-19 strike on the under-construction Darkhovin plant. (CBS News; NPR; Al Jazeera, July 20, 2026.)",
    "IRAN IMMOBILIZES A SECOND TANKER; INTERCEPTS FOUR VESSELS (July 19-20, 2026): Iran's Revolutionary Guards said a second oil tanker had been struck and immobilized at the Strait of Hormuz, and Iran said over the weekend it had intercepted four vessels transiting the Strait. Hormuz transits fell to only ~8 ships on Thursday — the lowest in three weeks — with the chokepoint functionally shut (<5% of pre-war flows). (CBS News; NPR; Reuters/TradingEconomics, July 20, 2026.)",
