   "summary": "On Day 144 the US-Iran war crossed a threshold it had avoided since the fighting resumed after the June 17 MOU: its first confirmed US COMBAT DEATHS of the resumed phase. An Iranian ballistic-missile and drone strike on a US base in Jordan on July 17 killed two US service members, left one missing and wounded four (medically evacuated, later discharged); CENTCOM called them 'the first confirmed U.S. combat fatalities since the current conflict resumed.' Al Jazeera's July 19 live blog reported a further US service member killed in Iraq on July 19 and cited a cumulative ~16 US service members killed since late February (reporting is inconsistent — some outlets cite one vs two for the Jordan strike, one still missing — but the count clearly rose from the briefing's prior standing 15/13-combat). Explicitly framing the wave as 'punishment' for the Jordan deaths, US CENTCOM ran an eighth consecutive night of strikes (~5.5 hours from ~6pm ET) that widened geographically into KHUZESTAN (Shadegan) and — for the first time in the war — hit an under-construction NUCLEAR plant (Darkhovin), which the IAEA confirmed held no nuclear material and posed no radiological risk, alongside Sirik and Qeshm Island (coastal-surveillance, air-defense, maritime and missile/drone-storage targets). Iran struck Kuwait's power-and-desalination complex for a second straight day (fires, power disruption), fired missiles/drones at Jordan (Aqaba; three intercepted), Bahrain (nationwide sirens, high alert) and Qatar (a child wounded by intercept debris), and declared the US-Iran MOU 'suspended'; the UAE condemned the Gulf strikes as a 'blatant violation of sovereignty.' Iran's three-week toll reached 50+ killed and 500+ wounded, with the Bonji desalination plant destroyed (~10,000 affected). Oil held elevated (Brent ~$88 at the July 17 close, +~14% on the week) with Hormuz functionally shut, and IRGC adviser Rezaei's 'full-scale offensive' ultimatum window (~July 19-20) lapsed without an Iranian ground offensive. Crucially, the ground rung stayed unchanged even though the US-casualty tripwire — reported July 18 as a condition Trump had set for authorizing a Kharg strike — was now crossed: no US troops in Iran, no Kharg landing or seizure, no new ARG/MEU order, no draft; ~50,000 personnel unchanged. Newsweek reported Trump 'floats a ground campaign,' but the actual US move was an intensified air/maritime campaign, not a landing — the corpus-predicted US GROUND seizure of Hormuz/Kharg stays disconfirmed.",
