   "source": "NPR, 'U.S. strikes bridges in Iran; Tehran targets U.S. bases in the Gulf,' July 17, 2026; CNN, 'US launches another round of strikes after Iran widens its attacks on regional allies,' July 17, 2026; ABC News, 'US launches more airstrikes on Iran for 7th night, CENTCOM says,' July 17, 2026; France 24, 'Kuwait desalination plant hit as US and Iran escalate infrastructure attacks,' July 16-17, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Gulf states come under Iranian fire as US strikes intensify,' July 17, 2026; CNBC, 'Oil tankers face worst case scenario in Hormuz,' and 'Oil prices rise after Kuwait says Iran attacked water desalination and power plant,' July 17, 2026; NBC News, 'Oil hits $87 per barrel again,' July 17, 2026; Wikipedia, '2026 Iranian strikes on the Kurdistan Region' and '2026 Iran war,' July 17, 2026.",
   "summary": "On Day 142 the US-Iran contest over the Strait of Hormuz ran into a SEVENTH consecutive night of exchanged fire, and both sides deepened their strikes on each other's — and third parties' — infrastructure, while the ground rung stayed empty. US CENTCOM ran a seventh straight night of strikes; Iranian state media said US strikes destroyed SIX BRIDGES in HORMOZGAN province facing the Strait, plus an airport, railway stations and power infrastructure across southern Iran, reporting at least 7 killed and 20 wounded. Iran WIDENED its retaliation across the region: it struck a KUWAITI water-DESALINATION plant and a power plant (Kuwait's military said several ground-forces members were wounded and military and civilian facilities damaged by ballistic missiles and drones), fired missiles/drones at Qatar and Jordan, and — in a notable horizontal widening — hit US-BACKED KURDISH targets in IRAQ, killing at least 8 members of the Komala party at a camp near Sulaimaniyah (the Erbil-based coalition downed several drones; KRG President Nechirvan Barzani condemned it as a violation of Iraq's sovereignty). Oil SURGED: Brent jumped ~4.6% to close around $88/bbl and WTI ~4.5% to ~$82.49 — Brent up more than 10% over roughly a week and at its highest in over a month — as the US Treasury's 60-day waiver on Iranian-oil sanctions EXPIRED at 12:01am EDT July 17, snapping sanctions back. Maritime-risk analysts called Hormuz a 'worst case scenario': transits fell below 5% of the pre-war baseline (~2% throughput), leaving the chokepoint functionally blocked. An IRGC commander (Mohsen Rezaei, via IRIB) warned that 'if US attacks continue for another two or three days, we will enter a phase of full-scale offensive operations,' and Iran again cast US interference in Hormuz as its 'invincible red line.' Trump declined to rule out taking Kharg Island but said it would be foolish to say so publicly, noting prior strikes deliberately spared the island's oil terminals as 'a chunk of the world economy.' GROUND RUNG UNCHANGED — no US troops in Iran, no Kharg landing or port seizure, no new ARG/MEU order, no draft; ~50,000 personnel unchanged. The US is asserting control of the Strait via blockade, air power and maritime interdiction; the predicted US GROUND seizure of Hormuz/Kharg stays disconfirmed.",
   "details": [
    "SEVENTH STRAIGHT NIGHT; US HITS SOUTHERN INFRASTRUCTURE (July 16-17, 2026): The US launched another round of strikes for a seventh consecutive night, CENTCOM said. Iran reported the targets included six bridges in Hormozgan province facing the Strait of Hormuz, an airport, railway stations and power infrastructure; Iranian state media said at least 7 people were killed and 20 wounded. The campaign stayed on the air rung; no US ground forces entered Iran. (NPR; CNN; ABC News; France 24, July 17, 2026.)",
    "IRAN STRIKES KUWAIT DESALINATION/POWER PLANT AND WIDENS GULF SALVO (July 16-17, 2026): Iran hit a Kuwaiti water-desalination plant and a power plant; Kuwait's military said several members of its ground forces were wounded and military and civilian facilities damaged after a ballistic-missile-and-drone attack — exposing the region's water vulnerability. Iran also fired at Qatar and Jordan as its retaliation for the intensifying US strikes broadened. (CNBC; France 24; Al Jazeera; ABC News, July 17, 2026.)",
    "IRAN KILLS AT LEAST 8 US-BACKED KURDS IN IRAQ (July 17, 2026): Iran attacked with drones and rockets a camp of the Komala party and its vicinity near Sulaimaniyah early in the morning, killing at least 8 members and wounding others; the US-led anti-jihadist coalition shot down several drones over Erbil. KRG President Nechirvan Barzani condemned the strikes as 'a serious escalation and a blatant violation of Iraq's sovereignty.' A horizontal widening of the war onto Iraqi soil, not a US or Iranian ground campaign in Iran. (France 24; Al Jazeera; Wikipedia, '2026 Iranian strikes on the Kurdistan Region,' July 17, 2026.)",
    "OIL SURGES TO ~$88 BRENT AS SANCTIONS WAIVER EXPIRES (July 17, 2026): Brent crude advanced about 4.6% to close near $88.10/bbl and WTI gained ~4.5% to settle ~$82.49 — Brent up more than 10% over roughly a week, to levels not seen in over a month — after the US Treasury's 60-day waiver on Iranian-oil sanctions lapsed at 12:01am EDT July 17, snapping sanctions back. Still elevated but far below the ~$126 April wartime peak and the $130+ a sustained US GROUND commitment would imply. (Al Jazeera; NBC News; CNBC, July 17, 2026.)",
    "HORMUZ FUNCTIONALLY BLOCKED — 'WORST CASE SCENARIO' (July 17, 2026): Maritime-risk analysts said tanker traffic faced a 'worst case scenario' in Hormuz, with transits below 5% of the pre-war baseline (~2% throughput) — the chokepoint functionally blocked under the re-imposed US blockade and Iranian attacks on shipping. (CNBC, 'Oil tankers face worst case scenario in Hormuz,' July 17, 2026.)",
    "IRGC WARNS OF 'FULL-SCALE OFFENSIVE'; KHARG STAYS AIR-ONLY (July 17, 2026): An IRGC commander (Mohsen Rezaei, via IRIB) warned that 'if US attacks continue for another two or three days, we will enter a phase of full-scale offensive operations,' and Iran reiterated that US interference in the Strait is its 'invincible red line.' Trump declined to rule out taking Kharg Island but said it would be foolish to announce it, noting prior strikes spared the island's oil terminals; reporting said he had authorized strikes on Kharg contingent on verified US casualties or an Iranian strike on critical infrastructure — but no landing was ordered. (CNBC; ABC News; Britannica, July 17, 2026.)",
    "GROUND RUNG UNCHANGED (July 17-18, 2026): The seventh night of strikes, the widened Gulf/Iraq retaliation, and the oil surge added NO US ground forces: no landing, port seizure, Kharg assault, new ARG/MEU order, or draft; ~50,000 US personnel in the CENTCOM AOR unchanged. The July 16 US Marine BOARDING of the commercial vessel M/T Wen Yao and the earlier disabling of the Kharg-bound tanker M/T Belma were maritime interdiction actions, not a landing on Iranian soil. The predicted US ground seizure of Hormuz/Kharg stays disconfirmed. (CBS News; NPR; The Hill; Task & Purpose, July 2026.)"
