  {
   "date": "2026-07-16",
   "headline": "DAY 140 — WITH THE NAVAL BLOCKADE BACK ON, THE US RUNS A FIFTH STRAIGHT NIGHT OF STRIKES AND, IN A COMBAT FIRST, HITS THE BANDAR ABBAS NAVAL BASE WITH ONE-WAY ATTACK SEA DRONES; IRAN'S IRGC THREATENS TO HALT ALL MIDDLE EAST ENERGY EXPORTS ('EITHER FOR EVERYONE OR FOR NO ONE') AND STRIKES JORDAN, KUWAIT AND BAHRAIN, AS BRENT HOLDS A ONE-MONTH HIGH NEAR $85. STILL AIR, NAVAL AND UNMANNED-MARITIME COERCION — NOT A GROUND SEIZURE.",
   "summary": "On Day 140 the re-imposed US blockade hardened into a sustained air-and-naval campaign, but the ground rung stayed empty. US CENTCOM ran a FIFTH consecutive night/round of strikes (a fresh wave opened ~1:30pm Tehran time July 15), and — in a US combat FIRST — employed one-way attack SEA DRONES (three 'Corsair' unmanned surface vessels) against the Bandar Abbas Naval Base, a maritime/unmanned weapon debut CENTCOM said 'degraded Iran's ability to continue attacking commercial shipping,' NOT a landing. Within ~17 hours of restarting the blockade the US said it had 'redirected' two commercial vessels attempting to breach it — maritime interdiction, not a port seizure. US strikes killed at least 7 Iranian troops at an army barracks in Sistan and Baluchestan province, with 260+ wounded across the country (Iran reporting 30+ deaths in recent days). CENTCOM commander Adm. Brad Cooper said Iran had 'deliberately' attacked seven commercial vessels over the previous week, leaving 'nearly a dozen' crew killed, wounded or missing. Iran's IRGC answered by threatening to halt ALL regional energy exports — 'The export of oil and gas from the region will be either for everyone or for no one' — and warned it could block more vital seaways; it launched dozens of missiles and drones at US-hosting Gulf Arab states, striking (or attempting to strike) Jordan (which shot down three incoming missiles), Kuwait and Bahrain (missile alerts sounded). Oil held its one-month high but did NOT spike further: Brent's September contract settled ~$84.95/bbl (+22 cents) and WTI August ~$79.60 (+26 cents), 'little changed'/'steadying' after the prior day's surge — still >15% above the late-February pre-war level, with analysts warning of a retest of $100 if the intensity persists for weeks. Only ~21 ships transited Hormuz July 15; the running tally reached ~56 confirmed shipping incidents and 17 seafarer deaths. Talks stayed stalled, the interim deal derailed. GROUND RUNG UNCHANGED: no US troops in Iran, no Kharg landing or port seizure, no new ARG/MEU order, no draft; ~50,000 personnel unchanged. The predicted US GROUND seizure of Hormuz/Kharg stays disconfirmed — the US is now asserting control of the Strait through blockade, air power and, newly, unmanned maritime strike, but still not with a ground landing.",
   "details": [
    "FIFTH NIGHT/ROUND OF US STRIKES (July 15, 2026): US CENTCOM launched a new wave of strikes on Iranian military positions beginning ~1:30pm Tehran time Wednesday July 15 and continued overnight-plus-daytime attacks, hitting missile and drone facilities, naval assets and coastal-defense systems — the fifth consecutive round since the blockade re-imposition. CENTCOM framed the campaign as degrading 'Iran's ability to continue attacking commercial shipping.' It stayed on the air/naval rung; no ground forces entered Iran. (IranWire; NPR, 'The U.S. fires a new wave of strikes on Iran and hits a tanker trying to skirt its blockade,' July 15, 2026; CBS News, July 15, 2026.)",
    "US COMBAT FIRST — ONE-WAY ATTACK SEA DRONES HIT BANDAR ABBAS (reported July 15, 2026; CENTCOM statement July 12): For the first time in combat, US forces used one-way attack unmanned surface vessels — three 'Corsair' sea drones — against the Bandar Abbas Naval Base, CENTCOM video showing the drones speeding toward and detonating on a raised dock holding a small submarine. CENTCOM said the strikes 'degraded Iran's ability to continue attacking commercial shipping.' This is a new UNMANNED-MARITIME rung of the campaign, not a ground operation. (Task & Purpose; CBS News, 'U.S. uses sea drones in combat for first time to attack Iranian naval base'; Breaking Defense; Army Recognition, July 12-15, 2026.)",
    "US 'REDIRECTS' TWO VESSELS UNDER THE RESTARTED BLOCKADE (July 15, 2026): Within roughly 17 hours of restarting the naval blockade of Iranian ports, US forces said they had 'redirected' two commercial vessels attempting to breach it, and CENTCOM reported hitting a tanker trying to skirt the blockade. This is maritime INTERDICTION — the instrument the US has used throughout — not a landing or port seizure. (CNN, 'US military says it has redirected 2 commercial vessels since restarting blockade,' July 15, 2026; NPR, July 15, 2026.)",
    "CASUALTIES (July 15, 2026): US strikes killed at least seven Iranian troops at an army barracks in Sistan and Baluchestan province, with more than 260 people wounded across the country; Iranian officials reported more than 30 deaths in recent days overall. CENTCOM commander Adm. Brad Cooper said Iran had 'deliberately' targeted civilians and attacked seven commercial vessels over the previous week, leaving 'nearly a dozen' crew members killed, wounded or missing. (Fortune; Washington Times; Al Jazeera, July 15, 2026.)",
    "IRAN THREATENS TO HALT ALL REGIONAL ENERGY EXPORTS (July 15, 2026): Iran's Revolutionary Guard declared, 'The export of oil and gas from the region will be either for everyone or for no one,' threatening to stop all Middle East energy exports over the US blockade and warning it could block additional vital seaways. Analysts read it as Iran again weaponizing the chokepoint to coerce a lifting of the blockade. (Fortune, \"'Either for everyone or for no one': Iran turns oil into a weapon again\"; Washington Times; MSNBC/ms.now; Gulf Today, July 15, 2026.)",
    "IRAN STRIKES JORDAN, KUWAIT AND BAHRAIN (July 15, 2026): Iran launched dozens of missiles and drones at neighboring Gulf Arab states hosting US forces; Jordan said it shot down three incoming Iranian missiles, and missile-alert warnings sounded in Bahrain and Kuwait. Iran said it had targeted US sites in Jordan, Kuwait and Bahrain. These were Iranian air/missile strikes on US partners, not a US or Iranian ground move. (AP, 'Tehran attacks Jordan as US reimposes its blockade on Iran'; Al Jazeera, 'Iran launches attacks across region as US strikes continue,' July 15, 2026.)",
    "OIL HOLDS A ONE-MONTH HIGH BUT DOES NOT SPIKE FURTHER (July 15, 2026): Brent's September contract settled about $84.95/bbl (+22 cents, ~+0.3%) and WTI's August contract about $79.60 (+26 cents) — 'little changed'/'steadying near a one-month high' after the prior day's ~10% weekly surge. Brent stayed >15% above the late-February pre-war level; one analyst said the escalation 'shows how expectations of a rapid opening of the Strait were premature' and warned oil could retest $100 if the intensity persists for weeks. (CNBC, 'Oil little changed after U.S. reimposes naval blockade on Iran, launches more airstrikes'; Bloomberg, July 15, 2026.)",
    "HORMUZ TRAFFIC AND SHIPPING TOLL (July 15, 2026): Maritime tracking showed only about 21 ships transiting the Strait of Hormuz on July 15; the running tally of the tanker-war reached roughly 56 confirmed shipping incidents and 17 seafarer fatalities. Talks remained stalled and the interim US-Iran deal derailed, with regional mediators still trying to return the parties to the table. (2026 Strait of Hormuz crisis tracking; Fortune; NPR, July 15, 2026.)",
    "GROUND RUNG UNCHANGED (July 15, 2026): No US troops entered Iran; no Kharg landing or port seizure; no new ARG/MEU order; no draft or mobilization. ~50,000 US personnel in the CENTCOM AOR unchanged. The renewed coercion of the chokepoint ran through the blockade (interdiction), air strikes and — newly — unmanned sea drones, all stand-off or maritime instruments, not a troop landing. (CBS News; NPR; Stars and Stripes, July 15, 2026.)"
