   "summary": "Day 140 secondary theatres. RUSSIA-UKRAINE: Kostiantynivka stayed contested — Russian and Ukrainian positions remain interspersed inside the 'fortress belt' city, the frontline hard to fix; Putin rejected calls to negotiate and was reported likely to escalate, his resolve stiffened by Ukraine's drone strikes on Russian oil refineries and ports (Syzran July 12, Omsk July 6). Zelensky again sought face-to-face talks but rejects Moscow as a venue; polling shows majorities on both sides favor negotiations (Russia ~60%, Ukraine ~66%). No US ground surge; the US had withdrawn another Brigade Combat Team from Europe. LATIN AMERICA: Cuba's grid crisis persisted after its mid-July island-wide collapses under the US oil blockade and the Venezuelan-crude cutoff; FM Bruno Rodríguez kept blaming the US embargo as 'collective punishment.' Venezuela's constitutional crisis stayed unresolved under interim president Delcy Rodríguez; the Maduro/Flores SDNY hearing stays JULY 22; Colombia's de la Espriella is inaugurated AUG 7. US-CHINA: the tariff truce held; the base Section 122 10% tariff is set to sunset ~July 24 (under legal challenge — CIT ruled it unlawful May 7, Federal Circuit stayed that June 11, so CBP keeps collecting), with Section 301 measures (effective ~35% on China) the intended replacement; no new military escalation. DPRK: no new missile test after Kim's July 3-4 Kang Kon destroyer weapons tests; analysts watch the July 27 Korean War armistice anniversary. HORMUZ-EXPOSED ASIA: Japan (~1.6M bpd via Hormuz, >90% of crude from the Mideast), South Korea (~70% of crude via Hormuz) and India (~2.6M bpd, ~52% dependency) braced for supply/price stress as Brent held >$85 and the blockade squeezed transit. No US ground move in any theatre.",
