  {
   "date": "2026-07-15",
   "headline": "DAY 139 — THE US RE-IMPOSES ITS NAVAL BLOCKADE OF IRANIAN PORTS AND TRUMP ANNOUNCES (THEN REVERSES) A 20% HORMUZ 'TOLL,' CASTING THE US AS THE STRAIT'S 'GUARDIAN,' AS BRENT SURGES ABOVE $85 (HIGHEST SINCE JUNE 15) AND CENTCOM RUNS A FOURTH STRAIGHT NIGHT OF STRIKES; IRAN HITS THREE MORE TANKERS. THE SHARPEST US COERCION OF THE CHOKEPOINT YET — BUT VIA BLOCKADE AND AIR POWER, NOT A GROUND SEIZURE.",
   "summary": "On Day 139 the war re-escalated toward US coercive control of the Strait of Hormuz — the closest the conflict has come to the corpus-predicted 'US takes the Strait' scenario — but the instruments stayed maritime and air, not ground. On July 14 (4pm ET) the US RESTARTED its naval blockade of Iranian ports, ending the ~27-day 'lifted' period that had run since the June 17 MOU; CENTCOM cited 'more than 20 US Navy warships and hundreds of military aircraft operating' in the region. Trump announced a 20% 'toll' on cargo transiting Hormuz — framing the US as the Strait's 'guardian' and demanding reimbursement from Gulf beneficiaries (Saudi Arabia, UAE, Qatar, Bahrain, Kuwait) — then reversed the fee the same day ('I don't think anybody should be able to charge a fee'), substituting Gulf-state investment deals. CENTCOM ran a fourth consecutive night of strikes (Bushehr, Chah Bahar, Jask, Konarak, Abu Musa, Bandar Abbas). Iran answered with three more tanker attacks early July 14 — the chemical tanker Stolt Magnesium (projectile NE of Qalhat) and the UAE-owned oil tankers Mombasa B and Al Bahyah (cruise missiles), the IRGC claiming it hit 'offending supertankers' on a 'mined route' (one Indian crewman killed aboard Mombasa B, ~8 wounded incl. six Indians and two Ukrainians, three missing aboard Al Bahyah) — plus a missile/drone salvo at US assets in Kuwait and Bahrain (Kuwait reported intercepting 33 drones, five cruise missiles, one ballistic missile). The GFS Galaxy's missing crewman (from Pune, India) was confirmed dead, his body recovered by the Omani Navy. Brent's September contract surged to ~$85.92/bbl (08:00 GMT July 14) and traded above $86 — its highest since June 15, up ~19% from pre-conflict levels — as Hormuz crossings dropped more than half (only ~57 transits Fri-Sun). Indirect talks via Qatar, Pakistan and Oman continued. GROUND RUNG UNCHANGED: no US troops in Iran, no Kharg landing or port seizure, no new ARG/MEU order, no draft; ~50,000 personnel unchanged; Kharg plans stay 'continuously shelved' as too risky. The predicted US GROUND seizure of Hormuz/Kharg stays disconfirmed — the US is asserting control of the Strait from the SEA and AIR, not with a landing.",
