   "summary": "Away from Hormuz, the tracked theatres moved only incrementally on Day 139. Cuba's national grid collapsed again on July 14 (~11am local) — the third nationwide blackout in under 10 days and the fifth total outage of 2026 — leaving ~10 million without power under the US oil blockade and the Venezuelan-crude cutoff; FM Bruno Rodríguez told the UN the embargo is a 'systematic violation of human rights'/'collective punishment.' Venezuela's constitutional crisis stayed unresolved under acting President Delcy Rodríguez (Maduro/Flores SDNY hearing July 22; Colombia's de la Espriella inaugurated Aug 7). In Ukraine, control of Kostiantynivka stayed contested — Ukraine's General Staff said the city 'remained under the control of the Ukrainian Armed Forces' despite Russian infantry infiltration, Zelensky again called Putin's 'liberation' claim a lie, and the Kremlin insisted the city was 'already part of Russia'; Russia proposed and Ukraine rejected a six-hour local ceasefire for a body handover. The US-China tariff truce held (Section 122 10% tariff still set to expire ~July 24), with Trump reported weighing a Taiwan arms package and Taiwan's new Littoral Combat Command (commissioned July 1) integrating US missile systems. North Korea conducted no new July launch after the July 3-4 Kang Kon tests, with analysts watching the July 27 armistice anniversary. No US ground-force move accompanied any of these developments — the pressure on Cuba is economic, not manpower.",
   "details": [
    "CUBA — THIRD JULY BLACKOUT (July 14, 2026): Cuba's national grid collapsed for the third time in under 10 days (fifth total outage of 2026), ~11am local, cutting power to ~10 million; FM Bruno Rodríguez told the UN the US fuel embargo and sanctions amount to a 'systematic violation of the human rights of an entire people'/'collective punishment.' Driven by the loss of Venezuelan oil after Maduro's January removal, the US oil blockade, and aging generation — a second-order effect of US economic pressure applied without ground manpower. (Al Jazeera; WHBL; Cubaheadlines, July 14, 2026.)",
