   "summary": "On Day 138 Iran's July 12 IRGC 'closed until further notice' declaration ran into a third day while both sides signalled a diplomatic off-ramp. US officials briefed reporters that Iranian officials had privately told Trump advisers the tanker strikes were the work of an 'errant' hardliner faction trying to undermine negotiations, and that the Trump team expected FM Abbas Araghchi to publicly or implicitly acknowledge after his Oman meetings that hitting the ships 'was a mistake'; a further US-Iran round (Vance, Kushner, Witkoff, Rubio) was reported for Oman on Saturday, with Washington insisting talks 'can't progress until ships are assured safe passage.' Analysts cautioned the private-messaging claim was 'implausible but convenient' for the US, since blaming a rogue faction leaves the door to diplomacy ajar. Markets read the standoff as a supply risk, not a war-restart: Brent's September contract jumped more than 4% to about $78.82/bbl (its highest since June 22) as the US and Iran kept trading strikes over control of the waterway, while vessel-tracking held transit near a third of the pre-war baseline. Firming detail on the July 11 IRGC strike put the container ship M/V GFS Galaxy's crew at 11 Indian nationals (10 rescued, one missing), and Iranian outlets reported an Iranian navy lieutenant, Hamidreza Dehghani, killed by a US strike at the southern port of Jask. The imminent-US-ground-seizure-of-Hormuz/Kharg thesis stays disconfirmed-and-inverted: the coercion at the chokepoint is Iran's (a third-day closure and tanker strikes), while the US applies air/stand-off strikes and diplomacy, its naval blockade lifted (Day 26).",
