   "headline": "DAY 137 — THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ CLOSURE ENTERS A SECOND DAY AS WASHINGTON AND TEHRAN PUBLICLY DISPUTE WHETHER IT IS OPEN; TRANSIT RUNS AT ~A THIRD OF PRE-WAR WHILE THE IRGC TANKER STRIKE ON THE M/V GFS GALAXY (ONE CREWMAN MISSING) AND IRAN'S JULY 12 MULTI-STATE RETALIATION SHARPEN THE MARITIME/AIR PICTURE — THE COERCION AT THE CHOKEPOINT REMAINS IRANIAN AND THE US ANSWER STAYS AIR/DIPLOMACY, NOT A GROUND MOVE.",
   "summary": "On Day 137 Iran's July 12 IRGC Navy declaration that the Strait of Hormuz is 'closed until further notice and until regional interference by the US ceases' ran into a second day, hardening into a public open-vs-closed dispute: US CENTCOM insisted the Strait 'is open' and 'traffic is flowing,' while Iran's Persian Gulf Strait Authority said passage 'is currently not possible.' Vessel-tracking put transit at only ~33-35% of the pre-war baseline. Clarifying detail firmed up the July 11 IRGC strike on the Cyprus-flagged container ship M/V GFS Galaxy — disabled by an engine-room hit and fire, with 23 crew rescued (largely by Oman) and one Indian crewman missing/presumed dead — and Iran's July 12 retaliatory salvo of drones and ballistic/cruise missiles at up to five US-partner states (Bahrain, Kuwait, Jordan, Qatar and, newly, Oman), most intercepted; Qatar reported three wounded (including a child) from interceptor debris and condemned 'a dangerous escalation.' The US had run a third strike wave in a week (~140 targets July 11 per live reporting; ~300+ across three nights), and Trump kept the ceasefire rhetorically 'over' while saying talks would 'continue.' Qatar and Pakistan pressed mediation, with another US-Iran round reported possible the following week (Switzerland floated). Brent held near $76/bbl (a ~5% weekly gain), far below the $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. The imminent-US-ground-seizure-of-Hormuz/Kharg thesis stays disconfirmed-and-inverted: at the chokepoint it is again Iran using force (a unilateral closure and tanker strikes), while the US applies air/stand-off strikes and diplomacy.",
   "details": [
    "HORMUZ CLOSURE INTO A SECOND DAY / OPEN-VS-CLOSED DISPUTE (July 12-13, 2026): Iran's IRGC Navy July 12 declaration that the Strait is 'closed until further notice and until regional interference by the US ceases' persisted. US CENTCOM publicly countered that 'the Strait of Hormuz is open... traffic is flowing,' while Iran's Persian Gulf Strait Authority said passage 'is currently not possible' — a direct public contradiction. Independent vessel-tracking showed transit running at only ~33-35% of the pre-war 7-day baseline. (Al Jazeera; Time; CNBC; iransitrep.com, July 12, 2026.)",
    "M/V GFS GALAXY TANKER STRIKE (July 11, 2026): The IRGC disabled the Cyprus-flagged container ship M/V GFS Galaxy with an engine-room strike and fire; 23 crew were rescued (largely by Oman) and one Indian national was reported missing/presumed dead — a sharper incident than the 'warning shots at a vessel' initially reported for the July 12 closure declaration. (Iran Focus; iransitrep.com 'Day 135'; Al Jazeera, July 12, 2026.)",
    "IRAN'S JULY 12 MULTI-STATE RETALIATION (July 12, 2026): Iran launched one-way drones and ballistic/cruise missiles at up to five US-partner states — Bahrain, Kuwait, Jordan, Qatar and, for the first time in this cycle, Oman — framed as retaliation for US strikes on Iran's southern coast; most were intercepted. Qatar reported three wounded, including a child, from falling interceptor debris and condemned 'a dangerous escalation'; Bahrain activated air-raid sirens; the UAE said it engaged incoming missiles/UAVs; Saudi Arabia and Oman condemned the attacks. (Al Jazeera; Khaleej Times, July 12, 2026.)",
