   "headline": "DAY 136 — IRAN'S IRGC DECLARES THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ 'CLOSED UNTIL FURTHER NOTICE' AFTER FIRING WARNING SHOTS AT A VESSEL ON AN 'UNAPPROVED ROUTE,' HOURS AFTER OMAN FLOATS A TWO-CORRIDOR TRANSIT PLAN; WASHINGTON DEMANDS TEHRAN PUBLICLY DECLARE THE STRAIT OPEN AND STOP FIRING — THE RE-CLOSURE IS AGAIN AN IRANIAN, NOT US, ASSERTION OF FORCE AT THE CHOKEPOINT, AND THE US ANSWER STAYS DIPLOMATIC/AIR, NOT A GROUND MOVE. Early Sunday July 12 (local time) Iran's IRGC Navy announced the Strait of Hormuz was 'closed until further notice and until regional interference by the US ceases,' citing 'outside interference from foreign powers' and vessels using 'unauthorized routes' — after the Guards fired warning shots at one such ship. The declaration came hours after Oman, in Muscat talks with Iranian FM Araghchi, proposed a two-corridor scheme (a Southern Corridor through Omani waters with free pre-war navigation, and a Northern Corridor through Iranian waters requiring prior Iranian approval but no tolls; both to stay open under the not-yet-finalized draft). Washington rejected any Iranian-controlled regime, demanding Tehran issue a public statement that all channels are open and that it will stop firing at ships, and insisting Iran 'does not control' the Strait. No major new US-Iran kinetic exchange was confirmed for July 11-12 after the July 9 ~90-target US wave; the US stayed in its 'strike-then-pause for diplomacy' posture while Pakistan's PM Sharif worked the phones (Pezeshkian, Qatar's emir) and Qatari negotiators traveled to Iran. Brent held above $76/bbl (a ~5% weekly gain), far below the $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. The imminent US-ground-seizure-of-Hormuz/Kharg thesis stays disconfirmed-and-inverted: at the chokepoint it is again IRAN using force (a unilateral closure and warning shots), while the US applies air/stand-off strikes and diplomacy — the opposite of a forming US ground invasion.",
   "summary": "On Day 136 the Hormuz contest sharpened on the Iranian side: early July 12 (local) the IRGC Navy declared the Strait 'closed until further notice and until regional interference by the US ceases,' after firing warning shots at a vessel using an 'unapproved route.' The move followed Oman's July 11 two-corridor proposal (Southern Corridor in Omani waters, free pre-war navigation; Northern Corridor in Iranian waters, prior Iranian approval, no tolls) discussed by FM Araghchi and Omani FM Badr Albusaidi in Muscat — a plan Tehran had resisted. Washington demanded Iran publicly declare the Strait open and stop firing, insisting Iran 'does not control' the waterway. No major new US-Iran kinetic exchange was confirmed for July 11-12; the US held its 'strike-then-pause for diplomacy' posture while Pakistan and Qatar pressed mediation. Brent held above $76/bbl (~5% weekly gain). The US naval blockade stayed lifted (Day 24) and ~50,000 US personnel were unchanged, with no landing, ARG/MEU order, or draft. The predicted US ground seizure of Hormuz/Kharg remains disconfirmed and inverted — the coercion at the chokepoint is Iranian (a closure and warning shots), and the US answer is air and diplomacy, not a ground assault. The live question stays Hormuz governance (the two-corridor plan vs Iran's closure) and deal durability, not a forming US ground invasion.",
