   "headline": "GROUND INVASION TRACKER — DAY 136: STATUS HOLDS no_ground_troops. Iran's IRGC unilaterally re-closes the Strait of Hormuz (warning shots on an 'unapproved route') — a coercive move at the chokepoint by IRAN, not a US ground action; the US answer stays 'strike-then-pause for diplomacy,' the naval blockade stays lifted (Day 24), and no landing, ARG/MEU order, or draft follows. ~50,000 US personnel unchanged; USS Boxer/11th MEU and USS Tripoli/31st MEU in routine/contingency posture.",
   "summary": "Every US ground-execution indicator stayed at zero through Day 136 while the chokepoint escalation ran the other direction. Early July 12 (local) the IRGC Navy declared Hormuz 'closed until further notice and until regional interference by the US ceases' after firing warning shots at a vessel on an 'unapproved route' — an Iranian, not US, assertion of force. The US did not answer with troops: no major new kinetic exchange was confirmed for July 11-12, Washington stayed in its 'strike-then-pause for diplomacy' posture, its naval blockade remained lifted (Day 24), and no landing, port seizure, new ARG/MEU order, or draft followed. ~50,000 US personnel were unchanged; the USS Boxer ARG / 11th MEU (2,200 Camp Pendleton Marines, USS Portland) and USS Tripoli ARG / 31st MEU (~3,500 troops) remained in the Arabian Sea in routine/contingency posture. On Kharg, reporting reiterated that a takeover 'would require a significant number of ground troops' and that seizure plans 'drawn up for months' have been 'continuously shelved because the operation was considered too risky.' The predicted US Hormuz/Kharg ground seizure stays disconfirmed-and-inverted — the force at the chokepoint is Iran's, and the US answer is air and diplomacy.",
