  {
   "date": "2026-07-12",
   "headline": "MULTI-THEATRE ROUNDUP — JULY 11-12: RUSSIA-UKRAINE talks stay stalled (Putin maximalist; US NATO envoy says Kyiv 'seized the initiative,' time for Moscow to negotiate; Kherson-direction strikes). VENEZUELA constitutional crisis deepens as acting President Rodríguez's 180-day interim mandate lapses without the National Assembly declaring Maduro's 'absolute absence'; Maduro/Flores hearing stays July 22; Cuba grid crisis persists. US-CHINA: China Coast Guard patrols east of Taiwan, China-Russia 'Joint Sea-2026' drills (July 6-13) and a July 6 PLA SLBM test, while the sides 'agree in principle' to add farm goods to a tariff-reduction framework. DPRK: no new July launch; activity expected around the July 27 armistice anniversary. HORMUZ-DEPENDENT ASIA (Japan, South Korea, India, Pakistan) faces renewed pressure from Iran's July 12 re-closure.",
   "summary": "A cross-theatre snapshot for July 11-12. RUSSIA-UKRAINE: peace talks stay frozen — Putin reiterates maximalist aims (recognition of occupied land, no NATO, force limits, sanctions relief) while the US Permanent Representative to NATO says Kyiv has 'seized the initiative' and it is 'time for Moscow to negotiate'; Kherson-direction airstrikes continued July 11 and ~66% of Ukrainians favor negotiating an end. No US ground-deployment surge. LATIN AMERICA: Venezuela's constitutional crisis deepened as acting President Delcy Rodríguez's 180-day interim mandate lapsed without the National Assembly declaring Maduro's 'absolute absence,' with the NGO Acceso a la Justicia warning of 'blatant and unprecedented unconstitutionality' and Rodríguez facing ~63% disapproval; Maduro and Flores's SDNY hearing stays set for July 22; Cuba's grid/fuel crisis persists under the US oil blockade; Colombia's de la Espriella takes office Aug 7. US-CHINA: China began Coast Guard patrols east of Taiwan (July 4), ran 'Joint Sea-2026' drills with Russia off Qingdao (July 6-13) and tested an SLBM in the South Pacific (July 6), while Beijing said the sides 'agreed in principle' to include agricultural products in a reciprocal tariff-reduction framework; the base Section 122 10% tariff is set to expire ~July 24. DPRK: no new July missile launch after the July 3 Kang Kon destroyer weapons tests; analysts still expect activity around the July 27 armistice anniversary. HORMUZ-DEPENDENT ASIA: Iran's July 12 re-closure renews acute exposure for Japan (~75% of oil via Hormuz), South Korea (~68%, ~200-day reserves), India (~half its crude, ~60% of gas) and Pakistan (99% of LNG from Qatar/UAE — the most vulnerable).",
   "details": [
    "RUSSIA-UKRAINE (July 11, 2026): Talks remain stalled; the US Permanent Representative to NATO said 'Kyiv has seized the initiative, it's time for Moscow to negotiate,' while Putin's demands (recognition of occupied territory, Ukrainian neutrality, force curbs, sanctions relief) are unchanged and Russia 'touts war with NATO' amid battlefield losses. Front-line summaries noted Kherson-direction airstrikes and artillery July 11; polling shows ~66% of Ukrainians want a negotiated end. No US ground-deployment surge. (Pravda NATO digest; Russia Matters War Report Card; Al Jazeera, July 7-11, 2026.)",
    "VENEZUELA / CUBA / COLOMBIA (July 2026): Experts and the NGO Acceso a la Justicia say the lapse of the 180-day period without the National Assembly declaring Maduro's 'absolute absence' calls Delcy Rodríguez's interim presidency into question ('a realm of blatant and unprecedented unconstitutionality'); Rodríguez faces ~63.3% disapproval. Maduro and Cilia Flores's next SDNY hearing stays set for July 22 (delayed from June 30). Cuba's energy crisis persists under the US oil blockade and the Venezuelan-crude cutoff; Colombia's Trump-endorsed de la Espriella takes office Aug 7. No US ground-force moves. (Cubaheadlines; PBS NewsHour; Atlantic Council; Wikipedia '2026 US intervention in Venezuela,' July 2026.)",
    "US-CHINA / TAIWAN (July 4-10, 2026): China started new Coast Guard patrols in waters east of Taiwan (July 4; Taiwan called them illegal); the PRC and Russia ran the 'Joint Sea-2026' naval exercise off Qingdao (July 6-13); the PLA tested an SLBM in the South Pacific (July 6). On trade, China's Commerce Ministry said the sides 'agreed in principle' to include agricultural products in a reciprocal tariff-reduction framework; the base Section 122 10% tariff expires ~July 24, with Section 301 measures intended to replace it for China. (AEI China-Taiwan Updates, July 2-10; China Briefing, 2026.)",
    "DPRK / KOREAN PENINSULA (July 3-10, 2026): No new July missile launch confirmed after Kim Jong Un's July 3 weapons tests aboard the destroyer Kang Kon (including a 'strategic' cruise-missile launch evaluation); its sister ship Choe Hyon was commissioned in June. Analysts still expect DPRK activity around the July 27 Korean War armistice anniversary; South Korea's President Lee Jae-myung had attended the NATO Ankara summit as a partner. (AEI Korean Peninsula Update, July 7; ISW, July 2026.)",
    "HORMUZ-DEPENDENT ASIA / EUROPE (July 2026): Iran's July 12 re-closure renews acute exposure for the Strait's largest customers — Japan (~75% of oil imports, ~1.6M bpd via Hormuz), South Korea (~68%, ~1.7M bpd, ~200 days of strategic reserves), India (~half of crude and ~60% of gas), and Pakistan (99% of LNG from Qatar and the UAE, with limited storage — the most vulnerable). (CNBC; Seatrade Maritime; IEA, 2026.)"
