  {
   "date": "2026-07-11",
   "headline": "DAY 135 — THE BREACH-RETALIATION SPIKE GIVES WAY TO A DIPLOMACY-FORWARD PAUSE: AFTER THE JULY 7-9 TWO-SIDED REGIONAL EXCHANGE, US OFFICIALS SAY WASHINGTON IS 'STRIKING THEN PAUSING TO LET DIPLOMACY WORK,' PAKISTAN AND QATAR PRESS BOTH SIDES BACK TOWARD TALKS, AND IRAN HAS REPORTEDLY ASKED TO REOPEN A DEAL — EVEN AS TRUMP POSTS HIS SHARPEST-EVER SEIZURE THREAT ('WE WILL BE TAKING KHARG ISLAND… AND ASSUME TOTAL CONTROL OF THEIR OIL AND GAS MARKETS'). No major new US-Iran kinetic exchange was confirmed for July 10-11 after the July 9 second-day ~90-target US wave and Iran's four-state retaliation; instead, US officials told CNN the administration is 'deliberately striking and then pausing to avoid escalation and let diplomacy work' and is 'currently letting diplomacy take the lead,' with back-channel diplomacy under way and technical nuclear talks continuing. Pakistan and Qatar kept pressing both sides back to the table to salvage the Islamabad MOU, and Trump said Iran had contacted the US seeking a new deal ('Iran wants to make a deal so bad'). Yet in a Truth Social post Trump issued the war's most explicit oil-seizure threat — 'At some point in the not too distant future, we will be taking Kharg Island, and other oil infrastructure points, and assume total control of their Oil and Gas Markets' — a prospect that would 'all but assuredly require boots on the ground.' CRITICALLY, THE THREAT STAYED RHETORIC: the July 8 Kharg action was an AIR strike that deliberately spared oil terminals, no landing or port seizure was ordered or executed, no new ARG/MEU order or draft followed, ~50,000 US personnel were unchanged, and the blockade stayed lifted (Day 23). Brent, after settling +5.2% at $78.02 on July 8, eased back toward $77/bbl on July 9-10 — still far below the ~$126 April peak and the $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. Tanker traffic through Hormuz was near a standstill (only ~2 transits early July 9; ~40/day over the prior two weeks vs a pre-war 125-140), with owners disabling AIS and marine-war underwriters reviewing cover. Two days of US strikes killed at least 14 and wounded ~78 in Iran (Iran Health Ministry). The war widened HORIZONTALLY on July 9 (four partner states, ~90 targets) and then, by July 10-11, turned back toward DIPLOMACY — it never climbed to the ground rung, and it is still Iran, not the US, that has used force at the chokepoint.",
   "summary": "By July 10-11 the sharpest breach-retaliation spike since the MOU had cooled into a diplomacy-forward pause. After the July 7 tanker attacks, the US 80+-target and July 9 ~90-target waves, and Iran's July 9 retaliation against Kuwait, Qatar, Bahrain and Jordan, US officials told CNN the administration was 'deliberately striking and then pausing to avoid escalation and let diplomacy work' and 'currently letting diplomacy take the lead,' with back-channel contacts and technical nuclear talks continuing. Pakistan and Qatar kept working to return both sides to the table, and Trump said Iran had contacted Washington seeking a new deal. Against that de-escalatory turn Trump simultaneously posted the war's most explicit seizure threat — that the US would 'be taking Kharg Island, and other oil infrastructure points, and assume total control of their Oil and Gas Markets' — which analysts noted would require boots on the ground. Every US ground-execution indicator nonetheless stayed at zero: no troops in Iran, no Kharg landing or port seizure, no new amphibious/Marine order, no draft; the blockade stayed lifted (Day 23) and ~50,000 US personnel were unchanged. Oil eased off the July 8 spike toward $77/bbl and Hormuz tanker traffic was near a standstill (~2 transits July 9; ~40/day over two weeks vs a pre-war 125-140). The predicted US Hormuz/Kharg ground seizure remains disconfirmed and inverted — even at peak takeover rhetoric, Kharg was struck only from the air, oil terminals were spared, and it is Iran, not the US, that has used force at the chokepoint. The live question stays deal durability and Hormuz governance, not a forming ground invasion.",
   "details": [
    "US 'STRIKE-THEN-PAUSE FOR DIPLOMACY' (July 10, 2026): US officials told CNN the administration has been 'deliberately striking and then pausing to avoid escalation and let diplomacy work,' with preparations for further strikes 'if needed' but officials 'currently letting diplomacy take the lead.' A US official said Washington and Tehran were 'continuing to engage in technical negotiations on nuclear issues with an eye to a diplomatic solution.' No major new US-Iran kinetic exchange was confirmed for July 10-11 after the July 9 second-day wave. (CNN, 'Ceasefire with Iran hangs in the balance,' July 9-10, 2026; NPR, July 10, 2026.)",
    "MEDIATORS PRESS BOTH SIDES BACK TO TALKS; IRAN SEEKS A DEAL (July 10, 2026): Pakistan and Qatar kept working to bring the US and Iran back to the negotiating table to salvage the Islamabad MOU, regional sources told CNN; Pakistan said it expected the MOU to hold. Trump said Iran had contacted the US seeking a new deal after the strikes resumed — 'Iran wants to make a deal so bad' — while questioning whether Iran was 'worthy.' Talks are paused, not terminated. (CNN; Al Jazeera; Pakistan Today, July 9-10, 2026.)",
    "TRUMP'S SHARPEST-EVER SEIZURE THREAT — 'TOTAL CONTROL OF THEIR OIL AND GAS MARKETS' (July 9-10, 2026): In a Truth Social post Trump wrote that 'at some point in the not too distant future, we will be taking Kharg Island, and other oil infrastructure points, and assume total control of their Oil and Gas Markets' — the war's most explicit oil-seizure rhetoric — a step reporting noted 'would all but assuredly require boots on the ground.' The July 8 Kharg action was an AIR strike that deliberately spared the island's oil terminals; no US targeting posture, landing, or port-seizure order for Kharg was reported. (The Hill; NBC News; Washington Examiner, July 9, 2026.)",
    "CASUALTIES AND OIL (July 9-10, 2026): Iran's Health Ministry said two days of US airstrikes killed at least 14 and wounded ~78 in Iran; Kuwait reported one person wounded by falling debris in Iran's July 9 retaliation, with no reported casualties in Qatar or Jordan (all incoming intercepted). Brent, after settling +5.2% at $78.02/bbl on July 8, eased back toward $77/bbl on July 9-10 — well below the ~$126 April peak. (NPR; Al Jazeera; TradingEconomics, July 9-10, 2026.)",
    "HORMUZ TRAFFIC NEAR STANDSTILL (July 9-10, 2026): Oil-tanker traffic through the Strait was at a near standstill — only about two tankers transited in the early hours of July 9 — after averaging ~40 sailings a day over the prior two weeks, still far below the pre-war 125-140 daily average. Owners increasingly disabled AIS transponders and marine-war underwriters reviewed cover; Rystad's Jorge Leon: tanker traffic 'has essentially stopped, which tells you more about risk perception right now than any statement from Washington or Tehran.' The Strait stayed physically open (blockade lifted, Day 23). (Al-Monitor, 'Oil tanker traffic through Hormuz at near standstill,' July 9, 2026.)",
    "GROUND RUNG STILL ZERO AT PEAK RHETORIC (July 10-11, 2026): Despite Trump's 'total control' seizure threat and the July 9 regional exchange, there were no US troops in Iran, no Kharg landing or port seizure ordered or executed, no new ARG/MEU landing order, and no draft; the blockade stayed lifted (Day 23) and ~50,000 US personnel were unchanged. The standing USS Boxer ARG / 11th MEU and USS Tripoli ARG / 31st MEU remained in the Arabian Sea in routine/contingency posture. (Stars and Stripes, July 1; NBC News; CNN, July 9-10, 2026.)"
