   "prediction_impact": "July 10-11 leaves the sharpest test of Predictive History's imminent-US-ground-invasion / Hormuz-and-Kharg-seizure thesis where the July 9 spike did: NOT rehabilitated. The single most provocative fact for the thesis to date is Trump's Truth Social post that the US 'will be taking Kharg Island, and other oil infrastructure points, and assume total control of their Oil and Gas Markets' — verbatim the seizure the corpus predicts, and reporting flags it 'would all but assuredly require boots on the ground.' But the execution again inverted the prediction: Kharg was struck only from the AIR with oil terminals deliberately spared, no landing or port seizure was ordered, no ARG/MEU order or draft followed, the blockade stayed lifted (Day 23), and by July 10-11 the US had shifted to a 'strike-then-pause for diplomacy' posture while Iran sought a deal and Pakistan/Qatar reconvened the parties. The gap between maximal takeover rhetoric and zero ground execution is now the defining pattern of the war. The prediction stays disconfirmed and inverted: it is Iran, not the US, that has used force at the chokepoint. The episode bears on two live open questions — deal durability (iran-us-deal-reached-june-2026), breached-but-not-cancelled and now in a diplomacy-forward pause, and the coercion-vs-return-to-war ambiguity (june3-kuwait-kinetic-escalation-2026), sharpened by the seizure threat but blunted by the July 10-11 diplomatic turn and the deliberate sparing of oil infrastructure. Brent near $77/bbl is war-risk repricing, still far below ground-commitment pricing.",
