   "date": "2026-07-10",
   "tag": "Multi-Theatre Roundup",
   "theatre": null,
   "headline": "MULTI-THEATRE ROUNDUP — JULY 9-10: RUSSIA-UKRAINE talks stay stalled after Putin rejected an advisers' frontline-freeze proposal and Trump worked the phones around the NATO Ankara summit; VENEZUELA's Maduro drug-trafficking trial is delayed to July 22 as Delcy Rodriguez leads the interim government; CUBA suffered its third nationwide blackout of 2026 (July 6) amid the US-driven fuel crisis; US-CHINA reached an 'in-principle' agriculture tariff framework even as biotech restrictions and Taiwan stay flashpoints; NORTH KOREA's Kim oversaw destroyer cruise-missile tests and pressed a 10,000-ton cruiser program; and the HORMUZ-EXPOSED ASIAN economies (Japan, South Korea, India) remain the most vulnerable to any renewed supply shock though no acute shortage has hit.",
   "summary": "Roundup of the non-Iran theatres as of July 9-10. Russia-Ukraine: negotiations remain stalled — Putin rebuked advisers who proposed freezing the war along current front lines, believing Russia will take the rest of Donbas, while Russian forces posted only marginal net gains (DeepState ~+31 sq mi / ISW ~+6 sq mi, June 9-July 7) around Kostyantynivka-Druzhkivka, Sumy, Kharkiv and Zaporizhia; Trump spoke with Putin and Zelensky on July 4 and met Zelensky at the NATO Ankara summit. Venezuela: interim President Delcy Rodriguez continues to govern after the US capture of Maduro, whose drug-trafficking trial (with wife Cilia Flores) was pushed from June 30 to July 22, 2026 after not-guilty pleas. Cuba: a third islandwide blackout of 2026 hit July 6 as fuel reserves dwindled under the US 'energy blockade'; Cuba produces only ~40% of its fuel needs and the late-March Russian tanker delivery ran out by end-April. US-China: Beijing and Washington 'agreed in principle' to fold agricultural products into a reciprocal tariff-reduction framework, but China warned of 'resolute' retaliation over US biotech restrictions, and Xi reiterated Taiwan as the 'most important' issue; Taiwan ran a June 25 tabletop exercise against a PRC maritime 'quarantine' and sent a multipartisan delegation to the US Congress. North Korea: Kim Jong Un observed strategic cruise-missile and air-defense tests aboard the destroyer Kang Kon (July 3) and pushed development of a 10,000-ton guided-missile cruiser, targeting a September commissioning. Hormuz-exposed Asia: Japan (75% of oil from the Middle East), South Korea (~70%) and India (~60%) remain most at risk from any renewed Hormuz disruption, though Seoul says it sees no supply problem for over a year and no acute shortage has materialized while the Strait stays open.",
   "details": [
    "RUSSIA-UKRAINE — TALKS STALLED, MARGINAL RUSSIAN GAINS (July 1-8, 2026): Putin dismissed advisers' proposal to freeze the war along current front lines and rebuked them, judging Russia will eventually seize all of Donbas; Russian forces netted only ~31 sq mi (DeepState) / ~6 sq mi (ISW) June 9-July 7, with fighting around Kostyantynivka-Druzhkivka, Sumy, Kharkiv and Zaporizhia. Trump spoke by phone with Putin and Zelensky on July 4 and met Zelensky at the NATO Ankara summit. (Russia Matters War Report Card, July 8, 2026; ISW Russian Offensive Campaign Assessment, July 5, 2026; Kyiv Post/Reuters.)",
    "VENEZUELA — MADURO TRIAL DELAYED TO JULY 22 (July 2026): Interim President Delcy Rodriguez continues to lead Venezuela after the US nighttime military operation that captured Maduro; his and Cilia Flores's return to court in the SDNY drug-trafficking case was pushed from June 30 to July 22, 2026 after not-guilty pleas. (Wikipedia, 'Prosecution of Nicolas Maduro and Cilia Flores'; PBS NewsHour; NPR, 2026.)",
    "CUBA — THIRD NATIONWIDE BLACKOUT OF 2026 (July 6, 2026): Cuba suffered its third islandwide blackout since January as fuel reserves dwindled under the US-driven fuel blockade; the island produces only ~40% of its fuel needs, and 730,000 barrels delivered by a Russian tanker in late March ran out by end-April. Havana areas saw 24h+ outages; tens of thousands of surgeries were cancelled. (Al Jazeera; PBS NewsHour; Washington Times, July 6-7, 2026.)",
    "US-CHINA — AG TARIFF FRAMEWORK 'IN PRINCIPLE,' BIOTECH AND TAIWAN TENSE (July 2, 2026): China's Commerce Ministry said the two sides 'agreed in principle' to include agricultural goods in a reciprocal tariff-reduction framework, but Beijing warned it would 'resolutely' retaliate over US biotech restrictions, and Xi called Taiwan the 'most important' issue in the relationship. Taiwan held a June 25 tabletop exercise simulating a PRC maritime 'quarantine' and sent a multipartisan delegation to the US Congress. (AEI China-Taiwan Update, July 2, 2026; South China Morning Post.)",
    "NORTH KOREA — DESTROYER CRUISE-MISSILE TESTS, 10,000-TON CRUISER PUSH (July 3-4, 2026): Kim Jong Un observed strategic cruise-missile, anti-ship, anti-submarine and air-defense tests aboard the destroyer Kang Kon and called for its commissioning by September; he also pressed development of a 10,000-ton guided-missile cruiser to expand naval nuclear deterrence. (KCNA via US News/Reuters, July 4, 2026; AEI Korean Peninsula Update, July 7, 2026.)",
    "HORMUZ-EXPOSED ASIA — JAPAN, SOUTH KOREA, INDIA MOST AT RISK (July 2026): Japan (Middle East supplies ~75% of its oil), South Korea (~70%) and India (~60%, plus >50% of LNG Gulf-linked) remain the economies most exposed to any renewed Hormuz disruption; Korea's energy minister said the country faces no supply problem for over a year, and no acute shortage has materialized while the Strait stays physically open. (Zero Carbon Analytics; CNBC; Wikipedia, '2026 Iran war fuel crisis,' July 2026.)"
   ],
