   "theatre": null,
   "prediction_impact": "July 8-9 is the sharpest test to date of Predictive History's imminent-US-ground-invasion / Hormuz-and-Kharg-seizure thesis — and it still does NOT rehabilitate it; it reinforces the disconfirmed-and-inverted pattern. Trump publicly declared the ceasefire and MOU 'over' and threatened renewed bombing and blockade — the strongest US escalation rhetoric since the war paused — and CENTCOM ran its largest post-MOU strike wave (80+ targets, 60+ IRGC boats). Yet the execution stayed on the air/missile and maritime rungs: no US troops in Iran, no Kharg landing or port seizure, no new ARG/MEU order, no draft, and the blockade stayed lifted (Day 21). The prediction's specific mechanism — a US ground seizure of Kharg/Iranian oil and physical US control of Hormuz — remains disconfirmed and inverted: it is Iran, not the US, using force to assert control over the Strait, and the US answered from the air. The episode bears directly on two live open questions — deal durability (iran-us-deal-reached-june-2026), now under maximal strain with the US president calling the MOU 'over,' and the coercion-vs-return-to-war ambiguity (june3-kuwait-kinetic-escalation-2026), sharpened by the peak rhetoric and immediately blunted by the same-day walk-back. Brent's move to $78/bbl is war-risk repricing, still far below ground-commitment pricing.",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "Trump's July 8 declaration that the ceasefire and MOU are 'over,' paired with the largest US strike wave of the post-MOU period, admits the same two readings that have run through every breach-retaliation round — now at their sharpest. One treats it as coercive bargaining and deterrence signalling inside a still-recoverable framework: the president's own same-day walk-back ('I don't think it's going to start again'), the absence of any ground move, casualties, or formal cancellation, and talks only paused rather than terminated. The other treats the presidential repudiation of the MOU plus the largest-yet strike package as the leading edge of a genuine return to open war.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Both readings rest on the same on-record facts: CENTCOM ran 'additional strikes' (80+ targets, 60+ IRGC boats) on July 8; Trump called the ceasefire/MOU 'over,' 'a waste of time,' and threatened renewed bombing/blockade, then hours later said the fighting would not lead to long-term military action; Brent settled +5.2% at $78.02 then eased on the walk-back; no US ground troops, Kharg assault, ARG/MEU order, or draft followed; the blockade stayed lifted. Which reading is correct turns on whether the US resumes the blockade or mounts a ground move, whether Iran executes a materially escalatory retaliation, and whether the post-funeral round reconvenes — none determinable from open sources on July 9.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "President Trump (July 8, 2026): the ceasefire/MOU is 'over' and 'a waste of time'; Iran is 'vicious, violent'; 'every time they hit us, we hit them 20' — then, same day, 'I don't think it's going to start again.'",
      "US CENTCOM (July 8, 2026): the additional strikes are to 'degrade their ability to threaten freedom of navigation,' framed as calibrated deterrence rather than a war restart.",
      "Some commentators/markets: read the presidential repudiation of the MOU plus the largest-yet strike wave as evidence the interim peace is unravelling (reflected in the Brent spike).",
      "Other analysts: read the same-day walk-back, the absence of any ground move, and talks only paused as coercive bargaining inside a still-live framework."
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "Resolution requires knowing whether the US reinstates the blockade or moves to the ground, whether Iran mounts a materially escalatory retaliation, and whether the post-funeral negotiation reconvenes; as of July 9 open sources show only the July 7-8 air/maritime exchange, the oil-waiver revocation, the ceasefire-'over' remark and its same-day walk-back — consistent with both a contained coercive round and the start of a slide back to war.",
     "open_question_ref": "iran-us-deal-reached-june-2026"
    }
