  {
   "date": "2026-07-08",
   "headline": "DAY 132 — CEASEFIRE DAY 92 (BREACHED) / US BLOCKADE LIFTED DAY 20 — KINETIC CYCLE RESUMES: THE WAR'S FIVE-DAY OFF-THE-KINETIC STREAK BROKE ON JULY 7. IRAN'S IRGC ATTACKED THREE TANKERS NEAR THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ OFF OMAN'S COAST — THE QATARI LNG CARRIER AL-REKAYYAT (STRUCK ~8 MI EAST OF LIMAH, ENGINE-ROOM FIRE), THE SAUDI CRUDE SUPERTANKER WEDYAN (IRANIAN MISSILE), AND A THIRD TANKER HIT BY DRONE (MINOR DAMAGE) — TO ENFORCE ITS 'DESIGNATED ROUTES'; NO CASUALTIES REPORTED. US CENTCOM ANSWERED WITH 'A SERIES OF POWERFUL STRIKES' — ITS LARGEST RETALIATORY PACKAGE OF THE POST-MOU PERIOD — ON AIR-DEFENSE, COASTAL-SURVEILLANCE, SAM, ANTI-SHIP-CRUISE-MISSILE, DRONE-LAUNCH AND PORT SITES; EXPLOSIONS REPORTED NEAR BANDAR ABBAS, QESHM ISLAND, BANDAR KHAMIR AND SIRIK. THE US TREASURY (OFAC) REVOKED THE GL X WAIVER LETTING IRAN SELL OIL (WIND-DOWN BY JULY 17). IRAN'S DEPUTY FM GHARIBABADI CALLED IT A 'BLATANT VIOLATION' OF THE ISLAMABAD MOU AND VOWED 'DECISIVE ACTIONS'; TEHRAN WARNED FINAL-DEAL TALKS 'WILL NOT COMMENCE IF THREATS CONTINUE.' BRENT JUMPED ~5.5% TO ~$76/BBL (WTI ~$72). CRITICALLY, THE EXCHANGE STAYED AIR AND MARITIME — NO US GROUND TROOPS IN IRAN, NO KHARG ASSAULT, NO NEW ARG/MEU ORDER, NO DRAFT; THE BLOCKADE STAYS LIFTED (DAY 20). SEPARATELY, KHAMENEI'S FUNERAL CORTEGE CROSSED INTO IRAQ FOR RITES AT NAJAF AND KARBALA (JULY 8) AHEAD OF BURIAL AT MASHHAD'S IMAM REZA SHRINE JULY 9; NEW SUPREME LEADER MOJTABA KHAMENEI (NAMED MARCH 9) STILL HAD NOT APPEARED PUBLICLY.",
   "summary": "After five straight days off the kinetic register, the Iran war's breach-retaliation cycle resumed on July 7 during the funeral week. Iran's Revolutionary Guard attacked three commercial vessels near the Strait of Hormuz off Oman's coast: the Qatar-flagged LNG carrier Al-Rekayyat (struck by a projectile ~8 miles east of Limah, sparking an engine-room fire), the Saudi-flagged crude supertanker Wedyan (damaged by an Iranian-fired missile), and a third tanker hit by a drone (minor structural damage per UKMTO); no casualties were reported. Iranian state TV said the ships had ignored warnings about Tehran-approved transit routes. US Central Command responded with what it called 'a series of powerful strikes against Iran' — described as larger than prior retaliatory packages — hitting air defenses, coastal-surveillance and radar sites, surface-to-air and anti-ship cruise-missile batteries, drone-launch sites and port facilities, with explosions reported near Bandar Abbas, Qeshm Island, Bandar Khamir and the port of Sirik along Iran's southern coast. The Treasury's OFAC revoked the general license that had allowed Iranian oil and petrochemical sales, effective immediately with a wind-down by July 17. Iran's Foreign Ministry called the strikes and revocation a 'blatant violation' of the mid-June Islamabad MOU, vowed 'decisive actions to preserve its security,' and warned that final-deal negotiations 'will not commence if threats continue'; US-Iran talks were already paused for the funeral week. Oil, which had slid to a four-month low near the pre-war ~$72/bbl, spiked: Brent rose ~5.5% to about $76/bbl (settling ~$74.16, +3%) and WTI ~5% to about $72. The escalation stayed on the air/missile and maritime rungs — the US answer to Iranian maritime coercion was an air-strike package, not a Kharg landing or port seizure, and every US ground-execution indicator remained at zero: no US troops in Iran, no Kharg assault, no new ARG/MEU order, no draft; the naval blockade stayed lifted (Day 20). In parallel, the state funeral for slain Supreme Leader Ayatollah Ali Khamenei moved into Iraq on July 8 — an official reception at Najaf International Airport, a procession toward the Shrine of Imam Ali, and rites at the Imam Hussein shrine in Karbala, with President Pezeshkian, Speaker Ghalibaf and Khamenei's eldest son attending — ahead of final burial at the Imam Reza shrine in Mashhad, Khamenei's birthplace, on July 9. New Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei, named March 9, still had not appeared publicly.",
   "details": [
    "IRAN ATTACKS THREE TANKERS NEAR HORMUZ (July 7, 2026): Iran's IRGC was blamed for near-simultaneous attacks on three vessels transiting off Oman's coast — the Qatar-flagged LNG carrier Al-Rekayyat (unidentified projectile to the port side ~8 mi east of Limah, engine-room fire, explosion risk, no casualties), the Saudi-flagged crude supertanker Wedyan (Iranian-fired missile), and a third tanker struck by a drone (minor structural damage per UK Maritime Trade Operations). Iranian state TV said the ships ignored warnings about Tehran-approved routes. (CBS News; NBC News; Al Jazeera, July 7, 2026.)",
    "US CENTCOM LAUNCHES 'POWERFUL STRIKES' (July 7, 2026): CENTCOM announced 'a series of powerful strikes against Iran … to impose heavy costs for targeting and attacking commercial shipping,' calling Iran's attacks a clear ceasefire violation. The package — reported as larger than previous US retaliatory strikes — hit air defenses, coastal-surveillance/radar sites, SAM batteries, anti-ship cruise-missile sites, drone-launch sites and port facilities; Iranian media reported explosions near Bandar Abbas, Qeshm Island, Bandar Khamir and the port of Sirik. The strikes were air/stand-off — no ground operation, Kharg assault, or troop movement was reported. (CNBC; NBC News; The Hill; Times of Israel, July 7, 2026.)",
    "TREASURY REVOKES IRAN OIL WAIVER (July 7, 2026): OFAC revoked the GL X authorization that had permitted Iranian oil and petrochemical sales on the global market, effective immediately, with production/delivery/sales to be wound down by July 17. A US official: 'Iran will only reap benefits if they exhibit good behavior.' Iran's deputy FM Kazem Gharibabadi called it a blatant violation of the Islamabad MOU. (CBS News; CNBC, July 7, 2026.)",
    "IRAN VOWS 'DECISIVE ACTIONS'; TALKS AT RISK (July 7-8, 2026): Iran's Foreign Ministry accused the US of violating the MOU and said Iran will take 'decisive actions to preserve its security'; the FM warned that negotiations on a final deal 'will not commence if threats continue.' Indirect US-Iran talks remained paused for the funeral week (to resume after the July 9 burial per Qatar). No Iranian retaliatory strike had been confirmed by the morning of July 8. (Washington Times; Times of Israel; Al Jazeera, July 7-8, 2026.)",
    "OIL SPIKES OFF THE FOUR-MONTH LOW (July 7, 2026): Brent rose ~5.5% to about $76/bbl (September) and settled ~$74.16 (+3%); WTI advanced ~5% to about $72 (August). The move reversed the post-ceasefire slide that had taken Brent to a four-month low near the pre-war ~$72/bbl, but stayed far below the $110+ wartime peaks and the $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. (CNBC; CBS News, July 7, 2026.)",
    "FUNERAL MOVES TO IRAQ, THEN MASHHAD (July 8-9, 2026): Khamenei's cortege crossed into Iraq for a reception at Najaf International Airport and processions at the Shrine of Imam Ali (Najaf) and Imam Hussein shrine (Karbala) on July 8, with President Pezeshkian, Speaker Ghalibaf and Khamenei's eldest son attending, ahead of burial at the Imam Reza shrine in Mashhad on July 9. New Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei (named March 9) still had not appeared publicly, keeping the who-runs-Iran question live. (State funeral of Ali Khamenei; The National; Al Jazeera, July 2-8, 2026.)"
   ],
   "source": "CNBC, 'U.S. resumes powerful strikes on Iran after Hormuz ship attacks,' July 7, 2026; NBC News, 'U.S. launches new attacks on Iran in retaliation for attacks on commercial ships,' July 7, 2026; CBS News live, 'U.S. launches retaliatory strikes on Iran after commercial ship attacks,' July 7, 2026; The Hill; Times of Israel liveblog July 7, 2026; Washington Times, July 7, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Ships attacked in the Strait of Hormuz,' July 7, 2026; State funeral of Ali Khamenei (Najaf/Karbala July 8, Mashhad July 9); CNN NATO summit live; Time; Al Jazeera Ankara summit, July 7-8, 2026; Washington Post / Bloomberg / Fox News, Cuba nationwide blackout, July 6, 2026; USTR Section 301 hearing July 7, 2026; Washington Post, DPRK Kang Kon, July 4, 2026.",
