   "headline": "LATIN AMERICA — CUBA NATIONWIDE BLACKOUT (JULY 6) DEEPENS AS US OIL BLOCKADE DRAGS ON; VENEZUELA MANDATE DISPUTE, MADURO TRIAL JULY 22: CUBA'S ENTIRE NATIONAL GRID COLLAPSED AT MIDDAY JULY 6 — THE THIRD/FOURTH TOTAL BLACKOUT OF 2026 — LEAVING ~10 MILLION WITHOUT POWER; GRID OPERATOR UNE SERVED ~1% OF HAVANA'S DEMAND BY LATE AFTERNOON, WITH RESTORATION UNDERWAY JULY 7-8. THE COLLAPSE IS DRIVEN BY A DE FACTO US OIL BLOCKADE, A CRUMBLING GRID, AND THE CUTOFF OF VENEZUELAN CRUDE (AND PRESSURED MEXICAN SHIPMENTS); DÍAZ-CANEL SAYS CUBA HAS BEEN UNABLE TO IMPORT FUEL SINCE JANUARY EXCEPT ONE RUSSIAN SHIPMENT. IN VENEZUELA, ACTING PRESIDENT DELCY RODRÍGUEZ FACES A DISPUTE THAT HER 180-DAY INTERIM MANDATE HAS LAPSED; MADURO AND CILIA FLORES'S NEXT SDNY HEARING STAYS SET FOR JULY 22 (POSTPONED FROM JUNE 30). NO US GROUND-FORCE MOVES.",
   "summary": "Cuba's national electric grid suffered a total collapse at midday on July 6 — the island's third full nationwide blackout in six months (the fourth total outage of 2026) — leaving nearly 10 million people without power; grid operator UNE said it could serve only about 1% of Havana's demand by late afternoon and prioritized hospitals and food-production centers, with restoration underway July 7-8. The failure reflects a de facto US oil blockade, decades of infrastructure decay, and the loss of Venezuelan crude after the Trump administration cut Venezuelan shipments to Cuba and pressured Mexico to halt deliveries; President Díaz-Canel says Cuba has been unable to import fuel since January except for a single Russian shipment. In Venezuela, acting President Delcy Rodríguez — who continues the US-cooperation pattern (oil concessions, detainee releases) — faces a dispute that her 180-day interim mandate has lapsed. Nicolás Maduro and Cilia Flores's next SDNY hearing remains set for July 22, postponed from June 30 over World-Cup-related security logistics. Colombia's transition to Trump-endorsed president-elect Abelardo de la Espriella (handover August 7) continued. No US ground-force moves were reported.",
