   "prediction_impact": "July 7 keeps Predictive History's imminent-US-ground-invasion and Hormuz-and-Kharg-seizure thesis disconfirmed-and-inverted and extends the off-the-kinetic-track register into a fifth straight day: the war's dominant event was the Qom leg of a six-day state funeral, with US-Iran talks paused and every US ground-execution indicator at zero — no landing, Kharg assault, ARG/MEU surge, or draft — and the blockade still lifted (Day 19). The structural inversion holds: the chokepoint is being reopened and governed by negotiation and coercive Iranian claims (mid-August 'service fees'), not a US seizure, and Brent near pre-war ~$72/bbl is the inverse of ground-commitment pricing. Two open questions stay live — deal durability (iran-us-deal-reached-june-2026), since talks are only paused and no frozen funds have moved, and Hormuz governance (iran-hormuz-administration-tolls-dispute-june-2026), with a mid-August toll deadline the US rejects. The succession question (iran-succession-mojtaba-khamenei-2026) remains open with the new Supreme Leader still absent, now tied to reported strike injuries — bearing on regime cohesion and who can deliver any deal, not on US ground posture.",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "Mojtaba Khamenei's continued public absence through the Qom leg admits the same two readings tracked since July 5, now sharpened by reporting on injuries. One treats the absence as the sum of wartime security prudence (an Israeli threat to kill Iran's new leader) and genuine incapacity from the Feb 28 strike that US officials said left him 'wounded and likely disfigured' — with authority still exercised through the SNSC and IRGC as designed. The other treats the months-long non-appearance since his March 9 designation as evidence of a leadership vacuum: that Mojtaba may be too incapacitated to govern or is a figurehead, and that the security establishment is effectively running Iran, which would bear on who can actually deliver on any US-Iran deal.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Both readings rest on the same on-record facts: Mojtaba was named successor March 9; he has not appeared publicly since; his other brothers prayed at the coffin July 5 while he did not; US officials said in March he was 'wounded and likely disfigured' and later reports described severe facial/leg injuries; and Israel renewed a threat against Iran's new leader. Which reading is correct depends on non-public information about the severity of his injuries, his current health, and his real decision-making role, none of it determinable from open sources today.",
