   "headline": "RUSSIA-UKRAINE — JULY 5-6: KOSTIANTYNIVKA CONTROL STILL CONTESTED; DEEP-STRIKE WAR CONTINUES. RUSSIA'S CLAIM (GERASIMOV TO PUTIN, JULY 3) THAT IT HAD 'LIBERATED' KOSTIANTYNIVKA REMAINS DISPUTED — UKRAINE'S 19TH ARMY CORPS COMMANDER MADYAR REPORTED HIS FORCES PUSHING RUSSIAN INFILTRATORS BACK OUT OF THE CITY AT A LOSS RATIO OF ROUGHLY 3 UKRAINIANS TO 53 RUSSIANS, AND ISW/DEEPSTATE CONTINUE TO ASSESS SMALL-GROUP INFILTRATION, NOT CONSOLIDATED CONTROL (DEEPSTATE LOGGED ~12 SQ MI NET RUSSIAN GAIN OVER THE FOUR WEEKS TO JUNE 23). UKRAINE KEPT UP ITS REFINERY CAMPAIGN (LUKOIL-NIZHEGORODORGSINTEZ, RUSSIA'S 4TH-LARGEST, HALTED AFTER A JULY 2 STRIKE; UFA HIT TWICE IN A WEEK; ST. PETERSBURG OIL TERMINAL / KRONSTADT JULY 4). RUSSIA PRESSED MASS STRIKES ON KYIV (THE JULY 1-2 BARRAGE KILLED AT LEAST 17-31). ZELENSKY, AFTER A ~40-MIN JULY 4 CALL WITH TRUMP, PRESSED ALLIES TO ACCELERATE PATRIOT DELIVERIES AND WARNED OF ANOTHER LARGE-SCALE RUSSIAN ATTACK. TALKS STAY STALLED (PUTIN STILL DEMANDS THE 2022 ISTANBUL PROTOCOLS); RUSSIA HOLDS ~20% WITH NO CONFIRMED NET CHANGE. NO US GROUND-DEPLOYMENT SURGE.",
   "summary": "The Russia-Ukraine war stayed on its established July track — contested Donbas ground claims, a deep-strike energy war, and stalled diplomacy. Russia's July 3 claim (Gerasimov to Putin) that its forces had 'liberated' Kostiantynivka remained disputed: Ukraine's 19th Army Corps commander Yuriy 'Madyar' Butusov reported his troops pushing Russian infiltrators back out of the city at a loss ratio of about three Ukrainians to 53 Russians, and ISW and DeepState continued to characterize the fighting as small-group infiltration rather than consolidated control (DeepState logged only ~12 square miles of net Russian gain over the four weeks to June 23, underscoring that 2024-25 momentum has largely stalled). Ukraine kept striking Russia's oil sector — the Lukoil-Nizhegorodorgsintez refinery (Russia's fourth-largest, second-largest gasoline producer) halted after a July 2 drone strike, Ufa was hit twice in a week, and the St. Petersburg oil terminal and Kronstadt were struck July 4. Russia pressed mass strikes on Kyiv (the July 1-2 barrage killed at least 17, with earlier tallies up to 31). After a roughly 40-minute July 4 call with Trump that agreed to strengthen Ukraine's air defenses and expand joint drone/weapons production, Zelensky pressed allies to accelerate Patriot deliveries and warned Russia was preparing another large-scale attack. Peace talks stayed stalled, with Putin still insisting on the 2022 Istanbul Protocols; Russia holds about 20% of Ukraine with no confirmed net change, and there was no US ground-deployment surge.",
   "details": [
    "KOSTIANTYNIVKA CONTROL STILL CONTESTED (July 3-6, 2026): Russia's Gerasimov told Putin (July 3) that Russian forces had 'liberated' Kostiantynivka; Ukraine's General Staff and Zelensky denied it. Ukraine's 19th Army Corps commander 'Madyar' reported forces pushing Russian infiltrators back out of the city at a ~3:53 loss ratio, and ISW assessed the gains as small-group infiltration, not consolidated control. Russia lacks the density to sustain simultaneous Pokrovsk and Kostiantynivka offensives; DeepState logged ~12 sq mi net Russian gain over the four weeks to June 23. (GlobalSecurity, 'Russo-Ukraine War - June 2026'; ISW, July 3-5, 2026.)",
    "UKRAINE'S REFINERY / DEEP-STRIKE CAMPAIGN CONTINUES (July 1-4, 2026): Ukraine struck Russia's Ufa refinery twice in a week (July 1), then the Lukoil-Nizhegorodorgsintez plant — Russia's fourth-largest refinery and second-largest gasoline producer — which halted operations after a July 2 overnight strike; on July 4 Ukraine hit the St. Petersburg oil terminal and military targets at Kronstadt (~1,100 km, among the deepest strikes yet on Putin's home city). The campaign has hit 8 of Russia's top 10 refineries in 2026. (Euronews, July 1, 2026; Kyiv Independent, July 2, 2026.)",
    "RUSSIA'S MASS STRIKES ON KYIV (July 1-2, 2026): A major Russian drone-and-missile barrage on Kyiv killed at least 17 civilians (earlier tallies ran to 20-31) and injured scores, described by Moscow as retaliation for Ukrainian strikes on its oil facilities. (NPR, 'A major Russian attack kills 17 in Kyiv as Ukraine keeps striking Moscow's oil sector,' July 2, 2026.)",
    "DIPLOMACY AND AIR DEFENSE (July 4-6, 2026): A ~40-minute Zelensky-Trump call (July 4, 'the best conversation in all this time') agreed to strengthen Ukraine's air defenses and expand joint drone/weapons production, days after a US DoD pause on Patriot and precision-artillery shipments; Zelensky pressed allies to accelerate Patriot deliveries and warned of another large-scale Russian attack. Peace talks stayed stalled, with Putin insisting on the 2022 Istanbul Protocols. (CBS News; ABC News, July 4-5, 2026.)"
   ],
