   "headline": "OTHER THEATRES — JULY 5-6: (VENEZUELA) MADURO'S NEXT HEARING PUSHED FROM JUNE 30 TO JULY 22 OVER WORLD-CUP SECURITY LOGISTICS (SDNY; METLIFE FINAL JULY 19); CUBA MAINTAINS 32 OF ITS PERSONNEL DIED IN THE JANUARY US OPERATION THAT CAPTURED MADURO. (NORTH KOREA) KIM JONG UN OVERSAW A STRATEGIC CRUISE-MISSILE AND WEAPONS TEST ABOARD THE NEW 5,000-TON DESTROYER KANG KON (JULY 4), THE 7TH MISSILE TEST OF 2026. (US-CHINA) SECTION 301 INVESTIGATIONS SET TO CONCLUDE THIS SUMMER WITH CHINA EXPECTED TO ACCEPT RESTORED PRIOR TARIFF RATES; A BASE SECTION 122 10% TARIFF EXPIRES ~JULY 24. NO US GROUND-FORCE MOVES TIED TO ANY OF THESE.",
   "summary": "Secondary theatres moved incrementally. In Venezuela, US District Judge Alvin Hellerstein (SDNY) postponed Nicolas Maduro and Cilia Flores's next court appearance from June 30 to July 22, 2026, at the prosecution's request (backed by the defense), citing secure-transport and security logistics around the FIFA World Cup (eight matches at MetLife Stadium, the final July 19); Cuba continued to maintain that 32 of its military and intelligence personnel died in the January 2026 US operation that captured Maduro. On the Korean Peninsula, North Korean state media said Kim Jong Un supervised the test-firing of a strategic cruise missile and evaluations of anti-ship, anti-submarine and air-defense systems aboard the new 5,000-ton destroyer Kang Kon on July 4 — reported as the North's seventh missile test of 2026 amid an ordered production surge. On US-China trade, the two Section 301 investigations are set to conclude this summer with Treasury Secretary Bessent expecting China to accept restoration of prior tariff rates, while a base Section 122 10% tariff applying to all countries is set to expire around July 24. None of these involved US ground-force movements.",
   "details": [
    "VENEZUELA / CUBA (July 2026): Maduro and Flores's return to court, set for June 30, was postponed to July 22, 2026 (12:00 p.m. ET) by Judge Alvin K. Hellerstein at the prosecution's request (defense-supported), citing secure-transport and security logistics tied to the 2026 FIFA World Cup (eight MetLife Stadium matches, final July 19). Cuba continued to say 32 of its military/intelligence personnel were 'killed in combative actions' during the January 2026 US operation that captured Maduro. (NBC News; Wikipedia, 'Prosecution of Nicolas Maduro and Cilia Flores,' July 2026; CubaHeadlines, 2026.)",
    "NORTH KOREA (July 4, 2026): State media said Kim Jong Un observed the test-firing of a strategic (nuclear-capable) cruise missile and evaluations of anti-ship, anti-submarine and air-defense systems aboard the new 5,000-ton destroyer Kang Kon — reported as the North's seventh missile test of 2026, amid Kim's ordered surge in missile and munitions production. (Washington Post; US News/Reuters, July 4, 2026.)",
    "US-CHINA TRADE (late June-July 2026): The two Section 301 investigations are set to conclude this summer, with Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent expecting China to accept restoration of prior tariff rates; a base Section 122 10% tariff applying to all countries is set to expire around July 24, and a Section 301 tariff of 10-12.5% on products from ~60 investigated countries is under proposal. No new Taiwan military crisis was reported. (China Briefing, US-China timeline, June 23, 2026; Tax Foundation tariff tracker, 2026.)",
    "HORMUZ-AFFECTED ECONOMIES (early July 2026): South Korea's energy minister said the country would face no supply problem for over a year; Japan and South Korea kept up non-Gulf sourcing; Pakistan and Bangladesh stayed among the worst-hit (Pakistan urged fans to watch cricket from home to conserve fuel); India managed an LPG/gas squeeze while lifting record discounted Russian crude. The IEA chief reiterated the war is the 'greatest global energy security challenge in history,' easing as Hormuz flows recovered above 10M bpd. (IEA; Al Jazeera; Zero Carbon Analytics, July 2026.)"
   ],
   "source": "NBC News, 'Maduro and his wife plead not guilty at first court appearance since capture,' 2026; Wikipedia, 'Prosecution of Nicolas Maduro and Cilia Flores,' July 2026; Washington Post, 'North Korean leader Kim observes weapons tests from new naval destroyer,' July 4, 2026; US News/Reuters, July 4, 2026; China Briefing, 'US-China Relations in the Trump 2.0 Era: A Timeline,' June 23, 2026; Tax Foundation, 'Tariff Tracker,' 2026; IEA, 'The Middle East and Global Energy Markets,' July 2026.",
