   "date": "2026-07-05",
   "headline": "DAY 129 — CEASEFIRE DAY 89 / US BLOCKADE LIFTED DAY 17 — SECOND DAY OF KHAMENEI'S SIX-DAY STATE FUNERAL: LYING-IN-STATE AT TEHRAN'S GRAND MOSALLA CONTINUED AND THE RITES MOVED TOWARD QOM (JULY 5 PRAYERS AND PROCESSION AT THE FATIMA MASOUMEH SHRINE AND JAMKARAN MOSQUE) AMID CROWDS CHANTING 'REVENGE' AND 'DEATH TO AMERICA' IN ~100°F HEAT. OFFICIALS PROJECT 15-20 MILLION MOURNERS OVER THE WEEK (BILLED AS THE LARGEST STATE FUNERAL IN IRANIAN HISTORY, EXCEEDING KHOMEINI'S 1989 RITES), WITH DELEGATIONS FROM 70+ COUNTRIES — RUSSIA (EX-PRESIDENT MEDVEDEV), PAKISTAN (PM SHARIF), IRAQ'S PRESIDENT AND PM, THE AFGHAN TALIBAN FM, PLUS HAMAS AND HEZBOLLAH — BUT NO HIGH-LEVEL GCC PRESENCE AND NO WESTERN INVITEES. A SUCCESSION QUESTION SHARPENED: THE WHEREABOUTS OF NEW SUPREME LEADER MOJTABA KHAMENEI (NAMED MARCH 9) REMAIN UNKNOWN — HE HAS NOT APPEARED PUBLICLY SINCE TAKING THE POST, FUELING QUESTIONS OVER WHO IS ACTUALLY RUNNING IRAN AND WHETHER HE WOULD ATTEND HIS FATHER'S FUNERAL. US-IRAN TALKS STAYED PAUSED FOR THE FUNERAL WEEK (NO NEW ROUND JULY 4-5). ON HORMUZ, IRAN'S DEPUTY FM ISSUED A 'SERIOUS WARNING' TO FRANCE AND THE UK OVER MILITARY MOVEMENTS IN THE STRAIT AND IRAN'S AMBASSADOR TO CHINA REITERATED TEHRAN WILL CHARGE 'SERVICE FEES' FOR TRANSIT ONCE THE 60-DAY PAUSE ENDS (MID-AUGUST), WHILE THE UK AND FRANCE PLEDGED TO WORK WITH OMAN ON 'SAFE TRANSIT FOR SHIPS OF ALL NATIONS' (A POTENTIAL MULTINATIONAL FREEDOM-OF-NAVIGATION MISSION, OMAN COOPERATING). OIL MARKETS WERE CLOSED (US INDEPENDENCE-DAY WEEKEND); BRENT'S LAST PRINT WAS ~$72/BBL (JULY 3), WITH SAUDI AND UAE EXPORTS BACK TO ~90% OF PRE-WAR. THE GROUND RUNG STAYED UNCHANGED AND DE-ESCALATORY ON THE US SIDE — NO US TROOPS IN IRAN, NO KHARG ASSAULT, NO NEW ARG/MEU ORDER, NO DRAFT; BLOCKADE STILL LIFTED (DAY 17). NO NEW STRIKES OR SHIPPING INCIDENTS WERE REPORTED JULY 4-5.",
   "summary": "The Iran war ran entirely on the ceremonial and diplomatic register through July 4-5, not the ground one. Iran's six-day state funeral for Ayatollah Ali Khamenei — killed in the Feb 28 US-Israeli opening strike and buried only now after repeated wartime postponement — continued into its second day: the coffin lay in state at Tehran's Grand Mosalla with mourners chanting 'revenge' and 'death to America' in roughly 100°F heat (authorities sprayed vaporized water on the crowds), ahead of July 5 funeral prayers and a procession in Qom (Fatima Masoumeh shrine and Jamkaran mosque), with the cortege due to reach Najaf and Karbala in Iraq on July 8 and burial at the Imam Reza shrine in Mashhad on July 9. Officials project 15-20 million mourners over the week, which would make it the largest state funeral in Iranian history, exceeding Khomeini's 1989 rites; delegations from more than 70 countries attended, including Russia (former president Dmitry Medvedev as Putin's special emissary), Pakistan (PM Shehbaz Sharif), Iraq's president and prime minister, the Afghan Taliban foreign minister, and Hamas and Hezbollah representatives, while no high-level GCC officials and no Western countries were invited. IRGC chief Ahmad Vahidi — who surfaced from hiding July 2 — said 'You will take to your grave the dream of seeing this nation surrender,' Tehran warned the US and Israel against any attack during the funeral, and US national security adviser Mike Waltz publicly disparaged the late leader. A succession question sharpened as CNN reported the whereabouts of the newly named Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei (designated March 9) remain unknown — he has not appeared publicly since taking the post, raising open questions over his health and who is actually running Iran. US-Iran talks stayed paused for the funeral week, to resume 'at the earliest possible time' afterward. On the Strait, Iran's deputy foreign minister issued a 'serious warning' to France and the UK over military movements in Hormuz and Iran's ambassador to China reiterated Tehran will levy 'service fees' on transit once the 60-day toll-free window ends in mid-August, while the UK and France pledged to work with Oman to ensure 'safe transit for ships of all nations,' with reports of a possible Franco-British-led multinational freedom-of-navigation mission and Oman agreeing to cooperate. Oil markets were closed for the US Independence Day weekend (last print Brent ~$72/bbl on July 3, near pre-war levels, with Saudi and UAE crude exports recovered to about 90% of the pre-war baseline). The ground-invasion rung stayed unchanged and de-escalatory on the US side — no US troops in Iran, no Kharg assault, no new ARG/MEU order, no draft; the naval blockade stays lifted (Day 17). No new strikes or shipping incidents were reported on July 4-5.",
   "details": [
    "KHAMENEI FUNERAL ENTERS SECOND DAY; QOM PROCESSION JULY 5 (July 4-5, 2026): The coffin of Ayatollah Ali Khamenei lay in state at Tehran's Grand Mosalla on July 4 as mourners chanted 'revenge, revenge' and 'death to America' in ~100°F heat; authorities sprayed vaporized water on the crowds. July 5 was set for funeral prayers and a procession in Qom (Fatima Masoumeh shrine and Jamkaran mosque), with Najaf and Karbala (Iraq) on July 8 and burial at the Imam Reza shrine in Mashhad on July 9. Officials project 15-20 million mourners over the six days — billed as the largest state funeral in Iranian history, exceeding Khomeini's 1989 rites (~10M). (CBS News; NPR; CNN; Wikipedia, July 4, 2026.)",
    "FOREIGN DELEGATIONS AND HARD-LINE RHETORIC (July 4, 2026): Delegations from 70+ countries attended, including Russia (ex-president Dmitry Medvedev), Pakistan (PM Shehbaz Sharif), Iraq's president and prime minister, the Afghan Taliban foreign minister, and Hamas and Hezbollah representatives; no high-level GCC officials and no Western nations were invited. IRGC chief Ahmad Vahidi (who resurfaced from hiding July 2) said 'You will take to your grave the dream of seeing this nation surrender'; an army commander vowed to 'avenge the blood' of the slain leader; Iran warned the US and Israel against any attack during the funeral; and US NSA Mike Waltz publicly disparaged the late leader. (CBS News; Euronews; Al Jazeera, July 3-4, 2026.)",
    "SUCCESSION QUESTION SHARPENS — NEW SUPREME LEADER'S WHEREABOUTS UNKNOWN (July 4, 2026): CNN reported that the whereabouts of Iran's new Supreme Leader, Mojtaba Khamenei — named to succeed his father on March 9 — remain unknown; he has not appeared publicly since taking the post, fueling questions over his health, whether he would attend the funeral, and who is actually running Iran. (CNN, 'Whereabouts of Iran's new supreme leader unknown,' July 4, 2026.)",
    "HORMUZ — IRAN WARNS FRANCE/UK, REAFFIRMS MID-AUGUST FEES; UK/FRANCE BACK OMAN (July 4, 2026): Iran's deputy foreign minister issued a 'serious warning' to France and the UK over military movements in the Strait, and Iran's ambassador to China reiterated Tehran will charge 'service fees' for Hormuz transit once the 60-day toll-free pause ends around mid-August. The UK and France pledged to work with Oman to ensure 'safe transit for ships of all nations,' with reports of a possible Franco-British-led multinational freedom-of-navigation mission and Oman agreeing to cooperate. US-Iran talks remained paused for the funeral week. (CBS News; GlobalSecurity/PressTV, July 3-4, 2026.)",
    "OIL MARKETS CLOSED; LAST PRINT NEAR PRE-WAR LEVELS (July 3-5, 2026): US commodity and financial markets were closed for the Independence Day weekend, so no fresh oil settlement printed on July 4-5. The last trade (Fri July 3) held Brent near $72/bbl (intraday ~$71.30-72.49), close to late-February pre-war levels, as Saudi crude exports rebounded to ~90% of the pre-war baseline alongside a UAE recovery and gradual Hormuz reopening. Hormuz tanker traffic continued its uneven recovery (43 transits July 1; well below the ~84/day pre-crisis baseline), with many vessels running with AIS off. (HDFCSky; TradingEconomics; Wikipedia/PortWatch, July 1-3, 2026.)",
    "HORMUZ-AFFECTED ECONOMIES (early July 2026): India's Russian-crude imports hit a record ~2.66M bpd in June and India began exporting gasoline to Russia amid Russia's own fuel shortage; European benchmark gas (TTF) hovered ~€43/MWh with EU storage ~48% full (below the five-year average) on US-Iran talk optimism; Japan and South Korea continued oil-supply cooperation and non-Gulf sourcing. No discrete July 4-5 escalation in these economies. (Al Jazeera; Reuters; Trading Economics, July 1-3, 2026.)"
