   "date": "2026-07-05",
   "headline": "RUSSIA-UKRAINE — JULY 4-5: KOSTIANTYNIVKA CONTROL CONTESTED. RUSSIA'S GENERAL-STAFF CHIEF GERASIMOV TOLD PUTIN (JULY 3) THAT RUSSIAN FORCES HAD 'LIBERATED' KOSTIANTYNIVKA — A KEY SLOVIANSK-KRAMATORSK-KOSTIANTYNIVKA DEFENSIVE HUB — AND PUTIN HAILED IT; ON JULY 4 ZELENSKY AND UKRAINE'S GENERAL STAFF FLATLY DENIED IT ('ANOTHER RUSSIAN LIE'), WITH ZELENSKY CHALLENGING PUTIN TO MEET HIM IN THE CITY. ISW ASSESSED NO CONFIRMED ADVANCES JULY 3 AND CONTINUES TO CHARACTERIZE GAINS AS SMALL-GROUP INFILTRATION, NOT CONSOLIDATED CONTROL. UKRAINE STRUCK THE ST. PETERSBURG OIL TERMINAL AND MILITARY TARGETS AT KRONSTADT (JULY 4, ~1,100 KM — ONE OF THE DEEPEST STRIKES YET ON PUTIN'S HOME CITY), PART OF A CAMPAIGN THAT HAS HIT 8 OF RUSSIA'S TOP 10 REFINERIES IN 2026. RUSSIA CONTINUED MASS STRIKES (OVERNIGHT ATTACKS INJURED ~23 IN KYIV; A JULY 4 DRONE HIT A POLTAVA GAS FACILITY), AFTER THE JULY 1-2 KYIV BARRAGE (74 MISSILES + 496 DRONES, 20-31 KILLED). A ~40-MINUTE ZELENSKY-TRUMP CALL (JULY 4) — 'THE BEST CONVERSATION IN ALL THIS TIME' — AGREED TO STRENGTHEN UKRAINE'S AIR DEFENSES AND EXPAND JOINT DRONE/WEAPONS PRODUCTION, DAYS AFTER A US DEFENSE DEPARTMENT PAUSE ON PATRIOT AND PRECISION-ARTILLERY SHIPMENTS. NO US GROUND-DEPLOYMENT SURGE.",
   "summary": "The Russia-Ukraine war stayed at a grinding, no-breakthrough tempo over July 4-5, with a sharp information contest over Kostiantynivka. On July 3 Russia's General Staff chief Gen. Valery Gerasimov reported to Putin — filmed at a command post — that Russian forces had 'liberated' Kostiantynivka, calling it one of the main defensive hubs of the Sloviansk-Kramatorsk-Kostiantynivka fortified area, and said forces were closing on Lyman; Putin hailed it as a strategic achievement and Russia's MoD posted flag-planting images. On July 4 President Zelensky and Ukraine's General Staff flatly denied the claim — 'It is just another Russian lie' — and Zelensky challenged Putin to meet him in Kostiantynivka if Russia truly held it. ISW assessed no confirmed advances on July 3 and continues to characterize Russian progress there as small-group infiltration (~50 m/day) rather than consolidated control; the Pokrovsk sector remained the heaviest, with Ukraine reporting 22 assaults repelled and 268 combat engagements along the front (July 3, per the General Staff), against Russian use of 1 missile, 93 airstrikes, 273 guided bombs and ~10,100 attack drones over the day. Ukraine extended its long-range energy-strike campaign, hitting the St. Petersburg Oil Terminal and military targets at Kronstadt on July 4 (~1,100 km, among the deepest strikes yet on Putin's home city) — part of a 2026 campaign Zelensky calls a '40-day blitz' that RBC-Ukraine says has struck 8 of Russia's top 10 refineries. Russia continued mass strikes on Ukraine: after the July 1-2 Kyiv barrage (74 missiles and 496 drones; 20-31 killed, 90-100+ injured), overnight attacks into July 4 injured at least 23 more in Kyiv and a July 4 Russian drone hit a Naftogaz gas-production facility in Poltava region. Diplomacy stayed stalled — no new round July 4-5, with Putin still insisting on the 2022 Istanbul Protocols — but a roughly 40-minute Zelensky-Trump call on July 4 (which Zelensky called 'the best conversation in all this time, extremely productive') agreed to strengthen Ukraine's air defenses and expand joint drone and weapons production, days after a US Defense Department pause on shipments of Patriot missiles and precision-guided artillery; Zelensky also spoke with Chancellor Merz, naming Patriot interceptors the top priority. Russia holds ~20% of Ukraine with no confirmed net change July 3-4; a widely cited think-tank estimate put total war casualties above 2 million, including as many as 600,000 deaths. The theatre continues to stress US bandwidth but triggered no US ground-deployment surge.",
   "details": [
    "KOSTIANTYNIVKA — CAPTURE CLAIMED AND DENIED (July 3-4, 2026): Gen. Gerasimov told Putin on July 3 that Russian forces had 'liberated' Kostiantynivka and were closing on Lyman; Russia's MoD posted flag images. On July 4 Zelensky and Ukraine's General Staff denied it as 'another Russian lie,' and Zelensky challenged Putin to meet him in the city. ISW assessed no confirmed advances July 3 and continues to describe gains as small-group infiltration, not consolidated control. (Reuters via Moscow Times/US News; Mezha; ISW/Kyiv Post, July 3-4, 2026.)",
    "FRONTLINE TEMPO (July 3-4, 2026): The Pokrovsk sector stayed heaviest — 22 Russian assaults repelled near Nykanorivka, Novooleksandrivka, Hryshyne, Rodynske, Kotlyne, Udachne and toward Myrnohrad. 268 combat engagements along the front (July 3, per the General Staff as of 08:00 July 4); Russia used 1 missile, 93 airstrikes, 273 guided bombs and ~10,106 attack drones. Clashes and a Russian airstrike also hit the Huliaipole/Zaporizhzhia direction. (Ukrinform; liveuamap, July 4, 2026.)",
    "UKRAINE STRIKES ST. PETERSBURG OIL TERMINAL AND KRONSTADT (July 4, 2026): A major Ukrainian long-range drone strike hit the St. Petersburg Oil Terminal (Great Port; one of Russia's largest fuel-storage/export facilities) and military targets at Kronstadt (~1,100 km from Ukraine) — one of the deepest strikes yet on Putin's home city. Zelensky confirmed the strike; RBC-Ukraine reports Ukraine has hit 8 of Russia's top 10 refineries in 2026, driving a Russian fuel crisis. (Kyiv Independent; Al Jazeera, July 4, 2026.)",
    "RUSSIAN MASS STRIKES CONTINUE (July 1-4, 2026): After the July 1-2 Kyiv barrage (74 missiles + 496 drones; 20-31 killed, 90-100+ injured, a nine-storey block partially collapsed), Putin vowed July 3 to 'continue' large-scale strikes; overnight attacks into July 4 injured at least 23 in Kyiv, and a July 4 Russian drone struck a Naftogaz gas-production facility in Poltava region, causing a fire. (Al Jazeera; Kyiv Independent; RFE/RL; Naftogaz, July 1-4, 2026.)",
    "ZELENSKY-TRUMP CALL; US PATRIOT PAUSE (July 4, 2026): A ~40-minute Zelensky-Trump call — which Zelensky called 'the best conversation in all this time, extremely productive' — agreed to strengthen Ukraine's air defenses, hold a teams-level meeting, and cooperate on joint weapons production, drone tech and mutual investment. It followed a US Defense Department pause on shipments of Patriot missiles and precision-guided artillery to Ukraine; Zelensky also spoke with Chancellor Merz, naming Patriot interceptors the top priority. No US ground-deployment surge occurred. (Kyiv Independent; Newsweek; Kyiv Post, July 4, 2026.)"
   ],
   "source": "Reuters via Moscow Times/US News/Arab News, 'Zelensky Denies Russian Capture of Key Eastern City Kostiantynivka,' July 4, 2026; Mezha, 'Russian forces claim they captured Kostiantynivka as Gerasimov reports to Putin,' July 3, 2026; ISW/Critical Threats, July 3, 2026; Ukrinform, 'War update: 268 combat engagements,' July 4, 2026; Kyiv Independent, 'Zelensky confirms Ukrainian strike on St. Petersburg oil infrastructure,' July 4, 2026; Al Jazeera, 'Ukraine hits oil and military facilities near Russia's St Petersburg,' July 4, 2026; Kyiv Independent, 'Zelensky, Trump discuss air defense, joint drone production,' July 4, 2026; Newsweek; RFE/RL; Naftogaz, July 1-4, 2026.",
