   "date": "2026-07-05",
   "headline": "EAST ASIA — JULY 3-5: NORTH KOREA'S KIM JONG UN OBSERVED TEST-FIRING OF A STRATEGIC CRUISE MISSILE PLUS ANTI-SHIP, ANTI-SUBMARINE AND AIR-DEFENSE EVALUATIONS ABOARD THE NEW NAVAL DESTROYER KANG KON (CONDUCTED JULY 3, REPORTED BY KCNA OVER THE WEEKEND), CALLING FOR EXPANDED 'WAR DETERRENCE.' US-CHINA: JULY 3 THE US LIFTED RESTRICTIONS ON CHIP-DESIGN (EDA) SOFTWARE AND ETHANE EXPORTS TO CHINA IN A DE-ESCALATORY STEP UNDER THE JUNE 11 TARIFF TRUCE; JULY 4 THE ADMINISTRATION MOVED TO RESTRICT AI-CHIP SHIPMENTS TO THAILAND AND MALAYSIA OVER CHINA-SMUGGLING CONCERNS. TAIWAN: NO NEW JULY 4-5 MILITARY EVENT (LATEST WAS THE JULY 1 STAND-UP OF A LITTORAL COMBAT COMMAND).",
   "summary": "In East Asia, North Korea's Kim Jong Un observed test-firing of a strategic cruise missile along with anti-ship, anti-submarine and air-defense system evaluations aboard the new destroyer Kang Kon — tests conducted Friday July 3 as an assessment of the warship's combat systems and reported by state media (KCNA) over the July 4-5 weekend; Kim called for expanded 'war deterrence,' extending a run that included June 25 ballistic-missile-warhead and MLRS tests. On US-China, the two developments cut in opposite directions inside the June 11 tariff truce (which froze tariffs at 30% and paused higher rates for 60 days): on July 3 the US lifted restrictions on chip-design (EDA) software and ethane exports to China, a de-escalatory step, while on July 4 the administration moved to restrict AI-chip shipments to Thailand and Malaysia over concerns about smuggling into China. Taiwan saw no new July 4-5 military event; the most recent development was the July 1 commissioning of a new Littoral Combat Command, and analysts continue to assess a 2026 PRC blockade or invasion as unlikely. None of these developments bears directly on the Iran ground-invasion track, but they sustain the multi-theatre strain on US attention that the corpus tracks.",
   "details": [
    "DPRK DESTROYER WEAPONS TESTS (July 3, reported July 4-5, 2026): Kim Jong Un observed test-firing of a strategic cruise missile plus anti-ship, anti-submarine and air-defense evaluations aboard the new destroyer Kang Kon, assessing target-detection, integrated firepower, naval guns and electronic warfare; he called for expanded 'war deterrence.' Follows June 25 ballistic-missile-warhead and MLRS tests. (Reuters via WHBL/KCNA, July 4, 2026.)",
    "US-CHINA — MIXED TRADE SIGNALS (July 3-4, 2026): July 3 — the US lifted restrictions on chip-design (EDA) software and ethane exports to China, easing tensions under the June 11 truce (tariffs frozen at 30%, higher rates paused 60 days; Section 122 10% tariff expires July 24). July 4 — the administration moved to restrict AI-chip shipments to Thailand and Malaysia over concerns about diversion/smuggling into China. (Tax Foundation Tariff Tracker; China-Briefing/Dimerco, July 3-4, 2026.)",
    "TAIWAN — NO NEW JULY 4-5 MILITARY EVENT: The latest development was the July 1 commissioning of a new Littoral Combat Command (integrating US/Taiwan missile systems and an unmanned-surface-vessel unit); interdepartmental blockade-escort drills are planned for July. Analysts assess a 2026 blockade/invasion as unlikely. (AEI 'China & Taiwan Update,' July 1-2, 2026.)"
   ],
   "source": "Reuters via WHBL, 'North Korea's Kim observed naval destroyer cruise missile launch, weapons tests, state media says,' July 4, 2026; Tax Foundation, 'Tariff Tracker: 2026 Trump Tariffs'; China-Briefing / Dimerco US Tariff Update 2026, July 3-4, 2026; AEI, 'China & Taiwan Update, July 2, 2026.'",
