  {
   "date": "2026-07-04",
   "headline": "DAY 128 — CEASEFIRE DAY 88 / US BLOCKADE LIFTED DAY 16 — THE WAR MOVED FULLY OFF THE KINETIC TRACK AND INTO TWO PARALLEL PROCESSES: A PAUSED US-IRAN NEGOTIATION AND A SIX-DAY STATE FUNERAL FOR SLAIN SUPREME LEADER KHAMENEI. THE JULY 1-2 DOHA ROUND (INDIRECT, VIA QATAR AND PAKISTAN) CONCLUDED WITH 'POSITIVE PROGRESS' BUT NO BREAKTHROUGH — TANGIBLE IMPLEMENTATION STEPS (A GOODS-PURCHASE MECHANISM FOR THE FIRST TRANCHE OF FROZEN ASSETS, A VIOLATIONS-DECONFLICTION COMMS CHANNEL, AN AGREED FOCUS ON RESTORING HORMUZ PASSAGE) BUT THE CORE DISPUTES UNRESOLVED: WASHINGTON REJECTS ANY IRANIAN-LED TOLL MECHANISM WHILE TEHRAN ASSERTS JOINT SOVEREIGNTY OVER THE CORRIDOR WITH OMAN AND SAYS IT WILL IMPOSE TOLLS FROM MID-AUGUST; NO FROZEN FUNDS ACTUALLY MOVED; THE NUCLEAR-INSPECTIONS DISPUTE PERSISTS (SPEAKER GHALIBAF DENIED IAEA ACCESS TO THE BOMBED FORDOW/NATANZ/ISFAHAN SITES, OFFERING ONLY BUSHEHR AND TEHRAN). THE US AGREED TO HALT TALKS FOR A WEEK; THE NEXT ROUND IS POSTPONED UNTIL AFTER THE FUNERAL. ON JULY 4 IRAN OPENED A SIX-DAY STATE FUNERAL FOR AYATOLLAH ALI KHAMENEI (KILLED FEB 28) — LYING IN STATE AT TEHRAN'S GRAND MOSALLA JULY 4-5, A TEHRAN PROCESSION JULY 6, QOM JULY 7, NAJAF/KARBALA (IRAQ) JULY 8, AND BURIAL IN MASHHAD JULY 9 — EXPECTED TO DRAW 15-20 MILLION MOURNERS (THE LARGEST STATE FUNERAL IN IRANIAN HISTORY), WITH DELEGATIONS FROM ~30 COUNTRIES INCLUDING RUSSIA (MEDVEDEV AS PUTIN'S 'SPECIAL EMISSARY') AND CHINA. IRAN'S JOINT MILITARY COMMAND WARNED ALL TANKERS MUST USE ITS APPROVED HORMUZ ROUTES OR FACE A 'FORCEFUL RESPONSE'; GENERAL AHMAD VAHIDI: 'YOU WILL TAKE TO YOUR GRAVE THE DREAM OF SEEING THIS NATION SURRENDER.' OIL EASED TO A FOUR-MONTH LOW (BRENT ~$71.5/BBL, TRUMP: 'THEY'VE COME A LONG WAY'). THE GROUND RUNG WAS UNCHANGED AND DE-ESCALATORY ON THE US SIDE — NO US TROOPS IN IRAN, NO KHARG ASSAULT, NO NEW ARG/MEU ORDER, NO DRAFT; BLOCKADE STILL LIFTED (DAY 16), USS BOXER ARG DISAGGREGATED. THE WEEK'S ONLY US MILITARY EVENT WAS A NON-COMBAT MISHAP — A US NAVY HELICOPTER MADE AN EMERGENCY WATER LANDING IN THE ARABIAN SEA WITH ONE CREW MEMBER MISSING.",
   "summary": "The Iran war on July 1-4 ran entirely on the diplomatic and ceremonial register, not the ground one. The July 1-2 US-Iran round in Doha (indirect, mediated by Qatar and Pakistan; Witkoff and Kushner present on the US side) concluded with Qatar reporting 'positive progress' but no breakthrough toward lasting peace. Negotiators produced implementation mechanics from the June 17 MOU — a goods-purchase channel for the first tranche of frozen Iranian assets (reports of ~$3B for humanitarian goods; US officials denied any final understanding was reached), a communications channel to flag and address ceasefire violations, and an agreed focus on restoring reliable Hormuz passage — while the core disputes stayed open: the US rejects any Iranian-led toll regime and Tehran asserts joint Iran-Oman sovereignty over the corridor and plans tolls from mid-August; no frozen funds actually moved; and the nuclear-inspections dispute persists, with Speaker Ghalibaf denying IAEA access to the US-bombed Fordow, Natanz and Isfahan sites (offering only Bushehr and Tehran). Trump said 'denuclearisation of Iran is moving along well' and 'I think they've come a long way'; VP Vance said nuclear issues would be 'addressed later' and warned that if Iran tries to rebuild its nuclear program 'that's going to change our calculus.' The US agreed to halt talks for a week, postponing the next round until after the funeral. On July 4 Iran opened a six-day state funeral for Ayatollah Ali Khamenei — killed in the Feb 28 US-Israeli opening strike and buried only now after repeated wartime postponement — lying in state at Tehran's Grand Mosalla (July 4-5) ahead of a Tehran procession (July 6), Qom (July 7), Najaf and Karbala in Iraq (July 8) and burial in Mashhad (July 9); officials expect 15-20 million mourners, the largest state funeral in Iranian history, with delegations from about 30 countries including Russia (Dmitry Medvedev as Putin's 'special emissary') and China. Iran's joint military command warned all oil tankers must follow Tehran-designated Hormuz routes or face a 'forceful response,' and a foreign container ship reportedly ran aground outside the Iranian-designated corridor. Oil fell to a four-month low (Brent about $71.5/bbl on July 3, down ~0.4%), far below the wartime peaks above $110 and the $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. The ground-invasion rung stayed unchanged and de-escalatory on the US side — no US troops in Iran, no Kharg assault, no new ARG/MEU order, no draft; the naval blockade stays lifted (Day 16) and the USS Boxer ARG stays disaggregated. The only US military event of the week was a non-combat mishap: a US Navy helicopter made an emergency water landing in the Arabian Sea, with one crew member reported missing.",
   "details": [
    "DOHA ROUND CONCLUDES WITH 'POSITIVE PROGRESS,' NO BREAKTHROUGH (July 1-2, 2026): The US-Iran round in Doha — held indirectly through Qatari and Pakistani mediators, with US envoy Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner present — concluded July 2. VP JD Vance said on July 1 the talks were 'going well.' Qatar's spokesman reported 'positive progress' on technical matters; negotiators focused on Hormuz maritime traffic and unfreezing Iranian funds — issues both sides had previously indicated were largely resolved — rather than the core disagreements, yielding no apparent breakthrough toward lasting peace. (CNN; Al Jazeera; The National, July 1-2, 2026.)",
    "IMPLEMENTATION STEPS AGREED, CORE DISPUTES UNRESOLVED (July 2, 2026): The round produced a goods-purchase mechanism for the first tranche of frozen assets (reports of a ~$3B humanitarian-goods arrangement; US officials denied any understanding was finalized), a communications channel to flag and address ceasefire violations, and an agreed focus on restoring reliable Hormuz passage. But the sessions stayed indirect throughout; the toll dispute is unresolved (Washington rejects any Iranian-led fee mechanism, Tehran asserts joint sovereignty over the corridor with Oman); no frozen funds actually moved; and the nuclear-inspection dispute persists. (Al Jazeera; The National, July 2, 2026.)",
    "NUCLEAR-INSPECTIONS STANDOFF PERSISTS (July 2, 2026): Speaker Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf denied IAEA access to the US-bombed nuclear sites (Fordow, Natanz, Isfahan), saying inspectors would reach only Bushehr and Tehran facilities. Trump said 'denuclearisation of Iran is moving along well' and later 'I think they've come a long way'; sources said nuclear matters were absent from the technical discussions, with VP Vance saying the issue would be 'addressed later' and warning that if Iran tries to rebuild its nuclear program 'that's going to change our calculus.' (Al Jazeera; The Bridge Chronicle, July 2, 2026.)",
    "US HALTS TALKS FOR A WEEK; NEXT ROUND AFTER FUNERAL (July 3, 2026): Mediators Qatar and Pakistan said the next negotiation round would resume 'at the earliest possible time' after Khamenei's funeral ceremonies conclude; the US agreed to pause talks for roughly a week. No specific date for the resumption was set. (Fox News Digital; CBS News, July 3, 2026.)",
    "IRAN OPENS SIX-DAY STATE FUNERAL FOR KHAMENEI (July 3-4, 2026): Ayatollah Ali Khamenei's coffin was placed on display at Tehran's Grand Mosalla on July 3 ahead of a six-day funeral beginning July 4 — lying in state in Tehran July 4-5, a Tehran procession July 6, ceremonies in Qom July 7, processions in Najaf and Karbala (Iraq) July 8, and burial at the Imam Reza shrine in Mashhad on July 9. Officials expect 15-20 million mourners, which would make it the largest state funeral in Iranian history; First VP Mohammad Reza Aref called it 'the most important event of this century.' The funeral had been postponed repeatedly since early March because of the war. (Al Jazeera; TIME; CNN, July 3, 2026.)",
    "FOREIGN DELEGATIONS AND HARD-LINE RHETORIC (July 3-4, 2026): Delegations from about 30 countries were expected, including Russia — former president Dmitry Medvedev attending as Vladimir Putin's 'special emissary' — and China, plus Palestinian and Lebanese militant-group representatives. Iranian General Ahmad Vahidi said 'You will take to your grave the dream of seeing this nation surrender'; Tehran warned of 'harsh retaliation' against the US as the funeral opened, while US national security adviser Mike Waltz publicly disparaged the late leader. (CBS News; Fox News Digital, July 3, 2026.)",
    "IRAN ENFORCES HORMUZ ROUTES; TRAFFIC RECOVERS (July 2-3, 2026): Iran's joint military command warned that all oil tankers moving through the Strait of Hormuz must use its approved routes or face a 'forceful response,' and Iranian state media reported a foreign container ship ran aground in shallow water outside the Iran-designated corridor. Traffic kept recovering — 258 vessels transited in the prior week, up from 138 the week before, still far below the pre-war ~130/day — as major container lines resumed Gulf services. Iran reiterated it will impose transit tolls from mid-August once the MOU's toll-free window expires. (CBS News; Gulf News; The Bridge Chronicle, July 2-3, 2026.)",
    "OIL AT FOUR-MONTH LOW; NON-COMBAT US NAVY MISHAP (July 2-3, 2026): Brent crude extended its decline, sliding to about $71.54/bbl on July 3 (-0.36%) — a four-month low — as easing Middle East tensions and the gradual Hormuz reopening reduced supply-disruption fears; Trump's remarks that Iran had 'come a long way' contributed to the drop. Separately, a US Navy helicopter made an emergency water landing in the Arabian Sea, with one crew member reported missing — a non-combat mishap, not a ground-operation indicator. Over 640,000 displaced Lebanese have returned home amid the ceasefire. (TradingEconomics/CNBC; Gulf News; CBS News, July 2-3, 2026.)"
   ],
