   "prediction_impact": "July 1-4 keeps Predictive History's imminent-US-ground-invasion and Hormuz-and-Kharg-seizure thesis disconfirmed-and-inverted, and adds a new register: the war has moved off the kinetic track entirely and into a paused negotiation plus a six-day state funeral. The Doha round advanced MOU implementation mechanics (a frozen-asset goods-purchase channel, a violations-deconfliction line, a Hormuz-passage focus) without a US landing, a Kharg assault, an ARG/MEU surge, or a draft; the blockade stayed lifted (Day 16) and the USS Boxer ARG stayed disaggregated. The structural inversion holds: the chokepoint is being reopened and governed by negotiation (US-Iran comms channel; Iran-Oman corridor claim; toll dispute), not US seizure. The deal-durability open question (iran-us-deal-reached-june-2026) stays live — the round produced 'positive progress' but no breakthrough, the talks are only paused for the funeral week, and the toll and nuclear-inspection disputes are unresolved — and the Hormuz-governance question (iran-hormuz-administration-tolls-dispute-june-2026) is unresolved, with Iran now setting a mid-August toll deadline the US rejects. The one US military event, a Navy helicopter's emergency water landing, is a non-combat mishap, not a ground indicator. Any renewed collapse would return the war to the air/missile/maritime register, not vindicate a ground invasion.",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "The July 1-2 Doha round reads two ways from the same facts. One reading treats the 'positive progress,' the goods-purchase mechanism, the violations-deconfliction channel and the Hormuz-passage focus as real forward motion on the MOU — the ceasefire holding, both sides institutionalizing de-escalation and deferring the hard questions to a next round. The other reading treats it as a stall dressed as progress: the sessions were indirect throughout, negotiators revisited issues 'both sides had previously indicated were largely resolved' rather than the core disputes, no frozen funds actually moved, the toll and nuclear-inspection disputes are openly unresolved, Iran set a mid-August toll deadline the US flatly rejects, and the round ended with talks merely paused for a funeral week rather than scheduled to reconvene.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Both readings rest on the same on-record facts: Qatar's 'positive progress' statement; the reported goods-purchase mechanism, comms channel and Hormuz-passage focus; the US denial that any frozen-asset understanding was finalized; Ghalibaf's denial of IAEA access to the bombed sites; Iran's mid-August toll assertion and the US rejection of any Iranian-led toll regime; and the agreement to halt talks for a week with no fixed resumption date. Which reading prevails depends on whether the next round convenes after the funeral, whether the goods-purchase/asset tranche actually moves, and whether the toll and inspection disputes are bridged — none resolvable from open sources today.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "Qatar (mediator spokesman): the talks made 'positive progress' and further discussions will be scheduled 'at the earliest possible time' after the funeral.",
      "US (Trump; VP Vance): 'denuclearisation of Iran is moving along well,' 'I think they've come a long way,' talks are 'going well' — while US officials denied any frozen-asset understanding was finalized and Vance said nuclear issues would be 'addressed later.'",
      "Iran (Speaker Ghalibaf; joint military command; Gen. Vahidi): no IAEA access to the bombed sites; tankers must use Iran-approved Hormuz routes or face a 'forceful response'; tolls to be imposed from mid-August; 'you will take to your grave the dream of seeing this nation surrender.'"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "It is not yet known whether the post-funeral round will convene, whether the first frozen-asset tranche and goods-purchase channel will actually execute, or whether the toll and nuclear-inspection disputes will be bridged — all forward-looking and undeterminable from open sources today.",
     "open_question_ref": "iran-us-deal-reached-june-2026"
    }
   ]
