   "headline": "DAY 123 GROUND-INVASION TRACKER — STATUS HOLDS no_ground_troops. THE BREACH-RETALIATION CYCLE ENTERED A THIRD DAY AND BROADENED ON JUNE 28 — CENTCOM STRUCK 10 IRANIAN TARGETS NEAR HORMUZ AND IRAN'S IRGC RETALIATED WITH BALLISTIC MISSILES AND DRONES AGAINST US BASES IN KUWAIT (ALI AL SALEM) AND BAHRAIN (FIFTH FLEET HQ) — STILL AN AIR/MISSILE-REGISTER EXCHANGE, NOT A GROUND OPERATION. ALL IRANIAN WEAPONS WERE INTERCEPTED OR FAILED (NO US INJURIES); ONE QATARI CIVILIAN WAS KILLED BY SHRAPNEL. EVERY US GROUND-EXECUTION INDICATOR STAYED AT ZERO: NO US TROOPS IN IRAN, NO KHARG ASSAULT, NO NEW ARG/MEU ORDER, NO DRAFT; BLOCKADE LIFTED (DAY 11), USS BOXER ARG DISAGGREGATED.",
   "summary": "JUNE 29 (Day 123): The ground-invasion EXECUTION track produced no new indicator even as the conflict's breach-retaliation cycle entered a third straight day and broadened qualitatively on June 28. US Central Command said it struck 10 Iranian military targets in and near the Strait of Hormuz (reported sites Sirik, Bandar-e Lengeh, Qeshm Island) in response to the June 27 M/T Kiku drone attack; Iran's IRGC retaliated with ballistic missiles and drones against US bases in the Gulf — Ali Al Salem airbase in Kuwait and the US Fifth Fleet HQ (Port Salman) in Bahrain — the first Iranian strikes on US bases since June 10-11. A US official said all Iranian weapons were 'shot down, intercepted, or failed to reach their intended targets' with 'no US injuries,' though one Qatari civilian was killed by shrapnel. Critically for this tracker, the entire exchange ran on the air/missile/maritime rung: the US instrument was airstrikes from existing in-theatre assets, Iran's was missiles/drones from its own territory, and neither was a ground move. It remains the structural inverse of the predicted US seizure of the Strait — Iran (not the US) is using force to assert coercive control over Hormuz ('total oversight and management,' per Araghchi) while the US keeps the chokepoint open. Every US ground-execution indicator stayed at zero: no US troops in Iran, no Kharg Island assault, no new ARG/MEU order, no draft; the naval blockade stayed lifted (Day 11) and the USS Boxer ARG stayed disaggregated. The live risk is deal durability — now sharply elevated by the cycle's third-day persistence, its broadening to US bases, a Gulf-state civilian fatality, an IRGC 'complete halt' threat and an Iranian talks boycott — but in the air/missile/maritime register, not a forming ground invasion. (Al Jazeera; RFE/RL; NPR; ABC News; CBS News, June 28, 2026.)",
   "details": [
    "US RESPONSE WAS AGAIN AIR, NOT GROUND (June 28, 2026): CENTCOM's response to the M/T Kiku attack was a 10-target air/naval strike package near the Strait of Hormuz (surveillance, communications, air-defense, drone storage and minelayer capabilities), not a landing or a Kharg assault. No US ground forces entered Iran. (Al Jazeera; NPR; CBS News, June 28, 2026.)",
    "IRAN'S ESCALATION WAS ALSO AIR/MISSILE, FROM ITS OWN TERRITORY (June 28, 2026): Iran's broadening of the cycle — ballistic missiles and drones at US bases in Kuwait and Bahrain — was a stand-off strike from Iranian soil, not a ground move, and was wholly intercepted/failed per the US. The escalation was vertical (more targets, US bases) but stayed off the ground rung. (Al Jazeera; ABC News, June 28, 2026.)",
    "STRUCTURAL INVERSION HARDENS (June 28, 2026): Iran reasserted that the Strait is under its 'total oversight and management' (Araghchi) and struck US Gulf bases, while the US kept Hormuz open, the blockade lifted (Day 11), and Gulf exports near ~75% of pre-war. That is the inverse of a forcible US seizure of Hormuz and Kharg. (RFE/RL; Al Jazeera, June 28, 2026.)",
    "KHARG / ARG / DRAFT ALL UNCHANGED (June 29, 2026): No Kharg Island assault ordered or executed; no third ARG and no new MEU surge; the USS Boxer ARG / 11th MEU stays disaggregated and the USS Tripoli ARG / 31st MEU stays in a routine forward posture. No Selective Service activation or mobilization order. The standing items remain unchanged from prior days. (Al Jazeera; ABC News; CBS News, June 28, 2026.)"
