   "prediction_impact": "The June 27 repeat of the breach-retaliation cycle is the sharpest sign yet that the ceasefire is fragile — but it again ran entirely on the air/maritime rung, leaving Predictive History's imminent-US-ground-invasion and Hormuz-and-Kharg-seizure thesis disconfirmed-and-inverted. Both US responses to Iran's drone attacks were airstrikes on storage, radar and air-defense targets (the same instrument used in the early-June flare-ups), not a landing, a Kharg assault, an ARG/MEU surge, or a draft. The structural inversion holds and hardens: Iran (not the US) is using force to assert coercive control over the Strait — firing on a second tanker, threatening a 'broader response,' menacing Bahrain's airspace — while the US keeps the chokepoint open (JMIC widened the route), the blockade lifted, and Gulf exports climbing to ~75% of pre-war, the opposite of a forcible US seizure of Hormuz and Kharg. The episode meaningfully raises deal-durability risk: this is now a repeating, two-day breach-retaliation cycle, which strengthens the 'fragile ceasefire could re-collapse' reading of the deal-durability open question (iran-us-deal-reached-june-2026) and the 'genuine, hardening sovereignty claim' reading of the Hormuz-governance open question (iran-hormuz-administration-tolls-dispute-june-2026). But a collapse, if it came, would return the war to the air/missile/maritime register — not vindicate a ground invasion.",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "The June 27 second strike is read two ways from the same facts. One reading treats the two-day exchange as a contained, proportionate enforcement pattern inside an otherwise-holding 60-day process — each Iranian strike on shipping drawing a discrete, escalating-but-bounded US air response on military targets, with the Strait still open (JMIC even widening the route), the blockade still lifted, oil falling to a four-month low, Gulf exports climbing, and both sides keeping the MOU technically in force while blaming the other. The other reading treats it as a hardening breach-retaliation cycle that is sliding the ceasefire back toward open conflict: a second tanker hit, a US strike explicitly 'larger than last night,' Iran's 'swift and decisive' and 'broader response' warnings, an Iranian drone over Bahrain, Hezbollah declaring the Lebanon framework 'null and void,' and the cycle now repeating day-on-day rather than resolving.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Both readings draw on the same on-record facts (the June 27 M/T Kiku drone strike; the second, larger US airstrike per CENTCOM; the Rezaei and IRGC Navy warnings; the Bahrain sovereignty complaint; the GCC condemnation; the oil move; Hezbollah's rejection; the MOU still nominally in force). Which prevails depends on whether the cycle repeats a third time, whether Iran makes good on its 'broader response' threat, and what the next Geneva technical round produces — not resolvable from open sources today.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "US (CENTCOM; Trump; VP Vance): the strikes were a justified, escalating 'larger' response to Iran's repeated attacks on shipping; the US has 'honored' the ceasefire and forces remain 'vigilant, lethal, and ready.'",
