   "headline": "DAY 121 — CEASEFIRE DAY 81 / US BLOCKADE LIFTED DAY 9 — THE FIRST US KINETIC STRIKES ON IRAN SINCE THE JUNE 17 MOU. AFTER IRAN FIRED AT LEAST FOUR ONE-WAY ATTACK DRONES AT SHIPS IN THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ ON JUNE 25 — ONE HITTING THE UPPER DECK OF THE SINGAPORE-FLAGGED CARGO SHIP M/V EVER LOVELY (EVERGREEN MARINE; BRIDGE WINDOWS DAMAGED, NO INJURIES) WHILE US FORCES DOWNED THE OTHER THREE — THE US LAUNCHED RETALIATORY AIRSTRIKES ON JUNE 26: CENTCOM SAID IT STRUCK IRANIAN MISSILE/DRONE STORAGE SITES, COASTAL RADAR AND QESHM ISLAND IN A 'POWERFUL RESPONSE.' TRUMP CALLED IRAN'S ATTACK A 'FOOLISH VIOLATION' OF THE CEASEFIRE; VP VANCE SAID 'VIOLENCE WILL BE MET WITH VIOLENCE.' THE IRGC TURNED BACK THREE FOREIGN TANKERS FOR CROSSING WITHOUT IRANIAN COORDINATION, AND IRAN SIGNALED IT WILL RESUME HORMUZ TRANSIT FEES AFTER THE 60-DAY WINDOW. SEPARATELY, RUBIO ANNOUNCED AN ISRAEL-LEBANON FRAMEWORK AGREEMENT (HEZBOLLAH EXCLUDED; A LAWMAKER WARNED OF 'CIVIL WAR'). OIL EASED — BRENT FELL ~2% TO ~$74/BBL AFTER SPIKING ~4%. DESPITE THE KINETIC FLARE-UP THE GROUND RUNG WAS UNCHANGED: THE US RESPONSE WAS AIR, NOT GROUND — NO US TROOPS IN IRAN, NO KHARG ASSAULT, NO NEW ARG/MEU, NO DRAFT, BLOCKADE STILL LIFTED, USS BOXER ARG DISAGGREGATED.",
   "summary": "JUNE 27 (Day 121): The maritime re-escalation became a two-sided kinetic exchange — the first US strikes on Iran since the June 17 MOU — without crossing onto the ground rung. The June 25 incident was identified and widened: Iran's IRGC fired at least four one-way attack drones at vessels transiting the Strait of Hormuz; one struck the upper deck of the Singapore-flagged cargo ship M/V Ever Lovely (owned by Evergreen Marine), damaging bridge windows with no injuries and the cargo safe, while US forces shot down the other three. On June 26 the US retaliated: US Central Command conducted airstrikes that, per CENTCOM and a senior defense official, hit Iranian missile and drone storage facilities, coastal radar sites and locations on and near Qeshm Island and the Strait — a 'powerful response' to Iran's 'dangerous behavior.' President Trump called Iran's drone attack a 'foolish violation' of the ceasefire ('One of the Drones solidly hit the upper deck of a large and very expensive Cargo Carrying Ship'... 'We knocked down three other Drones'), and when asked about consequences said 'You'll find out'; VP JD Vance declared 'Violence will be met with violence,' adding 'Iran signed a ceasefire agreement. We have honored it.' The IRGC separately forced three foreign oil tankers to turn around for attempting to cross 'without coordinating with Iranian authorities,' and Iranian negotiator Hossein Ghorbanzadeh said Tehran intends to resume collecting Strait fees once the 60-day suspension expires. The 60-day MOU remained technically in place but visibly strained. On the Lebanon track, Secretary of State Marco Rubio announced an Israel-Lebanon framework agreement aimed at 'lasting peace and security' — signed by the Israeli and Lebanese ambassadors to Washington and State Department counselor Dan Holler, contingent on a full Hezbollah cessation of fire and evacuation south of the Litani, with two 'pilot projects' of Israeli withdrawal and Lebanese army deployment; Hezbollah was not a party and a senior Hezbollah-affiliated lawmaker, Hassan Fadlallah, condemned it and warned enforcement would mean 'civil war,' while Netanyahu called the framework 'a major blow to Iran.' Oil eased despite the strikes: Brent fell more than 2% Friday after rising as much as 4%, trading around $74/bbl (TradingEconomics: $74.43, -1.11%) — still only ~2% above pre-conflict levels and far below the sustained $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. Hormuz traffic held a war-period high (70 vessels June 25, the most in a day since March 1) even as the UN IMO kept its shepherding program paused. The ground rung was unchanged: the US answered Iran's maritime strike with air power, not ground forces — no US troops in Iran, no Kharg assault, no new ARG/MEU order, no draft; the naval blockade stayed lifted (Day 9) and the USS Boxer ARG stayed disaggregated. (Fox News; NBC News; CBS News; CNBC; Al Jazeera; CNN; Axios; Euronews, June 25-26, 2026.)",
   "details": [
    "US LAUNCHES AIRSTRIKES ON IRAN — FIRST SINCE THE MOU (June 26, 2026): US Central Command conducted airstrikes on Iran in response to the June 25 drone attack on shipping, striking Iranian missile and drone storage facilities, coastal radar sites and locations on and near Qeshm Island and along the Strait of Hormuz; CENTCOM called it a 'powerful response' to Iran's 'dangerous behavior' and a senior defense official said all strikes were complete. These were the first US kinetic strikes on Iran since the June 17 MOU — an air/naval-register response, not a ground operation. (NBC News; CBS News; Fox News, June 26, 2026.)",
    "THE JUNE 25 ATTACK, IDENTIFIED (June 25, 2026): Iran's IRGC fired at least four one-way attack drones at vessels in the Strait of Hormuz; one hit the upper deck of the Singapore-flagged cargo ship M/V Ever Lovely (owner Evergreen Marine), damaging bridge windows with no injuries and the cargo intact, while US forces downed the other three. Evergreen Marine confirmed the damage. (NBC News; CNN, June 25, 2026.)",
    "TRUMP AND VANCE — 'FOOLISH VIOLATION' / 'VIOLENCE WILL BE MET WITH VIOLENCE' (June 25-26, 2026): Trump called the drone attack a 'foolish violation' of the ceasefire — 'One of the Drones solidly hit the upper deck of a large and very expensive Cargo Carrying Ship'... 'We knocked down three other Drones' — and, asked about consequences, said 'You'll find out.' VP Vance said 'Violence will be met with violence,' adding 'Iran signed a ceasefire agreement. We have honored it.' (CBS News; Fox News, June 26, 2026.)",
    "IRGC TURNS BACK THREE TANKERS; IRAN SIGNALS FEES WILL RESUME (June 26, 2026): The IRGC forced three foreign oil tankers to turn around for attempting to cross the Strait 'without coordinating with Iranian authorities,' and Iran's Persian Gulf Strait Authority warned that 'consequences arising from passage through unauthorized routes shall be the responsibility of the owner, operator, and vessel commander.' Iranian negotiator Hossein Ghorbanzadeh said Tehran intends to resume collecting transit fees after the 60-day suspension expires, having accepted the pause only 'so that the agreement could be reached.' (Fox News; Al Jazeera, June 26, 2026.)",
    "ISRAEL-LEBANON FRAMEWORK AGREEMENT (June 26, 2026): Secretary of State Rubio announced a US-mediated Israel-Lebanon framework agreement aimed at 'lasting peace and security,' signed by the Israeli and Lebanese ambassadors to Washington and State Department counselor Dan Holler and called a 'first step' toward peace. It is contingent on a complete Hezbollah cessation of fire and evacuation of operatives from the South Litani Sector, with two 'pilot projects' in which the IDF withdraws from small occupied areas and the Lebanese army deploys. Hezbollah was not a party; senior Hezbollah-affiliated lawmaker Hassan Fadlallah condemned it and warned enforcement would require a 'civil war,' while Netanyahu called it 'a major blow to Iran.' (CNBC; Axios; Al Jazeera; Euronews, June 26, 2026.)",
    "OIL EASES DESPITE THE STRIKES (June 26, 2026): Brent fell more than 2% Friday after rising as much as 4%, trading around $74/bbl (TradingEconomics: $74.43, -1.11%; Al Jazeera cited August Brent ~$73.85), as investors weighed rising Hormuz shipping activity against the strikes; Treasury Secretary Bessent said crude had fallen below pre-conflict levels and gasoline prices would ease. Brent is down ~19% over the month and only ~2% above pre-conflict levels — far below the sustained $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. (Al Jazeera Economy; TradingEconomics, June 26, 2026.)",
    "GROUND RUNG (June 27, 2026): Unchanged despite the two-sided kinetic flare-up. The US answered Iran's maritime drone strike with airstrikes on missile/drone storage and radar — an air/naval-register response, not a ground operation. There were no US troops in Iran, no Kharg Island assault, no new ARG/MEU order, no draft; the US naval blockade stayed lifted (Day 9) and the USS Boxer ARG stayed disaggregated. The day's escalation lever was air power, not ground."
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "June 26-27 is the sharpest US-Iran kinetic exchange since the deal was signed, but it runs on the air/naval rung, not the ground one — leaving Predictive History's imminent-US-ground-invasion and Hormuz-and-Kharg-seizure thesis disconfirmed-and-inverted. The US response to Iran's drone attack was airstrikes on missile/drone storage and radar (the same instrument used in the early-June flare-ups), not a landing, a Kharg assault, an ARG/MEU surge, or a draft. The structural inversion holds and even sharpens: Iran (not the US) is asserting coercive control over the Strait — firing on shipping, turning back tankers, vowing to resume fees — while the US keeps the chokepoint open and the blockade lifted, the opposite of a forcible US seizure of Hormuz and Kharg. The episode does raise deal-durability risk (it is the first ceasefire breach met by US fire), which strengthens the 'fragile ceasefire could re-collapse' reading of the deal-durability open question and the 'genuine, hardening sovereignty claim' reading of the Hormuz-governance open question — but a collapse, if it came, would return the war to the air/missile register, not vindicate a ground invasion.",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "The June 26 US strikes are read two ways from the same facts. One reading treats them as a contained, proportionate self-defense package inside an otherwise-holding 60-day process — discrete strikes on missile/drone storage and radar (the same air-register response used June 2-3 and June 6), with the blockade still lifted, Hormuz still open, oil falling, and both sides framing the other as the violator while keeping the MOU technically in force. The other reading treats them as the first breach-and-retaliation cycle that could unravel the deal: Iran firing on shipping and turning back tankers, the US striking Iranian soil for the first time since the MOU, Vance's 'violence will be met with violence,' Trump's 'You'll find out,' and Iran's vow to resume fees after 60 days taken together as evidence the ceasefire is sliding back toward open conflict.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Both readings draw on the same on-record facts (the June 25 Ever Lovely drone strike; the June 26 US airstrikes per CENTCOM; the three tankers turned back; the Trump/Vance statements; the oil move; the MOU still nominally in force). Which prevails depends on whether Iran responds to the US strikes and on what the next Geneva technical round produces — not resolvable from open sources today.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
      "US (CENTCOM; Trump; VP Vance): the strikes were a justified 'powerful response' to Iran's 'foolish violation'; the US has 'honored' the ceasefire.",
      "Iran (Persian Gulf Strait Authority; negotiator Hossein Ghorbanzadeh): vessels crossing 'unauthorized routes' bear the consequences; Iran will resume transit fees after the 60-day window.",
      "Israel (Netanyahu): the Israel-Lebanon framework is 'a major blow to Iran'; Hezbollah (Hassan Fadlallah): enforcement would mean 'civil war.'"
     ],
     "why_unresolvable": "It is not yet known whether Iran will retaliate for the US strikes, whether the breach-retaliation cycle will repeat, or whether the 60-day roadmap survives the exchange; the dispositive facts (Iran's response, the next technical round, whether Hormuz fees are levied after the window) do not yet exist.",
     "open_question_ref": "iran-hormuz-administration-tolls-dispute-june-2026"
