   "headline": "DAY 120 — CEASEFIRE DAY 80 / US BLOCKADE LIFTED DAY 8 — THE MARITIME TRACK ESCALATED FOR THE FIRST TIME IN DAYS: IRAN FIRED ON A CARGO VESSEL NEAR OMAN (JUNE 25), A US OFFICIAL TOLD REUTERS TEHRAN WAS BEHIND IT, AND THE UN INTERNATIONAL MARITIME ORGANIZATION PAUSED ITS HORMUZ SHIP-SHEPHERDING/EVACUATION PROGRAM. THE IRGC NAVY WARNED SAFE PASSAGE WOULD BE PERMITTED ONLY VIA TEHRAN-DESIGNATED ROUTES AND THAT VIOLATORS WOULD 'FACE ACTION.' OIL REVERSED ITS SLIDE AND ROSE — WTI +2% TO SETTLE $71.92 (AFTER BRIEFLY DIPPING BELOW $70 JUNE 24), BRENT +2.1% TO $75.26 — ON RENEWED MIDDLE-EAST RISK. THE NUCLEAR-INSPECTIONS DISPUTE WIDENED (IAEA'S GROSSI: INSPECTIONS 'GOING TO HAPPEN'; IRAN'S DEPUTY FM GHARIBABADI: ONLY AFTER A FINAL DEAL, AND NO GROSSI MEETING OCCURRED IN SWITZERLAND). THE SENATE WALKED BACK ITS JUNE 23 WAR-POWERS REBUKE OF TRUMP (A SECOND, PROCEDURAL MOTION FAILED 50-47 JUNE 24). DESPITE THE MARITIME ESCALATION THE GROUND RUNG WAS UNCHANGED: IRAN'S STRIKE IS A MARITIME MOVE, NOT A US GROUND OPERATION — NO US TROOPS IN IRAN, NO KHARG ASSAULT, NO NEW ARG/MEU, NO DRAFT, BLOCKADE STILL LIFTED, USS BOXER ARG DISAGGREGATED.",
   "summary": "JUNE 26 (Day 120): The de-escalation of the prior week saw its first reversal on the maritime track. On June 25 a cargo ship reported it was struck on its starboard side by a projectile about 7.5 nautical miles southeast of Oman's port of Dahit; a US official told Reuters that Iran was behind the attack. The UN International Maritime Organization (IMO) paused the program it had been running to shepherd ships and seafarers through the Strait of Hormuz, and Iran's IRGC Navy warned that safe passage would be permitted only via routes designated by Tehran and that vessels violating its transit instructions would 'face action' — an enforcement of the sovereignty/authorization claim Iran had been asserting verbally. Oil, which had fallen to a war-period low (WTI briefly under $70 on June 24), reversed and rose: WTI settled up more than 2% at $71.92 and Brent climbed 2.1% to $75.26, as the strike refueled fears that Middle East tensions could stay elevated. The parallel nuclear-inspections dispute widened: IAEA chief Rafael Grossi said his inspectors would visit Iran's enrichment sites under the interim deal ('whether this happens the day after tomorrow or in one week or in 10 days... this is going to happen'), while Iranian deputy foreign minister Kazem Gharibabadi said any such visit would come only within the framework of a final agreement and that Tehran had not met Grossi in Switzerland. On the domestic-political track, the Senate walked back its June 23 war-powers rebuke of Trump: a day after passing a war-powers resolution 50-48, a procedural motion to advance a similar measure by Sen. Tim Kaine failed 50-47 on June 24 (Sens. Cassidy and Paul reversed; Trump hailed the result). Lebanon's June 19 ceasefire stayed fragile, with continued Israeli strikes and a Hezbollah demand for full Israeli withdrawal. The ground rung was unchanged: Iran's strike on shipping is a maritime move, not a US ground operation — there were no US troops in Iran, no Kharg assault, no new ARG/MEU order, no draft; the US naval blockade stayed lifted (Day 8) and the USS Boxer ARG stayed disaggregated. The escalation this cycle ran on the maritime rung (Iran firing on a ship), not the ground one. (CNBC; CBC News; NBC News; CBS News; The Times of Israel; NPR; CNN, June 24-25, 2026.)",
   "details": [
    "IRAN FIRES ON A CARGO VESSEL NEAR OMAN; IMO PAUSES HORMUZ SHEPHERDING (June 25, 2026): A cargo ship reported being struck on its starboard side by a projectile roughly 7.5 nautical miles southeast of Oman's port of Dahit; a US official told Reuters that Iran was responsible. The UN International Maritime Organization paused the program it had been running to escort ships and seafarers through the Strait of Hormuz, and Iran's IRGC Navy warned that safe passage would be permitted only via Tehran-designated routes and that vessels violating its instructions would 'face action.' The strike — the first Iranian fire on shipping in several days — reignited fears over the durability of the war-ending deal. (CBC News; NBC News; CBS News; The Times of Israel, June 25, 2026.)",
    "OIL REVERSES AND RISES ON THE STRIKE (June 25, 2026): After briefly dipping below $70 on June 24, oil turned around and climbed to session highs after the attack: WTI rose more than 2% to settle at $71.92/bbl and Brent climbed 2.1% to $75.26/bbl, as the strike 'refueled fears that tensions in the Middle East could stay elevated.' The move partially retraced the war-period low but stayed far below the early-2026 wartime peaks above $110 and well under the sustained $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. (CNBC, 'Oil reverses losses, rises after an Iran attack on cargo vessel,' June 25, 2026.)",
    "NUCLEAR-INSPECTIONS DISPUTE WIDENS — GROSSI vs. GHARIBABADI (June 24, 2026): IAEA chief Rafael Grossi gave his firmest statement yet, saying inspectors would visit Iran's enrichment sites under the interim deal — 'the accord says explicitly that the nuclear activities... will be supervised by the IAEA... Obviously, to do that, we will have to inspect,' and 'whether this happens the day after tomorrow or in one week or in 10 days... this is going to happen.' Iranian deputy foreign minister Kazem Gharibabadi countered that any visit would come 'only within the framework of a final agreement and as a result of practical action by the other side to end all sanctions,' and said Tehran had not met Grossi while in Switzerland. A verification dispute negotiated in public, not a ground indicator. (NPR; PBS NewsHour, June 24, 2026.)",
    "SENATE WALKS BACK ITS WAR-POWERS REBUKE OF TRUMP (June 24, 2026): One day after the Senate passed a war-powers resolution 50-48 (June 23) directing the president to remove US forces from hostilities with Iran — the first time such a measure cleared both chambers — a procedural motion to advance a similar resolution by Democratic Sen. Tim Kaine failed 50-47 on June 24. GOP Sens. Cassidy and Paul reversed (Cassidy voting against, Paul voting present); only Collins and Murkowski still backed advancing it. The resolution is non-binding and will not be sent to the White House; Trump hailed the second vote as putting Iran 'on notice.' (CNN Politics; CBS News; Time, June 23-24, 2026.)",
    "LEBANON CEASEFIRE STAYS FRAGILE (June 24-25, 2026): The June 19 US/Qatar/Iran-mediated Israel-Hezbollah ceasefire remained the live regional fault line: Israeli strikes continued in southern Lebanon and Hezbollah demanded a full Israeli withdrawal as a condition of the truce, with the cumulative Lebanon toll since March 2 near 4,000 killed. Israel is not a party to the US-Iran deal. (Al Jazeera; Time, June 24, 2026.)",
    "GROUND RUNG (June 26, 2026): Unchanged despite the maritime escalation. Iran's strike on a cargo vessel and its 'Tehran-designated routes' enforcement are maritime/coercive moves by Tehran, not US ground operations — there were no US troops in Iran, no Kharg Island assault, no new amphibious ready group or Marine Expeditionary Unit order, no draft; the US naval blockade stayed lifted (Day 8) and the USS Boxer ARG stayed disaggregated. The day's escalation lever was maritime (Iran firing on shipping), not ground."
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "June 25-26 marks the first re-escalation in days, but it runs on the maritime rung, not the ground one — leaving Predictive History's imminent-US-ground-invasion and Hormuz-and-Kharg-seizure thesis disconfirmed-and-inverted. Iran (not the US) fired on a ship; the US naval blockade stays lifted, amphibious mass stays disaggregated, and no US ground-execution indicator moved. If anything the episode underscores the structural inversion of the prediction: the chokepoint friction is Iran asserting coercive control over a Strait the US is reopening by negotiation, the opposite of a US forcible seizure. The cargo-vessel strike strengthens the 'genuine, unresolved sovereignty claim' reading of the Hormuz-governance open question (Iran is now enforcing its authorization claim by fire, not just rhetoric), and the oil reversal shows the residual risk premium is live — but neither bears on whether a US ground invasion is underway. The Senate war-powers walk-back removes a near-term legislative constraint on the president's war powers without itself ordering any force movement.",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "The June 25 attack on a cargo vessel is read two ways from the same facts. One reading treats it as a contained, possibly rogue-IRGC enforcement incident inside an otherwise-holding 60-day process — a single projectile, no confirmed casualties, the deal and the lifted blockade both intact, and Iran signaling 'use our designated routes' rather than re-closing the Strait. The other reading treats it as Iran beginning to enforce by fire the sovereignty/authorization claim it had only asserted verbally — the IMO shepherding pause, the IRGC 'face action' warning, and the oil reversal taken together as evidence the Hormuz-governance dispute is hardening into coercive control that could unravel the talks Rubio said would end if Iran charges for transit.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Both readings draw on the same on-record facts (the June 25 strike near Dahit; the US official's Reuters attribution to Iran; the IMO pause; the IRGC Navy statement; the oil move). Which prevails depends on whether the strike is a one-off or the first of a pattern, and on what the next Geneva technical round produces — not resolvable from open sources today.",
