   "headline": "DAY 119 — CEASEFIRE DAY 79 / US BLOCKADE LIFTED DAY 7 — THE HORMUZ-SOVEREIGNTY/TOLLS DISPUTE MOVED TO CENTER STAGE ALONGSIDE THE NUCLEAR-INSPECTIONS FRICTION. SECRETARY OF STATE MARCO RUBIO OPENED A GULF TOUR IN THE UAE (JUNE 24), CALLED THE DISCUSSIONS 'FRANK,' SAID TECHNICAL TALKS WITH IRAN WOULD RESUME 'NEXT WEEK' IN GENEVA, AND DREW A HARD LINE ON THE STRAIT: 'IT'S AN INTERNATIONAL WATERWAY. NO COUNTRY IS ALLOWED TO CHARGE TOLLS OR FEES' — WARNING THAT IF IRAN PROPOSES TOLLS 'THERE'S NOT GOING TO BE A FINAL DEAL.' IRAN'S CHIEF NEGOTIATOR MOHAMMAD BAGHER GHALIBAF COUNTERED THAT HORMUZ 'WILL NEVER RETURN TO ITS PRE-WAR CONDITIONS AND WILL BE ADMINISTERED BY THE ISLAMIC REPUBLIC OF IRAN,' WHILE IRAN'S PERSIAN GULF STRAIT AUTHORITY SAID ITS PLANNED TRANSIT FEES ARE SUSPENDED DURING THE 60-DAY MOU WINDOW. THE NUCLEAR-INSPECTIONS DISPUTE PERSISTED (TRUMP: IRAN 'FULLY AND COMPLETELY AGREED TO HIGHEST LEVEL NUCLEAR INSPECTIONS'; IRAN STILL DENIES ANY GROSSI MEETING OR SCHEDULED IAEA VISIT TO THE US-BOMBED SITES). OIL FELL FURTHER — BRENT DROPPED BELOW ~$74/BBL JUNE 24, ITS LOWEST SINCE LATE FEBRUARY — AS TANKER TRAFFIC HELD ITS WAR-PERIOD HIGH AND THE BLOCKADE STAYED LIFTED. THE GROUND RUNG WAS UNCHANGED AND DE-ESCALATORY: NO US TROOPS IN IRAN, NO KHARG ASSAULT, NO DRAFT, BLOCKADE LIFTED, USS BOXER ARG DISAGGREGATED.",
   "summary": "JUNE 25 (Day 119): With the 60-day Bürgenstock roadmap intact but its two open frictions unresolved, the Strait-of-Hormuz governance question moved alongside the nuclear-inspections dispute as the central fault line. Secretary of State Marco Rubio opened a Gulf tour in the United Arab Emirates on June 24, described his consultations with Gulf partners as 'frank,' and said the next round of US-Iran technical talks would convene 'next week' in Geneva. On the Strait he drew the firmest US line yet against Iranian transit charges: 'It's an international waterway. No country is allowed to charge tolls or fees on an international waterway,' adding that if Iran tries to propose tolls 'there's not going to be a final deal.' Iran pushed the opposite claim: chief negotiator and parliament speaker Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf said the Strait 'will never return to its pre-war conditions and will be administered by the Islamic Republic of Iran, in accordance with international law,' even as both sides had agreed June 22 to a communications mechanism to keep it open. Iran's Persian Gulf Strait Authority said its planned fees would be suspended for the duration of the 60-day MOU window, deferring rather than abandoning the toll claim. The parallel nuclear-inspections dispute carried over: Trump posted that Iran had 'fully and completely agreed to highest level Nuclear inspections long into the future,' while Iran's Foreign Ministry continued to deny any meeting with IAEA chief Grossi and any scheduled inspection of the sites the US bombed in 2025. Markets read continued de-escalation: Brent fell below ~$74/bbl on June 24, its lowest since late February, as Hormuz tanker traffic held near its war-period high (109 vessels Sat-Mon; ~25 transits the prior Thursday) via the open northern (Iranian) and southern (Omani) routes while ~80 mines on the central channel await clearing, and the US naval blockade stayed lifted. The Lebanon track stayed the live fault line — the June 19 ceasefire remained fragile amid continued Israeli strikes, with Hezbollah demanding a full Israeli withdrawal from southern Lebanon. The ground rung was unchanged and de-escalatory on the US side: no US troops in Iran, no Kharg assault, no draft, the blockade still lifted, and the USS Boxer ARG disaggregated; the only levers in play were diplomatic (the tolls and inspections disputes) and air, not ground. (Al Jazeera; The Times of Israel; The National; France 24; CBS News; Washington Times; TradingEconomics/CNBC; Fox Business, June 23-24, 2026.)",
   "details": [
    "HORMUZ SOVEREIGNTY / TOLLS DISPUTE SHARPENS (June 24, 2026): Secretary of State Marco Rubio, opening a Gulf tour in the UAE, said no country may charge for passage through the Strait of Hormuz: 'It's an international waterway. No country is allowed to charge tolls or fees on an international waterway.' He warned that any Iranian attempt to impose tolls would forfeit a final deal ('there's not going to be a final deal'), framed Hormuz security as a matter for Iran and the Gulf Coast Coalition, and said US-Iran technical talks would resume next week in Geneva. Iran's chief negotiator Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf countered that the Strait 'will never return to its pre-war conditions and will be administered by the Islamic Republic of Iran, in accordance with international law.' Iran's Persian Gulf Strait Authority said its planned transit fees are suspended during the 60-day MOU window — a deferral, not a withdrawal, of the toll claim. (Al Jazeera; The Times of Israel; The National; France 24, June 24, 2026.)",
    "NUCLEAR-INSPECTIONS DISPUTE CARRIES OVER (June 23-24, 2026): Trump posted that Iran had 'fully and completely agreed to highest level Nuclear inspections long into the future,' reprising VP Vance's June 22 claim that Iran would invite IAEA inspectors back. Iran's Foreign Ministry continued to deny it: spokesman Esmail Baghaei said no meeting with IAEA chief Rafael Grossi had taken place and that UN inspectors were not scheduled to examine the nuclear sites the US bombed in 2025. The two governments are offering 'different stories' on whether inspections were agreed — a verification dispute, not a ground indicator. (CBS News; Washington Times; NBC News, June 23-24, 2026.)",
    "OIL AT A WAR-PERIOD LOW (June 24, 2026): Brent crude fell below ~$74/bbl on June 24, its lowest level since late February, as rising Hormuz tanker traffic and progress in the US-Iran talks eased supply fears — far below the early-2026 wartime peaks above $110 and the sustained $130+ a US ground commitment would imply. (TradingEconomics; CNBC, June 24, 2026.)",
    "HORMUZ TRAFFIC HOLDS WAR-PERIOD HIGH (June 22-24, 2026): Tanker traffic stayed at its highest since the conflict began — 109 vessels transited Saturday-Monday and ~25 ships crossed the prior Thursday (13 west-to-east, 12 east-to-west) — still a fraction of the pre-war ~120-130/day. The northern (Iranian) and southern (Omani) routes are open; the central channel remains closed pending clearance of ~80 mines. Tehran is allowing toll-free transit for the 60-day window. (Fox Business; CNBC, June 22-24, 2026.)",
    "LEBANON CEASEFIRE STAYS FRAGILE (June 24, 2026): The June 19 US/Qatar/Iran-mediated Israel-Hezbollah ceasefire remained the live fault line: Israel continued strikes in southern Lebanon and Hezbollah demanded a full Israeli withdrawal as a condition of the truce, with the cumulative Lebanon toll since March 2 near 4,000 killed. Israel is not a party to the US-Iran deal. (Al Jazeera; France 24, June 23-24, 2026.)",
    "GROUND RUNG (June 25, 2026): Unchanged and de-escalatory on the US side — no US troops in Iran, no Kharg Island assault, no draft, the naval blockade still lifted, and the USS Boxer ARG disaggregated. The day's escalation levers were diplomatic (the Hormuz-tolls and IAEA-inspections disputes), not ground."
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "June 24-25 leaves the disconfirmation of Predictive History's imminent-US-ground-invasion and Hormuz-and-Kharg-seizure thesis intact while sharpening the settlement track's two open frictions — the Hormuz tolls/administration clash and the IAEA-inspections dispute. Both are diplomatic/verification disputes, not ground indicators: the chokepoint is being reopened by negotiation and mine-clearing (the structural inverse of a predicted seizure), the US naval blockade stays lifted, oil sits at a war-period low, and the only escalation levers in play are diplomatic and air. The contested Hormuz-governance question (Iran's 'administered by Tehran' claim and suspended-but-not-withdrawn fees vs. the US 'international waterway, no tolls' line) is logged as a separate open question; it bears on how durable the war-ending settlement is, not on whether a ground invasion is underway.",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "The June 24 Hormuz exchange is read two ways from the same documented facts. One reading treats it as a normal negotiating gap inside a live 60-day process: Iran's 'administered by Tehran' language and its Persian Gulf Strait Authority suspending fees for the MOU window are a face-saving sovereignty posture that the agreed communications mechanism and the US 'no tolls' redline will reconcile before a final deal — the blockade is lifted, traffic is at a war-period high, and oil is falling. The other reading treats Ghalibaf's 'will never return to its pre-war conditions' and the merely-suspended (not abandoned) fee schedule as a genuine, unresolved sovereignty claim that could harden into a toll regime and collapse the talks Rubio warned would end 'if Iran tries to propose tolls.'",
     "evidentiary_status": "Both readings draw on the same on-record statements (Rubio June 24; Ghalibaf June 23-24; the PGSA fee-suspension notice). Which prevails depends on the next Geneva technical round and on whether the suspended fees are withdrawn or reinstated after the 60-day window — not resolvable from open sources today.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
