   "headline": "GROUND INVASION TRACKER — DAY 118 (June 24): STATUS UNCHANGED AT 'NO_GROUND_TROOPS' AND STILL DE-ESCALATORY ON THE US SIDE. THE DAY'S FRICTION WAS DIPLOMATIC (A US-IRAN NUCLEAR-INSPECTIONS DISPUTE; TRUMP'S THREAT TO 'CUT OFF TALKS') AND MARITIME (IRAN'S CLAIM THAT HORMUZ TRANSITS NEED ITS AUTHORIZATION) — NEITHER A GROUND MOVE. THE NAVAL BLOCKADE REMAINS LIFTED, THE USS BOXER ARG STAYS DISAGGREGATED, AND THERE ARE STILL ~50,000 US TROOPS IN THE CENTCOM AOR WITH NO NEW DEPLOYMENT, NO KHARG ASSAULT, NO THIRD ARG / NEW MEU, AND NO DRAFT. THE ONLY ESCALATION LEVER AIRED WAS A THREAT TO END NEGOTIATIONS, NOT A TROOP MOVEMENT.",
   "summary": "JUNE 24 (Day 118): No change to the ground-invasion picture on the US side. The roadmap's first public friction was a US-Iran dispute over whether Iran agreed to IAEA inspections of its bombed nuclear sites (Vance/Trump say yes; Iran's Baghaei says no), capped by Trump's threat to 'cut off talks immediately' — a diplomatic, not a ground, lever. Iran's parallel hard move was maritime: asserting that Hormuz transits now require its authorization and floating transit charges via a compulsory-insurance framework, the structural opposite of a US ground seizure of the strait. The US naval blockade remains lifted and the USS Boxer ARG / 11th MEU stays disaggregated. There are still ~50,000 US troops in the CENTCOM AOR with no new deployment ordered; the USS Tripoli (LHA-7) ARG / 31st MEU remains forward-deployed in a routine posture; no Kharg assault has been ordered or executed; no third ARG or new MEU has been ordered; and there is no draft signal. The 82nd Airborne forward headquarters remains a fading standing forcible-entry contingency, not an execution order. Tanker traffic actually rose to a war-period high (109 vessels Sat-Mon) and oil fell to ~$75/bbl — the opposite of the supply-shock, ground-commitment pricing the invasion thesis implies.",
   "details": [
    "NO GROUND MOVES IN THE DAY'S DEVELOPMENTS (June 23-24): The roadmap's first friction — the nuclear-inspections dispute and Trump's threat to cut off talks — is diplomatic; Iran's Hormuz authorization/insurance claim is maritime. Neither involves US ground forces. (Washington Post; NPR; Times of Israel, June 23, 2026.)",
    "AMPHIBIOUS / MARINE POSTURE STILL DE-ESCALATING (June 24): The USS Boxer ARG / 11th MEU remains disaggregated (amphibious mass removed, not concentrated); the USS Tripoli (LHA-7) ARG / 31st MEU remains forward in a routine posture, its blockade-enforcement role wound down. No third ARG, no new MEU surge, no landing toward Iran or Kharg. (The War Zone; armyrecognition.com, June 2026.)",
    "NO DRAFT SIGNAL (June 23-24): The inspections dispute and Lebanon fighting produced no Selective Service action, no expanded Guard/Reserve call-up language, and no brigade-scale activation. The legislated automatic Selective Service registration (men 18-26, effective Dec 2026) is unrelated to the war and is not an active draft. (CNN Politics/CNBC; PolitiFact, 2026.)",
    "TRAFFIC UP, OIL DOWN — THE INVERSE OF GROUND-COMMITMENT PRICING (June 22-23): Hormuz tanker traffic rose to its highest since the war began (109 vessels Sat-Mon) and Brent fell to ~$75/bbl; a US ground campaign to seize the strait and Kharg would drive the opposite. (AOL/Fox Business; Al Jazeera Economy, June 22-23, 2026.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "Reinforces the disconfirmation and clarifies its boundary. The ground-invasion thesis required US troops moving toward Iran, a Kharg seizure, an amphibious buildup, or a draft; the June 23-24 window delivered a nuclear-inspections dispute, a Hormuz sovereignty claim by Iran, rising tanker traffic, and falling oil — diplomatic and maritime developments, with US forces still drawn down and the blockade still lifted. The only US escalation lever aired was a threat to end talks. A chokepoint contested through authorization claims and insurance tolls is the structural inverse of a US ground seizure of Hormuz and Kharg.",
   "source": "Washington Post, 'Iran says no to nuclear inspections,' June 23, 2026; NPR, 'A U.S.-Iran dispute over nuclear inspections clouds work to finalize a war-ending deal,' June 23, 2026; Times of Israel, June 23, 2026; The War Zone (USS Boxer ARG disaggregation; naval posture), June 2026; AOL/Fox Business (Hormuz traffic), June 22-23, 2026; CNN Politics / CNBC (Selective Service), April 9, 2026; War on the Rocks (Kharg), April 2026."
