   "headline": "GROUND INVASION TRACKER — DAY 116 (June 22): STATUS UNCHANGED AT 'NO_GROUND_TROOPS' AND STILL DE-ESCALATORY ON THE US SIDE. THE WEEKEND'S DEFINING US-SIDE MOVES WERE DIPLOMATIC (SWISS TECHNICAL TALKS RECONVENED JUNE 21) AND DRAWDOWN-LEANING (THE USS BOXER ARG / 11TH MEU WAS DISAGGREGATED — A FRESH DE-ESCALATION SIGNAL). CENTCOM DISPUTED IRAN'S HORMUZ CLOSURE ('TRAFFIC CONTINUES TO FLOW'); THE US NAVAL BLOCKADE REMAINS LIFTED. STILL ~50,000 US TROOPS IN THE CENTCOM AOR; NO NEW DEPLOYMENT; NO KHARG ASSAULT; NO THIRD ARG / NEW MEU; NO DRAFT. ESCALATION TALK (TRUMP: 'HIT IRAN VERY HARD AGAIN') STAYED ON THE AIR-POWER RUNG, NOT THE GROUND ONE.",
   "summary": "JUNE 22 (Day 116): No change to the ground-invasion picture on the US side, which stayed de-escalatory through the deal's most precarious weekend. The two US-side developments were diplomatic and drawdown-leaning, not ground moves: the postponed in-person US-Iran technical talks reconvened Sunday June 21 at the Bürgenstock resort, and the USS Boxer amphibious ready group / 11th MEU was disaggregated — a fresh de-escalation signal that removes, rather than adds, amphibious mass. CENTCOM publicly disputed Iran's June 20 Hormuz closure ('Iran does not control the Strait of Hormuz. Traffic continues to flow'), and the US naval blockade itself remains lifted. There are still ~50,000 US troops in the CENTCOM area of responsibility with no new deployment ordered; the USS Tripoli (LHA-7) ARG / 31st MEU (~2,200-2,500 Marines) remains forward-deployed in a routine posture (its blockade-enforcement role wound down); no Kharg assault has been ordered or executed; no third ARG or new MEU has been ordered; and there is no draft signal. The escalation Trump voiced over the weekend ('hit Iran very hard again') is an air-power threat, not a ground deployment. The 82nd Airborne forward headquarters remains a fading standing forcible-entry contingency, not an execution order.",
   "details": [
    "TROOP LEVEL: Still ~50,000 US troops in the CENTCOM AOR (official figure, holding since March 2026); no net change and no new ground deployment ordered over the June 21-22 weekend, which was dominated by the Swiss talks and rhetoric, not force movement. (CBS News; Al Jazeera, June 20-21, 2026.)",
    "BOXER ARG DISAGGREGATED — DRAWDOWN SIGNAL (June 20-21): The USS Boxer amphibious ready group / 11th MEU (~4,000 Marines) was disaggregated despite earlier reporting it might join the war — a notable de-escalation data point that removes amphibious mass rather than concentrating it for any landing. (The War Zone; armyrecognition.com, June 2026.)",
    "HORMUZ CLOSURE DISPUTED, BLOCKADE STILL LIFTED (June 20-21): Iran declared the strait closed, but CENTCOM said 'Iran does not control the Strait of Hormuz. Traffic continues to flow' (55 ships / >17M barrels June 20), and the US naval blockade remains lifted. This is a maritime/diplomatic contest, not a US ground operation. (CNN live; CBS News, June 20-21, 2026.)",
    "KHARG ISLAND: The seizure pathway remains superseded. No US ground troops are on Kharg and no assault has been ordered or executed; the weekend's Hormuz dispute changes the maritime picture but not the ground one. (Al Jazeera; War on the Rocks 'The Folly of Seizing Kharg Island,' April 2026, as standing context.)",
    "MARINE / AMPHIBIOUS — POSTURE TRENDING TO ROTATION/DRAWDOWN: No new amphibious or Marine deployment ordered. The USS Tripoli (LHA-7) ARG / 31st MEU remains forward-deployed in a routine posture (not invasion staging), its enforcement role wound down; the USS Boxer ARG / 11th MEU was disaggregated; the USS Abraham Lincoln carrier strike group was earlier flagged as likely first to rotate out. No third ARG, no new MEU surge, no landing toward Iran or Kharg. (The War Zone; armyrecognition.com, June 2026.)",
    "DRAFT / MOBILIZATION: No draft or conscription signal. The weekend's escalatory rhetoric, the Hormuz dispute and the Lebanon flare-up produced no Selective Service action, no expanded Guard/Reserve call-up language, and no brigade-scale activation. The previously legislated automatic Selective Service registration (effective Dec 2026) is unrelated to the war. (CNN Politics; CNBC, April 9, 2026, as standing context.)",
    "ASSESSMENT: Ground-invasion execution probability is LOW and stable at Day 116. The weekend's stress — the disputed Hormuz status, the violated Lebanon ceasefire, the open toll dispute, and the only-just-reconvened Swiss talks — bears on the deal's durability, not on any US ground move. Every fresh US-side data point (talks reconvening, blockade lifted, Boxer ARG disaggregated) points to de-escalation; the live escalation lever is air-power, not ground."
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "Reinforces the disconfirmation and tightens its boundary. The ground-invasion thesis required US troops moving toward Iran, a Kharg seizure, an amphibious buildup, or a draft; the June 21-22 weekend delivered the opposite on every axis — talks reconvened, the blockade stayed lifted, and the USS Boxer ARG was disaggregated (less amphibious mass, not more). The escalation that did occur was verbal and air-power-framed (Trump's 'hit Iran very hard again'), never ground. The disputed Hormuz status keeps the deal-durability question live, but a chokepoint contested by declaration, coercion and blockade-lift is the structural inverse of the predicted US seizure of Hormuz and Kharg by ground force.",
   "source": "The War Zone (USS Boxer ARG disaggregation; naval posture), June 2026; CBS News live blog and CNN live, June 20-21, 2026; Al Jazeera live updates, June 20-21, 2026; NPR, June 21, 2026; CNN Politics / CNBC (Selective Service), April 9, 2026; War on the Rocks (Kharg), April 2026."
