   "headline": "DAY 115 — CEASEFIRE DAY 75 / US BLOCKADE LIFTED DAY 3 — THE WAR-ENDING DEAL ENTERED ITS MOST PRECARIOUS STRETCH SINCE SIGNING. IRAN'S IRGC RE-CLOSED THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ ON JUNE 20 — REVERSING THE FOUR-DAY-OLD REOPENING FROM ITS OWN SIDE (THE US NAVAL BLOCKADE REMAINS LIFTED) — IN PROTEST AT ISRAEL'S CONTINUED LEBANON STRIKES, WHICH IRAN CASTS AS A US FAILURE TO HONOR THE MOU; TEHRAN WARNED OF 'RECIPROCAL ACTION' IF THE US DOES NOT ENFORCE ITS COMMITMENTS. YET THE TALKS DID NOT COLLAPSE: VP JD VANCE DEPARTED FOR THE POSTPONED TECHNICAL US-IRAN MEETING IN SWITZERLAND (BÜRGENSTOCK), WHICH PAKISTAN SAID WAS DUE TO RECONVENE SUNDAY JUNE 21 WITH PAKISTANI/SAUDI/TURKISH MEDIATION — SO THE PROCESS THAT WAS POSTPONED FRIDAY APPEARS TO BE RESUMING. THE LEBANON FAULT LINE WORSENED: ISRAELI STRIKES KILLED AT LEAST 32 IN LEBANON ON JUNE 20 DESPITE FRIDAY'S RENEWED ISRAEL-HEZBOLLAH CEASEFIRE (CUMULATIVE TOLL ~4,000 SINCE MARCH 2; MSF CALLED NABATIEH A 'DEATH TRAP'; THE UK CONDEMNED AN ISRAELI MINISTER OVER 'INFLAMMATORY' LEBANON REMARKS), 'PUTTING US-IRAN TALKS IN PERIL.' TRUMP VOWED IRAN WILL NOT CHARGE HORMUZ TOLLS BUT SAID THE US MIGHT; THE PENTAGON TOLD LAWMAKERS IT NEEDS ~$80B FOR THE IRAN WAR AND OTHER EXPENSES. OIL ROSE ON JUNE 19 ON THE LEBANON FIGHTING AND STILL-SLOW HORMUZ TRAFFIC (BRENT ~$80, WTI ~$77). THE GROUND RUNG IS UNCHANGED AND STILL DE-ESCALATORY — IRAN'S HORMUZ RE-CLOSURE IS A MARITIME/COERCIVE MOVE BY IRAN, NOT A US GROUND OPERATION; THE US POSTURE IS DRAWDOWN (BLOCKADE ENFORCEMENT CEASED, LINCOLN CSG EXPECTED TO ROTATE OUT), WITH NO US TROOPS IN IRAN, NO KHARG ASSAULT, AND NO DRAFT.",
   "summary": "JUNE 21 (Day 115): The war-ending deal survived its first full week but entered its most fragile stretch since the June 17 signing, as Iran reversed the Strait of Hormuz reopening from its own side while the diplomatic track simultaneously moved to reconvene. On June 20 Iran's Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps declared the Strait of Hormuz closed again — only two days after commercial transits resumed for the first time in ~110 days — casting Israel's continued strikes on Lebanon as a violation of the US-Iran memorandum of understanding and warning of 'reciprocal action' if Washington does not enforce its commitments. The US naval blockade itself remains lifted (CENTCOM enforcement 'ceased'); the new closure is Iranian leverage, not a return of the US interdiction campaign. Crucially, the talks did not break: Vice President JD Vance departed for the technical US-Iran meeting in Switzerland (the Bürgenstock session postponed on Friday), which Pakistan said was due to reconvene on Sunday June 21 with Pakistani, Saudi and Turkish mediators coordinating — so the process that had stalled appeared to be resuming rather than failing. Iran separately said it was 'ready to move forward' with the deal provided Israel abides by its terms. The Lebanon track, the deal's live fault line, deteriorated: Israeli airstrikes killed at least 32 people in Lebanon on June 20 despite Friday's Trump-brokered Israel-Hezbollah ceasefire renewal, with Al Jazeera reporting the strikes were 'putting US-Iran talks in peril'; MSF said conditions in Nabatieh resembled a 'death trap,' the UK condemned an Israeli minister over 'inflammatory' Lebanon remarks, and the cumulative Lebanon toll since March 2 stood near 4,000 killed. The US announced a new round of Israel-Lebanon talks in Washington the following week. On the tolls dispute, Trump vowed Iran would not be allowed to charge Hormuz tolls but said the US itself might, and reporting indicated the MOU does not rule out future US tolls after an initial 60-day period. The Pentagon told lawmakers it needed roughly $80 billion for the Iran war and other expenses. Oil rose on June 19 on the Lebanon fighting and still-slow Hormuz traffic, with Brent near $80 and WTI near $77, then held in that band — far below wartime peaks above $110. The ground-invasion rung was unchanged and still de-escalatory: Iran's Hormuz re-closure is a coercive maritime move by Tehran, not a US ground operation, and the US posture remained one of drawdown (blockade enforcement ceased, the USS Abraham Lincoln carrier strike group flagged as likely first to rotate out); there are no US troops in Iran, no Kharg assault, and no draft signal. (Al Jazeera live updates; CBS News; The War Zone; oilprice.com; Navy Times, June 19-20, 2026.)",
   "details": [
    "IRAN RE-CLOSES THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ (June 20, 2026): Iran's IRGC announced the Strait of Hormuz closed again, only two days after transits resumed, tying the move to Israel's continued strikes on Lebanon, which Tehran frames as a breach of the US-Iran MOU. The US naval blockade itself stays lifted (CENTCOM enforcement 'ceased'); the closure is an Iranian coercive measure against the deal's perceived non-enforcement, reversing the reopening from the Iranian side rather than a return of the US interdiction campaign. Iran also warned of 'reciprocal action' if the US does not honour its MOU commitments. (Al Jazeera, 'Iran closes Strait of Hormuz as Pakistan says new talks with US due Sunday' / 'Iran shuts Strait of Hormuz as Israel tests MOU with Lebanon strikes,' June 20, 2026.)",
    "POSTPONED SWISS TALKS SET TO RESUME; VANCE DEPARTS (June 20, 2026): Despite Friday's postponement, VP JD Vance left for the technical US-Iran meeting in Switzerland (Bürgenstock, above Lake Lucerne), and Pakistan said a new round of talks was due Sunday June 21, with Pakistani, Saudi and Turkish mediators coordinating. Iran said it was 'ready to move forward' with the deal provided Israel abides by its terms. The process that had stalled Friday thus appeared to be reconvening, not collapsing — though as of this update the Sunday session's outcome was not yet reported. (Al Jazeera, 'IRGC says Strait of Hormuz closed; Vance leaves for technical Iran-US talks,' June 20, 2026.)",
    "LEBANON CEASEFIRE VIOLATED; 32 KILLED, TALKS 'IN PERIL' (June 20, 2026): Israeli strikes killed at least 32 people in Lebanon on June 20, one day after the Trump-brokered Israel-Hezbollah ceasefire renewal, with Al Jazeera reporting the strikes were 'putting US-Iran talks in peril.' MSF said conditions in Nabatieh resembled a 'death trap'; the UK condemned an Israeli minister over 'inflammatory' Lebanon remarks; the cumulative Lebanon toll since March 2 stood near 4,000 killed. The US announced a new round of Israel-Lebanon talks in Washington the following week. (Al Jazeera, 'Israeli strikes kill 32 in Lebanon, putting US-Iran talks in peril'; 'US announces new round of Israel-Lebanon talks in Washington next week,' June 19-20, 2026.)",
    "TOLLS DISPUTE — TRUMP: NO IRAN TOLLS, BUT US MIGHT (June 20, 2026): Trump said Iran would not be permitted to charge tolls on Hormuz traffic but that the US itself might; reporting indicated the MOU does not rule out future US tolls after an initial 60-day toll-free period. Iran's re-closure of the strait is the live pressure point in the dispute. (Al Jazeera, 'Trump vows Iran will not charge Strait of Hormuz tolls, but says US might,' June 20, 2026.)",
    "PENTAGON $80B WAR-COST REQUEST; CONGRESSIONAL REVIEW (June 19, 2026): The Pentagon told lawmakers it needs roughly $80 billion for the Iran war and other expenses; Al Jazeera examined whether Trump must submit the Iran MOU to Congress. The funding/oversight debate stays in a constrain-and-account register, alongside the House's June 3 war-powers resolution (215-208) and GOP Sen. Wicker's complaint that the deal 'negotiates away' war gains. (WSJ via Military.com; Al Jazeera, 'Does Trump have to submit the Iran memorandum of understanding to Congress?,' June 19, 2026.)",
    "OIL RISES ON LEBANON + SLOW HORMUZ (June 19-20, 2026): Brent crude rose on June 19 as Lebanon fighting erupted and Hormuz traffic stayed slow, trading near $80/bbl with WTI near $77 — reversing part of the post-deal slide but remaining far below the early-2026 wartime peaks above $110. The June 20 Iranian re-closure added fresh upward pressure on the war-risk premium. De-escalation/deal pricing overall, not the sustained $130+ that ground-commitment pricing would require. (Al Jazeera Economy, 'Oil prices rise as Lebanon fighting erupts and Hormuz traffic still slow'; oilprice.com, June 19-20, 2026.)",
    "GROUND RUNG — US POSTURE STILL DRAWDOWN (June 15-20, 2026): The War Zone assessed America's naval force posture as largely unchanged but trending toward rotation-out: two carriers in-theatre (USS Abraham Lincoln, USS George H.W. Bush), the USS Tripoli (LHA-7) forward-deployed with the 31st MEU embarked (routine, not invasion staging), and 20+ surface combatants regionally, with the Lincoln CSG flagged as likely first to rotate out. No troop movement toward Iran, no Kharg developments, no draft. (The War Zone, 'America's Naval Force Posture Largely Unchanged...,' June 15, 2026; Navy Times, June 18, 2026.)",
    "OTHER THEATRES (June 18-20, 2026): RUSSIA-UKRAINE — Ukrainian deep-strike drones hit Moscow oil-refinery/Moscow-City targets three days running (June 17-19; all four Moscow airports grounded) and a Russian glide-bomb attack on Zaporizhzhia killed at least 5 on June 20; no active ceasefire talks (Lavrov rejected Ukraine's June 7 proposal). VENEZUELA — Delcy Rodríguez remains acting president; the Maduro/Flores trial was postponed to July 22 (next hearing). COLOMBIA — presidential runoff held June 21 (de la Espriella vs Cepeda; result pending). US-CHINA — fragile post-truce stabilization; Beijing announced more marine surveys east of Taiwan; trade frictions continued. NORTH KOREA — Kim Yo Jong denounced the G7 denuclearization reaffirmation; no missile test reported. ASIAN HORMUZ-EXPOSED IMPORTERS (Japan, South Korea, India, Pakistan, Bangladesh) — initial reopening optimism (KOSPI crossed 9,000; Nikkei rose) was complicated by Iran's June 20 re-closure. (ISW; Al Jazeera; SCMP; NK News; Korea JoongAng Daily, June 18-20, 2026.)"
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "June 20-21 leaves Predictive History's imminent-US-ground-invasion / Hormuz-and-Kharg-seizure thesis disconfirmed, but the week's events sharpen the distinction the audit has drawn all along between a chokepoint reopened or closed by negotiation/coercion and one seized by US ground force. Iran's re-closure of Hormuz is a genuine reversal of the reopening — but it is an Iranian coercive maritime move, the structural opposite of the predicted US Kharg landing and 'total control' of Iranian oil. The US naval blockade stays lifted, US forces remain set to draw down (the Lincoln CSG flagged to rotate out), and no US ground operation, Kharg assault, amphibious surge, or draft has materialized; the only US escalation levers aired remain air-power and the tolls dispute. The genuine open question is durability — the deal is now visibly stressed (Hormuz re-closed, Lebanon ceasefire violated with 32 killed, the toll dispute unresolved, the postponed talks only tentatively reconvening) — but even a full collapse points back to the air/naval/missile register, not to a US ground invasion. The ground-invasion prediction therefore stays in the strongly-disconfirmed band, with the chokepoint contest still being fought through negotiation, coercion and blockade rather than ground seizure.",
   "alternate_readings": [
    {
     "reading": "Iran's June 20 re-closure of the Strait of Hormuz is read either as ordinary coercive bargaining inside a deal that both sides still intend to consummate — Tehran pressuring Washington to rein in Israel's Lebanon strikes while Vance flies to Switzerland and talks reconvene Sunday — or as the first substantive unravelling of an over-sold accord, with the chokepoint that the deal was supposed to reopen now shut again, the Lebanon ceasefire already violated (32 killed the day after renewal), and the toll dispute open, such that the 'deal in peril' framing marks the beginning of a return to the pre-signing impasse.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Both readings rest on the same June 20 record: the IRGC's declared Hormuz closure tied to Israel's Lebanon strikes, Iran's 'reciprocal action' warning and its 'ready to move forward if Israel abides' statement, Vance's departure for the Bürgenstock technical talks, Pakistan's announcement that talks were due Sunday June 21, the 32 killed in Lebanon despite the renewed ceasefire, the unresolved toll dispute, and the still-lifted US naval blockade are all documented. Open sources cannot yet establish whether the re-closure is transient leverage that the Sunday talks resolve or the leading edge of a breakdown.",
     "evidentiary_status_short": "Same June 20 facts (IRGC closes Hormuz over Israel's Lebanon strikes + Iran 'reciprocal action' / 'ready to move forward if Israel abides' + Vance departs for Bürgenstock + Pakistan says talks due Sunday + 32 killed in Lebanon despite renewed ceasefire + toll dispute open + US blockade still lifted) read as coercive bargaining vs. a deal unravelling.",
     "public_assertion_by": [
