   "headline": "GROUND INVASION TRACKER — DAY 115 (June 21): STATUS UNCHANGED AT 'NO_GROUND_TROOPS' AND STILL DE-ESCALATORY ON THE US SIDE EVEN AS THE DEAL FRAYED. IRAN RE-CLOSED THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ ON JUNE 20 — A COERCIVE IRANIAN MARITIME MOVE OVER ISRAEL'S LEBANON STRIKES, NOT A US GROUND OPERATION; THE US NAVAL BLOCKADE REMAINS LIFTED (ENFORCEMENT 'CEASED') AND THE US POSTURE IS DRAWDOWN (THE USS ABRAHAM LINCOLN CSG FLAGGED AS LIKELY FIRST TO ROTATE OUT). STILL ~50,000 US TROOPS IN THE CENTCOM AOR; NO NEW DEPLOYMENT; NO KHARG ASSAULT; NO THIRD ARG / NEW MEU; NO DRAFT. THE PENTAGON TOLD LAWMAKERS IT NEEDS ~$80B FOR THE WAR — A BUDGET/SUSTAINMENT ASK, NOT A MOBILIZATION ORDER. THE ONLY US ESCALATION LEVERS REMAIN AIR-POWER ('RESTART THE BOMBING') AND THE TOLLS DISPUTE, NOT GROUND.",
   "summary": "JUNE 21 (Day 115): No change to the ground-invasion picture on the US side, which stayed de-escalatory even as the deal entered its most precarious stretch. The day's defining development — Iran's June 20 re-closure of the Strait of Hormuz — is a coercive Iranian maritime move (protest at Israel's continued Lebanon strikes, framed as a US failure to enforce the MOU), the structural opposite of, and unconnected to, any US ground operation: the US naval blockade itself remains lifted (CENTCOM enforcement 'ceased'), and the US posture is drawdown, with The War Zone flagging the USS Abraham Lincoln carrier strike group as likely first to rotate out of theatre. There remain ~50,000 US troops in the CENTCOM area of responsibility with no new deployment ordered; no Kharg assault has been ordered or executed; no third amphibious ready group or new Marine Expeditionary Unit has been ordered (USS Tripoli ARG / 31st MEU remains on station in a routine posture, its enforcement mission wound down); and there is no draft signal. The Pentagon's request to lawmakers for roughly $80 billion for the Iran war is a budget/sustainment ask, not a mobilization order. The 82nd Airborne forward headquarters remains a fading standing forcible-entry contingency, not an execution order. The only US escalation levers on the table are air-power (Trump's standing 'restart the bombing' threat) and the tolls dispute — neither a ground move.",
   "details": [
    "TROOP LEVEL: Still ~50,000 US troops in the CENTCOM AOR (official figure, holding since March 2026); no net change and no new ground deployment ordered. The Pentagon told lawmakers it needs ~$80B for the Iran war and other expenses — a sustainment/budget request, not a mobilization or activation order. (The War Zone; WSJ via Military.com, June 15-19, 2026.)",
    "HORMUZ RE-CLOSURE IS AN IRANIAN MOVE, NOT A US GROUND ACTION (June 20): Iran's IRGC re-closed the Strait of Hormuz over Israel's Lebanon strikes; this is Iranian coercive leverage, not a US operation, and does not involve any US ground force. The US naval blockade remains lifted (enforcement 'ceased'); the chokepoint contest continues to run through negotiation, coercion and blockade — not a Kharg landing. (Al Jazeera, June 20, 2026.)",
    "KHARG ISLAND: The seizure pathway remains superseded. No US ground troops are on Kharg and no assault has been ordered or executed; Iran's re-closure of Hormuz changes the maritime picture but not the ground one. (Al Jazeera; The War Zone, June 18-20, 2026.)",
    "MARINE / AMPHIBIOUS — POSTURE TRENDING TO ROTATION: No new amphibious or Marine deployment ordered. The USS Tripoli (LHA-7) ARG / 31st MEU (~2,200 Marines) remains forward-deployed in a routine posture (not invasion staging), its blockade-enforcement role wound down; two carriers (Lincoln, Bush) plus 20+ surface combatants are in-theatre, with the Lincoln CSG flagged as likely first to rotate out. No third ARG or additional MEU ordered. (The War Zone, 'America's Naval Force Posture Largely Unchanged...,' June 15, 2026.)",
    "DRAFT / MOBILIZATION: No draft or conscription signal. The Hormuz re-closure, the Lebanon flare-up and the Pentagon's $80B funding request produced no Selective Service action, no expanded Guard/Reserve call-up language, and no brigade-scale activation; the House's June 3 war-powers resolution (215-208) and GOP Sen. Wicker's 'negotiates away war gains' critique keep the domestic debate in a constrain-the-executive register. The only standing item is the pre-existing administrative plan for automatic Selective Service registration of men 18-26 by end-2026 (not a draft activation); fact-checkers (PolitiFact, Poynter) continue to rate reinstatement claims false.",
    "ASSESSMENT: Ground-invasion execution probability is LOW and stable at Day 115. The week's stress — Iran's Hormuz re-closure, the violated Lebanon ceasefire, the unresolved toll dispute, and the only-tentatively-reconvening Swiss talks — bears on the deal's durability, not on any US ground move; every live failure mode points back to the air/naval/missile register. The disconfirmation holds."
   ],
   "prediction_impact": "Reinforces the disconfirmation while clarifying its boundary. The ground-invasion thesis required US troops moving toward Iran, a Kharg seizure, an amphibious buildup, or a draft; June 20-21 delivered none of these — the US posture is drawdown (Lincoln CSG rotating out, blockade enforcement ceased), and even the $80B Pentagon ask is sustainment, not mobilization. The day's dramatic event, Iran re-closing Hormuz, is a chokepoint move by Tehran — coercion over a contested deal — which is the inverse of the predicted US 'taking' of the strait and Kharg by force. Even the worst live failure mode (deal collapse over Lebanon/tolls) points to resumed US bombing, not a ground push.",
   "source": "The War Zone, 'America's Naval Force Posture Largely Unchanged...,' June 15, 2026; Al Jazeera live updates, June 20, 2026; WSJ via Military.com, 'Pentagon tells lawmakers it needs $80 billion for Iran war,' June 19, 2026; Navy Times, June 18, 2026; PolitiFact / Poynter draft fact-checks, 2026."
