   "theatre": null,
   "headline": "DAY 112 — CEASEFIRE DAY 72 / BLOCKADE DAY 67 — G7 WRAPS WITH THE DEAL INTACT BUT THE TEXT STILL UNRELEASED AND LEBANON UNRESOLVED. TRUMP CLOSED THE G7 SUMMIT IN THE FRENCH ALPS ON JUNE 17 WITH A NEWS CONFERENCE DEFENDING WHAT HE CALLED 'THE TRUMP DEAL' — A 14-POINT AGREEMENT HE SAYS COMMITS IRAN TO NEVER HAVING A NUCLEAR WEAPON AND TO REOPENING THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ — WHILE WARNING THE US 'MAY RESTART ITS BOMBING CAMPAIGN' IF IRAN DOES NOT ABIDE. G7 LEADERS THREW THEIR SUPPORT BEHIND THE FRAMEWORK (MACRON: 'A VERY GOOD DEAL'), BUT NO FULL MOU TEXT HAD BEEN RELEASED PUBLICLY DAYS AFTER THE 'WITHIN 24-48 HOURS' PLEDGE; OFFICIALS DESCRIBE THE ~PAGE-AND-A-HALF DOCUMENT AS 'A COMMITMENT TO NEGOTIATE' WITH A SECOND PHASE OF NUCLEAR TALKS AND NO STATED PLAN YET FOR IRAN'S NUCLEAR CAPABILITIES. THE IN-PERSON SIGNING REMAINS SET FOR FRIDAY JUNE 19 IN SWITZERLAND (BÜRGENSTOCK/LUZERN), WITH VP VANCE REPRESENTING THE US. THE LEBANON FAULT LINE HARDENED FURTHER: IRAN'S ARAGHCHI INSISTS THE DEAL REQUIRES AN ISRAELI WITHDRAWAL FROM LEBANON; ISRAELI MINISTERS SAID ISRAEL 'WON'T BE BOUND' BY THE IRAN DEAL AND THREATENED TO HIT HEZBOLLAH WITH 'FULL FORCE,' WHILE NETANYAHU VOWED TO STAY IN 'SECURITY ZONES' AND TRUMP REITERATED HE IS 'NOT HAPPY' WITH ISRAEL'S HANDLING OF LEBANON. OIL FELL A FOURTH STRAIGHT SESSION — BRENT ~$79/BBL WEDNESDAY, A THREE-MONTH LOW — ON SUPPLY-RETURN EXPECTATIONS (FRIDAY SIGNING, OPEC+ QUOTAS, UAE OUTPUT). HORMUZ REMAINS CLOSED IN PRACTICE: ADNOC CEO SULTAN AL JABER SAID THE STRAIT IS STILL NOT OPEN BECAUSE IRAN IS CONDITIONING TRAFFIC, WITH ~230 LOADED OIL TANKERS WAITING INSIDE THE GULF. THE GROUND RUNG IS UNCHANGED — NO US TROOPS IN IRAN, NO KHARG ASSAULT, NO DRAFT; THE ONLY US ESCALATION THREAT ON THE TABLE IS A RESUMPTION OF BOMBING, NOT A GROUND OPERATION. THE G7 ALSO DELIVERED A JOINT UKRAINE STATEMENT (AIR-DEFENSE PLEDGES, RUSSIA PRESSURE) THAT TRUMP SIGNED, AND THE UK UNVEILED NEW SANCTIONS ON RUSSIA'S 'SHADOW FLEET.'",
   "summary": "JUNE 18 (Day 112): The June 14 war-ending deal survived the close of the G7 summit, but two unresolved questions — an unreleased text and the Lebanon sticking point — kept the settlement short of consummation. Trump wrapped his final day at the G7 in the French Alps on June 17 with a news conference defending what he branded 'the Trump deal': a 14-point agreement he says includes commitments that Iran 'will never have a nuclear weapon' and that the Strait of Hormuz will reopen. He warned that if Iran does not abide by the agreement, the United States 'may restart its bombing campaign' — an air-power threat, not a ground one. G7 allies endorsed the framework (French President Macron called it 'a very good deal'), yet no full MOU text had been released publicly days after a senior US official's 'within 24 to 48 hours' pledge. Officials and reports continue to describe the document as roughly a page and a half — characterized as essentially a commitment to negotiate, opening a second phase of direct talks on Iran's nuclear program, with no stated plan yet to deal with Iran's nuclear capabilities. The in-person signing remained set for Friday June 19 in Switzerland (Bürgenstock/Luzern), with Vice President Vance representing the United States. The Lebanon fault line hardened: Iran's FM Araghchi reiterated that the deal requires Israel to withdraw from Lebanon, a US official says it does not, Netanyahu pledged to remain in 'security zones,' and Israeli ministers said Israel 'won't be bound' by the Iran deal and threatened to strike Hezbollah with 'full force' if attacked. Trump repeated he is 'not happy' with Israel's handling of its 'minor war' in Lebanon. Oil fell a fourth straight session, with Brent near $79/bbl on Wednesday — a three-month low — pressured by expected supply returns once the deal takes effect (Friday signing, higher OPEC+ quotas, additional UAE output after its wartime OPEC exit). Despite the ceasefire, the strait remained closed in practice: ADNOC CEO Sultan Al Jaber said Hormuz is still not open because Iran is restricting and conditioning traffic, with about 230 loaded oil tankers waiting inside the Gulf and transit expected to resume only once the agreement takes effect. On the side tracks, the G7 issued a joint statement pledging renewed support for Ukraine — including strengthened air defense, per Zelensky — and pressure on Russia, which Trump signed onto; the UK unveiled dozens of new sanctions targeting Russia's 'shadow fleet' and sanctions-evasion networks, and the G7 weighed tougher measures on Russia's energy sector.",
   "details": [
    "TRUMP CLOSES THE G7 DEFENDING 'THE TRUMP DEAL'; THREATENS TO 'RESTART BOMBING' IF IRAN BREAKS IT (June 17, 2026): Wrapping his final day at the G7 summit in the French Alps, Trump held a news conference defending the 14-point agreement aimed at ending the conflict with Iran, calling it 'the Trump deal.' He said the agreement includes statements that Iran will never have a nuclear weapon and that the Strait of Hormuz will reopen, and warned that if Iran does not abide by the agreement, the US 'may restart its bombing campaign.' The threat is explicitly an air-power resumption, not a ground operation. (WGME/Sinclair; NBC News live blog, June 17, 2026.)",
    "G7 ALLIES ENDORSE THE FRAMEWORK; TEXT STILL UNRELEASED, 'A COMMITMENT TO NEGOTIATE' (June 17, 2026): G7 leaders threw their support behind the tentative US-Iran agreement to reopen Hormuz and extend the ceasefire, with French President Macron calling it 'a very good deal' that 'puts a stop to a situation of great instability.' But no MOU text had been released publicly days after the 'within 24-48 hours' pledge. Officials describe the document as about a page and a half — essentially a commitment to negotiate, launching a second phase of direct talks on Iran's nuclear program, with no stated plan yet to deal with Iran's nuclear capabilities. (PBS NewsHour; NewsNation; NBC News, June 17, 2026.)",
    "FRIDAY JUNE 19 SWISS SIGNING HOLDS; VANCE TO REPRESENT THE US (June 17, 2026): The in-person signing of the MOU remained scheduled for Friday June 19 in Switzerland (Swiss FM: Bürgenstock resort near Luzern; some reports still cite Geneva), with Vice President JD Vance slated to represent the United States. Vance characterized the MOU as 'about a page and a half' and 'a very general document.' (CNN; Al Jazeera; Fortune, June 15-17, 2026.)",
    "LEBANON HARDENS: ISRAELI MINISTERS SAY ISRAEL 'WON'T BE BOUND' BY THE IRAN DEAL (June 16-17, 2026): Iran's FM Araghchi reiterated the deal requires Israel to withdraw from Lebanon ('the war has not fully come to an end' without it); a US official says the deal 'did not call for an Israeli withdrawal.' Netanyahu said Israel's 'struggle is not over' and pledged to hold 'security zones' in southern Lebanon, while Israeli ministers said Israel will not be bound by the Iran deal and threatened to hit Hezbollah with 'full force' if struck. Trump said he is 'not happy with the way Israel has handled themselves with Lebanon and with Hezbollah.' (NPR; CBS News; The Times of Israel; PBS NewsHour, June 16-17, 2026.)",
    "OIL FALLS A FOURTH STRAIGHT SESSION TO A THREE-MONTH LOW (June 17, 2026): Brent traded around $79/bbl on Wednesday after falling for four straight sessions — a three-month low — pressured by expectations of increased supply once the interim deal takes effect Friday. Analysts cited the immediate resumption of Iranian oil exports, the expected resumption of third-country tanker transits through Hormuz, higher OPEC+ export quotas, and increased UAE production after the UAE exited the cartel during the conflict. De-escalation/deal pricing, not ground-commitment pricing. (IG International; TradingEconomics, June 16-17, 2026.)",
    "HORMUZ STILL CLOSED IN PRACTICE — ~230 TANKERS WAITING (June 17, 2026): ADNOC CEO Sultan Al Jaber said the Strait of Hormuz is still not open despite the ceasefire because Iran is restricting and conditioning traffic, and that about 230 loaded oil tankers are waiting inside the Gulf. Third-country tankers are expected to resume transit only once the agreement takes effect, with additional supplies then boosting global refinery inventories. (Industry reporting via Wikipedia '2026 Strait of Hormuz crisis,' June 2026.)",
    "GROUND-INVASION RUNG UNCHANGED — THE ESCALATION THREAT IS AIR, NOT GROUND (June 17-18, 2026): No new ground deployment was ordered; ~50,000 US troops remain in the CENTCOM AOR. The deal's terms (blockade lifted within 30 days, US forces withdrawn from around Iran, Hormuz reopened by negotiated international mine-clearing) point toward an eventual drawdown. The only US escalation threat on the table — Trump's warning to 'restart the bombing campaign' if Iran breaks the deal — is an air/naval threat, not a ground operation. No Kharg assault, no new ARG/MEU, no draft signal. (Derived from G7 coverage, June 17, 2026.)",
    "SIDE TRACKS — G7 UKRAINE STATEMENT AND NEW UK RUSSIA SANCTIONS (June 17, 2026): The G7 issued a joint statement pledging renewed support for Ukraine — including strengthened air defense, per Zelensky — and pressure on Russia, which Trump signed onto despite past reluctance. The UK unveiled dozens of new sanctions targeting Russia's 'illicit shadow fleet' and financial networks, and the G7 weighed tougher sanctions on Russia's energy sector. (NBC News; WGME/Sinclair, June 17, 2026.)"
   ],
   "source": "WGME/Sinclair, 'Trump wraps up G7 summit focused on Iran agreement, Ukraine support and AI,' June 17, 2026; NBC News live blog, 'Trump and Iran sign peace deal, G7 members pledge tougher sanctions on Russia,' June 17, 2026; PBS NewsHour, 'Questions linger over U.S.-Iran deal as details remain murky,' June 17, 2026; PBS NewsHour, 'Iran says the deal to end the war with the U.S. requires Israel to withdraw from Lebanon,' June 16, 2026; NewsNation, 'Trump Iran peace deal: Key questions on nuclear talks, Lebanon and Hormuz ahead of G7,' June 2026; NPR, 'Israel's stance on Lebanon ceasefire complicates U.S.-Iran deal,' June 16, 2026; CBS News live updates, 'Iran says deal with U.S. requires Israeli forces to leave Lebanon,' June 16-17, 2026; The Times of Israel, 'Ministers say Israel won't be bound by Iran deal,' June 2026; IG International, 'US-Iran June deal: oil price outlook and key levels,' June 16, 2026; TradingEconomics, Brent crude, June 17, 2026; Wikipedia, '2026 Strait of Hormuz crisis' (ADNOC Al Jaber, ~230 tankers waiting), June 2026; CNN live news, 'Trump and Vance virtually sign US-Iran agreement,' June 15, 2026; Fortune, 'Iran pushes differing versions of deal as U.S. sticks to timeline,' June 14, 2026.",
   "prediction_impact": "June 18 extends the cleanest disconfirmation to date of Predictive History's imminent-US-ground-invasion / resource-seizure framing for Iran: the deal survived the close of the G7, allies endorsed it, and the core mechanism remains a negotiated reopening of Hormuz plus withdrawal of US forces and a lifting of the naval blockade — the structural inverse of the predicted seizure of the strait, Kharg Island, and Iranian oil infrastructure. Notably, the one escalation threat Trump put on the table at the G7 — to 'restart the bombing campaign' if Iran breaks the deal — is an air-power threat, reinforcing that even the contingency path is air/naval, not a ground invasion. The disconfirmation remains not fully consummated: the in-person Friday June 19 ceremony has not occurred, the full text is still unreleased days after the '24-48 hours' pledge, Hormuz is still closed in practice (ADNOC: ~230 tankers waiting), and the Lebanon dispute has hardened — Israeli ministers now say Israel 'won't be bound' by the Iran deal — keeping a kinetic-resumption tripwire live. A signed, holding reopening of Hormuz would be the decisive falsification of the ground-invasion thesis; a collapse over Lebanon would return the question to active air/naval conflict rather than vindicate a ground invasion.",
   "alternate_readings": [
