     "reading": "The June 14-18 deal is read either as a now-broadly-endorsed, war-ending settlement that survived the full G7 (allies behind it, Macron calling it 'a very good deal,' oil at a three-month low, a Friday Swiss signing locked) or as an increasingly stalled/contested announcement, because the full text is STILL unreleased days after the 'within 24-48 hours' pledge, the document is described as merely 'a commitment to negotiate' with no plan yet for Iran's nuclear capabilities, Hormuz is still closed in practice (~230 tankers waiting), and Israeli ministers now say Israel 'won't be bound' by it.",
     "evidentiary_status": "Both readings rest on the same June 16-17 record: the G7 endorsement, Macron's 'very good deal,' Trump's 'Trump deal' news conference, the fourth straight oil decline to ~$79, and the held Friday-signing date are documented, and so are the still-unreleased text, the 'commitment to negotiate' characterization, ADNOC's statement that Hormuz remains closed with ~230 tankers waiting, and the Israeli ministers' 'won't be bound' / 'full force' statements. Open sources cannot yet establish whether Friday's ceremony occurs and the Hormuz reopening and ceasefire hold, or whether the Lebanon dispute reopens the kinetic track before the deal is executed.",
